var data={"123":{"blocks":{}},"en":{"blocks":{"acceleration_of":{"name":"acceleration_of","markdown":"# Acceleration of\n\nReports the acceleration value from one axis on the Analog Accelerometer.\n\n![acceleration_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/acceleration_of/acceleration_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Acceleration of** reports a range from **-2.0 G to 2.0 G** or **-6.0 G to 6.0 G** depending upon the jumper setting on the Analog Accelerometer.\n\nChoose which Accelerometer to use.\n\n![acceleration_of_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/acceleration_of/acceleration_of_device.png)\n\nThe reporter block **acceleration of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![acceleration_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/acceleration_of/acceleration_of_inserted.png)"},"act":{"name":"act","markdown":"# Act\n\nAct sad, happy, or crazy by having the 123 Robot perform a sequence of drive, turn, and sound behaviors.\n\n![act_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/act/act_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Act** block has the 123 Robot perform a sequence of behaviors to mimic a sad, happy, or crazy emotion.\n\n* **sad:** the 123 Robot will drive in reverse, turn left, then right, play an ‘uh oh’ sound, and then will drive forward to mimic a sad behavior.\n* **happy:** the 123 Robot will turn right 360 degrees and play a laughing sound to mimic a happy behavior.\n* **crazy:** the 123 Robot will turn left in a circle, and then turn right in a circle, all while playing a ‘loopy’ sound to mimic a crazy behavior.\n\n\n![select_act](static/help/vr/blocks/en/act/select_act.png)"},"activate_emergency_stop":{"name":"activate_emergency_stop","markdown":"# Activate emergency stop\n\nActivates the emergency stop (or E-stop) button for the V5 Robotic Arm.\n\n![estop_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/activate_emergency_stop/estop_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe emergency stop button in the Workcell will shut off any V5 Smart Motors and Sensors connected to the V5 Brain. \n\n![estop_robot](static/help/vr/blocks/en/activate_emergency_stop/estop_robot.png)\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![select_estop_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/activate_emergency_stop/select_estop_arm.png)\n\nThe **activate emergency stop** block is often used with the When bumper block from the Events category. This ensures that all V5 Smart Motors and Sensors connected to the V5 Brain will shut off once the Bumper Switch (E-stop) is pressed.\n![estop_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/activate_emergency_stop/estop_example.png)"},"add":{"name":"add","markdown":"# Add\n\nAdds any two values together and reports the sum.\n\n![add](static/help/vr/blocks/en/add/add.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Add** can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nThe **Add** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces, and can accept other circular blocks in it as well. \n\n\nAdd two numbers.\n\n![add_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/add/add_numbers.png)\n\nAdd with values from variables.\n\n![add_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/en/add/add_variables.png)  \n\nAdd with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![add_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/add/add_sensors.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Add**, enter the quantities to be added before and after the `+`. \n\nAdd two numbers. \n\n![switch_add_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/add/switch_add_numbers.png)\n\nAdd with values from variables.\n\n![switch_add_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/add/switch_add_variable.png)\n\nAdd with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![switch_add_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/add/switch_add_sensor.png)"},"ai_sees":{"name":"ai_sees","markdown":"# Rover sees\n\nReports whether the VR Rover sees detectable objects using the built-in AI technology.\n\n![ai_sees](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees/ai_sees.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose whether to report if the VR Rovers's built-in AI sees minerals, enemies, base, obstacles, or hazards using the drop-down.\n\n![ai_sees_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees/ai_sees_choose.png)\n\nThis Boolean block reports a true or false value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![ai_sees_wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees/ai_sees_wait_until.png)\n\nThe **Rover sees** Boolean block reports true when the selected option is less than 1000 millimeters (mm) away from the VR Rover and within its field of view.\n\nThe field of view of the VR Rover is 40 degrees. This can be seen in the mini-map on the Playground indicated by the grey translucent cone from the front of the VR Rover.\n\nThe block will report false when the selected option is not within the sight range of the VR Rover. \n\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Rover sees** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![ai_sees_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees/ai_sees_monitor.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n"},"ai_sees_direction":{"name":"ai_sees_direction","markdown":"# Rover direction\n\nReports the direction from the VR Rover to minerals, enemies, or base using the built-in AI technology.\n\n![ai_sees_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_direction/ai_sees_direction.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose whether to report the direction of minerals, enemies, or base using the drop-down.\n\n![ai_sees_direction_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_direction/ai_sees_direction_choose.png)\n\n### Enemy and minerals Direction\n\nThe **Rover direction** reporter block reports the direction in degrees from the object when the enemy or minerals is less than 1000 millimeters (mm) away from the VR Rover and within its field of view.\n\nThe field of view of the VR Rover is 40 degrees. This can be seen in the mini-map on the Playground indicated by the grey translucent cone from the front of the VR Rover.\n\nThis block reports a numeric value from -40 degrees to 40 degrees and is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![ai_sees_direction_wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_direction/ai_sees_direction_wait_until.png)\n\nIf there are multiple enemies or pieces of minerals detected at once, the block will report the direction of the closest object.\n\n### Base Direction\n\nIf 'base' is selected for the block, the direction will be reported even if the VR Rover's base is not in the field of view. The VR Rover's AI technology allows it to always detect base.\n\nThis block will report a value from -180 to 180 degrees when reporting the direction to base.\n\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Rover direction** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![ai_sees_direction_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_direction/ai_sees_direction_monitor.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n"},"ai_sees_distance":{"name":"ai_sees_distance","markdown":"# Rover distance\n\nReports the distance from the VR Rover to minerals, enemies, obstacles, hazards, or base using the built-in AI technology.\n\n![ai_sees_distance](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_distance/ai_sees_distance.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose whether to report the distance of minerals, enemies, obstacles, hazards or base using the drop-down.\n\n![ai_sees_distance_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_distance/ai_sees_distance_choose.png)\n\nChoose whether distance is reported in millimeters or inches.\n\n![ai_sees_distance_units](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_distance/ai_sees_distance_units.png)\n\nThe **Rover distance** reporter block reports the distance in millimeters or inches from the object when the enemy, obstacle, hazard, or minerals is less than 1000 millimeters (mm) away from the VR Rover and within its field of view.\n\nThe field of view of the VR Rover is 40 degrees. This can be seen in the mini-map on the Playground indicated by the grey translucent cone from the front of the VR Rover.\n\nThis block reports a numeric value and is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![ai_sees_distance_if](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_distance/ai_sees_distance_if.png)\n\nIf there are multiple obstacles, enemies, or pieces of minerals detected at once, the block will report the distance of the closest object.\n\n### Base Distance\n\nIf 'base' is selected for the block, the distance will be reported even if the VR Rover's base is not in the field of view. The VR Rover's AI technology allows it to always detect base.\n\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Rover distance** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![ai_sees_distance_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_distance/ai_sees_distance_monitor.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n"},"ai_sees_location":{"name":"ai_sees_location","markdown":"# Rover location\n\nReports the X or Y coordinate location of minerals, enemies, obstacles, hazards, or base using the AI technology built into the VR Rover. \n\n![ai_sees_location](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_location/ai_sees_location.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose whether to report the coordinate location of minerals, enemies, obstacles, hazards or base using the drop-down.\n\n![ai_sees_location_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_location/ai_sees_location_choose.png)\n\nChoose whether to return the X coordinate, or Y coordinate.\n\n![ai_sees_location_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_location/ai_sees_location_coord.png)\n\nChoose whether the coordinate location is reported in millimeters or inches.\n\n![ai_sees_location_units](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_location/ai_sees_location_units.png)\n\nThe **Rover location** reporter block reports the coordinate location of minerals, enemies, obstacles, or hazards when the object is less than 1000 millimeters (mm) away from the VR Rover and within its field of view.\n\nThe field of view of the VR Rover is 40 degrees. This can be seen in the mini-map on the Playground indicated by the grey translucent cone from the front of the VR Rover.\n\nThis block reports a numeric value and is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![ai_sees_location_if](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_location/ai_sees_location_if.png)\n\nIf there are multiple obstacles, enemies, or minerals detected at once, the block will report the coordinate location of the closest object.\n\n### Base & Rover location\n\nIf 'base' or 'rover' is selected for the block, the location will be reported even if the VR Rover's base is not in the field of view. The VR Rover's AI technology allows it to always detect the base and be aware of its current position.\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Rover location** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![ai_sees_location_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_sees_location/ai_sees_location_monitor.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n"},"ai_smells":{"name":"ai_smells","markdown":"# Rover detects\n\nReports whether the VR Rover detects an enemy or minerals using the built-in AI technology.\n\n![ai_detect](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_smells/ai_detect.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose whether to report if the VR Rover detects minerals or enemies using the drop-down.\n\n![ai_detect_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_smells/ai_detect_choose.png)\n\nThis Boolean block reports a true or false value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![ai_detects_wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_smells/ai_detects_wait_until.png)\n\nThe **Rover detects** Boolean block reports true when the VR Rover detects the selected option (minerals or enemy). It will report false when the selected option is not within the detect radius of the VR Rover.\n\nThe detect radius of the VR Rover is 800 millimeters (mm). This can be seen in the mini-map on the Playground indicated by the yellow circle around the VR Rover.\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Rover detects** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![ai_detect_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/ai_smells/ai_detect_monitor.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n"},"and":{"name":"and","markdown":"# And\n\nReports if two Boolean conditions are both true. \n\n![and](static/help/vr/blocks/en/and/and.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **and** block reports **true** when both conditions are **true**. \n\nThe **and** block reports **false** when one, or both, of the conditions are **false**.\n\nThe **and** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and fits in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![and_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/en/and/and_shape.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will check if the **FrontEye** is near an object and if that object is red.\n\n![and_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/and/and_sensors.png)\n\nThe **and** block will return **true** when both the **FrontEye** is near objects and the **FrontEye** is red.\n\nThe **and** block will return **false** when no objects are near the **FrontEye**, the **FrontEye** is not red, or both.\n\n![and_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/en/and/and_truth.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **And**, specify the Boolean conditions that will be evaluated in the project before and after the `and`.\n\nIf no Boolean conditions are specified, the Switch Python command will default to `False and False`.\n\n![switch_and_false](static/help/vr/blocks/en/and/switch_and_false.png)\n\nIn this example, the Switch Python **And** command will report as **true** when both Boolean conditions of the Front Eye Sensor near object and Front Eye Sensor detects RED report as **true**.\n\n![switch_and_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/and/switch_and_example.png)"},"angle_of":{"name":"angle_of","markdown":"# Angle of\n\nReturns the angular position of the Potentiometer.\n\n![angle_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/angle_of/angle_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Angle of** reports a range from **0% to 100%** or **0.0 to 250.0 degrees** for a V5 Potentiometer.\n\nFor a V5 Potentiometer V2, the degree range is from **0.0 to 330.0 degrees**.\n\nChoose which Potentiometer to use.\n\n![angle_of_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/angle_of/angle_of_device.png)\n\nChoose to report the value in % or degrees.\n\n![angle_of_block_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/angle_of/angle_of_parameters.png)\n\nThe reporter block **angle of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![angle_of_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/angle_of/angle_of_inserted.png)\n"},"arm_get_joint_value":{"name":"arm_get_joint_value","markdown":"# Joint potentiometer value\n\nReports the potentiometer value of one of the four joints on the V5 Workcell.\n\n![joint_potentiometer_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_joint_value/joint_potentiometer_value.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **joint potentiometer value** reporter block is used to report the potentiometer value of one of the four joints on the Workcell. This reporter block can be used when determining mastering values.\n\n**Joint potentiometer value** reports a range of **0 to 4095**.\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![joint_potentiometer_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_joint_value/joint_potentiometer_arm.png)\n\nChoose which joint to report. \n\n![joint_option_potentiometer_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_joint_value/joint_option_potentiometer_value.png)\n\nThe **joint potentiometer value** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![print_potentiometer_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_joint_value/print_potentiometer_value.png)"},"arm_get_position":{"name":"arm_get_position","markdown":"# Arm position\n\nReports the current x, y, or z-coordinate position of the arm on the Workcell.\n\n![arm_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_position/arm_position_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **arm position** reporter block is used to report the current x, y, or z-coordinate position of the arm on the Workcell.\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use. \n\n![arm_position_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_position/arm_position_arm.png)\n\nChoose which axis to report. \n\n![arm_position_axis](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_position/arm_position_axis.png)\n\nThe **arm position** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![arm_position_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_position/arm_position_inserted.png)\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the arm on the Workcell will move to the coordinate (6, 0, 4). Then the **print** block will print the arm's current position on the x-axis, reported from the **arm position** block, to the Console.\n\n![arm_position_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/arm_get_position/arm_position_example.png)"},"battery_current":{"name":"battery_current","markdown":"# Battery current\n\nReports the current of the V5 Brain's battery.\n\n![battery_current_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/battery_current/battery_current_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Battery current** reports a range from **0.0 amps to 20.0 amps**.\n\nThe reporter block **battery current** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![battery_current_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/battery_current/battery_current_inserted.png)\n\n\n"},"battery_level":{"name":"battery_level","markdown":"# Battery level\n\nReports the charge level of the V5 Brain's battery.\n\n![battery_capacity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/battery_level/battery_capacity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Battery level** reports a range from **0% to 100%**.\n\nThe reporter block **battery level** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![battery_capacity_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/battery_level/battery_capacity_inserted.png)"},"battery_voltage":{"name":"battery_voltage","markdown":"# Battery voltage\n\nReports the voltage of the V5 Brain's battery.\n\n![battery_voltage_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/battery_voltage/battery_voltage_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Battery voltage** reports a range from **10.0 volts to 14.4 volts**.\n\nThe reporter block **battery voltage** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![battery_voltage_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/battery_voltage/battery_voltage_inserted.png)\n"},"boolean_variable":{"name":"boolean_variable","markdown":"# Boolean variable\n\nReports the value of a Boolean variable.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable/boolean_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Boolean variable** blocks are used to report a **true** or **false** value stored inside of the variable. Use the **set boolean variable** block to set or update the value of a boolean variable.\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable/boolean_set_value.png)\n\nThe **boolean variable** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nBoolean variables are created using the **Make a Boolean** button.\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable/boolean_button.png)  \n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo change what variable is being reported, type the name of the variable into the **Switch Boolean** block. \n\n![switch_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable/switch_boolean_variable.png)\n"},"boolean_variable_virtual":{"name":"boolean_variable_virtual","markdown":"# Boolean variable\n\nReports the value of a Boolean variable.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual/boolean_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Boolean variable** blocks are used to report a **true** or **false** value stored inside of the variable. Use the **set boolean variable** block to set or update the value of a boolean variable.\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual/boolean_set_value.png)\n\nThe **boolean variable** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nBoolean variables are created using the **Make a Boolean** button.\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual/boolean_button.png)  \n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of a **boolean** block, select the checkbox next to its reporter block.\n\n![boolean_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual/boolean_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the **boolean** reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![boolean_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual/boolean_monitor.png)\n"},"boolean_variable_virtual_viqc":{"name":"boolean_variable_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Boolean variable\n\nReports the value of a Boolean variable.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual_viqc/boolean_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Boolean variable** blocks are used to report a **true** or **false** value stored inside of the variable. Use the **set boolean variable** block to set or update the value of a boolean variable.\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual_viqc/boolean_set_value.png)\n\nThe **boolean variable** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual_viqc/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nBoolean variables are created using the **Make a Boolean** button.\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual_viqc/boolean_button.png)  \n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of a **boolean** block, select the checkbox next to its reporter block.\n\n![boolean_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual_viqc/boolean_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the **boolean** reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![boolean_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/boolean_variable_virtual_viqc/boolean_monitor.png)"},"break":{"name":"break","markdown":"# Break\n\nExits a repeating loop immediately.\n\n![break](static/help/vr/blocks/en/break/break.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nWhen added to a repeating 'C' block, the **break** block will exit the loop it is currently in. \n\n## Example\n\nThis example will move the Drivetrain forward and check if the FrontEye is near an object. If an object is detected the **break** will cause program execution to exit out of the forever block. The **stop driving** block is then executed, and the Drivetrain stops.\n\n![break_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/break/break_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Break** does not require any parameters in the command.\n\n![switch_break](static/help/vr/blocks/en/break/switch_break.png)"},"bright_object":{"name":"bright_object","markdown":"# Eye detects bright object\n\nReports if the Eye Sensor senses a bright or dark value.\n\n![eye_bright_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bright_object/bright_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\nThe **Eye detects bright object** block reports **TRUE** when the Eye Sensor senses a brightness value greater than 70%.\n\nThe **Eye detects bright object** block reports **FALSE** when the Eye Sensor senses a brightness less than or equal to 70%.\n\nThe **Eye detects bright object** block is a Boolean block that reports a **TRUE** or **FALSE** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![found_object_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bright_object/found_object_condition.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Eye detects bright object** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![found_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bright_object/found_object_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the Boolean block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![found_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bright_object/found_object_monitor.png)"},"brightness_of":{"name":"brightness_of","markdown":"# Brightness of\n\nReports the amount of light detected by the Light Sensor.\n\n![brightness_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of/brightness_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Brightness of** reports a range from **0% to 100%**.\n\nChoose which Light Sensor to use.\n\n![brightness_of_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of/brightness_of_color.png)\n\nThe reporter block **brightness of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![brightness_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of/brightness_of_inserted.png)\n\n"},"brightness_of_go":{"name":"brightness_of_go","markdown":"# Brightness of\n\nReports the brightness of the object detected by the Eye Sensor.\n\n![brightness_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_go/brightness_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Brightness of** block reports a range from 0% to 100%.\n\nThe Eye Sensor will report a higher brightness value for a brighter object and a lower brightness value for a darker object.\n\nThe **Brightness of** reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![brightness_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_go/brightness_of_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Brightness of** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![brightness_of_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_go/brightness_of_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![brightness_of_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_go/brightness_of_monitor.png)\n"},"brightness_of_virtual_viqc":{"name":"brightness_of_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Brightness of\n\nReports the amount of light detected by the VEX IQ Color Sensor.\n\n![brightness_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_virtual_viqc/brightness_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Brightness of** reports a range from **0% to 100%**.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor to use.\n\n![brightness_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_virtual_viqc/brightness_of_color.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Brightness of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![brightness_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_virtual_viqc/brightness_of_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Brightness of** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![brightness_of_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_virtual_viqc/brightness_of_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![brightness_of_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/brightness_of_virtual_viqc/brightness_of_monitor.png)"},"broadcast":{"name":"broadcast","markdown":"# Broadcast\n\nBroadcasts a message to activate any stacks that begin with a **when I receive** block listening for the broadcasted message.\n\n![broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/en/broadcast/broadcast.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe stack that sends the **broadcast** will continue to run blocks at the same time as other stacks that receive the broadcasted message.\n\nChoose which message to **broadcast**. A new message can also be created.\n\n![broadcast_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/broadcast/broadcast_select.png)"},"broadcast_and_wait":{"name":"broadcast_and_wait","markdown":"# Broadcast and wait\n\nBroadcasts a message to activate any stacks that begin with a **when I receive** block listening for the broadcasted message while also pausing the rest of the stack.\n\n![broadcast_and_wait_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/broadcast_and_wait/broadcast_and_wait_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe stack that sends the **broadcast and wait** will pause until the other stacks that receive the broadcasted message have completed.\n\nChoose which message to **broadcast**. A new message can also be created.\n\n![broadcast_and_wait_event_option](static/help/vr/blocks/en/broadcast_and_wait/broadcast_and_wait_event_option.png)"},"bumper":{"name":"bumper","markdown":"# Pressing bumper\n\nReports if the Bumper Switch is pressed.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **true** if the Bumper Switch is being pressed.\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **false** if the Bumper Switch is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nThe **Pressing bumper** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Pressing bumper**, specify the BUMPER that is to be evaluated in the project.\n\n![switch_bumper_pressed_parameter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bumper/switch_bumper_pressed_parameter.png)\n\nSet the BUMPER to indicate which Bumper on the VR Robot to evaluate: \n\n* BUMPER - either `left_bumper` or `right_bumper`\n\nThe Switch Python command for **Pressing bumper** does not require any additional parameters inside the parentheses. In this example, the `left_bumper` will be evaluated.\n\n![switch_bumper_pressed](static/help/vr/blocks/en/bumper/switch_bumper_pressed.png)"},"calibrate_gyro":{"name":"calibrate_gyro","markdown":"# Calibrate\n\nCalibrating the Gyro Sensor / Inertial Sensor is used to reduce the amount of drift generated by Gyro Sensor / Inertial Sensor.\n\n![calibrate_gyro_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/calibrate_gyro/calibrate_gyro_block.png)\n\nDrifting occurs when the Gyro Sensor / Inertial Sensor incorrectly detects movement even though the sensor is not physically moving.\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Gyro Sensor / Inertial Sensor must remain still during the calibration process. The calibration process will take approximately 2 seconds to complete.\n\nChoose which Gyro / Inertial Sensor to use.\n\n![calibrate_gyro_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/en/calibrate_gyro/calibrate_gyro_gyro.png)"},"change_variable":{"name":"change_variable","markdown":"# Change variable\n\nChanges a variable by the given value.\n\n![change_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/change_variable/change_variable.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which variable to use - the selected variable can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![change_variable_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/change_variable/change_variable_options.png)\n\nThe **Change variable** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![change_variable_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/change_variable/change_variable_value.png)\n\n## Example\nThis example will increase the variable that sets the drive speed by 10, each time the **repeat loop** loops.\n\n![change_variable_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/change_variable/change_variable_example2.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo change the value of a variable with a Switch Python command, you can set the variable to the variable value plus your desired increase. \n\n![change_variable_switch](static/help/vr/blocks/en/change_variable/change_variable_switch.png)\n\nThe variable can be increased by any numeric integer value.\n\n![change_variable_switch_timer](static/help/vr/blocks/en/change_variable/change_variable_switch_timer.png)\n"},"clear_all_rows":{"name":"clear_all_rows","markdown":"# Clear all rows\n\nClears all the rows in the Print Console.\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUse the **clear all rows** block to remove all of the lines in the Print Console.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" for one second and then clear the entire display before printing \"Goodbye\".\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Clear all rows** does not require any parameters inside the parentheses.\n\n![switch_clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_all_rows/switch_clear_all_rows.png)\n\n\n"},"clear_all_rows_virtual":{"name":"clear_all_rows_virtual","markdown":"# Clear all rows\n\nClears all the rows in the Print Console.\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_all_rows_virtual/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUse the **clear all rows** block to remove all of the lines in the Print Console.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" for one second and then clear the entire display before printing \"Goodbye\".\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_all_rows_virtual/clear_all_rows_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"clear_all_rows_virtual_viqc":{"name":"clear_all_rows_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Clear all rows\n\nClears all the rows in the Print Console.\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_all_rows_virtual_viqc/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUse the **clear all rows** block to remove all of the lines in the Print Console.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" for one second and then clear the entire display before printing \"Goodbye\".\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_all_rows_virtual_viqc/clear_all_rows_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"clear_row":{"name":"clear_row","markdown":"# Clear row\n\nClears a single row on the VEX IQ Brain's screen.\n\n![clear_row](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_row/clear_row.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Clear row** block can accept integers or numeric blocks between **1 - 5** to set which row to clear.\n\n![row_column_index](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_row/row_column_index.png)  \n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" at row 2 column 1 for three seconds before clearing row 2.\n\n![clear_row_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/clear_row/clear_row_example.png)"},"color_detects_color_go":{"name":"color_detects_color_go","markdown":"# Detects color\n\nReports if the Eye Sensor detects the specified color of an object.\n\n![detects_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_go/detects_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\nChoose which color you want the Eye Sensor to check for.\n\n![detects_color_colors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_go/detects_color_colors.png)\n\nThe **Detects color** block reports TRUE when the Eye Sensor detects the specified color of an object.\n\nThe **Detects color** block reports FALSE when the Eye Sensor does not detect the specified color of an object.\n\nThe **Detects color** block is a Boolean block that reports TRUE or FALSE and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![detects_color_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_go/detects_color_insert.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Detects color** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![detects_color_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_go/detects_color_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the Boolean block selected for monitoring.\n\n![detects_color_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_go/detects_color_monitor.png)\n"},"color_detects_color_virtual_viqc":{"name":"color_detects_color_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Color detects\n\nReports if the VEX IQ Color Sensor detects a specific color.\n\n![color_detects](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_virtual_viqc/color_detects.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Color detects** reports **true** when the Color Sensor detects the selected color.\n\n**Color detects** reports **false** when the Color Sensor detects a different color than the one selected.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor to use.\n\n![color_detects_chosen](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_virtual_viqc/color_detects_chosen.png)\n\nChoose which color to detect.\n\n![color_detects_selected](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_virtual_viqc/color_detects_selected.png)\n\nThe **Color detects** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![color_detects_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_virtual_viqc/color_detects_shape.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Color Red\" or \"Other Color\" depending on whether or not the Color Sensor detects the color \"red\".\n\n![color_detects_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_virtual_viqc/color_detects_example.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Color detects** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![color_detects_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_virtual_viqc/color_detects_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![color_detects_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_detects_color_virtual_viqc/color_detects_monitor.png)"},"color_of_virtual_viqc":{"name":"color_of_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Color of\n\nReports the name of the color detected by the VEX IQ Color Sensor.\n\n![color_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_of_virtual_viqc/color_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Color of** reports one the following colors: **red, red-violet, violet, blue-violet, blue, blue-green, green, yellow-green, yellow, yellow-orange, orange, or red-orange.**\n\nChoose which Color Sensor to use.\n\n![color_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_of_virtual_viqc/color_of_color.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Color of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![color_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_of_virtual_viqc/color_of_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Color of** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![color_of_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_of_virtual_viqc/color_of_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![color_of_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/color_of_virtual_viqc/color_of_monitor.png)\n"},"comment":{"name":"comment","markdown":"# Comment\n\nAllows programmers to write information to help describe their program. \n\n![comment_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/comment/comment_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nProfessional programmers use comments throughout their programs to help others understand their code. Comments can also help with troubleshooting and collaboration. \n\nAdd text into the **Comment** block by first selecting the transparent rectangle and deleting any existing text. Then, type in the new text.\n\n![comment_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/comment/comment_example3.png)\n\n## Example\n\nComments do not change the program or blocks surrounding it.\n\n![comment_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/comment/comment_example2.png)\n\n"},"controller_is_enabled":{"name":"controller_is_enabled","markdown":"# Controller enabled\n\nEnables or disables Controller configured actions from the Devices menu.\n\n![controllerDisable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/controller_is_enabled/controller_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose to either enable or disable the configured Controller actions by selecting the desired state from the dropdown. By default, the Controller is Enabled in every project.\n\n![controllerDisable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/controller_is_enabled/controller_enable_disable.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThe configured action in this example is using the robot's Drivetrain behavior set by the Controller's configuration in the Devices window.\n\n![configured_action](static/help/vr/blocks/en/controller_is_enabled/configured_action.png)\n\nThis example will have the configured Controller disabled when the program starts. \n\nThen, the robot will drive forward for 6 inches.\n\nFinally, the **controller enabled** block will enable the Controller to restore Controller driving behaviors.\n\n![controllerDisable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/controller_is_enabled/controller_example.png)\n"},"controller_play_rumble":{"name":"controller_play_rumble","markdown":"# Controller play rumble\n\nCreates a vibrating effect on your V5 Controller.\n\n![controller_play_rumble](static/help/vr/blocks/en/controller_play_rumble/controller_play_rumble.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **controller play rumble** feature uses the rumble motor in your V5 controller to provide feedback.\n\nChoose which pattern to use.\n\n![controller_play_rumble_pattern](static/help/vr/blocks/en/controller_play_rumble/controller_play_rumble_pattern.png)\n\nChoose which V5 controller to use.\n\n![controller_play_rumble_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/controller_play_rumble/controller_play_rumble_device.png)\n\n\n"},"crash":{"name":"crash","markdown":"# Crash detected\n\nReports if the robot has come in contact with a wall or other object.\n\n![detect_crash](static/help/vr/blocks/en/crash/detect_crash.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Crash detected** reports TRUE if the robot comes in contact with a wall or other object.\n\n**Crash detected** reports FALSE if the robot does **NOT** come in contact with a wall or other object.\n\nThe **Crash detected** Boolean block reports a TRUE or FALSE value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![crash_detected_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/crash/crash_detected_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Crash detected** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![crash_detected_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/crash/crash_detected_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![crash_detected_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/crash/crash_detected_monitor.png)"},"current_of_motor":{"name":"current_of_motor","markdown":"# Current of motor\n\nReports the amount of current a V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group is drawing in amperes (amps).\n\n![current_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/current_of_motor/current_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Current of motor** reports a range from **0.0 to 2.5 amps**. \n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![current_of_motor_options_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/current_of_motor/current_of_motor_options_motor.png)\n\nThe reporter block **current of motor** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![current_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/current_of_motor/current_of_motor_inserted.png)\n"},"cursor_column":{"name":"cursor_column","markdown":"# Cursor column\n\nReports the column number of the V5 Brain or Controller screen cursor location.\n\n![cursor_column_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/cursor_column/cursor_column_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Cursor column** reports a range from **1 to 80**.\n\nThe reporter block **cursor column** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![cursor_column_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/cursor_column/cursor_column_inserted.png)\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Brain allows you to change the size of the font printed on the screen. Changing the font will affect the number of columns available on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* Extra Small - 80 Columns\n* Small - 68 Columns\n* Medium - 48 Columns (Default)\n* Large - 32 Columns\n* Extra Large - 24 Columns\n* Super Large - 16 Columns\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/blocks/en/cursor_column/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Controller does not allow you to change the size of the font printed on the screen.\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* Standard Font - 20 Columns (Default)\n\n![brain_screen_info_2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/cursor_column/v5_controller_rows_columns.jpg)"},"cursor_row":{"name":"cursor_row","markdown":"# Cursor row\n\nReports the row number of the V5 Brain or Controller screen cursor location.\n\n![cursor_row_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/cursor_row/cursor_row_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Cursor row** reports a range from **1 to 20**.\n\nThe reporter block **cursor row** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![cursor_row_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/cursor_row/cursor_row_inserted.png)\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Brain allows you to change the size of the font printed on the screen. Changing the font will affect the number of rows available on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* Extra Small - 20 Rows\n* Small - 16 Rows\n* Medium - 12 Rows (Default)\n* Large - 8 Rows\n* Extra Large - 6 Rows\n* Super Large - 4 Rows\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/blocks/en/cursor_row/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Controller does not allow you to change the size of the font printed on the screen.\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* Standard Font - 3 Rows (Default)\n\n![brain_screen_info_2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/cursor_row/v5_controller_rows_columns.jpg)"},"digital_in_signal":{"name":"digital_in_signal","markdown":"# Digital in\n\nReports if the digital in signal is high.\n\n![digital_in_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/digital_in_signal/digital_in_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe V5 3-Wire ports function at a 5V logic signal voltage level.\n\nThe **digital in** block reports **true** when the digital input signal is high.\n\nThe **digital in** block reports **false** when the digital input signal is low.\n\nChoose which digital in device to use. \n\n![digital_in_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/digital_in_signal/digital_in_select.png)\n\nThe **digital in** Boolean block reports a true or false value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![digital_in_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/digital_in_signal/digital_in_insert.png)\n"},"distance_distance":{"name":"distance_distance","markdown":"# Distance from\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object.\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_distance/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Distance Sensor to use. The **FrontDistance** is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, the **LeftDistance** is mounted on the left side of the robot and faces left, the **RightDistance** is mounted on the right side of the robot and faces right, and the **RearDistance** is mounted on the backside of the robot and faces backward.\n\nThe base VR robot only has the **FrontDistance** as a sensor option.\n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_distance/distance_from_sensorbase.png)\n\nThe enhanced VR Robot also has **LeftDistance**, **RightDistance**, and **RearDistance** sensors.\n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_distance/distance_from_sensor.png)\n\nChoose whether **distance from** is reported in millimeters or inches. \n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_distance/distance_from_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **distance from** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_distance/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Distance from**, there are two parameters that can be changed (DISTANCE and UNITS).\n\n![switch_distance_get_distance_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_distance/switch_distance_get_distance_parameters.png)\n\nSet the DISTANCE Sensor to indicate which sensor on the VR Robot to evaluate, and the UNITS of measure: \n\n* DISTANCE - `front_distance`, `left_distance`, or `right_distance`\n* UNITS - `MM` (millimeters) or `INCHES`\n\nNote that the Left and Right Distance Sensors are only present on the VR Robot in the Maze+ Playground.\n\nIn this example, the value reported will be the distance from an object of the `front_distance` Sensor in millimeters.\n\n![switch_distance_get_distance](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_distance/switch_distance_get_distance.png)\n"},"distance_found":{"name":"distance_found","markdown":"# Distance found object\n\nReports whether the built in Distance sensor has found an object in front of the robot. \n\n![distance_found_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found/distance_found_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Distance Sensor to use. The **FrontDistance** is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, The **LeftDistance** is mounted on the left side of the robot and faces left, the **RightDistance** is mounted on the right side of the robot and faces right, and the **RearDistance** is mounted on the backside of the robot and faces backward.\n\nThe base VR robot only has the **FrontDistance** as a sensor option.\n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found/distance_found_sensorbase.png)\n\nThe enhanced VR Robot also has **LeftDistance**, **RightDistance**, and **RearDistance** sensors.\n\n![distance_found_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found/distance_found_dropdown.png)\n\n**Distance found object** reports **true** when the built in Distance sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view, and within 3000 mm of the sensor.\n\n**Distance found object** reports **false** when the built in Distance sensor does not detect an object or surface within 3000 mm.\n\nThe **distance found object** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![distance_found_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found/distance_found_insert.png)\n\n## Example\n\nIn the Castle Crashers Playground, this example will drive forward if the Distance sensor detects an object, otherwise it will make a 45° right turn.\n\n![distance_found_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found/distance_found_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Distance found object**, specify the DISTANCE Sensor that is to be evaluated in the project.\n\n![switch_distance_found_parameter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found/switch_distance_found_parameter.png)\n\nSet the DISTANCE Sensor to indicate which sensor on the VR Robot to evaluate: \n\n* DISTANCE - `front_distance`, `left_distance`, or `right_distance`\n\nNote that the Left and Right Distance Sensors are only options for the VR Robot in the Maze+ Playground.\n\nThe Switch Python command for **Distance found object** does not require any additional parameters inside the parentheses. In this example, the `front_distance` will be evaluated.\n\n![switch_distance_found](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found/switch_distance_found.png)\n"},"distance_found_object":{"name":"distance_found_object","markdown":"# Distance object found\n\nReports if the Distance sensor sees an object within its field of view.\n\n![distance_found_object_block1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found_object/distance_found_object_block1.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Distance object found** reports true when the Distance sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view.\n\n**Distance object found** reports false when the Distance sensor does not detect an object or surface.\n\nChoose which Distance sensor to use.\n\n![distance_found_object_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found_object/distance_found_object_block2.png)\n\n\nThe **distance object found** block reports **true** or **false** and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![distance_found_object_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found_object/distance_found_object_block3.png)\n"},"distance_found_object_virtual":{"name":"distance_found_object_virtual","markdown":"# Distance found an object\n\nReports if the Distance sensor sees an object within its field of view.\n\n![distance_found_object_block1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found_object_virtual/distance_found_object_block1.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Distance found an object** reports true when the Distance sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view.\n\n**Distance found an object** reports false when the Distance sensor does not detect an object or surface.\n\nChoose which Distance sensor to use.\n\n![distance_found_object_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found_object_virtual/distance_found_object_block2.png)\n\n\nThe **Distance found an object** block reports **true** or **false** and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![distance_found_object_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found_object_virtual/distance_found_object_block3.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Distance found an object** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![distance_found_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found_object_virtual/distance_found_object_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![distance_found_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_found_object_virtual/distance_found_object_monitor.png)"},"distance_from":{"name":"distance_from","markdown":"# Distance from\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object from the Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor.\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Distance from** reports a range from **30mm to 3000mm** or **2 inches to 115 inches**.\n\nChoose which Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor to use.\n\n![distance_from_distance](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from/distance_from_distance.png)\n\nChoose whether **distance from** is reported in inches or millimeters. \n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from/distance_from_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **distance from** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from/distance_from_inserted.png)\n"},"distance_from_virtual_viqc":{"name":"distance_from_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Distance from\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object from the VEX IQ Distance Sensor.\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from_virtual_viqc/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Distance from** reports a range from **24mm to 1000mm** or **1 inch to 40 inches**.\n\nChoose which Distance Sensor to use.\n\n![distance_from_distance](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from_virtual_viqc/distance_from_distance.png)\n\nChoose whether **Distance from** is reported in inches or millimeters. \n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from_virtual_viqc/distance_from_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Distance from** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from_virtual_viqc/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Distance from** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![distance_from_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from_virtual_viqc/distance_from_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![distance_from_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_from_virtual_viqc/distance_from_monitor.png)"},"distance_object_distance":{"name":"distance_object_distance","markdown":"# Object distance\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object from the Distance sensor.\n\n![distance_object_distance_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance/distance_object_distance_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Object distance** reports a range from **20mm to 2000mm**.\n\nChoose which Distance sensor to use.\n\n![distance_object_distance_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance/distance_object_distance_block2.png)\n\nChoose whether **object distance** is reported in inches or millimeters. \n\n![distance_object_distance_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance/distance_object_distance_block3.png)\n\nThe reporter block **object distance** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![distance_object_distance_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance/distance_object_distance_block4.png)\n"},"distance_object_distance_virtual":{"name":"distance_object_distance_virtual","markdown":"# Object distance\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object from the Distance sensor.\n\n![distance_object_distance_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance_virtual/distance_object_distance_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Object distance** reports a range from **20mm to 2000mm**.\n\nChoose which Distance sensor to use.\n\n![distance_object_distance_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance_virtual/distance_object_distance_block2.png)\n\nChoose whether **Object distance** is reported in inches or millimeters. \n\n![distance_object_distance_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance_virtual/distance_object_distance_block3.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Object distance** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![distance_object_distance_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance_virtual/distance_object_distance_block4.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Object distance** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![distance_object_distance_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance_virtual/distance_object_distance_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![distance_object_distance_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_distance_virtual/distance_object_distance_monitor.png)"},"distance_object_size":{"name":"distance_object_size","markdown":"# Object size is\n\nReports if the Distance Sensor detects the specified object size.  \n\n![distance_object_size_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_size/distance_object_size_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe sensor determines the size of the object detected (none, small, medium, large) based on the amount of light reflected and returned to the sensor.\n\nChoose a Distance Sensor to use.\n\n![distance_object_size_block1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_size/distance_object_size_block1.png)\n\nChoose which size of the object you want the sensor to check for.\n\n![distance_object_size_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_size/distance_object_size_block2.png)\n\nThe **Object size is** block reports **true** when the Distance Sensor detects the specified size.\n\nThe **Object size is** block reports **false** if it doesn't detect the specified size.\n\nThe **Object size is** block reports **true** or **false** and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![distance_object_size_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_size/distance_object_size_block3.png)\n"},"distance_object_velocity":{"name":"distance_object_velocity","markdown":"# Object velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of an object in m/s.\n\n![distance_object_velocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_velocity/distance_object_velocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Object velocity** reports the current velocity of an object in m/s (meters per second).\n\nChoose which Distance sensor to use.\n\n![distance_object_velocity_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_velocity/distance_object_velocity_block2.png)\n\n\nThe reporter block **object velocity** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![distance_object_velocity_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/distance_object_velocity/distance_object_velocity_block3.png)\n"},"divide":{"name":"divide","markdown":"# Divide\n\nDivides the first value by the second value and reports the dividend.\n\n![divide](static/help/vr/blocks/en/divide/divide.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Divide** can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nThe report block **divide** fits in blocks that accept circular spaces, and can accept circular blocks in it as well. \n\nEnter numbers to be divided.\n\n![divide_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/divide/divide_numbers.png)\n\nDivide with values of variables.\n\n![divide_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/en/divide/divide_variables.png)\n\nDivide with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![divide_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/divide/divide_sensors.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Divide**, enter the quantities to be divided before and after the `/`. \n\nDivide two numbers. \n\n![switch_divide_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/divide/switch_divide_numbers.png)\n\nDivide with values from variables.\n\n![switch_divide_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/divide/switch_divide_variable.png)\n\nDivide with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![switch_divide_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/divide/switch_divide_sensor.png)\n\n"},"draw_circle":{"name":"draw_circle","markdown":"# Draw circle\n\nDraws a circle on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n![draw_circle](static/help/vr/blocks/en/draw_circle/draw_circle.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **draw circle** block requires 3 values:\n\n* Value 1: Center Point X\n* Value 2: Center Point Y\n* Value 3: Radius of Circle (in pixels)\n\nThe outside line color of the circle is determined by the **set pen color** block. The default line color is white.\n\nThe inside fill color of the circle is determine by the **set fill color** block. The default fill color is black.\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/blocks/en/draw_circle/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)\n"},"draw_line":{"name":"draw_line","markdown":"# Draw line\n\nDraws a line on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n![draw_line](static/help/vr/blocks/en/draw_line/draw_line.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **draw line** block requires 4 values:\n\n* Value 1: Beginning X coordinate\n* Value 2: Beginning Y coordinate\n* Value 3: Ending X coordinate\n* Value 4: Ending Y coordinate\n\nThe line color is determined by the **set pen color** block. The default line color is white.\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/blocks/en/draw_line/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)"},"draw_pixel":{"name":"draw_pixel","markdown":"# Draw pixel\n\nDraws a pixel on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n![draw_pixel](static/help/vr/blocks/en/draw_pixel/draw_pixel.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **draw pixel** block requires 2 values:\n\n* Value 1: X Coodinate\n* Value 2: Y Coodinate\n\nThe pixel color is determined by the **set pen color** block. The default pixel color is white.\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/blocks/en/draw_pixel/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)"},"draw_rectangle":{"name":"draw_rectangle","markdown":"# Draw rectangle\n\nDraws a rectangle on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n![draw_rectangle](static/help/vr/blocks/en/draw_rectangle/draw_rectangle.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **draw rectangle** block requires 4 values:\n\n* Value 1: Top Left Corner X coordinate\n* Value 2: Top Left Corner Y coordinate\n* Value 3: Width of the rectangle\n* Value 4: Height of the rectangle\n\nThe outside line color of the rectangle is determined by the **set pen color** block. The default line color is white.\n\nThe inside fill color of the rectangle is determine by the **set fill color** block. The default fill color is black.\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/blocks/en/draw_rectangle/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)"},"drive":{"name":"drive","markdown":"# Drive\n\nMoves the Drivetrain forever.\n\n![drive_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive/drive_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **drive** block will run the Drivetrain forever, until a new Drivetrain block is used, or the program is stopped.\n\nChoose the direction the Drivetrain will move.\n\n![drive_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive/drive_direction.png)  \n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Drive**, there is one parameter that can be changed (DIRECTION).\n\n![switch_drive_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive/switch_drive_parameters.png)\n\nSet the direction that the Drivetrain will move by providing the following:\n\n- DIRECTION - either `FORWARD` or `REVERSE`\n\n![switch_drive](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive/switch_drive.png)"},"drive_current":{"name":"drive_current","markdown":"# Drive current\n\nReports the amount of current (power) that the Drivetrain is currently using.\n\n![drive_current_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_current/drive_current_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive current** reports a range from **0.0 to 2.5 amps**. \n\nThe reporter block **drive current** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_current_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_current/drive_current_inserted.png)"},"drive_efficiency":{"name":"drive_efficiency","markdown":"# Drive efficiency\n\nReports the efficiency of the Drivetrain.\n\n![drive_efficiency](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_efficiency/drive_efficiency.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive efficiency** reports the value of the power (in watts) the motor is using (input), versus the amount of power (in watts) the motor is providing (output). \n\nA V5 Drivetrain typically reaches a maximum efficiency of 65% under normal use cases.\n\n**Drive efficiency** reports a range from **0% to 100%**. \n\nThe report block **drive efficiency** fits in blocks that accept circular spaces. \n\n![drive_efficiency_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_efficiency/drive_efficiency_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_for":{"name":"drive_for","markdown":"# Drive for\n\nMoves the Drivetrain for a given distance.\n\n![drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for/drive_for.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which direction the Drivetrain will move. \n\n\n![drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for/drive_for_direction.png)\n\n\nSet how far the Drivetrain will move by entering a value, and choosing the unit of measurement (inches or millimeters).\n\nThe **Drive for** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for/drive_for_units.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Drivetrain is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Drivetrain moves.\n\n![drive_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for/drive_for_anddontwait.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Drive for**, there are three parameters that can be changed (DIRECTION, DISTANCE, and UNITS).\n\n![switch_drive_for_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for/switch_drive_for_parameters.png)\n\nSet the distance and the direction that the Drivetrain will move by providing each of the following:\n\n- DIRECTION - either `FORWARD` or `REVERSE`\n- DISTANCE - a numeric integer value\n- UNITS - either `MM`(millimeters) or `INCHES`\n\n![switch_drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for/switch_drive_for.png)\n\nThe optional parameter of \"and don't wait\" can be added to the Switch Python command for **Drive for** by typing `wait=False` at the end of the command.\n\n![switch_drive_for_dont_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for/switch_drive_for_dont_wait.png)\n"},"drive_for_123":{"name":"drive_for_123","markdown":"# Drive for\n\nMoves the Drivetrain for a given distance.\n![123_drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for_123/123_drive_for.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which direction the Drivetrain will move.\n![123_drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for_123/123_drive_for_direction.png)\n\nSet how far the Drivetrain will move by entering a value into the oval and choosing the unit of measurement (steps or millimeters). One step is equivalent to one Tile square, 3 inches, or approximately 75 millimeters.\n\n![123_drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for_123/123_drive_for_units.png)\n\nThe **Drive for** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n![123_drive_for_reporter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for_123/123_drive_for_reporter.png)"},"drive_for_go":{"name":"drive_for_go","markdown":"# Drive for\n\nMoves the Drivetrain for a given distance.\n![drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for_go/drive_for.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which direction the Drivetrain will move.\n![drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for_go/drive_for_direction.png)\n\nSet how far the Drivetrain will move by entering a value into the oval and choosing the unit of measurement (millimeters or inches).\n![drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for_go/drive_for_units.png)\n\nThe **Drive for** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n![drive_for_reporter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_for_go/drive_for_reporter.png)\n"},"drive_go":{"name":"drive_go","markdown":"# Drive\n\nMoves the Drivetrain forever.\n\n![drive_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_go/drive_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Drive** block will run the Drivetrain forever until a new block from the Drivetrain category is used, or the project is stopped.\n\nChoose the direction the Drivetrain will move.\n\n![drive_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_go/drive_direction.png)  \n"},"drive_heading":{"name":"drive_heading","markdown":"# Drive heading\n\nReports the direction that the Drivetrain is facing by using the Gyroscopic (Gyro) sensor's current angular position.\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading/drive_heading.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe reporter block **drive heading** reports an increase in heading when rotating clockwise.\n\n**Drive heading** reports a range from **0.00 to 359.99 degrees**.\n\nThe reporter block **drive heading** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Drive heading** uses the parameter of `DEGREES` within the parentheses.\n\n![switch_drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading/switch_drive_heading.png)\n"},"drive_heading_go":{"name":"drive_heading_go","markdown":"# Drive heading\n\nReports the direction that the Drivetrain is facing.\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_go/drive_heading.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Drive heading** block reports a range from 0.00 to 359.99 degrees.\n\nThe **Drive heading** block reports a clockwise heading as shown in the image below.\n\n![TopDownBot01](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_go/TopDownBot01.png)\n\nThe **Drive heading** reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_go/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive heading** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_heading_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_go/drive_heading_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_heading_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_go/drive_heading_monitor.png)\n"},"drive_heading_virtual":{"name":"drive_heading_virtual","markdown":"# Drive heading\n\nReports the direction that the Drivetrain is facing by using the Gyroscopic (Gyro) sensor's current angular position.\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual/drive_heading.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe reporter block **Drive heading** reports an increase in heading when rotating clockwise.\n\n**Drive heading** reports a range from **0.00 to 359.99 degrees**.\n\nThe reporter block **Drive heading** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive heading** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_heading_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual/drive_heading_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_heading_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual/drive_heading_monitor.png)"},"drive_heading_virtual_viqc":{"name":"drive_heading_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Drive heading\n\nReports the direction that the Drivetrain is facing.\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual_viqc/drive_heading.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive heading** reports a range from **0.00 to 359.99 degrees**.\n\nThe **Drive heading** reports a counter-clockwise heading as shown in the image below.\n\n![TopDownBot01](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual_viqc/TopDownBot01.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Drive heading** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual_viqc/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive heading** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_heading_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual_viqc/drive_heading_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_heading_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_heading_virtual_viqc/drive_heading_monitor.png)"},"drive_is_done":{"name":"drive_is_done","markdown":"# Drive is done\n\nReports if the Drivetrain has completed its movement.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive is done** reports **true** when the Drivetrain's motors have completed their movement.\n\n**Drive is done** reports **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are still moving.\n\nThe **drive is done** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Drive is done** does not require any parameters inside the parentheses. \n\n![switch_drive_is_done](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done/switch_drive_is_done.png)"},"drive_is_done_go":{"name":"drive_is_done_go","markdown":"# Drive is done\n\nReports if the Drivetrain has completed its movement.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_go/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive is done** reports TRUE when the Drivetrain's motors have completed their movement.\n\n**Drive is done** reports FALSE when the Drivetrain's motors are still moving.\n\nThe **Drive is done** Boolean block reports a TRUE or FALSE value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_go/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive is done** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![drive_is_done_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_go/drive_is_done_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_is_done_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_go/drive_is_done_monitor.png)\n"},"drive_is_done_virtual":{"name":"drive_is_done_virtual","markdown":"# Drive is done\n\nReports if the Drivetrain has completed its movement.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_virtual/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive is done** reports **true** when the Drivetrain's motors have completed their movement.\n\n**Drive is done** reports **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are still moving.\n\nThe **Drive is done** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_virtual/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive is done** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_is_done_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_virtual/drive_is_done_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_is_done_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_virtual/drive_is_done_monitor.png)"},"drive_is_done_virtual_viqc":{"name":"drive_is_done_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Drive is done\n\nReports if the Drivetrain has completed its movement.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_virtual_viqc/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive is done** reports **true** when the Drivetrain's motors have completed their movement.\n\n**Drive is done** reports **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are still moving.\n\nThe **Drive is done** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_virtual_viqc/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive is done** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_is_done_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_virtual_viqc/drive_is_done_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_is_done_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_done_virtual_viqc/drive_is_done_monitor.png)"},"drive_is_moving":{"name":"drive_is_moving","markdown":"# Drive is moving\n\nReports if the Drivetrain is currently moving.\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive is moving** reports **true** when the Drivetrain's motors are moving.\n\n**Drive is moving** reports **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are stopped.\n\nThe **drive is moving** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Drive is moving** does not require any parameters inside the parentheses. \n\n![switch_drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving/switch_drive_is_moving.png)\n"},"drive_is_moving_virtual":{"name":"drive_is_moving_virtual","markdown":"# Drive is moving\n\nReports if the Drivetrain is currently moving.\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving_virtual/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive is moving** reports **true** when the Drivetrain's motors are moving.\n\n**Drive is moving** reports **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are stopped.\n\nThe **Drive is moving** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving_virtual/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive is moving** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_is_moving_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving_virtual/drive_is_moving_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_is_moving_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving_virtual/drive_is_moving_monitor.png)"},"drive_is_moving_virtual_viqc":{"name":"drive_is_moving_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Drive is moving\n\nReports if the Drivetrain is currently moving.\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving_virtual_viqc/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive is moving** reports **true** when the Drivetrain's motors are moving.\n\n**Drive is moving** reports **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are stopped.\n\nThe **Drive is moving** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving_virtual_viqc/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive is moving** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_is_moving_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving_virtual_viqc/drive_is_moving_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_is_moving_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_is_moving_virtual_viqc/drive_is_moving_monitor.png)"},"drive_power":{"name":"drive_power","markdown":"# Drive power\n\nReports the amount of power output the Drivetrain is currently generating.\n\n![drive_power](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_power/drive_power.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive power** reports a range from **0.0 to 22.0 watts**. \n\nThe reporter block **drive power** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_power_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_power/drive_power_inserted.png)"},"drive_rotation":{"name":"drive_rotation","markdown":"# Drive rotation\n\nReports the Drivetrain's angle of rotation when configured with a Gyro Sensor.\n\n![drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive rotation** reports a **positive** value when the Drivetrain turns in a **clockwise** direction.\n\n**Drive rotation** reports a **negative** value when the Drivetrain turns in a **counter-clockwise** direction.\n\nThe reporter block **drive rotation** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_rotation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Drive rotation** uses the parameter of `DEGREES` inside the parentheses.\n\n![switch_drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation/switch_drive_rotation.png)"},"drive_rotation_go":{"name":"drive_rotation_go","markdown":"# Drive rotation\n\nReports the Drivetrain's angle of rotation in degrees.\n\n![drive_rotation_reporter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_go/drive_rotation_reporter.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive rotation** reports a positive (+) value when the Drivetrain turns in a *clockwise* direction.\n\n**Drive rotation** reports a negative (-) value when the Drivetrain turns in a *counter-clockwise* direction.\n\nThe **Drive rotation** reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_rotation_reporter_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_go/drive_rotation_reporter_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive rotation** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_rotation_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_go/drive_rotation_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_rotation_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_go/drive_rotation_monitor.png)\n"},"drive_rotation_virtual":{"name":"drive_rotation_virtual","markdown":"# Drive rotation\n\nReports the Drivetrain's angle of rotation when configured with a 3-Wire Gyro Sensor or V5 Inertial Sensor.\n\n![drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_virtual/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive rotation** reports a **positive** value when the Drivetrain turns in a **clockwise** direction.\n\n**Drive rotation** reports a **negative** value when the Drivetrain turns in a **counter-clockwise** direction.\n\nThe reporter block **Drive rotation** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_rotation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_virtual/drive_rotation_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive rotation** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_rotation_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_virtual/drive_rotation_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_rotation_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_virtual/drive_rotation_monitor.png)"},"drive_rotation_virtual_viqc":{"name":"drive_rotation_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Drive rotation\n\nReports the Drivetrain's angle of rotation in degrees when configured with a VEX IQ Gyro Sensor.\n\n![iq_drive_rotation_reporter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_virtual_viqc/iq_drive_rotation_reporter.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive rotation** reports a **positive** value when the Drivetrain turns in a **counter-clockwise** direction.\n\n**Drive rotation** reports a **negative** value when the Drivetrain turns in a **clockwise** direction.\n\nThe reporter block **Drive rotation** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![iq_drive_rotation_reporter_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_virtual_viqc/iq_drive_rotation_reporter_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive rotation** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_rotation_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_virtual_viqc/drive_rotation_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_rotation_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_rotation_virtual_viqc/drive_rotation_monitor.png)"},"drive_temperature":{"name":"drive_temperature","markdown":"# Drive temperature\n\nReports the temperature of the V5 Smart Motors powering the Drivetrain.\n\n![drive_temperature](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_temperature/drive_temperature.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive temperature** reports a range from **0% to 100%**. \n\nThe reporter block **drive temperature** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_temperature_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_temperature/drive_temperature_inserted.png)"},"drive_to_object":{"name":"drive_to_object","markdown":"# Drive to object\n\nMoves the VR Rover forward until a specified object.\n\n![drive_to_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_to_object/drive_to_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which object the VR Rover will drive to. This could be minerals, an enemy, or base.\n\n![drive_to_object_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_to_object/drive_to_object_choose.png)\n\nIf the VR Rover detects multiple minerals or multiple enemies, it will drive towards the closer option.\n\nBy default, other blocks will wait until the VR Rover is done moving. You can select the arrow to expand 'and don't wait.' This will cause the other blocks to continue running while the Drivetrain moves.\n\n![drive_to_object_dont_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_to_object/drive_to_object_dont_wait.png)\n"},"drive_torque":{"name":"drive_torque","markdown":"# Drive torque\n\nReports the amount of torque (rotational force) the Drivetrain is currently using.\n\n![drive_torque](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_torque/drive_torque.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Drive torque** reports a range from **0.0 to 18.6 inch-pounds (InLB)** or **0.0 to 2.1 Newton-meters (Nm)**. \n\nChoose whether the **drive torque** is reported in inch-pounds (InLb) or Newton-meters (Nm).\n\n![drive_torque_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_torque/drive_torque_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **drive torque** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_torque_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_torque/drive_torque_inserted.png)"},"drive_until_123":{"name":"drive_until_123","markdown":"# Drive until\n\nDrives the robot until one of two conditions is met:\n\n* An object is detected by the Eye Sensor on the front of the robot.\n* The robot crashes into an object or wall.\n\n![drive_until_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_123/drive_until_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Drive until** block will begin driving the robot either forward or reverse. \n\nWhile driving, the robot will continuously check for one of the three conditions to occur. Once the selected condition occurs, the robot will stop driving.\n\nChoose which direction the robot will move.\n\n![drive_until_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_123/drive_until_direction.png)\n\nChoose the condition that will cause the robot to stop driving.\n\n![drive_until_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_123/drive_until_condition.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Drivetrain is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Drivetrain moves.\n![drive_until_and_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_123/drive_until_and_dont_wait.png)"},"drive_until_123_with_line":{"name":"drive_until_123_with_line","markdown":"# Drive until\n\nDrives the 123 Robot until one of three conditions is met:\n\n* An object is detected by the Eye Sensor on the front of the 123 Robot.\n* The 123 Robot crashes into an object or wall.\n* The Line Detector Sensor detects a line underneath the 123 Robot.\n\n![drive_until_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_123_with_line/drive_until_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Drive until** block will begin driving the 123 Robot either forward or reverse. \n\nWhile driving, the 123 Robot will continuously check for one of the three conditions to occur. Once the selected condition occurs, the 123 Robot will stop driving.\n\nChoose which direction the 123 Robot will move.\n![drive_until_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_123_with_line/drive_until_direction.png)\n\nChoose the condition that will cause the 123 Robot to stop driving.\n![drive_until_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_123_with_line/drive_until_condition.png)"},"drive_until_go":{"name":"drive_until_go","markdown":"# Drive until\n\nDrives the robot until one of two conditions is met:\n\n* An object is detected by the Eye Sensor on the front of the robot.\n* The robot crashes into an object or wall.\n\n![drive_until_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_go/drive_until_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Drive until** block will begin driving the robot either forward or reverse. \n\nWhile driving, the robot will continuously check for one of the three conditions to occur. Once the selected condition occurs, the robot will stop driving.\n\nChoose which direction the robot will move.\n\n![drive_until_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_go/drive_until_direction.png)\n\nChoose the condition that will cause the robot to stop driving.\n\n![drive_until_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_go/drive_until_condition.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Drivetrain is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Drivetrain moves.\n![drive_until_and_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_until_go/drive_until_and_dont_wait.png)"},"drive_velocity":{"name":"drive_velocity","markdown":"# Drive velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of the Drivetrain.\n\n![drive_velocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity/drive_velocity_block.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\n**Drive velocity** reports a range from **-100% to 100%**.\n\nThe reporter block **drive velocity** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_velocity_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity/drive_velocity_inserted.png)\n\n"},"drive_velocity_go":{"name":"drive_velocity_go","markdown":"# Drive velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of the Drivetrain.\n\n![drive_velocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_go/drive_velocity_block.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\n**Drive velocity** reports a range from 1% to 100%.\n\nThe **Drive velocity** reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_velocity_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_go/drive_velocity_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive velocity** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_velocity_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_go/drive_velocity_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_velocity_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_go/drive_velocity_monitor.png)\n\n\n"},"drive_velocity_virtual":{"name":"drive_velocity_virtual","markdown":"# Drive velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of the Drivetrain.\n\n![drive_velocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_virtual/drive_velocity_block.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\n**Drive velocity** reports a range from **-100% to 100%**.\n\nThe reporter block **Drive velocity** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_velocity_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_virtual/drive_velocity_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive velocity** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_velocity_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_virtual/drive_velocity_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_velocity_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_virtual/drive_velocity_monitor.png)\n"},"drive_velocity_virtual_viqc":{"name":"drive_velocity_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Drive velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of the Drivetrain.\n\n![drive_velocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_virtual_viqc/drive_velocity_block.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\n**Drive velocity** reports a range from **-100% to 100%**\n\nThe reporter block **Drive velocity** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![drive_velocity_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_virtual_viqc/drive_velocity_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Drive velocity** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![drive_velocity_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_virtual_viqc/drive_velocity_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![drive_velocity_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/drive_velocity_virtual_viqc/drive_velocity_monitor.png)\n"},"duration":{"name":"duration","markdown":"# Set electromagnet duration\n\nSets how long the V5 Electromagnet is turned on for.\n\n![set_duration_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/duration/set_duration_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nSelect to set how long the electromagnet is powered on for a boost:\n\n* **short** - powers the electromagnet for **250ms**\n* **medium** - powers the electromagnet for **500ms**\n* **long** - powers the electromagnet for **1000ms**\n\n![set_duration_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/en/duration/set_duration_block_setting.png)\n\nChoose which electromagnet to use.\n\n![set_duration_block_magnet](static/help/vr/blocks/en/duration/set_duration_block_magnet.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will tell the robot to drive forward 200mm, use the **set electromagnet duration** block to set the duration to **long**, then the **Electromagnet** will boost to pick up an object, drive forward 200mm, then drop the object.\n\n![set_duration_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/duration/set_duration_example.png)\n"},"efficiency_of_motor":{"name":"efficiency_of_motor","markdown":"# Efficiency of motor\n\nReports the efficiency of a V5 Smart Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group.\n\n![drive_efficiency](static/help/vr/blocks/en/efficiency_of_motor/motor_efficiency.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Efficiency of motor** reports the value of the power (in watts) the motor is using (input), versus the amount of power (in watts) the motor is providing (output). \n\nA V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group typically reaches a maximum efficiency of 65% under normal use cases.\n\n**Efficiency of motor** reports a range from **0% to 100%**. \n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![motor_efficiency_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/efficiency_of_motor/motor_efficiency_device.png)\n\nThe report block **Efficiency of motor** fits in blocks that accept circular spaces. \n\n![motor_efficiency_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/efficiency_of_motor/motor_efficiency_inserted.png)"},"enable_manual_movement":{"name":"enable_manual_movement","markdown":"# Set manual movement\n\nAllows the arm of the V5 Workcell to be manually moved by hand.\n\n![set_manual_movement](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enable_manual_movement/set_manual_movement.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nBy default, the motors on the Workcell lock in place when a project is running. To be able to manually move the arm, use the **set manual movement** block in your project.\n\n![movement](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enable_manual_movement/movement.png)\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![set_manual_movement_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enable_manual_movement/set_manual_movement_arm.png)"},"enemy_charge":{"name":"enemy_charge","markdown":"# Enemy radiation\n\nThe **Enemy radiation** block reports the radiation of the closest enemy detected in the VR Rover's vision.\n\n![enemy_radiation_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enemy_charge/enemy_radiation_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nWhen no enemies are detected in the VR Rover's vision range, the **Enemy radiation** block returns 0.\n\nThe reporter block can be used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![enemy_radiation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enemy_charge/enemy_radiation_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Enemy radiation** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block in the toolbox.\n\n![enemy_radiation_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enemy_charge/enemy_radiation_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![enemy_radiation_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enemy_charge/enemy_radiation_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"enemy_level":{"name":"enemy_level","markdown":"# Enemy level\n\nThe **Enemy level** block reports the level of the closest enemy detected in the VR Rover's vision.\n\n![enemy_level_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enemy_level/enemy_level_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nWhen no enemies are detected in the VR Rover's vision range, the **Enemy level** block returns 0.\n\nThe reporter block can be used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![enemy_level_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enemy_level/enemy_level_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Enemy level** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block in the toolbox.\n\n![enemy_level_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enemy_level/enemy_level_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![enemy_level_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enemy_level/enemy_level_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"energize":{"name":"energize","markdown":"# Energize electromagnet\n\nSets the V5 Electromagnet to two different modes: **boost** or **drop**.\n\n![set_magnet_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/energize/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nSelect to set that electromagnet to boost or drop\n\n* **boost** - turns on the electromagnet for as long as the duration was set\n* **drop** - releases any object the electromagnet is holding\n\nAfter energizing the magnet, the magnet will need to cool down before it can be energized again.\n\n\n![set_magnet_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/en/energize/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\nChoose which electromagnet to use.\n\n![set_magnet_block_magnet](static/help/vr/blocks/en/energize/set_magnet_block_magnet.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the robot to drive forward 200mm, **Electromagnet** will boost to pick up an object, drive forward 200mm, then drop the object.\n\n![set_magnet_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/energize/set_magnet_example.png)\n"},"enter_mastering_mode":{"name":"enter_mastering_mode","markdown":"# Start mastering process\n\nStarts the mastering process for the four joints of the  arm on the Workcell to allow it to be calibrated to the same known location.\n\n![start_mastering_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/enter_mastering_mode/start_mastering_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **start mastering process** block begins the mastering process for the arm on the V5 Workcell. \n\nThis block is used in the 'Arm Mastering' example project."},"equal_to":{"name":"equal_to","markdown":"# Equal to\n\nReports if the first value is the same as, or equal to, the second value. \n\n![equal_to](static/help/vr/blocks/en/equal_to/equal_to.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Equal to** reports **true** when the two values are exactly the same.\n\n**Equal to** reports **false** when the two values are not the same.\n\nThe **equal to** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nThe **equal to** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and fits in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![equal_to_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/equal_to/equal_to_inserted.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will stop driving if the distance in mm equals 500.\n\n![equal_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/equal_to/equal_to_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Equal to**, enter the quantities to be compared before and after the `==`. \n\nCompare two numbers. \n\n![switch_equal_to_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/equal_to/switch_equal_to_numbers.png)\n\nCompare with values from variables.\n\n![switch_equal_to_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/equal_to/switch_equal_to_variable.png)\n\nCompare with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![switch_equal_to_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/equal_to/switch_equal_to_sensor.png)"},"eye_brightness":{"name":"eye_brightness","markdown":"# Brightness of\n\nReports the ambient brightness of the environment, and the brightness of an object detected by the Eye Sensor.\n\n![brightness_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_brightness/brightness_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Brightness of** block reports a range from 0% to 100%.\n\nThe Eye Sensor on the 123 Robot will report a higher brightness value for a brighter object and a lower brightness value for a darker object.\n\nThe **Brightness of** reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![brightness_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_brightness/brightness_of_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Brightness of** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![brightness_of_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_brightness/brightness_of_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![brightness_of_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_brightness/brightness_of_monitor.png)\n"},"eye_detect":{"name":"eye_detect","markdown":"# Detects color\n\nReports if the Eye Sensor detects the specified color.\n\n![detects_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_detect/detects_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\nChoose which color you want the Eye Sensor to check for.\n\n![detects_color_colors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_detect/detects_color_colors.png)\n\nThe **Detects color** block reports **TRUE** when the Eye Sensor detects the specified color.\n\nThe **Detects color** block reports **FALSE** when the Eye Sensor does not detect the specified color.\n\nThe **Detects color** block is a Boolean block that reports **TRUE** or **FALSE** and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![detects_color_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_detect/detects_color_insert.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Detects color** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![detects_color_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_detect/detects_color_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the Boolean block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![detects_color_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/eye_detect/detects_color_monitor.png)"},"fill_color_plus":{"name":"fill_color_plus","markdown":"# Fill area with color\n\nUses the VEX VR Pen to fill an area of the Playground with a specified color.\n\n![set_fill_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/fill_color_plus/set_fill_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUsing the sliders, select the red, green, and blue values for the fill color you want to create. The numeric value will be listed next to the color (0 to 255).\n\n![color_sliders_fill](static/help/vr/blocks/en/fill_color_plus/color_sliders_fill.png)\n\nOpacity describes the transparency of the color. The opacity of the fill color can be selected using the bottom slider.\n\n![opacity_selection_fill](static/help/vr/blocks/en/fill_color_plus/opacity_selection_fill.png)\n\nThe color window will update to show the fill color on the Playground. In this example, the pen would fill the Playground with a dark green color.\n\n![dark_green_fill](static/help/vr/blocks/en/fill_color_plus/dark_green_fill.png)\n\n## Example\n\nUsing this project, the Playground will be filled with dark green.\n\n![example_fill_green](static/help/vr/blocks/en/fill_color_plus/example_fill_green.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Fill area with color** using the VR Pen, there are four parameters that can be changed (RED, GREEN, BLUE, and OPACITY).\n\n![switch_fill_area_with_color_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/fill_color_plus/switch_fill_area_with_color_parameters.png)\n\nSpecify the color of the pen by providing each of the following:\n\n- RED - specify the intensity of the color as an integer between 0 - 255\n- GREEN - specify the intensity of the color as an integer between 0 - 255\n- BLUE - specify the intensity of the color as an integer between 0 - 255\n- OPACITY - specify the transparency of the color as a value from 0 - 100% \n\nNote the default OPACITY is set to 100%.\n\nIn this example, the pen fill color is set to black, as all color parameters are set to 0.\n\n![switch_fill_area_with_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/fill_color_plus/switch_fill_area_with_color.png)\n\nIn this example, the pen fill color is set to red, as the RED parameter is set to its highest value (255) and the others are set to 0.\n\n![switch_fill_area_with_color_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/fill_color_plus/switch_fill_area_with_color_example.png)"},"forever":{"name":"forever","markdown":"# Forever\n\nA 'C' block that repeats any blocks contained inside forever.\n\n![forever](static/help/vr/blocks/en/forever/forever.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nPlace blocks in a **forever** 'C' block to have those actions repeat **forever**.\n\nYou can exit a **forever** loop by placing a **break** block inside.\n\n'C' blocks can also be put inside of each other. This concept is called nesting which can help save time when programming different behaviors.\n\n## Example 1\n\nThis example will keep driving forward for one to three seconds, then turn right for one to three seconds. It will repeat until the program is stopped. \n\n![forever_example_2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/forever/forever_example_2.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Forever** creates a ***while*** loop where the CONDITION is `True`. The commands nested within the loop will then be repeated forever, as the condition is always `True`.\n\n![switch_forever](static/help/vr/blocks/en/forever/switch_forever.png)\n\nTo nest other commands within the Switch Python command, be mindful of indentation. In this example, the VR Robot will drive in a square continuously by repeating the indented Drivetrain commands forever.\n\n![switch_forever_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/forever/switch_forever_example.png)\n"},"function":{"name":"function","markdown":"# Function\n\nPerforms a selected math function.\n\n![function](static/help/vr/blocks/en/function/function.png)\n\n* **abs** - absolute value\n* **floor** - rounds down to the nearest integer\n* **ceiling** - rounds up to the nearest integer\n* **sqrt** - square root\n* **sin** - sine\n* **cos** - cosine\n* **tan** - tangent\n* **asin** - arcsine or the inverse of sine\n* **acos** - arccosine or the inverse of cosine\n* **atan** - arctan or the inverse of tangent\n* **ln** - the natural logarithm base e\n* **log** - logarithm base 10\n* **e ^** - powers of e\n* **10 ^** - powers of 10\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **function** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nChoose the math **function** from a list of options.\n\n![function_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/function/function_select.png)\n\nThe **function** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces. \n\n![function_fit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/function/function_fit.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo use a function with a the Switch Python block, there are many options depending on the math function desired. For all functions, the decimals, integers, or numeric reporters being uses should be placed in the parenthesis. \n\n* `math.fabs(0)` - absolute value\n* `math.floor(0)` - rounds down to the nearest integer\n* `math.ceil(0)` - rounds up to the nearest integer\n* `math.sqrt(0)` - square root\n* `math.log(0)` - the natural logarithm base e\n* `math.log(0, 10)` - logarithm base 10 (denoted by the `, 10` in the parameters)\n* `math.exp(0)` - powers of e\n* `math.pow(10, 0)` - powers of 10\n* `math.sin(RADIANS)` - sine\n* `math.cos(RADIANS)` - cosine\n* `math.tan(RADIANS)` - tangent\n* `math.asin(RADIANS)` - arcsine or the inverse of sine\n* `math.acos(RADIANS)` - arccosine or the inverse of cosine\n* `math.atan(RADIANS)` - arctangent or the inverse of tangent\n\nFunctions such as sine, cosine, and tangent use RADIANS for parameters in a Switch Python command. You can convert from degrees to radians manually, or you can include the calculation in the command. This calculation to convert the units from degrees to radians is included when you convert from blocks to Switch. \n\n![switch_sine](static/help/vr/blocks/en/function/switch_sine.png)\n\nArcsine, arccosine, and arctangent also use RADIANS for parameters. You can convert from degrees to radians manually, or you can include the calculation in the command. This calculation to convert the units from degrees to radians is included when you convert from blocks to Switch. \n\n![switch_arctan](static/help/vr/blocks/en/function/switch_arctan.png)\n\n"},"glow":{"name":"glow","markdown":"# Glow\nSets the glow color of the indicator light in the center of the 123 Robot. \n\n![glow_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/glow/glow_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\nThe 123 Robot has an indicator light in the center that can be set to glow different colors. \n![robot_light](static/help/vr/blocks/en/glow/robot_light.png)\n\nChoose the color that will be displayed. The glow can also be set to \"off\" to turn the indicator light off.\n\n![glow_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/glow/glow_color.png)\n\nThe 123 Robot will continue to glow the specified color until the **Glow** block is set to a different glow color, or the project is stopped. \n\n![glow_robot](static/help/vr/blocks/en/glow/glow_robot.png)\n\n\n"},"go_to_object":{"name":"go_to_object","markdown":"# Go to object\n\nTurns and drives the VR Rover to face a specified object.\n\n![go_to_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/go_to_object/go_to_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which object the VR Rover will go to. This could be minerals, an enemy, or the base.\n\n![go_to_object_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/go_to_object/go_to_object_choose.png)\n\nIf the VR Rover detects multiple minerals or multiple enemies, it will move to the closer option.\n\nBy default, other blocks will wait until the VR Rover is done moving. You can select the arrow to expand 'and don't wait.' This will cause the other blocks to continue running while the VR Rover moves.\n\n![go_to_object_dont_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/go_to_object/go_to_object_dont_wait.png)\n"},"gps_acceleration":{"name":"gps_acceleration","markdown":"# GPS acceleration\n\nReports the acceleration value of the specified axis on a GPS Sensor.\n\n![gps_acceleration_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_acceleration/gps_acceleration_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**GPS acceleration** reports a range of values from **-4.0 to 4.0** Gs.\n\nSelect the axis to read values from:\n\n* The **X** axis reports acceleration of the GPS Sensor's **forward and backward** rotations\n\n* The **Y** axis reports acceleration of the GPS Sensor's **left and right** rotations\n\n* The **Z** axis reports acceleration of the GPS Sensor's **up and down** movements\n\n![gps_acceleration_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_acceleration/gps_acceleration_block2.png)\n\nThe **GPS acceleration** block can also be used inside blocks with circular inputs.\n\n![gps_acceleration_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_acceleration/gps_acceleration_block3.png)\n\n"},"gps_calibrate":{"name":"gps_calibrate","markdown":"# GPS calibrate\n\nStarts the calibration process for a GPS Sensor.\n\n![gps_calibrate_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_calibrate/gps_calibrate_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe GPS Sensor must remain still during the calibration process.\n\nChoose a GPS Sensor to calibrate.\n\n![gps_calibrate_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_calibrate/gps_calibrate_block2.png)"},"gps_gyro_rate":{"name":"gps_gyro_rate","markdown":"# GPS gyro rate\n\nReports the rate of rotation from the specified axis on a GPS Sensor.\n\n![gps_gyro_rate_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_gyro_rate/gps_gyro_rate_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nSelect the axis to get rate of rotation from:\n\n* The **X** axis reports rate of rotation when a GPS Sensor rotates in the X-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n\n* The **Y** axis reports rate of rotation when a GPS Sensor rotates in the Y-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n\n* The **Z** axis reports rate of rotation when a GPS Sensor rotates in the Z-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n\n![gps_gyro_rate_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_gyro_rate/gps_gyro_rate_block2.png)\n\n**GPS gyro rate** can report values in either dps (degrees per second) or rpm (revolutions per minute).\n\nThe range of values reported is **-1000** to **1000** for dps, and **-167** to **167** for rpm.\n\n![gps_gyro_rate_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_gyro_rate/gps_gyro_rate_block3.png)\n\nThe **GPS gyro rate** reporter block is used inside blocks with circular inputs.\n\n![gps_gyro_rate_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_gyro_rate/gps_gyro_rate_block4.png)\n"},"gps_heading":{"name":"gps_heading","markdown":"# GPS heading\n\nReports the heading that a robot is currently facing based on a GPS Sensor's readings from the VEX GPS Field Code.\n\n![gps_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_heading/gps_heading_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **GPS heading** reporter block reports an increase in heading when a GPS Sensor rotates clockwise.\n\n**GPS heading** reports a range from **0.00 to 359.99 degrees**.\n\nUsually, a GPS Sensor should be mounted facing behind a robot. In the GPS configuration, the Angle Offset is set to **180** degrees. \n\nThen, for example, if the GPS Sensor is facing the South wall as defined by the VEX GPS Field Code, the reported heading will be **0.00** degrees, which is the heading that the front of the robot is facing.\n\nHowever, a GPS Sensor can also be mounted facing other directions (e.g. the front of a robot).\n\nIn this situation, for example, the GPS Sensor's Angle Offset configuration would then need to be set to **0** degrees, which is the angular difference between the forward heading of the robot, and the heading that the GPS is facing. This way, the reported heading value will shift correctly in line with a robot's headings as it turns.\n\nIt can be used inside blocks with circular inputs.\n\n![gps_heading_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_heading/gps_heading_block2.png)\n\n"},"gps_heading_virtual":{"name":"gps_heading_virtual","markdown":"# GPS heading\n\nReports the heading that a robot is currently facing based on a GPS Sensor's readings from the VEX GPS Field Code.\n\n![gps_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_heading_virtual/gps_heading_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **GPS heading** reporter block reports an increase in heading when a GPS Sensor rotates clockwise.\n\n**GPS heading** reports a range from **0.00 to 359.99 degrees**.\n\nIt can be used inside blocks with circular inputs.\n\n![gps_heading_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_heading_virtual/gps_heading_block2.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **GPS heading** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![gps_heading_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_heading_virtual/gps_heading_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![gps_heading_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_heading_virtual/gps_heading_monitor.png)\n"},"gps_orientation":{"name":"gps_orientation","markdown":"# GPS orientation\n\nReports the angle of the specified orientation axis on a GPS Sensor.\n\n![gps_orientation_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_orientation/gps_orientation_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe orientation reported is determined by the specified orientation axis.\n\n* The **roll** axis reports a value between **-180 to +180** degrees, representing the angle of a GPS Sensor when tilted left or right\n\n* The **pitch** axis reports a value between **-90 to +90** degrees, representing the angle of a GPS Sensor when tilted forward or backwards\n\n* The **yaw** axis reports a value between **-180 to +180** degrees, representing the angle of a GPS Sensor when rotated about the vertical axis\n\n![gps_orientation_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_orientation/gps_orientation_block2.png)\n\nThe **GPS orientation** reporter block can also be used in blocks with circular inputs.\n\n![gps_orientation_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_orientation/gps_orientation_block3.png)\n"},"gps_position":{"name":"gps_position","markdown":"# GPS position\n\nReports the positional offset of a GPS Sensor or a defined reference point from the center of a field.\n\n![gps_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position/gps_position_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **GPS position** reporter block reports either the X or Y axis offset of a GPS Sensor or a defined reference point from the center of a field.\n\n![gps_position_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position/gps_position_block2.png)\n\nBy default, the **GPS position** reporter block will report the offset of a GPS Sensor, but a different reference point can be set in a GPS Sensor's configuration such that the offset reported is relative to the reference point.\n\nIf a GPS Sensor is not mounted at the turning reference point of a robot (the axis around which a robot turns), the X and Y offset settings can be adjusted in the GPS Sensor's configuration so that reported values from the **GPS position** block will not change the robot is turning in-place.\n\nTo calculate the X and Y offset settings, measure the distance from the reference point to the mounted position of a GPS Sensor, and filling in the X and Y offset settings in the GPS Sensor's configuration, respectively.\n\nThe block can report offset values in mm or inches.\n\n![gps_position_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position/gps_position_block3.png)\n\nIt can also be used inside blocks with circular inputs.\n\n![gps_position_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position/gps_position_block4.png)"},"gps_position_virtual":{"name":"gps_position_virtual","markdown":"# GPS position\n\nReports the positional offset of the robot's turning reference point from the center of a field.\n\n![gps_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position_virtual/gps_position_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **GPS position** reporter block reports either the X or Y axis offset of the robot's turning reference point from the center of a field.\n\n![gps_position_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position_virtual/gps_position_block2.png)\n\nThe block can report offset values in mm or inches.\n\n![gps_position_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position_virtual/gps_position_block3.png)\n\nIt can also be used inside blocks with circular inputs.\n\n![gps_position_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position_virtual/gps_position_block4.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **GPS position** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![gps_position_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position_virtual/gps_position_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![gps_position_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_position_virtual/gps_position_monitor.png)"},"gps_set_location":{"name":"gps_set_location","markdown":"# GPS set location\n\nSets the location for a GPS Sensor to a defined positional value.\n\n![gps_set_location_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_set_location/gps_set_location_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nWhen a GPS Sensor is too close to the field walls to read accurate data (for example, when a robot is in a game's starting position on a field), unexpected behaviors can often occur if the program relies on the GPS's positional and heading data to work correctly.\n\nThe **GPS set location** block can be used to set the location and heading of a GPS Sensor if this information is known before the program is ran, which may help reduce GPS data inaccuracies due a GPS Sensor's proximity to the field walls when a program is started.\n\nThe first two numerical inputs are the X and Y offsets of the GPS Sensor, or a defined origin point on a robot relative to the center of a field. The X and Y offsets can be passed as either **mm** or **inches** units.\n\nThe last numerical input is the heading of the robot in its defined position. For example, if the GPS Sensor is mounted facing behind a robot and the robot is facing the West wall (heading of 270), the heading value that should be passed is 270.\n\nChoose a GPS Sensor to set location on.\n\n![gps_set_location_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_set_location/gps_set_location_block2.png)\n\nChoose which positional unit to use.\n\n![gps_set_location_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_set_location/gps_set_location_block3.png)"},"gps_signal_quality":{"name":"gps_signal_quality","markdown":"# GPS signal quality\n\nReports a GPS Sensor's current signal quality.\n\n![gps_signal_quality_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_signal_quality/gps_signal_quality_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **GPS signal quality** reporter block reports a numerical percentage value representing the current signal quality of a GPS Sensor.\n\nWhen the reported value is 100, it means that all reported positional and heading data from a GPS Sensor is valid.\n\nHowever, a reported value of 90 signals that any positional data is no longer being calculated by capturing information from the VEX Field Code, but rather through alternative means.\n\nAt 80, only GPS Sensor heading values are valid, but as more time goes by where a GPS Sensor is not scanning enough of the VEX Field Code to accurately determine position and heading information, the reported signal quality will continue to drop until 0, where any GPS Sensor data is effectively frozen and no longer valid.\n\nIt can also be used inside blocks with circular inputs.\n\n![gps_signal_quality_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gps_signal_quality/gps_signal_quality_block2.png)"},"greater_than":{"name":"greater_than","markdown":"# Greater than\n\nReports if the first value is larger (greater) than the second value.  \n\n![greater_than](static/help/vr/blocks/en/greater_than/greater_than.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Greater than** reports **true** when the first value is larger than the second value. \n\n**Greater than** reports **false** when the first value is smaller than or equal to the second value.\n\nThe **greater than** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nThe **greater than** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and fits in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![greater_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/greater_than/greater_than_inserted.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will stop the Drivetrain if the position angle in degrees is greater than 90.\n\n![greater_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/greater_than/greater_than_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Greater than**, enter the quantities to be compared before and after the `>`. \n\nCompare two numbers. \n\n![switch_greater_than_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/greater_than/switch_greater_than_numbers.png)\n\nCompare with values from variables.\n\n![switch_greater_than_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/greater_than/switch_greater_than_variable.png)\n\nCompare with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![switch_greater_than_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/greater_than/switch_greater_than_sensor.png)\n\n"},"gyro_heading_go":{"name":"gyro_heading_go","markdown":"# Heading in degrees\n\nReports the Gyro Sensor's current heading in degrees. The Gyro Sensor is built into the Brain.\n\n![heading_of_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_heading_go/heading_of_gyro.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Heading in degrees** block reports a range between 0.00 to 359.99.\n\nThe **Heading in degrees** block reports a clockwise heading as shown in the image below.\n\n![TopDownBot01](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_heading_go/TopDownBot01.png)\n\nThe **Heading in degrees** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_heading_go/heading_of_gyro_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Heading in degrees** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_heading_go/heading_of_gyro_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_heading_go/heading_of_gyro_monitor.png)\n"},"gyro_rotation_go":{"name":"gyro_rotation_go","markdown":"# Rotation in degrees\n\nReports the Gyro Sensor's current angle of rotation in degrees. The Gyro Sensor is built into the Brain.\n\n![rotation_in_degrees](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_rotation_go/rotation_in_degrees.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Rotation in degrees** block reports a positive (+) value when the Gyro Sensor built into the Brain turns in the *clockwise* direction.\n\nThe **Rotation in degrees** block reports a negative (-) value when the Gyro Sensor built into the Brain turns in the *counter-clockwise* direction.\n\nThe **Rotation in degrees** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![rotation_in_degrees_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_rotation_go/rotation_in_degrees_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Rotation in degrees** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![rotation_in_degrees_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_rotation_go/rotation_in_degrees_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![rotation_in_degrees_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/gyro_rotation_go/rotation_in_degrees_monitor.png)"},"heading_of_gyro":{"name":"heading_of_gyro","markdown":"# Angle of heading\n\nReports the 3-Wire Gyro Sensor or V5 Inertial Sensor's current heading in degrees.\n\n![heading_of_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/en/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe reporter block **heading of** reports an increase in heading when rotating clockwise.\n\n**Heading of** reports a range between **0.00 to 359.99**.\n\nChoose which Gyro Sensor / Inertial Sensor to check.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/en/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro_gyro.png)\n\nThe **heading of** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro_inserted.png)\n"},"hue":{"name":"hue","markdown":"# Hue of\n\nReports the hue value of the color detected by the Eye Sensor.\n\n![hue_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue/hue_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Hue of** reporter block provides a hue value between 0 to 359 degrees. This provides a more precise measurement of color.\n\n![color_wheel](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue/color_wheel.png)\n\nThe **Hue of** reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![hue_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue/hue_of_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Hue of** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![hue_of_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue/hue_of_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![hue_of_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue/hue_of_monitor.png)\n"},"hue_of_go":{"name":"hue_of_go","markdown":"# Hue of\n\nReports the hue value of the color detected by the Eye Sensor.\n\n![hue_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_go/hue_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Hue of** reporter block provides a hue value between 0 to 359 degrees. This provides a more precise measurement of color.\n\n![color_wheel](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_go/color_wheel.png)\n\nThe **Hue of** reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![hue_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_go/hue_of_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Hue of** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![hue_of_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_go/hue_of_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![hue_of_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_go/hue_of_monitor.png)\n"},"hue_of_virtual_viqc":{"name":"hue_of_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Hue of\n\nReports the hue of the color detected by the VEX IQ Color Sensor.\n\n![hue_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_virtual_viqc/hue_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Hue of** reports a range from **0 to 360**.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor to check.\n\n![hue_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_virtual_viqc/hue_of_color.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Hue of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![hue_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_virtual_viqc/hue_of_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Hue of** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![hue_of_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_virtual_viqc/hue_of_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![hue_of_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/hue_of_virtual_viqc/hue_of_monitor.png)"},"if_elseif_else":{"name":"if_elseif_else","markdown":"# If then else if then else\n\nA 'C' Block that runs the blocks inside the first set of internal blocks in the **if then else if then else** block where the condition returns true.\n\n![if_elseif_else](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_elseif_else/if_elseif_else.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **if then else if then else** 'C' Block will only check the Boolean condition once. \n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as **true** on the **if** line, the blocks inside of **if** section will be run.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is **false**, the **if then else if then else** block will check the first of the **else if** lines. For each **else if** line, the block will check if the Boolean condition is reported as **true**. If it is **true**, the internal blocks directly under that line will run before continuing on to the next block under the **if then else if then else** block.\n\nIf all the Boolean conditions for the **else if** lines are **false**, the blocks inside of **else** section will be run.\n\nThe **if then else if then else** 'C' block can accept hexagonal (six-sided) shaped blocks for its conditions.\n\n![if_elseif_else_2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_elseif_else/if_elseif_else_2.png)\n\n## How to Add and Remove Conditions\n\nThe **if then else if then else** block is unique amongst the conditional blocks in that you can add additional conditional statements. The block allows for 6 **else if** conditions.\n\n![if_elseif_else_expanded](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_elseif_else/if_elseif_else_expanded.png)\n\nTo add another **else if** condition, click on the \"+\" on the **else** line. This will add a new **else if** condition to the bottom of the current list.\n\nTo remove an **else if** condition, click on the \"-\" on the **else** line. This will remove the last **else if** condition line, but will not delete the blocks in the condition.\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **If then else if then else**, set both of the CONDITIONS that are to be evaluated in the project, in each of the **if** branches of the Switch Python command.\n\nIf no condition is set, the Switch Python command will default to `if False`, `elif False`, and `else`.\n\n![switch_if_if_else](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_elseif_else/switch_if_if_else.png)\n\nSet the CONDITION that is to be evaluated in the `if` and the `elif` branches. In this example, the CONDITION of `left_bumper.pressed` is set in the `if` branch, and `right_bumper.pressed` is set in the `elif` branch.\n\n![switch_if_if_else_conditions](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_elseif_else/switch_if_if_else_conditions.png)\n\nNested commands can be added to all branches of the Switch Python command as blocks or as text. In both of these examples, the VR Robot will turn right 90 degrees if the left bumper reports as pressed; turn left 90 degrees if the right bumper reports as pressed; else it will drive forward. \n\n![switch_if_if_else_example_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_elseif_else/switch_if_if_else_example_block.png)\n![switch_if_if_else_example_text](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_elseif_else/switch_if_if_else_example_text.png)\n\nThe expander can be used to add or remove an additional branch to the Switch Python **If then else if then else** command. Select the + to add a branch, and the – to remove one.\n\n![switch_if_if_else_expander](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_elseif_else/switch_if_if_else_expander.png)"},"if_then":{"name":"if_then","markdown":"# If then\n\nA 'C' Block that runs the blocks inside, if the Boolean condition is reported to be **true**.  \n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then/if_then.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **if then** 'C' block will only check the Boolean condition once. \n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as **true**, the blocks inside of **if then** will run.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as **false**, the blocks inside of **if then** will be skipped.\n\nThe **if then** 'C' block can accept hexagonal (six-sided) shaped blocks as its condition.\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then/if_then_2.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **If then**, set the CONDITION that is to be evaluated in the project. \n\nIf no condition is set, the Switch Python command will default to `if False`.\n\n![switch_if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then/switch_if_then.png) \n\nSet the CONDITION that is to be evaluated. In this example, the CONDITION of `left_bumper.pressed` is set.\n\n![switch_if_then_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then/switch_if_then_condition.png)\n\nTo nest other commands within the Switch Python command, be mindful of indentation. In this example, the VR Robot will turn right 90 degrees **if** the Left Bumper reports as pressed.\n\n![switch_if_then_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then/switch_if_then_example.png)"},"if_then_else":{"name":"if_then_else","markdown":"# If then else\n\nA 'C' Block that runs the blocks inside the first or second parts of the **if then else**, based on the Boolean value reported.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then_else/if_then_else.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **if then else** 'C' Block will only check the Boolean condition once. \n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as **true**, the blocks inside of **if** section will be run.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is **false**, the blocks inside of **else** section will be run.\n\nThe **if then else** 'C' block can accept hexagonal (six-sided) shaped blocks as its condition.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then_else/if_then_else_2.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **If then else**, set the CONDITION that is to be evaluated in the project, in the **if** branch of the Switch Python command.\n\nIf no condition is set, the Switch Python command will default to 'if False' and 'else'.\n\n![switch_if_else](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then_else/switch_if_else.png)\n\nSet the CONDITION that is to be evaluated. In this example, the CONDITION of `left_bumper.pressed` is set.\n\n![switch_if_else_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then_else/switch_if_else_condition.png)\n\nNested commands can be added to both branches of the Switch Python command as blocks or as text. In both of these examples, the VR Robot will turn right 90 degrees if the left bumper reports as pressed; else it will drive forward. \n\n![switch_if_else_example_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then_else/switch_if_else_example_block.png)\n![switch_if_else_example_text](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then_else/switch_if_else_example_text.png)\n\nThe expander can be used to add or remove an additional branch to the Switch Python **If then else** command. Select the + to add a branch, and the – to remove one.\n\n![switch_if_else_expander](static/help/vr/blocks/en/if_then_else/switch_if_else_expander.png)"},"inertial_acceleration":{"name":"inertial_acceleration","markdown":"# Acceleration of\n\nReports the acceleration value from one of the axes (x, y, or z) on the Intertial Sensor.\n\n![acceleration_of_inertial_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_acceleration/acceleration_of_inertial_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n**Acceleration of** reports a range from **-4.0 to 4.0** Gs. \n\nChoose which Inertial Sensor to use.\n\n![inertial_acceleration_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_acceleration/inertial_acceleration_device.png)\n\nChoose which axis to use:\n\n* The **X-axis** reports acceleration when the Inertial Sensor moves **forward to backward**\n* The **Y-axis** reports acceleration when the Inertial Sensor moves **side to side**\n* The **Z-axis** reports acceleration when the Inertial Sensor moves **up to down**\n\n![inertial_acceleration_axis](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_acceleration/inertial_acceleration_axis.png)\n\nThe sensing block **acceleration of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![acceleration_of_inertial_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_acceleration/acceleration_of_inertial_inserted.png)\n"},"inertial_gyrorate":{"name":"inertial_gyrorate","markdown":"# Gyro rate\n\nGets the rate of rotation from one of the axes (x, y, or z) on the Inertial Sensor.\n\n![gyro_rate_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_gyrorate/gyro_rate_block.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\n**Gyro rate** reports a range from **-1000.0 to 1000.0** in dps (degrees per second). \n\nChoose which Inertial Sensor to use.\n\n![gyroRate_included_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_gyrorate/gyroRate_included_block.png)\n\nChoose which axis to use.\n\n* The **X-axis** reports rate of rotation when the Inertial Sensor rotates in the X-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n* The **Y-axis** reports rate of rotation when the Inertial Sensor rotates in the Y-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n* The **Z-axis** reports rate of rotation when the Inertial Sensor rotates in the Z-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n\n![gyroRate_axis](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_gyrorate/gyroRate_axis.png)\n\nThe sensing block **gyro rate** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![gyroRate_of_inertial_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_gyrorate/gyroRate_of_inertial_inserted.png)\n"},"inertial_orientation":{"name":"inertial_orientation","markdown":"# Orientation of\n\nGets an orientation angle of the inertial sensor. Reports the unit value specified by the parameter of the inertial sensor.\n\n![orientation_inertial_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_orientation/orientation_inertial_block.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\nThe orientation reported is determined by the selected axis (x, y, or z).\n\n* The **X-axis** represents **pitch**, which reports a value between -90 to +90 degrees.\n* The **Y-axis** represents **roll**, which reports a value between -180 to +180 degrees)\n* The **Z-axis** represents **yaw**, which reports a value between -180 to +180 degrees)\n\n---\n\n**Pitch** is the angle of the robot tilted forwards and backwards.\n\n![V5_Pitch_rev2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_orientation/V5_Pitch_rev2.jpg)\n\n**Roll** is the angle of the robot tilted left and right.\n\n![V5_Roll_rev2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_orientation/V5_Roll_rev2.jpg)\n\n**Yaw** is angular rotation of the robot.\n\n![V5_Yaw_rev2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_orientation/V5_Yaw_rev2.jpg)\n\n---\n\nChoose which Inertial Sensor to use.\n\n![orientation_included_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_orientation/orientation_included_block.png)\n\nChoose which axis to use.\n\n![orientation_axis](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_orientation/orientation_axis.png)\n\nThe sensing block **orientation of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![orientation_of_inertial_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/inertial_orientation/orientation_of_inertial_inserted.png)\n\n"},"interact_with_enemy":{"name":"interact_with_enemy","markdown":"# Absorb enemy radiation\n\nThe **Absorb enemy radiation** block allows the VR Rover to interact with enemies in the Playground.\n\n![absorb_enemy](static/help/vr/blocks/en/interact_with_enemy/absorb_enemy.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUsing the **Absorb enemy radiation** block in a project will absorb the enemy a single time. Use it with a **Repeat** or **Forever** loop to absorb an enemy multiple times.\n\n![absorb_enemy_forever](static/help/vr/blocks/en/interact_with_enemy/absorb_enemy_forever.png)\n\n"},"interact_with_minerals":{"name":"interact_with_minerals","markdown":"# Minerals action\n\nThe **Minerals action** block allows the VR Rover to interact with minerals in the Playground.\n\n![minerals](static/help/vr/blocks/en/interact_with_minerals/minerals.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose what the VR Rover should do with the minerals.\n\n![minerals_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/interact_with_minerals/minerals_options.png)\n\nPicking up the minerals will place the minerals in the VR Rover's storage. It will be carried around until the **Minerals action** block with either the 'use' or 'drop' is used in the project.\n\nUsing the minerals will replenish some of the VR Rover's energy, as indicated by the battery icon in the Playground window.\n\n![partial_battery](static/help/vr/blocks/en/interact_with_minerals/partial_battery.png)\n\nThe VR Rover will earn Experience Points (XP) by using minerals or dropping the minerals at the base.\n\n![experience_points_added](static/help/vr/blocks/en/interact_with_minerals/experience_points_added.png)\n\n\n\n\n"},"item_of_2d_array":{"name":"item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Item of 2D list\n\nReports the value of an item in a 2D list. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which 2D list to use. The 2D list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nEnter the item's row (first number) and column (second number) position within the list to be reported. Here, the item in position row 2, column 3 in the 2D list is being reported.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\nThe **item of 2D list** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block.\n\nThe data elements in a 2D list can be referenced using an index. An index is the location of a value in the list variable. To reference an element from a list in a Switch block, place the index of the element in square brackets after the name of the list. 2D lists need to reference the row and column of the element.\n\n![switch_list2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array/switch_list2.png)\n\nElements from lists can be referenced anywhere a reporter block can be used.\n\n![switch_list_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array/switch_list_example.png)\n\n"},"item_of_2d_array_virtual":{"name":"item_of_2d_array_virtual","markdown":"# Item of 2D list\n\nReports the value of an item in a 2D list. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which 2D list to use. The 2D list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nEnter the item's row (first number) and column (second number) position within the list to be reported. Here, the item in position row 2, column 3 in the 2D list is being reported.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\nThe **item of 2D list** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of a 2D list, open the Monitor Console and click the 'Add Lists' button on the bottom right corner.\n\n![array_monitor_add](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual/array_monitor_add.png)\n\nThen, select the checkbox for the 2D list that should be added. Multiple checkboxes can be selected if more than one 2D list is defined. Click 'Save' to add the list(s) to the Monitor Console.\n\n![array_monitor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual/array_monitor_select.png)\n\n Return to the Monitor Console to view the 2D list(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![array_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual/array_monitor.png)"},"item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc":{"name":"item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Item of 2D list\n\nReports the value of an item in a 2D list. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which 2D list to use. The 2D list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nEnter the item's row (first number) and column (second number) position within the list to be reported. Here, the item in position row 2, column 3 in the 2D list is being reported.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\nThe **item of 2D list** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of a 2D list, open the Monitor Console and click the 'Add Lists' button on the bottom right corner.\n\n![array_monitor_add](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc/array_monitor_add.png)\n\nThen, select the checkbox for the 2D list that should be added. Multiple checkboxes can be selected if more than one 2D list is defined. Click 'Save' to add the list(s) to the Monitor Console.\n\n![array_monitor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc/array_monitor_select.png)\n\n Return to the Monitor Console to view the 2D list(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![array_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_2d_array_virtual_viqc/array_monitor.png)"},"item_of_array":{"name":"item_of_array","markdown":"# Item of\n\nReports the value of an item in a list. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array/item_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which list to use. The list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array/item_of_options.png)\n\nEnter the number of the item's position within the list. Here, the second item in list (item 2) is being reported. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array/item_of_block_2.png)\n\nThe **item of** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Drivetrain speed to the value stored in item 3 of my list. The Drivetrain will now move forward at a 90% velocity.\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array/item_of_example2.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block.\n\nThe data elements in a list variable can be accessed using an index. An index is the location of a value in the list variable. Each element inside of an array is assigned an integer, beginning with **zero.**\n\n![switch_list2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array/switch_list2.png)\n\nTo reference an element from a list in a Switch block, place the index of the element in square brackets after the name of the list. When converting from blocks to Switch blocks, the default will be to present the index as the number `- 1` rather than starting counting from zero.\n\n* Block being converted: \n\n![item_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array/item_of.png)\n\n* After conversion to Switch:\n\n![switch_list_item](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array/switch_list_item.png)\n\n\nElements from lists can be referenced anywhere a reporter block can be used.\n\nFor example, this project would turn the robot for `180` degrees because the 3rd element in the list is being called (zero being the 1st, one being the 2nd, two being the 3rd) \n\n![switch_list_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array/switch_list_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"item_of_array_virtual":{"name":"item_of_array_virtual","markdown":"# Item of\n\nReports the value of an item in a list. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual/item_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which list to use. The list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual/item_of_options.png)\n\nEnter the number of the item's position within the list. Here, the second item in list (item 2) is being reported. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual/item_of_block_2.png)\n\nThe **item of** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Drivetrain velocity to the value stored in item 3 of my list. The Drivetrain will now move forward at a 90% velocity.\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual/item_of_example2.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of a list, open the Monitor Console and click the 'Add Lists' button on the bottom right corner.\n\n![array_monitor_add](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual/array_monitor_add.png)\n\nThen, select the checkbox for the list that should be added. Multiple checkboxes can be selected if more than one list is defined. Click 'Save' to add the list(s) to the Monitor Console.\n\n![array_monitor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual/array_monitor_select.png)\n\nReturn to the Monitor Console to view the list(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![array_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual/array_monitor.png)"},"item_of_array_virtual_viqc":{"name":"item_of_array_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Item of\n\nReports the value of an item in a list. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual_viqc/item_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which list to use. The list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual_viqc/item_of_options.png)\n\nEnter the number of the item's position within the list. Here, the second item in list (item 2) is being reported. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual_viqc/item_of_block_2.png)\n\nThe **item of** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Drivetrain velocity to the value stored in item 3. The Drivetrain will now move forward at a 90% velocity.\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual_viqc/item_of_example2.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of a list, open the Monitor Console and click the 'Add Lists' button on the bottom right corner.\n\n![array_monitor_add](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual_viqc/array_monitor_add.png)\n\nThen, select the checkbox for the list that should be added. Multiple checkboxes can be selected if more than one list is defined. Click 'Save' to add the list(s) to the Monitor Console.\n\n![array_monitor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual_viqc/array_monitor_select.png)\n\nReturn to the Monitor Console to view the list(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![array_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/item_of_array_virtual_viqc/array_monitor.png)"},"joint_move":{"name":"joint_move","markdown":"# Joint move\n\nMoves the V5 Robotic Arm to the specified (x, y, z) coordinate using joint movement.\n\n![joint_move_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/joint_move/joint_move_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **joint move** block moves the V5 Robotic Arm in the x, y, and z-direction using joint movement. \n\n![xyz_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/en/joint_move/xyz_coord.png)\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![select_joint_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/joint_move/select_joint_arm.png)\n\nThe **joint move** block accepts decimals, integers, and numeric blocks.\n\n![joint_input](static/help/vr/blocks/en/joint_move/joint_input.png)"},"length_of_2d_array":{"name":"length_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Length of 2D list\n\nReports the number of rows or columns in a 2D list.  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/en/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\nChoose which 2D list to use. The 2D list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_select.png)\n\nChoose which value to report. The 2D list can report either **rows** or **columns**.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_option.png)\n\nThe **length of 2D list** reporter block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_insert.png)\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block.\n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Length of list**, there are two parameters that can be changed, `LISTNAME` and whether the length of the columns or rows is being reported.\n\n![switch_list_name](static/help/vr/blocks/en/length_of_2d_array/switch_list_name.png)\n\nIn Switch Python, a 2D list is best described as a list made up of other lists. Using just the `len(LISTNAME)` command will report back the number of rows (also known as the number of lists) inside of the 2D list.\n\nTo determine the number of columns, add `[0]` to the Switch Python command. This will check the length of the first list inside of the 2D list, which is equal to the number of columns.\n\n![switch_length_column](static/help/vr/blocks/en/length_of_2d_array/switch_length_column.png)\n\n\n"},"less_than":{"name":"less_than","markdown":"# Less than\n\nReports if the first value is less (smaller) than the second value. \n\n![less_than](static/help/vr/blocks/en/less_than/less_than.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Less than** reports **true** when the first value is smaller than the second value. \n\n**Less than** reports **false** when the first value is greater than or equal to the second value.\n\nThe **less than** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and fits in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\nThe **less than** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![less_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/less_than/less_than_inserted.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will energize the **Magnet** if the drive heading in degrees is less than 50.\n\n![less_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/less_than/less_than_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Less than**, enter the quantities to be compared before and after the `<`. \n\nCompare two numbers. \n\n![switch_less_than_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/less_than/switch_less_than_numbers.png)\n\nCompare with values from variables.\n\n![switch_less_than_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/less_than/switch_less_than_variable.png)\n\nCompare with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![switch_less_than_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/less_than/switch_less_than_sensor.png)"},"linear_move":{"name":"linear_move","markdown":"# Linear move\n\nMoves the V5 Robotic Arm to the specified (x, y, z) coordinate using linear movement.\n\n![linear_move_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/linear_move/linear_move_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **linear move** block moves the V5 Robotic Arm in the x, y, and z-direction using linear movement. \n\n![xyz_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/en/linear_move/xyz_coord.png)\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![select_linear_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/linear_move/select_linear_arm.png)\n\nThe **linear move** block accepts decimals, integers, and numeric blocks.\n\n![linear_input](static/help/vr/blocks/en/linear_move/linear_input.png)"},"mixed_c":{"name":"mixed_c","markdown":"# Switch C Block\n\n**Switch C** blocks loop the blocks within until the condition in the open space of the block is met. They are shaped to attach stack or other C blocks above, below, or inside them.\n\n![switch_c](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c/switch_c.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo add Switch Python commands into a **Switch C** block, select the open space of the block indicated in the image here and begin typing.\n\n![switch_c_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c/switch_c_cursor.png)\n\nSome possible Switch Python commands to use with the **Switch C** block include:\n\n* `while`\n* `if`\n* `for`\n\nIn order for a **Switch C** block to function as intended, the Switch Python commands must be entered with the correct spelling, punctuation, and indentation. Refer to the Help of each block to see the matching Switch Python command.\n\n## Example\n\n### Forever Example\n\nIn this example, the robot will repeatedly drive forward then turn right while the condition is reported as True. Because the condition is set to be `True`, this will be repeated forever.\n\n![switch_forever_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c/switch_forever_example.png)\n\n### Repeat Example\n\nIn this example, the robot will drive forward and turn right four times. \n\n![switch_repeat_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c/switch_repeat_example.png)"},"mixed_c_expandable":{"name":"mixed_c_expandable","markdown":"# Switch C Expandable\n\n**Switch C Expandable** blocks loop the blocks within a specific branch when the condition in the open space of the block is met. Additional branches can be added by expanding the block. \n\nThey are shaped to attach stack and other C blocks above, below, or inside them.\n\n![switch_c_expand](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c_expandable/switch_c_expand.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo add Switch Python commands into a **Switch C Expandable** block, select the open space of the block indicated in the image here and begin typing.\n\n![switch_c_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c_expandable/switch_c_cursor.png)\n\nThe open spaces in all branches can accept Switch Python commands.\n\n![switch_c_branch](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c_expandable/switch_c_branch.png)\n\nTo expand the block to include additional branches, select the `+` icon. \n\n![switch_c_add](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c_expandable/switch_c_add.png)\n\nTo remove a branch from the block, select the `-` icon. This will remove the lowest branch.\n\n![switch_c_remove](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c_expandable/switch_c_remove.png)\n\nSome possible Switch Python commands to use with the **Switch C Expandable** block include:\n\n* `if`\n* `elif`\n* `else`\n\nIn order for a **Switch C Expandable** block to function as intended, the Switch Python commands must be entered with the correct spelling, punctuation, and indentation. Refer to the Help of each block to see the matching Switch Python command.\n\n## Example\n\n### If Else If Else If Else Example\n\nIn this example, the robot will check the color detected by the Front Eye Sensor. The robot will execute different commands depending on the color detected.\n\n* No color detected? The robot will drive forward.\n* Red detected? The robot will stop driving.\n* Blue detected? The robot will turn left.\n* Green detected? The robot will turn right.\n\nThe **Forever** loop is used to repeatedly check the conditions in the **Switch C Expandable** branches.\n\n![switch_expand_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_c_expandable/switch_expand_example.png)\n"},"mixed_definition":{"name":"mixed_definition","markdown":"# Switch Hat\n\n**Switch Hat** blocks run the attached stack of blocks when the function is called.\n\n![switch_hat](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_definition/switch_hat.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe attached stack of blocks are a function. The **Switch Hat** block is used to define the name of the function and any optional parameters.\n\nTo define the function, select the open space of the block indicated in the image here and begin typing.\n\n![switch_hat_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_definition/switch_hat_cursor.png)\n\nParameters should be enclosed in parentheses and followed by a colon as shown below. If a function has no parameters, use closed parentheses: `()`. \n\n![switch_hat_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_definition/switch_hat_parameters.png)\n\nIn order for a **Switch Hat** block to function as intended, the Switch Python commands must be entered with the correct spelling, and punctuation. Refer to the Help of each block to see the matching Switch Python command.\n\n## Examples\n\nIn this example, the function is used to draw squares of different sides. The robot will draw a square with a side length defined by the parameter in the block that calls the function.\n\nSince the `sideLength` is set to 400, the robot will draw a square with a side length of 400 millimeters.\n\n![switch_hat_function](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_definition/switch_hat_function.png)"},"mixed_multiline_command":{"name":"mixed_multiline_command","markdown":"# Switch Stack\n\n**Switch Stack** blocks execute Switch Python commands typed within the open space of the block. They are shaped to attach above or below other stack blocks.\n\n![switch_stack](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_multiline_command/switch_stack.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo add Switch Python commands into a **Switch Stack** block, select the open space of the block indicated in the image here and begin typing.\n\n![switch_stack_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_multiline_command/switch_stack_cursor.png)\n\n**Switch Stack** blocks can be used for single lines of Switch Python commands or multiple lines. \n\n![single_line](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_multiline_command/single_line.png)\n\nTo use the block for multiple lines of Switch Python commands, press 'Enter'/'Return' on the keyboard at the end of a row to move to the next row and continue to type additional commands. \n\n![multi_line](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_multiline_command/multi_line.png)\n\nIn order for a **Switch Stack** block to function as intended, the Switch Python commands must be entered with the correct spelling, punctuation, and indentation. Refer to the Help of each block to see the matching Switch Python command.\n\n## Examples\n\n### Single Line Example\n\nIn this example, the robot will drive forward for 200 millimeters then turn right 90 degrees.\n\n![switch_stack_single](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_multiline_command/switch_stack_single.png)\n\n### Multiple Lines Example\n\nThis example has the robot put the Pen down and drive forward until the Bumper Switch is pressed. Both the command for the Pen and drive forward are inside a single **Switch Stack** block. \n\n![switch_stack_multi](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_multiline_command/switch_stack_multi.png)"},"mixed_output_boolean":{"name":"mixed_output_boolean","markdown":"# Switch Boolean\n\n**Switch Boolean** blocks return a condition as either true or false. These conditions are defined with Switch Python functions. They are shaped to fit inside any blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) inputs. \n\n![switch_boolean](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_boolean/switch_boolean.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo add Python commands into a **Switch Boolean** block, select the open space of the block indicated in the image here and begin typing.\n\n![switch_boolean_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_boolean/switch_boolean_cursor.png)\n\n**Switch Boolean** blocks can be used for single lines of Switch Python functions that directly will report a true or false condition.\n\n![single_line](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_boolean/single_line.png)\n\nOperators can be used to evaluate multiple Switch Python functions in a single **Switch Boolean** block. This includes:\n\n-  `and`\n-   `or`\n-   `not`\n\n![switch_boolean_and](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_boolean/switch_boolean_and.png)\n\nA **Switch Boolean** block can also combine Switch Python functions with comparison operators to evaluate how a value reported compares to the given number using the following operators: \n\n-   Greater Than: `>` \n-   Less Than: `<`\n-   Equal to: `=`\n-   Additional operators that can be used include `<=`, `!=`, and `>=` \n\n![switch_boolean_less](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_boolean/switch_boolean_less.png)\n\nIn order for a **Switch Boolean** block to function as intended, the Switch Python functions must be entered with the correct spelling and punctuation. Refer to the Help of each block to see the matching Python command.\n\n## Example\n\n\nIn this example, the robot will turn right until the Front Distance Sensor detects an object, then the robot will drive forward towards that object.\n\n![switch_boolean_single](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_boolean/switch_boolean_single.png)\n\nIn this example, the **Switch Boolean** block is used to evaluate if the distance between the Distance Sensor is less than 500 millimeters. If it is, then the robot will drive forward. If not, it will turn right.\n\n![switch_boolean_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_boolean/switch_boolean_example2.png)\n"},"mixed_output_number":{"name":"mixed_output_number","markdown":"# Switch Reporter\n\n**Switch Reporter** blocks return a numeric value. These conditions are defined with Switch Python functions. They are shaped to fit inside any blocks with circular inputs. \n\n![switch_reporter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_number/switch_reporter.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo add Python functions into a **Switch Reporter** block, select the open space of the block indicated in the image here and begin typing.\n\n![switch_reporter_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_number/switch_reporter_cursor.png)\n\n**Switch Reporter** blocks can be used for single lines of Switch Python functions that directly will report a numeric value.\n\n![single_line](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_number/single_line.png)\n\nMath functions can be used to calculate values within a **Switch Reporter** block. This includes:\n\n-   Addition: `+` \n-   Subtraction: `-`\n-   Division: `/`\n-   Multiplication:`*`\n-   and more.\n\n![math_functions_switch](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_number/math_functions_switch.png)\n\nTo see the full list of math functions that can be used, see the Help for the **Function** block.\n\n![function_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_number/function_block.png)\n\n## Example\n\n\nIn this example, the robot will repeatedly turn right until the Front Distance Sensor detects an object less than 500 millimeters away, then the robot will drive forward for the distance reported by the sensor to collide with the object that was detected.\n\n![switch_reporter_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_output_number/switch_reporter_example.png)"},"mixed_singleline":{"name":"mixed_singleline","markdown":"# Mixed Mode Single Line\n\nA mixed mode single line block allows user to enter Python code into this block. The system will treat this entire line as a line of Python code.\n\n![block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_singleline/block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUser can drag this single line blocks into any place where it will match the shape of a block.\n\n![block_placed](static/help/vr/blocks/en/mixed_singleline/block_placed.png)\n"},"motor_is_done":{"name":"motor_is_done","markdown":"# Motor is done\n\nReports if the selected V5 Motor or Motor Group has completed its movement.\n\n![motor_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Motor is done** reports **true** when the selected Motor or Motor Group has completed its movement.\n\n**Motor is done** reports **false** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is still moving.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![motor_is_done_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_motor.png)\n\nThe **motor is done** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![motor_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n\n"},"motor_is_done_go":{"name":"motor_is_done_go","markdown":"# Motor is done\n\nReports if the selected motor has completed its movement.\n\n![motor_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_go/motor_is_done_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Motor is done** block reports TRUE when the selected motor has completed its movement.\n\nThe **Motor is done** block reports FALSE when the selected motor is still moving.\n\nChoose which motor to use.\n\n![motor_is_done_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_go/motor_is_done_motor.png)\n\nThe **Motor is done** Boolean block reports a TRUE or FALSE value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![motor_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_go/motor_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Motor is done** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![motor_is_done_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_go/motor_is_done_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the Boolean block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![motor_is_done_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_go/motor_is_done_monitor.png)\n"},"motor_is_done_virtual":{"name":"motor_is_done_virtual","markdown":"# Motor is done\n\nReports if the selected V5 Motor or Motor Group has completed its movement.\n\n![motor_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual/motor_is_done_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Motor is done** reports **true** when the selected Motor or Motor Group has completed its movement.\n\n**Motor is done** reports **false** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is still moving.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![motor_is_done_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual/motor_is_done_motor.png)\n\nThe **Motor is done** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![motor_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual/motor_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Motor is done** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![motor_is_done_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual/motor_is_done_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![motor_is_done_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual/motor_is_done_monitor.png)\n"},"motor_is_done_virtual_viqc":{"name":"motor_is_done_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Motor is done\n\nReports if the selected VEX IQ Smart Motor or Motor Group has completed its movement.\n\n![motor_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual_viqc/motor_is_done_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Motor is done** reports **true** when the selected Motor/Motor Group has completed its movement.\n\n**Motor is done** reports **false** when the selected Motor/Motor Group is still moving.\n\nChoose which Motor/Motor Group to use.\n\n![motor_is_done_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual_viqc/motor_is_done_motor.png)\n\nThe **Motor is done** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![motor_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual_viqc/motor_is_done_inserted.png)\n\nThe block for reporting if the motor is done is used inside blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces for other blocks. \n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Motor is done** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![motor_is_done_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual_viqc/motor_is_done_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![motor_is_done_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_done_virtual_viqc/motor_is_done_monitor.png)"},"motor_is_spinning":{"name":"motor_is_spinning","markdown":"# Motor is spinning\n\nReports if the selected V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group is currently spinning.\n\n![motor_is_spinning](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Motor is spinning** reports **true** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is spinning.\n\n**Motor is spinning** reports **false** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is stopped.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning_motor.png)\n\nThe **motor is spinning** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning_inserted.png)\n"},"motor_is_spinning_go":{"name":"motor_is_spinning_go","markdown":"# Motor is spinning\n\nReports if the selected motor is currently spinning.\n\n![motor_is_spinning](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_go/motor_is_spinning.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Motor is spinning** block reports TRUE when the selected motor is spinning.\n\nThe **Motor is spinning** block reports FALSE when the selected motor is stopped.\n\nChoose which motor to use.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_go/motor_is_spinning_motor.png)\n\nThe **Motor is spinning** Boolean block reports a TRUE or FALSE value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_go/motor_is_spinning_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Motor is spinning** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_go/motor_is_spinning_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the Boolean block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_go/motor_is_spinning_monitor.png)"},"motor_is_spinning_virtual":{"name":"motor_is_spinning_virtual","markdown":"# Motor is spinning\n\nReports if the selected V5 Motor or Motor Group is currently spinning.\n\n![motor_is_spinning](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual/motor_is_spinning.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Motor is spinning** reports **true** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is spinning.\n\n**Motor is spinning** reports **false** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is stopped.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual/motor_is_spinning_motor.png)\n\nThe **Motor is spinning** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual/motor_is_spinning_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Motor is spinning** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual/motor_is_spinning_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual/motor_is_spinning_monitor.png)\n"},"motor_is_spinning_virtual_viqc":{"name":"motor_is_spinning_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Motor is spinning\n\nReports if the selected VEX IQ Smart Motor or Motor Group is currently spinning.\n\n![motor_is_spinning](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual_viqc/motor_is_spinning.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Motor is spinning** reports **true** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is spinning.\n\n**Motor is spinning** reports **false** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is stopped.\n\nChoose which Motor/Motor Group to use.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual_viqc/motor_is_spinning_motor.png)\n\nThe **Motor is spinning** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual_viqc/motor_is_spinning_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Motor is spinning** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual_viqc/motor_is_spinning_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/motor_is_spinning_virtual_viqc/motor_is_spinning_monitor.png)"},"move_pen":{"name":"move_pen","markdown":"# Move Robot Pen\n\nSets the position of the VEX VR Pen.\n\n![move_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/en/move_pen/move_pen.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the position of the VR Pen\n\n![move_pen2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/move_pen/move_pen2.png)\n\n* up - the pen will not draw a line on the board.\n* down - the pen will draw a colored line on the board.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will draw a red line on the board.\n\n![move_pen3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/move_pen/move_pen3.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Move robot pen**, there is one parameter that can be changed (DIRECTION).\n\n![switch_move_robot_pen_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/move_pen/switch_move_robot_pen_parameters.png)\n\nSet the DIRECTION you want the VR Pen to move by providing the following:\n\n- DIRECTION - `UP` or `DOWN`\n\n![switch_move_robot_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/en/move_pen/switch_move_robot_pen.png)"},"multiply":{"name":"multiply","markdown":"# Multiply\n\nMultiplies any two values together and reports the product.\n\n![multiply](static/help/vr/blocks/en/multiply/multiply.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Multiply** can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nThe  **multiply** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces, and can accept circular blocks in it as well. \n\nMultiply two numbers.\n\n![multiply_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/multiply/multiply_numbers.png)\n\nMultiply with values of variables.\n\n![multiply_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/en/multiply/multiply_variables.png)\n\nMultiply with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![multiply_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/multiply/multiply_sensors.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Multiply**, enter the quantities to be multiplied before and after the `*`. \n\nMultiply two numbers. \n\n![switch_multiply_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/multiply/switch_multiply_numbers.png)\n\nMultiply with values from variables.\n\n![switch_multiply_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/multiply/switch_multiply_variable.png)\n\nMultiply with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![switch_multiply_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/multiply/switch_multiply_sensor.png)"},"near_object":{"name":"near_object","markdown":"# Found object\n\nReports if the Eye Sensor on the front of the 123 Robot detects an object within approximately 40 millimeters (mm) or 1.5 inches.\n\n\n![eye_found_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/near_object/near_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\nThe **Found object** block reports **TRUE** when the Eye Sensor detects an object within approximately 40 millimeters (mm) or 1.5 inches.\n\nThe **Found object** block reports **FALSE** if an object is **NOT** within approximately 40 millimeters (mm) or 1.5 inches.\n\nThe **Found object** block is a Boolean block that reports a **TRUE** or **FALSE** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![found_object_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/near_object/found_object_condition.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Found object** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![found_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/near_object/found_object_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the Boolean block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![found_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/near_object/found_object_monitor.png)"},"next_row":{"name":"next_row","markdown":"# Set cursor to next row\n\nSets the cursor to a new line in the Print Console when using the **print** blocks. \n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/en/next_row/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## How To Use\n\nUse the **set cursor to next row** block to set the cursor to a new line in the display.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" and on the next line in the display it will print \"World!\"\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/next_row/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Set cursor to next row** does not require any parameters inside the parentheses.\n\n![switch_set_cursor_to_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/en/next_row/switch_set_cursor_to_next_row.png)\n"},"next_row_virtual":{"name":"next_row_virtual","markdown":"# Set cursor to next row\n\nSets the cursor to a new line in the Print Console when using the **print** blocks. \n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/en/next_row_virtual/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## How To Use\n\nUse the **set cursor to next row** block to set the cursor to a new line in the display.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" and on the next line in the display it will print \"World!\"\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/next_row_virtual/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"next_row_virtual_viqc":{"name":"next_row_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Set cursor to next row\n\nSets the cursor to a new line in the Print Console when using the **print** blocks. \n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/en/next_row_virtual_viqc/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## How To Use\n\nUse the **set cursor to next row** block to set the cursor to a new line in the display.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" and on the next line in the display it will print \"World!\"\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/next_row_virtual_viqc/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"not":{"name":"not","markdown":"# Not\n\nReports the opposite (inverse) of the reported Boolean value.\n\n![not](static/help/vr/blocks/en/not/not.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **not** block reports **true** when the Boolean block inside reports **false**.\n\nThe **not** block reports **false** when the Boolean block inside reports **true**.\n\nThe **not** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and fits in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will report **true** when the the **Distance sensor** does not detect an object. \n\n![not_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/not/not_sensors.png)\n\n![not_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/en/not/not_truth.png)  \n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Not**, specify the Boolean condition that will be evaluated in the project after the `not`.\n\nIf no Boolean condition is specified, the Switch Python command will default to `not False`.\n\n![switch_not_false](static/help/vr/blocks/en/not/switch_not_false.png)\n\nIn this example, the Switch Python **Not** command will report as **true** when the Boolean condition of the `front_distance.found_object()` reports as **false**.\n\n![switch_not_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/not/switch_not_example.png)\n\n"},"object_count":{"name":"object_count","markdown":"# Object count\n\nReports how many objects the Vision Sensor detects. \n\nAn object will need to be configured before the  **object count** block can detect it.\n\n![object_count_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_count/object_count_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Vision Sensor to use.\n\n![object_count_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_count/object_count_device.png)\n\nThe **snapshot of** block will need to be used before the **object count** block reports a number of objects.\n\nThe **object count** block will only detect the number of objects from the last snapshot signature.\n\n\n"},"object_exists":{"name":"object_exists","markdown":"# Object exists\n\nReports if the Vision Sensor detects a configured object. \n\nAn object will need to be configured before the **object exists** block can detect it.\n\n![object_exists_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_exists/object_exists_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Vision Sensor to use.\n\n![object_exists_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_exists/object_exists_device.png)\n\n\n**Object exists** reports **true** when the Vision Sensor detects a configured object.\n\n**Object exists** reports **false** when the Vision Sensor does not detect a configured object.\n\nThe **object exists** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![object_exists_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_exists/object_exists_inserted.png)\n\nThe **snapshot of** block will need to be used before the **object exists** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value."},"object_in_front":{"name":"object_in_front","markdown":"# Object found\n\nReports if the Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor sees an object within its field of view. \n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_in_front/object_in_front_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Object found** reports **true** when the Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view.\n\n**Object found** reports **false** when the Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor does not detect an object or surface.\n\nChoose which Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor to use.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_in_front/object_in_front_distance.png)\n\nThe **object found** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_in_front/object_in_front_inserted.png)\n "},"object_is_near_go":{"name":"object_is_near_go","markdown":"# Found object\n\nReports if the Eye Sensor detects an object within approximately 40 millimeters (mm) or 1.5 inches.\n\n\n![eye_found_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_go/eye_found_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\nThe **Found object** block reports TRUE when the Eye Sensor detects an object within approximately 40 millimeters (mm) or 1.5 inches.\n\nThe **Found object** block reports FALSE if an object is NOT within approximately 40 millimeters (mm) or 1.5 inches.\n\nThe **Found object** block is a Boolean block that reports a TRUE or FALSE value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![found_object_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_go/found_object_condition.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Found object** block, select the checkbox next to the Boolean block.\n\n![found_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_go/found_object_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the Boolean block selected for monitoring.\n\n![found_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_go/found_object_monitor.png)\n"},"object_is_near_virtual_viqc":{"name":"object_is_near_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Object is near\n\nReports if the VEX IQ Color Sensor detects an object nearby.\n\n![object_is_near](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_virtual_viqc/object_is_near_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Object is near** reports **true** when the Color Sensor detects an object or surface close to the front of the sensor.\n\n**Object is near** reports **false** when the Color Sensor detects empty space in front of the sensor.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor to use.\n\n![object_is_near_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_virtual_viqc/object_is_near_color.png)\n\nThe **Object is near** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![object_is_near_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_virtual_viqc/object_is_near_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Object is near** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![object_is_near_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_virtual_viqc/object_is_near_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![object_is_near_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/object_is_near_virtual_viqc/object_is_near_monitor.png)\n "},"optical_brightness":{"name":"optical_brightness","markdown":"# Color brightness\n\nReports the brightness of an object from one of the Color Sensor Eyes.\n\n![color_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness/color_brightness.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Color brightness** reports the brightness of an object. White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%. All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between those values.\n\nChoose which Distance Sensor to use. The **FrontEye** is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, The **LeftEye** is mounted on the left side of the robot and faces left, the **RightEye** is mounted on the right side of the robot and faces right, the **RearEye** is mounted on the backside of the robot and faces backward, and the **DownEye** is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\nThe base VR robot only has the **FrontEye** and **DownEye** as sensor options.\n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness/color_brightness_sensorbase.png)\n\nThe enhanced VR Robot also has **LeftEye**, **RightEye**, and **RearEye** sensors.\n\n![color_brightness_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness/color_brightness_eyes.png)\n\nThe **color brightness** Number block reports a value between 0 and 100 value and is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![color_brightness_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness/color_brightness_insert.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example should be carried out in the **Disk Maze** Playground. The robot will drive forward until the DownEye reports the brightness of an object beneath it to be less than 100%. Then it will turn left and drive forward again.\n\n![color_brightness_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness/color_brightness_example.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Color brightness**, specify the EYE Sensor that is to be evaluated in the project.\n\n![switch_color_brightness_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness/switch_color_brightness_parameters.png)\n\nSet the EYE Sensor to indicate which sensor on the VR Robot to evaluate: \n\n* EYE - `front_eye` or `down_eye`\n\nNote that the DownEye is the only option for the VR Robot in the Maze+ Playground.\n\nThe Switch Python Command for **Color brightness** does not require any additional parameters inside the parentheses. In this example, the brightness of the `down_eye` will be reported.\n\n![switch_color_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness/switch_color_brightness.png)\n\n\n"},"optical_brightness_virtual":{"name":"optical_brightness_virtual","markdown":"# Optical brightness\n\nReports the amount of light detected by the Optical Sensor.\n\n![optical_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness_virtual/optical_brightness.png)\n\n## How To Use\nChoose an Optical Sensor to use.\n\n![optical_brightness_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness_virtual/optical_brightness_device.png)\n\nThe **Optical brightness** block reports a number value from **0%** to **100%**.\n\nA large amount of light detected will report a high brightness value.\n\nA small amount of light detected will report a low brightness value.\n\nThe **Optical brightness** block reports number values and is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![optical_brightness_use](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness_virtual/optical_brightness_use.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Optical brightness** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![optical_brightness_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness_virtual/optical_brightness_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![optical_brightness_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_brightness_virtual/optical_brightness_monitor.png)"},"optical_color":{"name":"optical_color","markdown":"# Color sensing\n\nReports if one of the Color Sensor Eyes detects a specific color.\n\n![color_sensing_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_color/color_sensing_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Color sensing** reports **true** when the Color Sensor Eye detects the selected color.\n\n**Color sensing** reports **false** when the Color Sensor Eye detects a different color than the one selected.\n\nChoose which Distance Sensor to use. The **FrontEye** is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, The **LeftEye** is mounted on the left side of the robot and faces left, the **RightEye** is mounted on the right side of the robot and faces right, the **RearEye** is mounted on the backside of the robot and faces backward, and the **DownEye** is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\nThe base VR robot only has the **FrontEye** and **DownEye** as sensor options.\n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_color/color_sensing_color_sensorbase.png)\n\nThe enhanced VR Robot also has **LeftEye**, **RightEye**, and **RearEye** sensors.\n\n![color_sensing_color_eye](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_color/color_sensing_color_eye.png)\n\nChoose which color to detect.\n\n![color_sensing_color_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_color/color_sensing_color_color.png)\n\nThe **color sensing** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![color_sensing_color_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_color/color_sensing_color_shape.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example should be carried out in the **Disk Maze** Playground. It will drive forward with the pen down until it detects an object with a color, and then it will change the pen color and turn around.\n\n![color_sensing_color_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_color/color_sensing_color_example.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Color sensing**, there are two parameters that can be changed (EYE and COLOR).\n\n![switch_color_sensing_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_color/switch_color_sensing_parameters.png)\n\nSet the EYE Sensor to indicate which sensor on the VR Robot to evaluate, and the COLOR to detect: \n\n* EYE - `front_eye` or `down_eye`\n* COLOR - `RED`, `GREEN`, `BLUE`, or `NONE`\n\nNote that the `down_eye` is the only option for the VR Robot in the Maze + Playground. \n\nIn this example, the condition of the `down_eye` detecting RED will be evaluated.\n\n![switch_color_sensing](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_color/switch_color_sensing.png)\n\n"},"optical_detect":{"name":"optical_detect","markdown":"# Optical detects color\n\nReports if the Optical Sensor detects the specified color.\n\n![optical_detect](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect/optical_detect.png)\n\n## How To Use\nChoose an Optical Sensor to use.\n\n![optical_detect_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect/optical_detect_device.png)\n\nChoose which color you want the sensor to check for.\n\n![optical_detect_colors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect/optical_detect_colors.png)\n\nThe **Optical detects color** block reports **true** when the Optical Sensor detects the specified color.\n\nThe **Optical detects color** block reports **false** if it doesn't detect the specified color.\n\nThe **Optical detects color** block reports **true** or **false** and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![optical_detect_use](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect/optical_detect_use.png)\n"},"optical_detect_gesture":{"name":"optical_detect_gesture","markdown":"# When Optical detects a gesture \n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the Optical sensor detects a gesture.\n\n![optical_detect_gesture_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_gesture/optical_detect_gesture_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nBefore using the **When Optical detects a gesture** event, the **Optical set mode** block must be used to set the Optical Sensor to detect gestures.\n\n![optical_set_mode_gesture_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_gesture/optical_set_mode_gesture_block.png)\n\nThe **When Optical detects a gesture** event can be used to create actions or behaviors based on gestures that the Optical Sensor detects.\n\nChoose an Optical Sensor.\n\n![optical_detects_gesture_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_gesture/optical_detects_gesture_device.png)\n\nChoose which gesture triggers the **When Optical detects a gesture** event.\n\n![optical_detect_gesture_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_gesture/optical_detect_gesture_block2.png)"},"optical_detect_object":{"name":"optical_detect_object","markdown":"# When Eye Sensor detects/loses \n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the Eye Sensor detects or loses an object.\n\n![optical_detect_object_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_object/optical_detect_object_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **When Eye detects/loses** event can be used to create actions or behaviors based on whether the Eye Sensor **detects** an object in range of the sensor or **loses** an object that was in range of the sensor.\n\nChoose an Eye sensor.\n\n![optical_detect_object_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_object/optical_detect_object_device.png)\n\nChoose whether the **When Eye detects/loses** event is triggered when the sensor **detects** an object, or **loses** an object.\n\n![optical_detect_object_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_object/optical_detect_object_block2.png)"},"optical_detect_virtual":{"name":"optical_detect_virtual","markdown":"# Optical detects color\n\nReports if the Optical Sensor detects the specified color.\n\n![optical_detect](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_virtual/optical_detect.png)\n\n## How To Use\nChoose an Optical Sensor to use.\n\n![optical_detect_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_virtual/optical_detect_device.png)\n\nChoose which color you want the sensor to check for.\n\n![optical_detect_colors](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_virtual/optical_detect_colors.png)\n\nThe **Optical detects color** block reports **true** when the Optical Sensor detects the specified color.\n\nThe **Optical detects color** block reports **false** if it doesn't detect the specified color.\n\nThe **Optical detects color** block reports **true** or **false** and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![optical_detect_use](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_virtual/optical_detect_use.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Optical detects color** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![optical_detect_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_virtual/optical_detect_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![optical_detect_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_detect_virtual/optical_detect_monitor.png)\n"},"optical_found_object":{"name":"optical_found_object","markdown":"# Optical found object\n\nReports if the Optical Sensor detects an object close to it.\n\n\n![optical_found_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_found_object/optical_found_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\nChoose an Optical Sensor to use.\n\n![optical_found_object_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_found_object/optical_found_object_device.png)\n\nThe **Optical found object** block reports **true** when the Optical Sensor detects an object close to it.\n\nThe **Optical found object** block reports **false** if an object is not within range.\n\nThe **Optical found object** block should be used to check if an object is close to the Optical Sensor so that the color readings from the **Optical detects color** block are accurate.\n\nThe **Optical found object** block reports **true** or **false** and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![optical_found_object_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_found_object/optical_found_object_condition.png)"},"optical_found_object_virtual":{"name":"optical_found_object_virtual","markdown":"# Optical found object\n\nReports if the Optical Sensor detects an object close to it.\n\n\n![optical_found_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_found_object_virtual/optical_found_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\nChoose an Optical Sensor to use.\n\n![optical_found_object_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_found_object_virtual/optical_found_object_device.png)\n\nThe **Optical found object** block reports **true** when the Optical Sensor detects an object close to it.\n\nThe **Optical found object** block reports **false** if an object is not within range.\n\nThe **Optical found object** block should be used to check if an object is close to the Optical Sensor so that the color readings from the **Optical detects color** block are accurate.\n\nThe **Optical found object** block reports **true** or **false** and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![optical_found_object_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_found_object_virtual/optical_found_object_condition.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Optical found object** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![optical_found_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_found_object_virtual/optical_found_object_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![optical_found_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_found_object_virtual/optical_found_object_monitor.png)"},"optical_gesture_detected":{"name":"optical_gesture_detected","markdown":"# Optical gesture detected\n\nReports whether an Optical Sensor has detected the specified gesture.\n\n![optical_gesture_detected_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_gesture_detected/optical_gesture_detected_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Optical gesture detected** block reports whether a gesture has been detected.\n\nBefore using the **Optical gesture detected** block, the Optical Sensor needs to be set to detect gestures with the **Optical set mode** block.\n\n![optical_set_mode_gesture_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_gesture_detected/optical_set_mode_gesture_block.png)\n\nChoose an Optical Sensor to use.\n\n![optical_gesture_detected_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_gesture_detected/optical_gesture_detected_device.png)\n\nChoose which gesture to check for.\n\n![optical_gesture_detected_gestures](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_gesture_detected/optical_gesture_detected_gestures.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below waits until the Optical Sensor detects an **up** gesture before driving the robot forward.\n\n![optical_gesture_detected_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_gesture_detected/optical_gesture_detected_example.png)"},"optical_hue":{"name":"optical_hue","markdown":"# Optical hue\n\nReports the hue of the color of an object.\n\n![optical_hue](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_hue/optical_hue.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose an optical sensor.\n\n![optical_hue_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_hue/optical_hue_device.png)\n\nThe **Optical hue** block reports a number value that is the hue of the color of an object. It returns a number between **0** and **359**, which can be thought of as the location of the color on a color wheel.\n\nThe **Optical hue** block reports number values and is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![optical_hue_use](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_hue/optical_hue_use.png)"},"optical_hue_virtual":{"name":"optical_hue_virtual","markdown":"# Optical hue\n\nReports the hue of the color of an object.\n\n![optical_hue](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_hue_virtual/optical_hue.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose an optical sensor.\n\n![optical_hue_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_hue_virtual/optical_hue_device.png)\n\nThe **Optical hue** block reports a number value that is the hue of the color of an object. It returns a number between **0** and **359**, which can be thought of as the location of the color on a color wheel.\n\nThe **Optical hue** block reports number values and is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![optical_hue_use](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_hue_virtual/optical_hue_use.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Optical hue** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![optical_hue_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_hue_virtual/optical_hue_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![optical_hue_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_hue_virtual/optical_hue_monitor.png)"},"optical_near_object":{"name":"optical_near_object","markdown":"# Eye Sensor near object\n\nReports if the Color Sensor Eye is close enough to an object to detect a color.\n\n![color_near_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_near_object/color_near_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Color near object** reports **true** when the Color Sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.\n\n**Color near object** reports **false** when the Color Sensor isn't close enough to an object with detectable colors.\n\nChoose which Distance Sensor to use. The **FrontEye** is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, The **LeftEye** is mounted on the left side of the robot and faces left, the **RightEye** is mounted on the right side of the robot and faces right, the **RearEye** is mounted on the backside of the robot and faces backward, and the **DownEye** is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\nThe base VR robot only has the **FrontEye** and **DownEye** as sensor options.\n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_near_object/color_near_object_sensorbase.png)\n\nThe enhanced VR Robot also has **LeftEye**, **RightEye**, and **RearEye** sensors.\n\n![color_near_object_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_near_object/color_near_object_eyes.png)\n\nThe **near object** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![color_near_object_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_near_object/color_near_object_shape.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example should be carried out in the **Disk Maze** Playground. It will drive forward until the FrontEye Color Sensor reports it is near an object with detectable color, then drive in reverse.\n\n![color_near_object_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_near_object/color_near_object_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Eye Sensor near object**, there are two parameters that can be changed (EYE and UNITS).\n\n![switch_eye_sensor_near_object_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_near_object/switch_eye_sensor_near_object_parameters.png)\n\nSet the EYE Sensor to indicate which sensor on the VR Robot to evaluate: \n\n* EYE - `front_eye` or `down_eye`\n\nNote that the DownEye is the only option for the VR Robot in the Maze+ Playground.\n\nThe Switch Python command for **Eye Sensor near object** does not require any additional parameters inside the parentheses. In this example, the `down_eye` will be evaluated.\n\n![switch_eye_sensor_near_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_near_object/switch_eye_sensor_near_object.png)\n\n\n"},"optical_set_light":{"name":"optical_set_light","markdown":"# Set Optical light\n\nSets the light on the Optical Sensor to on or off.\n\n![optical_set_light](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_light/optical_set_light.png)\n\n## How To Use\nThe **Set optical light** block allows you to turn the Optical Sensor's light **on** or **off**. The light lets the sensor see objects if it is looking at an object in a dark area.\n\nChoose an Optical Sensor to use.\n\n![optical_set_light_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_light/optical_set_light_device.png)\n\nChoose whether you want to set the light on the Optical Sensor to **on** or **off**.\n\n![optical_set_light_state](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_light/optical_set_light_state.png)\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, we use the **Set optical light** block to turn the light on. Then, if the Optical Sensor detects an object, we print the hue.\n\n\n![optical_set_light_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_light/optical_set_light_example.png)"},"optical_set_light_power":{"name":"optical_set_light_power","markdown":"# Set optical light power \n\nSet the light power of V5 Optical sensor\n\n![optical_set_light_power_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_light_power/optical_set_light_power_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Set optical light power** block accepts a range of **0%** to **100%**. This will change the brightness of the light on the Optical Sensor. If the light is off, this block will turn the light on.\n\nChoose an Optical Sensor.\n\n![optical_set_power_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_light_power/optical_set_power_device.png)\n\nThe set optical light power block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![optical_set_power_num](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_light_power/optical_set_power_num.png)\n\n## Example\nIn this example we use the **Set optical light power** block to set the power of the light to **50%**, then we use the **Set optical light** block to turn the light on. Then, if the Optical Sensor detects an object, we print the hue.\n\n![optical_set_power_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_light_power/optical_set_power_example.png)"},"optical_set_mode":{"name":"optical_set_mode","markdown":"# Set Optical mode\n\nSets an Optical Sensor to either detect colors or gestures.\n\n![optical_set_mode_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_mode/optical_set_mode_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Set optical mode** block allows you to set an Optical Sensor to either detect colors or gestures.\n\nChoose an Optical Sensor to use.\n\n![optical_set_mode_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_mode/optical_set_mode_device.png)\n\nChoose whether you want to set the mode of the Optical Sensor to either detect colors or gestures.\n\n![optical_set_mode_mode](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_mode/optical_set_mode_mode.png)\n\nBy default, an Optical Sensor is set to always detect colors. Before using any Optical Sensor gesture blocks, the Optical Sensor must be set to detect gestures.\n\n![optical_set_mode_gesture_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/optical_set_mode/optical_set_mode_gesture_block.png)"},"or":{"name":"or","markdown":"# Or\n\nReports if one of two Boolean conditions are true.\n\n![or](static/help/vr/blocks/en/or/or.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **or** block reports **true** when one (or both) of the conditions are **true**. \n\nThe **or** block reports **false** when both of the conditions are **false**.\n\nThe **or** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and fits in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![or_sensors2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/or/or_sensors2.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThe **or** block reports **true** when either the **Distance sensor** detects and object, and/or the **FrontEye** detects an object. \n\nThe **or** block reports **false** only when both the **Distance sensor** does not detect an object and the **FrontEye** does not detect an object.\n\n![or_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/en/or/or_truth.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Or**, specify the Boolean conditions that will be evaluated in the project before and after the `or`.\n\nIf no Boolean conditions are specified, the Switch Python command will default to `False or False`.\n\n![switch_or_false](static/help/vr/blocks/en/or/switch_or_false.png)\n\nIn this example, the Switch Python **Or** command will report as **true** when either Boolean conditions of the Down Eye Sensor near object or Down Eye Sensor detects RED report as **true**.\n\n![switch_or_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/or/switch_or_example.png)"},"play_sound":{"name":"play_sound","markdown":"# Play sound\nPlays the sound effect that is selected.\n\n![play_sound](static/help/vr/blocks/en/play_sound/play_sound.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the sound effect from a list of options. \n\n* **honk**: a sound similar to a car horn.\n* **doorbell**: a sound similar to a doorbell chime.\n* **crash**: a sound similar to a collision.\n* **random**: a randomly selected sound effect. \n\nThe **Play sound** block is a waiting block, which means that no other blocks will run until this block has completed its action. Once the sound effect specified in the **Play sound** block has completed, the project will move on to the next block in the stack.\n\n![play_sound_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/play_sound/play_sound_select.png)"},"position":{"name":"position","markdown":"# Position of Robot\n\nReports the X or Y coordinate position of the Robot.\n\n![position_of_robot_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position/position_of_robot_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of robot** reports the X or Y coordinate position of the Robot in integer numbers as either millimeters (mm) or inches (inches).\n\nChoose whether to return the X coordinate, or Y coordinate.\n\n![position_of_robot_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position/position_of_robot_coord.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in mm or inches.\n\n![position_of_robot_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position/position_of_robot_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **position of robot** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_robot_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position/position_of_robot_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Position of robot**, there are two parameters that can be changed (AXIS and UNITS).\n\n![switch_position_of_robot_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position/switch_position_of_robot_parameters.png)\n\nSet the AXIS and UNITS that will be reported by providing the following: \n\n* AXIS - `X` or `Y`\n* UNITS - `MM` (millimeters) or `INCHES`\n\nIn this example, the position of the VR Robot on the `X` Axis will be reported in millimeters.\n\n![switch_position_of_robot](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position/switch_position_of_robot.png)\n"},"position_angle":{"name":"position_angle","markdown":"# Position angle in degrees\n\nReports angle of the Robot in degrees.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position angle in degrees** reports the current angle of the robot.\n\nThe reporter block **position angle in degrees** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_robot_angle_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Position angle in degrees** will always report a numerical value in DEGREES.\n\n![switch_position_angle_in_degrees](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_angle/switch_position_angle_in_degrees.png)"},"position_of_controller":{"name":"position_of_controller","markdown":"# Position of controller\n\nReports the position of a joystick on the V5 Controller along an axis.\n\n![position_of_controller_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_controller/position_of_controller_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of controller** reports a range between **-100 to 100**.\n\n**Position of controller** will report zero (0) when a joystick axis is centered.\n\n![controller_button_front](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_controller/controller_button_front.png)\n\nChoose the V5 Controller to use.\n\n![position_of_controller_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_controller/position_of_controller_devices.png)\n\nChoose the joystick's axis to check its position.\n\n![position_of_controller_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_controller/position_of_controller_options.png)\n\nThe **position of controller** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![position_of_controller_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_controller/position_of_controller_inserted.png)\n\n"},"position_of_motor":{"name":"position_of_motor","markdown":"# Position of motor\n\nReports the distance a V5 Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group has traveled.\n\n![position_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor/position_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of motor** reports the position of a V5 Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group in integer numbers for degrees, and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nChoose which V5 Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![position_of_motor_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor/position_of_motor_motor.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in degrees rotated or in turns.\n\n![position_of_motor_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor/position_of_motor_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **position of motor** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor/position_of_motor_inserted.png)\n"},"position_of_motor_go":{"name":"position_of_motor_go","markdown":"# Position of motor\n\nReports the distance the motor has traveled.\n\n![position_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_go/position_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Position of motor** block reports the position of the motor in integer numbers for degrees and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nChoose which motor to use.\n\n![position_of_motor_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_go/position_of_motor_motor.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in degrees rotated or in turns of the motor. \n\n![position_of_motor_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_go/position_of_motor_unit.png)\n\nThe **Position of motor** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_go/position_of_motor_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Position of motor** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![position_of_motor_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_go/position_of_motor_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![position_of_motor_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_go/position_of_motor_monitor.png)"},"position_of_motor_virtual":{"name":"position_of_motor_virtual","markdown":"# Position of motor\n\nReports the distance a V5 Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group has traveled.\n\n![position_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual/position_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of motor** reports the position of a V5 Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group in integer numbers for degrees, and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nChoose which V5 Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![position_of_motor_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual/position_of_motor_motor.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in degrees rotated or in turns.\n\n![position_of_motor_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual/position_of_motor_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Position of motor** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual/position_of_motor_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Position of motor** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![position_of_motor_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual/position_of_motor_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![position_of_motor_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual/position_of_motor_monitor.png)\n"},"position_of_motor_virtual_viqc":{"name":"position_of_motor_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Position of motor\n\nReports the distance a VEX IQ Motor or the first motor in a Motor Group has traveled.\n\n![position_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual_viqc/position_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of motor** reports the position of a Motor or the first motor in a Motor Group in integer numbers for degrees and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nChoose which Motor/Motor Group to use.\n\n![position_of_motor_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual_viqc/position_of_motor_motor.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in degrees rotated or in turns. \n\n![position_of_motor_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual_viqc/position_of_motor_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Position of motor** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual_viqc/position_of_motor_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Position of motor** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![position_of_motor_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual_viqc/position_of_motor_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![position_of_motor_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_motor_virtual_viqc/position_of_motor_monitor.png)\n"},"position_of_shaft_encoder":{"name":"position_of_shaft_encoder","markdown":"# Position of shaft encoder\n\nReports the distance the Shaft Encoder has rotated.\n\n![position_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_shaft_encoder/position_of_encoder_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of shaft encoder** reports the distance the Shaft Encoder has rotated in integer numbers for degrees, and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nChoose which Shaft Encoder to use.\n\n![position_of_motor_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_shaft_encoder/position_of_encoder_device.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in degrees rotated or in turns of the Shaft Encoder. \n\n![position_of_motor_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_shaft_encoder/position_of_encoder_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **position of shaft encoder** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/position_of_shaft_encoder/position_of_encoder_inserted.png)"},"power":{"name":"power","markdown":"# Set electromagnet power\n\nSets the V5 Electromagnet power that will be used when picking up and object with the energize block.\n\n![set_magnet_power_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/power/set_magnet_power_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nYou may need to pick up objects with a varying weights. The **set electromagnet power** block lets you control how much power the elctromagnet will use. The **set electromagnet power** block has a range of **0% to 100%**\n\nChoose which magnet to use.\n\n![set_magnet_dropdown_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/power/set_magnet_dropdown_block.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the robot to drive forward 200mm, then it will increase the power using the **set electromagnet power** block, then the **Electromagnet** will boost to pick up an object, drive forward 200mm, then drop the object.\n\n![set_magnet_power_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/power/set_magnet_power_example.png)\n"},"power_of_motor":{"name":"power_of_motor","markdown":"# Power of motor\n\nReports the amount of power output a V5 Smart Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group is currently generating.\n\n![motor_power](static/help/vr/blocks/en/power_of_motor/motor_power.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Power of motor** reports a range from **0.0 to 11.0 watts**. \n\nChoose a Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![motor_power_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/power_of_motor/motor_power_device.png)\n\nThe reporter block **power of motor** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![motor_power_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/power_of_motor/motor_power_inserted.png)\n"},"pressing_bumper":{"name":"pressing_bumper","markdown":"# Pressing bumper\n\nReports if the Bumper Switch is pressed.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **true** if the Bumper Switch is being pressed.\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **false** if the Bumper Switch is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nThe **pressing bumper** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n\n\n"},"pressing_bumper_virtual":{"name":"pressing_bumper_virtual","markdown":"# Pressing bumper\n\nReports if the Bumper Switch is pressed.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **true** if the Bumper Switch is being pressed.\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **false** if the Bumper Switch is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nThe **Pressing bumper** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n \n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Pressing bumper** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)"},"pressing_bumper_virtual_viqc":{"name":"pressing_bumper_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Pressing bumper\n\nReports if the VEX IQ Bumper Switch is pressed.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual_viqc/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **true** if the Bumper Switch is being pressed.\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **false** if the Bumper Switch is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual_viqc/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nThe **Pressing bumper** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual_viqc/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Pressing bumper** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual_viqc/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_bumper_virtual_viqc/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)"},"pressing_controller":{"name":"pressing_controller","markdown":"# Pressing controller\n\nReports if a button on the V5 Controller is pressed.\n\n![pressing_controller_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_controller/pressing_controller_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Pressing controller** reports **true** if the selected Controller button is being pressed.\n\n**Pressing controller** reports **false** if the selected Controller button is not pressed.\n\nChoose which V5 Controller to use.\n\n![pressing_controller_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_controller/pressing_controller_device.png)\n\nChoose which Controller button to use.\n\n![pressing_controller_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_controller/pressing_controller_options.png)\n\nThe **pressing controller** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![pressing_controller_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_controller/pressing_controller_inserted.png)"},"pressing_limit":{"name":"pressing_limit","markdown":"# Pressing limit\n\nReports if the Limit Switch is pressed.\n\n![pressing_limit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_limit/pressing_limit.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Pressing limit** reports **true** if the Limit Switch is being pressed.\n\n**Pressing limit** reports **false** if the Limit Switch is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Limit Switch to use.\n\n![pressing_limit_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_limit/pressing_limit_bumper.png)\n\nThe **pressing limit** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![pressing_limit_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/pressing_limit/pressing_limit_inserted.png)\n "},"print":{"name":"print","markdown":"# Print\n\nPrints values or text in the Print Console.\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/look_print.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **print** block will print data within the Monitor Window tab\n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nAll reporter blocks will be printed as integer (whole) numbers by default. Use the **set print precision** block to adjust the number of decimal places printed.\n\nBy default, the cursor will remain on the same row after printing for any subsequent print commands.\n\nTo set the cursor on the device to the next row after printing, you can select the arrow to expand **and set cursor to next row** - this will move the cursor to the next row after printing the statement.\n\n![print_expanded](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/print_expanded.png)\n\nPrint words and numbers.\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/print_words.png)\n\nPrint the reported value from a variable.\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/print_variable1.png)  \n\nPrint the reported calculation of an operator.\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/print_addition.png)  \n\nPrint the reported value from a sensor or device.\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/print_sensor.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Print**, there is one parameter that can be changed (DATA). \n\n![switch_print_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/switch_print_parameters.png) \n\nEnter the numbers or text that you wish to print in quotation marks (' ' or \" \") within the parentheses.\n\n- DATA - numbers or text to be printed on the Print Console\n\nThis example will print \"VEXcode\" on the Print Console.\n\n![switch_print](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/switch_print.png)\n\nTo print values from a variable or reporter block, enter the matching command within the parentheses. This example will print `3` on the Print Console by printing the value of `myVariable`. \n\n![switch_print_variable_number](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print/switch_print_variable_number.png)\n\nThe optional parameter of `print_precision` can be added. To learn more about the `print_precision` variable and how to include it in the Switch Python command for the **Print** block, see the Help for **Print precision**.\n"},"print_virtual":{"name":"print_virtual","markdown":"# Print\n\nPrints values or text in the Print Console.\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual/look_print.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **print** block will print data within the Monitor Window tab\n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nAll reporter blocks will be printed as integer (whole) numbers by default. Use the **set print precision** block to adjust the number of decimal places printed.\n\nPrint words and numbers.\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual/print_words.png)\n\nPrint the reported value from a variable.\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual/print_variable1.png)  \n\nPrint the reported calculation of an operator.\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual/print_addition.png)  \n\nPrint the reported value from a sensor or device.\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual/print_sensor.png)\n\n\n"},"print_virtual_viqc":{"name":"print_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Print\n\nPrints values or text in the Print Console.\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual_viqc/look_print.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **print** block will print data within the Monitor Window tab\n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual_viqc/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nAll reporter blocks will be printed as integer (whole) numbers by default. Use the **set print precision** block to adjust the number of decimal places printed.\n\nPrint words and numbers.\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual_viqc/print_words.png)\n\nPrint the reported value from a variable.\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual_viqc/print_variable1.png)  \n\nPrint the reported calculation of an operator.\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual_viqc/print_addition.png)  \n\nPrint the reported value from a sensor or device.\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/print_virtual_viqc/print_sensor.png)\n\n\n"},"procedures_call":{"name":"procedures_call","markdown":"# My blocks\n\nMy Blocks are used to create custom blocks.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/my_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**My blocks** are used to create a sequence of blocks that can be used multiple times throughout a project. \n\n**My blocks** are made using the **Make a Block** button.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/make_a_block.png)\n\n**My blocks** can have multiple parameters - text labels, numeric variables, and boolean variables - to add more functionality to your custom block.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/make_variable.png)  \n\nTo use a **my block**, attach the calling **my block** to a hat block. When the **my block** is reached in your project, the stack inside of the **my block** will run using the parameter values specified.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/trigger_function.png)\n\nAttach a stack of blocks to the **my blocks** \"define\" hat block to set the actions that occur when a **my block** is used.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/attach_function.png)\n\n***Note: Creating multiple My Blocks with the same labels and variables may cause errors when running your project in the Playgrounds.***\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the robot will use **my blocks** to:\n\n* Drive forward 300mm\n* Draw a 300 mm square \n* Turn right for 15 degrees\n* Set the pen color to red\n* Draw a 400mm square\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/square_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nTo use a **My block** in Switch mode, a function needs to be created. \n\nTo define the function, select the open space of the **Switch Hat** block indicated in the image here and begin typing.\n\n![switch_hat_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/switch_hat_cursor.png)\n\nParameters should be enclosed in parentheses and followed by a colon as shown below. If a function has no parameters, use closed parentheses: `()`. \n\n![switch_hat_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/switch_hat_parameters.png)\n\nPotential parameters include:\n\n* Boolean variables\n* Numeric variables \n* Text input\n\nAttach blocks beneath the **Switch Hat** block to set the behaviors that will occur when the function is called. Use additional **Switch Reporter** or **Switch Boolean** blocks to incorporate parameters defined by the function.\n\n![switch_hat_blocks](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/switch_hat_blocks.png)\n\nTo call the function in a project, attach a **Switch Stack** block to the stack and include any parameters needed.\n\n![switch_call_function](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_call/switch_call_function.png)\n\n"},"procedures_definition":{"name":"procedures_definition","markdown":"# My blocks\n\nMy Blocks are used to create custom blocks.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/my_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**My blocks** are used to create a sequence of blocks that can be used multiple times throughout a project. \n\n**My blocks** are made using the **Make a Block** button.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/make_a_block.png)\n\n**My blocks** can have multiple parameters - text labels, numeric variables, and boolean variables - to add more functionality to your custom block.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/make_variable.png)  \n\nTo use a **my block**, attach the calling **my block** to a hat block. When the **my block** is reached in your project, the stack inside of the **my block** will run using the parameter values specified.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/trigger_function.png)\n\nAttach a stack of blocks to the **my blocks** \"define\" hat block to set the actions that occur when a **my block** is used.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/attach_function.png)\n\n***Note: Creating multiple My Blocks with the same labels and variables may cause errors when running your project in the Playgrounds.***\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the robot will use **my blocks** to:\n\n* Drive forward 300mm\n* Draw a 300 mm square \n* Turn right for 15 degrees\n* Set the pen color to red\n* Draw a 400mm square\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/square_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nTo use a **My block** in Switch mode, a function needs to be created. \n\nTo define the function, select the open space of the **Switch Hat** block indicated in the image here and begin typing.\n\n![switch_hat_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/switch_hat_cursor.png)\n\nParameters should be enclosed in parentheses and followed by a colon as shown below. If a function has no parameters, use closed parentheses: `()`. \n\n![switch_hat_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/switch_hat_parameters.png)\n\nPotential parameters include:\n\n* Boolean variables\n* Numeric variables \n* Text input\n\nAttach blocks beneath the **Switch Hat** block to set the behaviors that will occur when the function is called. Use additional **Switch Reporter** or **Switch Boolean** blocks to incorporate parameters defined by the function.\n\n![switch_hat_blocks](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/switch_hat_blocks.png)\n\nTo call the function in a project, attach a **Switch Stack** block to the stack and include any parameters needed.\n\n![switch_call_function](static/help/vr/blocks/en/procedures_definition/switch_call_function.png)"},"random":{"name":"random","markdown":"# Random\nReports a random value between the lowest and highest values in the block.\n\n![random](static/help/vr/blocks/en/random/random.png)\n\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe  **random** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces. \n\nThe **random** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n## Examples\n\nThis example uses a **random** block to randomize the drivetrain's velocity.\n\n![random_drivetrain](static/help/vr/blocks/en/random/random_drivetrain.png)\n\nThis example uses a **random** block to randomize the length of a wait block.\n\n![random_control](static/help/vr/blocks/en/random/random_control.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo generate a random integer value with the Switch Python command for **Random**, there are two parameters that can be changed (LOWEST and HIGHEST).\n\n![switch_random](static/help/vr/blocks/en/random/switch_random.png)\n\nThe parameters are separated by a comma. All integers, including the LOWEST and HIGHEST values, could be generated when the **Random** Switch Python command is run.\n\n![switch_random2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/random/switch_random2.png)\n"},"reflectivity_of_virtual":{"name":"reflectivity_of_virtual","markdown":"# Reflectivity of\n\nReports the amount of light reflected using the Line Tracker sensor.\n\n![reflectivity_of](static/help/vr/blocks/en/reflectivity_of_virtual/reflectivity_of_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nLight colored surfaces will reflect more light and will report a high reflectivity value.\n\nDark colored surfaces will reflect less light and will report a low reflectivity value.\n\n**Reflectivity of** reports a range from **0% to 100%**.\n\nChoose which Line Tracker sensor to use.\n\n![reflectivity_of_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/reflectivity_of_virtual/reflectivity_of_color.png)\n\nThe reporter block **reflectivity of** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![reflectivity_of_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/reflectivity_of_virtual/reflectivity_of_inserted.png)\n\n\n\n"},"remainder":{"name":"remainder","markdown":"# Remainder\n\nDivides the first value by the second value and reports the remainder.\n\n![remainder](static/help/vr/blocks/en/remainder/remainder.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **remainder** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nNumbers that divide evenly will report a remainder of zero.\n\nThe length of the **remainder** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will divide 7 and 2 and return the remainder to turn the VEX VR Robot 1 degree to the right.\n\n![remainder_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/remainder/remainder_numbers.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Remainder**, enter the values to be divided on either side of the `%`. The Switch Python command for **Remainder** will divide the first value by the second value and report the remainder. \n\nReport the remainder of decimals or integers. \n\n![switch_remainder_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/remainder/switch_remainder_numbers.png)\n\nReport the remainder with values from a variable.\n\n![switch_remainder_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/remainder/switch_remainder_variable.png)  \n\nReport the remainder with values from a sensor or device.\n\n![switch_remainder_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/remainder/switch_remainder_sensor.png)"},"repeat":{"name":"repeat","markdown":"# Repeat\n\nA 'C' block that repeats the blocks contained inside for a set number of times.\n\n![repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat/repeat.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nFirst enter a value to indicate how many times the stack will **repeat** the blocks contained inside.\n \nThe **repeat** 'C' block can accept integers or numeric blocks.\n\n'C' blocks can also be put inside of each other. This concept is called nesting which can help save time when programming different behaviors.\n\n![repeat_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat/repeat_select.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Repeat**, there are two parameters that can be changed when a ***for loop*** is created (VALUE and ITERATION).\n\n![switch_repeat_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat/switch_repeat_parameters.png)\n\nSet the VALUE and the number of ITERATION that the project will repeat by providing each of the following:\n\n- VALUE - either `repeat_count` or a `LIST`\n- ITERATION - a numeric integer value\n\nNote the default VALUE for a Switch **Repeat** command (***for loop***) is `repeat_count` and the default ITERATION is 10. \n\n![switch_repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat/switch_repeat.png)\n\nTo nest other commands within the Switch Python command, be mindful of indentation. In this example, the VR Robot will drive in a square by repeating the indented Drivetrain commands 4 times. \n\n![switch_repeat_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat/switch_repeat_example.png)\n"},"repeat_until":{"name":"repeat_until","markdown":"# Repeat until\n\nA 'C' block that repeats the blocks inside until the Boolean condition is reported to be **true**.  \n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat_until/repeat_until.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **repeat until** block will only check the Boolean condition at the beginning of each loop. \n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as **false**, the blocks inside of **repeat until** will run.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as **true**, the blocks inside of **repeat until** will be skipped.\n\nThe **repeat until** 'C' block can accept hexagonal (six-sided) shaped blocks as its condition.\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat_until/repeat_until_2.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for a **Repeat until** block is a ***while not*** loop. To edit the Switch Python command, set the Boolean CONDITION that is to be evaluated in the project. \n\n![switch_repeat_until_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat_until/switch_repeat_until_parameters.png)\n\nIf no Boolean CONDITION is set, the Switch Python command will default to `while not False:`. \n\n![switch_repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat_until/switch_repeat_until.png)\n\nSet the Boolean CONDITION that is to be evaluated. In this example, the VR Robot will drive forward 200 millimeters repeatedly until the the Boolean CONDITION of `left_bumper.pressed()` reports `True`. Then the VR Robot will stop driving.\n\n![switch_repeat_until_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/repeat_until/switch_repeat_until_example.png)\n"},"reset_timer":{"name":"reset_timer","markdown":"# Reset timer\n\nResets the Brain's timer.\n\n![resetTimer](static/help/vr/blocks/en/reset_timer/resetTimer.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Brain's timer begins at the beginning of each project. The **reset timer** block is used to reset the timer back to 0 seconds.\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the timer will be reset before moving the Drivetrain forward for 3 seconds. After 3 seconds has elapsed, the Drivetrain will stop.\n\n![resetTimer_exampleRevised](static/help/vr/blocks/en/reset_timer/resetTimer_exampleRevised.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Reset timer** does not require any parameters in the parentheses.\n\n![switch_reset_timer](static/help/vr/blocks/en/reset_timer/switch_reset_timer.png)"},"robot_battery_capacity":{"name":"robot_battery_capacity","markdown":"# Battery level\n\nReports the current battery level of the VR Rover.\n\n![robot_battery_level](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_battery_capacity/robot_battery_level.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Battery level** block reports the current battery level of the VR Rover from 0 to 100 percent.\n\nThis reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![robot_battery_less_than](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_battery_capacity/robot_battery_less_than.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Battery level** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block in the Toolbox.\n\n![monitor_robot_battery_level](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_battery_capacity/monitor_robot_battery_level.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n\n\n"},"robot_carry_capacity":{"name":"robot_carry_capacity","markdown":"# Storage Capacity\n\nReports the carrying capacity of the VR Rover's storage.\n\n![carry_capacity](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_carry_capacity/carry_capacity.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![carry_capacity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_carry_capacity/carry_capacity_example.png)\n\nThe VR Rover begins by being able to carry two minerals in its storage. That capacity increases as the VR Rover levels up.\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Storage Capacity** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block in the Toolbox. \n\n![monitor_carry_capacity](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_carry_capacity/monitor_carry_capacity.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n\n\n"},"robot_exp":{"name":"robot_exp","markdown":"# XP\n\nReports current number of Experience Points (XP) that the VR Rover has.\n\n![robot_exp](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_exp/robot_exp.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![print_robot_exp](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_exp/print_robot_exp.png)\n\nThe VR Rover collects XP by using minerals, bringing minerals to the base, and absorbing enemies.\n\n| VR Rover Action                         | Earned XP        |\n|-----------------------------------------|------------------|\n| Using minerals                          | 2 XP             |\n| Taking minerals to the base             | 5 XP per minerals|\n| Neutralizing a VR Spider                | 5 XP             |\n| Neutralizing a blue/orange VR Serpent   | 10 XP            |\n| Neutralizing a purple VR Serpent        | 15 XP            |\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **XP** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block in the Toolbox. \n\n![monitor_robot_exp](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_exp/monitor_robot_exp.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n"},"robot_level":{"name":"robot_level","markdown":"# Level\n\nReports the current level of the VR Rover.\n\n![robot_level](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_level/robot_level.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![robot_level_if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_level/robot_level_if_then.png)\n\nThe VR Rover begins at Level 1 and moves up a level after collecting a certain amount of Experience Points (XP).\n\n| Level | Total XP Required    | VR Rover Color|\n|-------|----------------------|---------------|\n| 1     | 0 XP - start of game | Blue          |\n| 2     | 10 XP                | Black         |\n| 3     | 30 XP                | Orange        |\n| 4     | 70 XP                | Red           |\n| 5     | 125 XP               | Purple        |\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Level** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block in the Toolbox. \n\n![monitor_robot_level](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_level/monitor_robot_level.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n\n\n"},"robot_location":{"name":"robot_location","markdown":"# VR Rover Location\n\nReports the current X or Y coordinate position of the VR Rover.\n\n![robot_location](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_location/robot_location.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**VR Rover location** block reports the X or Y coordinate position of the VR Rover in integer numbers as either millimeters (mm) or inches (inches).\n\nChoose whether to return the X coordinate, or Y coordinate.\n\n![robot_location_cord](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_location/robot_location_cord.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in mm or inches.\n\n![robot_location_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_location/robot_location_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **VR Rover location** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![robot_location_wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_location/robot_location_wait_until.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **VR Rover location** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![robot_location_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_location/robot_location_monitor.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n"},"robot_minerals_stored":{"name":"robot_minerals_stored","markdown":"# Minerals in storage\n\nReports the current amount of minerals the VR Rover has in its storage.\n\n![robot_minerals_stored](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_minerals_stored/robot_minerals_stored.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Minerals in storage** block reports the current number of minerals in the storage of the VR Rover.\n\nThis reporter block is used inside blocks with circular spaces\n\n![minerals_stored_inside_repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_minerals_stored/minerals_stored_inside_repeat.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Minerals in storage** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block in the Toolbox. \n\n![monitor_minerals_stored](static/help/vr/blocks/en/robot_minerals_stored/monitor_minerals_stored.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n\n\n"},"rotation_angle":{"name":"rotation_angle","markdown":"# Angle of rotation \n\nReports a V5 Rotation sensor's current angle of rotation in degrees.\n\n![rotation_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_angle/rotation_angle_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Angle of rotation** block reports values in the range of **0.00** to **359.99**.\n\nThe **Angle of rotation** block will either report increasing or decreasing values as the Rotation sensor turns.\n\nChoose which Rotation sensor to get values from.\n\n![rotation_angle_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_angle/rotation_angle_block2.png)\n\nThe **angle of rotation** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![rotation_angle_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_angle/rotation_angle_block3.png)\n"},"rotation_angle_virtual":{"name":"rotation_angle_virtual","markdown":"# Angle of rotation \n\nReports a V5 Rotation sensor's current angle of rotation in degrees.\n\n![rotation_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_angle_virtual/rotation_angle_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Angle of rotation** block reports values in the range of **0.00** to **359.99**.\n\nThe **Angle of rotation** block will either report increasing or decreasing values as the Rotation sensor turns.\n\nChoose which Rotation sensor to get values from.\n\n![rotation_angle_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_angle_virtual/rotation_angle_block2.png)\n\nThe **Angle of rotation** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![rotation_angle_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_angle_virtual/rotation_angle_block3.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Angle of rotation** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![rotation_angle_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_angle_virtual/rotation_angle_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![rotation_angle_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_angle_virtual/rotation_angle_monitor.png)"},"rotation_of_gyro":{"name":"rotation_of_gyro","markdown":"# Angle of rotation\n\nReports the 3-Wire Gyro Sensor or V5 Inertial Sensor's current angle of rotation in degrees.\n\n![rotation_position.png](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_of_gyro/rotation_position.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Gyro rotation** reports a **positive** value when the Gyro Sensor turns in the **clockwise** direction.\n\n**Gyro rotation** reports a **negative** value when the Gyro Sensor turns in the **counter-clockwise** direction.\n\nChoose which Gyro Sensor / Interial Sensor to check.\n\n![rotation_position_devices](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_of_gyro/rotation_position_devices.png)\n\nThe **Gyro rotation** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![rotation_position_inserted.png](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_of_gyro/rotation_position_inserted.png)\n"},"rotation_position":{"name":"rotation_position","markdown":"# Position of rotation \n\nReports the current rotational position of the selected V5 Rotation sensor.\n\n![rotation_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position/rotation_position_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of rotation** reports the position of a V5 Rotation sensor in integer numbers for degrees, and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nChoose which V5 Rotation sensor to use.\n\n![rotation_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position/rotation_position_block2.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in degrees rotated or in turns of the V5 Rotation sensor.\n\n![rotation_position_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position/rotation_position_block3.png)\n\nThe reporter block **position of rotation** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![rotation_position_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position/rotation_position_block4.png)\n"},"rotation_position_virtual":{"name":"rotation_position_virtual","markdown":"# Position of rotation \n\nReports the current rotational position of the selected V5 Rotation sensor.\n\n![rotation_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position_virtual/rotation_position_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of rotation** reports the position of a V5 Rotation sensor in integer numbers for degrees, and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nChoose which V5 Rotation sensor to use.\n\n![rotation_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position_virtual/rotation_position_block2.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in degrees rotated or in turns of the V5 Rotation sensor.\n\n![rotation_position_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position_virtual/rotation_position_block3.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Position of rotation** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![rotation_position_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position_virtual/rotation_position_block4.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Position of rotation** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![rotation_position_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position_virtual/rotation_position_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![rotation_position_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_position_virtual/rotation_position_monitor.png)\n"},"rotation_set_position":{"name":"rotation_set_position","markdown":"# Set position \n\nSets a V5 Rotation sensor's current position to a defined value.\n\n![rotation_set_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_set_position/rotation_set_position_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set position** block can be used to set a Rotation sensor's position.\n\nThis block is typically used to reset the position of the Rotation sensor when the value is set to 0.\n\nThe **set position** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks as an input.\n\n![rotation_set_position_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_set_position/rotation_set_position_block3.png)\n\nChoose which Rotation sensor to use, then enter the desired position value.\n\n![rotation_set_position_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_set_position/rotation_set_position_block2.png)\n"},"rotation_velocity":{"name":"rotation_velocity","markdown":"# Velocity of rotation\n\nReports the current velocity of a V5 Rotation sensor.\n\n![rotation_velocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity/rotation_velocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Rotation sensor to get values from.\n\n![rotation_velocity_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity/rotation_velocity_block2.png)\n\nChoose whether the velocity is reported in percent (%), revolutions per minute (rpm) or degrees per second (dps).\n\n![rotation_velocity_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity/rotation_velocity_block3.png)\n\nThe reporter block velocity of rotation is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![rotation_velocity_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity/rotation_velocity_block4.png)\n"},"rotation_velocity_virtual":{"name":"rotation_velocity_virtual","markdown":"# Velocity of rotation\n\nReports the current velocity of a V5 Rotation sensor.\n\n![rotation_velocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity_virtual/rotation_velocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Rotation sensor to get values from.\n\n![rotation_velocity_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity_virtual/rotation_velocity_block2.png)\n\nChoose whether the velocity is reported in percent (%), revolutions per minute (rpm) or degrees per second (dps).\n\n![rotation_velocity_block3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity_virtual/rotation_velocity_block3.png)\n\nThe reporter block velocity of rotation is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![rotation_velocity_block4](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity_virtual/rotation_velocity_block4.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Velocity of rotation** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![rotation_velocity_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity_virtual/rotation_velocity_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![rotation_velocity_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/rotation_velocity_virtual/rotation_velocity_monitor.png)"},"round":{"name":"round","markdown":"# Round\n\nRounds the inputed value to the nearest integer.\n\n![round](static/help/vr/blocks/en/round/round.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nDecimals that are 0.5 or greater **round** up, and decimals less than 0.5 **round** down.\n\nThe **round** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces, and can accept circular blocks in them as well. \n\nThe **round** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![round_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/round/round_numbers.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will round 2.7 and drive the VEX VR Robot forward \"3\" inches.\n\n![round_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/en/round/round_variables.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Round**, enter the value to be rounded after `round`. \n\nRound a decimal or integer. \n\n![switch_round_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/round/switch_round_numbers.png)\n\nRound values from variables.\n\n![switch_round_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/round/switch_round_variable.png)\n\nRound values from sensors or devices.\n\n![switch_round_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/round/switch_round_sensor.png)\n"},"screen_position":{"name":"screen_position","markdown":"# Screen position\n\nReports the location of the last detected touch on the V5 Brain's touchscreen.\n\n![screen_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/screen_position/screen_position_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **screen position** reporter block is used to capture the X/Y location of the last press on the V5 Brain's touchscreen. This report block can be used to create interactivity between the V5 and the end user.\n\n**Screen position** reports a range of **0 to 480 pixels** when set to report the **X** coordinate.\n\n**Screen position** reports a range of **0 to 240 pixels** when set to report the **Y** coordinate.\n\n![v5_row_column_brain](static/help/vr/blocks/en/screen_position/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)\n\nChoose which axis to report. \n\n![screen_position_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/screen_position/screen_position_value.png)\n\nThe **screen position** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![screen_position_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/screen_position/screen_position_inserted.png)"},"screen_pressed":{"name":"screen_pressed","markdown":"# Screen pressed\n\nReports if the V5 Brain's touchscreen is currently being pressed.\n\n![screen_pressed_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/screen_pressed/screen_pressed_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **screen pressed** Boolean block can be used together with the **screen position** to detect when and where a touch has occured on the V5 Brain's touchscreen.\n\n**Screen pressed** reports **true** when the V5 Brain's touchscreen is being pressed.\n\n**Screen pressed** reports **false** when the V5 Brain's touchscreen is not being pressed.\n\nThe **screen pressed** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![screen_pressed_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/screen_pressed/screen_pressed_inserted.png)\n\n"},"set_2d_array_to":{"name":"set_2d_array_to","markdown":"# Set 2D list to\n\nSets every item in the 2D list to the entered values.\n\n![set_2d_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nWhen creating a 2D list, you can set the number of **columns** of the list between 1 to 10 items and the number of **rows** of the list between 1 to 10 items.\n\nChoose which 2D list to use. The 2D list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_options.png)\n\nThe **set 2D list to** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo set the value of a 2D list in Switch Python, type the name of the list into a **Switch Stack** block.  \n\nThe following rules should be applied when naming a list:\n\n* The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n* The name cannot begin with a number\n* The name cannot use spaces\n* The name cannot use a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n\n![switch_list_name](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_2d_array_to/switch_list_name.png) \n\nIn Switch Python, a 2D list is best described as a list made up of other lists. This is reflected in the structure.  There is a set of outer square brackets that define the 2D list.\n\n![switch_2d_bracket](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_2d_array_to/switch_2d_bracket.png)\n\nEach row is a list that is made up of another set of square brackets. Notice that each row is enclosed in square brackets and that a comma comes after one list and before another. This separates the elements of each row.\n\n![switch_empty_list](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_2d_array_to/switch_empty_list.png)\n\nThe data elements in a 2D list can be accessed using an index for the row and column. An index is the location of a value in the list variable. Each element inside of an array is assigned an integer, beginning with **zero.**"},"set_array_to":{"name":"set_array_to","markdown":"# Set list to\n\nSets every item in the list to the entered values.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/set_list_to.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nWhen creating a list, you can set the length of the list between 1 to 10 items.\n\nChoose which list to use. The list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/set_list_to_options.png)\n\nThe **set list to** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/set_list_to_2.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the list to initial values, and then report item #3 (90) from the list, to set the drive rotation. \n\n![set_list_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/set_list_to_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nTo set the value of a list in Switch Python, type the name of the list into a **Switch Stack** block.  \n\nThe following rules should be applied when naming a list:\n\n* The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n* The name cannot begin with a number\n* The name cannot use spaces\n* The name cannot use a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n\n![switch_list](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/switch_list.png) \n\nTo assign a list of values to a List variable, use the = operator followed by comma-separated values, called elements, enclosed in square brackets. If the values are numbers, then the commas should come right after each number.\n\n![switch_list_angles](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/switch_list_angles.png) \n\nIf the values are strings, the commas should come outside of the quotation marks.\n\n![switch_list_string](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/switch_list_string.png) \n\nThe data elements in a list variable can be accessed using an index. An index is the location of a value in the list variable. Each element inside of an array is assigned an integer, beginning with **zero.**\n\n![switch_list2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/switch_list2.png) \n\nFor example, this project would turn the robot for `180` degrees because the 3rd element in the list is being called (zero being the 1st, one being the 2nd, two being the 3rd) \n\n![switch_list_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_array_to/switch_list_example.png)\n\n\n\n\n"},"set_boolean_variable":{"name":"set_boolean_variable","markdown":"# Set boolean variable\n\nSets a boolean variable to a given value.\n\n![set_boolean_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which boolean variable to use. Boolean variables can also be renamed or deleted. \n\n![set_boolean_block_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_choice.png)\n\nChoose a Boolean value.\n\n![set_boolean_block_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_value.png)\n\nThe **set boolean variable** block can accept hexagonal (six-sided) shaped blocks.\n\n![set_boolean_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_inserted.png)\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nTo set the value of a Boolean variable with Switch Python, type the name of the variable into the **Switch Reporter** block and what the variable should be equal to.\n\n![switch_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_boolean_variable/switch_boolean_variable.png) \n\nBoolean variables should be set to `True` or `False` values.\n\n![switch_boolean_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_boolean_variable/switch_boolean_bumper.png) \n\nThe following rules should be applied when naming a variable:\n\n* The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n* The name cannot begin with a number\n* The name cannot use spaces\n* The name cannot use a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n"},"set_bumper":{"name":"set_bumper","markdown":"# Set LED Bumper color\n\nSets the color of the VEX VR LED Bumper.\n\n![set_bumper_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_bumper/set_bumper_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the color that will be displayed.  \n\n![set_bumper_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_bumper/set_bumper_color2.png)\n\nTo turn the **LED Bumper** off, set the color to off.\n\nThis example will set the **LED Bumper** to green.\n\n![set_bumper_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_bumper/set_bumper_example.png)"},"set_cursor":{"name":"set_cursor","markdown":"# Set cursor\n\nSets the cursor location for **print** blocks on the V5 Brain or Controller screen.\n\n![set_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_cursor/set_cursor.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nSet the cursor's row and column position to have a **print** command display at a specific location on the screen.\n\n**Set cursor** accepts a range for **row** of **1 to 20**.\n\n**Set cursor** accepts a range for **column** of **1 to 80**.\n\nChoose which device to use. V5 Controllers will only appear if they are added in the Devices window.\n\n![set_cursor_dropdown](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_cursor/set_cursor_dropdown.png)\n\nThe **set cursor** block can accept integers or numeric blocks.\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Brain allows you to change the size of the font printed on the screen. Changing the font will affect the number of columns available on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* Extra Small - 20 Rows\n* Small - 16 Rows\n* Medium - 12 Rows (Default)\n* Large - 8 Rows\n* Extra Large - 6 Rows\n* Super Large - 4 Rows\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* Extra Small - 80 Columns\n* Small - 68 Columns\n* Medium - 48 Columns (Default)\n* Large - 32 Columns\n* Extra Large - 24 Columns\n* Super Large - 16 Columns\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_cursor/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Controller does not allow you to change the size of the font printed on the screen.\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* Standard Font - 3 Rows (Default)\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* Standard Font - 20 Columns (Default)\n\n![brain_screen_info_2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_cursor/v5_controller_rows_columns.jpg)"},"set_digital_out":{"name":"set_digital_out","markdown":"# Digital out\n\nSets the logic level of a digital out 3-Wire port.\n\n![digital_out_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_digital_out/digital_out_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe V5 3-Wire ports function at a 5V logic signal voltage level.\n\nThe **digital out** block sets the digital signal to output a high or low logic signal.\n\nChoose which digital out port to use. \n\n![digital_out_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_digital_out/digital_out_select.png)\n\nChoose to output a high or low digital logic signal.\n\n![digital_out_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_digital_out/digital_out_choose.png)\n\n"},"set_drive_heading":{"name":"set_drive_heading","markdown":"# Set drive heading\n\nSets the Drivetrain's Gyro heading value.\n\n![setDriveHeading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_heading/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set drive heading** block can be used to set the Drivetrain's position to any given heading. This block can be used to reset the orientation of the Drivetrain's gyro when the heading is set to a value of 0.\n\n**Set drive heading** accepts a range of **0 to 360** degrees.\n\nThe **set drive heading** block can accept integers or numeric blocks.\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Set drive heading**, there are two parameters that can be changed (VALUE and UNITS).\n\n![switch_set_drive_heading_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_heading/switch_set_drive_heading_parameters.png)\n\nSet the heading that the Drivetrain's position will be set to by providing each of the following:\n\n- VALUE - a numeric integer value from 0 - 360\n- UNITS - `DEGREES`\n\n![switch_set_drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_heading/switch_set_drive_heading.png)\n\n\n"},"set_drive_heading_go":{"name":"set_drive_heading_go","markdown":"# Set drive heading\n\nSets the Drivetrain's Gyro heading value. The Gyro Sensor is built into the Brain.\n\n![setDriveHeading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_heading_go/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Set drive heading** block accepts a range of 0 to 359.99 degrees.\n\nThe **Set drive heading** block can accept integers or reporter blocks.\n\n![setDriveHeading_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_heading_go/setDriveHeading_variable.png)\n\nThe **Set drive heading** block can be used to set the Drivetrain's position to any given clockwise heading as shown in the image below.\n\n![TopDownBot01](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_heading_go/TopDownBot01.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will rotate the Drivetrain a total of 135 degrees.\n\n- Turn right to heading 45 degrees.\n- Set the Drivetrain's current position as heading of 0 degrees.\n- Turn right an additional 90 degrees based on the set heading value from the first **Turn to heading** block.\n\n![setDriveHeading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_heading_go/setDriveHeading_example.png)\n\n"},"set_drive_rotation":{"name":"set_drive_rotation","markdown":"# Set drive rotation\n\nSets the Drivetrain's angle of rotation.\n\n![drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_rotation/drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set drive rotation** block can be used to set the Drivetrain's angle of rotation to any given positive or negative value.\n\nThe **set drive rotation** block can accept integers or numeric blocks.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will turn the robot a total of 210 degrees:\n\n![turntorotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_rotation/turntorotation_example.png)\n\n- Turn right (clockwise) to rotation 120 degrees.\n- Set the robot's current position as rotation of -45 degrees.\n- Turn right (clockwise) an additional 90 degrees (-45 degrees to +45 degrees) based on the set rotation value from the previous block.\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Set drive rotation**, there are two parameters that can be changed (VALUE and UNITS).\n\n![switch_set_drive_rotation_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_rotation/switch_set_drive_rotation_parameters.png)\n\nSet the heading that the Drivetrain's position will be set to by providing each of the following:\n\n- VALUE - a numeric integer value\n- UNITS - `DEGREES`\n\n![switch_set_drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_rotation/switch_set_drive_rotation.png)"},"set_drive_rotation_go":{"name":"set_drive_rotation_go","markdown":"# Set drive rotation\n\nSets the Drivetrain's angle of rotation.\n\n![drivetrain_setdriverotation](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_rotation_go/drivetrain_setdriverotation.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Set drive rotation** block can be used to set the Drivetrain's angle of rotation to any given positive or negative value.\n\nThe **Set drive rotation** block can accept integers or reporter blocks.\n\n![setdriverotation_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_rotation_go/setdriverotation_insert.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will turn the Drivetrain a total of 210 degrees.\n\n- Turn right (clockwise) to rotation 120 degrees.\n- Set the Drivetrain's current position as rotation of -45 degrees.\n- Turn right (clockwise) an additional 90 degrees (-45 degrees to +45 degrees) based on the set rotation value from the first Turn to rotation block.\n\n![setdriverotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_rotation_go/setdriverotation_example.png)\n"},"set_drive_stopping":{"name":"set_drive_stopping","markdown":"# Set drive stopping\n\nSets the behavior of the Drivetrain once it stops moving.\n\n![setDriveStopping_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_stopping/setDriveStopping_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n* **Brake** will cause the Drivetrain to come to an immediate stop, and will not correct for outside forces.\n* **Coast** lets the Drivetrain spin gradually to a stop.\n* **Hold** will cause the Drivetrain to come to an immediate stop, and returns it to its stopped position if moved by an outside force, by using the built-in encoder.\n\n![setDriveStopping_type](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_stopping/setDriveStopping_type.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will make the Drivetrain move forward for 3 seconds and then hold its position once stopped.\n\n![setDriveStopping_example_revised](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_stopping/setDriveStopping_example_revised.png)"},"set_drive_timeout":{"name":"set_drive_timeout","markdown":"# Set drive timeout\n\nSets a time limit for Drivetrain movement commands.\n\n![setDriveTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_timeout/setDriveTimeout_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Drivetrain's time limit is used to prevent drive blocks that do not reach their position from preventing other blocks in the stack from running.\n\nAn example of a Drivetrain not reaching its position is when a robot is driven against a wall and cannot completed its movement.\n\nThe **set drive timeout** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will end the **drive** command after 2 second if the Drivetrain has not reached the target of 24 inches. Once the time limit or distance is reached, the Drivetrain will move on to the **turn** command.\n\n![setDriveTimeout_example_revised2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_timeout/setDriveTimeout_example_revised2.png)\n\n"},"set_drive_timeout_go":{"name":"set_drive_timeout_go","markdown":"# Set drive timeout\n\nSets a time limit for Drivetrain movement blocks.\n\n![setDriveTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_timeout_go/setDriveTimeout_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Drivetrain's time limit is used to prevent blocks from the Drivetrain category that do not reach their position from preventing other blocks in the stack from running.\n\nAn example of a Drivetrain not reaching its position is when a robot has driven into a wall or other object and cannot complete its movement.\n\nThe **Set drive timeout** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![setDriveTimeout_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_timeout_go/setDriveTimeout_variable.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will end the **Drive for** block after 3 seconds if the Drivetrain has not reached the target of 300 mm. \n\nOnce the time limit or distance is reached, the project flow will move on to the **Turn for** block.\n\n![setDriveTimeout_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_timeout_go/setDriveTimeout_example.png)\n\n"},"set_drive_velocity":{"name":"set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Set drive velocity\n\nSets the speed of the Drivetrain.\n\n![set_drive_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Set drive velocity** accepts a range from **1% to 100%**.\n\nYou can use this block to get more accurate movement from the robot. The default velocity is **50%**\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the drive to go spin at 10% speed before driving.\n\n![set_drive_velocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Set drive velocity**, there are two parameters that can be changed (VELOCITY and UNITS).\n\n![switch_set_drive_velocity_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_velocity/switch_set_drive_velocity_parameters.png)\n\nSet the velocity that the Drivetrain will move by providing each of the following:\n\n- VELOCITY - a numeric integer value from 0 - 100\n- UNITS - `PERCENT`\n\nNote the default Drivetrain velocity is 50%.\n\n![switch_set_drive_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_velocity/switch_set_drive_velocity.png)\n\n"},"set_drive_velocity_go":{"name":"set_drive_velocity_go","markdown":"# Set drive velocity\n\nSets the speed of the Drivetrain.\n\n![setDriveVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_velocity_go/setDriveVelocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Set drive velocity** block will only set the speed of the Drivetrain, but will not cause the Drivetrain to move. A Drive or Drive for block is still required.\n\nThe **Set drive velocity** block accepts a range of 1% to 100%.\n\nSetting a Drivetrain's velocity to less than 1% will set the velocity to 1%.\n\nSetting a Drivetrain's velocity to above 100% will set the velocity to 100%.\n\nThe **Set drive velocity** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![setDriveVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_velocity_go/setDriveVelocity_unit.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will make the Drivetrain drive at 30% speed for 200 mm.\n\n![setDriveVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_drive_velocity_go/setDriveVelocity_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_fill_color":{"name":"set_fill_color","markdown":"# Set fill color\n\nSets the color that fills in shapes drawn on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n![set_fill_color_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_fill_color/set_fill_color_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set fill color** block is used to set the color of the inside of circles and rectangles drawn on the V5 Brain's screen. \n\nChoose a fill color. \n\n![set_fill_color_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_fill_color/set_fill_color_color.png)  \n\nThe fill color will be used for any future objects drawn on the V5 Brain's screen."},"set_font":{"name":"set_font","markdown":"# Set font\n\nSets the style and size of font used on the V5 Brain's screen when printing numbers or text.\n\n![set_font_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_font/set_font_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThere are two different types of fonts available on the V5 Brain's screen:\n\n* Monospaced (Mono) - each character takes up the same width.\n* Proportional (Prop) - each character takes up different size widths based on the character.\n\nChoose which font type and size to use.\n\n![set_font_block_font](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_font/set_font_block_font.png)  \n\nThe new font size will be used for any future text written to the V5 Brain's screen."},"set_gyro_heading":{"name":"set_gyro_heading","markdown":"# Set heading\n\nSets the Gyroscopic (Gyro) / Inertial sensor's current heading position to a set value.\n\n![set_gyro_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_gyro_heading/set_gyro_heading_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set gyro / inertial heading** block can be used to set the Gyro's position to any clockwise heading.\n\nThis block is typically used reset the heading orientation of the Gyro / Inertial sensor when value is set to 0.\n\n**Set gyro heading** accepts a range of **0.0 to 359.99** degrees.\n\nThe **set heading** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nChoose which Gyro / Inertial Sensor to use, then enter the desired value.\n\n![set_gyro_heading_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_gyro_heading/set_gyro_heading_gyro.png)\n\n"},"set_gyro_rotation":{"name":"set_gyro_rotation","markdown":"# Set rotation\n\nSets the 3-Wire Gyro Sensor or V5 Inertial Sensor's angle of rotation to the value set within the command block.\n\n![setrotation](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_gyro_rotation/setrotation.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set gyro rotation** block can be used to set the Drivetrain's angle of rotation to any given positive (clockwise) or negative (counter-clockwise) value.\n\n**Set gyro rotation** accepts a range of any positive or negative decimal or integer number.\n\nThe **set gyro rotation** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nChoose which Gyro Sensor to use.\n\n![setrotation_devices](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_gyro_rotation/setrotation_devices.png)\n"},"set_item_of_2d_array":{"name":"set_item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Replace item of 2D list\n\nUpdates an item in the 2D list to a new value.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nEnter the item's row (first number) and column (second number) position within the list to be replaced.\n\nChoose which list to use. The list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_options.png)\n\nThe **replace item** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block_2.png)\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block.\n\nTo update an element in a 2D list to a new value, use the index of the element in a **Switch Stack** block. The index should be inside square brackets after the name of the list with the first being the row and the second being the column.\n\n![switch_index](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_2d_array/switch_index.png)\n\nRemember that an index is the location of a value in the list variable. Each element inside of a list is assigned an integer, beginning with **zero.**\n\nSet the new value of the element after the equal sign. These can be decimals, integers, numeric values, or strings. In this example, the fourth element in the first row will be set to the current distance.\n\n![switch_update_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_2d_array/switch_update_value.png)\n"},"set_item_of_array":{"name":"set_item_of_array","markdown":"# Replace item\n\nUpdates an item in the list to a new value.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which list to use. The list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_array/replace_item_options.png)\n\nThe **replace item** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block_2.png)\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block.\n\nTo update an element in a list to a new value, use the index of the element in a **Switch Stack** block. The index should be inside square brackets after the name of the list.\n\n![switch_index](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_array/switch_index.png)\n\nRemember that an index is the location of a value in the list variable. Each element inside of a list is assigned an integer, beginning with **zero.**\n\nSet the new value of the element after the equal sign. These can be decimals, integers, numeric values, or strings. \n\n![switch_update_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_array/switch_update_value.png)\n\nIn this example, the **Replace item** command is used to set the 3rd element (because the integers begin with zero, this makes 2 the third integer) in `myList` to the value reported by the Front Distance Sensor.\n\n![switch_replace](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_item_of_array/switch_replace.png)"},"set_joint_move_speed":{"name":"set_joint_move_speed","markdown":"# Set joint speed\n\nSets the speed of the V5 Robotic Arm's joint movements.\n\n![joint_speed_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_joint_move_speed/joint_speed_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![select_joint_speed_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_joint_move_speed/select_joint_speed_arm.png)\n\nChoose the speed of the V5 Robotic Arm's joint movements. The default speed is **normal**.\n\n![joint_speed_select.png](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_joint_move_speed/joint_speed_select.png)"},"set_led":{"name":"set_led","markdown":"# Set LED\n\nTurns an LED on or off.\n\n![set_led_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_led/set_led_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nSelect which LED to use.\n\n![set_led_block_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_led/set_led_block_device.png)\n\nSelect if the LED is on or off.\n\n![set_led_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_led/set_led_block_setting.png)\n\n"},"set_linear_move_speed":{"name":"set_linear_move_speed","markdown":"# Set linear speed\n\nSets the speed of the V5 Robotic Arm's linear movements.\n\n![linear_speed_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_linear_move_speed/linear_speed_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![select_linear_speed_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_linear_move_speed/select_linear_speed_arm.png)\n\nChoose the speed of the V5 Robotic Arm's linear movements. The default speed is **normal**.\n\n![linear_speed_select.png](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_linear_move_speed/linear_speed_select.png)"},"set_magnet_state":{"name":"set_magnet_state","markdown":"# Energize Electromagnet\n\nSets the VEX VR Robot Electromagnet to two different modes: **boost** or **drop**.\n\n![set_magnet_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nSelect to set that electromagnet to boost or drop\n\n* **boost** - attracts an object to the electromagnet\n* **drop** - releases any object the electromagnet is holding\n\n\n![set_magnet_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\nThis example will set the robot to drive forward 200mm, **Electromagnet** will boost to pick up an object, drive forward 200mm, then drop the object.\n\n![set_magnet_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Energize electromagnet**, there is one parameter that can be changed (MODE).\n\n![switch_energize_electromagnet_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_magnet_state/switch_energize_electromagnet_parameters.png) \n\nSet the electromagnet MODE in the parentheses:\n\n- MODE - `BOOST` or `DROP`\n\n![switch_energize_electromagnet](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_magnet_state/switch_energize_electromagnet.png)"},"set_mastering":{"name":"set_mastering","markdown":"# Set mastering\n\nSets the mastering values of the four joints of the V5 Robotic Arm to allow it to be calibrated to the same known location.\n\n![set_mastering_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_mastering/set_mastering_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nInput the mastering values from the V5 Brain to the **set mastering** block. \n\nThe passable threshold values for the joints are as follows:\n\n* Joint 1: 1600 - 2000\n* Joint 2: 1900 - 2400\n* Joint 3: 1700 - 2100\n* Joint 4: 200 - 650\n\n![mastering_values](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_mastering/mastering_values.png)\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![select_mastering_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_mastering/select_mastering_arm.png)\n\nThe **set mastering** block accepts integers and numeric blocks.\n\n![mastering_input](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_mastering/mastering_input.png)"},"set_motor_controller_velocity":{"name":"set_motor_controller_velocity","markdown":"# Set motor controller velocity\n\nSets the speed of a Motor 393 connected to a Motor Controller 29.\n\n![set_motor_controller_velocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_controller_velocity/set_motor_controller_velocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Set motor controller velocity** accepts a range of **-100% to 100%**.\n\nSetting a Motor 393's velocity to a negative value will cause the Motor 393 to spin in reverse.\n\nSetting a Motor 393's velocity to 0 will cause the Motor 393 to stop.\n\nChoose which Motor 393 to use.\n\n![set_motor_controller_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_controller_velocity/set_motor_controller_motor.png)"},"set_motor_position":{"name":"set_motor_position","markdown":"# Set motor position\n\nSets the V5 Motor or Motor Group's encoder(s) position to the given value.\n\n![setMotorPosition_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position/setMotorPosition_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set motor position** block can be used to set a Motor or Motor Group's position to any given value.\n\nUsually, the **set motor position** block is used to set the position to 0 to reset the Motor or Motor Group's encoder(s) position.\n\nChoose which V5 Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![setMotorPosition_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position/setMotorPosition_motor.png)\n\nChoose the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![setMotorPosition_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position/setMotorPosition_unit.png)\n"},"set_motor_position_go":{"name":"set_motor_position_go","markdown":"# Set motor position\n\nSets a Motor's encoder position to the entered value.\n\n![setMotorPosition_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position_go/setMotorPosition_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Set motor position** block can be used to set a motor's position to any given value.\n\nUsually, the **Set motor position** block is used to set the position to 0 to reset the motor's encoder position.\n\nChoose which motor to use.\n\n![setMotorPosition_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position_go/setMotorPosition_motor.png)\n\nChoose the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![setMotorPosition_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position_go/setMotorPosition_unit.png)"},"set_motor_position_viqc":{"name":"set_motor_position_viqc","markdown":"# Set motor position\n\nSets a VEX IQ Motor's encoder position(s) to the entered value.\n\n![setMotorPosition_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position_viqc/setMotorPosition_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **motor position** block can be used to set a Motor's position to any given value.\n\nUsually, the **motor position** block is used to set the position to 0 to reset a Motor's encoder position.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![setMotorPosition_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position_viqc/setMotorPosition_motor.png)\n\nChoose the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![setMotorPosition_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_position_viqc/setMotorPosition_unit.png)\n"},"set_motor_stopping":{"name":"set_motor_stopping","markdown":"# Set motor stopping\n\nSets the behavior of the V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group once it stops spinning.\n\n![setMotorStopping_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_stopping/setMotorStopping_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n* **Brake** will cause the Motor or Motor Group to come to an immediate stop.\n* **Coast** lets the Motor or Motor Group spin gradually to a stop.\n* **Hold** will cause the Motor or Motor Group to come to an immediate stop, and returns it to its stopped position if moved.\n\nThe behavior set by the **set motor stopping** block will be used for the remainder of the program until otherwise changed.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![setMotorStopping_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_stopping/setMotorStopping_motor.png)\n\nChoose the Motor or Motor Group's setting after it stops.\n\n![setMotorStopping_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_stopping/setMotorStopping_setting.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will make the ArmMotor move forward for 90 degrees and then will resist outside forces (like gravity) to hold its position.\n\n![setMotorStopping_example_revised](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_stopping/setMotorStopping_example_revised.png)\n\n\n"},"set_motor_timeout":{"name":"set_motor_timeout","markdown":"# Set motor timeout\n\nSets a time limit for the V5 Motor or Motor Group's movement commands.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_timeout/setMotorTimeout_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Motor or Motor Group's time limit is used to prevent motion blocks that do not reach their position from preventing other blocks in the stack from running. An example of a Motor or Motor Group not reaching its position is an arm or claw that reaches its mechanical limit and cannot complete its movement.\n\nThe **set motor timeout** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will end the **set motor** command after 2 second if the ClawMotor has not reached the position of 270 degrees. Once the time limit or target is reached, the ClawMotor will move to position 0 degrees.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_timeout/setMotorTimeout_example.png)\n"},"set_motor_timeout_go":{"name":"set_motor_timeout_go","markdown":"# Set motor timeout\n\nSets a time limit for Motor movement blocks.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_timeout_go/setMotorTimeout_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Motor's time limit is used to prevent blocks from the Motion category that do not reach their position from preventing other blocks in the stack from running.\n\nAn example of a Motor not reaching its position is a robot that reaches its mechanical limit and cannot complete its movement.\n\nThe **Set motor timeout** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_timeout_go/setMotorTimeout_variable.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will end the Spin for block after 2 second if the robot has not reached the position of 180 degrees. \n\nOnce the time limit or target is reached, the project flow will move on to the Spin to position block.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_timeout_go/setMotorTimeout_example.png)"},"set_motor_timeout_viqc":{"name":"set_motor_timeout_viqc","markdown":"# Set motor timeout\n\nSets a time limit for VEX IQ Motor movement commands.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_timeout_viqc/setMotorTimeout_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nA Motor's timeout is used to prevent movement blocks that do not reach their position from preventing other blocks in the stack from running. An example of a Motor not reaching its position is an arm or claw that reaches its mechanical limit and cannot complete its movement.\n\nThe **set motor timeout** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will end the **set motor** command after 2 second if the ClawMotor has not reached the position of 270 degrees. Once the time limit or target is reached, the ClawMotor will move to position 0 degrees.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_timeout_viqc/setMotorTimeout_example.png)\n"},"set_motor_torque":{"name":"set_motor_torque","markdown":"# Set motor torque\n\nSets the strength of the V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group.\n\n![setMotorTorque_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_torque/setMotorTorque_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Set motor torque** accepts a range of **0% to 100%**.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![setMotorTorque_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_torque/setMotorTorque_motor.png)\n\nThe **set motor torque** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will spin the Claw Motor to the 40 degree position with only 20% maximum torque. This allows the claw to grab an object without damaging it by using too much force.\n\n![setMotorTorque_example_revised](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_torque/setMotorTorque_example_revised.png)"},"set_motor_velocity":{"name":"set_motor_velocity","markdown":"# Set motor velocity\n\nSets the speed of a V5 Motor or Motor Group.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity/setMotorVelocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Set motor velocity** accepts a range from **-100% to 100%**.\n\nSetting a V5 Motor's or Motor Group's velocity to a negative value will cause the Motor/Motor Group to spin in reverse.\n\nSetting a V5 Motor's or Motor Groups' velocity to 0 will cause the Motor/Motor Group to stop.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity/setMotorVelocity_motor.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Arm Motor to slowly spin at 25% speed to the 90 degree position.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity/setMotorVelocity_example.png)\n"},"set_motor_velocity_go":{"name":"set_motor_velocity_go","markdown":"# Set motor velocity\n\nSets the speed of a Motor.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity_go/setMotorVelocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Set motor velocity** block will only set the speed of the Motor. It will not cause the Motor to move. A Spin or Spin for block is still required.\n\nThe **Set motor velocity** block accepts a range of 1% to 100%.\n\nSetting a Motor's velocity to less than 1% will set the velocity to 1%.\n\nSetting a Motor's velocity to to above 100% will set the velocity to 100%.\n\nThe **Set motor velocity** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity_go/setMotorVelocity_insert.png)\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity_go/setMotorVelocity_motor.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the robot to slowly spin at 25% speed to the 90 degree position.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity_go/setMotorVelocity_example.png)"},"set_motor_velocity_viqc":{"name":"set_motor_velocity_viqc","markdown":"# Set motor velocity\n\nSets the speed of a VEX IQ Motor.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity_viqc/setMotorVelocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Set motor velocity** accepts a range of **-100% to 100%**.\n\nSetting a Motor's velocity to a negative value will cause the Motor to spin in reverse.\n\nSetting a Motor's velocity to 0 will cause the Motor to stop.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity_viqc/setMotorVelocity_motor.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Arm Motor is set to slowly spin at 25% speed to the 90 degree position.\n\n![setMotorVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_motor_velocity_viqc/setMotorVelocity_example.png)\n"},"set_origin":{"name":"set_origin","markdown":"# Set origin position\n\nSets the origin to the current location of the end of arm tooling.\n\n![set_origin_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_origin/set_origin_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nBy default, the origin on the Workcell is located in the center of the turntable.\n![turntable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_origin/turntable.png)\n\nThe **set origin position** block sets the origin to the current location of the end of arm tooling.\n\nThe end of arm tooling can be set to:\n\n* None\n* Marker (shown in image)\n* Electromagnet \n\n![marker](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_origin/marker.png)\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![set_origin_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_origin/set_origin_insert.png)"},"set_pen_color":{"name":"set_pen_color","markdown":"# Set Pen color\n\nSets the color of the VEX VR Pen.\n\n![set_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color/set_pen_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the color of the line that will be drawn.  \n\n![set_pen_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color/set_pen_color2.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Pen color to blue.\n\n![set_pen_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color/set_pen_color_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Set pen color**, there is one parameter that can be changed (COLOR).\n\n![switch_set_pen_color_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color/switch_set_pen_color_parameters.png)\n\nSet the COLOR you want the VR Pen to draw by providing the following:\n\n- COLOR - `BLACK`, `RED`, `GREEN` or `BLUE`\n\n![switch_set_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color/switch_set_pen_color.png)"},"set_pen_color_plus":{"name":"set_pen_color_plus","markdown":"# Set Pen color\n\nSets the color of the VEX VR Pen with a specified color.\n\n![set_pen_color_enhanced](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color_plus/set_pen_color_enhanced.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUsing the sliders, select the red, green, and blue values for the color you want to create. The numeric value will be listed next to the color (0 to 255).\n\n![opacity_slider](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color_plus/opacity_slider.png)\n\nOpacity describes the transparency of the color. The opacity of the pen color can be selected using the bottom slider.\n\n![color_sliders](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color_plus/color_sliders.png)\n\nThe color window will update to show the final color the pen will draw on the Playground. In this example, the pen would draw a teal color.\n\n![color_selection_teal](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color_plus/color_selection_teal.png)\n\nThe color will also appear at the top of the pen once the project is run.\n\n![vr_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color_plus/vr_pen_color.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Set pen color** using the VR Pen, there are four parameters that can be changed (RED, GREEN, BLUE, and OPACITY).\n\n![switch_set_pen_color_plus_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color_plus/switch_set_pen_color_plus_parameters.png)\n\nSpecify the color of the pen by providing each of the following:\n\n- RED - specify the intensity of the color as an integer between 0 - 255\n- GREEN - specify the intensity of the color as an integer between 0 - 255\n- BLUE - specify the intensity of the color as an integer between 0 - 255\n- OPACITY - specify the transparency of the color as a value from 0 - 100% \n\nNote the default OPACITY is set to 100%.\n\nIn this example, the pen color is set to black, as all color parameters are set to 0.\n\n![switch_set_pen_color_plus](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color_plus/switch_set_pen_color_plus.png)\n\nIn this example, the pen color is set to blue, as the BLUE parameter is set to its highest value (255) and the others are set to 0.\n\n![switch_set_pen_color_plus_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_color_plus/switch_set_pen_color_plus_example.png)\n"},"set_pen_width":{"name":"set_pen_width","markdown":"# Set Pen width\n\nSets the width of the line the VR Pen will draw.\n\n![set_pen_width](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_width/set_pen_width.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nSelect the dropdown on the block to choose between the five Pen widths.\n\n![select_width](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_width/select_width.png)\n\nThis Playground image was created using all five pen widths. It begins with the 'extra thin' at the left and moves in order through the 'extra wide' at the right.\n\n![pen_width_examples](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_width/pen_width_examples.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Set Pen width**, there is one parameter that can be changed (WIDTH).\n\n![switch_set_pen_width_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_width/switch_set_pen_width_parameters.png)\n\nSet the WIDTH you want the VR Pen to draw by providing the following: \n\n* WIDTH - `EXTRA_THIN`, `THIN`, `MEDIUM`, `WIDE`, or `EXTRA_WIDE`\n\nNote that the default width for the VR Pen is `THIN`.\n\n![switch_set_pen_width](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_pen_width/switch_set_pen_width.png)"},"set_print_color":{"name":"set_print_color","markdown":"# Set print color\n\nSets the color of text printed to the Print Console.\n\n![set_print_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_color/set_print_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set print color** block is used with the **print** block to set the color of text printed in the Print Console. The color can be set to black, red, green or blue. \n\n![set_print_color_options](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_color/set_print_color_options.png)  \n\nLetters, numbers, sensor values and variable values can all be printed in the color set by the **set print color** block. \n\n![set_print_color_hello](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_color/set_print_color_hello.png)  \n\nAny **print** block used after the **set print color** will print in the color most recently set within the program.\n\n![set_print_color_text](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_color/set_print_color_text.png) \n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Set print color**, there is one parameter that can be changed (COLOR).\n\n![switch_set_print_color_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_color/switch_set_print_color_parameters.png)\n\nSet the color that the data printed on the Print Console will appear by providing the following:\n\n- COLOR - `BLACK`, `RED`, `GREEN`, or `BLUE` \n\n![switch_set_print_color](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_color/switch_set_print_color.png)\n"},"set_print_precision":{"name":"set_print_precision","markdown":"# Set print precision\n\nSets the number of digits that appear after the decimal point when using print blocks to print variables and values to the Print Console.\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the level of precision that inserted reporter blocks will be printed to the screen.\n\n* Ones (1) (Default)\n* Tenths (0.1)\n* Hundredths (0.01)\n* Thousandths (0.001)\n* All Digits (0.000001)\n\nReporter blocks printed to the display will round to the nearest level of precision. For example, 1.67 will be printed as 2 at ones precision and 1.7 at tenths precision.\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision/print_precision_choice.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo set the print precision with a Switch Python command, a variable needs to be set to determine the level of precision. To edit the Switch Python command for **Set print precision**, there is one parameter that can be changed (PRECISION).\n\n![switch_print_precision_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision/switch_print_precision_parameters.png)\n\nSet the level of precision that will be reported by providing the number of decimal places that should be printed. \n\n* Ones (1) (Default) = `0`\n* Tenths (0.1) = `1`\n* Hundredths (0.01) = `2`\n* Thousandths (0.001) = `3`\n* All Digits (0.000001) = `-1`\n\nFor example, to set the `print_precision` variable to the tenths place the variable in the **Switch stack** block would look like the following:\n\n![switch_print_precision_tenths](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision/switch_print_precision_tenths.png)\n\nThe `print_precision` variable is then referenced when printing to the Print Console, as an optional parameter within the **Print** command.  \n\nThis example will print the value from the timer (to the tenths place) to the Print Console using the `print_precision` variable.\n\n![switch_set_print_precision_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision/switch_set_print_precision_example.png)"},"set_print_precision_virtual":{"name":"set_print_precision_virtual","markdown":"# Set print precision\n\nSets the number of digits that appear after the decimal point when using print blocks to print variables and values to the Print Console.\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision_virtual/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the level of precision that inserted reporter blocks will be printed to the screen.\n\n* Ones (1)\n* Tenths (0.1) (Default)\n* Hundredths (0.01)\n* Thousandths (0.001)\n* All Digits (0.000001)\n\nReporter blocks printed to the display will round to the nearest level of precision. For example, 1.67 will be printed as 2 at ones precision and 1.7 at tenths precision.\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision_virtual/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_print_precision_virtual_viqc":{"name":"set_print_precision_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Set print precision\n\nSets the number of digits that appear after the decimal point when using print blocks to print variables and values to the Print Console.\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision_virtual_viqc/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the level of precision that inserted reporter blocks will be printed to the screen.\n\n* Ones (1)\n* Tenths (0.1) (Default)\n* Hundredths (0.01)\n* Thousandths (0.001)\n* All Digits (0.000001)\n\nReporter blocks printed to the display will round to the nearest level of precision. For example, 1.67 will be printed as 2 at ones precision and 1.7 at tenths precision.\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_print_precision_virtual_viqc/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_shaft_encoder_position":{"name":"set_shaft_encoder_position","markdown":"# Set shaft encoder position\n\nSets the Shaft Encoder's position to the given value.\n\n![set_encoder_position_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_shaft_encoder_position/set_encoder_position_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set shaft encoder position** block can be used to set the Shaft Encoder's position to any given value.\n\nUsually, the **set shaft encoder position** block is used to set the position to 0, to reset the Shaft Encoder's position.\n\nChoose which Shaft Encoder to use, then set the desired value.\n\n![set_encoder_position_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_shaft_encoder_position/set_encoder_position_motor.png)\n\n"},"set_tool":{"name":"set_tool","markdown":"# Set tool\n\nSets the end effector or the end-of-arm tooling (EOAT) on the V5 Robotic Arm.\n\n![set_tool_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_tool/set_tool_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe end effector on the V5 Robotic Arm can be none (no attachment), an electromagnet, or a marker. \n\n![set_marker](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_tool/set_marker.png)\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![select_tool_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_tool/select_tool_arm.png)\n\nChoose the tool on your V5 Robotic Arm. The default is set to **none**.\n\n![choose_tool](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_tool/choose_tool.png)"},"set_turn_velocity":{"name":"set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Set turn velocity\n\nSets the speed of Drivetrain turns.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Set turn velocity** will set the speed of the drivetrain, but will not cause the Drivetrain to move. A **turn** block is still required.\n\n**Set turn velocity** accepts a range from **1% to 100%**.\n\nThe **set turn velocity** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\n## Example\nThis example will make the Drivetrain turn slowly, at 25% speed, to the left.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Set turn velocity**, there are two parameters that can be changed (VELOCITY and UNITS).\n\n![switch_set_turn_velocity_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_turn_velocity/switch_set_turn_velocity_parameters.png)\n\nSet the velocity that the Drivetrain will move by providing each of the following:\n\n- VELOCITY - a numeric integer value from 0 - 100\n- UNITS - `PERCENT`\n\nNote the default Drivetrain velocity is 50%.\n\n![switch_set_turn_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_turn_velocity/switch_set_turn_velocity.png)\n"},"set_turn_velocity_go":{"name":"set_turn_velocity_go","markdown":"# Set turn velocity\n\nSets the speed of Drivetrain turns.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_turn_velocity_go/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Set turn velocity** block will only set the speed of the Drivetrain. It will not cause the Drivetrain to move. A Turn or Turn for block is required.\n\nThe **Set turn velocity** block accepts a range of 1% to 100%.\n\nSetting a Drivetrain's turn velocity to less than 1% will set the velocity to 1%.\n\nSetting a Drivetrain's turn velocity to above 100% will set the velocity to 100%.\n\nThe **Set turn velocity** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_turn_velocity_go/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example turns the Drivetrain slowly at 25% speed.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_turn_velocity_go/setTurnVelocity_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_variable":{"name":"set_variable","markdown":"# Set variable\n\nSets a variable to a given value.\n\n![set_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_variable/set_variable.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which variable to use. Variables can also be renamed or deleted. \n\n![set_variable_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_variable/set_variable_choose.png)\n\nThe **set variable** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![set_variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_variable/set_variable_inserted.png)\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo set the value of a variable with Switch Python, type the name of the variable into the **Switch Reporter** block and what the variable should be equal to.\n\n![switch_set_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_variable/switch_set_variable.png)\n\nThere are multiple types of Switch Python variables.\n\n* Numeric variable: used to store numeric integer values.\n\n![switch_numeric_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_variable/switch_numeric_variable.png) \n\n* String variable: used to store strings which are a sequence of characters \n\n![switch_string_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_variable/switch_string_variable.png) \n\n* Boolean variable: used to store `True` or `False` values\n\n![switch_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_variable/switch_boolean_variable.png) \n\nThe following rules should be applied when naming a variable:\n\n* The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n* The name cannot begin with a number\n* The name cannot use spaces\n* The name cannot use a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)"},"set_vision_object_index":{"name":"set_vision_object_index","markdown":"# Set vision object item\n\nSets the object item (of the object you want to learn more information about) out of the number of objects detected.\n\n![set_vision_object_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_vision_object_index/set_vision_object_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **object count** block can be used before the **set vision object item** block to determine the number of objects detected.\n\nChoose which Vision Sensor to use.\n\n![set_vision_object_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_vision_object_index/set_vision_object_device.png)\n\nThe **set vision object item** block can accept integers or numeric blocks.\n\n\n\n"},"set_width":{"name":"set_width","markdown":"# Set pen width\n\nSets the width of the outline for shapes drawn on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n![set_width_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_width/set_width_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set width** block is used to set the width of the line on the outside of circles and rectangles drawn on the V5 Brain's screen. \n\nThe **set width** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![v5_row_column_brain](static/help/vr/blocks/en/set_width/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)\n"},"setangle":{"name":"setangle","markdown":"# Set angle\n\nSets the angle of the tool plate before the next [Linear move] or [Joint move] block is executed.\n\n![set_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/setangle/set_angle_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set angle** block sets the tool plate to be tilted at an angle either **up** or **down** before the next [Linear move] or [Joint move] block in a project. \n\nNote that the **set angle** block sets the angle for all future [Linear move] and [Joint move] blocks, unless it is set otherwise.\n\n![tool_plate](static/help/vr/blocks/en/setangle/tool_plate.png)\n\nChoose which Robotic Arm to use.\n\n![select_set_angle_arm](static/help/vr/blocks/en/setangle/select_set_angle_arm.png)\n\nChoose the angle of the tool plate. The default is set to **up**.\n\n* The tool plate will be **horizontal** when it is set to **down**. \n* The tool plate will be **vertical** when it is set to **up**. \n\n![select_angle](static/help/vr/blocks/en/setangle/select_angle.png)\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the tool plate will begin to tilt once the [Joint move] block is executed and complete its tilting movement before the [Linear move] block.\n\n![set_angle_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/setangle/set_angle_example.png)"},"size_of_array":{"name":"size_of_array","markdown":"# Length of list\n\nReports the number of items in a list.  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/en/size_of_array/size_of_array.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\nChoose which list to use. The list can also be renamed or deleted.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/size_of_array/size_of_array_select.png)\n\nThe **length of list** reporter block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces.\n\n![size_of_array_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/en/size_of_array/size_of_array_insert.png)\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block.\n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Length of list**, there is one parameter that can be changed (LISTNAME).\n\n![switch_list_name](static/help/vr/blocks/en/size_of_array/switch_list_name.png)\n\nSet the list that the block should reference by inserting the name of the list into the parenthesis.\n\n![switch_length_mylist](static/help/vr/blocks/en/size_of_array/switch_length_mylist.png)\n\nIn this example, **Length of list** command is used to determine how many times the **Repeat** loop will repeat.\n\n![switch_length_repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/en/size_of_array/switch_length_repeat.png)"},"snapshot":{"name":"snapshot","markdown":"# Snapshot\n\nTakes a snapshot from the Vision Sensor.\n\n![snapshot_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/snapshot/snapshot_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **snapshot** block will capture the current image from the Vision Sensor to be processed and analyzed for color signatures and codes. \n\nTypically, a **snapshot** is required first before using any other Vision Sensor blocks.\n\nSelect which Vision Sensor to use.\n\n![snapshot_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/snapshot/snapshot_device.png)\n\nSelect which vision signature to use. Vision signatures are configured from the Devices window.\n\n![snapshot_signature](static/help/vr/blocks/en/snapshot/snapshot_signature.png)\n"},"spin":{"name":"spin","markdown":"# Spin\n\nSpins a V5 Motor or Motor Group until stopped.\n\n![spin_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin/spin_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **spin** block will run a Motor or Motor Group forever, until a new motion block is used or the program is stopped.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![spin_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin/spin_motor.png)\n\nChoose which direction to spin. Direction names can be changed in the Devices window. \n\n![spin_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin/spin_direction.png)\n"},"spin_at_voltage":{"name":"spin_at_voltage","markdown":"# Spin at Voltage\n\nSpins a V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group at a specified voltage.\n\n![spin_at_voltage](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_at_voltage/spin_at_voltage.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Spin at voltage** accepts a range from -12V to 12V.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![spin_at_voltage_option](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_at_voltage/spin_at_voltage_option.png)\n\nChoose which direction to spin. Direction names can be changed in the Devices window.\n\n![spin_at_voltage_option2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_at_voltage/spin_at_voltage_option2.png)\n\nThe **spin at voltage** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nSpinning a Motor or Motor Group at a negative voltage will cause the Motor/Motor Group to spin in the opposite direction than the one specified.\n"},"spin_for":{"name":"spin_for","markdown":"# Spin for\n\nSpins a V5 Motor or Motor Group for a given distance.\n\n![spin_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for/spin_for_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Spin for** will tell a Motor or Motor Group to travel in a given direction, for a given distance, from where it is currently located.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![spin_for_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for/spin_for_motor.png)\n\nChoose which direction to spin. Direction names can be changed in the Devices window. \n\n![spin_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for/spin_for_direction.png)  \n\nThe **spin for** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nChoose the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![spin_for_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for/spin_for_value.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Motor or Motor Group is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Motor or Motor Group moves.\n\n![spin_for_dont_wait_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for/spin_for_dont_wait_block.png)\n"},"spin_for_go":{"name":"spin_for_go","markdown":"# Spin for\n\nSpins a Motor for a given distance.\n\n![spin_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_go/spin_for_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Spin for** block will spin a Motor in a given direction for a given distance from where it is currently located.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![spin_for_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_go/spin_for_motor.png)\n\nChoose which direction to spin.\n\n![spin_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_go/spin_for_direction.png)  \n\nDirection names change depending on the motor selected. \n\n![spin_for_direction1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_go/spin_for_direction1.png)  \n\nThe **Spin for** for block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![spin_for_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_go/spin_for_variable.png)  \n\nChoose the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![spin_for_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_go/spin_for_value.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Motor or Motor Group is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Motor or Motor Group moves.\n\n![spin_for_and_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_go/spin_for_and_dont_wait.png)"},"spin_for_viqc":{"name":"spin_for_viqc","markdown":"# Spin for\n\nSpins a VEX IQ Motor for a given distance.\n\n![spin_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_viqc/spin_for_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Spin for** will tell a Motor to travel in a given direction for a given distance from where it is currently located.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![spin_for_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_viqc/spin_for_motor.png)\n\nChoose which direction to spin. Direction names can be changed in the Devices window. \n\n![spin_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_viqc/spin_for_direction.png)  \n\nThe **spin for** for block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nChoosing the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![spin_for_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_viqc/spin_for_value.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Motor is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Motor moves.\n\n![spin_for_dont_wait_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_for_viqc/spin_for_dont_wait_block.png)\n"},"spin_go":{"name":"spin_go","markdown":"# Spin\n\nSpins a Motor until stopped.\n\n![spin_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_go/spin_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Spin** block will run a Motor forever until a new block from the Motion category is used, or the project is stopped.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![spin_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_go/spin_motor.png)\n\nChoose which direction to spin. \n\n![spin_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_go/spin_direction.png)\n\nDirection names change depending on the motor selected. \n\n![spin_direction1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_go/spin_direction1.png)\n\n"},"spin_motor_controller":{"name":"spin_motor_controller","markdown":"# Spin motor controller\n\nSpins a Motor 393 connected to a Motor Controller 29 until stopped.\n\n![spin_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_motor_controller/spin_393_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **spin** block will run a Motor 393 forever until a new motor block is used, or the program is stopped.\n\nChoose which Motor 393 to use.\n\n![spin_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_motor_controller/spin_393_motor.png)\n\nChoose which direction to spin. Direction names can be changed in the Devices window. \n\n![spin_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_motor_controller/spin_393_direction.png)"},"spin_servo":{"name":"spin_servo","markdown":"# Spin servo\n\nSpins a Servo Motor to a set position.\n\n![spin_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_servo/servo_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Spin servo** accepts a position range from **0 to 100 degrees**.\n\n![spin_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_servo/servo_port.png)\n\n"},"spin_to_position":{"name":"spin_to_position","markdown":"# Spin to position\n\nSpins a V5 Motor or Motor Group to a set position.\n\n![spin_to_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position/spin_to_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Spin to position** will tell a Motor or Motor Group to travel to a specific position. Based on the current position of the Motor or Motor Group, **spin to position** will determine the direction to rotate.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![spin_to_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position/spin_to_motor.png)\n\nThe **spin to position** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nChoose the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![spin_to_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position/spin_to_value.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Motor or Motor Group is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Motor or Motor Group moves.\n\n![spin_to_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position/spin_to_anddontwait.png)\n"},"spin_to_position_go":{"name":"spin_to_position_go","markdown":"# Spin to position\n\nSpins a Motor to a set position.\n\n![spin_to_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_go/spin_to_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Spin to position** block will spin a Motor to a specific position. \n\nBased on the current position of the Motor, the **Spin to position** block will determine the direction to rotate.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![spin_to_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_go/spin_to_motor.png)\n\nThe **Spin to position** for block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![spin_to_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_go/spin_to_variable.png)\n\nChoose the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![spin_to_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_go/spin_to_value.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Motor or Motor Group is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Motor or Motor Group moves.\n\n![spin_to_and_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_go/spin_to_and_dont_wait.png)"},"spin_to_position_viqc":{"name":"spin_to_position_viqc","markdown":"# Spin to position\n\nSpins a VEX IQ Motor to a set position.\n\n![spin_to_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_viqc/spin_to_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Spin to position** will tell a Motor to travel to a specific position. Based on the current position of the Motor, **spin to position** will determine the direction to rotate.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![spin_to_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_viqc/spin_to_motor.png)\n\nThe **spin to position** for block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nChoose the unit of measurement in degrees or turns.\n\n![spin_to_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_viqc/spin_to_value.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Motor is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Motor moves.\n\n![spin_to_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_to_position_viqc/spin_to_anddontwait.png)\n"},"spin_viqc":{"name":"spin_viqc","markdown":"# Spin\n\nSpins a VEX IQ Motor until stopped.\n\n![spin_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_viqc/spin_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **spin** block will run a Motor forever until a new motion block is used or the program is stopped.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![spin_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_viqc/spin_motor.png)\n\nChoose which direction to spin. Direction names can be changed in the Devices window. \n\n![spin_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/spin_viqc/spin_direction.png)\n"},"standby":{"name":"standby","markdown":"# Standby\n\nThe **Standby** block allows the VR Rover to go into standby until the specified battery threshold is reached.\n\n![standby_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/standby/standby_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe default battery level is 50%. Choose a battery value and insert it into the circular space on the block. This will determine when the VR Rover should come out of standby.\n\n![circular_space](static/help/vr/blocks/en/standby/circular_space.png)\n\nWhen in standby, the VR Rover will use its battery slower, but the \"number of days survived\" counter will increase at a higher rate.\n\nOnce the battery level threshold is reached, the VR Rover will come out of standby, going back to consuming its battery and counting the \"number of days survived\" at the normal rate.\n\nUse project below to see an example of the VR Rover going into standby and observe the battery consumption and \"number of days survived\" counter.\n\n![standby_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/standby/standby_example.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"stop_driving":{"name":"stop_driving","markdown":"# Stop driving\n\nStops the Drivetrain.\n\n![stopDriving_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_driving/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **stop driving** block will cause the Drivetrain to come to a complete stop.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will stop the Drivetrain after 3 seconds.\n\n![stopDriving_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_driving/stopDriving_example.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Stop driving** does not require any parameters inside the parentheses.\n\n![switch_stop_driving](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_driving/switch_stop_driving.png)\n\n\n"},"stop_driving_go":{"name":"stop_driving_go","markdown":"# Stop driving\n\nStops the Drivetrain.\n\n![stopDriving_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_driving_go/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## How to Use\n\nThe **Stop driving** block will cause the Drivetrain to come to a complete stop.\n\nThe **Stop driving** block is used with a non-waiting block that moves the Drivetrain, such as the Drive block. This is important, because the Drive block will cause the Drivetrain will drive forever unless it is stopped.\n\n![stop_driving_project_flow](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_driving_go/stop_driving_project_flow.png)\n"},"stop_motor":{"name":"stop_motor","markdown":"# Stop motor\n\nStops a V5 Motor or Motor Group.\n\n![stopMotor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor/stopMotor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to stop.\n\n![stop_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor/stop_motor.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will spin the Claw Motor open for 3 seconds before stopping.\n\n![stopMotor_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor/stopMotor_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_motor_controller":{"name":"stop_motor_controller","markdown":"# Stop motor controller\n\nStops a Motor 393 connected to a Motor Controller 29.\n\n![stop_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_controller/stop_393_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Motor 393 to stop.\n\n![stop_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_controller/stop_393_motor.png)\n\nThis example will spin a Motor 393 forward for 3 seconds before stopping.\n\n![stop_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_controller/stop_393_example.png)"},"stop_motor_go":{"name":"stop_motor_go","markdown":"# Stop motor\n\nStops a Motor.\n\n![stopMotor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_go/stopMotor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Motor to stop.\n\n![stop_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_go/stop_motor.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will spin the motor of a robot up for 2 seconds before stopping.\n\n![stopMotor_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_go/stopMotor_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_motor_viqc":{"name":"stop_motor_viqc","markdown":"# Stop Motor\n\nStops a Motor.\n\n![stopMotor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_viqc/stopMotor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Motor to stop.\n\n![stop_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_viqc/stop_motor.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will spin the Claw Motor open for 3 seconds before stopping.\n\n![stopMotor_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_motor_viqc/stopMotor_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_project":{"name":"stop_project","markdown":"# Stop project\n\nStops a running project.\n\n![stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_project/stop_project.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **stop project** block will cause a running project to stop. \n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the VR Robot in a 500mm square and then stop the project.\n\n![stop_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_project/stop_project2.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Stop project** does not require any parameters inside the parentheses.\n\n![switch_stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_project/switch_stop_project.png)"},"stop_project_go":{"name":"stop_project_go","markdown":"# Stop project\n\nStops a running project.\n\n![stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_project_go/stop_project.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **stop project** block will cause a running project to stop. \n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the robot in a 100mm square and then stop the project.\n\n![stop_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/stop_project_go/stop_project2.png)"},"subtract":{"name":"subtract","markdown":"# Subtract\n\nSubtracts one value from another and reports the difference.\n\n![subtract](static/help/vr/blocks/en/subtract/subtract.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Subtract** can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nThe **subtract** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces, and will accept circular block in it as well. \n\nSubtract two numbers.\n\n![subtract_example1](static/help/vr/blocks/en/subtract/subtract_example1.png)\n\nSubtract with values from variables.\n\n![subtract_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/subtract/subtract_example2.png)  \n\nSubtract with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![subtract_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/en/subtract/subtract_example3.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Subtract**, enter the quantities to be subtracted before and after the `-`. \n\nSubtract two numbers. \n\n![switch_subtract_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/en/subtract/switch_subtract_numbers.png)\n\nSubtract with values from variables.\n\n![switch_subtract_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/subtract/switch_subtract_variable.png)\n\nSubtract with values from sensors or devices.\n\n![switch_subtract_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/subtract/switch_subtract_sensor.png)"},"temperature_of_motor":{"name":"temperature_of_motor","markdown":"# Temperature of motor\n\nReports the temperature of a V5 Smart Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group.\n\n![temp_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/temperature_of_motor/temp_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUsing the **temperature of motor** reporter block can help users develop logic to keep a V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group from overheating during heavy use.\n\n**Temperature of motor** reports a range from **0% to 100%**. \n\nChoose which V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![temp_of_motor_options_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/temperature_of_motor/temp_of_motor_options_motor.png)\n\nThe reporter block **temperature of motor** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![temp_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/temperature_of_motor/temp_of_motor_inserted.png)"},"timer_value":{"name":"timer_value","markdown":"# Timer value\n\nReports the value of the Brain's timer in seconds.\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe timer starts at 0 seconds when the program starts, and reports the timer's value as a decimal value.\n\nThe reporter block **timer value** is used inside of blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value/timerValue_inserted.png)\n\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python reporter for **Timer value** does not require any parameters inside the parentheses.\n\n![switch_timer_value](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value/switch_timer_value.png)"},"timer_value_virtual":{"name":"timer_value_virtual","markdown":"# Timer value\n\nReports the value of the V5 Brain's timer in seconds.\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe timer starts at 0 seconds when the program starts, and reports the timer's value as a decimal value.\n\nThe reporter block **Timer value** is used inside of blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual/timerValue_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Timer value** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![timerValue_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual/timerValue_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![timerValue_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual/timerValue_monitor.png)"},"timer_value_virtual_viqc":{"name":"timer_value_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Timer value\n\nReports the value of the VEX IQ Brain timer in seconds.\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual_viqc/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe timer starts at 0 seconds when the program starts and reports decimal values.\n\nThe reporter block **Timer value** is used inside of blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual_viqc/timerValue_inserted.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will move the Drivetrain forward and play a sound effect for 4.5 seconds before making a 90-degree turn.\n\n![timerValue_example_revised](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual_viqc/timerValue_example_revised.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Timer value** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![timerValue_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual_viqc/timerValue_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![timerValue_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/timer_value_virtual_viqc/timerValue_monitor.png)"},"torque_of_motor":{"name":"torque_of_motor","markdown":"# Torque of motor\n\nReports the amount of torque (rotational force) a V5 Smart Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group is currently using.\n\n![motor_torque](static/help/vr/blocks/en/torque_of_motor/motor_torque.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Torque of motor** reports a range from **0.0 to 18.6 inch-pounds (InLB)** or **0.0 to 2.1 Newton-meters (Nm)**. \n\nChoose which V5 Smart Motor's or Motor Group's torque to report.\n\n![motor_torque_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/torque_of_motor/motor_torque_device.png)\n\nChoose whether the **torque of motor** is reported in inch-pounds (InLb) or Newton-meters (Nm).\n\n![motor_torque_units](static/help/vr/blocks/en/torque_of_motor/motor_torque_units.png)\n\nThe reporter block **torque of motor** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![motor_torque_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/torque_of_motor/motor_torque_inserted.png)"},"turn":{"name":"turn","markdown":"# Turn\n\nRotates the Drivetrain forever.\n\n![turn_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn/turn_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **turn** block will rotate the Drivetrain forever, until a new Drivetrain block is used, or the program is stopped.\n\nChoose the direction the Drivetrain will turn.\n\n![turn_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn/turn_direction.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Turn**, there is one parameter that can be changed (DIRECTION).\n\n![switch_turn_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn/switch_turn_parameters.png)\n\nSet the direction that the Drivetrain will move by providing the following:\n\n- DIRECTION - either `RIGHT` or `LEFT`\n\n![switch_turn](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn/switch_turn.png)"},"turn_for":{"name":"turn_for","markdown":"# Turn for\n\nRotates the Drivetrain for a given number of degrees.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which direction the Drivetrain will turn.\n\n![turn_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for/turn_for_direction.png)  \n\n\nSet how far the Drivetrain will move by entering a number of degrees. Note that entering a negative value will cause the Drivetrain to turn in the opposite direction from what was selected in the dropdown.\n\nThe **turn for** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for/turn_for_block_2.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Drivetrain is done turning.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Drivetrain turns.\n\n![turn_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for/turn_for_anddontwait.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Turn for**, there are three parameters that can be changed (DIRECTION, ANGLE, and UNITS).\n\n![switch_turn_for_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for/switch_turn_for_parameters.png)\n\nSet the angle and the direction that the Drivetrain will move by providing each of the following:\n\n- DIRECTION - either `RIGHT` or `LEFT`\n- ANGLE - a numeric integer value\n- UNITS - `DEGREES`\n\n![switch_turn_for](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for/switch_turn_for.png)\n\nThe optional parameter of \"and don't wait\" can be added to the Switch Python command for **Turn for** by typing `wait=False` at the end of the command.\n\n![switch_turn_for_dont_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for/switch_turn_for_dont_wait.png)\n\n"},"turn_for_go":{"name":"turn_for_go","markdown":"# Turn for\n\nTurns the Drivetrain for a given distance.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for_go/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which direction the Drivetrain will turn.\n\n![turn_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for_go/turn_for_direction.png)  \n\nSet how far the Drivetrain will turn by entering a number of degrees into the oval. \n\n![turn_for_degrees](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for_go/turn_for_degrees.png)\n\nThe **Turn for** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![turn_for_reporter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_for_go/turn_for_reporter.png)\n"},"turn_go":{"name":"turn_go","markdown":"# Turn\n\nRotates the Drivetrain forever.\n\n![turn_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_go/turn_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Turn** block will rotate the Drivetrain forever until a new block from the Drivetrain category is used, or the project is stopped.\n\nChoose the direction the Drivetrain will turn.\n\n![turn_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_go/turn_direction.png)"},"turn_to_heading":{"name":"turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Turn to heading\n\nTurns a Drivetrain to a specific heading using the built-in Gyro sensor.\n\n![turn_to_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **turn to heading** block can be used to turn the Drivetrain to any given clockwise heading.\n\nBased on the current heading of the Gyro, **turn to heading** will determine which direction to turn.\n\n**Turn to heading** accepts a range of **0.00 to 359.99** degrees.\n\nThe **turn to heading** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Drivetrain is done turning.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Drivetrain turns.\n\n![turn_to_heading_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_anddontwait.png)\n\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will cause the Drivetrain to make four turns: \n\n![turn_to_heading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\n- Right to 45 degrees\n- Right to 90 degrees\n- Right to 270 degrees\n- Left to 180 degrees\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Turn to heading**, there are two parameters that can be changed (ANGLE and UNITS).\n\n![switch_turn_to_heading_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading/switch_turn_to_heading_parameters.png)\n\nSet the angle and the direction that the Drivetrain will move by providing each of the following:\n\n- ANGLE - a numeric integer value\n- UNITS - `DEGREES`\n\n![switch_turn_to_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading/switch_turn_to_heading.png)\n\nThe optional parameter of \"and don't wait\" can be added to the Switch Python command for **Turn to heading** by typing `wait=False` at the end of the command.\n\n![switch_turn_to_heading_dont_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading/switch_turn_to_heading_dont_wait.png)\n\n"},"turn_to_heading_go":{"name":"turn_to_heading_go","markdown":"# Turn to heading\n\nTurns a Drivetrain to a specific heading when configured with a Gyro Sensor. The Gyro Sensor is built into the Brain.\n\n![turn_to_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading_go/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Turn to heading** block can be used to turn the Drivetrain to any given clockwise heading as shown in the image below.\n\n![TopDownBot01](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading_go/TopDownBot01.png)\n\nBased on the current heading of the Gyro Sensor built into the Brain, the **Turn to heading** block will determine which direction to turn.\n\nThe **Turn to heading** block accepts a range of 0.00 to 359.99 degrees.\n\nThe **Turn to heading** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![turn_to_heading_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading_go/turn_to_heading_variable.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will cause the Drivetrain to make three turns: \n\n- Right (clockwise) to 45 degrees\n- Right (clockwise) to 270 degrees\n- Left (counter-clockwise) to 180 degrees\n\n![turn_to_heading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading_go/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Drivetrain is done moving.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Drivetrain moves.\n![turn_to_heading_and_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_heading_go/turn_to_heading_and_dont_wait.png)"},"turn_to_object":{"name":"turn_to_object","markdown":"# Turn to object\n\nTurn the VR Rover to face a specified object.\n\n![turn_to_object](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_object/turn_to_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which object the VR Rover will turn to. This could be food, an enemy, or home.\n\n![turn_to_object_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_object/turn_to_object_choose.png)\n\nIf the VR Rover detects multiple pieces of food or multiple enemies, it will turn towards the closer option.\n\nBy default, other blocks will wait until the VR Rover is done moving. You can select the arrow to expand 'and don't wait.' This will cause the other blocks to continue running while the VR Rover turns.\n\n![turn_to_object_dont_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_object/turn_to_object_dont_wait.png)\n"},"turn_to_rotation":{"name":"turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Turn to rotation\n\nTurns a Drivetrain to a specific angle of rotation using the built-in Gyro sensor.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_iq.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **turn to rotation** block can be used to turn the Drivetrain to a given positive (clockwise) or negative (counter-clockwise) value.\n\nBased on the current **rotation** value of the Drivetrain, **turn to rotation** will determine which direction to turn.\n\nThe **turn to rotation** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nBy default, other blocks will **wait** until the Drivetrain is done turning.\n\nYou can select the arrow to expand **and don't wait** - this will cause other blocks to continue running while the Drivetrain turns.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will cause the Drivetrain to make four turns: \n\n![turn_to_rotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_rotation/turn_to_rotation_example.png)\n\n- Right (clockwise) to 90 degrees\n- Right (clockwise) to 180 degrees\n- Left (counter-clockwise) to -45 degrees\n- Right (clockwise) to 0 degrees\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Turn to rotation**, there are two parameters that can be changed (ANGLE and UNITS).\n\n![switch_turn_to_rotation_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_rotation/switch_turn_to_rotation_parameters.png)\n\nSet the angle and the direction that the Drivetrain will move by providing each of the following:\n\n- ANGLE - a numeric integer value\n- UNITS - `DEGREES`\n\n![switch_turn_to_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_rotation/switch_turn_to_rotation.png)\n\nThe optional parameter of \"and don't wait\" can be added to the Switch Python command for **Turn to rotation** by typing `wait=False` at the end of the command.\n\n![switch_turn_to_rotation_dont_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_rotation/switch_turn_to_rotation_dont_wait.png)\n"},"turn_to_rotation_go":{"name":"turn_to_rotation_go","markdown":"# Turn to rotation\n\nTurns a Drivetrain to a specific angle of rotation using a Gyro Sensor. The Gyro Sensor is built into the Brain.\n\n![turntorotation_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_rotation_go/turntorotation_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Turn to rotation** block can be used to turn the Drivetrain to a given positive (clockwise) or negative (counter-clockwise) value.\n\nBased on the current rotation value of the Drivetrain, the **Turn to rotation** block will determine which direction to turn.\n\nThe **Turn to rotation** block can accept decimals, integers, or reporter blocks.\n\n![turntorotation_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/turn_to_rotation_go/turntorotation_variable.png)\n"},"under_attack":{"name":"under_attack","markdown":"# Under attack\n\nReports whether the VR Rover is under attack from an enemy or not.\n\n![under_attack](static/help/vr/blocks/en/under_attack/under_attack.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **Under attack** Boolean block reports a true or false value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![under_attack_if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/en/under_attack/under_attack_if_then.png)\n\nThe block reports true when the VR Rover is under attack from an enemy and will report false when the VR Rover is not under attack.\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Under attack** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![under_attack_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/under_attack/under_attack_monitor.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n"},"variable":{"name":"variable","markdown":"# Variable\n\nReports the numeric value assigned to the variable.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable/variable.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Variable** blocks are used to report a numeric value stored inside of the variable. Use the **set variable** block to set or update the numeric value of a variable.\n\n![variable_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable/variable_cursor.png)\n\nThe **variable** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable/variable_inserted.png)\n\nVariables are created using the **Make a Variable** button.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable/make_variable.png)  \n\n\n## Switch Python Command\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo change what variable is being reported, type the name of the variable into the **Switch Reporter** block. \n\n![switch_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable/switch_variable.png)\n"},"variable_virtual":{"name":"variable_virtual","markdown":"# Variable\n\nReports the numeric value assigned to the variable.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable_virtual/variable.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Variable** blocks are used to report a numeric value stored inside of the variable. Use the **set variable** block to set or update the numeric value of a variable.\n\n![variable_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable_virtual/variable_cursor.png)\n\nThe **variable** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable_virtual/variable_inserted.png)\n\nVariables are created using the **Make a Variable** button.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable_virtual/make_variable.png)  \n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of a **variable** block, select the checkbox next to its reporter block.\n\n![variable_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable_virtual/variable_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the **variable** reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![variable_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/variable_virtual/variable_monitor.png)"},"velocity_of_motor":{"name":"velocity_of_motor","markdown":"# Velocity of motor\n\nReports the current velocity of a V5 Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor/velocity_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To use\n\n**Velocity of motor** reports a range from **-100% to 100%**.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_options_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor/velocity_of_motor_options_motor.png)\n\nThe reporter block **velocity of motor** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor/velocity_of_motor_inserted.png)\n"},"velocity_of_motor_go":{"name":"velocity_of_motor_go","markdown":"# Velocity of motor\n\nReports the current velocity of a Motor.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_go/velocity_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To use\n\nThe **Velocity of motor** block reports a range from 1% to 100%.\n\nChoose which Motor to use.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_options_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_go/velocity_of_motor_options_motor.png)\n\n\nThe **Velocity of motor** reporter block is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_go/velocity_of_motor_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Velocity of motor** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_go/velocity_of_motor_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter block(s) selected for monitoring.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_go/velocity_of_motor_monitor.png)"},"velocity_of_motor_virtual":{"name":"velocity_of_motor_virtual","markdown":"# Velocity of motor\n\nReports the current velocity of a V5 Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual/velocity_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To use\n\n**Velocity of motor** reports a range from **-100% to 100%**.\n\nChoose which Motor or Motor Group to use.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_options_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual/velocity_of_motor_options_motor.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Velocity of motor** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual/velocity_of_motor_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Velocity of motor** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual/velocity_of_motor_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual/velocity_of_motor_monitor.png)\n"},"velocity_of_motor_virtual_viqc":{"name":"velocity_of_motor_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Velocity of motor\n\nReports the current velocity of a VEX IQ Smart Motor or the first motor in a Motor Group.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual_viqc/velocity_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## How To use\n\n**Velocity of motor** reports a range from **-100% to 100%**.\n\nChoose which Motor/Motor Group to use.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_options_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual_viqc/velocity_of_motor_options_motor.png)\n\nThe reporter block **Velocity of motor** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual_viqc/velocity_of_motor_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **Velocity of motor** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual_viqc/velocity_of_motor_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![velocity_of_motor_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_motor_virtual_viqc/velocity_of_motor_monitor.png)\n"},"velocity_of_shaft_encoder":{"name":"velocity_of_shaft_encoder","markdown":"# Velocity of shaft encoder\n\nReports the current velocity of a Shaft Encoder.\n\n![velocity_of_encoder_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_shaft_encoder/velocity_of_encoder_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Velocity of shaft encoder** reports a decimal value in either degrees per second (dps) or rotations per minute (rpm).\n\nChoose which Shaft Encoder to use.\n\n![velocity_of_encoder_options_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_shaft_encoder/velocity_of_encoder_options_motor.png)\n\nChoose whether the velocity is reported in degrees per second (dps) or rotations per minute (rpm).\n\n![velocity_of_encoder_options_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/velocity_of_shaft_encoder/velocity_of_encoder_options_unit.png)"},"vision_object":{"name":"vision_object","markdown":"# Vision object\n\nReports information about a detected object from the Vision Sensor.\n\n![vision_object_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/vision_object/vision_object_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nIn order to use the **Vision object** block, use the following steps:\n\n1. Configure your Vision Sensor using the Devices window.\n2. Use **snapshot** to capture a Vision Sensor image and look for a signature / color code.\n3. Use **object exists** to check if the Vision Sensor detected the requested signature / color code.\n\nOnce the Vision Sensor detects an object, you can use other Vision blocks to learn more about the object detected.\n\n* Use **set vision object item** to set which detected object you want to learn more information about. By default, the largest object detected is used.\n* Use **object count** to determine how many objects were detected of that requested signature / color code.\n* Use **vision object** to determine which information to report from the detected object.\n\nSelect which Vision Sensor to use.\n\n![vision_object_block_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/vision_object/vision_object_block_device.png)\n\nSelect which value to report from the Vision Sensor:\n\n* Width - How wide the object is in pixels. Returns a value of **2 - 316 px**.\n* Height - How tall the object is in pixels. Returns a value of **2 - 212 px**. \n* CenterX - Reports the X coordinate of the center of the detected object. Returns a value of **0 - 315 px**.\n* CenterY - Reports the Y coordinate of the center of the detected object. Returns a value of **0 - 211 px**.\n* Angle - Reports the angle of the detected object. Returns a value of **0 - 180 degrees**.\n\n![vision_object_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/en/vision_object/vision_object_block_setting.png)\n\nThe reporter block **vision object** is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![vision_object_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/vision_object/vision_object_block_inserted.png)\n"},"wait":{"name":"wait","markdown":"# Wait\n\nWaits for a specific amount of time before moving to the next block.\n\n![wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait/wait.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose an amount of time to **wait**.\n\nThe **wait** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\n![wait_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait/wait_select.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **Wait**, there are two parameters that can be changed (TIME and UNITS).\n\n![switch_wait_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait/switch_wait_parameters.png)\n\nSet the amount of time the project will wait before moving to the next command by providing each of the following:\n\n- TIME - a numeric decimal or integer value \n- UNITS - either `MSEC` (milliseconds) or `SECONDS`\n\n![switch_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait/switch_wait.png)\n"},"wait_until":{"name":"wait_until","markdown":"# Wait until\n\nWaits for a Boolean block to report **true** before moving to the next block.\n\n![wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait_until/wait_until.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Wait until** will repeatedly check a Boolean reporter block, and will not move to the next block until the Boolean report block reports **true**.\n\nThe **wait until** Boolean block can accept hexagonal (six-sided) shaped blocks.\n\n![wait_until_2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait_until/wait_until_2.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nThe Switch Python command for **Wait until** is a ***while not*** loop. To edit the Switch Python command, set the Boolean CONDITION that is to be evaluated in the project. \n\n![switch_wait_until_parameter](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait_until/switch_wait_until_parameter.png)\n\nNote the ***wait*** command also appears. Do not delete this command, as it ensures the project runs correctly.\n\nIf no Boolean CONDITION is set, the Switch Python command will default to `while not False:`.\n\n![switch_wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait_until/switch_wait_until.png)\n\nSet the Boolean CONDITION that is to be evaluated. In this example, the Boolean CONDITION of `left_bumper.pressed()` is set.\n\n![switch_wait_until_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/wait_until/switch_wait_until_example.png)"},"when_autonomous":{"name":"when_autonomous","markdown":"# When autonomous\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when an \"autonomous\" mode signal is received from a competition field or competition switch.\n\n![when_autonomous](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_autonomous/when_autonomous.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nAny blocks attached to the **when autonomous** event will be run when the robot is connected to a competition field or competition switch that is set to the \"autonomous\" mode.\n\nUsers can have up to 50 **when autonomous control** blocks in a project."},"when_brain_screen":{"name":"when_brain_screen","markdown":"# When brain screen\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the V5 Brain's touchscreen is pressed or released.\n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_brain_screen/when_brain_pressed.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **when brain screen** event can be used to create actions or behaviors based on touch interaction with the V5 Brain's touchscreen. \n\nUsed together with the **screen position** reporter block, users can create custom interfaces to their V5 Brain by detecting when and where touches occur on the screen.\n\nSelect which action will trigger the event - **pressed** or **released** \n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_brain_screen/when_brain_pressed_condition.png)"},"when_broadcasted":{"name":"when_broadcasted","markdown":"# When I receive\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the selected message is received from a **broadcasted** message.\n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_broadcasted/when_broadcast.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which message to listen for. A new message can also be created.\n\n![when_broadcast_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_broadcasted/when_broadcast_select.png)\n\nThe **when I receive** block will only be triggered to run from a **broadcast** or **broadcast and wait** block in a different stack."},"when_bumper":{"name":"when_bumper","markdown":"# When bumper\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the selected Bumper Switch sensor is pressed or released.\n\n![when_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_bumper/when_bumper.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Bumper to use.\n\n![when_bumper_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_bumper/when_bumper_select.png)\n\nSelect which action will trigger the event - **pressed** or **released** \n\n![when_bumper_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_bumper/when_bumper_condition.png)\n"},"when_controller_axis":{"name":"when_controller_axis","markdown":"# When controller axis\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the selected V5 Controller joystick axis is moved.\n\n![when_controller_axis](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_controller_axis/when_controller_axis.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which V5 Controller to use.\n\n![when_controller_axis_device](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_controller_axis/when_controller_axis_device.png)\n\nChoose which Controller joystick axis to use.\n\n![when_controller_axis_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_controller_axis/when_controller_axis_select.png)\n"},"when_controller_button":{"name":"when_controller_button","markdown":"# When controller button\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the selected V5 Controller button is pressed or released.\n\n![when_controller_button](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_controller_button/when_controller_button.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which V5 Controller to use.\n\n![when_controller_button_controller](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_controller_button/when_controller_button_controller.png)\n\nChoose which Controller button to use.\n\n![when_controller_button_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_controller_button/when_controller_button_select.png)\n\nSelect which action will trigger the event - **pressed** or **released** \n\n![when_controller_button_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_controller_button/when_controller_button_condition.png)"},"when_driver":{"name":"when_driver","markdown":"# When driver\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when a \"driver control\" mode signal is received from a competition field or competition switch.\n\n![when_started](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_driver/when_driver.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nAny blocks attached to the **when driver control** event will be run when the robot is connected to a competition field or competition switch that is set to the \"driver control\" mode.\n\nA **forever** loop is highly recommended to be used with a **when driver control** event, to allow V5 Controller based commands to be frequently updated during the driver control portion of a match.\n\nUsers can have up to 50 **when driver control** blocks in a project."},"when_gps_heading_changed":{"name":"when_gps_heading_changed","markdown":"# When GPS heading changed\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when a GPS Sensor detects a heading change.\n\n![when_gps_heading_changed_block](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_gps_heading_changed/when_gps_heading_changed_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **When GPS heading changed** event can be use to create actions or behaviors that can be triggered when a GPS Sensor detects a change in heading.\n\nChoose a GPS Sensor to detect a heading change on.\n\n![when_gps_heading_changed_block2](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_gps_heading_changed/when_gps_heading_changed_block2.png)"},"when_level_up":{"name":"when_level_up","markdown":"# When level up\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks each time the VR Rover moves from one level to another.\n\n![when_level_up](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_level_up/when_level_up.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nA **When level up** event will run when the VR Rover begins levels up.\n\nThe VR Rover begins at Level 1 and moves up a level after collecting a certain amount of Experience Points (XP).\n\n![level_up_experience_bar](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_level_up/level_up_experience_bar.png)\n"},"when_limit":{"name":"when_limit","markdown":"# When limit\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the selected Limit Switch sensor is pressed or released.\n\n![when_limit](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_limit/when_limit.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Limit Switch to use.\n\n![when_limit_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_limit/when_limit_select.png)\n\nSelect which action will trigger the event - **pressed** or **released** \n\n![when_limit_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_limit/when_limit_condition.png)\n"},"when_started":{"name":"when_started","markdown":"# When started\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the project is started.\n\n![when_started](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_started/when_started.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nA **when started** event will run when the project is started from the **start** button in VEXcode VR Blocks.\n\nAll new projects will automatically include a **when started** block.\n\n"},"when_timer":{"name":"when_timer","markdown":"# When timer\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the Brain's timer is greater than the given value.\n\n![when_timer](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_timer/when_timer.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Brain's timer begins at the beginning of each project.\n\nChoose an amount of time. The **when timer** event will run once the Brain's timer is **greater than** the entered amount.\n\n![when_timer_select](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_timer/when_timer_select.png)\n\nThe **when timer** block can accept decimals or integer values.\n\nThe **when timer** block **cannot** accept other reporter blocks."},"when_under_attack":{"name":"when_under_attack","markdown":"# When under attack\n\nRuns the attached stack of blocks when the VR Rover is being attacked by an enemy.\n\n![when_under_attack](static/help/vr/blocks/en/when_under_attack/when_under_attack.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nA **When under attack** event will run when the VR Rover begins to take damage from an enemy in the Playground.\n\nAdd blocks beneath to have the VR Rover respond to the attack by absorbing the enemy or by moving away from the area using Action or Drivetrain commands.\n"},"while":{"name":"while","markdown":"# While\n\nA 'C' block that repeats the blocks inside while the Boolean condition is reported to be **true**.  \n\n![while](static/help/vr/blocks/en/while/while.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **while** block will only check the Boolean condition at the beginning of each loop. \n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as **true**, the blocks inside of **while** will run.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as **false**, the blocks inside of **while** will be skipped.\n\nThe **while** 'C' block can accept hexagonal (six-sided) shaped blocks as its condition.\n\n![while_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/en/while/while_inserted.png)\n\n## Switch Python Commands\n\nSwitch blocks execute Python commands typed directly into the open space of the block. \n\nTo edit the Switch Python command for **While**, set the Boolean CONDITION that is to be evaluated in the project. \n\n![switch_while_parameters](static/help/vr/blocks/en/while/switch_while_parameters.png)\n\nIf no Boolean CONDITION is set, the Switch Python command for **While** will default to `while False:`. \n\n![switch_while](static/help/vr/blocks/en/while/switch_while.png)\n\nSet the Boolean CONDITION that is to be evaluated. In this example, the VR Robot will drive forward while the Boolean CONDITION of the Distance Sensor reporting greater than 100 millimeters is **true**. \n\n![switch_while_example](static/help/vr/blocks/en/while/switch_while_example.png)"}},"python":{"actions_absorb":{"name":"actions_absorb","markdown":"# Absorb Radiation\n\nAllows the VR Rover to absorb radiation from enemies in the playground.\n\n```python\nrover.absorb_radiation(ENEMY)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo absorb radiation from an enemy, drive up to an enemy and then use the `Absorb Radiation` command.\n\nThe `Absorb Radiation` command will absorb radiation from an enemy once, draining a portion of the enemy's radiation. When an enemy's radiation is completely drained, the VR Rover's battery is partially replenished.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the robot towards an enemy in visual range and then absorb radiation from it.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.go_to(ENEMY)\nrover.absorb_radiation(ENEMY)\n```","signature":"rover.absorb_radiation(ENEMY)"},"actions_drop":{"name":"actions_drop","markdown":"# Drop\n\nAllows the VR Rover to drop carried minerals from its storage.\n\n```python\nrover.drop(MINERALS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo drop carried minerals, use the `Drop` command. Minerals can be dropped at the base to gain experience points for leveling up, or can also be dropped so that it may be used.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the robot towards a minerals object in visual range and then pick it up. It will then drive to the base before dropping the minerals object off.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.go_to(MINERALS)\nrover.pickup(MINERALS)\ndrivetrain.go_to(BASE)\nrover.drop(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"rover.drop(MINERALS)"},"actions_pickup":{"name":"actions_pickup","markdown":"# Pick Up\n\nAllows the VR Rover to pick up minerals in the playground.\n\n```python\nrover.pickup(MINERALS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo pick up minerals in the playground to then store in the VR Rover's storage, drive up to a minerals object and then use the `Pick Up` command to pick the minerals up.\n\nIt will then carry the minerals around until it is dropped.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the robot towards a minerals object in visual range and then pick it up.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.go_to(MINERALS)\nrover.pickup(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"rover.pickup(MINERALS)"},"actions_standby":{"name":"actions_standby","markdown":"# Standby\n\nAllows the VR Rover to go into a state of standby until the specified battery threshold is reached.\n\n```python\nrover.standby(THRESHOLD)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nPass a battery level at which the VR Rover should come out of standby, replacing the `THRESHOLD` parameter. The range of accepted level inputs are between **0% to 100%**.\n\nIf the passed threshold is equal or above the current battery level, the VR Rover will not enter standby.\n\nWhile in standby mode, the \"number of days survived\" counter will increase and the \"Battery in %\" counter will decrease at a higher rate.\n\nOnce the battery level threshold is reached, the VR Rover will come out of standby, going back to consuming its battery and counting the \"number of days survived\" at the normal rate.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will standby the VR Rover until it reaches 50% battery.\n\n```python\nrover.standby(50)\nbrain.print(\"Done standing by!\")\n```","signature":"rover.standby(THRESHOLD)"},"actions_use":{"name":"actions_use","markdown":"# Use\n\nAllows the VR Rover to consume minerals to recharge its energy level.\n\n```python\nrover.use(MINERALS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo consume minerals, drive up to a minerals object and then use the `Use` command to recharge the Rover's energy level. The current energy level is indicated by the battery icon.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the robot towards a minerals object in visual range and then use it.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.go_to(MINERALS)\nrover.use(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"rover.use(MINERALS)"},"control_break":{"name":"control_break","markdown":"# Break\n\nExits a repeating loop immediately.\n\n```python\nbreak\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nWhen added inside of a loop, the `break` command will exit the loop it is currently in. \n\n## Example\n\nThis project will have the Drivetrain move forward until the eye sensor detects that it is near an object.  When that Boolean condition is true, the break command exists the while loop and stops the Drivetrain.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwhile True:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    if front_eye.near_object():\n        break\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nNotice how the if statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"break"},"control_elseif_statement":{"name":"control_elseif_statement","markdown":"# Elif\nA control statement that executes one block of commands if the condition evaluates as **True**, or another block of commands if a condition evaluates as **False**. \n\n```python\nif condition:\n    pass\nelif condition2:\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `elif` statement is a conditional that will check if its condition evaluates to **True** or **False**. It is possible to combine many conditions with the use of logical operators such as `and`, `or`, and `not`. \n\nIf the totality of the condition is **True** and all previous `if` and `elif` conditions are **False**, then the code block indented below the `elif condition:` will run.\n\nCode blocks below the `elif` should be indented four spaces.\n\nAn `elif` statement should not contain empty blocks of code. If necessary, add `pass` statements to a previously empty `elif` statement. \n\n## Example\n\nThis project uses a Boolean statement with the elif statement to move the Drivetrain according to value being reported by the Eye sensor.  If the Eye sensor detects an object, the VR Robot will drive forward. If no object is detected by the Eye sensor, the robot will turn.  This project can be used with the Castle Crasher playground.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    if distance.found_object(): \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    elif not distance.found_object():\n        drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nThe elif statement only checks the Boolean statement once.  Therefore, the elif statement is placed within a while True loop, thus allowing for the Boolean statement to be repeatedly checked.  \n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops.  Because of VEXcode VR's browser-based architecture, a brief wait command (5 milliseconds) needs to be placed in each loop in order for the Python project to continue communicating with the Playground.\n\nNotice how the elif statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows.","signature":"elif condition:\\n\\tpass"},"control_for":{"name":"control_for","markdown":"# For\n\nA loop that repeatedly executes commands for a number of iterations.\n\n```python\nfor repeat_count in range(10):\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nFor loops are used to repeat a block of code for a number of iterations. Each for loop contains an iterator and either the range() function or a list\n\n\n## Examples \n\nThis project uses a for loop to draw a 500 millimeter black square on the playground.  The variable repeat_count is the iterator.  The range() function tells Python to repeat the block of code within the For loop four times.  \n\n```python\npen.move(DOWN)\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 500,MM)\n    drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nThe variable repeat_count was used to replace value.\n\nThe project uses the variable y as the iterator and the list (myList) tells Python how many times to iterate through the for loop. \n\n```python\nmyList = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", ‘100%', \"fun!!\"]\nfor y in myList:\n    brain.print(y)\n    brain.new_line()\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n```\n\nThis project will print:\n\n```python\nVEXcode VR\nis\n100%\nfun!!\n```\n\nNotice in each project that the for loop contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"for repeat_count in range (10"},"control_for_loop":{"name":"control_for_loop","markdown":"# For\n\nA type of loop that repeats the code contained in its body for a set number of iterations.\n\n```don\nfor repeat_count in range(10):\n    pass\n```\n\n# How To Use\n\nFirst, replace `repeat_count` with a variable that will iterate through the `range()` function.\n\nSecond, replace `10` with a value to specify the end of the iteration.\n\nAdd a four-spaced indentation using the spacebar to include statements as part of the `for` control statement.\n\nA `for` statement should not contain an empty block of code. If necessary, add a `pass` statement to a previously empty for statement.\n\nNote that for loops start at the index 0 instead of 1.\n\n`For` loops can also be nested inside each other.\n\n# Example\nThe following for loop will print numbers 0 to 2:\n\n```don\nfor i in range(3):\n   brain.screen.print(i)\n   brain.screen.new_line()\n```\n   \nThis will print:\n\n```\n0\n1\n2\n```","signature":"for value in range (10"},"control_if":{"name":"control_if","markdown":"# If\n\nA control statement used to execute a block of commands if the condition evaluates as **True**\n\n```python\nif condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe if statement uses Boolean statement and Boolean expressions as its conditions.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as true, the blocks inside of the if statement will run.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as false, the blocks inside of the if statement will be skipped.\n\n## Examples\n\nThis project uses a Boolean expression to first move the Drivetrain forward for five seconds. While the Drivetrain is moving forward, the pen will be drawing a line.  It then uses another Boolean expression with an if statement to change the color of the line to red after 2.5 seconds. \n\n```python\npen.move(DOWN)\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if brain.timer_time(SECONDS) > 2.5:\n        pen.set_pen_color(RED)\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n    drivetrain.stop()\n```\nA wait command needs to be used with loops.  Because of VEXcode VR's browser-based architecture, a brief wait command (5 milliseconds) needs to be placed in each loop in order for the Python project to continue communicating with the Playground.. \n\nNotice how the if statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows\n\nThis project uses Boolean statements with if statements to move the Drivetrain forward if the Eye sensor does not detect a color, to turn right if the Eye sensor detects green, and stop if the Eye sensor detects blue.  \n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n    if front_eye.detect(NONE):\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    elif front_eye.detect(GREEN):\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\n    elif front_eye.detect(BLUE):\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        drivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"if condition:\\n\\tpass"},"control_if_else":{"name":"control_if_else","markdown":"# If/else\nA control statement that executes one block of commands if the condition evaluates as **True**, or another block of commands if a condition evaluates as **False**. \n\n```python\nif condition:\n    pass\nelse:\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe if/else statement uses Boolean statement and Boolean expressions as its conditions.  \n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as true, the blocks inside of the if branch will run.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is reported as false, the blocks inside of the if branch will be skipped, and the blocks inside of the else branch will run.\n\n## Example\n\nThis project uses a Boolean statement with the if/else statement to move the Drivetrain according to value being reported by the Eye sensor.  If the Eye sensor detects an object, the VR Robot will drive forward. If no object is detected by the Eye sensor, the robot will turn.  This project can be used with the Castle Crasher playground.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    if distance.found_object(): \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    else:\n        drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nThe if/else statement only checks the Boolean statement once.  Therefore, the if/else statement is placed within a while True loop, thus allowing for the Boolean statement to be repeatedly checked.  \n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops.  Because of VEXcode VR's browser-based architecture, a brief wait command (5 milliseconds) needs to be placed in each loop in order for the Python project to continue communicating with the Playground.\n\nNotice how the if statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows.","signature":"if conditio"},"control_if_else_statement":{"name":"control_if_else_statement","markdown":"# If/else\nA control flow statement that executes one block of commands if a condition evaluates to true, or another block of commands if a condition evaluates to false. \n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\nelse:\n    pass\n\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `if` statement is a conditional that will check if its condition evaluates to **True** or **False**. It is possible to combine many conditions with the use of logical operators such as `and`, `or`, and `not`. \n\nIf the totality of the condition is **True**, then the code block indented below the `if condition:` will run. If the totality of the condition is **False**, the code block indented below the `else:` statement will run instead.\n\nCode blocks below the `if` and `else` should be indented four spaces.\n\nAn `if/else` statement should not contain empty blocks of code. If necessary, add  `pass` statements to a previously empty `if/else` statement. \n\n## Example\n\nThis `if/else` code will drive the robot FORWARD if the variable equals 1. If it does not, it will drive the robot REVERSE. \n\n```don\nif my_variable == 1:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nelse:\n    drivetrain.drive(REVERSE)\n```","signature":"if conditio"},"control_if_statement":{"name":"control_if_statement","markdown":"# If\n\nA control statement used to decide whether a block of code will be executed based on the provided `condition`.\n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\n    \n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nReplace the word `condition` with an expression that can either evaluate to `True` or `False`. An evaluation to `True` will allow the statement(s) indented below the `if` statement to execute.\n\nAdd a four-spaced indentation using the **spacebar** to include statements as part of the `if` control statement. \n\nAn `if` statement should not contain an empty block of code. If necessary, add a `pass` statement to a previously empty `if` statement. \n\n## Example\n\nThis `if` statement will run if `my_variable` equals 1, causing the robot to drive forward.\n\n```don\nif my_variable == 1:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"if condition:\\n\\tpass"},"control_stop_project":{"name":"control_stop_project","markdown":"# Stop Project\n\nStops a running project.\n\n```python\nstop_project()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command will cause a running project to stop.\n\n## Example\n\nThis project draws a 500 millimeter black square on the playground.  The stop_project command stops the project after the square has been completed.\n\n```python\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 500,MM)\n    drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\nstop_project()\n```","signature":"stop_project()"},"control_wait":{"name":"control_wait","markdown":"# Wait\n\nWait for a specific amount of time before moving to the next command.\n\n```don\nwait(TIME, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nSet the amount of time to have a project wait before moving to the next command. The amount can be in seconds or milliseconds.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will stop the Drivetrain after it has driven forward for three seconds.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\nNote that the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters.","signature":"wait(1, SECONDS)"},"control_wait_until":{"name":"control_wait_until","markdown":"# While Not\nA while not loop repeatedly executes commands as long as the condition evaluates as **True**.  A while not loop blocks the project flow - make it similar to the wait until. \n\nThis allows a project to continually check the condition of the sensor and block the flow of a project until that sensor condition is met.  \n\n\n```don\nwhile not condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nPython does not contain a wait until command (like the one found in VEXcode’s block-based mode), therefore, the while not loop is referenced here.  A while not loop in Python can be used similarly to a wait until block in Python.  For example:\n\nIn both of these examples, the Drivetrain will drive forward until the Bumper sensor is pressed.\n\nThis same behavior can also be done with a while loop.  For example:\n\n```don\nwhile left_bumper.pressed() == False:\n    drive.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nThis will also result in the Drivetrain driving forward until the Bumper sensor is pressed.","signature":"while not expressio"},"control_while":{"name":"control_while","markdown":"# While\nA loop that repeatedly executes the code in its body as long as the condition expression evaluates to **true**.   \n\n```python\nwhile condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe while loop uses Boolean statements and Boolean expressions as its conditions.  \n\nThe while loop will only check the Boolean condition once at the beginning of the  loop.\n\nIf the Boolean condition is evaluated as TRUE, the blocks inside the while loop will run once before checking the condition again.  \n\nIf the Boolean condition is evaluated as FALSE, the blocks inside of the while loop will be skipped.\n\n\n## Examples\n\nThis project uses a Boolean expression to move the Drivetrain forward while the value of the Distance sensor is greater than 50 millimeters.  \n\n```python\nwhile distance.get_distance(MM) > 50:\n    drive.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nThis project uses a Boolean statement to move the Drivetrain forward until the Bumper sensor is pressed.\n\n```python\nwhile right_bumper.pressed() == False:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops.  Because of VEXcode VR's browser-based architecture, a brief wait command (5 milliseconds) needs to be placed in each loop in order for the Python project to continue communicating with the Playground.\n\nNotice how the while loop contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"while expressio"},"control_while_loop":{"name":"control_while_loop","markdown":"# While\nA loop that repeats the code in its body as long as the condition expression evaluates to **true**.   \n\n```python\nwhile condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `while` loop will only check the condition inside the parenthesis at the beginning of each loop. \n\nIf the condition evaluates to **true**, the code inside of `while` will run.\n\nIf the condition evaluates to **false**, the code inside of `while` will be skipped.\n\nThe `while` loop accepts anything that evaluates to `True` or `False` as its condition. You can place Booleans as well as expressions using comparison and logical operators. \n\nYou can also use numerical values; **0** evaluates to `False`, and any **non-zero numbers** evaluates to `True`. \n\nAdd a four-spaced indentation using the **spacebar** to include statements as part of the `while` control statement. \n\nA `while` statement should not contain an empty block of code. If necessary, add a `pass` statement to a previously empty `while` statement. \n\n## Example\n\nThis `while` loop will continue to print the word \"Hello\" on separate lines when the timer value is less than 50 seconds.\n\n```python\nwhile brain.timer.time(SECONDS) < 50:\n    brain.screen.print(\"Hello\")\n    brain.screen.next_row()\n```","signature":"while expressio"},"drivetrain_calibrate_drivetrain":{"name":"drivetrain_calibrate_drivetrain","markdown":"# Drivetrain Calibrate\n\nCalibrates the Drivetrain's Inertial, GPS, or 3-Wire Gyro Sensor based on which device is currently configured with the Drivetrain.\n\n```python\ncalibrate_drivetrain()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Drivetrain Calibrate` command will calibrate the Drivetrain's Inertial, GPS, or 3-Wire Gyro Sensor. The Drivetrain must remain stationary during the calibration process.","signature":"calibrate_drivetrain()"},"drivetrain_drive":{"name":"drivetrain_drive","markdown":"# Drive\n\nMoves the Drivetrain forever in the direction specified inside the parentheses.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.drive()` command will run the Drivetrain forever, until a new drivetrain command is used, or the project is stopped.\n\nThe default velocity for `drivetrain.drive()` is 50%.\n\n## Examples\n\nThis command will move the Drivetrain forever forward.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nThis command will drive the Drivetrain forever backwards.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(REVERSE)\n```\n\nNotice that the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters","signature":"drivetrain.drive(FOWARD)"},"drivetrain_drive_for":{"name":"drivetrain_drive_for","markdown":"# Drive For\n\nMoves the Drivetrain for a given distance.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(DIRECTION, distance, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nSet the distance and the direction that the Drivetrain will move by providing each of the following:\n\n- Direction - either `FORWARD` or `REVERSE`\n- Distance - a numeric value\n- Units - either `MM`(millimeters) or `INCHES`\n\n## Examples\nThis command will move the Drivetrain forward for 200 millimeters.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n```\n\nThis command will move the Drivetrain backwards for 150 inches.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 150, INCHES)\n```\n\nNotice the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters.","signature":"drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)"},"drivetrain_drive_to":{"name":"drivetrain_drive_to","markdown":"# Drive To\n\nDrives the VR Rover to a specified object.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.drive_to(OBJECT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nReplace the `OBJECT` parameter with one of the following options to drive towards.\n\n- MINERALS\n- ENEMY\n- BASE\n\nIf a minerals or enemy is not detected in the VR Rover's visual range when the `Drive To` command is called, the robot will not drive.\n\n## Optional Parameter\n\nYou can set a second parameter to `wait=False` to allow proceeding commands to run even before the Drivetrain is done driving.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.drive_to(MINERALS, wait=False)\nbrain.print(\"Not waiting\")\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the robot towards a minerals object in visual range and then pick it up.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to(MINERALS)\ndrivetrain.drive_to(MINERALS)\nrover.pickup(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.drive_to(OBJECT)"},"drivetrain_go_to":{"name":"drivetrain_go_to","markdown":"# Drive Go To\n\nTurns and drives the VR Rover to a specified object.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.go_to(OBJECT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nReplace the `OBJECT` parameter with one of the following options to turn and drive towards.\n\n- MINERALS\n- ENEMY\n- BASE\n\nIf a minerals or enemy is not detected in the VR Rover's visual range when the `Drive Go To` command is called, the robot will not drive.\n\nHowever, when the **BASE** option is passed, the robot will always turn towards the base and then drive towards it.\n\n## Optional Parameter\n\nYou can set a second parameter to `wait=False` to allow proceeding commands to run even before the Drivetrain is done driving.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.go_to(MINERALS, wait=False)\nbrain.print(\"Not waiting\")\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the robot towards a minerals object in visual range and then pick it up.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.go_to(MINERALS)\nrover.pickup(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.go_to(OBJECT)"},"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Set Drive Velocity\n\nSets the velocity of the Drivetrain\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()` command accepts a range form 0% to 100%. The default Drivetrain velocity is 50%.\n\n## Examples\n\nThis example project will move the Drivetrain forward for 200 millimeters at a velocity of 25%\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(25, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n```\nNote the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters","signature":"drivetrain.set_drive_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity_virtual":{"name":"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity_virtual","markdown":"# Set Drive Velocity\n\nSets the velocity of the Drivetrain for `drive` and `drive_for` commands.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.set_drive_velocity` will set the Drivetrain's velocity when used with a `drive` or `drive_for` command. It will not cause the Drivetrain to move when used by itself.\n\n`drivetrain.set_drive_velocity` accepts a range of values depending on what units are passed as the second parameter.\n\nIf `PERCENT` is used as the second parameter, **VELOCITY** accepts a range from **-100 to 100**.\n\n`drivetrain.set_drive_velocity` can also use `RPM` as a valid **UNITS** parameter. `drivetrain.set_drive_velocity` will accept a \"rotations per minute\" (RPM) value between -200 and 200.\n\nSetting a Drivetrain's drive velocity to a negative value will cause the Drivetrain to drive opposite of the `DIRECTION` passed into a `drive` or `drive_for` command.\n\nSetting a Drivetrain's drive velocity to 0 will prevent the Drivetrain from moving even if a `drive` or `drive_for` command is used.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example shows that the Drivetrain will go in reverse due to a negative value.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(-100, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nThis example will prevent the Drivetrain from moving.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(0, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.set_drive_velocity(VELOCITY, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_set_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_set_heading","markdown":"# Set Heading\n\nSets the Drivetrain's Gyro heading value.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_heading(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.set_heading()` command can be used to set the Drivetrain's position to any given heading. This command can be used to reset the orientation of the Drivetrain's Gyro when the heading is set to a value of 0.\n\nThe `drivetrain.set_heading()` command accepts a range of **0 to 360** degrees.","signature":"drivetrain.set_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_set_rotation","markdown":"# Set Rotation\n\nSets the drivetrain's angle of rotation.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.set_rotation()` command can be used to set the Drivetrain's angle of rotation to any given positive or negative value.","signature":"drivetrain.set_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_stopping":{"name":"drivetrain_set_stopping","markdown":"# Set Stopping\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_stopping(MODE)\n```\n\n## How to Use\n\nReplace the `MODE` parameter with one of the following options:\n\n* BRAKE: will cause the Drivetrain to come to an immediate stop.\n* COAST: lets the Drivetrain slow gradually to a stop.\n* HOLD: will cause the Drivetrain to come to an immediate stop, and returns it to its stopped position if moved. \n\nThe brake mode applied by the `drivetrain.set_stopping()` command will be used for subsequent Drivetrain commands in the program unless otherwise changed.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example shows the robot driving forward for 200 MM before coasting to a stop.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_stopping(COAST)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.set_stopping(BRAKE)"},"drivetrain_set_timeout":{"name":"drivetrain_set_timeout","markdown":"# Set Timeout\n\nSets a time limit for the Drivetrain movement commands.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_timeout(TIME, SECONDS)\n```\n\n## How to Use\n\nThe command is used to prevent movement commands that do not reach their position from preventing other proceeding commands from running.\n\nSpecify the amount of time in `SECONDS`.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below sets the timeout of the Drivetrain to 3 seconds.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_timeout(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 1000, MM)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.set_timeout(TIME, SECONDS)"},"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Set Turn Velocity\n\nSets the velocity of the Drivetrain's turns.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\nThe `drivetrain.set_turn_velocity()` command accepts a range from 0% to 100%. The default velocity is 50%.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example project will turn the Drivetrain right for 90 degrees at a velocity of 25%\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(25, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```\n\nNote that the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters","signature":"drivetrain.set_turn_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity_percent":{"name":"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity_percent","markdown":"# Set Turn Velocity\n\nSets the Drivetrain's turn velocity.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.set_turn_velocity` will set the velocity of the Drivetrain when turning, but will not cause the Drivetrain to move when used without a `turn` or `turn_for` command.\n\nThe second parameter, **VELOCITY** accepts a range from **-100 to 100**.\n\nSetting a Drivetrain's turn velocity to 0 will prevent the Drivetrain from turning, even when using a `turn` or `turn_for` command.\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the Drivetrain will turn in the opposite direction (LEFT) due to a negative value.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(-100, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)"},"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity_percent_virtual":{"name":"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity_percent_virtual","markdown":"# Set Turn Velocity\n\nSets the Drivetrain's turn velocity.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.set_turn_velocity` will set the velocity of the Drivetrain when turning, but will not cause the Drivetrain to move when used without a `turn` or `turn_for` command.\n\nThe second parameter, **VELOCITY** accepts a range from **-100 to 100**.\n\nSetting a Drivetrain's turn velocity to 0 will prevent the Drivetrain from turning, even when using a `turn` or `turn_for` command.\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the Drivetrain will turn in the opposite direction (LEFT) due to a negative value.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(-100, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)"},"drivetrain_stop":{"name":"drivetrain_stop","markdown":"# Stop\n\nStops the Drivetrain.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.stop()` command will cause the robot to come to a complete stop. The `drivetrain.stop()` command does not require any parameters inside of its parenthesis.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example project will stops the Drivetrain after it moves forward for 3 seconds. \n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nNotice the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters.","signature":"drivetrain.stop()"},"drivetrain_turn":{"name":"drivetrain_turn","markdown":"# Turn\n\nTurns the Drivetrain forever to the right or left.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.turn()` command will turn the Drivetrain forever until a new drivetrain command is used, or the project is stopped.\n\n## Examples\n\nThis command will turn the Drivetrain right forever.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n```\n\nThis command will turn the Drivetrain left forever.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(LEFT)\n```\n\nNotice that the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters.","signature":"drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)"},"drivetrain_turn_for":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_for","markdown":"# Turn For\n\nTurns the Drivetrain for a given angle.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(DIRECTION, ANGLE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nSet the angle and the direction that the Drivetrain will turn by providing each of the following:\n\n- Direction - either RIGHT or LEFT\n- Angle - a numeric value\n- Units - DEGREES\n\n## Examples\n\nThis command will turn the Drivetrain right for 90 degrees.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```\n\nThis example will turn the Drivetrain left for 90 degrees.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to","markdown":"# Turn To\n\nTurns the VR Rover to a specified object.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to(OBJECT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nReplace the `OBJECT` parameter with one of the following options to turn towards.\n\n- MINERALS\n- ENEMY\n- BASE\n\nIf a minerals or enemy is not detected in the VR Rover's visual range when the `Turn To` command is called, the robot will not turn.\n\n## Optional Parameter\n\nYou can set a second parameter to `wait=False` to allow proceeding commands to run even before the Drivetrain is done driving.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to(MINERALS, wait=False)\nbrain.print(\"Not waiting\")\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will turn the robot towards a minerals object in visual range and then drive towards and pick it up.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to(MINERALS)\ndrivetrain.drive_to(MINERALS)\nrover.pickup(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turnto(OBJECT)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Turn To Heading\n\nTurns a Drivetrain to a specific heading(angle) using the built in Gyro sensor.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(ANGLE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` command can be used to turn the Drivetrain to any given clockwise heading.\n\nBased on the current heading of the Gyro sensor, the  `drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` command will determine which direction to turn.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` accepts a range of **0.00 to 359.99** degrees.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` can accept decimals or integers.\n\n## Examples\n\nThis command will turn the Drivetrain right 45 degrees\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(45, DEGREES)\n```\n\nThis command will turn the Drivetrain left 45 degrees\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(315, DEGREES)\n```\nNote that the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters.","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Turn To Rotation\n\nTurns a Drivetrain to a specific angle of rotation using the built in Gyro sensor.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(ANGLE, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation()` command can be used to turn the Drivetrain to a given positive (clockwise) or negative (counter-clockwise) value.\n\nBased on the current **rotation** value of the Drivetrain, `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation()` will determine which direction to turn.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_rotation()` can accept decimals or integers.\n\n## Examples\n\nThis command will turn the Drivetrain to 90 degrees\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES)\n```\n\nThis command will turn the Drivetrain to -45 degrees\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(-45, DEGREES)\n```\n\nNote the parameter in each command is written in capitalized letters.","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"events_brain_screen_pressed":{"name":"events_brain_screen_pressed","markdown":"#  Brain Screen Pressed\n\nRuns the callback function when the V5 Brain’s touchscreen is pressed.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.pressed(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.pressed()` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors based on touch interaction with the V5 Brain's touchscreen. \n\nUsed together with the `brain.screen.x_position()` and `brain.screen.y_position()` commands, users can create custom interfaces to their V5 Brain by detecting when and where touches occur on the screen.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the screen gets pressed. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_event():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Event has occurred\")\n  \n# Register event with a callback function\nbrain.screen.pressed(run_on_event)\n\n```","signature":"brain.screen.pressed(callback)"},"events_brain_screen_released":{"name":"events_brain_screen_released","markdown":"#  Brain Screen Released\n\nRuns the callback function when the V5 Brain’s touchscreen is released.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.released(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.released()` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors based on touch interaction with the V5 Brain's touchscreen. \n\nUsed together with the `brain.screen.x_position()` and `brain.screen.y_position()` commands, users can create custom interfaces to their V5 Brain by detecting when and where touches occur on the screen.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the screen gets released. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_event():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Event has occurred\")\n  \n# Register event with a callback function\nbrain.screen.released(run_on_event)\n```","signature":"brain.screen.released(callback)"},"events_brain_timer_event":{"name":"events_brain_timer_event","markdown":"# Brain Timer Event\n\nRuns the specified callback function when the Brain's timer is greater than the given value.\n\n```python\nbrain.timer_event(callback, 1000)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Brain's timer begins at the beginning of each project.\n\nSpecify the amount of time. The `brain.timer.event()` event will run once the Brain's timer is **greater than** the entered amount.\n\nThe `brain.timer_event()` command can accept integer values for the number of milliseconds.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the timer is greater than the entered amount. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_event():\n    brain.print(\"Event has occurred\")\n  \n# Register event with a callback function\nbrain.timer_event(run_on_event, 2000)\n```","signature":"brain.timer.event(callback, 1000)"},"events_brain_timer_viqc":{"name":"events_brain_timer_viqc","markdown":"# Timer Event\n\nRuns the specified callback function when the Brain's timer is greater than the specified time value.\n\n```python\nbrain.timer.event(callback, time)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Brain's timer begins at the start of each program.\n\nThe `Timer Event` function takes 2 parameters.\n\nThe first is the **callback function** parameter. A function will need to be created to pass to the `Timer Event` function.\n\nThe second parameter is the time at which the function will run. The **callback function** will run once the Brain's timer is greater than the passed value. The amount of **time** should be in **milliseconds**.\n\n```python\n# Call the callback function after 1000 milliseconds\nbrain.timer.event(callback, 1000)\n```\n\n## Callback Functions\n\nA callback function is a function passed into another function as an argument. The code inside the **callback function** will run whenever the **event** occurs.\n\nIn the snippet below, \"Callback Function Called\" will be printed to the VEX IQ's Brain Screen after 10 seconds.\n\n```python\ndef callback_function():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Timer Elapsed\")\n\nbrain.timer.event(callback_function, 10000)\n```","signature":"brain.timer.event(callback, time)"},"events_bumper_pressed":{"name":"events_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Bumper Pressed\n\nRuns the specified function when the bumper is pressed.\n\n```python\nbumper.on_pressed(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the Bumper Sensor is pressed. \n\nProvide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_bumper_pressed():\n    brain.print(\"Bumper pressed!\")\n \n# Register event with a callback function\nright_bumper.on_pressed(run_on_bumper_pressed)\n```","signature":"bumper.on_pressed(callback)"},"events_bumper_released":{"name":"events_bumper_released","markdown":"# Bumper Released\n\nRuns the specified function when the bumper is released.\n\n```python\nbumper.on_released(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the Bumper Sensor is released. \n\nProvide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_bumper_released():\n    brain.print(\"Bumper released!\")\n \n# Register event with a callback function\nright_bumper.on_released(run_on_bumper_released)\n```","signature":"bumper.on_released(callback)"},"events_competition_autonomous":{"name":"events_competition_autonomous","markdown":"# Competition Autonomous\n\nRuns the specified function when an \"autonomous\" mode signal is received from a competition switch.\n\n```python\ndef autonomous():\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nA new competition instance can be created by passing a **driver_control** and **autonomous** callback functions.\n\nCreate an **autonomous** function to pass to a competition instance. \n\nThe specified **autonomous** function will run when an \"autonomous\" mode signal is received from a competition switch.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the \"driver control\" signal is received\ndef driver_control():\n    brain.screen.print(\"driver control signal received\")\n\n# Function to run when the \"autonomous\" signal is received\ndef autonomous():\n    brain.screen.print(\"autonomous signal received\")\n\n# Create a new Competition instance\ncompetition = Competition(driver_control, autonomous)\n```","signature":"def autonomous():\\n\\tpass"},"events_competition_drivercontrol":{"name":"events_competition_drivercontrol","markdown":"# Competition Driver Control\n\nRuns the specified function when a \"driver control\" mode signal is received from a competition switch.\n\n```python\ndef driver_control():\n    pass\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nA new competition instance can be created by passing a **driver_control** and **autonomous** callback functions.\n\nCreate a **driver_control** function to pass to a competition instance. \n\nThe specified **driver_control** function will run when a \"driver control\" mode signal is received from a competition switch.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the \"driver control\" signal is received\ndef driver_control():\n    brain.screen.print(\"driver control signal received\")\n\n# Function to run when the \"autonomous\" signal is received\ndef autonomous():\n    brain.screen.print(\"autonomous signal received\")\n\n# Create a new Competition instance\ncompetition = Competition(driver_control, autonomous)\n```","signature":"def driver_control():\\n\\tpass"},"events_competition_new_competition":{"name":"events_competition_new_competition","markdown":"# New Competition\n\nCreate a new competition instance.\n\n```python\ncompetition = Competition(driver_control, autonomous)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nA new competition instance can be created by passing a **driver_control** and **autonomous** callback functions.\n\nWhen a **driver control** signal is received from a competition switch, the **driver_control** callback function will be executed.\n\nSimilarly, when an **autonomous** signal is received from a competition switch, the **autonomous** callback function will be executed.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the \"driver control\" signal is received\ndef driver_control():\n    brain.screen.print(\"driver control signal received\")\n\n# Function to run when the \"autonomous\" signal is received\ndef autonomous():\n    brain.screen.print(\"autonomous signal received\")\n\n# Create a new Competition instance\ncompetition = Competition(driver_control, autonomous)\n```","signature":"competition = Competition(driver_control, autonomous)"},"events_controller_axis_changed":{"name":"events_controller_axis_changed","markdown":"# Controller Axis Changed\n\nRuns the specified function when the controller axis position changes.\n\n```python\ncontroller.axis.changed(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Controller axis to monitor.\n\n* `axis1`\n* `axis2`\n* `axis3`\n* `axis4`\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the Controller Axis position is changed. \n\nProvide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_axis_changed():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Axis changed!\")\n\n  \n# Register event with a callback function.\ncontroller_1.axis1.changed(run_on_axis_changed)\n```","signature":"controller.axis.changed(callback)"},"events_controller_button_pressed":{"name":"events_controller_button_pressed","markdown":"# Controller Button Pressed\n\nRuns the specified function when the controller button is pressed.\n\n```python\ncontroller.button.pressed(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Controller Button to monitor.\n\n* `buttonA`\n* `buttonB`\n* `buttonX`\n* `buttonY`\n* `buttonUp`\n* `buttonDown`\n* `buttonLeft`\n* `buttonRight`\n* `buttonL1`\n* `buttonL2`\n* `buttonR1`\n* `buttonR2`\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the Controller Button is pressed. \n\nProvide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_button_pressed():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Button pressed!\")\n\n# Register event with a callback function.\ncontroller_1.buttonA.pressed(run_on_button_pressed)\n```","signature":"controller.button.pressed(callback)"},"events_controller_button_released":{"name":"events_controller_button_released","markdown":"# Controller Button Released\n\nRuns the specified function when the controller button is released.\n\n```python\ncontroller.button.released(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which Controller Button to monitor.\n\n* `buttonA`\n* `buttonB`\n* `buttonX`\n* `buttonY`\n* `buttonUp`\n* `buttonDown`\n* `buttonLeft`\n* `buttonRight`\n* `buttonL1`\n* `buttonL2`\n* `buttonR1`\n* `buttonR2`\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the Controller Button is released. \n\nProvide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_button_released():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Button released!\")\n\n# Register event with a callback function.\ncontroller_1.buttonA.released(run_on_button_released)\n```","signature":"controller.button.released(callback)"},"events_event":{"name":"events_event","markdown":"# Event Register Callback\n\nRegister a callback function to run when an event is triggered.\n\n```python\nevent(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nRegister a callback to a user-defined event.\n\n```python\ndef run_on_broadcast():\n    brain.print(\"my_event triggered run_on_broadcast callback\")\n  \n# Create a new event.\nmy_event = Event()\n\n# Register a callback function to the myEvent event.\n# Wait to allow events to register\nmy_event(run_on_broadcast)\nwait(15, MSEC)\n\n# Broadcast the event.\nmy_event.broadcast()\n```","signature":"EVENT(callback);"},"events_event_broadcast":{"name":"events_event_broadcast","markdown":"# Event Broadcast\n\nBroadcast event message to run listener functions.\n\n```python\nevent.broadcast()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe code will continue to run the commands after the `event.broadcast()` command so that it will run at the same time as the event listener functions.\n\nBefore using this command, a user defined event must be created and register at least one callback.\n\n```python\ndef run_on_broadcast1():\n    brain.print(\"Broadcast1 Running\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\ndef run_on_broadcast2():\n    brain.print(\"Broadcast2 Running\")\n    brain.new_line()\n  \n# Create a new event.\nmy_event = Event()\n\n# Register callback functions to event.\n# Wait to allow events to register\nmy_event(run_on_broadcast1)\nmy_event(run_on_broadcast2)\nwait(15, MSEC)\n\n# Broadcast the event.\nmy_event.broadcast()\n\n```","signature":"my_event.broadcast()"},"events_event_broadcast_and_wait":{"name":"events_event_broadcast_and_wait","markdown":"# Event Broadcast and Wait\n\nBroadcast event message to run listener functions and wait for all listeners to finish before continuing.\n\n```cpp\nevent.broadcast_and_wait()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `event.broadcast_and_wait()` command will wait for all listeners to complete before returning. This will pause the code execution in the current function, until all the broadcast listeners have finished.\n\nBefore using this command, a user defined event must be created and register at least one callback.\n\n```python\ndef run_on_broadcast1():\n    brain.print(\"Broadcast1 Running\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\ndef run_on_broadcast2():\n    brain.print(\"Broadcast2 Running\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\n# Create a new event.\nmy_event = Event()\n\n# Register callback functions to event.\n# Wait to allow events to register\nmy_event(run_on_broadcast1)\nmy_event(run_on_broadcast2)\nwait(15, MSEC)\n\n# Broadcast the event and wait for completion.\nmy_event.broadcast_and_wait()\n\nbrain.print(\"Listeners finished running\")\n```","signature":"my_event.broadcast_and_wait()"},"events_event_new_event":{"name":"events_event_new_event","markdown":"# New Event\n\nCreate a new event.\n\n```python\nmy_event = Event()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nUsing the above syntax, a new user-defined event can be created before registering callback functions.\n\n```python\ndef run_on_broadcast1():\n    brain.print(\"Broadcast1 Running\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\ndef run_on_broadcast2():\n    brain.print(\"Broadcast2 Running\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\n# Create new events.\nmy_event1 = Event()\nmy_event2 = Event()\n\n# Register callback functions to the events.\n# Wait to allow events to register\nmy_event1(run_on_broadcast1)\nmy_event2(run_on_broadcast2)\nwait(15, MSEC)\n\n# Broadcast the events.\nmy_event1.broadcast()\nmy_event2.broadcast()\n```","signature":"my_event = Event()"},"events_eye_object_detected":{"name":"events_eye_object_detected","markdown":"# Eye Object Detected\n\nRuns the callback function when an object is detected by the Eye Sensor.\n\n```python\neye_sensor.object_detected(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `eye_sensor.object_detected()` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when an object is detected by an Eye Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when an object is detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\nChoose which Eye Sensor to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_object_detected():\n    brain.print(\"Object detected!\")\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\nfront_eye.object_detected(run_on_object_detected)\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.object_detected(callback)"},"events_eye_object_lost":{"name":"events_eye_object_lost","markdown":"# Eye Object Lost\n\nRuns the callback function when an object is no longer being detected by the Eye Sensor.\n\n```python\neye_sensor.object_lost(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `eye_sensor.object_lost()` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when an object is no longer being detected by an Eye Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when an object is no longer being detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\nChoose which Eye Sensor to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_object_lost():\n    brain.print(\"Object lost!\")\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\nfront_eye.object_lost(run_on_object_lost)\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.object_lost(callback)"},"events_gps_heading_changed":{"name":"events_gps_heading_changed","markdown":"# GPS When Heading Changed\n\nRuns the specified callback function when a GPS Sensor detects a heading change.\n\n```python\ngps.changed(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nYou will need to define a function to call when the GPS Sensor detects a heading change.\n\nProvide the name of the defined function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_heading_change():\n    brain.screen.print(\"GPS Heading Change Detected\")\n\n# Register event with a callback function\ngps.changed(run_on_heading_change)\n```","signature":"gps.changed(callback)"},"events_limit_pressed":{"name":"events_limit_pressed","markdown":"# Limit Pressed\n\nRuns the specified function when the Limit Switch is pressed.\n\n```python\nlimit.pressed(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the Limit Switch is pressed. \n\nProvide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_limit_pressed():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Limit pressed!\")\n \n# Register event with a callback function\nlimit_a.pressed(run_on_limit_pressed)\n```","signature":"limit.pressed(callback)"},"events_limit_released":{"name":"events_limit_released","markdown":"# Limit Released\n\nRuns the specified function when the Limit Switch is released.\n\n```python\nlimit.released(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the Limit Switch is released. \n\nProvide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the `callback` parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_limit_released():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Limit released!\")\n \n# Register event with a callback function\nlimit_a.released(run_on_limit_released)\n```","signature":"limit.released(callback)"},"events_optical_detect_gesture_down":{"name":"events_optical_detect_gesture_down","markdown":"# Optical Gesture Detected\n\nRuns the callback function when gesture is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.gesture_down(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Gesture Detected` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when a gesture is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when a gesture is detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\nYou will also need to ensure that the Optical Sensor is set to detect gestures by calling `optical.gesture_enable()` before gesture commands are used.\n\n`Optical Gesture Detected` can be used to detect four different gestures:\n\n- `gesture_up`\n- `gesture_down`\n- `gesture_left`\n- `gesture_right`\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_gesture_detected():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Gesture detected!\")\n\n# Set the Optical Sensor to detect gestures\noptical.gesture_enable()\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\noptical.gesture_down(run_on_gesture_detected)\n```","signature":"optical.gesture_down(callback)"},"events_optical_detect_gesture_left":{"name":"events_optical_detect_gesture_left","markdown":"# Optical Gesture Detected\n\nRuns the callback function when gesture is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.gesture_left(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Gesture Detected` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when a gesture is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when a gesture is detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\nYou will also need to ensure that the Optical Sensor is set to detect gestures by calling `optical.gesture_enable()` before gesture commands are used.\n\n`Optical Gesture Detected` can be used to detect four different gestures:\n\n- `gesture_up`\n- `gesture_down`\n- `gesture_left`\n- `gesture_right`\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_gesture_detected():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Gesture detected!\")\n\n# Set the Optical Sensor to detect gestures\noptical.gesture_enable()\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\noptical.gesture_left(run_on_gesture_detected)\n```","signature":"optical.gesture_left(callback)"},"events_optical_detect_gesture_right":{"name":"events_optical_detect_gesture_right","markdown":"# Optical Gesture Detected\n\nRuns the callback function when gesture is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.gesture_right(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Gesture Detected` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when a gesture is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when a gesture is detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\nYou will also need to ensure that the Optical Sensor is set to detect gestures by calling `optical.gesture_enable()` before gesture commands are used.\n\n`Optical Gesture Detected` can be used to detect four different gestures:\n\n- `gesture_up`\n- `gesture_down`\n- `gesture_left`\n- `gesture_right`\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_gesture_detected():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Gesture detected!\")\n\n# Set the Optical Sensor to detect gestures\noptical.gesture_enable()\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\noptical.gesture_right(run_on_gesture_detected)\n```","signature":"optical.gesture_right(callback)"},"events_optical_detect_gesture_up":{"name":"events_optical_detect_gesture_up","markdown":"# Optical Gesture Detected\n\nRuns the callback function when gesture is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.gesture_up(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Gesture Detected` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when a gesture is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when a gesture is detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\nYou will also need to ensure that the Optical Sensor is set to detect gestures by calling `optical.gesture_enable()` before gesture commands are used.\n\n`Optical Gesture Detected` can be used to detect four different gestures:\n\n- `gesture_up`\n- `gesture_down`\n- `gesture_left`\n- `gesture_right`\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_gesture_detected():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Gesture detected!\")\n\n# Set the Optical Sensor to detect gestures\noptical.gesture_enable()\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\noptical.gesture_up(run_on_gesture_detected)\n```","signature":"optical.gesture_up(callback)"},"events_optical_object_detected":{"name":"events_optical_object_detected","markdown":"# Optical Object Detected\n\nRuns the callback function when an object is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.object_detected(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `optical.object_detected` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when an object is detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when an object is detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_object_detected():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Object detected!\")\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\noptical.object_detected(run_on_object_detected)\n```","signature":"optical.object_detected(callback)"},"events_optical_object_detected_viqc":{"name":"events_optical_object_detected_viqc","markdown":"# Optical Object Detected\n\nRuns the callback function when an object is detected by an Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.object_detected(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Object Detected` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when an object is detected by an Optical Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when an object is detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_event():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Object detected!\")\n\n# Register event with a callback function\noptical.object_detected(run_on_event)\n```","signature":"optical.object_detected(callback)"},"events_optical_object_lost":{"name":"events_optical_object_lost","markdown":"# Optical Object Lost\n\nRuns the callback function when an object is no longer being detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.object_lost(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `optical.object_lost` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when an object is no longer being detected by a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when an object is no longer being detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_object_lost():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Object lost!\")\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\noptical.object_lost(run_on_object_lost)\n```","signature":"optical.object_lost(callback)"},"events_optical_object_lost_viqc":{"name":"events_optical_object_lost_viqc","markdown":"# Optical Object Lost\n\nRuns the callback function when a detected object is no longer being detected by an Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.object_lost(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Object Lost` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when an Optical Sensor no longer detects a previously-detected object.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when the object is no longer being detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_event():\n    brain.screen.print(\"Object lost!\")\n\n# Register event with a callback function\noptical.object_lost(run_on_event)\n```","signature":"optical.object_lost(callback)"},"events_rover_when_level_up":{"name":"events_rover_when_level_up","markdown":"# When Level Up\n\nRuns the callback function when the VR Rover moves from one level to the next.\n\n```python\nrover.on_level_up(callback)  \n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `When Level Up` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when the VR Rover moves from one level to the next.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when a level transition occurs. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below will print the VR Rover's new backpack capacity when it levels up.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_level_up():\n    brain.print(\"Capacity:\", rover.storage_capacity())\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\nrover.on_level_up(run_on_level_up)\n\n# Brief wait to make sure the event was properly registered\nwait(0.15, SECONDS)\n```","signature":"rover.on_level_up(callback)"},"events_rover_when_under_attack":{"name":"events_rover_when_under_attack","markdown":"# When Under Attack\n\nRuns the callback function when the VR Rover takes damage from an enemy.\n\n```python\nrover.on_under_attack(callback)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `When Under Attack` command can be used to trigger actions or behaviors when the VR Rover takes damage from an enemy.\n\nYou will need to create a function to call when damage is detected. Provide the name of the function that should run when the event occurs as the **callback** parameter.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below will stop the VR Rover and absorb radiation from an enemy when damage is detected.\n\n```python\n# Function to run when the event occurs\ndef run_on_under_attack():\n    drivetrain.stop()\n    while True:\n        rover.absorb_radiation(ENEMY)\n\n# Register an event with a callback function\nrover.on_under_attack(run_on_under_attack)\n\n# Brief wait to make sure the event was properly registered\nwait(0.15, SECONDS)\n\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"rover.on_under_attack(callback)"},"function_name":{"name":"function_name","markdown":"# Functions\n\nFunctions are blocks of code used to perform an action.\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo create a function, create a meaningful function name. This means that the function name should accurately describe what it does.\n\nUse the **keyword** `def` before your function name to define the function.\n\nInclude parentheses `()` enclosing optional parameters after the function name, followed by a colon`:`. Multiple parameters should be separated by commas `(parameter1, parameter2)`.\n\nThe body of the function should be indented four spaces. If there isn't any code for the function to execute, a `pass` should be included in the function definition.\n\n```don\ndef my_function():\n    pass\n```\n\n## Example 1\n\nThis function has no parameters and will print \"Hello\" to the Print Console.\n\n```don\ndef print_hello_world():\n    brain.print(\"Hello World\")\n```\n## Example 2\n\nThis function takes one parameter, `name` and will print the value of `name` to the Print Console.\n\n```don\ndef print_name(name):\n    brain.print(name)\n```","signature":"def function_name(parameters):\\n\\tpass"},"looks_brain_clear":{"name":"looks_brain_clear","markdown":"# Clear\n\nClears all the rows in the Print Console.\n\n```don\nbrain.clear()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.clear` command will clear all of the rows in the Print Console. \n\n## Example\n\nThis project will print Hello for one second and then clear rows in the Print Console before printing Goodbye. \n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nbrain.clear()\nbrain.print(\"Goodbye\")\n```","signature":"brain.clear()"},"looks_brain_new_line":{"name":"looks_brain_new_line","markdown":"# New Line\n\nSets the cursor to a new line in the Print Console.\n\n```don\nbrain.new_line()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe brain.new_line() command will set the cursor to the next row in the Print Console. \n\n## Example\n\nThis project will print Drive, then on the next line in the display, it will print Forward.\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Drive\")\nbrain.new_line()\nbrain.print(\"Forward\")\n```","signature":"brain.new_line()"},"looks_brain_print":{"name":"looks_brain_print","markdown":"# Print\n\nPrints values or text in the Print Console.\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.print(“HELLO”)` command will print data (words and numbers) to the Print Console.  All new projects will begin printing on the first line of the Print Console.\n\n## Examples\n\nThis example will print VEX.  When printing numbers or text, they must be surrounded with quotation marks (either single (‘) or double (“) quotation marks will work).\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"VEX\")\n```\n\nThis example will also print VEX.  Notice that since a variable is within the brain.print() command, the variable does not have to be enclosed with parentheses.  \n\n```don\nmy_variable = \"VEX\"\nbrain.print(my_variable)\n```","signature":"brain.print(\"Hello\")"},"looks_brain_screen_clear_line":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_clear_line","markdown":"# Clear Row\n\nClears a single row on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```python \nbrain.screen.clear_row()\n```\n\n## How To Use\nClears the current row on the V5 Brain screen.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.clear_row()\n```\n\nClears a specified row on the V5 Brain screen.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.clear_row(ROW)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.clear_row()` command can accept integer values between **1 - 20**, to set which row to clear. You can also call the command without any arguments to clear the current row.\n\n---\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_brain_screen_clear_line/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)","signature":"brain.screen.clear_line()"},"looks_brain_screen_clear_screen":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_clear_screen","markdown":"# Clear All Rows\n \nClears the entire V5 Brain screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.clear_screen()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`brain.screen.clear_screen()` will not change the screen's cursor. You will need to use `brain.screen.set_cursor()` command to set the screen's cursor to a specific position.","signature":"brain.screen.clear_screen()"},"looks_brain_screen_clear_screen_virtual":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_clear_screen_virtual","markdown":"# Clear All Rows\n \nClears the entire Print Console and resets the cursor to the first row.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.clear_screen()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n```python\n# Print \"VEXcode V5\" on the first line, wait 2 seconds, then clear the Print Console\nbrain.screen.print(\"VEXcode V5\")\nwait(2, SECONDS)\nbrain.screen.clear_screen()\n```","signature":"brain.screen.clear_screen()"},"looks_brain_screen_clear_screen_virtual_viqc":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_clear_screen_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Clear All Rows\n \nClears the entire Print Console and resets the cursor to the first row.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.clear_screen()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n```python\n# Print \"VEXcode IQ\" on the first line, wait 2 seconds, then clear the Print Console\nbrain.screen.print(\"VEXcode IQ\")\nwait(2, SECONDS)\nbrain.screen.clear_screen()\n```","signature":"brain.screen.clear_screen()"},"looks_brain_screen_draw_circle":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_draw_circle","markdown":"# Draw Circle\n\nDraws a circle on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.draw_circle(X, Y, RADIUS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.draw_circle()` command requires 3 values:\n\n* Value 1: Center Point X\n* Value 2: Center Point Y\n* Value 3: Radius of Circle (in pixels)\n\nThe outside line color of the circle is determined by the `brain.screen.set_pen_color()` command. The default line color is white.\n\nThe inside fill color of the circle is determined by the `brain.screen.set_fill_color()` command. The default fill color is black.\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_brain_screen_draw_circle/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)","signature":"brain.screen.draw_circle(1, 1, 10)"},"looks_brain_screen_draw_line":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_draw_line","markdown":"# Draw Line\n\nDraws a line on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.draw_line(START_X, START_Y, END_X, END_Y)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.draw_line()` command requires 4 values:\n\n* Value 1: Beginning X coordinate\n* Value 2: Beginning Y coordinate\n* Value 3: Ending X coordinate\n* Value 4: Ending Y coordinate\n\nThe line color is determined by the `brain.screen.set_pen_color()` command. The default line color is white.\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_brain_screen_draw_line/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)","signature":"brain.screen.draw_line(1, 1, 10, 10)"},"looks_brain_screen_draw_pixel":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_draw_pixel","markdown":"# Draw Pixel\n\nDraws a pixel on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.draw_pixel(X, Y)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.draw_pixel` command requires 2 values:\n\n* Value 1: X Coodinate\n* Value 2: Y Coodinate\n\nThe pixel color is determined by the `brain.screen.set_pen_color()` command. The default pixel color is white.\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_brain_screen_draw_pixel/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)","signature":"brain.screen.draw_pixel(1, 1)"},"looks_brain_screen_draw_rectangle":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_draw_rectangle","markdown":"# Draw Rectangle\n\nDraws a rectangle on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.draw_rectangle(X, Y, WIDTH, HEIGHT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.draw_rectangle()` command requires 4 values:\n\n* Value 1: Top Left Corner X coordinate\n* Value 2: Top Left Corner Y coordinate\n* Value 3: Width of the rectangle\n* Value 4: Height of the rectangle\n\nThe border line color of the rectangle is determined by the `brain.screen.set_pen_color()` command. The default line color is white.\n\nThe inside fill color of the rectangle is determine by the `brain.screen.set_fill_color()` command. The default fill color is black.\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_brain_screen_draw_rectangle/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)","signature":"brain.screen.draw_rectangle(1, 1, 10, 10)"},"looks_brain_screen_new_line":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_new_line","markdown":"# Next Row\n\nSets the print output cursor on the V5 Brain's screen to the next available row.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.next_row()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nBy default, all projects begin with the screen cursor at row 1 column 1. The `brain.screen.next_row()` command will move the cursor down by a single row on the screen. `brain.screen.next_row()` will also set the cursor's column to position 1.\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Brain allows you to change the size of the font printed on the screen. Changing the font will affect the number of rows available on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* `FontType.MONO12` - 20 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO15` - 16 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO20` / `FontType.PROP20` - 12 Rows (Default)\n* `FontType.MONO30` / `FontType.PROP30` - 8 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO40` / `FontType.PROP40` - 6 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO60` / `FontType.PROP60` - 4 Rows\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_brain_screen_new_line/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)","signature":"brain.screen.new_line()"},"looks_brain_screen_new_line_virtual":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_new_line_virtual","markdown":"# Next Row\n\nSets the print output cursor of the Print Console to the next line.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.next_row()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nBy default, all projects begin with the print cursor at row 1. The `brain.screen.next_row()` command will move the cursor down by a single line on the Print Console.\n\n---\n\n```python\n# Print \"VEXcode\" on the first line, then \"V5\" on the next line\nbrain.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")\nbrain.screen.next_row()\nbrain.screen.print(\"V5\")\n```","signature":"brain.screen.new_line()"},"looks_brain_screen_new_line_virtual_viqc":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_new_line_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Next Row\n\nSets the print output cursor of the Print Console to the next line.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.next_row()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nBy default, all projects begin with the print cursor at row 1. The `brain.screen.next_row()` command will move the cursor down by a single line on the Print Console.\n\n---\n\n```python\n# Print \"VEXcode\" on the first line, then \"IQ\" on the next line\nbrain.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")\nbrain.screen.next_row()\nbrain.screen.print(\"IQ\")\n```","signature":"brain.screen.new_line()"},"looks_brain_screen_print":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_print","markdown":"# Print\n\nPrints values or text on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.print()` command will print data at a cursor location on the screen.\n\nAll new projects begin with the screen cursor at row 1 column 1.\n\n* Print words and numbers: \n`brain.screen.print(\"Number:\", 10)`\n\n* Print the reported value from a variable: \n`brain.screen.print(my_variable)`\n\n* Print the reported value from a sensor or device:  \n`brain.screen.print(drivetrain.is_done())`","signature":"brain.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")"},"looks_brain_screen_print_virtual":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_print_virtual","markdown":"# Print\n\nPrints values or text to the Print Console.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.print()` command will print data to the Print Console.\n\n* Print words and numbers: \n`brain.screen.print(\"Number:\", 10)`\n\n* Print numbers with decimal points:\n```brain.screen.print(\"Timer Value:\", brain.timer.time(SECONDS), precision=2)```\n\n* Print the reported value from a variable: \n`brain.screen.print(my_variable)`\n\n* Print the reported value from a sensor or device:  \n`brain.screen.print(drivetrain.is_done())`","signature":"brain.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")"},"looks_brain_screen_print_virtual_viqc":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_print_virtual_viqc","markdown":"# Print\n\nPrints values or text to the Print Console.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.print()` command will print data to the Print Console.\n\n* Print words and numbers: \n`brain.screen.print(\"Number:\", 10)`\n\n* Print numbers with decimal points:\n```brain.screen.print(\"Timer Value:\", brain.timer.time(SECONDS), precision=2)```\n\n* Print the reported value from a variable: \n`brain.screen.print(my_variable)`\n\n* Print the reported value from a sensor or device:  \n`brain.screen.print(drivetrain.is_done())`","signature":"brain.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")"},"looks_brain_screen_set_cursor":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_set_cursor","markdown":"# Set Cursor\n\n\nSets the cursor location on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.set_cursor(ROW, COLUMN)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.set_cursor()` command requires 2 values:\n\n* Value 1: Screen row position \n* Value 2: Screen column position\n\nSet the cursor's row and column position to have a `brain.screen.print()` command display at a specific location on the screen.\n\nAccepts a range for **row** of **1 to 20**.\n\nAccepts a range for **column** of **1 to 80**.\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Brain allows you to change the size of the font printed on the screen. Changing the font will affect the number of rows and columns available on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* `FontType.MONO12` - 20 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO15` - 16 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO20` / `FontType.PROP20` - 12 Rows (Default)\n* `FontType.MONO30` / `FontType.PROP30` - 8 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO40` / `FontType.PROP40` - 6 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO60` / `FontType.PROP60` - 4 Rows\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* `FontType.MONO12` - 80 Columns\n* `FontType.MONO15` - 68 Columns\n* `FontType.MONO20` / `FontType.PROP20` - 48 Columns (Default)\n* `FontType.MONO30` / `FontType.PROP30` - 32 Columns\n* `FontType.MONO40` / `FontType.PROP40` - 24 Columns\n* `FontType.MONO60` / `FontType.MONO60` - 16 Columns\n\n![brain_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_brain_screen_set_cursor/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)","signature":"brain.screen.set_cursor(1, 1)"},"looks_brain_screen_set_fill_color":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_set_fill_color","markdown":"# Set Fill Color\n\nSets the color that fills in shapes drawn on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.set_fill_color(COLOR)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.set_fill_color()` command is used to set the color of the inside of circles and rectangles drawn on the V5 Brain's screen. \n\nReplace the `COLOR` parameter with one of the following actions:\n\n* `Color.BLACK`\n* `Color.WHITE`\n* `Color.RED`\n* `Color.GREEN`\n* `Color.BLUE`\n* `Color.YELLOW`\n* `Color.ORANGE`\n* `Color.PURPLE`\n* `Color.CYAN`\n* `Color.TRANSPARENT`\n\nThe fill color will be used for any subsequent objects drawn on the V5 Brain's screen until it is changed again.","signature":"brain.screen.set_fill_color(Color.RED)"},"looks_brain_screen_set_font":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_set_font","markdown":"# Set Font\n\nSets the style and size of font used on the V5 Brain's screen when printing values.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.set_font(FONT_TYPE)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThere are two different types of fonts available on the V5 Brain's screen:\n\n* Monospaced (MONO) - each character takes up the same width.\n* Proportional (PROP) - each character takes up different size widths based on the character.\n\nChoose which font type and size to use by replacing the `FONT_TYPE` parameter. You can select from the following font options.\n\n* `FontType.MONO12`\n* `FontType.MONO15`\n* `FontType.MONO20`\n* `FontType.MONO30`\n* `FontType.MONO40`\n* `FontType.MONO60`\n* `FontType.PROP20`\n* `FontType.PROP30`\n* `FontType.PROP40`\n* `FontType.PROP60`\n\nThe new font size will be used for any future text written to the V5 Brain's screen until it is changed again.","signature":"brain.screen.set_font(FontType.MONO20)"},"looks_brain_screen_set_pen_color":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_set_pen_color","markdown":"# Set Pen Color\n\nSets the color of lines drawn on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.set_pen_color(COLOR)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.set_pen_color()` command is used to set the color of lines, pixels, and text drawn on the V5 Brain's screen. \n\n`brain.screen.set_pen_color()` can also be used to set the outline color of circles and rectangles drawn on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\nReplace the `COLOR` parameter with one of the following options:\n\n* `Color.BLACK`\n* `Color.WHITE`\n* `Color.RED`\n* `Color.GREEN`\n* `Color.BLUE`\n* `Color.YELLOW`\n* `Color.ORANGE`\n* `Color.PURPLE`\n* `Color.CYAN`\n* `Color.TRANSPARENT`\n\nThe pen color will be used for any subsequent objects drawn on the V5 Brain's screen until it is changed again.","signature":"brain.screen.set_pen_color(COLOR)"},"looks_brain_screen_set_pen_width":{"name":"looks_brain_screen_set_pen_width","markdown":"# Set Pen Width\n\nSets the width of the outline for shapes drawn on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.set_pen_width(PEN_WIDTH)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `brain.screen.set_pen_width()` command is used to set the width of the line on the outside of circles and rectangles drawn on the V5 Brain's screen. \n\nThe `brain.screen.set_pen_width()` command can accept integer values, with a larger value equating to a larger width. Replace the `PEN_WIDTH` parameter with an integer value.\n\n![v5_row_column_brain](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_brain_screen_set_pen_width/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)","signature":"brain.screen.set_pen_width(10)"},"looks_brain_set_print_color":{"name":"looks_brain_set_print_color","markdown":"# Set Print Color\n\nSets the color of the text printed to the Print Console.\n\n```don\nbrain.set_print_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the color of the text to be printed:\n\n- BLACK\n- RED\n- GREEN\n- BLUE\n\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print `GO!` in green, and `STOP!` in red to the Print Console.\n\n```don\nset_print_color(GREEN)\nbrain.print(\"GO!\")\nset_print_color(RED)\nbrain.print(\"STOP!\")\n```","signature":"brain.set_print_color(BLACK)"},"looks_console_clear_console":{"name":"looks_console_clear_console","markdown":"# Console Clear\n\nClears the Print Console.\n\n```python\nprint(\"\\033[2J\")\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `print` command used with the `\\033[2J` command sequence will clear the Print Console.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below will print \"VEXcode\" and then clear the Print Console after 3 seconds.\n\n```python\nprint(\"\\033[31mVEXcode\")\nwait(3, SECONDS)\nprint(\"\\033[2J\")\n```","signature":"print(\"\\033[2J\")"},"looks_console_print":{"name":"looks_console_print","markdown":"# Console Print\n\nPrints values or text to the Print Console.\n\n```python\nprint(\"VEXcode\")\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `print` command will print data at the cursor location on the Print Console.\n\nAll new projects begin with the cursor at row 1.\n\n## Printing Lines\n\nBy default, Python will always add a new line after each `print` command.\n\nTo print multiple values to a single line, you can utilize the `end` parameter.\n\n```python\nprint(\"This text will be \", end=\"\")\nprint(\"printed on the same line\")\n```\n\n## Printing Values\n\nYou can use the Python `format` method to format the values being printed in the print statements.\n\nTo use the `format` method, you must first provide a template string with replacement fields enclosed by a set of curly braces - `{}`.\n\nThen, call the `format` method on the template string. Any parameter(s) passed into the `format` method will be inserted into the replacement fields in sequence (if there are multiple replacement fields).\n\nYou can also provide formatting options into the template string to set the number of decimal places to be printed. Any formatting options must begin with a semicolon - `:`.\n\nIn the example below, the value of the Brain Timer will be inserted into the single replacement field in the template string and printed to two decimal places.\n\n```python\nprint(\"{:.2f}\".format(brain.timer.time(SECONDS)))\n```\n\nSimilarly, the example below will print the row/column position of the Brain Cursor. The number of replacement fields (enclosed by `{}`) must match the number of parameters passed into the `format` method.\n\n```python\n# The value of brain.screen.row() will be passed into the first replacement field\n# The value of brain.screen.column() will be passed into the second replacement field\nprint(\"Row: {:d}, Column: {:d}\".format(brain.screen.row(), brain.screen.column()))\n```\n\nHere is a list of commonly-used format specifiers. Remember that to use a format specifier inside a replacement field (enclosed by `{}`), it must follow a semicolon - `:`.\n\n* `d` - Decimal Integer\n* `f` - Floating Point Number\n* `o` - Octal Integer\n* `s` - String\n\nUsing the Floating Point Number format specifier, `f`, you can also specify how many decimal places to format a number.\n\n* `.2f` - Formats a floating point number with 2 decimal places\n* `.4f` - Formats a floating point number with 4 decimal places\n\n## Printing Colors\n\nYou can use any of the color codes below with a `print` command to set the color of the text being printed.\n\nThe example below will show two ways in which a color sequence command can be used when printing. All color codes will need to be attached to an escape sequence - `\\033`.\n\n* `[31m` - Red\n* `[32m` - Green\n* `[34m` - Blue\n* `[30m` - Black\n* `[37m` - White\n* `[33m` - Yellow\n* `[91m` - Orange\n* `[35m` - Purple\n* `[36m` - Cyan\n* `[97m` - Transparent\n\n```python\n# Using color codes in-line\n# This will print \"VEXcode\" in Red to the Print Console\nprint(\"\\033[31mVEXcode\")\n\n# Using color codes in separate commands\n# Sets the print color for any subsequent prints\nprint(\"\\033[31m\")\nprint(\"VEXcode\")\n```","signature":"print(\"VEXcode\")"},"looks_controller_rumble":{"name":"looks_controller_rumble","markdown":"# Rumble Controller\n\nTriggers a vibrating effect on your V5 Controller.\n\n```python\ncontroller.rumble(\".-.-\")\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `controller.rumble(\".-.-\")` command uses the rumble motor in your V5 controller to provide feedback.\n\nYou can create any pattern you want using a combination of \".\" and \"-\" characters in the string. The \".\" character will create a short rumble. The \"-\" character will create a long rumble.","signature":"controller.rumble(\"-.-.\")"},"looks_controller_screen_clear_line":{"name":"looks_controller_screen_clear_line","markdown":"# Clear Row\n\nClears the current row on the V5 Controller's screen.\n\n```don\ncontroller.screen.clear_row()\n```\n\nClears a specified row on the V5 Controller's screen.\n\n```don\ncontroller.screen.clear_row(ROW)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `controller.screen.clear_row()` command can accept integer values between **1 - 3**, to set which row to clear. You can also call the command without any arguments to clear the current row.\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Controller does not allow you to change the size of the font printed on the screen.\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* Standard Font - 3 Rows (Default)\n\n![controller_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_controller_screen_clear_line/v5_controller_rows_columns.jpg)","signature":"controller.screen.clear_line()"},"looks_controller_screen_clear_screen":{"name":"looks_controller_screen_clear_screen","markdown":"# Clear All Rows\n\nClears the entire V5 Controller's screen.\n\n```don\ncontroller.screen.clear_screen()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`controller.screen.clear_screen()` will not change the screen's cursor. \n\nUse the `controller.screen.set_cursor()` command to set the screen's cursor to a specific position.","signature":"controller.screen.clear_screen()"},"looks_controller_screen_new_line":{"name":"looks_controller_screen_new_line","markdown":"# Next Row\n\nSets the print output cursor on the V5 Controller's screen to the next available row.\n\n```don\ncontroller.screen.next_row()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nBy default, all projects begin with the screen cursor at row 1 column 1. The `controller.screen.next_row()` command will move the cursor down by a single row on the screen. `controller.screen.next_row()` will also set the cursor's column to position 1.\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Controller does not allow you to change the size of the font printed on the screen.\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* Standard Font - 3 Rows (Default)\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* Standard Font - 20 Columns (Default)\n\n![controller_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_controller_screen_new_line/v5_controller_rows_columns.jpg)","signature":"controller.screen.new_line()"},"looks_controller_screen_print":{"name":"looks_controller_screen_print","markdown":"# Print  \n\nPrints values on the V5 Controller's screen.\n\n```don\ncontroller.screen.print(\"VEXcode\") \n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `controller.screen.print()` command will print data at a cursor location on the screen.\n\nAll new projects begin with the screen cursor at row 1 column 1.\n\n* Print words and numbers: \n`controller.screen.print(\"Number:\", 10)`\n\n* Print the reported value from a variable:  \n`controller.screen.print(my_variable)`\n\n* Print the reported value from a sensor or device: \n`controller.screen.print(drivetrain.is_done())`","signature":"controller.screen.print(\"VEXcode\")"},"looks_controller_screen_set_cursor":{"name":"looks_controller_screen_set_cursor","markdown":"# Set Cursor\n\n\nSets the cursor location for **controller.screen.print()** command on the V5 Controller's screen.\n\n```don\ncontroller.screen.set_cursor(ROW, COLUMN)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `controller.screen.set_cursor()` command requires 2 values:\n\n* Value 1: Screen row position \n* Value 2: Screen column position\n\nSet the cursor's row and column position to have a `controller.screen.print()` command display at a specific location on the screen.\n\n`controller.screen.set_cursor()` accepts a range for **row** of **1 to 3**.\n\n`controller.screen.set_cursor()` accepts a range for **column** of **1 to 20**.\n\n---\n\nThe V5 Controller does not allow you to change the size of the font printed on the screen.\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* Standard Font - 3 Rows (Default)\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* Standard Font - 20 Columns (Default)\n\n![controller_screen_info](static/help/vr/python/en/looks_controller_screen_set_cursor/v5_controller_rows_columns.jpg)","signature":"controller.screen.set_cursor(1, 1)"},"looks_fill_color_plus":{"name":"looks_fill_color_plus","markdown":"# Set Pen Color\n\nUses the VEX VR Pen on the Art Canvas+ Playground to fill an area of the Playground with a specified color.\n\n```don\npen.fill(0, 0, 0, 100)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe color of the fill is specified with rgb values (red, green, blue) and opacity.\n\nEach parameter (red, green, and blue) defines the intensity of the color as an integer between 0 and 255.\n\nThe opacity describes the transparency of the color. Opacity is set using a value from 0 to 100 percent. This command will default to 100 percent for the opacity.\n\n \n## Example\n\nIn this project, the Playground will fill with the color yellow, because the red and green parameters are set to its highest value (255) and the blue parameter is set to 0. \n\nNote that the pen does not need to be set to DOWN in order to use this command and fill an area with color.\n\n```don\npen.fill(255, 255, 0, 100)\n```","signature":"pen.fill(0, 0, 0, 100)"},"looks_led_off":{"name":"looks_led_off","markdown":"# LED Off\n\nTurns a 3-Wire LED off.\n\n```python\nled.off()\n```","signature":"led.off()"},"looks_led_on":{"name":"looks_led_on","markdown":"# LED On\n\nTurns a 3-wire LED on.\n\n```python\nled.on()\n```","signature":"led.on()"},"looks_led_set":{"name":"looks_led_set","markdown":"# LED On/Off\n\nTurns a LED on or off.\n\n```don\nled.on()\n```\n```don\nled.off()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`led.on()` turns on the LED device.\n\n`led.off()` turns off the LED device.","signature":"led.set(False)"},"looks_monitor_sensor":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor","markdown":"# Monitor Sensor\n\nThe Monitor sensor command is used to add sensors to the monitor console so that the sensor values can then be seen and monitored. \n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo monitor sensor values in the monitor console, use the `monitor_sensor()` function by adding the identifiers of the sensors as string parameters. This function should be called at the start of the program.\n\nBelow is the list of supported sensor identifiers:\n\n- `brain.timer_time`\n- `drivetrain.is_done`\n- `drivetrain.is_moving`\n- `drivetrain.heading`\n- `drivetrain.rotation`\n- `left_bumper.pressed`\n- `right_bumper.pressed`\n- `front_distance.found_object`\n- `front_distance.get_distance`\n- `front_eye.near_object`\n- `front_eye.detect`\n- `front_eye.brightness`\n- `down_eye.near_object`\n- `down_eye.detect`\n- `down_eye.brightness`\n- `location.position`\n- `location.position_angle`\n\nWhen adding sensor identifiers to the monitor_sensor() command, they have to be added as strings - therefore enclosed with quotation marks:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"left_bumper.pressed\")\n```\nMultiple sensor identifier, separated by commas, can be added to the monitor_sensor() command:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(“distance.get_distance”, “drivetrain.rotation”, “brain.timer_time”)\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis project uses the location.position sensor identifier to show both the X and the Y position (in mm and inches) of the VR Robot as it moves in a square.  It also uses the location.position_angle sensor identifier to show the angle of the VR Robot in degrees .  Both of these sensor identifiers are in the motor_sensor() command.  \n\n```don\ndef main():\n    monitor_sensor(\"location.position\",\"location.position_angle\")\n    for i in range(4):\n        wait(1, SECONDS)\n        drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_sensor_rover":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor_rover","markdown":"# Monitor Sensor\n\nThe Monitor sensor command is used to add sensors to the monitor console so that the sensor values can then be seen and monitored. \n\n```python\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo monitor sensor values in the monitor console, use the `monitor_sensor()` function by adding the identifiers of the sensors as string parameters. This function should be called at the start of the program.\n\nBelow is the list of supported sensor identifiers:\n\n- `brain.timer_time`\n- `drivetrain.is_done`\n- `drivetrain.is_moving`\n- `drivetrain.heading`\n- `distance.found_object`\n- `distance.get_distance`\n- `rover.battery`\n- `rover.minerals_stored`\n- `rover.level`\n- `rover.exp`\n- `rover.storage_capacity`\n- `rover.under_attack`\n- `rover.enemy_radiation`\n- `rover.enemy_level`\n- `rover.angle`\n- `rover.location`\n- `rover.detects`\n- `rover.sees`\n- `rover.get_distance`\n\nWhen adding sensor identifiers to the monitor_sensor() command, they have to be added as strings - therefore enclosed with quotation marks:\n\n```python\nmonitor_sensor(\"rover.battery\")\n```\nMultiple sensor identifier, separated by commas, can be added to the monitor_sensor() command:\n\n```python\nmonitor_sensor(rover.battery”, “drivetrain.heading”, “brain.timer_time”)\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis project uses the `rover.location` sensor identifier to show both the X and the Y position (in mm) of the VR Rover and other objects as it moves in a square.\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    monitor_sensor(\"rover.location\")\n    for i in range(4):\n        wait(1, SECONDS)\n        drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_sensor_viqc":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor_viqc","markdown":"# Monitor Sensor\n\nThe `monitor_sensor()` command is used to add sensors to the monitor console so that the sensor values can monitored. \n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo monitor sensor values in the monitor console, use the `monitor_sensor()` function by adding the identifiers of the sensors as string parameters. This function should be called at the start of the program.\n\nBelow is the list of supported sensor identifiers:\n\n- `brain.timer.time`\n- `drivetrain.is_done`\n- `drivetrain.is_moving`\n- `drivetrain.heading`\n- `drivetrain.rotation`\n- `drivetrain.velocity_rpm`\n- `drivetrain.velocity_percent`\n- `arm_motor.is_done`\n- `arm_motor.is_spinning`\n- `arm_motor.position_degrees`\n- `arm_motor.position_turns`\n- `arm_motor.velocity_rpm`\n- `arm_motor.velocity_percent`\n- `intake_motor.is_done`\n- `intake_motor.is_spinning`\n- `intake_motor.position_degrees`\n- `intake_motor.position_turns`\n- `intake_motor.velocity_rpm`\n- `intake_motor.velocity_percent`\n- `catapult_motor.is_done`\n- `catapult_motor.is_spinning`\n- `catapult_motor.position_degrees`\n- `catapult_motor.position_turns`\n- `catapult_motor.velocity_rpm`\n- `catapult_motor.velocity_percent`\n- `catapult_tension_motor.is_done`\n- `catapult_tension_motor.is_spinning`\n- `catapult_tension_motor.position_degrees`\n- `catapult_tension_motor.position_turns`\n- `catapult_tension_motor.velocity_rpm`\n- `catapult_tension_motor.velocity_percent`\n- `intake_bumper.pressing`\n- `bumper.pressing`\n- `front_distance.is_object_detected`\n- `front_distance.object_distance_mm`\n- `front_distance.object_distance_inches`\n- `front_optical.is_near_object`\n- `front_optical.color`\n- `front_optical.brightness`\n- `front_optical.hue`\n- `color.is_near_object`\n- `color.color`\n- `color.brightness`\n- `color.hue`\n\nWhen adding sensor identifiers to the monitor_sensor() command, they have to be added as strings - therefore enclosed with quotation marks:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"intake_bumper.pressing\")\n```\nMultiple sensor identifiers, separated by commas, can be added to the monitor_sensor() command:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(front_distance.object_distance_mm”, “drivetrain.rotation”, “brain.timer.time”)\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis code will have the robot spin around and monitor the drivetrain heading and the drivetrain velocity.\n\n```don\ndef main():\n    monitor_sensor(\"drivetrain.heading\", \"drivetrain.velocity_percent\")\n    drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 10, TURNS)\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_sensor_vrc":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor_vrc","markdown":"# Monitor Sensor\n\nThe `monitor_sensor()` command is used to add sensors to the monitor console so that the sensor values can monitored. \n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo monitor sensor values in the monitor console, use the `monitor_sensor()` function by adding the identifiers of the sensors as string parameters. This function should be called at the start of the program.\n\nBelow is the list of supported sensor identifiers:\n\n- `brain.timer.time`\n- `drivetrain.is_done`\n- `drivetrain.is_moving`\n- `drivetrain.heading`\n- `drivetrain.rotation`\n- `drivetrain.velocity_rpm`\n- `drivetrain.velocity_percent`\n- `intake_motor_group.is_done`\n- `intake_motor_group.is_spinning`\n- `intake_motor_group.position_degrees`\n- `intake_motor_group.position_turns`\n- `intake_motor_group.velocity_rpm`\n- `intake_motor_group.velocity_percent`\n- `fork_motor_group.is_done`\n- `fork_motor_group.is_spinning`\n- `fork_motor_group.position_degrees`\n- `fork_motor_group.position_turns`\n- `fork_motor_group.velocity_rpm`\n- `fork_motor_group.velocity_percent`\n- `bottom_distance.is_object_detected`\n- `bottom_distance.object_distance_mm`\n- `bottom_distance.object_distance_inches`\n- `left_distance.is_object_detected`\n- `left_distance.object_distance_mm`\n- `left_distance.object_distance_inches`\n- `center_distance.is_object_detected`\n- `center_distance.object_distance_mm`\n- `center_distance.object_distance_inches`\n- `right_distance.is_object_detected`\n- `right_distance.object_distance_mm`\n- `right_distance.object_distance_inches`\n- `bottom_line_tracker.reflectivity`\n- `middle_line_tracker.reflectivity`\n- `top_line_tracker.reflectivity`\n- `roller_optical.is_near_object`\n- `roller_optical.color`\n- `roller_optical.brightness`\n- `roller_optical.hue`\n- `optical.is_near_object`\n- `optical.color`\n- `optical.brightness`\n- `optical.hue`\n- `gps.position_mm`\n- `gps.position_inches`\n- `gps.heading`\n- `rotation.angle`\n- `rotation.velocity`\n- `rotation.position`\n\nWhen adding sensor identifiers to the monitor_sensor() command, they have to be added as strings - therefore enclosed with quotation marks:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"bottom_line_tracker.reflectivity\")\n```\nMultiple sensor identifiers, separated by commas, can be added to the monitor_sensor() command:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(“distance.object_distance_mm”, “drivetrain.rotation”, “brain.timer.time”)\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis project uses the gps position sensor identifier to show both the X and the Y position (in mm and inches) of the robot as it drives toward the vertical center of the field. It also uses the timer sensor identifier to show the time in seconds. These sensor identifiers are passed into the motor_sensor() command.\n\n- Starting Position: A\n- Orientation: Facing Red Alliance Mobile Goal\n- Preload: Zero Rings\n\n```don\ndef main():\n    monitor_sensor(\"brain.timer.time\", \"gps.position_mm\", \"gps.position_inches\")\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    while gps.x_position(MM) < 0:\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n    drivetrain.stop()\n    drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n\nmain()\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_sensor_vrc24":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor_vrc24","markdown":"# Monitor Sensor\n\nThe `monitor_sensor()` command is used to add sensors to the monitor console so that the sensor values can monitored. \n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo monitor sensor values in the monitor console, use the `monitor_sensor()` function by adding the identifiers of the sensors as string parameters. This function should be called at the start of the program.\n\nBelow is the list of supported sensor identifiers:\n\n- `brain.timer.time`\n- `drivetrain.is_done`\n- `drivetrain.is_moving`\n- `drivetrain.heading`\n- `drivetrain.rotation`\n- `drivetrain.velocity`\n- `intake_motor.is_done`\n- `intake_motor.is_spinning`\n- `intake_motor.position`\n- `intake_motor.velocity`\n- `arm_motor.is_done`\n- `arm_motor.is_spinning`\n- `arm_motor.position`\n- `arm_motor.velocity`\n- `optical.is_near_object`\n- `optical.color`\n- `optical.brightness`\n- `optical.hue`\n- `gps.position`\n- `gps.heading`\n- `rotation.angle`\n- `rotation.velocity`\n- `rotation.position`\n\nWhen adding sensor identifiers to the monitor_sensor() command, they have to be added as strings - therefore enclosed with quotation marks:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"arm_motor.position\")\n```\nMultiple sensor identifiers, separated by commas, can be added to the monitor_sensor() command:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(“drivetrain.rotation”, “brain.timer.time”)\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis project uses the gps.position sensor identifier to show both the X and the Y position (in mm and inches) of the Robot as it moves in a square.  It also uses the gps.heading sensor identifier to show the angle of the Robot in degrees .  Both of these sensor identifiers are in the monitor_sensor() command.  \n\n```don\ndef main():\n    monitor_sensor(\"gps.position\", \"gps.heading\")\n    for i in range(4):\n        wait(1, SECONDS)\n        drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_variable":{"name":"looks_monitor_variable","markdown":"# Monitor Variable\n\nThe Monitor Variable command is used to add variables to the monitor console so that the value of the variables can be seen and monitored.\n\n```don\nmonitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\", ...)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo add variables to the monitor console, use the `monitor_variable()` command.Add the nae of the variable(s) as a string in the command. When using variables in the monitor_variable() command, they must be set as a global variable.\n\n## Examples\n\nIn this project, each time the VR Robot picks up a disk in the Disk Mover Playground, the value of the variable will increase by 1.\n\n```don\ndef main():\n    global diskCount\n    monitor_variable(\"diskCount\")\n    while diskCount<3:\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        while not down_eye.near_object():\n            wait(5, MSEC)\n        \n        magnet.energize(BOOST)\n        diskCount += 1\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT,180,DEGREES)\n        \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        while distance.get_distance(MM)>150:\n            wait(5,MSEC)\n        magnet.energize(DROP)\n        \n        drivetrain.turn_for(LEFT,180,DEGREES)\n```","signature":"monitor_variable()"},"looks_pen_move":{"name":"looks_pen_move","markdown":"# Set Pen Position\n\nSets the position of the VR Pen.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\n```\n\n## How To Use\nChoose the position of the VR Pen - either **UP** or **DOWN**\n  \n**UP** - the pen will not draw a line on the playground\n\n**DOWN** - the pen will draw a colored line on the playground\n\n## Example\n\nThis project has the VR Pen draw a black line while the Drivetrain is driving forward.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"pen.move(DOWN)"},"looks_pen_set_pen_color":{"name":"looks_pen_set_pen_color","markdown":"# Set Pen Color\n\nSets the color of the VR Pen.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose a color of the VR Pen:\n\n - BLACK\n - RED\n - GREEN\n - BLUE\n\n \n## Example\n\nThis example has the VR Pen draw a blue line while the Drivetrain is driving forward.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLUE)\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)"},"looks_set_pen_color_plus":{"name":"looks_set_pen_color_plus","markdown":"# Set Pen Color\n\nSets the color of the VR Pen.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color_rgb(0, 0, 0, 100)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe color of the pen is specified with: rgb values (red, green, blue).\n\nEach parameter (red, green, and blue) defines the intensity of the color as an integer between 0 and 255.\n\nThe opacity describes the transparency of the color. Opacity is set using a value from 0 to 100 percent. This command will default to 100 percent for the opacity.\n\n \n## Example\n\nIn this project, the Pen is set to the color blue, because the blue parameter is set to its highest value (255) and the others are set to 0. Then the VR Robot will drive forward 200 millimeters and draw a blue line.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color_rgb(0, 0, 255, 100)\npen.move(DOWN)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n```","signature":"pen.set_pen_color_RGB(0, 0, 0, 100)"},"looks_set_pen_width":{"name":"looks_set_pen_width","markdown":"# Set Pen Width\n\nSets the width of the VR Pen.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_width(THIN)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nSelect one of the following five values for the VR Pen width. \n\n- EXTRA_THIN\n- THIN\n- MEDIUM\n- WIDE\n- EXTRA_WIDE \n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the Pen will draw an extra thin line colored blue, then turn around and draw a wide line colored red.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_width(EXTRA_THIN)\npen.set_pen_color_rgb(0, 0, 255, 100)\npen.move(DOWN)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\npen.move(UP)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\npen.set_pen_width(WIDE)\npen.set_pen_color_rgb(255, 0, 0, 100)\npen.move(DOWN)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n\n```","signature":"pen.set_pen_width"},"magnet_drop":{"name":"magnet_drop","markdown":"# Electromagnet drop\n\nAllows the V5 Electromagnet to drop the object it is currently holding.\n\n```python\nmagnet.drop()\n```\nAfter using the `magnet.drop()` command, the magnet will need to cool down before it can be used again.\n\n## Example\nThis example will pickup an object, drive forward, then use `magnet.drop()` to drop the object.\n\n```python\nmagnet.pickup()\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\nmagnet.drop()\n```","signature":"magnet.drop()"},"magnet_energize":{"name":"magnet_energize","markdown":"# Energize Electromagnet\n\nSets the VR Robot Electromagnet to two different modes: **boost** or **drop**.\n\n```don\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nEnter either `BOOST` or `DROP` within the parentheses.  \n**BOOST**: will attract a nearby magnetic object to the Electromagnet  \n**DROP**: will release any object the Electromagnet is holding \n\n## Example\n\nIn the Disk Mover playground, this example will have the robot drive forward 800mm, pick up an object, and then drop the object after driving 200mm in reverse.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 800, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 200, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(DROP)\n```","signature":"magnet_energize(BOOST)"},"magnet_pickup":{"name":"magnet_pickup","markdown":"# Electromagnet pickup\n\nAllows the V5 Electromagnet to pick up an object.\n\n```python\nmagnet.pickup()\n```\nAfter using the `magnet.pickup()` command, the magnet will need to cool down before it can be used again.\n\n## Example\nThis example will use `magnet.pickup()` to pick up an object, drive forward, and then drop the object.\n\n```python\nmagnet.pickup()\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\nmagnet.drop()\n```","signature":"magnet.pickup()"},"magnet_set_power":{"name":"magnet_set_power","markdown":"# Electromagnet set power\n\nChanges the power used by the V5 Electromagnet when picking up an object.\n\n```python\nmagnet.set_power(50)\n```\nThe power can be set from **0% to 100%**.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will use `magnet.set_power()` to set the electromagnet to full power, pick up an object, drive forward, and then drop the object.\n\n```python\nmagnet.set_power(100)\nmagnet.pickup()\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\nmagnet.drop()\n```","signature":"magnet.set_power(50)"},"motion_motor393_set_velocity":{"name":"motion_motor393_set_velocity","markdown":"# Set Motor Controller Velocity\n\nSets the speed of a Motor393 connected to a Motor Controller 29.\n\n```python\nmotor393.set_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `VELOCITY` parameter accepts a range of -100% to 100%.\n\nSetting a Motor393's velocity to a negative value will cause the Motor393 to spin in reverse.\n\nThe sequence below will cause the Motor393 to spin in reverse, even though the `FORWARD` parameter is passed.\n\n```python\nmotor393.set_velocity(-100, PERCENT)\nmotor393.spin(FORWARD)\n```\n\nSetting a Motor393's velocity to 0 will prevent the Motor from spinning.","signature":"motor393.set_velocity(10, PERCENT)"},"motion_motor393_spin":{"name":"motion_motor393_spin","markdown":"# Spin Motor Controller\n\nSpins a Motor393 connected to a Motor Controller 29.\n\n```python\nmotor393.spin(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command spins a Motor393 forever, until a new motor command is used or the program is stopped.\n\nChoose which direction the motor will spin to by setting the `DIRECTION` parameter to either **FORWARD** or **REVERSE**.","signature":"motor393.spin(FORWARD)"},"motion_motor393_stop":{"name":"motion_motor393_stop","markdown":"# Stop Motor Controller\n\nStops a Motor393 connected to a Motor Controller 29.\n\n```python\nmotor393.stop()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe example below will stop a Motor393 device after spinning for 3 seconds.\n\n```python\nmotor393.spin(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\nmotor393.stop()\n```","signature":"motor393.stop()"},"motion_motor_set_max_torque":{"name":"motion_motor_set_max_torque","markdown":"# Set Motor Torque\n\nSets the torque of the V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group.\n\n```python\nmotor.set_max_torque(AMOUNT, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command accepts a range of 0% to 100%.\n\nThe `set_max_torque` command can accept decimals, integers, or numeric values.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will spin the Claw Motor to the 40 degree position with only 20% maximum torque. This allows the claw to grab an object without damaging it with too much force.\n\n```python\nclaw_motor.set_max_torque(20, PERCENT)\nclaw_motor.set_position(40, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"motor.set_max_torque(50, PERCENT)"},"motion_motor_set_position":{"name":"motion_motor_set_position","markdown":"# Set Motor Position\n\nSets a V5 Motor's or Motor Group's encoder position(s) to the given value.\n\n```python\nmotor.set_position(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command can be used to set a Motor or Motor Group's position to a given positional value.\n\nUsually, the `motor.set_position` command is used to set the position to 0 to reset a Motor or Motor Group's encoder position(s).\n\nThe `motor.set_position` command accepts **DEGREES** or **TURNS** as valid **UNITS**.","signature":"motor.set_position(0, UNITS)"},"motion_motor_set_position_viqc":{"name":"motion_motor_set_position_viqc","markdown":"# Set Motor Position\n\nSets an IQ Motor's encoder position to the given position value.\n\n```python\nmotor.set_position(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command can be used to set a Motor position to a given positional value.\n\nUsually, the `Set Motor Position` command is used to set the position to 0 to reset a Motor encoder position.\n\nThe `Set Motor Position` command accepts **DEGREES** or **TURNS** as valid **UNITS**.","signature":"motor.set_position(0, UNITS)"},"motion_motor_set_stopping":{"name":"motion_motor_set_stopping","markdown":"# Set Motor Stopping\n\nSets the stopping behavior of a V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group.\n\n```python\nmotor.set_stopping(MODE)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `MODE` parameter can be replaced with any of the following options:\n\n* BRAKE: will cause the Motor/Motor Group to come to an immediate stop.\n* COAST: lets the Motor/Motor Group spin gradually to a stop.\n* HOLD: will cause the Motor/Motor Group to come to an immediate stop, and returns it to its stopped position if moved.\n\nThe stopping behavior set by this command will apply to subsequent motion commands for the entirety of the project, unless otherwise changed.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will make the Arm Motor spin forward for 90 degrees (to raise the arm) and then will hold its position.\n\n```python\narm_motor.set_stopping(HOLD)\narm_motor.spin_for(FORWARD, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"motor.set_stopping(BRAKE)"},"motion_motor_set_timeout":{"name":"motion_motor_set_timeout","markdown":"# Set Motor Timeout\n\nSets a time limit for the V5 Motor or Motor Group movement commands.\n\n```python \nmotor.set_timeout(TIME, SECONDS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nA Motor or Motor Group's is used to prevent motion commands that do not reach their position from preventing subsequent commands from running. \n\nAn example of a Motor not reaching its position is an Arm or Claw that reaches its mechanical limit and cannot complete its movement.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the timeout of the `spin_to_position` commands to 2 seconds.\n\nIf the Claw Motor has not reached the position of 270 degrees after 2 seconds, the Claw Motor will stop spinning.\n\n```python\nclaw_motor.set_timeout(2, SECONDS)\nclaw_motor.spin_to_position(270, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"motor.set_timeout(TIME, SECONDS)"},"motion_motor_set_timeout_viqc":{"name":"motion_motor_set_timeout_viqc","markdown":"# Set Motor Timeout\n\nSets a time limit for an IQ Motor's movement commands.\n\n```python \nmotor.set_timeout(TIME, SECONDS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nA Motor's `Set Motor Timeout` command is used to prevent motion commands that do not reach their intended position from preventing subsequent commands from running. \n\nAn example of a Motor not reaching its position is an Arm or Claw that reaches its mechanical limit and cannot complete its movement.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the timeout of the `Spin To Position` command to 2 seconds.\n\nIf the Claw Motor has not reached the position of 270 degrees after 2 seconds, the Claw Motor will stop spinning.\n\n```python\nclaw_motor.set_timeout(2, SECONDS)\nclaw_motor.spin_to_position(270, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"motor.set_timeout(TIME, SECONDS)"},"motion_motor_set_velocity":{"name":"motion_motor_set_velocity","markdown":"# Set Motor Velocity\n\nSets the speed of a V5 Motor or Motor Group.\n\n```python \nmotor.set_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command accepts a range from **-100% to 100%**.\n\nSetting a V5 Smart Motor's velocity to a negative value will cause the Motor/Motor Group to spin in reverse.\n\nSetting a V5 Smart Motor's velocity to 0 will prevent the Motor/Motor Group from spinning.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Arm Motor to spin at 25% speed to the 90 degree position.\n\n```python\narm_motor.set_velocity(25, PERCENT)\narm_motor.spin_to_position(90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"motor.set_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)"},"motion_motor_set_velocity_viqc":{"name":"motion_motor_set_velocity_viqc","markdown":"# Set Motor Velocity\n\nSets the speed of an IQ Motor.\n\n```python \nmotor.set_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command accepts a range from **-100 to 100**.\n\nSetting an IQ Motor's velocity to a negative value will cause the Motor/Motor Group to spin in reverse.\n\nSetting the velocity to 0 will prevent the Motor/Motor Group from spinning.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Arm Motor to spin at 25% speed to the 90 degree position.\n\n```python\narm_motor.set_velocity(25, PERCENT)\narm_motor.spin_to_position(90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"motor.set_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)"},"motion_motor_set_velocity_virtual":{"name":"motion_motor_set_velocity_virtual","markdown":"# Set Motor Velocity\n\nSets the speed of a V5 Motor or Motor Group.\n\n```python \nmotor.set_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command accepts a range from **-100% to 100%**\n\nSetting a V5 Motor's velocity to a negative value will cause the Motor/Motor Group to spin in reverse.\n\nSetting a V5 Motor's velocity to 0 will prevent the Motor/Motor Group from spinning.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will set the Intake Motor Group to spin at 25% speed to the 90 degree position.\n\n```python\nintake_motor_group.set_velocity(25, PERCENT)\nintake_motor_group.spin_to_position(90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"motor.set_velocity(VELOCITY, UNITS)"},"motion_motor_spin":{"name":"motion_motor_spin","markdown":"# Spin \n\nThis command spins a V5 Motor or Motor Group forever, until a new motion command is used or the program is stopped.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin(FORWARD)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which direction the Motor or Motor Group will spin to either **FORWARD** or **REVERSE**.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin(REVERSE)\n```","signature":"motor.spin(FORWARD)"},"motion_motor_spin_at_voltage":{"name":"motion_motor_spin_at_voltage","markdown":"# Motor Spin At Voltage\nSpins a V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group at a specified voltage.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin(FORWARD, 10.0, VOLT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Spin at voltage** accepts a range from -12V to 12V.\n\nUse the `FORWARD` parameter to drive the V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group in the forward direction.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin(FORWARD, 10.0, VOLT)\n```\n\nUse the `REVERSE ` parameter to drive the V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group in the reverse direction.\n\n```cpp\nmotor.spin(REVERSE, 10.0, VOLT)\n```\n\nSpinning a Motor or Motor Group at a negative voltage will cause the Motor/Motor Group to spin in the opposite direction than the one specified.","signature":"motor.spin(FORWARD, 10.0, VOLT)"},"motion_motor_spin_for":{"name":"motion_motor_spin_for","markdown":"# Spin For\n \nThis command spins a V5 Motor or Motor Group for a given amount of degrees or turns.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin_for(DIRECTION, AMOUNT, UNIT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which `DIRECTION` the Motor or Motor Group will spin to: **FORWARD** or **REVERSE**.\n\nChoose the `UNIT` of measurement to be either **DEGREES** or **TURNS**.\n\nChoose whether or not this command should be waited on by proceeding commands by setting an optional fourth parameter to either **wait=True** or **wait=False**. \n\n```python\nmotor.spin_for(FORWARD, 90, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```\n\nBy default, this command is a blocking command unless **wait=False** is passed as the fourth parameter.","signature":"motor.spin_for(FORWARD, 90, DEGREES)"},"motion_motor_spin_for_viqc":{"name":"motion_motor_spin_for_viqc","markdown":"# Spin For\n \nThis command spins an IQ Motor for a given amount of degrees or turns.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin_for(DIRECTION, AMOUNT, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which `DIRECTION` the Motor or will spin to: **FORWARD** or **REVERSE**.\n\nChoose the `UNIT` of measurement to be either **DEGREES** or **TURNS**.\n\nChoose whether or not this command should be waited on by proceeding commands by setting an optional fourth parameter to either **wait=True** or **wait=False**. \n\n```python\nmotor.spin_for(FORWARD, 90, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```\n\nBy default, this command is a blocking command unless **wait=False** is passed as the fourth parameter.","signature":"motor.spin_for(FORWARD, 90, UNITS)"},"motion_motor_spin_to_position":{"name":"motion_motor_spin_to_position","markdown":"# Spin To Position\n\nSpins a V5 Motor's or Motor Group's encoder(s) to the given position.\n\n```python \nmotor.spin_to_position(POSITION, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command will tell a Motor or Motor Group to travel to a specific position. Based on the current position of the Motor or Motor Group, `spin_to_position` will determine the direction to rotate.\n\nChoose the unit of measurement to be either **DEGREES** or **TURNS**.\n\nChoose whether or not this command should be waited by other commands by changing the optional third parameter to either **wait=True** or **wait=False**.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin_to_position(90, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```\n\nSetting **wait=True** means that proceeding commands will not execute until the motor spin is completed. Conversely, **wait=False** will not wait for it to be completed.  \n\nBy default, this command will have the wait to be set to **True**.","signature":"motor.spin_to_position(POSITION, UNITS)"},"motion_motor_spin_to_position_viqc":{"name":"motion_motor_spin_to_position_viqc","markdown":"# Spin To Position\n\nSpins an IQ Motor to a given position.\n\n```python \nmotor.spin_to_position(POSITION, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command will tell a Motor to travel to a specific position. Based on the current position of the Motor, `Spin To Position` will determine the direction to rotate the Motor.\n\nChoose the unit of measurement to be either **DEGREES** or **TURNS**.\n\nChoose whether or not this command should be waited on by other commands by changing the optional third parameter to either **wait=True** or **wait=False**.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin_to_position(90, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```\n\nSetting **wait=True** means that proceeding commands will not execute until the motor spin is completed. Conversely, **wait=False** will not wait for it to be completed.  \n\nBy default, this command will have the wait to be set to **True**.","signature":"motor.spin_to_position(POSITION, UNITS)"},"motion_motor_spin_viqc":{"name":"motion_motor_spin_viqc","markdown":"# Spin \n\nSpins an IQ Motor indefinitely.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose which **DIRECTION** the Motor will spin to with either **FORWARD** or **REVERSE** as the parameter.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"motor.spin(DIRECTION)"},"motion_motor_stop":{"name":"motion_motor_stop","markdown":"# Stop Motor\n\nStops a V5 Motor or Motor Group.\n\n```python\nmotor.stop()\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis example shows a motor spinning for 3 seconds before stopping.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\nmotor.stop()\n```","signature":"motor.stop()"},"motion_motor_stop_viqc":{"name":"motion_motor_stop_viqc","markdown":"# Stop Motor\n\nStops an IQ Motor.\n\n```python\nmotor.stop()\n```\n\n## Example\n\nThis example shows a motor spinning for 3 seconds before stopping.\n\n```python\nmotor.spin(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\nmotor.stop()\n```","signature":"motor.stop()"},"motion_servo_set_position":{"name":"motion_servo_set_position","markdown":"# Spin Servo\n\nSpins a Servo Motor to a set position.\n\n```python\nservo.set_position(POSITION, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `servo.set_position()` command accepts a range of values from **-50** to **50** for the **POSITION** parameter.\n\n## Example\n\nThe below example spins the servo motor to 25 degrees, waits 2 seconds, then spins the motor to -25 degrees.\n\n```python\nservo.set_position(25, DEGREES)\nwait(2, SECONDS)\nservo.set_position(-25, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"servo.set_position(50, DEGREES)"},"sensing_accel_two_g.acceleration":{"name":"sensing_accel_two_g.acceleration","markdown":"# Acceleration Of Analog Accelerometer\n\nReports the acceleration value from one axis on the Analog Accelerometer.\n\n```don\naccel_2g.acceleration()\n```\n\n## How to Use\n\nThis command reports a range from **-2.0 G to 2.0 G** or **-6.0 G to 6.0 G** depending on the jumper setting on the Analog Accelerometer.","signature":"accel_2g.acceleration()"},"sensing_brain_battery_capacity":{"name":"sensing_brain_battery_capacity","markdown":"# Battery Capacity\n\n\nReports the charge level of the V5 Brain's battery.\n\n```don\nbrain.battery.capacity()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nBattery capacity reports a range from **0%** to **100%.**","signature":"brain.battery.capacity()"},"sensing_brain_battery_current":{"name":"sensing_brain_battery_current","markdown":"# Battery Current\n\nReports the current of the V5 Brain's battery.\n\n```don\nbrain.battery.current(CurrentUnits.AMP)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nBattery current reports a range from **0.0 amps** to **20.0 amps**.","signature":"brain.battery.current(CurrentUnits.AMP)"},"sensing_brain_battery_voltage":{"name":"sensing_brain_battery_voltage","markdown":"# Battery Voltage\n\nReports the voltage of the V5 Brain's battery.\n\n```don\nbrain.battery.voltage(VOLT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\nBattery voltage reports a range from **10.0 volts** to **14.4 volts**.","signature":"brain.battery.voltage(VoltageUnits.VOLT)"},"sensing_brain_screen_column":{"name":"sensing_brain_screen_column","markdown":"# Cursor Column\n\nReports the column value of the V5 Brain's screen cursor location.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.column()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nScreen column reports a range from **1 to 80**.\n\nThe V5 Brain allows you to change the size of the font printed on the screen. Changing the font will affect the number of columns available on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* `FontType.MONO12` - 80 Columns\n* `FontType.MONO15` - 68 Columns\n* `FontType.MONO20` / `FontType.PROP20` - 48 Columns (Default)\n* `FontType.MONO30` / `FontType.PROP30` - 32 Columns\n* `FontType.MONO40` / `FontType.PROP40` - 24 Columns\n* `FontType.MONO60` / `FontType.MONO60` - 16 Columns","signature":"brain.screen.column()"},"sensing_brain_screen_pressing":{"name":"sensing_brain_screen_pressing","markdown":"# Screen Pressed\n \nReports if the V5 Brain's touchscreen is currently being pressed.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.pressing()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`brain.screen.pressing` reports **True** if the V5 Brain's touchscreen is being pressed.\n\n`brain.screen.pressing` reports **False** if the V5 Brain's touchscreen is not being pressed.","signature":"brain.screen.pressing()"},"sensing_brain_screen_row":{"name":"sensing_brain_screen_row","markdown":"# Cursor Row\n \nReports the row value of the V5 Brain's screen cursor location.\n\n```don\nbrain.screen.row()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nScreen row reports a range from **1 to 20**.\n\nThe V5 Brain allows you to change the size of the font printed on the screen. Changing the font will affect the number of rows available on the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n**V5 Brain Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* `FontType.MONO12` - 20 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO15` - 16 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO20` / `FontType.PROP20` - 12 Rows (Default)\n* `FontType.MONO30` / `FontType.PROP30` - 8 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO40` / `FontType.PROP40` - 6 Rows\n* `FontType.MONO60` / `FontType.PROP60` - 4 Rows","signature":"brain.screen.row()"},"sensing_brain_screen_x_position":{"name":"sensing_brain_screen_x_position","markdown":"# Brain Screen xPosition\nReports the X coordinate of the location of the last detected touch on the V5 Brain's touchscreen.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.x_position()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`brain.screen.x_position()` is used to capture the X coordinate of the last press on the V5 Brain's touchscreen. \n\nIt can be used with `brain.screen.y_position()` to get the (x,y) coordinate, and create interactivity between the V5 and the end user.\n\n`brain.screen.x_position()` return an **integer** in a range of **0 to 480 pixels**.\n\n![v5_row_column_brain](static/help/vr/python/en/sensing_brain_screen_x_position/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)\n\n```python\nmy_x_coordinate = brain.screen.x_position()\n```","signature":"brain.screen.x_position()"},"sensing_brain_screen_y_position":{"name":"sensing_brain_screen_y_position","markdown":"# Brain Screen yPosition\nReports the Y coordinate of the location of the last detected touch on the V5 Brain's touchscreen.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.y_position()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`brain.screen.y_position()` is used to capture the Y coordinate of the last press on the V5 Brain's touchscreen. \n\nIt can be used with `brain.screen.x_position()` to get the (x,y) coordinate, and create interactivity between the V5 and the end user.\n\n`brain.screen.y_position()` return an **integer** in a range of **0 to 240 pixels**.\n\n![v5_row_column_brain](static/help/vr/python/en/sensing_brain_screen_y_position/v5_row_column_brain.jpg)\n\n```python\nmy_y_coordinate = brain.screen.y_position()\n```","signature":"brain.screen.y_position()"},"sensing_brain_timer_clear":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_clear","markdown":"# Reset Timer\nResets the V5 Brain's timer.\n\n```don \nbrain.timer.clear()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Brain's timer begins at the beginning of each project. The clear timer command is reset the timer value back to 0 seconds.\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the timer will be reset before moving the Drivetrain forward for 3 seconds. After 3 seconds has elapsed, the Drivetrain will stop.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer.clear()\nwhile not brain.timer.time(SECONDS) > 3:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"brain.timer.clear()"},"sensing_brain_timer_clear_viqc":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_clear_viqc","markdown":"# Reset Timer\n\nResets the Brain's timer.\n\n```python \nbrain.timer.clear()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Brain's timer begins at the beginning of each project. The `Reset Timer` command can be used to reset the timer value back to 0 seconds.\n\n## Example\n\nIn this example, the timer will be reset before moving the Drivetrain forward for 3 seconds. After 3 seconds has elapsed, the Drivetrain will stop.\n\n```python\nbrain.timer.clear()\nwhile brain.timer.time(SECONDS) <= 3:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"brain.timer.clear()"},"sensing_brain_timer_reset":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_reset","markdown":"# Reset Timer\n\nResets the Brain's timer.\n\n```python\nbrain.timer_reset()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe brain's timer begins at the beginning of each project. The `brain.timer_reset()` function is used to reset the timer back to 0 seconds. \n\n## Example\n\nThis project has the Drivetrain drive forward while the timer in the Brain is less than five seconds.  The brain.timer_reset() command is used at the beginning of the project to reset the Brain’s timer.  \n\n```python\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"brain.timer_reset()"},"sensing_brain_timer_time":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_time","markdown":"# Timer Value\n\nReports the value of the Brain's timer in seconds.\n\n```python\nbrain.timer_time(SECONDS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe timer starts at zero seconds when the project starts, and returns the timer's value as a decimal value.\n\n`brain.timer_time()` can be assigned to variables, be used in boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.\n\n## Example\n\nThis project has the Drivetrain drive forward while the timer in the Brain is less than five seconds.  The brain.timer_reset() command is used at the condition in the While loop.\n\n```python\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the while statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"brain.timer_time(SECONDS)"},"sensing_brain_timer_time_viqc":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_time_viqc","markdown":"# Timer Value\n\nReports the value of the Brain's timer.\n\n```python\nbrain.timer.time(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe timer starts at 0 seconds when the program starts, and reports the timer's value as a decimal value.\n\nThe **UNITS** parameter accepts either **SECONDS** or **MSEC** (milliseconds) as a valid argument.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    brain.screen.clear_screen()\n    brain.screen.set_cursor(1, 1)\n    brain.screen.print(\"Timer:\", brain.timer.time(SECONDS))\n\n    # A brief wait to print values without distortion or tearing\n    wait(20, MSEC)\n```","signature":"brain.timer.time(UNITS)"},"sensing_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Bumper Pressed\n\nReports if the Right Bumper or Left Bumper is pressed. \n\n`bumper.pressed()`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`bumper.pressed()` returns **true** if the Bumper is being pressed.\n\n`bumper.pressed()` returns **false** if the Bumper is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use. The VR Robot is equipped with a `right_bumper` and a `left_bumper`.\n\n```\nright_bumper.pressed()\nleft_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nThe `bumper.pressed()` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition for control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nleft_bumper_pressed = left_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif left_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Left bumper pressed\")\nif right_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Right bumper pressed\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(left_bumper.pressed())\n```","signature":"bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_bumper_pressing":{"name":"sensing_bumper_pressing","markdown":"# Pressing Bumper\n \nReports if the Bumper Switch is currently being pressed.\n\n```don\nbumper.pressing()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nPressing bumper reports **True** if the Bumper Switch is being pressed.\n\nPressing bumper reports **False** if the Bumper Switch is not being pressed.","signature":"bumper.pressing()"},"sensing_color_brightness_viqc":{"name":"sensing_color_brightness_viqc","markdown":"# Color Brightness\n\nReports the amount of light detected by a VEX IQ Color Sensor.\n\n```python\ncolor.brightness()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Color Brightness` reports a range of values from **0 to 100** percent.\n\nA larger amount of light will report a higher value, while a smaller amount of light will report a lower value.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below prints the amount of light detected by a Color Sensor.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.print(\"Brightness:\", color.brightness())\n```","signature":"color.brightness()"},"sensing_color_colorname_viqc":{"name":"sensing_color_colorname_viqc","markdown":"# Color\n\nReports the color currently being detected by a VEX IQ Color Sensor.\n\n```python\ncolor.color()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nCompare the value returned by `Color` to one of the following colors to check if a specific color is being detected:\n \n- `Color.RED`\n- `Color.RED_VIOLET`\n- `Color.VIOLET`\n- `Color.BLUE_VIOLET`\n- `Color.BLUE`\n- `Color.BLUE_GREEN`\n- `Color.GREEN`\n- `Color.YELLOW_GREEN`\n- `Color.YELLOW`\n- `Color.YELLOW_ORANGE`\n- `Color.ORANGE`\n- `Color.RED_ORANGE`\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below prints \"Red\" to the IQ Brain's screen if a red object is currently being detected by a Color Sensor.\n\n```python\nif color.color() == Color.RED:\n    brain.screen.print(\"Red\")\n```","signature":"color.color()"},"sensing_color_hue_viqc":{"name":"sensing_color_hue_viqc","markdown":"# Color Hue\n\nReports the hue of the color detected by a VEX IQ Color Sensor.\n\n```python\ncolor.hue()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Color Hue` reports a range of values from **0 to 360** degrees, which represents the position of the color on the color wheel.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example prints the hue of the color detected by the Color Sensor.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.print(\"Hue:\", color.hue())\n```","signature":"color.hue()"},"sensing_color_is_near_object_viqc":{"name":"sensing_color_is_near_object_viqc","markdown":"# Color Is Near Object\n\nReports if a VEX IQ Color Sensor detects an object or surface.\n\n```python\ncolor.is_near_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nReports **True** when a Color Sensor detects an object or surface close to the front of the sensor. \n\nReports **False** when a Color Sensor does not detect an object or surface close to the front of the sensor.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below prints whether an object or surface is being detected by a Color Sensor.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.print(\"True\" if color.is_near_object() else \"False\")\n```","signature":"color.is_near_object()"},"sensing_controller_axis_position":{"name":"sensing_controller_axis_position","markdown":"# Position Of Controller\n \nReports the position of a joystick on the V5 Controller along an axis.\n\n```don\ncontroller.axis1.position()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`controller.axis1.position` reports a range between **-100 to 100**.\n\n`controller.axis1.position` will report zero (0) when a joystick axis is centered.\n\nChoose which Controller axis to report.\n\n* axis1 - right Joystick (left and right)\n* axis2 - right Joystick (up and down)\n* axis3 - left Joystick (up and down)\n* axis4 - left Joystick (left and right)","signature":"controller.axis1.position()"},"sensing_controller_button_up_pressing":{"name":"sensing_controller_button_up_pressing","markdown":"# Pressing Controller\n \nReports if a button on the V5 Controller is pressed.\n\n```don\ncontroller.buttonUp.pressing()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nPressing controller reports **True** if the selected Controller button is being pressed.\n\nPressing controller reports **False** if the selected Controller button is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Controller button to detect for a press:\n\n* `buttonUp`\n* `buttonDown`\n* `buttonLeft`\n* `buttonRight`\n* `buttonX`\n* `buttonY`\n* `buttonA`\n* `buttonB`\n* `buttonL1`\n* `buttonL2`\n* `buttonR1`\n* `buttonR2`","signature":"controller.buttonUp.pressing()"},"sensing_controller_screen_column":{"name":"sensing_controller_screen_column","markdown":"# Cursor Column\n \nReports the column number of the Controller's screen cursor location.\n\n```don\ncontroller.screen.column()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nCursor row reports a range from **1 to 80**.\n\nThe V5 Controller does not allow you to change the size of the font printed on the screen.\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Columns:**\n\n* Standard Font - 20 Columns (Default)","signature":"controller.screen.column()"},"sensing_controller_screen_row":{"name":"sensing_controller_screen_row","markdown":"# Cursor Row\n\nReports the row number of the Controller's screen cursor location.\n\n```don\ncontroller.screen.row()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nCursor row reports a range from **1 to 20**.\n\nThe V5 Controller does not allow you to change the size of the font printed on the screen.\n\n**V5 Controller Font Size - Number of Rows:**\n\n* Standard Font - 3 Rows (Default)","signature":"controller.screen.row()"},"sensing_digital_in_signal":{"name":"sensing_digital_in_signal","markdown":"# Digital In\n\nReports value of the digital in signal.\n\n```cpp\ndigital_in.value()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe V5 3-Wire ports function at a 5V logic signal voltage level.\n\nThe `digital_in.value()` command reports **1** when the digital input signal is high.\n\nThe `digital_in.value()` command reports **0** when the digital input signal is low.","signature":"digital_in.value()"},"sensing_digital_out_set":{"name":"sensing_digital_out_set","markdown":"# Digital out\n\nSets the logic level of a digital out 3-Wire port.\n\n```cpp\ndigital_out.set(True)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe V5 3-Wire ports function at a 5V logic signal voltage level.\n\nThe `digital_out.set(True)` command sets the digital signal to output a high or low logic signal.\n\nChoose to output a high or low digital logic signal.\n\n* Set the output to high: `digital_out.set(True)`\n* Set the output to low: `digital_out.set(False)`","signature":"digital_out.set(True)"},"sensing_distance_found_object":{"name":"sensing_distance_found_object","markdown":"# Distance Found Object\n\nReports whether the built-in Distance Sensor sees an object or surface in front of the robot. \n\nFor the base VR robot, the only distance sensor available is the front distance sensor and can be accessed using the following command.\n\n```python\nfront_distance.found_object()\n```\n\nFor VR+ robots, the left, right, and rear distance sensors are also available. These sensors can be accessed using the following commands:\n\n```python\nleft_distance.found_object()\nright_distance.found_object()\nrear_distance.found_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n*\"distance\" is a place holder in the instructions below and should be replaced with one of the sensor names aboved when used in the following contexts.*\n\nThe `distance.found_object()` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\n`distance.found_object()` returns **true** when the built in Distance Sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view, and within 3000 mm of the sensor.\n\n`distance.found_object()` returns **false** when the built in Distance Sensor does not detect an object or surface within 3000 mm.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```python\nfound_object = front_distance.found_object()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```python\nif front_distance.found_object():\n    brain.print(\"Found object\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```python\nbrain.print(front_distance.found_object())\n```","signature":"distance.found_object()"},"sensing_distance_get_distance":{"name":"sensing_distance_get_distance","markdown":"# Distance From\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object from the Distance Sensor.\n\nFor the base VR robot, the only distance sensor available is the front distance sensor and can be accessed using the following command.\n\n```python\nfront_distance.get_distance(UNITS)\n```\n\nFor VR+ robots, the left, right, and rear distance sensors are also available. These sensors can be accessed using the following commands:\n\n```python\nleft_distance.get_distance(UNITS)\nright_distance.get_distance(UNITS)\nrear_distance.get_distance(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n*\"distance\" is a place holder in the instructions below and should be replaced with one of the sensor names aboved when used in the following contexts.*\n\nChoose whether `distance.get_distance(UNITS)` is reported in millimeters or inches by replacing `UNITS` with either `MM` or `INCHES` as parameters.\n\n```python\ndistance.get_distance(MM)\ndistance.get_distance(INCHES)\n```\n\nThe `distance.get_distance(UNITS)` function can be called and assigned to variables, be used in comparison statements, or be used in functions that take number values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```python\nmy_distance_mm = front_distance.get_distance(MM)\n```\nUsing it in a comparison statement:\n\n```python\nif front_distance.get_distance(INCHES) < 1:\n    brain.print(\"Close to object\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```python\nbrain.print(front_distance.get_distance(INCHES))\n```","signature":"distance.get_distance(MM)"},"sensing_distance_is_object_detected":{"name":"sensing_distance_is_object_detected","markdown":"# Distance Object Found\n\nReports if the Distance sensor sees an object within its field of view.\n\n```don\ndistance.is_object_detected()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Distance object found** reports True when the Distance sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view.\n\n**Distance object found** reports False when the Distance sensor does not detect an object or surface.","signature":"distance.is_object_detected()"},"sensing_distance_object_distance":{"name":"sensing_distance_object_distance","markdown":"# Object Distance\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object from the Distance sensor.\n\n```don\ndistance.object_distance(MM)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Object distance** reports a range from **20mm to 2000mm**.\n\nAcceptable values for `UNIT` are: MM or INCHES.","signature":"distance.object_distance(MM)"},"sensing_distance_object_size":{"name":"sensing_distance_object_size","markdown":"# Object Size\n\nReports the size of an object the Distance Sensor detects.  \n\n```\ndistance.object_size()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nDetermines the size of the object detected (none, small, medium, large) based on the amount of light reflected and returned to the sensor.\n\n\nIf an object is found, the function will return `ObjectSizeType.SMALL`, `ObjectSizeType.MEDIUM`, or `ObjectSizeType.LARGE`.\n\nIf there is no object found, the function will return `ObjectSizeType.NONE`.","signature":"distance.object_size()"},"sensing_distance_object_velocity":{"name":"sensing_distance_object_velocity","markdown":"# Object velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of an object in m/s.\n\n```\ndistance.object_velocity()\n```\n## How To Use\n\n**Object velocity** reports the current velocity of an object in m/s (meters per second).","signature":"distance.object_velocity()"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Down Eye sensor brightness\n\nReturns the brightness of an object.\n\n```python\ndown_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` command returns a numerical value between 0 and 100.  It returns a value in percent.  White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%.  All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between that range of values.  \n\n`down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Down Eye sensor color\n\nReturns the specific color detected by the Down Eye sensor.\n\n```python\ndown_eye.detect(COLOR)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `down_eye.detect(RED)` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Down Eye sensor detects the selected color.It returns **False** when the Down Eye sensor detects a color different from the one selected.  \n\nChoose the color for the Down Eye sensor to detect:\n\n* NONE\n* RED\n* GREEN \n* BLUE\n\n`down_eye.detect(RED)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` command is used as a Boolean statement.  If the `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` command returns **True**, Green will be printed to the Monitor Console.  If the `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` command returns **False**, Not Green will be displayed  in the Monitor Console.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if down_eye.near_object(): \n        drivetrain.stop()\n        if down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n            brain.print(\"Green\")\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n        stop_project()\n    else:\n        brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n        brain.new_line()\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"down_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Down Eye sensor near object\n\nReturns if the Down Eye sensor is close enough to an object to detect a color.\n\n```python\ndown_eye.near_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `down_eye.near_object()` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Down Eye sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors, for example, a Disk.  It returns **False** when the Down Eye sensor isn’t close enough to an object with detectable colors.  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` will not detect the Playground floor as an object, but does detect colors (with the exception of pen drawings) on the Playground floor.  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.  \n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `down_eye.near_object()` command is used as a Boolean statement.If the `down_eye.near_object()` command returns **True**, the Drivetrain will stop, Near a Disk will be printed to the Monitor Console, and the project will stop.If the `down_eye.near_object()` command returns **False**, Not Near a Disk will be continuously printed on new lines.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if down_eye.near_object(): \n        drivetrain.stop()\n        brain.print(\"Near a Disk\")\n        stop_project()\n    else:\n        brain.print(\"Not Near a Disk\")\n        brain.new_line()\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"down_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_drivetrain_current":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_current","markdown":"# Drive Current\n\nReports the amount of current that the Drivetrain is currently using.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.current(CurrentUnits.AMP)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.current` reports a range from **0.0 to 2.5** amps.","signature":"drivetrain.current(CurrentUnits.AMP)"},"sensing_drivetrain_efficiency":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_efficiency","markdown":"# Drive Efficiency\n \nReports the efficiency of the Drivetrain.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.efficiency(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\nDrive efficiency reports the value of the power (in watts) the motor is using (input), versus the amount of power (in watts) the motor is providing (output).\n\nA V5 Drivetrain typically reaches a maximum efficiency of **65%** under normal use cases.\n\nDrive efficiency reports a range from **0% to 100%**.","signature":"drivetrain.efficiency(PERCENT)"},"sensing_drivetrain_heading":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_heading","markdown":"# Drive Heading\n\nReturns the direction that the Drivetrain is facing by using the Gyro sensor's current angular position.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.heading(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` command returns a numeric value.  The numerical value (the DEGREES) will increase when rotating clockwise and a decrease when rotating counter-clockwise.  \n\nThe `drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` command returns a range from 0.00 to 359.99 degrees.  \n\n`drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)"},"sensing_drivetrain_heading_viqc":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_heading_viqc","markdown":"# Drive Heading\n \nReports the direction that the Drivetrain is facing.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.heading(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nDrive heading reports a range from **0.00 to 359.99 degrees**.","signature":"drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_done":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_done","markdown":"# Drive Is Done\n\nReturns if the Drivetrain has completed its movement.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.is_done()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` is a command that returns a Boolean value. It returns **true** when the Drivetrain's motors have completed their movement.\nIt returns **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are still moving.\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.","signature":"drivetrain.is_done()"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_done_viqc":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_done_viqc","markdown":"# Drive Is Done\n\nReports if the Drivetrain has completed its movement.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.is_done()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Drive Is Done` reports **True** when the robot's Drivetrain has completed its movement.\n\n`Drive Is Done` reports **False** when the robot's Drivetrain is still moving.","signature":"drivetrain.is_done()"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving","markdown":"# Drive Is Moving\n\nReports if the Drivetrain is currently moving.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` returns **true** when the Drivetrain's motors are moving.\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` returns **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are stopped.\n\nThe `drivetrain.is_moving()` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition for control statements, or be used as a function parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```python\nstill_driving = drivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```python\nif drivetrain.is_moving():\n    brain.print(\"Still driving\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```python\nbrain.print(drivetrain.is_moving())\n```","signature":"drivetrain.is_moving()"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving_viqc":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving_viqc","markdown":"# Drive Is Moving\n \nReports if the Drivetrain is currently moving.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Drive Is Moving` reports **True** when the Drivetrain's motors are moving.\n\n`Drive Is Moving` reports **False** when the Drivetrain's motors are stopped.\n\n**Note:** This command will always return **false** if the Drivetrain is moving because of a `drivetrain.drive` or `drivetrain.turn` command (which do *not* specify a set distance to drive).","signature":"drivetrain.is_moving()"},"sensing_drivetrain_power":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_power","markdown":"# Drive Power\n\nReports the amount of power output the Drivetrain is currently generating.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.power(PowerUnits.WATT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nDrive power reports a range from **0.0 to 22.0 watts**.","signature":"drivetrain.power(PowerUnits.WATT)"},"sensing_drivetrain_rotation":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_rotation","markdown":"# Drive Rotation\n\nReturns the Drivetrain's angle of rotation.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\nThe `drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` command reports a numeric value.  It returns a positive value when the Drivetrain turns in a clockwise direction.  It returns a negative value when the Drivetrain turns in a counter-clockwise direction.\n\n`drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)"},"sensing_drivetrain_rotation_viqc":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_rotation_viqc","markdown":"# Drive Rotation\n \nReports the Drivetrain's angle of rotation.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Drivetrain's `Drive Rotation` command reports an increasingly **positive** value when the Drivetrain turns in the **clockwise** direction.","signature":"drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)"},"sensing_drivetrain_temperature":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_temperature","markdown":"# Drive Temperature\n \nReports the temperature of the V5 Smart Motors powering the Drivetrain.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.temperature(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nDrive temperature reports a range from **0% to 100%**.","signature":"drivetrain.temperature(PERCENT)"},"sensing_drivetrain_torque":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_torque","markdown":"# Drive Torque\n\nReports the amount of torque (rotational force) the Drivetrain is currently using.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.torque(units)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.torque` reports a range from **0.0 to 18.6** inch-pounds (InLB) if the provided **UNITS** parameter is `TorqueUnits.INLB`.\n\nAlternatively, a range from **0.0 to 2.1** Newton-meters (Nm) is reported if the provided **UNITS** parameter is `TorqueUnits.NM`.","signature":"drivetrain.torque(units)"},"sensing_drivetrain_velocity":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_velocity","markdown":"# Drive Velocity\n \nReports the current velocity of the Drivetrain.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.velocity(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.velocity` returns a decimal value representing the current velocity of the Drivetrain.\n\nIf the provided `UNITS` parameter is **PERCENT**, the reported values ranges between **-100% to 100%**.","signature":"drivetrain.velocity(UNITS)"},"sensing_drivetrain_velocity_viqc":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_velocity_viqc","markdown":"# Drive Velocity\n \nReports the current velocity of the Drivetrain.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.velocity(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Drive Velocity` returns a decimal value representing the current velocity of the Drivetrain.\n\nThe reported values ranges between **0 to 100**.","signature":"drivetrain.velocity(UNITS)"},"sensing_encoder_position":{"name":"sensing_encoder_position","markdown":"# Shaft Encoder Position\n \nReports the rotational position of the Shaft Encoder.\n\n```don\nencoder.position(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `UNITS` parameter accepts either **DEGREES** or **TURNS** as a value.\n\nPosition of shaft encoder reports the distance the Shaft Encoder has rotated in integer numbers for degrees, and decimal numbers for turns.","signature":"encoder.position(DEGREES)"},"sensing_encoder_set_position":{"name":"sensing_encoder_set_position","markdown":"# Set Shaft Encoder Position\n \nSets the Shaft Encoder's position to the given positional value.\n\n```python\nencoder.set_position(POSITION, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe set shaft encoder position command can be used to set the Shaft Encoder's position to any given positional value.\n\nThe `UNITS` parameter accepts either **DEGREES** or **TURNS** as a valid argument.\n\nUsually, the set shaft encoder position command is used to set the position to 0, to reset the Shaft Encoder's position.","signature":"encoder.set_position(POSITION, UNITS)"},"sensing_encoder_velocity":{"name":"sensing_encoder_velocity","markdown":"# Shaft Encoder Velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of a Shaft Encoder.\n\n```don\nencoder.velocity(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nVelocity of shaft encoder reports a decimal value in either degrees per second (dps) or rotations per minute (rpm).\n\nThe `UNITS` parameter accepts either **VelocityUnits.DPS** or **RPM** as a value.","signature":"encoder.velocity(VelocityUnits.DPS)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Color Brightness\n\nReports the brightness of an object from one of the Color Sensor Eyes.\n\nFor the base VR robot, the only eye sensors available are the front and down eye sensors and can be accessed using the following commands.\n\n```python\nfront_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\ndown_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\nFor VR+ robots, the left, right, and rear eye sensors are also available. These sensors can be accessed using the following commands:\n\n```python\nleft_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\nright_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\nrear_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n*\"eye_sensor\" is a place holder in the instructions below and should be replaced with one of the sensor names aboved when used in the following contexts.*\n\nThe `eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` function returns a value between 0 and 100 value and can be assigned to variables, used in comparison statements, or in functions that take number values as a parameter.\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` reports the brightness of an object. White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%. All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between that range of values.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward, the `left_eye` is mounted on the left side of the robot and faces left, the `right_eye` is mounted on the right side of the robot and faces right, the `rear_eye` is mounted on the back of the robot and faces backward.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\ncolor_brightness = eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\nUsing it in a comparison statement:\n\n```\nif eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT) < 25:\n\tbrain.print(\"Dark color\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT))\n```","signature":"distance.distance(MM)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Color Sensing\n\nReports if one of the Color Sensor Eyes detects a specific color.\n\nFor the base VR robot, the only eye sensors available are the front and down eye sensors and can be accessed using the following commands.\n\n```python\nfront_eye.detect(COLOR)\ndown_eye.detect(COLOR)\n```\n\nFor VR+ robots, the left, right, and rear eye sensors are also available. These sensors can be accessed using the following commands:\n\n```python\nleft_eye.detect(COLOR)\nright_eye.detect(COLOR)\nrear_eye.detect(COLOR)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n*\"eye_sensor\" is a place holder in the instructions below and should be replaced with one of the sensor names aboved when used in the following contexts.*\n\nThe `eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` returns **true** when the Color Sensor Eye detects the selected color.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` returns **false** when the Color Sensor Eye detects a different color than the one selected.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward, the `left_eye` is mounted on the left side of the robot and faces left, the `right_eye` is mounted on the right side of the robot and faces right, the `rear_eye` is mounted on the back of the robot and faces backward.\n\nChoose which color to detect. Replace the `COLOR` parameter with the chosen color to detect.\n\n```\nNONE\nBLACK\nRED\nGREEN\nBLUE\n```\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nfound_red = eye_sensor.detect(RED)\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif eye_sensor.detect(GREEN):\n\tbrain.print(\"On top of green\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(eye_sensor.detect(BLUE))\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.detect(RED)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Color Near Object\n\nReports if the Color Sensor Eye is close enough to an object to detect a color.\n\nFor the base VR robot, the only eye sensors available are the front and down eye sensors and can be accessed using the following commands.\n\n```python\nfront_eye.near_object()\ndown_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nFor VR+ robots, the left, right, and rear eye sensors are also available. These sensors can be accessed using the following commands:\n\n```python\nleft_eye.near_object()\nright_eye.near_object()\nrear_eye.near_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n*\"eye_sensor\" is a place holder in the instructions below and should be replaced with one of the sensor names aboved when used in the following contexts.*\n\nThe `eye_sensor.near_object()` function returns a **true** or **false** value and can be assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` returns **true** when the Color Sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` returns **false** when the Color Sensor isn't close enough to an object with detectable colors.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward, the `left_eye` is mounted on the left side of the robot and faces left, the `right_eye` is mounted on the right side of the robot and faces right, the `rear_eye` is mounted on the back of the robot and faces backward.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\ncolor_obj_front = eye_sensor.near_object()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif eye_sensor.near_object():\n\tbrain.print(\"Object in front\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(eye_sensor.near_object())\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.near_object()"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Front Eye sensor brightness\n\nReturns the brightness of an object.\n\n```python\nfront_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` command returns a numerical value between 0 and 100.  It returns a value in percent.  White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%.  All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between that range of values.  \n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Front Eye sensor color\n\nReturns the specific color detected by the Front Eye sensor .\n\n```python\nfront_eye.detect(COLOR)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `front_eye.detect(RED)` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Front Eye sensor detects the selected color.  It returns **False** when the Front Eye sensor detects a color different from the one selected.  \n\nChoose the color for the Front Eye sensor to detect:\n\n* NONE\n* RED\n* GREEN \n* BLUE\n\n`front_eye.detect(RED)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter. \n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` command is used as a Boolean statement.If the `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` command returns **True**, Green will be printed to the Monitor Console. If the `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` command returns **False**, Not Green will be displayed  in the Monitor Console.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if front_eye.near_object(): \n        drivetrain.stop()\n        if front_eye.detect(GREEN):\n            brain.print(\"Green\")\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n        stop_project()\n    else:\n        brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n        brain.new_line()\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows.","signature":"front_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Front Eye sensor near object\n\nReports if the Front Eye sensor is close enough (within 100 mm) to an object to detect a color.\n\n```python\nfront_eye.near_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `front_eye.near_object()` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Front Eye sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.  It returns **False** when the Front Eye sensor isn’t close enough to an object with detectable colors.  \n\n`front_eye.near_object()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter. \n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `front_eye.near_object()` command is used as a Boolean statement.If the `front_eye.near_object()` command returns **True**, the Drivetrain will stop, Near an Object will be printed to the Monitor Console, and the project will stop.If the `front_eye.near_object()` command returns **False**, Not Near an Object will be continuously printed on new lines. \n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if front_eye.near_object(): \n        drivetrain.stop()\n        brain.print(\"Near an Object\")\n        stop_project()\n    else:\n        brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n        brain.new_line()\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n \nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"front_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_gps_acceleration":{"name":"sensing_gps_acceleration","markdown":"# GPS Acceleration\n\nReports the acceleration value of the specified axis on a GPS Sensor.\n\n```python\ngps.acceleration(AXIS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.acceleration()` command reports a numerical value in the range of **-4.0** to **4.0** Gs.\n\nIt can report acceleration values from three different axes based on the value passed as the `AXIS` parameter.\n\n* Used with the **AxisType.XAXIS** parameter, the value reported will be the acceleration value of a GPS Sensor's forward and backward rotations\n\n* Used with the **AxisType.YAXIS** parameter, the value reported will be the acceleration value of a GPS Sensor's left and right rotations\n\n* Used with the **AxisType.ZAXIS** parameter, the value reported will be the acceleration value of a GPS Sensor's up and down movements\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Print the current acceleration on the GPS Sensor's X axis\nbrain.screen.print(\"Acceleration on X: \", gps.acceleration(AxisType.XAXIS))\n```","signature":"gps.acceleration(AxisType.XAXIS)"},"sensing_gps_calibrate":{"name":"sensing_gps_calibrate","markdown":"# GPS Calibrate\n\nStarts the calibration process for a GPS Sensor.\n\n```python\ngps.calibrate()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nA GPS Sensor must remain still during the calibration process.\n\n```python\n# Calibrate the GPS Sensor before starting any program code\ngps.calibrate()\n```","signature":"gps.calibrate()"},"sensing_gps_gyro_rate":{"name":"sensing_gps_gyro_rate","markdown":"# GPS Gyro Rate\n\nReports the rate of rotation from the specified axis on a GPS Sensor.\n\n```python\ngps.gyro_rate(AXIS, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.gyro_rate()` command reports the rate of rotation on an axis with the range of values reported between **-1000** to **1000** if **VelocityUnits.DPS** is used as the `UNITS` parameter, or between **-167** to **167** if **VelocityUnits.RPM** is used.\n\nThe `AXIS` parameter accepts the following values for specifying which axis to get rate of rotation from:\n\n* **AxisType.XAXIS**: reports the rate of rotation when a GPS Sensor rotates in the X-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n\n* **AxisType.YAXIS**: reports the rate of rotation when a GPS Sensor rotates in the Y-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n\n* **AxisType.ZAXIS**: reports the rate of rotation when a GPS Sensor rotates in the Z-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Print gyro rate values from all GPS Sensor axes in a loop\nwhile True:\n    brain.screen.clear_screen()\n    brain.screen.set_cursor(1, 1)\n    brain.screen.print(\"GPS Gyro Rate - X Axis: \", gps.gyro_rate(AxisType.XAXIS, VelocityUnits.DPS))\n    brain.screen.next_row()\n    brain.screen.print(\"GPS Gyro Rate - Y Axis: \", gps.gyro_rate(AxisType.YAXIS, VelocityUnits.DPS))\n    brain.screen.next_row()\n    brain.screen.print(\"GPS Gyro Rate - Z Axis: \", gps.gyro_rate(AxisType.ZAXIS, VelocityUnits.DPS))\n    brain.screen.next_row()\n    wait(0.1, SECONDS)\n```","signature":"gps.gyro_rate(AxisType.XAXIS, VelocityUnits.DPS)"},"sensing_gps_heading":{"name":"sensing_gps_heading","markdown":"# GPS Heading\n\nReports the heading that a robot is currently facing based on a GPS Sensor's readings from the VEX GPS Field Code.\n\n```python\ngps.heading()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.heading()` command reports a range of heading values from **0.00** to **359.99** degrees, increasing in value when a GPS Sensor is rotated clockwise.\n\nUsually, a GPS Sensor should be mounted facing behind a robot. In the GPS configuration, the Angle Offset is set to **180** degrees.\n\nThen, for example, if the GPS Sensor is facing the South wall as defined by the VEX GPS Field Code, the reported heading will be **0.00** degrees, which is the heading that the front of the robot is facing.\n\nHowever, a GPS Sensor can also be mounted facing other directions (e.g. the front of a robot).\n\nIn this situation, for example, the GPS Sensor's Angle Offset configuration would then need to be set to **0** degrees, which is the angular difference between the forward heading of the robot, and the heading that the GPS is facing. This way, the reported heading value will shift correctly in line with a robot's headings as it turns.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below uses the `gps.heading()` command to stop the Drivetrain's turn when the reported GPS Sensor heading is more than or equal to 180 degrees.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n\nwhile gps.heading() < 180:\n    wait(50, MSEC)\n\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"gps.heading()"},"sensing_gps_heading_virtual":{"name":"sensing_gps_heading_virtual","markdown":"# GPS Heading\n\nReports the heading that a robot is currently facing based on a GPS Sensor's readings from the VEX GPS Field Code.\n\n```python\ngps.heading()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.heading()` command reports a range of heading values from **0.00** to **359.99** degrees, increasing in value when a GPS Sensor is rotated clockwise.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below uses the `gps.heading()` command to stop the Drivetrain's turn when the reported GPS Sensor heading is more than or equal to 180 degrees.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(25, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n\nwhile gps.heading() < 180:\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"gps.heading()"},"sensing_gps_orientation":{"name":"sensing_gps_orientation","markdown":"# GPS Orientation\n\nReports the angle of the specified orientation axis on a GPS Sensor.\n\n```python\ngps.orientation(AXIS, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`gps.orientation()` reports a numerical value representing the angle of rotation about the specified orientation `AXIS`:\n\n* The **OrientationType.ROLL** axis reports a value between **-180** to **180** degrees, representing the angle of a GPS Sensor when tilted left or right\n\n* The **OrientationType.PITCH** axis reports a value between **-90** to **90** degrees, representing the angle of a GPS Sensor when tilted forward or backwards\n\n* The **OrientationType.YAW** axis reports a value between **-180** to **180** degrees, representing the angle of a GPS Sensor when rotated about the vertical axis\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Print the orientation of a GPS Sensor's yaw axis in a loop\nwhile True:\n    brain.screen.clear_screen()\n    brain.screen.set_cursor(1, 1)\n    brain.screen.print(\"GPS Yaw Orientation: \", gps.orientation(OrientationType.YAW, DEGREES))\n    wait(0.1, SECONDS)\n```","signature":"gps.orientation(OrientationType.ROLL, DEGREES)"},"sensing_gps_quality":{"name":"sensing_gps_quality","markdown":"# GPS Signal Quality\n\nReports a GPS Sensor's current signal quality.\n\n```python\ngps.quality()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.quality()` command reports a numerical value representing the current signal quality of a GPS Sensor.\n\nWhen the reported value is **100**, it means that all reported positional and heading data from a GPS Sensor is valid.\n\nHowever, a reported value of **90** signals that any positional data is no longer being calculated by capturing information from the VEX Field Code, but rather through alternative means.\n\nAt **80**, only GPS Sensor heading values are valid, but as more time goes by where a GPS Sensor is not scanning enough of the VEX Field Code to accurately determine positiona and heading information, the reported signal quality will continue to drop until **0**, where any GPS Sensor data is effectively frozen and no longer valid.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Print a GPS Sensor's signal quality\nbrain.screen.print(\"GPS Signal Quality: \", gps.quality())\n```","signature":"gps.quality()"},"sensing_gps_set_location":{"name":"sensing_gps_set_location","markdown":"# GPS Set Location\n\nSets the location for a GPS Sensor to a defined positional value.\n\n```python\ngps.set_location(x, y, units, heading, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nWhen a GPS Sensor is too close to the field walls to read accurate data (for example, when a robot is in a game's starting position on a field), unexpected behaviors can often occur if the program relies on the GPS's positional and heading data to work correctly.\n\nThe `gps.set_location()` command can be used to set the location and heading of a GPS Sensor if this information is known before the program is ran, which may help reduce GPS data inaccuracies due to a GPS Sensor's proximity to the field walls when a program is started.\n\nThe **x** and **y** parameters are the X and Y offsets of the GPS Sensor, or a defined origin point on a robot relative to the center of a field. The **units** parameter can either be **MM** or **INCHES**.\n\nThe **heading** parameter is the heading of the robot in its defined position. For example, if the GPS Sensor is mounted facing behind a robot and the robot is facing the West wall (heading of 270), the **heading** value that should be passed is 270.\n\n```python\n# Setting the approximate location of a GPS Sensor\n# when a robot is facing West and\n# starting at the bottom right corner of a field\ngps.set_location(56, -45, INCHES, 270, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"gps.set_location()"},"sensing_gps_set_origin":{"name":"sensing_gps_set_origin","markdown":"# GPS Set Origin\n\nSets an origin point on a robot at which GPS Sensor positional data should be based upon.\n\n```python\ngps.set_origin(X, Y, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `GPS.set_origin()` command can be used to allow GPS positional data to correspond to another point of a robot rather than the location of a GPS Sensor.\n\nIt is commonly used when a GPS Sensor is not located on the reference point of a robot (i.e. the point about which a robot rotates). This way, when the origin is set to the reference point, reported positional data would not change when a robot is rotating in-place.\n\nThe `X` parameter takes the X axis (i.e. the axis running through the sides of a GPS Sensor) while the `Y` parameter takes the Y axis offset distance to a reference point.\n\nEither **MM** or **INCHES** are accepted for the `UNITS` parameter, depending on whether the x and y offset values are being passed as millimeters or inches, respectively.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Assuming a GPS Sensor is mounted 25mm forward from the reference point on a robot,\n# we can set the origin on the GPS Sensor to have a Y offset of 25\n\ngps.set_origin(0, 25, MM)\n```","signature":"gps.set_origin(0, 0, MM)"},"sensing_gps_xposition":{"name":"sensing_gps_xposition","markdown":"# GPS X Position\n\nReports the X positional offset of a GPS Sensor or defined origin point from the center of a field.\n\n```python\ngps.x_position(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.x_position()` command reports the current X positional offset of a GPS Sensor or defined origin point relative to the center of a field.\n\nIf a `gps.set_origin()` command is used to set an origin point to a different part of a robot, the reported value will be the positional offset of the defined origin point from the center of a field.\n\nAlternatively, the origin point can also be set in the GPS Sensor's configuration. This can be done by measuring the X and Y distances from the intended origin point to the GPS Sensor and filling in the X and Y offset settings, respectively.\n\nValues can be reported in mm or inches, depending on whether **MM** or **INCHES** is passed in the `UNITS` parameter.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Print the current X position of a GPS Sensor\nbrain.screen.print(\"GPS X Position (MM): \", gps.x_position(MM))\n```","signature":"gps.x_position(MM)"},"sensing_gps_xposition_virtual":{"name":"sensing_gps_xposition_virtual","markdown":"# GPS X Position\n\nReports the X positional offset of a GPS Sensor or defined origin point from the center of a field.\n\n```python\ngps.x_position(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.x_position()` command reports the current X positional offset of the V5 robot's turning reference (midpoint between the two front wheels) relative to the center of a field.\n\nValues can be reported in mm or inches, depending on whether **MM** or **INCHES** is passed in the `UNITS` parameter.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Print the current X position\nbrain.screen.print(\"GPS X Position (MM): \", gps.x_position(MM))\n```","signature":"gps.x_position(MM)"},"sensing_gps_yposition":{"name":"sensing_gps_yposition","markdown":"# GPS Y Position\n\nReports the Y positional offset of a GPS Sensor or defined origin point from the center of a field.\n\n```python\ngps.y_position(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.y_position()` command reports the current Y positional offset of a GPS Sensor or defined origin point relative to the center of a field.\n\nIf a `gps.set_origin()` command is used to set an origin point to a different part of a robot, the reported value will be the positional offset of the defined origin point from the center of a field.\n\nAlternatively, the origin point can also be set in the GPS Sensor's configuration. This can be done by measuring the X and Y distances from the intended origin point to the GPS Sensor and filling in the X and Y offset settings, respectively.\n\nValues can be reported in mm or inches, depending on whether **MM** or **INCHES** is passed in the `UNITS` parameter.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Print the current Y position of a GPS Sensor\nbrain.screen.print(\"GPS Y Position (MM): \", gps.y_position(MM))\n```","signature":"gps.y_position(MM)"},"sensing_gps_yposition_virtual":{"name":"sensing_gps_yposition_virtual","markdown":"# GPS Y Position\n\nReports the Y positional offset of a GPS Sensor or defined origin point from the center of a field.\n\n```python\ngps.y_position(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `gps.y_position()` command reports the current Y positional offset of the V5 robot's turning reference (midpoint between the two front wheels) relative to the center of a field.\n\nValues can be reported in mm or inches, depending on whether **MM** or **INCHES** is passed in the `UNITS` parameter.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\n# Print the current Y position\nbrain.screen.print(\"GPS Y Position (MM): \", gps.y_position(MM))\n```","signature":"gps.y_position(MM)"},"sensing_gyro_calibrate":{"name":"sensing_gyro_calibrate","markdown":"# Calibrate Gyro Sensor\n\nCalibrating the Gyro Sensor is used to reduce the amount of drift generated by Gyro Sensor.\n\n```don\ngyro.calibrate()\n```\n\nDrifting occurs when the Gyro Sensor incorrectly detects movement even though the sensor is not physically moving.\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Gyro Sensor must remain still during the calibration process. The calibration process will take approximately 2 seconds to complete.","signature":"gyro.calibrate()"},"sensing_gyro_heading":{"name":"sensing_gyro_heading","markdown":"# Gyro Angle Of Heading\n \nReports the 3-Wire Gyro Sensor's current heading in degrees.\n\n```don\ngyro.heading(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe command reports an increase in heading when rotating clockwise.\n\n`gyro.heading` reports a range between **0.00 to 359.99**.","signature":"gyro.heading(DEGREES)"},"sensing_gyro_rotation":{"name":"sensing_gyro_rotation","markdown":"# Gyro Angle Of Rotation\n \nReports the 3-Wire Gyro Sensor's current angle of rotation in degrees.\n\n```don\ngyro.rotation(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nGyro rotation reports a **positive** value when the Gyro Sensor turns in the **clockwise** direction.\n\nGyro rotation reports a **negative** value when the Gyro Sensor turns in the **counter-clockwise** direction.","signature":"gyro.rotation(DEGREES)"},"sensing_gyro_set_heading":{"name":"sensing_gyro_set_heading","markdown":"# Set Gyro Heading\n\nSets the Gyroscopic (Gyro) Sensor's current heading position to a set value.\n \n```don\ngyro.set_heading(HEADING, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe set gyro heading command can be used to set the Gyro's position to any clockwise heading.\n\nThis command is typically used reset the heading orientation of the Gyro sensor when value is set to 0.\n\nThe `HEADING` parameter accepts a range of 0.0 to 359.99 degrees.\n\nThe set heading command can accept decimals, integers, or numeric values.","signature":"gyro.set_heading(0, DEGREES)"},"sensing_gyro_set_rotation":{"name":"sensing_gyro_set_rotation","markdown":"# Set Gyro Rotation\n\nSets a 3-Wire Gyro Sensor's angle of rotation to a given value.\n\n```python \ngyro.set_rotation(ROTATION, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Set Gyro Rotation` command can be used to set a 3-Wire Gyro Sensor's angle of rotation to any given positive (clockwise) or negative (counter-clockwise) value.\n\nThe `Set Gyro Rotation` command can accept decimals, integers, or numeric values for its `ROTATION` parameter.","signature":"gyro.set_rotation(0, DEGREES)"},"sensing_inertial_acceleration":{"name":"sensing_inertial_acceleration","markdown":"# Inertial Acceleration\n\nReports the acceleration value from one of the axes (x, y, or z) on the Intertial Sensor.\n\n```don\ninertial.acceleration(AxisType.XAXIS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nAcceleration of reports a range from **-4.0 to 4.0 Gs**.\n\nChoose which axis to use:\n\n* The **X-axis** reports acceleration when the Inertial Sensor moves **forward to backward**\n* The **Y-axis** reports acceleration when the Inertial Sensor moves **side to side**\n* The **Z-axis** reports acceleration when the Inertial Sensor moves **up to down**","signature":"inertial.acceleration(AxisType.XAXIS)"},"sensing_inertial_calibrate":{"name":"sensing_inertial_calibrate","markdown":"# Calibrate Inertial Sensor\n \nCalibrating the Inertial Sensor is used to reduce the amount of drift generated by the Inertial Sensor.\n\n```don\ninertial.calibrate()\n```\n\nDrifting occurs when the Inertial Sensor incorrectly detects movement even though the sensor is not physically moving.\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe Inertial Sensor must remain still during the calibration process. The calibration process will take approximately 2 seconds to complete.","signature":"inertial.calibrate()"},"sensing_inertial_gyro_rate":{"name":"sensing_inertial_gyro_rate","markdown":"# Inertial Gyro Rate\n\nGets the rate of rotation from one of the axes (x, y, or z) on the Inertial Sensor.\n\n```don\ninertial.gyro_rate(AxisType.XAXIS, VelocityUnits.DPS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nGyro rate reports a range from **-1000.0 to 1000.0** in dps (degrees per second).\n\nChoose which axis to use.\n\n* The X-axis reports rate of rotation when the Inertial Sensor rotates in the X-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n* The Y-axis reports rate of rotation when the Inertial Sensor rotates in the Y-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)\n* The Z-axis reports rate of rotation when the Inertial Sensor rotates in the Z-Axis (based on the orientation of the sensor)","signature":"inertial.gyro_rate(AxisType.XAXIS, VelocityUnits.DPS)"},"sensing_inertial_heading":{"name":"sensing_inertial_heading","markdown":"# Inertial Angle Of Heading \n\nReports the V5 Inertial Sensor's current heading in degrees.\n\n```don\ninertial.heading(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `inertial.heading` command reports an increase in heading when rotating clockwise.\n\n`inertial.heading` reports a range from 0.00 to 359.99 degrees.","signature":"inertial.heading(DEGREES)"},"sensing_inertial_orientation":{"name":"sensing_inertial_orientation","markdown":"# Inertial Orientation\n\nGets an orientation angle of the inertial sensor. Reports the unit value specified by the parameter of the inertial sensor.\n\n```don\ninertial.orientation(OrientationType.ROLL, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\nThe orientation reported is determined by the selected axis (x, y, or z).\n\n* The X-axis represents pitch, which reports a value between -90 to +90 degrees.\n* The Y-axis represents roll, which reports a value between -180 to +180 degrees)\n* The Z-axis represents yaw, which reports a value between -180 to +180 degrees)","signature":"inertial.orientation(OrientationType.ROLL , DEGREES)"},"sensing_inertial_rotation":{"name":"sensing_inertial_rotation","markdown":"# Inertial Angle Of Rotation\n \nReports a V5 Inertial Sensor's current angle of rotation in degrees.\n\n```python\ninertial.rotation(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `inertial.rotation` command reports a positive value when the Inertial Sensor turns in the clockwise direction.\n\n`inertial.rotation` reports a negative value when the Inertial Sensor turns in the counter-clockwise direction.","signature":"inertial.rotation(DEGREES)"},"sensing_inertial_set_heading":{"name":"sensing_inertial_set_heading","markdown":"# Set Inertial Heading\n\nSets the Inertial sensor's current heading position to a set value.\n\n```don\ninertial.set_heading(HEADING, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`inertial.set_heading` can be used to set the Inertial Sensor's position to any clockwise heading. This command is typically used to reset the orientation of the Inertial Sensor when the heading is set to a value of 0.\n\n`inertial.set_heading` is typically used to reset the heading orientation of the Inertial sensor when a value is set to 0.\n\n`inertial.set_heading` accepts a range of 0.0 to 359.99 degrees.\n\n`inertial.set_heading` can accept decimals, integers, or numeric values.","signature":"inertial.set_heading(0, DEGREES)"},"sensing_inertial_set_rotation":{"name":"sensing_inertial_set_rotation","markdown":"# Set Inertial Rotation\n\nSets a V5 Inertial Sensor's angle of rotation to a given value.\n\n```python\ninertial.set_rotation(ROTATION, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `inertial.set_rotation` command can be used to set the inertial sensor's angle of rotation to any given positive (clockwise) or negative (counter-clockwise) value.\n\n`inertial.set_rotation` accepts a range of any positive or negative decimal or integer.","signature":"inertial.set_rotation(0, DEGREES)"},"sensing_left_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_left_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Left Bumper pressed\n\nReturns if the Left Bumper is pressed.\n\n```python\nleft_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `left_bumper.pressed()` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Bumper is pressed and **False** when the Bumper is not pressed.\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.  \n\n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `left_bumper.pressed()` command is used as a Boolean statement.  If the Left Bumper sensor is not pressed, the Drivetrain will move forward.  If the Left Bumper sensor is pressed, the Drivetrain will stop.  \n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    if left_bumper.pressed():\n        drivetrain.stop()\n    else:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"left_bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_light_brightness":{"name":"sensing_light_brightness","markdown":"# Light Sensor Brightness\n\nReports the amount of light detected by the Light Sensor.\n\n```don\nlight.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`light.brightness` reports a range from **0% to 100%**.\n\nA large amount of light detected will report a high brightness value.\n\nA small amount of light detected will report a low brightness value.","signature":"light.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_limit_switch_pressing":{"name":"sensing_limit_switch_pressing","markdown":"# Pressing Limit Switch\n\nReports if the Limit Switch is currently being pressed.\n\n```don\nlimit_switch.pressing()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nPressing limit reports **True** if the Limit Switch is being pressed.\n\nPressing limit reports **False** if the Limit Switch is not being pressed.","signature":"limit_switch.pressing()"},"sensing_line_tracker_reflectivity":{"name":"sensing_line_tracker_reflectivity","markdown":"# Line Tracker Reflectivity\n\nReports the amount of light reflected using the Line Tracker sensor.\n\n```don\nline_tracker.reflectivity(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nLight-colored surfaces will reflect more light and will report a high reflectivity value.\n\nDark-colored surfaces will reflect less light and will report a low reflectivity value.\n\nThe default unit is `PERCENT` that returns an integer value from **0% to 100%**.","signature":"line_tracker.reflectivity(PERCENT)"},"sensing_location_position":{"name":"sensing_location_position","markdown":"# Position \n\nReports the X or Y coordinate position of the Robot.\n\n```python\nlocation.position(AXIS, UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)` returns the X or Y coordinate position of the VR Robot as a numerical value.\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)`  command can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter. \n\n## Example\n\nThis project uses the location.position(AXIS, UNITS) command to code the VR robot to move to the number 28 on the Numbered Grid World Playground. The location.position(AXIS, UNITS) command is used as a Boolean expression in this project. \n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(X, MM) < 500:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT,90,DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(Y, MM) < -500:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"location.position(X, MM)"},"sensing_location_position_angle":{"name":"sensing_location_position_angle","markdown":"# Position Angle in Degrees\n\nReports angle of the Robot in degrees.\n\n```python\nlocation.position_angle(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `location.position_angle(DEGREES)` command returns the current angle of the VR robot as a numerical value.  \n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)` command can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"location.position_angle(DEGREES)"},"sensing_motor_current":{"name":"sensing_motor_current","markdown":"# Motor Current\n\nReports the amount of current a V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group is using in amperes (amps).\n\n```python\nmotor.current(CurrentUnits.AMP)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nMotor current reports a range from **0.0 to 2.5 amps**.","signature":"motor.current(CurrentUnits.AMP)"},"sensing_motor_efficiency":{"name":"sensing_motor_efficiency","markdown":"# Motor Efficiency\n \nReports the efficiency of a V5 Smart Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group.\n\n```python\nmotor.efficiency(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nEfficiency of motor reports the value of the power (in watts) the Motor or Motor Group is using (input), versus the amount of power (in watts) the Motor or Motor Group is providing (output).\n\nA V5 Smart Motor or Motor Group typically reaches a maximum efficiency of 65% under normal use cases.\n\nEfficiency of motor reports a range from **0% to 100%**.","signature":"motor.efficiency(PERCENT)"},"sensing_motor_is_done":{"name":"sensing_motor_is_done","markdown":"# Motor Is Done \n\nReports if the selected V5 Motor or Motor Group has completed its movement.\n\n```python\nmotor.is_done()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nMotor is done reports **True** when the selected Motor or Motor Group has completed its movement.\n\nMotor is done reports **False** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is still moving.","signature":"motor.is_done()"},"sensing_motor_is_done_viqc":{"name":"sensing_motor_is_done_viqc","markdown":"# Motor Is Done \n\nReports if an IQ Motor has completed its movement.\n\n```python\nmotor.is_done()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Motor Is Done` reports **True** when the selected Motor has completed its movement.\n\n`Motor Is Done` reports **False** when the selected Motor is still moving.","signature":"motor.is_done()"},"sensing_motor_is_spinning":{"name":"sensing_motor_is_spinning","markdown":"# Motor Is Spinning\n\nReports if the selected V5 Motor or Motor Group is currently spinning.\n\n```python\nmotor.is_spinning()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nMotor is spinning reports **True** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is still moving.\n\nMotor is spinning reports **False** when the selected Motor or Motor Group is stopped.","signature":"motor.is_spinning()"},"sensing_motor_is_spinning_viqc":{"name":"sensing_motor_is_spinning_viqc","markdown":"# Motor Is Spinning\n\nReports if an IQ Motor is currently spinning.\n\n```python\nmotor.is_spinning()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Motor Is Spinning` reports **True** when the selected Motor is still moving.\n\n`Motor Is Spinning` reports **False** when the selected Motor is stopped.","signature":"motor.is_spinning()"},"sensing_motor_position":{"name":"sensing_motor_position","markdown":"# Motor Position\n\nReports the current rotational position of the selected V5 Motor or the first motor of the specified Motor Group.\n\n```python\nmotor.position(UNIT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nMotor position reports the position of the V5 Motor or the first motor in a Motor Group in integer numbers for degrees, and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nAcceptable values for `UNIT` are: **DEGREES** or **TURNS**.","signature":"motor.position(DEGREES)"},"sensing_motor_position_viqc":{"name":"sensing_motor_position_viqc","markdown":"# Motor Position\n\nReports the current rotational position of the selected IQ Motor.\n\n```python\nmotor.position(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Motor Position` reports the position of an IQ Motor.\n\nAcceptable values for `UNITS` are: **DEGREES** or **TURNS**.","signature":"motor.position(UNITS)"},"sensing_motor_power":{"name":"sensing_motor_power","markdown":"# Motor Power\n\nReports the amount of power output a V5 Smart Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group is currently generating.\n\n```python\nmotor.power(PowerUnits.WATT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nMotor power reports a range from **0.0 to 11.0 watts**.","signature":"motor.power(PowerUnits.WATT)"},"sensing_motor_temperature":{"name":"sensing_motor_temperature","markdown":"# Motor Temperature\n\nReports the temperature of a V5 Smart Motor or the first motor in a Motor Group.\n\n```python\nmotor.temperature(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nUsing the `temperature` command can help users develop logic to keep their V5 Smart Motors or Motor Groups from overheating during heavy use.\n\nMotor temperature reports a range from **0% to 100%**.","signature":"motor.temperature(PERCENT)"},"sensing_motor_torque":{"name":"sensing_motor_torque","markdown":"# Motor Torque\n\nReports the amount of torque (rotational force) a V5 Smart Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group is currently using.\n\n```python\nmotor.torque(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`motor.torque` reports a range from **0.0 to 18.6** inch-pounds (InLB) if the provided **UNITS** parameter is `TorqueUnits.INLB`.\n\nAlternatively, a range from **0.0 to 2.1** Newton-meters (Nm) is reported if the provided **UNITS** parameter is `TorqueUnits.NM`.","signature":"motor.torque(UNITS)"},"sensing_motor_velocity":{"name":"sensing_motor_velocity","markdown":"# Motor Velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of a V5 Motor or the first motor of a Motor Group.\n\n```python\nmotor.velocity(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nMotor velocity reports a range from **-100% to 100%**.","signature":"motor.velocity(UNITS)"},"sensing_motor_velocity_viqc":{"name":"sensing_motor_velocity_viqc","markdown":"# Motor Velocity\n\nReports the current velocity of an IQ Motor.\n\n```python\nmotor.velocity(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nMotor velocity reports a range from **-100 to 100**.","signature":"motor.velocity(UNITS)"},"sensing_optical_brightness":{"name":"sensing_optical_brightness","markdown":"# Optical Brightness\n\nReports the amount of light detected by the Optical Sensor.\n\n```don\noptical.brightness()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`optical.brightness()` reports a range from **0%** to **100%**\n\nA large amount of light detected will report a high brightness value.\n\nA small amount of light detected will report a low brightness value.","signature":"optical.brightness()"},"sensing_optical_brightness_viqc":{"name":"sensing_optical_brightness_viqc","markdown":"# Optical Brightness\n\nReports the amount of light detected by a VEX IQ Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.brightness()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Optical Brightness` reports a range of values from **0 to 100** percent.\n\nA large amount of light detected will report a high brightness value.\n\nA small amount of light detected will report a low brightness value.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.print(\"Brightness:\", optical.brightness())\n```","signature":"optical.brightness()"},"sensing_optical_color_found":{"name":"sensing_optical_color_found","markdown":"# Optical Color Detection\n\nReports the color that the Optical Sensor is detecting.\n\n```python\noptical.color()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`optical.color()` will return a RGB (Red, Green, Blue) value that corresponds to a color. You can use the `Color` enums to compare to the value returned by `optical.color()`\n\nYou can compare to the following values\n\n* Color.BLACK\n* Color.WHITE\n* Color.RED\n* Color.GREEN\n* Color.BLUE\n* Color.YELLOW\n* Color.ORANGE\n* Color.PURPLE\n* Color.CYAN\n\nYou can use these in a conditional statement like this:\n\n```python\nif optical.color() == Color.RED:\n    brain.screen.print(\"Red Object\")\n```","signature":"optical.color()"},"sensing_optical_color_viqc":{"name":"sensing_optical_color_viqc","markdown":"# Optical Color\n\nReturns the color detected by a VEX IQ Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.color()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Color` command returns the closest color match base on the hue of a detected object.\n\nThe following colors can be used as a comparison to the color detected by an IQ Optical Sensor:\n\n- `Color.RED`\n- `Color.GREEN`\n- `Color.BLUE`\n- `Color.YELLOW`\n- `Color.ORANGE`\n- `Color.PURPLE`\n- `Color.CYAN`\n\n## Example\n\nThe example prints to a VEX IQ Brain's screen if a red object is detected by an Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\nif optical.color() == Color.RED:\n    brain.screen.print(\"Red!\")\n```","signature":"optical.color()"},"sensing_optical_gesture_detected":{"name":"sensing_optical_gesture_detected","markdown":"# Optical Gesture Detected\n\nReports detected gesture data from a V5 Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.get_gesture()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Gesture Detected` command returns an object with gesture information. To check the type of the detected gesture, access the `type` property on the returned object.\n\n```python\noptical.get_gesture().type\n```\n\nBefore using any V5 Optical Sensor gesture commands, a V5 Optical Sensor must be correctly set to detect gestures with the `optical.gesture_enable()` command.\n\nBelow are the gestures that a V5 Optical Sensor can detect:\n\n- `GestureType.UP`\n- `GestureType.DOWN`\n- `GestureType.LEFT`\n- `GestureType.RIGHT`\n\n## Example\n\nThe example shows how to correctly set a V5 Optical Sensor to detect gestures before checking for gestures.\n\n```python\noptical.gesture_enable()\nwhile optical.get_gesture().type != GestureType.UP:\n    wait(0.1, SECONDS)\nbrain.screen.print(\"Up!\")\n```","signature":"optical.get_gesture()"},"sensing_optical_hue":{"name":"sensing_optical_hue","markdown":"# Optical Hue\nReports the hue of the color of an object.\n\n```don\noptical.hue()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`optical.hue()` reports a number value that is the hue of the color of an object. \n\nIt returns a number between **0** and **359**, which can be thought of as the location of the color on a color wheel.","signature":"optical.hue()"},"sensing_optical_hue_viqc":{"name":"sensing_optical_hue_viqc","markdown":"# Optical Hue\n\nReports the hue of the object detected by a VEX IQ Optical Sensor.\n\n```python\noptical.hue()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Optical Hue` reports a range of values from **0 to 359**, which represents the location of the detected color on a color wheel.\n\n```python\nbrain.screen.print(\"Hue: \", optical.hue())\n```","signature":"optical.hue()"},"sensing_optical_is_near_object_viqc":{"name":"sensing_optical_is_near_object_viqc","markdown":"# Optical Is Near Object\n\nReports if a VEX IQ Optical Sensor detects an object in its range.\n\n```python\noptical.is_near_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`Optical Is Near Object` returns **True** if an object is detected, and returns **False** otherwise.\n\nIt should be used to check if an object is close to the IQ Optical Sensor so that the color readings from the Optical Sensor's `Color` command is accurate.\n\n```python\nif optical.is_near_object() and optical.color() == Color.Red:\n    brain.screen.print(\"Red object!\")\n```","signature":"optical.is_near_object()"},"sensing_optical_object_found":{"name":"sensing_optical_object_found","markdown":"# Optical Near Object\n\nReports if the Optical Sensor detects an object close to it.\n\n```don\noptical.is_near_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`optical.is_near_object()` reports **True** if an object is close enough to the Optical Sensor to get other sensing information.\n\n`optical.is_near_object()` reports **False** if an object is not close enough to the Optical Sensor to get accurate sensor readings.","signature":"optical.is_near_object()"},"sensing_optical_set_light":{"name":"sensing_optical_set_light","markdown":"# Set Optical Light\nSets the light on the Optical Sensor to **on** or **off**.\n\n```don\noptical.set_light(LedStateType.ON)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`optical.set_light(LedStateType.ON)` allows you to turn the Optical Sensor's light **on** or **off**. The light lets the sensor see objects if it is looking at an object in a dark area.\n\nTo turn the light **on** you pass `LedStateType.ON`\n\n```\noptical.set_light(LedStateType.ON)\n```\n\nTo turn the light **off** you pass `LedStateType.OFF`\n\n```\noptical.set_light(LedStateType.OFF)\n```","signature":"optical.set_light(LedStateType.ON)"},"sensing_optical_set_light_power":{"name":"sensing_optical_set_light_power","markdown":"# Set Optical Light Power\nSets the light power of V5 Optical sensor.\n\n```don\noptical.set_light_power(50, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`optical.set_light_power(50, PERCENT)` will change the brightness of the light on the Optical Sensor. \n\nThe command accepts a range of **0%** to **100%** for the intensity, and **PERCENT** as the unit. If the light is off, this command will turn the light on.","signature":"optical.set_light_power(50, PERCENT)"},"sensing_optical_set_mode_disable":{"name":"sensing_optical_set_mode_disable","markdown":"# Optical Gesture Disable\n\nSets a V5 Optical Sensor to detect colors.\n\n```python\noptical.gesture_disable()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Gesture Disable` command allows you to set a V5 Optical Sensor to detect colors.\n\nBy default, a V5 Optical Sensor will be set to detect colors.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example shows how to correctly set a V5 Optical Sensor to detect colors after checking for gestures.\n\n```python\n# Detect gestures\noptical.gesture_enable()\nwhile optical.get_gesture().type != GestureType.UP:\n    wait(0.1, SECONDS)\nbrain.screen.print(\"Up!\")\n\nwait(1, SECONDS)\n\n# Detect colors\noptical.gesture_disable()\nwhile True:\n    brain.screen.clear_screen()\n    brain.screen.set_cursor(1, 1)\n    if optical.color() == Color.RED:\n        brain.screen.print(\"Red Object!\")\n    else:\n        brain.screen.print(\"Not Red Object!\")\n    wait(0.02, SECONDS)\n\n```","signature":"optical.gesture_disable()"},"sensing_optical_set_mode_enable":{"name":"sensing_optical_set_mode_enable","markdown":"# Optical Gesture Enable\n\nSets a V5 Optical Sensor to detect gestures.\n\n```python\noptical.gesture_enable()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Optical Gesture Enable` command allows you to set a V5 Optical Sensor to detect gestures.\n \nBy default, a V5 Optical Sensor will be set to detect colors.\n\nBefore using any V5 Optical Sensor gesture commands, a V5 Optical Sensor must be correctly set to detect gestures.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example shows how to correctly set a V5 Optical Sensor to detect gestures before checking for gestures.\n\n```python\noptical.gesture_enable()\nwhile optical.get_gesture().type != GestureType.UP:\n    wait(0.1, SECONDS)\nbrain.screen.print(\"Up!\")\n```","signature":"optical.gesture_enable()"},"sensing_potentiometer_angle":{"name":"sensing_potentiometer_angle","markdown":"# Potentiometer Angle\n\nReturns the angular position of the Potentiometer.\n\n```don\npotentiometer.angle(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`potentiometer.angle` returns a decimal value to report the angular position of the Potentiometer.\n\nThe `UNITS` parameter accepts either **DEGREES** or **PERCENT** as an argument.\n\nIf the provided `UNITS` is in **DEGREES**, the range of returned values will be from **0.0 to 250.0 degrees** for a V5 Potentiometer, and from **0.0 to 330.0** degrees for a V5 Potentiometer V2.\n\nAlternatively, the returned values will range from **0 to 100%** if the provided `UNITS` is in **PERCENT**.","signature":"potentiometer.angle(UNITS)"},"sensing_range_finder_distance":{"name":"sensing_range_finder_distance","markdown":"# Distance From Range Finder\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object from the Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor.\n\n```don\nrange_finder.distance(UNITS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nDistance from reports a range from 30mm to 3000mm or 2 inches to 115 inches.\n\nThe `UNITS` parameter accepts either **MM** or **INCHES** as a value.","signature":"range_finder.distance(MM)"},"sensing_range_finder_found_object":{"name":"sensing_range_finder_found_object","markdown":"# Range Finder Found Object\n\nReports if the Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor detects an object within its field of view.\n\n```don\nrange_finder.found_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nObject found reports **True** when the Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view.\n\nObject found reports **False** when the Ultrasonic Range Finder sensor does not detect an object or surface.","signature":"range_finder.found_object()"},"sensing_remote_control_code_enabled":{"name":"sensing_remote_control_code_enabled","markdown":"# Controller Enabled\n\nEnables or disables Controller-configured actions from the Devices menu.\n\n```don\nremote_control_code_enabled = False\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose to either enable or disable the configured Controller actions. \n\nBy default, the Controller is Enabled in every project.","signature":"remote_control_code_enabled = False"},"sensing_right_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_right_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Right Bumper pressed\n\nReturns if the Right Bumper is pressed\n\n```python\nright_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `right_bumper.pressed()` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Bumper is pressed and **False** when the Bumper is not pressed.\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.  \n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `right_bumper.pressed()` command is used as a Boolean statement.  If the Right Bumper sensor is not pressed, the Drivetrain will move forward.  If the Right Bumper sensor is pressed, the Drivetrain will stop.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    if right_bumper.pressed():\n        drivetrain.stop()\n    else:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"right_bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_rotation_angle":{"name":"sensing_rotation_angle","markdown":"# Rotation Angle\nReturns the angular position of a Rotation Sensor.\n\n```don\nrotation.angle()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`rotation.angle()` returns a decimal value with the current angular position of a Rotation Sensor in degrees.","signature":"rotation.angle()"},"sensing_rotation_position":{"name":"sensing_rotation_position","markdown":"# Rotation Position\nReports the current rotational position of a Rotation Sensor.\n\n```don\nrotation.position(UNIT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`rotation.position(UNIT)`  reports the position of a Rotation Sensor in integer numbers for degrees, and decimal numbers for turns.\n\nAcceptable values for ```UNIT``` are: ```DEGREES``` or ```TURNS```.","signature":"rotation.position(DEGREES)"},"sensing_rotation_set_position":{"name":"sensing_rotation_set_position","markdown":"# Set Rotation Position\nSets a Rotation Sensor's position to the given value.\n\n```don\nrotation.set_position(0, DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`rotation.set_position(0, DEGREES)` can be used to set a Rotation Sensor to a given positional value.\n\nUsually, `rotation.set_position(0, DEGREES)` will be used to reset a Rotation Sensor to 0 degrees.","signature":"rotation.set_position(0, DEGREES)"},"sensing_rotation_velocity":{"name":"sensing_rotation_velocity","markdown":"# Rotation Velocity\nReports the current velocity of a Rotation Sensor.\n\n```don\nrotation.velocity(UNIT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n`rotation.velocity(UNIT)` will return the velocity of a Rotation Sensor in the ```UNIT``` that is passed.\n\nAcceptable values for the ```UNIT``` parameter are:\n\n* **VelocityUnits.RPM**\n* **VelocityUnits.DPS**","signature":"rotation.velocity(PERCENT)"},"sensing_rover_angle":{"name":"sensing_rover_angle","markdown":"# Rover Direction\n\nReports the direction of the specified object relative to the front-facing direction of the VR Rover.\n\n```python\nrover.angle(object)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n### Enemy and Food Direction\n\nThe `Rover Direction` command reports the direction in degrees of an object within the VR Rover's vision range (1000 millimeters) and within its field of view.\n\nThe field of view of the VR Rover is 40 degrees. This can be seen in the mini-map on the Rover Rescue playground indicated by the grey translucent cone projecting from the front of the VR Rover.\n\nTo check for an enemy or minerals's direction relative to the VR Rover, pass either **ENEMY** or **MINERALS** as the `object` parameter.\n\nIf there are multiple instances of the specified object in the VR Rover's vision range, the angle of the closest relevant object will be returned.\n\n### Base Direction\n\nHowever, if **BASE** is passed as the `object` parameter, the direction of the base relative to the VR Rover's heading will always be returned, even if the base is not in the vision range of the VR Rover.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below uses the `Rover Direction` command to orient towards a minerals object. After driving and picking up the minerals object, the command is then used to navigate the VR Rover the base to drop off the minerals.\n\n```python\n# Orient and drive to minerals\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, rover.angle(MINERALS), DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, rover.get_distance(MINERALS, MM), MM)\n\nbrain.pickup(MINERALS)\n\n# Orient and drive to the base\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, rover.angle(BASE), DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, rover.get_distance(BASE, MM), MM)\n\nrover.drop(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"rover.angle(object)"},"sensing_rover_battery":{"name":"sensing_rover_battery","markdown":"# Battery Level\n\nReports the current battery level of the VR Rover.\n\n```python\nrover.battery()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Battery Level` command reports the current battery level of the VR Rover from **0% to 100%**.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example checks the VR Rover's battery level, using minerals if the level is below 25%.\n\n```python\nif rover.battery() < 25:\n    rover.use(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"rover.battery()"},"sensing_rover_detects":{"name":"sensing_rover_detects","markdown":"# Rover Detects\n\nReports whether the VR Rover detects an enemy or minerals in its smelling range.\n\n```python\nrover.detects(object)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe detect radius of the VR Rover is 800 millimeters (mm). This can be seen in the mini-map on the Rover Rescue playground indicated by the yellow circle around the VR Rover.\n\nTo use the `Rover Detects` command, pass either **MINERALS** or **ENEMY** as the `object` parameter.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below checks to see if there's minerals in the smelling range of the VR Rover. If so, the Rover will turn until it sees the minerals, then drives towards it.\n\n```python\nif rover.detects(MINERALS):\n    drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n    while not rover.sees(MINERALS):\n        wait(0.1, SECONDS)\n    drivetrain.stop()\n    \n    # After it sees the minerals, drive towards it\n    # and pick it up\n    drivetrain.go_to(MINERALS)\n    rover.pickup(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"rover.detects(object)"},"sensing_rover_distance":{"name":"sensing_rover_distance","markdown":"# Rover Distance\n\nReports the distance from the VR Rover to minerals, enemies, obstacles, hazards, or the base.\n\n```python\nrover.get_distance(object, units)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Rover Distance` command works slightly different depending on which detectable object is passed as the `object` parameter.\n\nThe `units` parameter can take either **MM** or **INCHES** to return values in millimeters (mm) or inches, respectively.\n\n### Enemy and Food Distance\n\nWhen **ENEMY** or **MINERALS** is passed as the `object` parameter, the `Rover Distance` command reports the distance of the closest relevant object in its vision range (1000 millimeters and 40-degree field of view).\n\nIf there are no objects in its vision range of the specified type, the value returned is 0 mm/inches.\n\n### Obstacle and Hazard Distance\n\nWhen **OBSTACLE** or **HAZARD** is passed as the `object` parameter, the `Rover Distance` command reports the distance of the closest obstacle or hazard in its vision range (1000 millimeters and 40-degree field of view).\n\nIf there are no obstacles or hazards in its vision range, the value returned is 1000 mm or 39.37 inches.\n\n### Base Distance\n\nHowever, if **BASE** is passed as the `object` parameter, the distance from the base is always reported, regardless of whether the VR Rover can actively see the base in its vision range.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below uses the `Rover Distance` command to get the distance from the VR Rover to minerals and the base.\n\n```python\n# Orient and drive to minerals\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, rover.angle(MINERALS), DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, rover.get_distance(MINERALS, MM), MM)\n\nrover.pickup(MINERALS)\n\n# Orient and drive to the base\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, rover.angle_of(BASE), DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, rover.get_distance(BASE, MM), MM)\n\nrover.drop(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"rover.get_distance(object)"},"sensing_rover_enemy_level":{"name":"sensing_rover_enemy_level","markdown":"# Enemy Level\n\nReports the level of the closest enemy detected in the VR Rover's vision.\n\n```python\nrover.enemy_level()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Enemy Level` command reports the level of the closest enemy detected in the VR Rover's vision.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example checks to see if the enemy detected is at or below the VR Rover's level before deciding whether to drive towards and absorb radiation from it or not.\n\n```python\nif rover.enemy_level() <= rover.level():\n    drivetrain.go_to(ENEMY)\n    rover.absorb_radiation(ENEMY)\n```","signature":"rover.enemy_level()"},"sensing_rover_enemy_radiation":{"name":"sensing_rover_enemy_radiation","markdown":"# Enemy Radiation\n\nReports the radiation of the closest enemy detected in the VR Rover's vision.\n\n```python\nrover.enemy_radiation()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Enemy Radiation` command reports the radiation of the closest enemy detected in the VR Rover's vision.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example checks to see if the enemy detected is at or below the VR Rover's level before deciding whether to drive towards and absorb radiation from it or not.\n\nIf the VR Rover absorbs the enemy, it will then also print the enemy's radiation after each absorb.\n\n```python\nif rover.enemy_level() <= rover.level():\n    drivetrain.go_to(ENEMY)\n    \n    # Loop to check if enemy is still alive\n    while rover.get_distance(ENEMY, MM) < 100:\n        rover.absorb_radiation(ENEMY)\n        brain.print(\"Enemy Radiation:\", rover.enemy_radiation())\n        brain.new_line()\n        wait(0.02, SECONDS)\n```","signature":"rover.enemy_radiation()"},"sensing_rover_exp":{"name":"sensing_rover_exp","markdown":"# Rover Experience\n\nReports the number of Experience Points (XP) that the VR Rover has at the current level.\n\n```python\nrover.exp()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Rover Experience` command reports the number of Experience Points (XP) that the VR Rover has at the current level.\n\nThe VR Rover collects XP by using minerals, bringing minerals to the base, and absorbing enemies.\n\n| VR Rover Action                       | Earned XP     |\n|---------------------------------------|---------------|\n| Using minerals                        | 2 XP          |\n| Bringing minerals to the base         | 5 XP          |\n| Neutralizing a VR Spider              | 5 XP          |\n| Neutralizing a blue/orange VR Serpent | 10 XP         |\n| Neutralizing a purple VR Serpent      | 15 XP         |\n\n## Example\n\nThis example prints the amount of experience the VR Rover has at the current level.\n\n```python\nbrain.print(\"Exp:\", rover.exp())\n```","signature":"rover.exp()"},"sensing_rover_level":{"name":"sensing_rover_level","markdown":"# Rover Level\n\nReports the current level of the VR Rover.\n\n```python\nrover.level()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Rover Level` command reports the current level of the VR Rover.\n\nThe VR Rover begins at Level 1 and moves up a level after colling a certain amount of Experience Points (XP).\n\n| Level | Total XP Required    | VR Rover Color|\n|-------|----------------------|---------------|\n| 1     | 0 XP                 | Blue          |\n| 2     | 10 XP                | Black         |\n| 3     | 30 XP                | Orange        |\n| 4     | 70 XP                | Red           |\n| 5     | 125 XP               | Purple        |\n\n## Example\n\nThis example prints the current level of the VR Rover.\n\n```python\nbrain.print(\"Level:\", rover.level())\n```","signature":"rover.level()"},"sensing_rover_location":{"name":"sensing_rover_location","markdown":"# Rover Location\n\nReports the X or Y coordinate location of minerals, enemies, obstacles, hazards, or the base.\n\n```python\nrover.location(object, axis, units)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Rover Location` command works slightly different depending on which detectable object is passed as the `object` parameter.\n\nThe `axis` parameter can take either **X** or **Y** to return values either on the X or Y axis, respectively.\n\nThe `units` parameter can take either **MM** or **INCHES** to return values in millimeters (mm) or inches, respectively.\n\n### Enemy/Minerals/Obstacle/Hazard Location\n\nWhen **ENEMY**, **MINERALS**, **OBSTACLE**, or **HAZARD** is passed as the `object` parameter, the `Rover Location` command reports the location of the closest relevant object detected in its vision range (1000 millimeters and 40-degree field of view).\n\nIf there are no objects in its vision range of the specified type, the value returned is 0 mm/inches.\n\n### Base Location\n\nHowever, if **BASE** is passed as the `object` parameter, the location of the base is always reported, regardless of whether the VR Rover can actively see the base in its vision range.\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below uses the `Rover Location` command to print the coordinates of the base, which never changes regardless of where the VR Rover is on the map.\n\n```python\nbrain.print(\"X:\", rover.location(BASE, X, MM), \" \")\nbrain.print(\"Y:\", rover.location(BASE, Y, MM))\n```","signature":"rover.location(object, axis, units)"},"sensing_rover_minerals_stored":{"name":"sensing_rover_minerals_stored","markdown":"# Minerals in Storage\n\nReports the current amount of minerals the VR Rover has in its storage.\n\n```python\nrover.minerals_stored()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Minerals in Storage` command reports the current number of minerals in the backpack of the VR Rover.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example compares the amount of minerals stored against the storage capacity to know if the VR Rover can still carry more minerals.\n\n```python\nif rover.minerals_stored() < rover.storage_capacity():\n    brain.print(\"Can store more minerals!\")\n```","signature":"rover.minerals_stored()"},"sensing_rover_position":{"name":"sensing_rover_position","markdown":"# Brain Position\n\nReports the current X or Y coordinate position of the VR Rover.\n\n```python\nbrain.position(axis, units)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Brain Position` command reports the current X or Y coordinate position of the VR Rover.\n\nYou can replace the `axis` parameter with either **X** or **Y** to get the position along the X or Y axis, respectively.\n\nThen, replace the `units` parameter with either **MM** or **INCHES** to get values in millimeters or inches.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example prints the current X and Y coordinate position of the VR Rover.\n\n```python\nbrain.print(\"X:\", brain.position(X, MM), \" \")\nbrain.print(\"Y:\", brain.position(Y, MM))\n```","signature":"rover.position(axis, units)"},"sensing_rover_sees":{"name":"sensing_rover_sees","markdown":"# Rover Sees\n\nReports whether the VR Rover sees detectable objects in its vision range.\n\n```python\nrover.sees(object)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe vision range of the VR Rover is 1000 millimeters (mm) away from the Rover and within its field of view.\n\nThe field of view of the VR Rover is 40 degrees. This can be seen in the mini-map on the Rover Rescue playground indicated by the grey translucent cone projecting from the front of the VR Rover.\n\nTo use the `Rover Sees` command, pass any of the following options as the `object` parameter to check whether the specified object is in the vision range.\n\n- MINERALS\n- ENEMY\n- BASE\n- OBSTACLE\n- HAZARD\n\n## Example\n\nThe example below uses the `Rover Sees` command to stop turning once minerals appears in its vision range.\n\n```python\nif rover.detects(MINERALS):\n    drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n    while not rover.sees(MINERALS):\n        wait(0.1, SECONDS)\n    drivetrain.stop()\n    \n    # After it sees the minerals, drive towards it\n    # and pick it up\n    drivetrain.go_to(MINERALS)\n    rover.pickup(MINERALS)\n```","signature":"rover.sees(object)"},"sensing_rover_storage_capacity":{"name":"sensing_rover_storage_capacity","markdown":"# Rover Capacity\n\nReports the carrying capacity of the VR Rover.\n\n```python\nrover.storage_capacity()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Rover Capacity` command reports the carrying capacity of the VR Rover.\n\nThe VR Rover begins by being able to carry two minerals in its storage. As the Rover levels up, the capacity increases by one at each level.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example prints if the VR Rover has capacity to carry more minerals.\n\n```python\nif rover.storage_capacity() > rover.minerals_stored():\n    brain.print(\"Can carry more\")\n```","signature":"rover.storage_capacity()"},"sensing_rover_under_attack":{"name":"sensing_rover_under_attack","markdown":"# Rover Under Attack\n\nReports whether the VR Rover is under attack from an enemy. \n\n```python\nrover.under_attack()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `Rover Under Attack` command reports whether the VR Rover is under attack from an enemy.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example prints drives the VR Rover until it detects an enemy attack, then stops.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwhile not rover.under_attack():\n    wait(0.02, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"rover.under_attack()"},"sensing_vision_largest_object":{"name":"sensing_vision_largest_object","markdown":"# Largest Object\n\nReports information about the largest detected object from the Vision Sensor.\n\n```python\nvision.largest_object()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nConfigure the Vision Sensor by adding signatures / codes.\n\nA call to the `vision.take_snapshot` command is then required to capture an image in the Vision Sensor prior to using the `vision.largest_object` command.\n\nThe example below will print some information about a detected object onto the V5 Brain's screen.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    brain.screen.clear_screen()\n    brain.screen.set_cursor(1, 1)\n\n    # Take a snapshot with the Vision Sensor with the specified signature and store the object data into a variable\n    vision_object = vision.take_snapshot(signature)\n\n    # Check the variable to see if a valid object was detected when the snapshot was captured\n    # If yes, print the data\n    if vision_object is not None:\n        brain.screen.print(\"Center X: \", vision.largest_object().centerX)\n        brain.screen.next_row()\n\n        brain.screen.print(\"Center Y: \", vision.largest_object().centerY)\n        brain.screen.next_row()\n\n        # Take a new snapshot every 0.2 seconds\n        wait(0.2, SECONDS)\n    else:\n        brain.screen.print(\"No Object Detected\")\n```\n\nThe following is a list of properties that can be accessed on the object returned by the `vision.largest_object` command:\n\n- `id` - A unique ID assigned to each object by the Vision Sensor\n- `originX` - The top left X position of the object\n- `originY` - The top left Y position of the object\n- `centerX` - The center X position of the object\n- `centerY` - The center Y position of the object\n- `width` - The width of the object\n- `height` - The height of the object\n- `angle` - The angle of the object\n- `exists` - If the Vision Sensor detects the object or not","signature":"vision.largest_object()"},"sensing_vision_take_snapshot":{"name":"sensing_vision_take_snapshot","markdown":"# Snapshot\n\nTakes a snapshot from the Vision Sensor.\n\n```don\nvision.take_snapshot(SIGNATURE)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe snapshot command will capture the current image from the Vision Sensor to be processed and analyzed for color signatures and codes.\n\nTypically, a snapshot is required first before using any other Vision Sensor commands.","signature":"vision.take_snapshot(SIGNATURE)"},"variables_my_2d_list":{"name":"variables_my_2d_list","markdown":"# 2D List Variable\n\nSimilar to a List variable, 2D List variables are used to store multiple values in a single variable name.  However, 2D List variables store more than one list.  \n\n## How To Use\nThe default name of a 2D List variable is: \n\n```python\nmy_2d_list = [\n    [0,0,0],\n    [0,0,0],\n    [0,0,0]\n]\n```\n\nWhen renaming,  2D List variables have the same naming rules as all other variables:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n\nThe data elements in two dimensional arrays can be accessed using two indexes. An index is the location of a value in the List variable.  To access a value, first use the name of the 2D List and then the two indexes enclosed in square brackets.","signature":"my_2d_list = [\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0]\\n]"},"variables_my_boolean":{"name":"variables_my_boolean","markdown":"# Boolean Variable\n\nBoolean variables can hold either **True** or **False** values.\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo assign a Boolean variable a value, use the = operator.   \n\nThe default name of a numeric variable is my_boolean.  Apply  the following rules when renaming a numeric variable:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)","signature":"my_boolean = True"},"variables_my_list":{"name":"variables_my_list","markdown":"# List Variable\n\nList variables are used to store multiple values in a single variable name.\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe default name of a List variable is: `my_list = [0,0,0]`\n\nWhen renaming,  List variables have the same naming rules as all other variables:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n\nTo assign a list of values to a List variable, use the = operator followed by comma-separated values, called elements, enclosed in square brackets.  \n\nIf the values are numbers, then the commas should come right after each number. \n\n`my_list = [0,1,2]`\n\nIf the values are strings, the commas should come outside of the quotation marks.\n\n`my_list = [“Drive”,”Forward”]`\n\nThe data elements in a List variable can be accessed using an index. An index is the location of a value in the List variable.  Each element inside of an array is assigned an integer, beginning with zero. \n\n## Examples\n\nThe project uses list `my_list` in a for loop.  The values in `my_list` are strings.  The variable y is the iterator in the for loop.  Therefore, the for loop iterates, and then prints, each element within the List variable `my_list`.\n\n```python\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", ‘100%', \"fun!!\"]\nfor y in my_list:\n    brain.print(y)\n    brain.new_line()\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n```\n\nThis project will print:\n\n```\nVEXcode VR\nis\n100%\nfun!!\n```\n\nThis project does not need a for loop because it is just printing one element of the List variable `my_list`  To access one element, place the index of the element in square brackets after the name of the List variable.\n\n\n```python\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", '100%', \"fun!!\"]\nbrain.print(my_list[3])\nwait(5, MSEC)\n```  \n\nThis project will print:\n\n```\nfun!!\n```","signature":"my_list = [0,0,0]"},"variables_my_number":{"name":"variables_my_number","markdown":"# Numeric Variable\n\nNumeric variables are used to store numerical values.\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo assign a numeric variable a value, use the = operator.   \n\nThe default name of a numeric variable is my_number.  Apply  the following rules when renaming a numeric variable:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n\n## Example\n\nThis project will have the drivetrain drive forward for 150 mm and then turn right for 90 degrees. \n\n```don\ndistance_to_travel = 150\nangle_to_turn = 90\n\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, distance_to_travel, MM)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, angle_to_turn, DEGREES)\n```\nThis project created two variables and named them `distance_to_travel` and `angle_to_turn`.  A = is then used to assign a value to each variable. The variables are then used in the `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD)` and `drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT)` commands.","signature":"my_number = 1"},"variables_my_string":{"name":"variables_my_string","markdown":"# String Variable\n\nString variables are used to store strings, which is a sequence of characters.  Characters can include numbers, letters, and symbols. \n\n## How To Use\n\nTo assign a string variable a value, use the = operator.   \n\nThe default name of a numeric variable is my_string.  Apply  the following rules when renaming a numeric variable:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)","signature":"my_string = \\\"Hello World\\\""},"vr_thread":{"name":"vr_thread","markdown":"# VR Thread\n\nThe VR Thread function must be used to call functions at the global level of the script.\n\n## How To Use\n\nYou can take any function you have made previously, and pass it to `vr_thread()` at the global level. Calling at least one function inside of this is required to run a program. You do not need to do all function calls with `vr_thread()`, just ones that are at the global level.\n\nIf more than one `vr_thread()` call is made, the functions will execute in parallel.\n\nThe functions will, in general, execute in the order they are called. However, if a function waits, it will pass execution to the next function, and continue when the other function finishes, or when the other function also waits.\n\n```don\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nThe functions that cause threads to switch:\n\n```\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.set_heading(0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(0, DEGREES)\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nany user created function\n\n```\nCalling a method with `vr_thread()` inside of another method will cause a runtime error. If you run this program:\n\n```\ndef main():\n    vr_thread(print_hello)\n\ndef print_hello():\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nIt will give you an error that looks like this:\n\n```\nError: Traceback (most recent call last):\n  line 9, in vr_thread\n  line 5, in main\nNameError: name 'print_hello' is not defined\n```\n\n## Example 1\n\nWe make a single function that is executed in the global space with `vr_thread()`. The `move_drivetrain()` function will make the robot move 200 mm and then print when it is done.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain)\n```\n## Example 2\n\nNow we added the `move_pen_down()` function. This will move the pen down and print a message. We make the call `vr_thread(move_pen_down)` **after** the `vr_thread(move_drivetrain)` call. However, because `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)` makes the function wait, it will switch over to the `move_pen_down()` function, which will move the pen down before the robot is done moving.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain)\nvr_thread(move_pen_down)\n```\n\n## Example 3\n\nMoving the `move_pen_down()` in to the `move_drivetrain()` will now call it sequintially, so there will be no pen drawing since it doesn't drop until after the movement is finished. This also shows that calling functions within other functions can be done like normal function call.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n    move_pen_down()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain)\n```","signature":"vr_thread(function_name)"}}},"es":{"blocks":{"add":{"name":"add","markdown":"# Añadir\n\nAgrega dos valores juntos e informa la suma.\n\n![add](static/help/vr/blocks/es/add/add.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Añadir** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLa longitud de **add** bloque encaja en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\n\nAñadir dos números.\n\n![add_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es/add/add_numbers.png)\n\nAñadir con valores de variables.\n\n![add_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/es/add/add_variables.png)  \n\nAñadir con valores de sensores o dispositivos.\n\n![add_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es/add/add_sensors.png)\n\n"},"and":{"name":"and","markdown":"# Y\n\nInforma si dos condiciones booleanas son ambas verdaderas. \n\n![and](static/help/vr/blocks/es/and/and.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nEl bloque **y** reporta **true** cuando los dos condiciones son **true**. \n\nEl **y** bloque reporta **false** cuado uno (o los dos) condicione(s) son **false**.\n\nEl **y** bloque booleano reporta un **true** o **false** valor y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonal (de seis lados).\n\n![and_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/es/and/and_shape.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo comprobará si dos botones en el VEX IQ Controller se están presionando al mismo tiempo.\n\n![and_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es/and/and_sensors.png)\n\nEl **y** bloque volverá **true** cuando los dos **Controller E Up button** y el **Controller L Down button** están siendo presionados.\n\nEl **y** bloque volverá **false** cuando uno (o los dos) de los botones del control no se presionan.\n\n![and_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/es/and/and_truth.png)"},"boolean_variable":{"name":"boolean_variable","markdown":"# Variable BooleanaUse the\n\nReporta valor de una variable booleana.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es/boolean_variable/boolean_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nBloques de **Variable Booleana** se utilizan para reportar de un valor de **true** o **false** almacendo dentro de la variable. Utilice el **establecer variable booleana** bloque para establecer o actualizar el valor de una variable booleana.\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/es/boolean_variable/boolean_set_value.png)\n\nEl bloque **Variable Booleana** reporta **true** o **false** valor y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/boolean_variable/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nLas variables booleanas se crean utilizando el **Hacer un Booleano** botón.\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es/boolean_variable/boolean_button.png)    \n"},"break":{"name":"break","markdown":"# Romper\n\nSale de un bucle de repetición inmediatamente.\n\n![break](static/help/vr/blocks/es/break/break.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nCuando se añade a la repetición de bloque 'C', el bloque **romper** saldrá del bucle que se encuentra actualmente en. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo moverá el chasis hacia adelante y comprobará si el botón del VEX IQ Brain **up** esta presionado. \n\nSi el botón **up** esta **presionado**, el bloque **romper** saldrá del bucle de repetición para siempre y detendrá el chasis.\n\n![break_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/break/break_example.png)"},"bumper":{"name":"bumper","markdown":"# Presionando el Parachoque\n\nReporta si el Interruptor de Parachoques está pulsado.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/es/bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Presionando el Parachoque** reporta **true** si se presiona el interruptor de parachoques.\n\n**Presionando el Parachoque** reporta **false** si el interruptor de parachoques no está pulsado.\n\nElija el interruptor de parachoques que desea utilizar.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/es/bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano **Presionando el Parachoque** informa de un valor **true** o false y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis caras).\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **presionando el parachoque**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reportero\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es/bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es/bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)\n\n"},"change_variable":{"name":"change_variable","markdown":"# Cambiar Variable\n\nCambia una variable por el valor dado.\n\n![change_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es/change_variable/change_variable.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la variable que desea utilizar - la variable seleccionada también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![change_variable_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es/change_variable/change_variable_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **Cambiar Variable** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![change_variable_value](static/help/vr/blocks/es/change_variable/change_variable_value.png)\n\nEste ejemplo aumentará la variable que establece la velocidad del chasis por 10 cada vez **bucle de repetición** se repite.\n\n![change_variable_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/change_variable/change_variable_example2.png)\n"},"clear_all_rows":{"name":"clear_all_rows","markdown":"# Borrar todas las filas\n\nElimine todas las filas en Print Console.\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/es/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nUse el bloque ** eliminar todas las filas ** para eliminar todas las líneas en Print Console.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Hola\" por un segundo y luego borrará toda la pantalla antes de imprimir \"Adiós\".\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"comment":{"name":"comment","markdown":"# Comentario\n\nPermite a los programadores escribir información para ayudar a describir su programa.\n\n![comment_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/comment/comment_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nLos programadores profesionales utilizan comentarios a través de sus programas para ayudar a otros a entender su código. Los comentarios también pueden ayudar con la solución de problemas y la colaboración. \n\nAgregue texto en bloque **comentario** seleccionando primero el cuadrado transparente y eliminando cualquier texto existente. A continuación, escriba el nuevo texto.\n\n![comment_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/es/comment/comment_example3.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nLos comentarios no cambian el programa o los bloques que lo rodean.\n\n![comment_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/comment/comment_example2.png)\n\n"},"distance_distance":{"name":"distance_distance","markdown":"# Distancia de\n\nReporta la distancia del objeto más cercano.\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/distance_distance/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija si **distancia desde** se informa en milímetros o pulgadas. \n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/es/distance_distance/distance_from_unit.png)\n\nEl bloque de reportero **distancia desde** se utiliza dentro de bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/distance_distance/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **distancia desde**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reporteros.\n\n![distance_from_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es/distance_distance/distance_from_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![distance_from_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es/distance_distance/distance_from_monitor.png)\n\n"},"distance_found":{"name":"distance_found","markdown":"# Distancia encontrada objeto\n\nInforma si el sensor de distancia incorporado ha encontrado un objeto frente al robot.\n\n![distance_found_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/distance_found/distance_found_block.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Distance found object** reports **true** when the built in Distance sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view, and within 3000 mm of the sensor.\n\n**Distancia encontrada objeto** informa **falso** cuando el sensor de distancia incorporado no detecta un objeto o superficie dentro de 3000 mm.\n\nEl **objeto encontrado distancia** El bloque booleano informa un valor **verdadero** o **falso** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![distance_found_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/es/distance_found/distance_found_insert.png)\n\n\n\n\n"},"divide":{"name":"divide","markdown":"# Dividir\n\nDivide el primer valor por el segundo valor e informa del dividendo.\n\n![divide](static/help/vr/blocks/es/divide/divide.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Dividir** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **dividir** se adapta a bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nIntroduzca los números que se van a dividir.\n\n![divide_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es/divide/divide_numbers.png)\n\nDividir con valores de variables.\n\n![divide_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/es/divide/divide_variables.png)\n\nDividir con valores de sensores o dispositivos.\n\n![divide_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es/divide/divide_sensors.png)\n\n"},"drive":{"name":"drive","markdown":"# Conducir\n\nMueve el chasis por siempre.\n\n![drive_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive/drive_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **conducir** ejecutarรก el chasis para siempre hasta que se utilice un nuevo bloque Chasis o se detenga el programa.\n\nElija la direcciรณn en la que se moverรก el Chasis.\n\n![drive_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive/drive_direction.png)  \n"},"drive_for":{"name":"drive_for","markdown":"# Conducir por\n\nMueve el chasis para una distancia determinada.\n\n![drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_for/drive_for.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija en qué dirección se moverá el chasis.\n\n![drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_for/drive_for_direction.png)\n\nEl bloque **conducir por** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEstablezca hasta dónde se moverá el chasis introduciendo un valor y eligiendo la unidad de medida (pulgadas o milímetros).\n\n![drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_for/drive_for_units.png)\n\nAutomáticamente, otros bloques **esperarán** hasta que el chasis termine de moverse.\n\nPuede seleccionar la flecha para expandirla **y no espere** - esto hará que otros bloques continúen ejecutándose mientras el chasis se mueve.\n\n![drive_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_for/drive_for_anddontwait.png))\n"},"drive_heading":{"name":"drive_heading","markdown":"# Dirección del Chasis\n\nReporta la direcciรณn que enfrenta el chasis.\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_heading/drive_heading.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Dirección del Chasis** reporta de un rango de **0.00 a 359.99 grados**.\n\nThe **drive heading** reports a counter-clockwise heading as shown in the image below.\n\nEl bloque de reportero **dirección del chasis** se utiliza dentro de bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_heading/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_is_done":{"name":"drive_is_done","markdown":"# Conducir ya termino\n\nInforma si el chasis ha completado su movimiento.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Conducir ya termino** reporta **true** cuando los motores del chasis han completado su movimiento.\n\n**Conducir ya termino** reporta **false** cuando los motores del chasis todavía están en movimiento.\n\nEl bloque booleano **drive is done** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis caras).\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_is_moving":{"name":"drive_is_moving","markdown":"# Chasis esta Moviendo\n\nReporta si el chasis se estรก moviendo actualmente.\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Chasis esta Moviendo** reporta **true** cuando los motores del chasis se estรกn moviendo.\n\n**Chasis esta Moviendo** reporta **false** cuando los motores del chasis de mando se detienen.\n\nEl bloque booleano **chasis esta moviendo** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_rotation":{"name":"drive_rotation","markdown":"# Rotación del chasis\n\nInforma del ángulo de rotación del chasis cuando se configura con un sensor Gyro.\n\n![drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_rotation/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Rotación del chasis** reporta un valor **positivo** valor cuando el Drivetrain gira en una dirección **en el sentido de las agujas del reloj**.\n\n**Rotación del chasis** reporta un valor **negativo** valor cuando el Drivetrain gira en una dirección **en sentido opuesto al de las agujas del reloj**.\n\nEl bloque de reportero **rotación del chasis** se utiliza dentro de bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![drive_rotation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_inserted.png)\n"},"equal_to":{"name":"equal_to","markdown":"# Igual a\n\nInforma si el primer valor es el mismo, o igual al segundo valor.\n\n![equal_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es/equal_to/equal_to.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\n**Igual a** reporta **verdadero** cuando dos valores son exactamente igual.\n\n**Igual a** reporta **falso** cuando dos valores no son iguales.\n\nEl bloque **Igual a** aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEl bloque Booleano **Igual a** reporta un valor **verdadero** o **falso**  y encaja en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis lados).\n\n![equal_to_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/equal_to/equal_to_inserted.png)"},"forever":{"name":"forever","markdown":"# Por siempre\n\nUn bloque 'C' que repite cualquier bloque contenido adentro, para siempre.\n\n![forever](static/help/vr/blocks/es/forever/forever.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nColoca bloques en un bloque 'C' **Por siempre** para que esas acciones se repitan **Por siempre**.\n\nPuede salir de una repetición **Por siempre** poniendo un bloque de **Romper** dentro.\n\n![forever_example_2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/forever/forever_example_2.png)\n\nBloques 'C' también se pueden poner uno dentro del otro. Este concepto se llama anidamiento (nesting) y puede ayudar a ahorrar tiempo al programar diferentes comportamientos."},"function":{"name":"function","markdown":"# Función\n\nRealiza una función matemática seleccionada.\n\n![function](static/help/vr/blocks/es/function/function.png)\n\n* **abs** - valor absoluto\n* **floor** - redondea hacia abajo al entero más cercano\n* **ceiling** - redondea al entero más cercano\n* **sqrt** - Raiz Cuadrada\n* **sin** - sine\n* **cos** - coseno\n* **tan** - tangente\n* **asin** - Arcoseno o la inversa de sine\n* **acos** - arcocoseno o el inverso del coseno\n* **atan** - arctan o el inverso de la tangente\n* **ln** - base de logaritmo natural e\n* **log** - base de logaritmo 10\n* **e ^** - poderes de e\n* **10 ^** - poderes de 10\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl bloque **Función** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nElija la **Función** matemática de la lista de opciones.\n\n![function_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es/function/function_select.png)\n\nEl bloque de **Función** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares.\n\n![function_fit](static/help/vr/blocks/es/function/function_fit.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"greater_than":{"name":"greater_than","markdown":"# Más que\n\nReporta si el primer valor es más grande (mayor) que el segundo valor.  \n\n![greater_than](static/help/vr/blocks/es/greater_than/greater_than.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Más que** reporta **true** cuando el primer valor es mayor que el segundo valor. \n\n**Más que** reporta **false** cuando el primer valor es menor o igual que el segundo valor.\n\nEl bloque **más que** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEl bloque booleano **más que** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis caras).\n\n![greater_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/greater_than/greater_than_inserted.png)\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Nivel de batería bueno\" en la pantalla del VEX IQ Brain si el nivel de batería es superior al 75%.\n\n![greater_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/greater_than/greater_than_example.png)\n"},"if_then":{"name":"if_then","markdown":"# Si entonces\n\nUn bloque 'C' que ejecurta el bloque de adentro, si la condicion Booleana que reporta es **verdadera**.  \n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/es/if_then/if_then.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl bloque 'C' **Si entonces** solo verificará la condición booleana una vez. \n\nSi la condición booleana se informa como **verdadera**, el bloque de adentro **Si entonces** se ejecutara.\n\nSi la condición booleana se informa como **falsa**, el bloque de adentro **Si entonces** será saltado.\n\nEl 'C' block **Si entonces** puede aceptar bloques con forma hexagonal (seis lados) como su condición.\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/es/if_then/if_then_2.png)\n\n"},"if_then_else":{"name":"if_then_else","markdown":"# Si entonces más\n\nUn bloque 'C' Block que ejecuta los bloques dentro de la primera o segunda parte de **Si entonces más**, basado en el valor booleano informado.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/es/if_then_else/if_then_else.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl 'C' bloque **Si entonces más** solo verificará la condición booleana una vez.\n\nSi la condición booleana se reporta como **verdadero**, el bloque dentro de la seccion **si** sera ejecutado.\n\nSi la condición booleana es **falsa**, el bloque dentro de la seccion **mas** sera ejecutado.\n\nEl 'C' bloque **Si entonces más** puede aceptar bloques con forma hexagonal (seis lados) como su condición.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/es/if_then_else/if_then_else_2.png)"},"item_of_2d_array":{"name":"item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Elemento de la lista 2D\n\nInforma el valor de un elemento en una lista 2D.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nElija qué lista 2D usar. La lista 2D también se puede renombrar o eliminar.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nIngrese la posición de la fila del elemento (primer número) y la columna (segundo número) dentro de la lista que se reportara. Aquí, se informa el elemento en la fila de posición 2, columna 3 en la lista 2D.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\nEl bloque **Elemento de la lista 2D** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)"},"item_of_array":{"name":"item_of_array","markdown":"# Artículo de\n\nInforma el valor de un elemento en una lista.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/item_of_array/item_of_block.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nElija qué lista usar. La lista también se puede renombrar o eliminar.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es/item_of_array/item_of_options.png)\n\nIngrese el número de la posición del elemento dentro de la lista. Aquí, se reporta el segundo elemento de la lista (elemento 2).\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/item_of_array/item_of_block_2.png)\n\nEl bloque **Artículo de** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá la velocidad de la transmisión al valor almacenado en el elemento 3 de mi lista. El tren motriz ahora avanzará a una velocidad del 90%.\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/item_of_array/item_of_example2.png)\n\n"},"length_of_2d_array":{"name":"length_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Longitud de la lista 2D\n\nReporta el número de filas o columnas de una lista 2D.  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/es/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija qué lista 2D utilizar. La lista 2D también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_select.png)\n\nElija el valor que desea reportar. La lista 2D puede reportar **filas** o **columnas**.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_option.png)\n\nEl bloque de reportero **longitud de la lista 2D** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_insert.png)\n"},"less_than":{"name":"less_than","markdown":"# Menos que\n\nReporta si el primer valor es menos (más pequeño) que el segundo valor. \n\n![less_than](static/help/vr/blocks/es/less_than/less_than.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Menos que** reporta **true** cuando el primer valor es menos que el segundo valor. \n\n**Menos que** reporta **false** cuando el primer valor es mayor o igual que el segundo valor.\n\nEl bloque booleano **menos que** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\nEl bloque **menos que** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![less_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/less_than/less_than_inserted.png)\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Low Battery!\" en la pantalla del VEX IQ Brain si el nivel de batería es inferior al 25%.\n\n![less_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/less_than/less_than_example.png)\n"},"move_pen":{"name":"move_pen","markdown":"# Establecer la posición de la pluma\n\nEstablece la posición del VEX VR Pen.\n\n![move_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/es/move_pen/move_pen.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la posición de la pluma VR\n\n![move_pen2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/move_pen/move_pen2.png)\n\n* arriba - el bolígrafo no dibujará una línea en el tablero.\n* abajo - el bolígrafo dibujará una línea coloreada en el tablero.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo dibujará una línea roja en el tablero.\n\n![move_pen3](static/help/vr/blocks/es/move_pen/move_pen3.png)\n"},"multiply":{"name":"multiply","markdown":"# Multiplicar\n\nMultiplica los dos valores juntos e informa del producto.\n\n![multiply](static/help/vr/blocks/es/multiply/multiply.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Multiplicar** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **multiply** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nMultiplicar dos números.\n\n![multiply_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es/multiply/multiply_numbers.png)\n\nMultiplicar con valores de variables.\n\n![multiply_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/es/multiply/multiply_variables.png)\n\nMultiplique con los valores de los sensores o dispositivos.\n\n![multiply_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es/multiply/multiply_sensors.png\n"},"next_row":{"name":"next_row","markdown":"# Coloque el cursor en la siguiente fila.\n\nEste bloque establece el cursor en una nueva línea en Print Console cuando se usan los bloques **print**. \n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/es/next_row/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nUse el bloque ** establecer cursor en la fila siguiente ** para establecer el cursor en una nueva línea en la pantalla.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Hello\" y en la siguiente línea en la pantalla imprimirá \"World!\"\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/next_row/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"not":{"name":"not","markdown":"# No\n\nReporta de lo contrario (inverso) del resultado booleano\n\n![not](static/help/vr/blocks/es/not/not.png)\n\n## Cรณmo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **no** reporta **true** cuando el bloque booleano adentro reporta **false**.\n\nEl bloque **no** reporta **false** cuando el bloque booleano adentro reporta **true**.\n\nEl bloque booleano **no** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\nEste ejemplo reportara **true** cuando el botรณn de \"comprobar\" del VEX IQ Brain no se presiona. \n\n![not_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es/not/not_sensors.png)\n\n![not_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/es/not/not_truth.png))  \n\n"},"object_in_front":{"name":"object_in_front","markdown":"# Objeto encontrado\n\nReporta si el sensor del Buscador de rango ultrasónico ve un objeto dentro de su campo de visión.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/es/object_in_front/object_in_front_block.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\n**Objeto encontrado** reporta **verdadero** cuando el sensor del Buscador de rango ultrasónico ve un objeto o superficie dentro de su campo de visión.\n\n**Objeto encontrado** reporta **falso** cuando el sensor del Buscador de rango ultrasónico no detecta un objeto o superficie.\n\nElija qué sensor del Buscador de rango ultrasónico va usar.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/es/object_in_front/object_in_front_distance.png)\n\nEl bloque Booleano **Objeto encontrado** reporta un valor **verdadero** o **falso**  y se usa en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis lados).\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/es/object_in_front/object_in_front_inserted.png)\n "},"optical_brightness":{"name":"optical_brightness","markdown":"# Brillo de color\n\nInforma del brillo de un objeto desde uno de los sensores de ojo de color.\n\n![color_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_brightness/color_brightness.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Brillo de color** informa del brillo de un objeto. Los objetos blancos reportarán un brillo del 100%, los objetos negros notificarán un brillo del 0%. Todos los demás colores informarán de un brillo en algún lugar entre esos valores.\n\nElija qué Sensores de ojo de Color usar. El OjoDelantero está montado delante del robot y mira hacia adelante, mientras que el OjoAbajo está montado debajo del robot y mira hacia abajo.\n\n![color_brightness_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_brightness/color_brightness_eyes.png)\n\nEl bloque numerico **brillo de color** informa de un valor entre 0 y 100 valor y se utiliza en bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![color_brightness_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_brightness/color_brightness_insert.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo debe llevarse a cabo en el Playgorund de **Laberinto de Disco**. El robot avanzará hasta que el OjoAbajo reporte que el brillo de un objeto debajo de él es inferior al 100%. Luego girará a la izquierda y conducirá hacia adelante de nuevo.\n\n![color_brightness_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_brightness/color_brightness_example.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **brillo de color**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque reporter.\n\n![color_brightness_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_brightness/color_brightness_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![color_brightness_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_brightness/color_brightness_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_color":{"name":"optical_color","markdown":"# Detección de Color\n\nReporta si uno de los Color Sensor Eyes (Ojos del Sensor de Color) detecta un color específico.\n\n![color_sensing_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_color/color_sensing_color.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Detección de Color** informa **true** cuando el Color Sensor Eye (Ojos del Sensor de Color) detecta el color seleccionado.\n\n**Detección de Color** informa **false** cuando el Color Sensor Eye detecta un color diferente al seleccionado.\n\nElija qué Color Sensor Eye (Ojo del Sensor de Color) usar. El OjoDelantero está montado delante del robot y mira hacia adelante, mientras que el OjoAbajo está montado debajo del robot y mira hacia abajo.\n\n![color_sensing_color_eye](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_color/color_sensing_color_eye.png)\n\nElija el color que desea detectar.\n\n![color_sensing_color_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_color/color_sensing_color_color.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano de **detección de color** informa de un valor **true** o **false** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis caras).\n\n![color_sensing_color_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_color/color_sensing_color_shape.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo debe llevarse a cabo en el área de juegos de **Laberinto de Discos**. Conducirá hacia adelante con el lápiz hacia abajo hasta que detecte un objeto con un color, y luego cambiará el color del lápiz y se dará la vuelta.\n\n![color_sensing_color_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_color/color_sensing_color_example.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque de **detección de color**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reportero.\n\n![color_sensing_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_color/color_sensing_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![color_sensing_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_color/color_sensing_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_near_object":{"name":"optical_near_object","markdown":"# Sensor ocular cerca del objeto\n\nInforma si el sensor de color de ojos está lo suficientemente cerca de un objeto para detectar un color.\n\n![color_near_object](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_near_object/color_near_object.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Color cercano al objeto** informa **verdadero** cuando el Sensor de color está cerca de un objeto que tiene colores detectables.\n\n**Color cercano al objeto** informa **falso** cuando el Sensor de color no está lo suficientemente cerca de un objeto con colores detectables.\n\nElija qué sensor de color de ojo usar. FrontEye se monta frente al robot y mira hacia adelante, mientras que DownEye se monta debajo del robot y mira hacia abajo.\n\n![color_near_object_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_near_object/color_near_object_eyes.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano **objeto cercano** informa un valor **verdadero** o **falso** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![color_near_object_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/es/optical_near_object/color_near_object_shape.png)\n\n\n\n"},"or":{"name":"or","markdown":"# O\n\nReporta si una de las dos condiciones booleanas son verdaderas.\n\n![or](static/help/vr/blocks/es/or/or.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **o** reporta **true** cuando una (o ambas) de las condiciones son **true**. \n\nEl bloque **o** reporta **false** cuando ambas condiciones son **false**.\n\nEl bloque booleano **o** reporta **true** o **false** valor y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![or_sensors2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/or/or_sensors2.png)\n\nEl bloque **o** reporta **true** cuando (o ambos) se presionan el VEX IQ Bumbper y el VEX IQ Touch LED y reporta **false** sólo cuando el parachoques y el LED táctil no se presionan.\n\n##Ejemplo\n\nEl bloque **o** reporta **true** cuando el **sensor de distancia** detecta un objete, y/o el **OjoFrontal** detecta un objeto. \n\nEl bloque **or** reporta **false** solo cuando el **sensor de distancia** no detecta un objete y el **OjoFrontal** no detecta un objeto.\n\n![or_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/es/or/or_truth.png)\n"},"position":{"name":"position","markdown":"# Posición del robot\n\nInforma la posición de coordenadas X o Y del robot.\n\n![position_of_robot_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/position/position_of_robot_block.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Posición del robot** informa la posición de coordenadas X o Y del robot en enteros como milímetros (mm) o pulgadas (pulgadas).\n\nElija si desea devolver la coordenada X o la coordenada Y.\n\n![position_of_robot_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/es/position/position_of_robot_coord.png)\n\nElija si el valor se informa en mm o pulgadas.\n\n![position_of_robot_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/es/position/position_of_robot_unit.png)\n\nLa posición ** del bloque informador del robot ** se usa en bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![position_of_robot_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/position/position_of_robot_inserted.png)\n"},"position_angle":{"name":"position_angle","markdown":"# Ángulo de posición en grados\n\nInforma el ángulo del robot en grados.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_block.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Ángulo de posición en grados** informa el ángulo actual del robot.\n\nEl bloque reportero **ángulo de posición en grados** se usa en bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_inserted.png)\n"},"pressing_bumper":{"name":"pressing_bumper","markdown":"# Presionar bumper\n\nReporta si se presiona el bumper.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/es/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Como utilizar \n\n**Presionar bumper** reporta **verdadero** si se presiona el bumper.\n\n**Presionar bumper** reporta **falso** si no se presiona el bumper.\n\nElija qué bumper va usar.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/es/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nEl bloque Booleano **Presionar bumper** reporta **verdadero** o **falso** valor y se usa en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis lados).\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n "},"print":{"name":"print","markdown":"# Impresión\n\nImprime valores o texto en Print Console.\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/es/print/look_print.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nEl bloque **imprimir** imprimirá datos dentro de la pestaña Ventana del monitor\n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/es/print/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nTodos los bloques de reportero se imprimirán como números enteros (enteros) de forma predeterminada. Use el bloque **establecer precisión de impresión** para ajustar el número de decimales impresos.\n\nImprime palabras y números.\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/es/print/print_words.png)\n\nImprima el valor informado de una variable.\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/es/print/print_variable1.png)  \n\nImprimir el cálculo reportado de un operador.\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/es/print/print_addition.png)  \n\nImprima el valor informado desde un sensor o dispositivo.\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/es/print/print_sensor.png)\n\n\n\n\n"},"procedures_call":{"name":"procedures_call","markdown":"# Mis bloques\n\nMis bloques se utilizan para crear bloques personalizados.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_call/my_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Mis bloques** se utilizan para crear una secuencia de bloques que se pueden utilizar varias veces a lo largo de un proyecto. \n\n**Mis bloques** se hacen utilizando el botón **Crear un bloque**.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_call/make_a_block.png)\n\n**Mis bloques** puede tener múltiples parámetros - etiquetas de texto, variables numéricas y variables booleanas - para agregar más funcionalidad a su bloque personalizado.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_call/make_variable.png)  \n\nPara utilizar un **mi bloque**, adjuntar la llamada **mi bloque** a un bloque de tapa. Cuando un  **mi bloque** se alcanza en su proyecto, la pila dentro de **mi bloque** se ejecutará utilizando los valores de parámetro especificados.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_call/trigger_function.png)\n\nAdjunte una pila de bloques a la **Mis bloques** \"define\" un bloque tapa para establecer las acciones que se producen cuando un **mi bloque** se utiliza.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_call/attach_function.png)\n\n***Nota: La creación de varios Mis Bloques con las mismas etiquetas y variables puede provocar errores al ejecutar el proyecto en los Playgrounds.***\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este ejemplo, el robot utilizará **Mis bloques** para:\n\n* Conduzca hacia delante 300 mm\n* Dibuja un cuadrado de 300 mm\n* Gire a la derecha 15 grados\n* Establezca el color del lápiz en rojo\n* Dibuja un cuadrado de 400mm\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_call/square_example.png)\n"},"procedures_definition":{"name":"procedures_definition","markdown":"# Mis bloques\n\nMis bloques se utilizan para crear bloques personalizados.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_definition/my_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Mis bloques** se utilizan para crear una secuencia de bloques que se pueden utilizar varias veces a lo largo de un proyecto. \n\n**Mis bloques** se hacen utilizando el botón **Crear un bloque**.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_definition/make_a_block.png)\n\n**Mis bloques** puede tener múltiples parámetros - etiquetas de texto, variables numéricas y variables booleanas - para agregar más funcionalidad a su bloque personalizado.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_definition/make_variable.png)  \n\nPara utilizar un **mi bloque**, adjuntar la llamada **mi bloque** a un bloque de tapa. Cuando un  **mi bloque** se alcanza en su proyecto, la pila dentro de **mi bloque** se ejecutará utilizando los valores de parámetro especificados.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_definition/trigger_function.png)\n\nAdjunte una pila de bloques a la **Mis bloques** \"define\" un bloque tapa para establecer las acciones que se producen cuando un **mi bloque** se utiliza.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_definition/attach_function.png)\n\n***Nota: La creación de varios Mis Bloques con las mismas etiquetas y variables puede provocar errores al ejecutar el proyecto en los Playgrounds.***\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este ejemplo, el robot utilizará **Mis bloques** para:\n\n* Conduzca hacia delante 300 mm\n* Dibuja un cuadrado de 300 mm\n* Gire a la derecha 15 grados\n* Establezca el color del lápiz en rojo\n* Dibuja un cuadrado de 400mm\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/procedures_definition/square_example.png)\n"},"random":{"name":"random","markdown":"# Al azar\n![random](static/help/vr/blocks/es/random/random.png)\n\nInforma de un valor aleatorio entre los valores más bajos y más altos del bloque.\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nLa longitud del bloque **al azar** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nEl bloque **al azar** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEste ejemplo utiliza un bloque **al azar** para aleatorizar la velocidad del chasis.\n\n![random_drivetrain](static/help/vr/blocks/es/random/random_drivetrain.png)\n\nEste ejemplo utiliza un bloque **al azar** para aleatorizar la longitud de un bloque de espera.\n\n![random_control](static/help/vr/blocks/es/random/random_control.png)\n\n"},"remainder":{"name":"remainder","markdown":"# El resto\n\nDivide el primer valor por el segundo valor y reporta el resto.\n\n![remainder](static/help/vr/blocks/es/remainder/remainder.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **el resto** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLos números que se dividen uniformemente reportarán un resto de cero.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **el resto** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares.\n\nEste ejemplo dividirá 7 y 2 e informará el resto para imprimir \"1\" en la pantalla de VEX IQ Brain.\n\n![remainder_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es/remainder/remainder_numbers.png)"},"repeat":{"name":"repeat","markdown":"# Repetir\n\nUn bloque 'C' que repite los bloques contenidos en el interior durante un número determinado de veces.\n\n![repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/es/repeat/repeat.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPrimero introduzca un valor para indicar cuántas veces se va a utilizar la pila **repetir** los bloques contenidos en el interior.\n \nEl bloque 'C' **repetir** puede aceptar enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLos bloques 'C' también se pueden poner dentro unos de otros. Este concepto es llamado anidando que puede ayudar a ahorrar el tiempo programando comportamientos diferentes.\n\n![repeat_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es/repeat/repeat_select.png)\n\nEste ejemplo repetirá la reproducción de un efecto de sonido cada 3 segundos durante 5 veces.\n\n![repeat_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/repeat/repeat_example.png)"},"repeat_until":{"name":"repeat_until","markdown":"# Repita hasta que\n\nUn bloque 'C' que repite los bloques dentro hasta que se notifica que la condición booleana es **true**.  \n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/es/repeat_until/repeat_until.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **Repita hasta que** sólo comprobará la condición booleana al principio de cada bucle. \n\nSi la condición booleana se reporta como **false**, los bloques dentro de **repita hasta que** se ejecutará.\n\nSi la condición booleana se reporta como **true**, los bloques dentro de **repita hasta que** se omitirá.\n\nEl bloque 'C' **repita hasta que** puede aceptar bloques hexagonales (de seis lados) como su condición.\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/es/repeat_until/repeat_until_2.png)\n\n\n"},"reset_timer":{"name":"reset_timer","markdown":"# Restablecer temporizador\n\nRestablece el temporizador del VEX IQ Brain.\n\n![resetTimer](static/help/vr/blocks/es/reset_timer/resetTimer.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl temporizador del VEX IQ Brain comienza al principio de cada proyecto. El bloque **restablecer temporizador** se utiliza para restablecer el temporizador a 0 segundos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este ejemplo, el temporizador se restablecerá antes de mover el chasis hacia delante durante 3 segundos. Después de 3 segundos, el chasis se detendrá.\n\n![resetTimer_exampleRevised](static/help/vr/blocks/es/reset_timer/resetTimer_exampleRevised.png)"},"round":{"name":"round","markdown":"# Redondear\n\nRedondea el valor introducido al entero más cercano.\n\n![round](static/help/vr/blocks/es/round/round.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nDecimales que son 0.5 o mayores **redondean** hacia arriba y decimales menores de 0.5 **redondean** hacia abajo.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **redondear** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nEl bloque **redondear** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![round_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es/round/round_numbers.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo redondeará 2.7 e imprimirá \"3\".\n\n![round_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/es/round/round_variables.png)\n\n\n\n"},"set_2d_array_to":{"name":"set_2d_array_to","markdown":"# Establezca la lista 2D a\n\nEstablece cada elemento de la lista 2D en los valores introducidos.\n\n![set_2d_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nAl crear una lista 2D, puede establecer el número de las **columnas** de la lista entre 1 y 10 elementos y el número de las **filas** de la lista entre 1 y 10 elementos.\n\nElija la lista 2D que desea utilizar. La lista 2D también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **establezca la lista 2D a** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_inserted.png)\n\n\n"},"set_array_to":{"name":"set_array_to","markdown":"# Establecer lista a\n\nEstablece cada valor de una lista en los valores introducidos.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_array_to/set_list_to.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nLa longitud de la lista (1 a 10 elementos) se establece cuando se crea la lista.\n\nElija la lista que desea utilizar. La lista también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_array_to/set_list_to_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **establecer lista a** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_array_to/set_list_to_2.png)\n\nEn este ejemplo, se establecerá la lista en valores iniciales y, a continuación, se informará del elemento #3 (90) de la lista para establecer la posición del brazo. \n\n![set_list_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_array_to/set_list_to_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_boolean_variable":{"name":"set_boolean_variable","markdown":"# Establecer variable booleana\n\nEstablece una variable booleana en un valor dado.\n\n![set_boolean_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la variable booleana que desea utilizar. Las variables booleanas también se pueden cambiar de nombre o eliminar. \n\n![set_boolean_block_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_choice.png)\n\nElija un valor booleano.\n\n![set_boolean_block_value](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_value.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano **set boolean variable** puede aceptar bloques hexagonal (de seis lados).\n\n![set_boolean_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_inserted.png)"},"set_bumper":{"name":"set_bumper","markdown":"# Establecer el color del parachoques LED\n\nEstablece el color del VEX VR LED Bumper.\n\n![set_bumper_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_bumper/set_bumper_color.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija el color que se mostrará.  \n\n![set_bumper_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_bumper/set_bumper_color2.png)\n\nPara apagar el **parachoques LED**, ajustar el color a apagado.\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el **parachoques LED** a verde.\n\n![set_bumper_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_bumper/set_bumper_example.png)"},"set_drive_heading":{"name":"set_drive_heading","markdown":"# Establecer dirección del chasis\n\nEstablece el valor de rumbo del giroscopio de la transmisión.\n\n![setDriveHeading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_drive_heading/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **establecer dirección del chasis** se puede utilizar para ajustar la posición del chasis a cualquier rumbo en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj, como se muestra en la imagen de abajo.\n\n**Establecer dirección del chasis** acepta de un rango de **0 a 360** grados.\n\nEl bloque **establecer dirección del chasis** puede aceptar enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n\n"},"set_drive_rotation":{"name":"set_drive_rotation","markdown":"# Establecer la rotación del chasis\n\nEstablece el ángulo de rotación del chasis.\n\n![drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_drive_rotation/drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **establecer la rotación del chasis** bloque se puede utilizar para establecer el ángulo de rotación del chasis a cualquier valor positivo o negativo dado.\n\nEl bloque **establecer la rotación del chasis** bloque puede aceptar enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará girar el robot un total de 210 grados:\n\n![turntorotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_drive_rotation/turntorotation_example.png)\n\n- Da veulta a la derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) para girar 120 grados.\n- Establezca la posición actual del robot como rotación de -45 grados.\n- Da veulta a la derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) 90 grados adicionales (-45 grados a 45 grados) en función del valor de rotación establecido del bloque anterior."},"set_drive_velocity":{"name":"set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Establecer velocidad de Conducción\n\nEstablece la velocidad de Conducción.\n\n![set_drive_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Establecer velocidad de Conducción** aceptando un rango de **-100% a 100%**. Puede utilizar este bloque para obtener un movimiento mรกs preciso del robot. La velocidad predeterminada es **50%**\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo establecerรก la conducciรณn para que gire a una velocidad del 10% antes de conducir.\n\n![set_drive_velocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity_example.png)\n"},"set_item_of_2d_array":{"name":"set_item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Reemplazar elemento de la lista 2D\n\nActualiza un elemento de la lista 2D a un nuevo valor.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nIntroduzca la fila del elemento (primer número) y la posición de la columna (segundo número) dentro de la lista que se va a reemplazar.\n\nElija la lista que desea utilizar. La lista también se puede cambiar de nombre o eliminar.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **reemplazar elemento** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block_2.png)\n"},"set_item_of_array":{"name":"set_item_of_array","markdown":"# Reemplazar elemento\n\nActualiza un elemento de la lista a un nuevo valor.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la lista que desea utilizar. La lista también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_item_of_array/replace_item_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **reemplazar elemento** bloque puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block_2.png)\n"},"set_magnet_state":{"name":"set_magnet_state","markdown":"# Energizar electroimán\n\nEstablece el VEX VR Robot Electromagnet en dos modos diferentes: **elevar** o **bajar**.\n\n![set_magnet_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nSeleccione esta opción para configurar ese electroimán para elevar o bajar.\n\n* **elevar** - atrae un objeto al electroimán\n* **bajar** - libera cualquier objeto que el electroimán está sosteniendo\n\n\n![set_magnet_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el robot para conducir 200 mm hacia adelante, **Electromagnet** eleva para recoger un objeto, conducir hacia adelante 200mm, a continuación, baja el objeto.\n\n![set_magnet_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_example.png)\n"},"set_pen_color":{"name":"set_pen_color","markdown":"# Establecer el color de la pluma\n\nEstablece el color del VEX VR Pen.\n\n![set_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_pen_color/set_pen_color.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija el color de la línea que se dibujará.  \n\n![set_pen_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_pen_color/set_pen_color2.png)\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el color de la pluma en azul.\n\n![set_pen_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_pen_color/set_pen_color_example.png)\n"},"set_print_color":{"name":"set_print_color","markdown":"# Establecer color de impresión\n\nEstablece el color del texto impreso en Print Console.\n\n![set_print_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_print_color/set_print_color.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nEl bloque **establecer color de impresión** se usa con el bloque **imprimir** para establecer el color del texto impreso en Print Console. El color se puede configurar en negro, rojo, verde o azul.\n\n![set_print_color_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_print_color/set_print_color_options.png)  \n\nLas letras, los números, los valores de los sensores y los valores variables se pueden imprimir en el conjunto de colores utilizando el bloque **establecer color de impresión**.\n\n![set_print_color_hello](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_print_color/set_print_color_hello.png)  \n\nCualquier bloque **print** utilizado después del **color de impresión establecido** se imprimirá en el color establecido más recientemente dentro del programa.\n\n![set_print_color_text](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_print_color/set_print_color_text.png) "},"set_print_precision":{"name":"set_print_precision","markdown":"# Establecer precisión de impresión\n\nEstablece el número de dígitos que aparecen después del punto decimal cuando se utilizan bloques de impresión para imprimir variables y valores en Print Console.\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_print_precision/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nElija el nivel de precisión que los bloques de reportero insertados se imprimirán en la pantalla.\n\n* Unos (1) (Defecto)\n* Décimas (0.1)\n* Centésimos (0.01)\n* Milésimas (0.001)\n* Todos los dígitos (0.000001)\n\nLos bloques informadores impresos en la pantalla se redondearán al nivel de precisión más cercano. Por ejemplo, 1.67 se imprimirá como 2 con precisión y 1.7 con precisión de décimas.\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_print_precision/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_turn_velocity":{"name":"set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Establecer la velocidad de dar vuelta\n\nEstablece la velocidad de las vueltas del chasis.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Establecer la velocidad de dar vuelta** sólo establecerá la velocidad del chasis, pero no hará que el chasis se mueva. Un bloque **vuelta** todavía se requiere.\n\n**Establecer la velocidad de dar vuelta** acepta de un rango de **-100% a 100%** o **-127rpm a 127rpm**.\n\nEstableciendo la velocidad de giro del chasis a un valor negativo hará que el chasis de vuelta en la dirección opuesta.\n\nEstableciendo la velocidad de giro de una transmisión en 0 hará que la transmisión se detenga.\n\nEl bloque **Establecer la velocidad de dar vuelta** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis gire a la izquierda lentamente a una velocidad del 25%.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_variable":{"name":"set_variable","markdown":"# Establecer variable\n\nEstablece una variable en un valor determinado.\n\n![set_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_variable/set_variable.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la variable que desea utilizar. Las variables también se pueden cambiar de nombre o eliminar. \n\n![set_variable_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_variable/set_variable_choose.png)\n\nEl bloque **establecer variable** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![set_variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/set_variable/set_variable_inserted.png)\n\n"},"size_of_array":{"name":"size_of_array","markdown":"# Longitud de la lista\n\nReporta el número de elementos de una lista.  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/es/size_of_array/size_of_array.png)\n\n# Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la lista que desea utilizar. La lista también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es/size_of_array/size_of_array_select.png)\n\nEl bloque reportero **Longitud de la lista** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares.\n\n![size_of_array_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/es/size_of_array/size_of_array_insert.png)"},"stop_driving":{"name":"stop_driving","markdown":"# Dejar de conducir\n\nDetiene el chasis.\n\n![stopDriving_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/stop_driving/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **dejar de conducir** hará que el Drivetrain se detenga por completo.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo detendrá el chasis después de 3 segundos.\n\n![stopDriving_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/stop_driving/stopDriving_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_project":{"name":"stop_project","markdown":"# Detener proyecto\n\nDetiene un proyecto en ejecución.\n\n![stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/es/stop_project/stop_project.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **detener proyecto** hará que un proyecto en ejecución se detenga.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo conducirá el Robot VR en un cuadrado de 500 mm y luego detendrá el proyecto.\n\n![stop_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/stop_project/stop_project2.png)"},"subtract":{"name":"subtract","markdown":"# Restar\n\nResta un valor de otro y reporta la diferencia.\n\n![subtract](static/help/vr/blocks/es/subtract/subtract.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Restar** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **restar** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nRestar dos números.\n\n![subtract_example1](static/help/vr/blocks/es/subtract/subtract_example1.png)\n\nRestar con valores de variables.\n\n![subtract_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/subtract/subtract_example2.png)  \n\nReste con los valores de los sensores o dispositivos\n\n![subtract_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/es/subtract/subtract_example3.png)"},"timer_value":{"name":"timer_value","markdown":"# Valor del temporizador\n\nReporta el valor del temporizador VEX IQ Brain en segundos.\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/timer_value/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl temporizador comienza a 0 segundos cuando el programa se inicia e informa de valores decimales.\n\nEl bloque de reportero **valor del temporizador** se utiliza dentro de bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/timer_value/timerValue_inserted.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo moverá el Drivetrain hacia adelante y reproducirá un efecto de sonido durante 4.5 segundos antes de realizar una vuelta de 90 grados.\n\n![timerValue_example_revised](static/help/vr/blocks/es/timer_value/timerValue_example_revised.png)\n"},"turn":{"name":"turn","markdown":"# Dar vuelta\n\nGira el chasis por siempre.\n\n![turn_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn/turn_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta** girará el Drivetrain para siempre hasta que se utilice un nuevo bloque Drivetrain o se detenga el programa.\n\nElija la dirección en la que se moverá el chasis.\n\n![turn_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn/turn_direction.png)"},"turn_for":{"name":"turn_for","markdown":"# Dar vuelta por\n\nGira el chasis durante una distancia determinada.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_for/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la dirección en la que girará el chasis.\n\n![turn_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_for/turn_for_direction.png)  \n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEstablezca la distancia que se moverá el tren de mando introduciendo un número de grados.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_for/turn_for_block_2.png)\n\nPor defecto, otros bloques van a **esperar** hasta que el chasis termine de girar.\n\nPuede seleccionar la flecha para expandir **y no espera** - esto hará que otros bloques continúen funcionando mientras el chasis gira.\n\n![turn_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_for/turn_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"turn_to_heading":{"name":"turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Dar vuelta a dirección de\n\nGira un Drivetrain a un dirección específico con el VR Gyro Sensor.\n\n![turn_to_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta a dirección de** se puede utilizar para girar el chasis a cualquier encabezado en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj como se muestra en la imagen de abajo.\n\nBasado en el rumbo actual del Gyro, **dar vuelta a dirección de** determinará la dirección a girar.\n\n**Dar vuelta a dirección de** acepta de un rango **0.00 a 359.99** grados.\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta a dirección de** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![turn_to_heading_dont_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_dont_wait.png)\n\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis haga cuatro vueltas: \n\n![turn_to_heading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\n- Izquierda a 45 grados\n- Izquierda a 90 grados\n- Izquierda a 270 grados\n- Derecha a 180 grados\n\nPor defecto, otros bloques van a **esperar** hasta que el chasis termine de girar.\n\nPuede seleccionar la flecha para expandir **y no espera** - esto hará que otros bloques continúen funcionando mientras el chasis gira.\n\n![turn_to_heading_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_anddontwait.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"turn_to_rotation":{"name":"turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Dar vuelta a rotación\n\nDa vuelta al chasis a un ángulo de rotación específico utilizando el sensor Gyro de entrada.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_iq.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta a rotación** se puede utilizar para girar el Chasis a un valor positivo (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) o negativo (en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj).\n\nSobre la base del valor **rotación** actual del chasis, **dar vuelta a rotación** determinará en qué dirección girar.\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta a rotación** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis haga cuatro vueltas: \n\n![turn_to_rotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_to_rotation/turn_to_rotation_example.png)\n\n- Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) a 90 grados\n- Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) a 180 grados\n- Izquierda (en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj) a -45 grados\n- Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) a 0 grados\n\nPor defecto, otros bloques  **esperarán** hasta que el Chasis termine de girar.\n\nPuede seleccionar la flecha para expandir **y no espera** - esto hará que otros bloques continúen funcionando mientras el Chasis da vuelta.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/es/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq.png)"},"variable":{"name":"variable","markdown":"# Variable\n\nReporta del valor asignado a la variable.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es/variable/variable.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nBloques **variable** se utilizan para informar de un valor almacenado dentro de la variable. Utilice el bloque **definir variable** para definir o actualizar el valor de una variable.\n\n![variable_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/es/variable/variable_cursor.png)\n\nEl bloque de reportero **variable** se utiliza en bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/variable/variable_inserted.png)\n\nLas variables se crean con el botón **hacer una variable**.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es/variable/make_variable.png)  \n"},"wait":{"name":"wait","markdown":"# Espera\n\nEspera una cantidad específica de tiempo antes de pasar al siguiente bloque.\n\n![wait](static/help/vr/blocks/es/wait/wait.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nElija una cantidad de tiempo para **espera**.\n\nEl bloque **espera** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![wait_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es/wait/wait_select.png)\n\n\n"},"wait_until":{"name":"wait_until","markdown":"# Espera hasta que\n\nEspera a que un bloque booleano reporta **true** antes de pasar al siguiente bloque.\n\n![wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/es/wait_until/wait_until.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Espera hasta que** comprobará repetidamente un bloque de reportero booleano y no se moverá al siguiente bloque hasta que el bloque de informe booleano informe informe **true**.\n\nEl bloque booleano **espera hasta que** puede aceptar bloques con forma hexagonal (seis lados).\n\n![wait_until_2](static/help/vr/blocks/es/wait_until/wait_until_2.png)\n\n"},"when_started":{"name":"when_started","markdown":"# Cuando empieza\n\nEjecuta la pila adjunta de bloques cuando se inicia el proyecto.\n\n![when_started](static/help/vr/blocks/es/when_started/when_started.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl evento **cuando empieza** Se ejecutará cuando el programa se inicie desde el menú de VEX IQ Brain o desde el botón **correr** en bloques de VEXcode IQ.\n\nTodos los nuevos programas incluirán automáticamente un bloque **cuando empieza**. Los proyectos pueden soportar hasta 3 bloques **cuando empieza**.\n\n"},"while":{"name":"while","markdown":"# Mientras\n\nUn bloque 'C' que repite los bloques dentro mientras que la condición booleana se notifica que es **true**.\n\n![while](static/help/vr/blocks/es/while/while.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **mientras** sólo comprobará la condición booleana al principio de cada bucle. \n\nSi la condición booleana se notifica como **true**, los bloques dentro de **while** se ejecutará.\n\nSi la condición booleana se notifica como **false**, los bloques dentro de **while** se omitirá.\n\nEl bloque 'C' **mientras** puede aceptar bloques hexagonales (de seis lados) como su condición.\n\n![while_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es/while/while_inserted.png)"}},"python":{"control_break":{"name":"control_break","markdown":"# Romper\n\nSale de un ciclo repetitivo inmediatamente.\n\n```don\nbreak\n```\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nCuando se agrega dentro de un bucle, el comando `break` saldrá del bucle en el que se está ejecutando actualmente.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo conducirá al robot y verificará si se ha presionado su `right_bumper`.\n\nSi el parachoques se **presiona**, el comando `break` saldrá del ciclo de mientras. El robot dejará de conducir hacia adelante.\n\n```don\nwhile(true):\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if(right_bumper.pressed()):\n        break\n\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"break"},"control_for":{"name":"control_for","markdown":"# Por\n\nUn tipo de bucle que repite el código contenido en su cuerpo para un número determinado de iteraciones.\n\n```\nfor value in range(10):\n    pass\n\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nPrimero, reemplace `value` con una variable que **iterará** a través de la función `range ()`.\n\nSegundo, reemplace `10` con un valor para especificar el final de la iteración.\n\nAgregue un espacio de cuatro espacios usando la **barra espaciadora** para incluir declaraciones como parte de la declaración de control `for`.\n\nUna declaración `for` no debe contener un bloque de código vacío. Si es necesario, agregue una declaración `pass` a una declaración` for` previamente vacía.\n\nTenga en cuenta que los bucles for comienzan en el índice **0** en lugar de **1**.\n\nLos bucles **Por** también se pueden anidar uno dentro del otro.\n\n## Ejemplo \n\nEl siguiente bucle imprimirá los números del 0 al 2:\n\n```\nfor i in range(3):\n   brain.print(i)\n   brain.new_line()\n```\nEsto imprimirá:\n```\n0\n1\n2\n```","signature":"for value in range (10"},"control_if":{"name":"control_if","markdown":"# Si\n\nSe utiliza una declaración de control para decidir si se ejecutará un bloque de código en función de lo proporcionado `condition`.\n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\n    \n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nReemplace la palabra `condition` con una expresión que puede evaluar como 'True' o 'False'. Una evaluación a `True` permitirá que se ejecuten las declaraciones con sangría debajo de la declaración `if`.\n\n\nAgregue una espacio de cuatro espacios usando **la barra espaciadora** para incluir declaraciones como parte de la declaración de control `if`.\n\nUna declaracion `if` no debe contener un bloque de código vacío. Si es necesario, agregue una declaración `pass` a una declaración` if` previamente vacía.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEsta instrucción `if` se ejecutará si `my_variable` es igual a 1, haciendo que el robot avance.\n\n```don\nmyVariable = 1\n\nif (my_variable == 1):\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"if condition:\\n\\tpass"},"control_if_else":{"name":"control_if_else","markdown":"# Si / si no\nUna declaración de control de flujo que ejecuta un bloque de comandos si una condición se evalúa como verdadera, u otro bloque de comandos si una condición se evalúa como falsa.\n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\nelse:\n    pass\n\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nLa instrucción `if` es condicional y verificará si su condición se evalúa como **Verdadero** o **Falso**. Es posible combinar muchas condiciones con el uso de operadores lógicos como `and`,`or`, y `not`. \n\nSi la totalidad de la condición es **Verdadero**, se ejecutará el código de bloqueo debajo de `if condition:`. Si la totalidad de la condición es **Falsa**, el código de bloqueo debajo de la instrucción `else:` se ejecutará en su lugar.\n\nLos bloques de código debajo de `if` y` else` deben tener margen de cuatro espacios.\n\nUna instrucción `if / else` no debe contener bloques de código vacíos. Si es necesario, agregue las declaraciones `pass` a una declaración `if / else` previamente vacía.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste código `if / else` establecerá el color del lápiz en NEGRO si la variable es igual a 1. Si no lo hace, establecerá el color del lápiz en VERDE.\n\n```don\nif (myVariable == 1):\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\nelse:\n    pen.set_pen_color(GREEN)\n```","signature":"if conditio"},"control_stop_project":{"name":"control_stop_project","markdown":"# Stop Project\n\nStops a running project.\n\n```don\nstop_project()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThis command will cause a running project to stop.\n\n## Example\n\nThis project draws a 500 millimeter black square on the playground.  The stop_project command stops the project after the square has been completed.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 500,MM)\n\tdrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\nstop_project()\n```","signature":"stop_project()"},"control_wait":{"name":"control_wait","markdown":"# Esperar\n\nEspere una cantidad de tiempo específica antes de pasar al siguiente comando.\n\n```don\nwait(TIME, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEstablezca una cantidad de tiempo en `UNITS` para que su programa **espere** antes de ejecutar comandos adicionales. `UNITS` puede ser `SECONDS` o` MSEC` (milisegundos).\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo detendrá el tren motriz después de que un robot haya sido impulsado hacia adelante durante 3 segundos.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"wait(1, SECONDS)"},"control_wait_until":{"name":"control_wait_until","markdown":"# Mientras no\nUna declaración de control que continúa en bucle hasta que la expresión se vuelve **verdadera**.\n\n```don\nwhile not condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nLa instrucción `while not` verificará repetidamente la condición y no se moverá al siguiente comando hasta que la condición informe **verdadero**\n\nSi la condición se evalúa como **falso**, se ejecutará el código dentro de `while not`.\n\nSi la condición se evalúa como **verdadera**, se omitirá el código dentro de 'while not' y se ejecutará el siguiente comando después de la instrucción 'while not'.\n\nLa instrucción `while not` acepta cualquier expresión que se evalúe como `True` o `False` como su condición. Puede colocar booleanos y expresiones utilizando operadores lógicos y de comparación.\n\nTambién puede usar valores numéricos; **0** se evalúa como `False`, y cualquier **número distinto de cero** se evalúa como`True`.\n\nAgregue un margen de cuatro espacios usando la **barra espaciadora** para incluir declaraciones como parte de la instrucción de control `while not`.\n\nUna instrucción `while not` no debe contener un bloque de código vacío. Si es necesario, agregue una declaración `pass` a una declaración anteriormente vacía` while not`.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste bucle `while not` imprimirá \"Hello\" en líneas separadas hasta que el valor de my_variable sea igual a 5.\n\nmyVariable = 0\n\n```\nwhile not my_variable == 5:\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n    brain.new_line()\n    my_variable += 1\n```","signature":"while not expressio"},"control_while":{"name":"control_while","markdown":"# Mientras\nUn bucle que repite el código en siempre que la expresión de condición se evalúe como **verdadero**.\n\n```don\nwhile condition:\n   pass\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl ciclo `while` solo verificará la condición dentro del paréntesis al comienzo de cada ciclo.\n\nSi la condición se evalúa como **verdadera**, se ejecutará el código dentro de `while`.\n\nSi la condición se evalúa como **falsa**, se omitirá el código dentro de `while`.\n\nEl bucle `while` acepta cualquier cosa que se evalúe como`True` o `False` como condición. Puede colocar booleanos y expresiones utilizando operadores lógicos y de comparación.\n\nTambién puede usar valores numéricos; **0** se evalúa como `False`, y cualquier **número distinto de cero** se evalúa como`True`.\n\nAgregue un margen de cuatro espacios usando la **barra espaciadora** para incluir declaraciones como parte de la instrucción de control `while`.\n\nUna instrucción `while` no debe contener un bloque de código vacío. Si es necesario, agregue una declaración `pass` a una declaración `while` previamente vacía.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste bucle `while` continuará imprimiendo la palabra \"Hello\"en líneas separadas cuando el valor del temporizador sea inferior a 50 segundos.\n\n```\nwhile(brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 50.0):\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n    brain.new_line()\n```","signature":"while expressio"},"drivetrain_drive":{"name":"drivetrain_drive","markdown":"# Conducir\n\nMueve el tren motriz para siempre en la dirección especificada dentro de los paréntesis. Todos los motores del tren motriz conducen hacia adelante o hacia atrás a la velocidad establecida usando el comando `drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()`. La velocidad predeterminada es 50%. Los valores negativos harán que el tren motriz avance para enfrente con una entrada REVERSA y en reversa con una entrada ADELANTE.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.drive()` activara el tren motriz  para siempre, hasta que se use un nuevo comando el tren motriz o se detenga el programa.\n\nUse el argumento `FORWARD` en letras mayúsculas para mover el Drivetrain hacia adelante.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nUse el argumento `REVERSE` en letras mayúsculas para mover el Drivetrain en reversa.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(REVERSE)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.drive(FOWARD)"},"drivetrain_drive_for":{"name":"drivetrain_drive_for","markdown":"# Conducir por\n\nMueve el tren motriz por una distancia dada. Todos los motores del tren motriz conducen hacia adelante o hacia atrás a la velocidad establecida por el comando `drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()`. Después de que el tren motriz haya movido la distancia de entrada, los motores se detendrán.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(DIRECTION, distance, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEstablezca la distancia que recorrerá el tren motriz proporcionando tres argumentos.\n`drivetrain.drive_for(direction, distance, units)`\n\n- dirección - ya sea `FORWARD` o `REVERSE`\n- distancia - valor numerico\n- unidad - ya sea `MM` o `INCHES`\n\nEl `drivetrain.drive_for()` acepta valores numéricos para `DISTANCE`. Los valores negativos harán que el robot VR funcione en el lado opuesto de la entrada 'DIRECTION'.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 5.0, INCHES)\n```\n\nEl comando 'Drive for' es por defecto un comando de bloqueo; no se ejecutará ningún otro comando hasta que el tren de transmisión haya completado su movimiento.\n\n## Parámetro opcional\n\nSe puede proporcionar un cuarto parámetro opcional para hacer que el comando 'Drive For' sea un comando sin bloqueo:`wait=False`\n\n## Ejemplo\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 50, MM, wait=False)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)"},"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Establecer velocidad de conducción\n\nEstablece la velocidad del Drivetrain para los comandos de conducir y conducir por. La velocidad predeterminada es 50 por ciento.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\n`drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()` establecerá la velocidad del tren motriz cuando se use con un comando `drive()` o `drive_for()`. No hará que el tren motriz se mueva cuando se use solo.\n\n`drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()` acepta dos argumentos, un valor numérico de **- 100 a 100**, y el argumento de unidad 'PORCENTAJE' escrito en mayúsculas.\n\nEstablecer la velocidad de manejo de un tren motriz en un valor negativo hará que el tren motriz conduzca opuesto a la 'DIRECTION' colocada entre paréntesis del comando `drive()` o `drive_for()`.\n\nEstablecer la velocidad de conducción de un tren motriz a 0 hará que el tren motriz no se mueva, incluso cuando se usa un comando `drive()` o `drive_for()`","signature":"drivetrain.set_drive_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_set_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_set_heading","markdown":"# Establecer direccion\n\nEstablece la direccion exacta del giroscopio en tren motriz.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_heading(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.set_heading()` se puede usar para establecer la posición del tren motriz en cualquier encabezado dado. Este comando se puede usar para restablecer la orientación del giroscopio en el tren motriz cuando la direccion se establece en un valor de 0.\n\n`drivetrain.set_heading()` acepta un valor numérico entre  **0 y 360** como es el primer argumento, y `DEGREES` escrito en mayúsculas como su segundo argumento.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_heading(0, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.set_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_set_rotation","markdown":"# Establecer rotación\n\nEstablece el ángulo de rotación del giroscopio en el tren motriz.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.set_rotation()` se puede usar para establecer el ángulo de rotación del Drivetrain en cualquier valor positivo o negativo dado.\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.set_rotation()` puede aceptar valores enteros o decimales.\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.set_rotation()` **no** tiene un límite de 0-359 grados. El robot girará tantos grados como se establezca el primer argumento, los números positivos girarán el robot en sentido horario y los números negativos lo girarán en sentido antihorario. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que gire el robot en un total de 210 grados:\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(120, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(-45, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(45, DEGREES)\n```\n\n- Turn right (clockwise) to rotation 120 degrees.\n- Set the robot's current position as rotation of -45 degrees.\n- Turn right (clockwise) an additional 90 degrees (-45 degrees to +45 degrees) based on the set rotation value from the previous command.","signature":"drivetrain.set_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Establecer la Velocidad de Dar Vuelta\n\nEstablece la velocidad de veuletas del chasis.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.set_turn_velocity()` establecerá la velocidad del chasis al girar, pero no hará que el chasis se mueva cuando se usa sin un comando de 'turn' o 'turn_for'.\n\n`drivetrain.set_turn_velocity` acepta un rango de **-100 a 100** como primer argumento. Toma las unidades, `PERCENT`, escrito en letras mayúsculas como el segundo argumento.\n\nEstablecer la velocidad de vuelta del chasis en un valor negativo hará que el chasis gire en el opuesto de la dirección pasada en los siguientes comandos de giro.\n\nEstablecer la velocidad de giro del chasis en 0 evitará que el chasis gire, incluso cuando se utiliza un comando 'turn' o 'turn_for'.","signature":"drivetrain.set_turn_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_stop":{"name":"drivetrain_stop","markdown":"# Parar\n\nPara el chasis.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando 'drivetrain.stop()' detiene el chasis. No toma ningún argumento entre paréntesis. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo detendrá el chasis después de que el Robot haya conducido hacia adelante durante 3 segundos.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"drivetrain.stop()"},"drivetrain_turn":{"name":"drivetrain_turn","markdown":"# Dar Vuelta\n\nHara que el chasis de vuelta a la derecha o a la izquierda.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando \"drivetrain.turn\" girará el Drivetrain en la dirección dada por siempre hasta que se utilice un nuevo comando drivetrain, o hasta que se detenga el programa.\n\nUtilice el argumento 'LEFT' en letras mayúsculas para girar el chasis a la izquierda.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(LEFT)\n```\n\nUtilice el argumento 'RIGHT' en letras mayúsculas para girar el chasis a la derecha.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)"},"drivetrain_turn_for":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_for","markdown":"# Dar Vuelta Por\n\nGira el chasis durante un número especificado de grados.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(DIRECTION, ANGLE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEstablezca el número de grados que girará el chasis proporcionando tres argumentos.\n\n- direction (dirección) - RIGHT o LEFT (DERECHA O IZQUIERDA)\n- angle (ángulo) - un valor numérico\n- units (unidades) - DREGREES (grados)\n\nEl drivetrain.turn_for() acepta valores numéricos para el ángulo. Los valores negativos harán que el robot VR gire en lo contrario de la entrada por DIRECTION.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_for(LEFT, 90, DEGREES)`\n\nEl comando 'turn_for' es de forma predeterminada un comando de bloqueo. Impide que se ejecute cualquier código hasta que el chasis haya completado su turno.\n\n## Parámetro opcional\n\nSi el cuarto argumento se establece en 'wait=False', comandos de procedimiento se les permitirá ejecutar incluso antes de que el chasis ha completado su turno.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT, 90, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Dar Vuelta a la Dirección\n\nGira el chasis a una dirección específica cuando se utilizan los sensores Gyro o Inertial.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading(ANGLE, UNITS)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.turn_to_heading` se puede utilizar para girar el chasis a cualquier dirección dado en el sentido de las agujas del reloj.\n\nBasado en la dirección actual del Giroscopio, `drivetrain.turn_to_heading` determinará la dirección a girar.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` acepta valores numéricos en el rango de **0.00 a 359.99** cuando utilice la unidad `DEGREES` (grados).\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis haga cuatro vueltas: \n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(45.0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(90.0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(270.0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(180.0, DEGREES)\n```\n\n- Right to 45 degrees (Izquierda a 45 grados)\n- Right to 90 degrees (Izquierda a 90 grados)\n- Left to 270 degrees (Derecha a 270 grados)\n- Left to 180 degrees (Derecha a 180 grados)\n\nEl comando `turn_to_heading` por defecto bloqueará los comandos de procedimiento hasta que el giro del chasis se haya completado.\n\n## Parámetros Optionales\n\nPuede establecer `wait=False` como tercer parámetro para prevenir el comando `turn_to_heading` de bloquear los comandos de procedimiento de ejecución hasta que el giro del chasis se haya completado.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(180.0, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Dar vuelta a la Rotación\n\nGira el chasis a un ángulo de rotación específico.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(ANGLE, DEGREES)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation` se puede utilizar para girar el chasis a un valor positivo (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) o negativo (en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj).\n\nBasado en la rotación actual del chasis, `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation` determinará la dirección a girar..\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_rotation()` puede aceptar valores numéricos.\n\nLos valores numéricos **no** son limitados al rango de **0 - 359.99** grados. Los giros serán absolutos y pueden hacer que el robot gire más de una vez si es necesario. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis haga cuatro vueltas:\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90.0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(180, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(-45, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(0, DEGREES)\n```\n\n- Right (Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj)) a 90 grados\n- Right (Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj)) a 180 grados\n- Left (Izquierda (en sentido opuesto al de las agujas del reloj)) a -45 grados\n- Right (Derecha(en el sentido de las agujas del reloj)) a 0 grados\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation` por defecto bloqueará los comandos de procedimiento hasta que el giro del chasis se haya completado.\n\n## Parámetro opcional\n\nPuede establecer un tercer parámetro  `wait=False` para evitar el comando `turn_to_rotation' de bloquear los comandos de procedimiento hasta que se complete el giro del chasis.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(180.0, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"function_name":{"name":"function_name","markdown":"# Función\n\nLas funciones son bloques de código que se utilizan para realizar una acción.\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara crear una función, cree un nombre de función significativo. Esto significa que el nombre de la función debe describir con precisión lo que hace.\n\nUtilice la **palabra clave** `def` antes del nombre de la función para definir la función.\n\nIncluya paréntesis `()` y encierra parámetros opcionales después del nombre de la función, seguido por un colon`:`. Múltiples parámetros deben estar separados por comas `(parameter1, parameter2)`.\n\nEl cuerpo de la función debe sangrar cuatro espacios. Si no hay ningún código para que la función se ejecute, un `pass` debe incluirse en la definición de la funciónn.\n\n```don\ndef my_function():\n    pass\n```\n\n## Ejemplo 1\n\nEsta función no tiene parámetros e imprimirá \"Hello\" en la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\ndef print_hello_world():\n    brain.print(\"Hello World\")\n```\n## Ejemplo 2\n\nEsta función toma un parámetro, `name` e imprimirá el valor de `name` en la pantalla de la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\ndef print_name(name):\n    brain.print(name)\n```","signature":"def function_name(parameters):\\n\\tpass"},"looks_brain_clear":{"name":"looks_brain_clear","markdown":"# Borrar\n\nBorra todas las filas de la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nbrain.clear()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nUtilícelo para eliminar todas las líneas de la Consola de impresión. Este comando no toma argumentos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Hello\" durante un segundo y luego borrará toda la pantalla antes de imprimir \"Goodbye\"\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nbrain.clear()\nbrain.print(\"Goodbye\")\n```","signature":"brain.clear()"},"looks_brain_new_line":{"name":"looks_brain_new_line","markdown":"# Nueva Fila\n\nEstablece el cursor en una nueva línea en la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nbrain.new_line()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nUtilice esta opción para ir a una nueva fila dentro de la Consola de impresión. Cualquier comando posterior de 'brain.print()' se imprimirá en una nueva línea dentro de la Consola de impresión. Este comando no toma ningún argumento.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Hello\", luego en la línea siguiente de la pantalla, imprimirá \"World\".\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\nbrain.new_line()\nbrain.print(\"World\")\n```","signature":"brain.new_line()"},"looks_brain_print":{"name":"looks_brain_print","markdown":"# Imprimir\n\nImprime valores o texto en la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `brain.print()` imprimirá sus argumentos en la Consola de Impresión. \n\nEste comando puede tomar cualquier número de argumentos colocados entre sus paréntesis, incluyendo:\n\n- strings (cadenas) - cualquier combinación de letras, números o símbolos que se imprimirán exactamente como están escritos. Incluya la frase entre comillas y coloque esta frase directamente dentro de los paréntesis:  \n\t`brain.print(\"This is my string.\")`\n- numeric values (valores numéricos) - estos se imprimirán como números enteros por defecto*\n- variables - el valor almacenado de cualquier variable declarada previamente\n- valores reportados de otros comandos, como comandos de detección \n\n*La precisión de impresión se puede cambiar añadiendo una final argumento de `precision=` separados de otros argumentos por una coma. Establece `precision=1` imprimir con el decimal de décimas '0.1', establece `precision=2` para imprimir con el decimal centésimas, coloque '0.01'. El número asignado será igual al número de decimales impreso después del punto deicimal; hasta 'precisión=9' '0.000000001'.\n\nEl número impreso se redondea, hacia arriba o hacia abajo, al valor más cercano del lugar de precisión seleccionado.\n\n## Ejemplo 1\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"1 + 2 + Words\")\n```\nEsto imprimirá:\n \n`1 + 2 + Words` \n\na la Consola de Impresión.\n\n## Ejemplo 2\n\n```don\nbrain.print(1.67, precision=0)\nbrain.new_line()\nbrain.print(1.67, precision=1)\n```\nEsto imprimirá:\n\n```don\n2\n1.7\n```\na la Consola de Impresión.\n\n## Ejemplo 3\n\n```don\nmy_variable = 10\nbrain.print(my_variable + 5)\n```\n\nEsto imprimirá: \n\n`15` \n\na la Consola de Impresión.","signature":"brain.print(\"Hello\")"},"looks_brain_set_print_color":{"name":"looks_brain_set_print_color","markdown":"# Establecer Color de Impresión\n\nEstablece el color del texto impreso en la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nbrain.set_print_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija el color del texto impreso en la Consola de Impresión. El argumento debe estar escrito en mayúsculas. El color se puede establecer en `BLACK` (Negro), `RED` (Rojo), `GREEN` (Verde), or `BLUE` (Azul).\n\nLas letras, los números, los valores del sensor y los valores variables se pueden imprimir en el color seleccionado establecido por el comando 'set_print_color()'. \n\nCualquier comando 'brain.print()' usado después del 'set_print_color()' lo hará imprima en el color que haya establecido más recientemente en el programa.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá 'GO!' en verde, y 'STOP!' en rojo a la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nset_print_color(GREEN)\nbrain.print(\"GO!\")\nset_print_color(RED)\nbrain.print(\"STOP!\")\n```","signature":"brain.set_print_color(BLACK)"},"looks_monitor_sensor":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor","markdown":"# Sensor del Monitor\n\nLa función Sensor del Monitor se utiliza para añadir sensores a la consola del monitor.\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara supervisar los valores del sensor en la consola del monitor, utilice la función 'monitor_sensor()' añadiendo los identificadores de los sensores como parámetros de cadena. Esta función debe llamarse al inicio de la programa.\n\nA continuación se muestra la lista de identificadores de sensor compatibles:\n\n- `brain.timer_time`\n- `drivetrain.is_done`\n- `drivetrain.is_moving`\n- `drivetrain.heading`\n- `drivetrain.rotation`\n- `left_bumper.pressed`\n- `right_bumper.pressed`\n- `distance.found_object`\n- `distance.get_distance`\n- `front_eye.near_object`\n- `front_eye.detect`\n- `front_eye.brightness`\n- `down_eye.near_object`\n- `down_eye.detect`\n- `down_eye.brightness`\n- `location.position`\n- `location.position_angle`\n\nAñadir sensores a la consola del monitor:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"brain.timer_time\", \"drivetrain.is_done\")\n```\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn el siguiente ejemplo, `brain.timer_time` y `drivetrain.is_done` son identificadores de sensor que se añaden a la consola del monitor con la función 'monitor_sensor'.\n\nJusto al principio del programa (antes de que el robot se mueva), ambos sensores mostrarán el valor de '0' y 'True', respectivamente. \n\nMientras que se ejecuta el programa, el valor de 'brain.timer_time' aumenta con el tiempo, mientras que el 'drivetrain.is_done' se convierte en 'False'. Luego, después de que el robot haya completado sus movimientos, 'drivetrain.is_done' se vuelve 'True' de nuevo.\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"brain.timer_time\", \"drivetrain.is_done\")\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 600, MM, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM, wait=True)\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_variable":{"name":"looks_monitor_variable","markdown":"# Variable del Monitor\n\nLa función Variable del Monitor se utiliza para agregar variables a la consola del monitor.\n\n```don\nmonitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\", ...)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara supervisar los valores de las variables en la consola del monitor, utilice la función 'monitor_variable()' añadiendo los nombres de las variables como parámetros de cadena. Las variables que se deben supervisar deben estar en el ámbito mundial del programa.\n\n```don\nmy_variable = 0\nmy_boolean = False\n\ndef main():\n    global my_variable, my_boolean\n    brain.print(my_variable)\n    brain.new_line()\n    brain.print(my_boolean)\nvr_thread(main)\n```\n\nAdding variables to the monitor console:\n\n```don\nmonitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\")\n```\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn el ejemplo siguiente, 'my_variable' y 'my_boolean' son variables globales que se agregan a la consola del monitor con la función 'monitor_variable'.\n\nAl inicio del programa, ambas variables mostrarán el valor de '0' y 'False', respectivamente.A medida que se ejecuta el programa, el valor de 'mi_variable' se puede observar para aumentar de 0 a 4. \n\nAl final del programa, el valor de 'my_boolean' se convierte en 'True'.\n\n```don\nmy_variable = 0\nmy_boolean = False\n\ndef main():\n    global my_variable, my_boolean\n    monitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\")\n    for x in range(5):\n        my_variable = x\n    my_boolean = True\nvr_thread(main)\n```","signature":"monitor_variable()"},"looks_pen_move":{"name":"looks_pen_move","markdown":"# Establecer la posición de la pluma\n\nEstablece la posición de la pluma en VEX VR.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\nIntroduzca 'UP' (hacia arriba)  o 'DOWN' (hacia abajo) entre paréntesis.  \n**UP**: establecerá la pluma hacia arriba y no dibujará.\n**DOWN**: establecerá la pluma hacia abajo y se dibujará una línea en el Playground.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el lápiz hacia abajo y luego para arriba durante cuatro ciclos en incrementos de 50 mm, dibujando una línea discontinua en el Playground. \n\n```don\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 50, MM, wait=True)\n    pen.move(UP)\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 50, MM, wait=True)\n```","signature":"pen.move(DOWN)"},"looks_pen_set_pen_color":{"name":"looks_pen_set_pen_color","markdown":"# Establecer el color de la pluma\n\nEstablece el color de la pluma en VEX VR.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando 'pen.set pen color(BLACK)' acepta un argumento, una opción de color escrita en letras mayúsculas. Las posibles entradas son `BLACK` (negro), `RED` (rojo), `GREEN` (verde), or `BLUE` (azul).\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el color de la pluma en azul.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color(BLUE)\n```","signature":"pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)"},"magnet_energize":{"name":"magnet_energize","markdown":"# Energize Electromagnet\n\nSets the VR Robot Electromagnet to two different modes: **boost** or **drop**.\n\n```don\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nEnter either `BOOST` or `DROP` within the parentheses.  \n**BOOST**: will attract a nearby magnetic object to the Electromagnet  \n**DROP**: will release any object the Electromagnet is holding \n\n## Example\n\nIn the Disk Mover playground, this example will have the robot drive forward 800mm, pick up an object, and then drop the object after driving 200mm in reverse.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 800, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 200, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(DROP)\n```","signature":"magnet_energize(BOOST)"},"sensing_brain_timer_reset":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_reset","markdown":"# Reset Timer\n\nResets the Brain's timer.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe brain's timer begins at the beginning of each project. The `brain.timer_reset()` function is used to reset the timer back to 0 seconds. \n\n## Example\n\nThis project has the Drivetrain drive forward while the timer in the Brain is less than five seconds.  The brain.timer_reset() command is used at the beginning of the project to reset the Brain’s timer.  \n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"brain.timer_reset()"},"sensing_brain_timer_time":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_time","markdown":"# Timer Value\n\nReports the value of the Brain's timer in seconds.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_time(SECONDS)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe timer starts at zero seconds when the project starts, and returns the timer's value as a decimal value.\n\n`brain.timer_time()` can be assigned to variables, be used in boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.\n\n## Example\n\nThis project has the Drivetrain drive forward while the timer in the Brain is less than five seconds.  The brain.timer_reset() command is used at the condition in the While loop.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the while statement contains a colon at the end - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"brain.timer_time(SECONDS)"},"sensing_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Bumper Pressed\n\nReports if the Right Bumper or Left Bumper is pressed. \n\n`bumper.pressed()`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`bumper.pressed()` returns **true** if the Bumper is being pressed.\n\n`bumper.pressed()` returns **false** if the Bumper is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use. The VR Robot is equipped with a `right_bumper` and a `left_bumper`.\n\n```\nright_bumper.pressed()\nleft_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nThe `bumper.pressed()` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition for control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nleft_bumper_pressed = left_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif left_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Left bumper pressed\")\nif right_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Right bumper pressed\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(left_bumper.pressed())\n```","signature":"bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_distance_found_object":{"name":"sensing_distance_found_object","markdown":"# Distance Found Object\n\nReports whether the built-in Distance Sensor sees an object or surface in front of the robot. \n\n`distance.found_object()`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`distance.found_object()` returns **true** when the built in Distance Sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view, and within 3000 mm of the sensor.\n\n`distance.found_object()` returns **false** when the built in Distance Sensor does not detect an object or surface within 3000 mm.\n\nThe `distance.found_object()` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nfound_object = distance.found_object()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif distance.found_object():\n\tbrain.print(\"Found object\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(distance.found_object())\n```","signature":"distance.found_object()"},"sensing_distance_get_distance":{"name":"sensing_distance_get_distance","markdown":"# Distance From\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object from the Distance Sensor.\n\n`distance.get_distance(UNITS)`\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose whether `distance.get_distance(UNITS)` is reported in millimeters or inches by replacing `UNITS` with either `MM` or `INCHES` as parameters.\n\n```\ndistance.get_distance(MM)\ndistance.get_distance(INCHES)\n```\n\nThe `distance.get_distance(UNITS)` function can be called and assigned to variables, be used in comparison statements, or be used in functions that take number values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nmy_distance_mm = distance.get_distance(MM)\n```\nUsing it in a comparison statement:\n\n```\nif distance.get_distance(INCHES) < 1:\n\tbrain.print(\"Close to object\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(distance.get_distance(INCHES))\n```","signature":"distance.get_distance(MM)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Down Eye sensor brightness\n\nReturns the brightness of an object.\n\n`down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` command returns a numerical value between 0 and 100.  It returns a value in percent.  White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%.  All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between that range of values.  \n\n`down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Down Eye sensor color\n\nReturns the specific color detected by the Down Eye sensor.\n\n`down_eye.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `down_eye.detect(RED)` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Down Eye sensor detects the selected color.It returns **False** when the Down Eye sensor detects a color different from the one selected.  \n\nChoose the color for the Down Eye sensor to detect:\n\n* NONE\n* RED\n* GREEN \n* BLUE\n\n`down_eye.detect(RED)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` command is used as a Boolean statement.  If the `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` command returns **True**, Green will be printed to the Monitor Console.  If the `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` command returns **False**, Not Green will be displayed  in the Monitor Console.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if down_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            if down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n                brain.print(\"Green\")\n\n            else:\n                brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"down_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Down Eye sensor near object\n\nReturns if the Down Eye sensor is close enough to an object to detect a color.\n\n`down_eye.near_object()`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `down_eye.near_object()` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Down Eye sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors, for example, a Disk.  It returns **False** when the Down Eye sensor isn’t close enough to an object with detectable colors.  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` will not detect the Playground floor as an object, but does detect colors (with the exception of pen drawings) on the Playground floor.  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.  \n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `down_eye.near_object()` command is used as a Boolean statement.If the `down_eye.near_object()` command returns **True**, the Drivetrain will stop, Near a Disk will be printed to the Monitor Console, and the project will stop.If the `down_eye.near_object()` command returns **False**, Not Near a Disk will be continuously printed on new lines.\n\n```\n while True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if down_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            brain.print(\"Near a Disk\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near a Disk\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"down_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_drivetrain_heading":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_heading","markdown":"# Drive Heading\n\nReturns the direction that the Drivetrain is facing by using the Gyro sensor's current angular position.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.heading(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` command returns a numeric value.  The numerical value (the DEGREES) will increase when rotating clockwise and a decrease when rotating counter-clockwise.  \n\nThe `drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` command returns a range from 0.00 to 359.99 degrees.  \n\n`drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_done":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_done","markdown":"# Drive Is Done\n\nReturns if the Drivetrain has completed its movement.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.is_done()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` is a command that returns a Boolean value. It returns **true** when the Drivetrain's motors have completed their movement.\nIt returns **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are still moving.\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.","signature":"drivetrain.is_done()"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving","markdown":"# Drive Is Moving\n\nReports if the Drivetrain is currently moving.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` returns **true** when the Drivetrain's motors are moving.\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` returns **false** when the Drivetrain's motors are stopped.\n\nThe `drivetrain.is_moving()` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition for control statements, or be used as a function parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nstill_driving = drivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif drivetrain.is_moving():\n\tbrain.print(\"Still driving\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(drivetrain.is_moving())\n```","signature":"drivetrain.is_moving()"},"sensing_drivetrain_rotation":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_rotation","markdown":"# Drive Rotation\n\nReturns the Drivetrain's angle of rotation.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## How To Use\nThe `drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` command reports a numeric value.  It returns a positive value when the Drivetrain turns in a clockwise direction.  It returns a negative value when the Drivetrain turns in a counter-clockwise direction.\n\n`drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Color Brightness\n\nReports the brightness of an object from one of the Color Sensor Eyes.\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` reports the brightness of an object. White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%. All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between that range of values.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```\nfront_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\ndown_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\nThe `eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` function returns a value between 0 and 100 value and can be assigned to variables, used in comparison statements, or in functions that take number values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\ncolor_brightness = front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\nUsing it in a comparison statement:\n\n```\nif down_eye.brightness(PERCENT) < 25:\n\tbrain.print(\"Dark color\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(front_eye.brightness(PERCENT))\n```","signature":"distance.distance(MM)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Color Sensing\n\nReports if one of the Color Sensor Eyes detects a specific color.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` returns **true** when the Color Sensor Eye detects the selected color.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` returns **false** when the Color Sensor Eye detects a different color than the one selected.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```\nfront_eye.detect(RED)\ndown_eye.detect(RED)\n```\n\nChoose which color to detect. Replace the `COLOR` parameter with the chosen color to detect.\n\n```\nNONE\nBLACK\nRED\nGREEN\nBLUE\n```\n\n\nThe `eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nfound_red = front_eye.detect(RED)\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n\tbrain.print(\"On top of green\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(down_eye.detect(BLUE))\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.detect(RED)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Color Near Object\n\nReports if the Color Sensor Eye is close enough to an object to detect a color.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` returns **true** when the Color Sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` returns **false** when the Color Sensor isn't close enough to an object with detectable colors.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```\nfront_eye.near_object()\ndown_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nThe `eye_sensor.near_object()` function returns a **true** or **false** value and can be assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\ncolor_obj_front = front_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif front_eye.near_object():\n\tbrain.print(\"Object in front\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(down_eye.near_object())\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.near_object()"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Front Eye sensor brightness\n\nReturns the brightness of an object.\n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` command returns a numerical value between 0 and 100.  It returns a value in percent.  White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%.  All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between that range of values.  \n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Front Eye sensor color\n\nReturns the specific color detected by the Front Eye sensor .\n\n`front_eye.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `front_eye.detect(RED)` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Front Eye sensor detects the selected color.  It returns **False** when the Front Eye sensor detects a color different from the one selected.  \n\nChoose the color for the Front Eye sensor to detect:\n\n* NONE\n* RED\n* GREEN \n* BLUE\n\n`front_eye.detect(RED)` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter. \n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` command is used as a Boolean statement.If the `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` command returns **True**, Green will be printed to the Monitor Console. If the `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` command returns **False**, Not Green will be displayed  in the Monitor Console.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if front_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            if front_eye.detect(GREEN):\n                brain.print(\"Green\")\n\n            else:\n                brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows.","signature":"front_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Front Eye sensor near object\n\nReports if the Front Eye sensor is close enough (within 100 mm) to an object to detect a color.\n\n`front_eye.near_object()`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `front_eye.near_object()` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Front Eye sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.  It returns **False** when the Front Eye sensor isn’t close enough to an object with detectable colors.  \n\n`front_eye.near_object()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter. \n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `front_eye.near_object()` command is used as a Boolean statement.If the `front_eye.near_object()` command returns **True**, the Drivetrain will stop, Near an Object will be printed to the Monitor Console, and the project will stop.If the `front_eye.near_object()` command returns **False**, Not Near an Object will be continuously printed on new lines. \n\n```\n while True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if front_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            brain.print(\"Near an Object\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n\n```\n \nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"front_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_left_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_left_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Left Bumper pressed\n\nReturns if the Left Bumper is pressed.\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `left_bumper.pressed()` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Bumper is pressed and **False** when the Bumper is not pressed.\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.  \n\n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `left_bumper.pressed()` command is used as a Boolean statement.  If the Left Bumper sensor is not pressed, the Drivetrain will move forward.  If the Left Bumper sensor is pressed, the Drivetrain will stop.  \n\n```\nwhile True:\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n        if left_bumper.pressed():\n            drivetrain.stop()\n        else:\n            drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"left_bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_location_position":{"name":"sensing_location_position","markdown":"# Position \n\nReports the X or Y coordinate position of the Robot.\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)` returns the X or Y coordinate position of the VR Robot as a numerical value.\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)`  command can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter. \n\n## Example\n\nThis project uses the location.position(AXIS, UNITS) command to code the VR robot to move to the number 28 on the Numbered Grid World Playground. The location.position(AXIS, UNITS) command is used as a Boolean expression in this project. \n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(X, MM) < 500:\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n\twait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT,90,DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(Y, MM) < -500:\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n\twait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"location.position(X, MM)"},"sensing_location_position_angle":{"name":"sensing_location_position_angle","markdown":"# Position Angle in Degrees\n\nReports angle of the Robot in degrees.\n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `location.position_angle(DEGREES)` command returns the current angle of the VR robot as a numerical value.  \n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)` command can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean expressions, or be used in other commands that take numerical values as a parameter.","signature":"location.position_angle(DEGREES)"},"sensing_right_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_right_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Right Bumper pressed\n\nReturns if the Right Bumper is pressed\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()`\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe `right_bumper.pressed()` command returns a Boolean value.  It returns **True** when the Bumper is pressed and **False** when the Bumper is not pressed.\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()` can be assigned to variables, be used in Boolean statements, or be used in other commands that take Boolean values as a parameter.  \n\n## Example\n\nIn this project, the `right_bumper.pressed()` command is used as a Boolean statement.  If the Right Bumper sensor is not pressed, the Drivetrain will move forward.  If the Right Bumper sensor is pressed, the Drivetrain will stop.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n        if right_bumper.pressed():\n            drivetrain.stop()\n        else:\n            drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nA wait command needs to be used with loops. \n\nNotice how the if/else statement contains a colon at the end of each line - this indicates to Python that a block of statements follows","signature":"right_bumper.pressed()"},"variables_my_2d_list":{"name":"variables_my_2d_list","markdown":"# 2D List Variable\n\nSimilar to a List variable, 2D List variables are used to store multiple values in a single variable name.  However, 2D List variables store more than one list.  \n\n## How To Use\nThe default name of a 2D List variable is: \n\n```don\nmy_2d_list = [\n    \t[0,0,0],\n    \t[0,0,0],\n    \t[0,0,0]\n    ]\n```\n\nWhen renaming,  2D List variables have the same naming rules as all other variables:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n\nThe data elements in two dimensional arrays can be accessed using two indexes. An index is the location of a value in the List variable.  To access a value, first use the name of the 2D List and then the two indexes enclosed in square brackets.","signature":"my_2d_list = [\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0]\\n]"},"variables_my_boolean":{"name":"variables_my_boolean","markdown":"# Boolean Variable\n\nBoolean variables can hold either **True** or **False** values.\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo assign a Boolean variable a value, use the = operator.   \n\nThe default name of a numeric variable is my_boolean.  Apply  the following rules when renaming a numeric variable:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)","signature":"my_boolean = True"},"variables_my_list":{"name":"variables_my_list","markdown":"# List Variable\n\nList variables are used to store multiple values in a single variable name.\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe default name of a List variable is: `my_list = [0,0,0]`\n\nWhen renaming,  List variables have the same naming rules as all other variables:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n\nTo assign a list of values to a List variable, use the = operator followed by comma-separated values, called elements, enclosed in square brackets.  \n\nIf the values are numbers, then the commas should come right after each number. \n\n`my_list = [0,1,2]`\n\nIf the values are strings, the commas should come outside of the quotation marks.\n\n`my_list = [“Drive”,”Forward”]`\n\nThe data elements in a List variable can be accessed using an index. An index is the location of a value in the List variable.  Each element inside of an array is assigned an integer, beginning with zero. \n\n## Examples\n\nThe project uses list `my_list` in a for loop.  The values in `my_list` are strings.  The variable y is the iterator in the for loop.  Therefore, the for loop iterates, and then prints, each element within the List variable `my_list`.\n\n```don\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", ‘100%', \"fun!!\"]\nfor y in my_list:\n\tbrain.print(y)\n\tbrain.new_line()\n\twait(5, MSEC)\n```\n\nThis project will print:\n\n```don\nVEXcode VR\nis\n100%\nfun!!\n```\n\nThis project does not need a for loop because it is just printing one element of the List variable `my_list`  To access one element, place the index of the element in square brackets after the name of the List variable.\n\n\n```don\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", '100%', \"fun!!\"]\nbrain.print(my_list[3])\nwait(5, MSEC)\n```  \n\nThis project will print:\n\n```\nfun!!\n```","signature":"my_list = [0,0,0]"},"variables_my_number":{"name":"variables_my_number","markdown":"# Numeric Variable\n\nNumeric variables are used to store numerical values.\n\n## How To Use\n\nTo assign a numeric variable a value, use the = operator.   \n\nThe default name of a numeric variable is my_number.  Apply  the following rules when renaming a numeric variable:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)\n\n## Example\n\nThis project will have the drivetrain drive forward for 150 mm and then turn right for 90 degrees. \n\n```don\ndistance_to_travel = 150\nangle_to_turn = 90\n\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, distance_to_travel, MM)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, angle_to_turn, DEGREES)\n```\nThis project created two variables and named them `distance_to_travel` and `angle_to_turn`.  A = is then used to assign a value to each variable. The variables are then used in the `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD)` and `drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT)` commands.","signature":"my_number = 1"},"variables_my_string":{"name":"variables_my_string","markdown":"# String Variable\n\nString variables are used to store strings, which is a sequence of characters.  Characters can include numbers, letters, and symbols. \n\n## How To Use\n\nTo assign a string variable a value, use the = operator.   \n\nThe default name of a numeric variable is my_string.  Apply  the following rules when renaming a numeric variable:\n\n- The name cannot use special characters (e.g. an exclamation point)\n- The name cannot begin with a number\n- The name cannot use spaces\n- The name cannot be a reserved word in VEXcode VR (e.g. Drivetrain)","signature":"my_string = \\\"Hello World\\\""},"vr_thread":{"name":"vr_thread","markdown":"# VR Thread\n\nThe VR Thread function must be used to call functions at the global level of the script.\n\n## How To Use\n\nYou can take any function you have made previously, and pass it to `vr_thread()` at the global level. Calling at least one function inside of this is required to run a program. You do not need to do all function calls with `vr_thread()`, just ones that are at the global level.\n\nIf more than one `vr_thread()` call is made, the functions will execute in parallel.\n\nThe functions will, in general, execute in the order they are called. However, if a function waits, it will pass execution to the next function, and continue when the other function finishes, or when the other function also waits.\n\n```don\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nThe functions that cause threads to switch:\n\n```\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.set_heading(0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(0, DEGREES)\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nany user created function\n\n```\nCalling a method with `vr_thread()` inside of another method will cause a runtime error. If you run this program:\n\n```\ndef main():\n    vr_thread(print_hello())\n\ndef print_hello():\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nIt will give you an error that looks like this:\n\n```\nError: Traceback (most recent call last):\n  line 9, in vr_thread\n  line 5, in main\nNameError: name 'print_hello' is not defined\n```\n\n## Example 1\n\nWe make a single function that is executed in the global space with `vr_thread()`. The `move_drivetrain()` function will make the robot move 200 mm and then print when it is done.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\n```\n## Example 2\n\nNow we added the `move_pen_down()` function. This will move the pen down and print a message. We make the call `vr_thread(move_pen_down())` **after** the `vr_thread(move_drivetrain())` call. However, because `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)` makes the function wait, it will switch over to the `move_pen_down()` function, which will move the pen down before the robot is done moving.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\nvr_thread(move_pen_down())\n```\n\n## Example 3\n\nMoving the `move_pen_down()` in to the `move_drivetrain()` will now call it sequintially, so there will be no pen drawing since it doesn't drop until after the movement is finished. This also shows that calling functions within other functions can be done like normal function call.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n    move_pen_down()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\n```","signature":"vr_thread(function_name())"}}},"es-419":{"blocks":{"add":{"name":"add","markdown":"# Añadir\n\nAgrega dos valores juntos e informa la suma.\n\n![add](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/add/add.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Añadir** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLa longitud de **add** bloque encaja en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\n\nAñadir dos números.\n\n![add_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/add/add_numbers.png)\n\nAñadir con valores de variables.\n\n![add_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/add/add_variables.png)  \n\nAñadir con valores de sensores o dispositivos.\n\n![add_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/add/add_sensors.png)\n\n"},"and":{"name":"and","markdown":"# Y\n\nInforma si dos condiciones booleanas son ambas verdaderas. \n\n![and](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/and/and.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nEl bloque **y** reporta **true** cuando los dos condiciones son **true**. \n\nEl **y** bloque reporta **false** cuado uno (o los dos) condicione(s) son **false**.\n\nEl **y** bloque booleano reporta un **true** o **false** valor y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonal (de seis lados).\n\n![and_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/and/and_shape.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo comprobará si dos botones en el VEX IQ Controller se están presionando al mismo tiempo.\n\n![and_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/and/and_sensors.png)\n\nEl **y** bloque volverá **true** cuando los dos **Controller E Up button** y el **Controller L Down button** están siendo presionados.\n\nEl **y** bloque volverá **false** cuando uno (o los dos) de los botones del control no se presionan.\n\n![and_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/and/and_truth.png)"},"boolean_variable":{"name":"boolean_variable","markdown":"# Variable BooleanaUse the\n\nReporta valor de una variable booleana.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/boolean_variable/boolean_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nBloques de **Variable Booleana** se utilizan para reportar de un valor de **true** o **false** almacendo dentro de la variable. Utilice el **establecer variable booleana** bloque para establecer o actualizar el valor de una variable booleana.\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/boolean_variable/boolean_set_value.png)\n\nEl bloque **Variable Booleana** reporta **true** o **false** valor y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/boolean_variable/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nLas variables booleanas se crean utilizando el **Hacer un Booleano** botón.\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/boolean_variable/boolean_button.png)    \n"},"break":{"name":"break","markdown":"# Romper\n\nSale de un bucle de repetición inmediatamente.\n\n![break](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/break/break.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nCuando se añade a la repetición de bloque 'C', el bloque **romper** saldrá del bucle que se encuentra actualmente en. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo moverá el chasis hacia adelante y comprobará si el botón del VEX IQ Brain **up** esta presionado. \n\nSi el botón **up** esta **presionado**, el bloque **romper** saldrá del bucle de repetición para siempre y detendrá el chasis.\n\n![break_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/break/break_example.png)"},"bumper":{"name":"bumper","markdown":"# Presionando el Parachoque\n\nReporta si el Interruptor de Parachoques está pulsado.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Presionando el Parachoque** reporta **true** si se presiona el interruptor de parachoques.\n\n**Presionando el Parachoque** reporta **false** si el interruptor de parachoques no está pulsado.\n\nElija el interruptor de parachoques que desea utilizar.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano **Presionando el Parachoque** informa de un valor **true** o false y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis caras).\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **presionando el parachoque**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reportero\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)\n\n"},"change_variable":{"name":"change_variable","markdown":"# Cambiar Variable\n\nCambia una variable por el valor dado.\n\n![change_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/change_variable/change_variable.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la variable que desea utilizar - la variable seleccionada también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![change_variable_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/change_variable/change_variable_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **Cambiar Variable** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![change_variable_value](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/change_variable/change_variable_value.png)\n\nEste ejemplo aumentará la variable que establece la velocidad del chasis por 10 cada vez **bucle de repetición** se repite.\n\n![change_variable_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/change_variable/change_variable_example2.png)\n"},"clear_all_rows":{"name":"clear_all_rows","markdown":"# Borrar todas las filas\n\nElimine todas las filas en Print Console.\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nUse el bloque ** eliminar todas las filas ** para eliminar todas las líneas en Print Console.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Hola\" por un segundo y luego borrará toda la pantalla antes de imprimir \"Adiós\".\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"comment":{"name":"comment","markdown":"# Comentario\n\nPermite a los programadores escribir información para ayudar a describir su programa.\n\n![comment_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/comment/comment_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nLos programadores profesionales utilizan comentarios a través de sus programas para ayudar a otros a entender su código. Los comentarios también pueden ayudar con la solución de problemas y la colaboración. \n\nAgregue texto en bloque **comentario** seleccionando primero el cuadrado transparente y eliminando cualquier texto existente. A continuación, escriba el nuevo texto.\n\n![comment_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/comment/comment_example3.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nLos comentarios no cambian el programa o los bloques que lo rodean.\n\n![comment_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/comment/comment_example2.png)\n\n"},"distance_distance":{"name":"distance_distance","markdown":"# Distancia de\n\nReporta la distancia del objeto más cercano.\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/distance_distance/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija si **distancia desde** se informa en milímetros o pulgadas. \n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/distance_distance/distance_from_unit.png)\n\nEl bloque de reportero **distancia desde** se utiliza dentro de bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/distance_distance/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **distancia desde**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reporteros.\n\n![distance_from_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/distance_distance/distance_from_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![distance_from_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/distance_distance/distance_from_monitor.png)\n\n"},"distance_found":{"name":"distance_found","markdown":"# Distancia encontrada objeto\n\nInforma si el sensor de distancia incorporado ha encontrado un objeto frente al robot.\n\n![distance_found_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/distance_found/distance_found_block.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Distance found object** reports **true** when the built in Distance sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view, and within 3000 mm of the sensor.\n\n**Distancia encontrada objeto** informa **falso** cuando el sensor de distancia incorporado no detecta un objeto o superficie dentro de 3000 mm.\n\nEl **objeto encontrado distancia** El bloque booleano informa un valor **verdadero** o **falso** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![distance_found_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/distance_found/distance_found_insert.png)\n\n\n\n\n"},"divide":{"name":"divide","markdown":"# Dividir\n\nDivide el primer valor por el segundo valor e informa del dividendo.\n\n![divide](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/divide/divide.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Dividir** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **dividir** se adapta a bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nIntroduzca los números que se van a dividir.\n\n![divide_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/divide/divide_numbers.png)\n\nDividir con valores de variables.\n\n![divide_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/divide/divide_variables.png)\n\nDividir con valores de sensores o dispositivos.\n\n![divide_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/divide/divide_sensors.png)\n\n"},"drive":{"name":"drive","markdown":"# Conducir\n\nMueve el chasis por siempre.\n\n![drive_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive/drive_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **conducir** ejecutarรก el chasis para siempre hasta que se utilice un nuevo bloque Chasis o se detenga el programa.\n\nElija la direcciรณn en la que se moverรก el Chasis.\n\n![drive_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive/drive_direction.png)  \n"},"drive_for":{"name":"drive_for","markdown":"# Conducir por\n\nMueve el chasis para una distancia determinada.\n\n![drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_for/drive_for.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija en qué dirección se moverá el chasis.\n\n![drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_for/drive_for_direction.png)\n\nEl bloque **conducir por** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEstablezca hasta dónde se moverá el chasis introduciendo un valor y eligiendo la unidad de medida (pulgadas o milímetros).\n\n![drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_for/drive_for_units.png)\n\nAutomáticamente, otros bloques **esperarán** hasta que el chasis termine de moverse.\n\nPuede seleccionar la flecha para expandirla **y no espere** - esto hará que otros bloques continúen ejecutándose mientras el chasis se mueve.\n\n![drive_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_for/drive_for_anddontwait.png))\n"},"drive_heading":{"name":"drive_heading","markdown":"# Dirección del Chasis\n\nReporta la direcciรณn que enfrenta el chasis.\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_heading/drive_heading.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Dirección del Chasis** reporta de un rango de **0.00 a 359.99 grados**.\n\nThe **drive heading** reports a counter-clockwise heading as shown in the image below.\n\n![TopDownBot01](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_heading/TopDownBot01.png)\n\nEl bloque de reportero **dirección del chasis** se utiliza dentro de bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_heading/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_is_done":{"name":"drive_is_done","markdown":"# Conducir ya termino\n\nInforma si el chasis ha completado su movimiento.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Conducir ya termino** reporta **true** cuando los motores del chasis han completado su movimiento.\n\n**Conducir ya termino** reporta **false** cuando los motores del chasis todavía están en movimiento.\n\nEl bloque booleano **drive is done** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis caras).\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_is_moving":{"name":"drive_is_moving","markdown":"# Chasis esta Moviendo\n\nReporta si el chasis se estรก moviendo actualmente.\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Chasis esta Moviendo** reporta **true** cuando los motores del chasis se estรกn moviendo.\n\n**Chasis esta Moviendo** reporta **false** cuando los motores del chasis de mando se detienen.\n\nEl bloque booleano **chasis esta moviendo** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_rotation":{"name":"drive_rotation","markdown":"# Rotación del chasis\n\nInforma del ángulo de rotación del chasis cuando se configura con un sensor Gyro.\n\n![drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_rotation/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Rotación del chasis** reporta un valor **positivo** valor cuando el Drivetrain gira en una dirección **en el sentido de las agujas del reloj**.\n\n**Rotación del chasis** reporta un valor **negativo** valor cuando el Drivetrain gira en una dirección **en sentido opuesto al de las agujas del reloj**.\n\nEl bloque de reportero **rotación del chasis** se utiliza dentro de bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![drive_rotation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_inserted.png)\n"},"equal_to":{"name":"equal_to","markdown":"# Igual a\n\nInforma si el primer valor es el mismo, o igual al segundo valor.\n\n![equal_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/equal_to/equal_to.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\n**Igual a** reporta **verdadero** cuando dos valores son exactamente igual.\n\n**Igual a** reporta **falso** cuando dos valores no son iguales.\n\nEl bloque **Igual a** aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEl bloque Booleano **Igual a** reporta un valor **verdadero** o **falso**  y encaja en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis lados).\n\n![equal_to_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/equal_to/equal_to_inserted.png)"},"forever":{"name":"forever","markdown":"# Por siempre\n\nUn bloque 'C' que repite cualquier bloque contenido adentro, para siempre.\n\n![forever](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/forever/forever.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nColoca bloques en un bloque 'C' **Por siempre** para que esas acciones se repitan **Por siempre**.\n\nPuede salir de una repetición **Por siempre** poniendo un bloque de **Romper** dentro.\n\n![forever_example_2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/forever/forever_example_2.png)\n\nBloques 'C' también se pueden poner uno dentro del otro. Este concepto se llama anidamiento (nesting) y puede ayudar a ahorrar tiempo al programar diferentes comportamientos."},"function":{"name":"function","markdown":"# Función\n\nRealiza una función matemática seleccionada.\n\n![function](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/function/function.png)\n\n* **abs** - valor absoluto\n* **floor** - redondea hacia abajo al entero más cercano\n* **ceiling** - redondea al entero más cercano\n* **sqrt** - Raiz Cuadrada\n* **sin** - sine\n* **cos** - coseno\n* **tan** - tangente\n* **asin** - Arcoseno o la inversa de sine\n* **acos** - arcocoseno o el inverso del coseno\n* **atan** - arctan o el inverso de la tangente\n* **ln** - base de logaritmo natural e\n* **log** - base de logaritmo 10\n* **e ^** - poderes de e\n* **10 ^** - poderes de 10\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl bloque **Función** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nElija la **Función** matemática de la lista de opciones.\n\n![function_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/function/function_select.png)\n\nEl bloque de **Función** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares.\n\n![function_fit](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/function/function_fit.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"greater_than":{"name":"greater_than","markdown":"# Más que\n\nReporta si el primer valor es más grande (mayor) que el segundo valor.  \n\n![greater_than](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/greater_than/greater_than.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Más que** reporta **true** cuando el primer valor es mayor que el segundo valor. \n\n**Más que** reporta **false** cuando el primer valor es menor o igual que el segundo valor.\n\nEl bloque **más que** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEl bloque booleano **más que** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis caras).\n\n![greater_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/greater_than/greater_than_inserted.png)\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Nivel de batería bueno\" en la pantalla del VEX IQ Brain si el nivel de batería es superior al 75%.\n\n![greater_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/greater_than/greater_than_example.png)\n"},"if_then":{"name":"if_then","markdown":"# Si entonces\n\nUn bloque 'C' que ejecurta el bloque de adentro, si la condicion Booleana que reporta es **verdadera**.  \n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/if_then/if_then.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl bloque 'C' **Si entonces** solo verificará la condición booleana una vez. \n\nSi la condición booleana se informa como **verdadera**, el bloque de adentro **Si entonces** se ejecutara.\n\nSi la condición booleana se informa como **falsa**, el bloque de adentro **Si entonces** será saltado.\n\nEl 'C' block **Si entonces** puede aceptar bloques con forma hexagonal (seis lados) como su condición.\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/if_then/if_then_2.png)\n\n"},"if_then_else":{"name":"if_then_else","markdown":"# Si entonces más\n\nUn bloque 'C' Block que ejecuta los bloques dentro de la primera o segunda parte de **Si entonces más**, basado en el valor booleano informado.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/if_then_else/if_then_else.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl 'C' bloque **Si entonces más** solo verificará la condición booleana una vez.\n\nSi la condición booleana se reporta como **verdadero**, el bloque dentro de la seccion **si** sera ejecutado.\n\nSi la condición booleana es **falsa**, el bloque dentro de la seccion **mas** sera ejecutado.\n\nEl 'C' bloque **Si entonces más** puede aceptar bloques con forma hexagonal (seis lados) como su condición.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/if_then_else/if_then_else_2.png)"},"item_of_2d_array":{"name":"item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Elemento de la lista 2D\n\nInforma el valor de un elemento en una lista 2D.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nElija qué lista 2D usar. La lista 2D también se puede renombrar o eliminar.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nIngrese la posición de la fila del elemento (primer número) y la columna (segundo número) dentro de la lista que se reportara. Aquí, se informa el elemento en la fila de posición 2, columna 3 en la lista 2D.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\nEl bloque **Elemento de la lista 2D** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)"},"item_of_array":{"name":"item_of_array","markdown":"# Artículo de\n\nInforma el valor de un elemento en una lista.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/item_of_array/item_of_block.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nElija qué lista usar. La lista también se puede renombrar o eliminar.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/item_of_array/item_of_options.png)\n\nIngrese el número de la posición del elemento dentro de la lista. Aquí, se reporta el segundo elemento de la lista (elemento 2).\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/item_of_array/item_of_block_2.png)\n\nEl bloque **Artículo de** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá la velocidad de la transmisión al valor almacenado en el elemento 3 de mi lista. El tren motriz ahora avanzará a una velocidad del 90%.\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/item_of_array/item_of_example2.png)\n\n"},"length_of_2d_array":{"name":"length_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Longitud de la lista 2D\n\nReporta el número de filas o columnas de una lista 2D.  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija qué lista 2D utilizar. La lista 2D también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_select.png)\n\nElija el valor que desea reportar. La lista 2D puede reportar **filas** o **columnas**.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_option.png)\n\nEl bloque de reportero **longitud de la lista 2D** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_insert.png)\n"},"less_than":{"name":"less_than","markdown":"# Menos que\n\nReporta si el primer valor es menos (más pequeño) que el segundo valor. \n\n![less_than](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/less_than/less_than.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Menos que** reporta **true** cuando el primer valor es menos que el segundo valor. \n\n**Menos que** reporta **false** cuando el primer valor es mayor o igual que el segundo valor.\n\nEl bloque booleano **menos que** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\nEl bloque **menos que** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![less_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/less_than/less_than_inserted.png)\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Low Battery!\" en la pantalla del VEX IQ Brain si el nivel de batería es inferior al 25%.\n\n![less_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/less_than/less_than_example.png)\n"},"move_pen":{"name":"move_pen","markdown":"# Establecer la posición de la pluma\n\nEstablece la posición del VEX VR Pen.\n\n![move_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/move_pen/move_pen.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la posición de la pluma VR\n\n![move_pen2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/move_pen/move_pen2.png)\n\n* arriba - el bolígrafo no dibujará una línea en el tablero.\n* abajo - el bolígrafo dibujará una línea coloreada en el tablero.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo dibujará una línea roja en el tablero.\n\n![move_pen3](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/move_pen/move_pen3.png)\n"},"multiply":{"name":"multiply","markdown":"# Multiplicar\n\nMultiplica los dos valores juntos e informa del producto.\n\n![multiply](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/multiply/multiply.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Multiplicar** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **multiply** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nMultiplicar dos números.\n\n![multiply_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/multiply/multiply_numbers.png)\n\nMultiplicar con valores de variables.\n\n![multiply_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/multiply/multiply_variables.png)\n\nMultiplique con los valores de los sensores o dispositivos.\n\n![multiply_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/multiply/multiply_sensors.png\n"},"next_row":{"name":"next_row","markdown":"# Coloque el cursor en la siguiente fila.\n\nEste bloque establece el cursor en una nueva línea en Print Console cuando se usan los bloques **print**. \n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/next_row/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nUse el bloque ** establecer cursor en la fila siguiente ** para establecer el cursor en una nueva línea en la pantalla.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Hello\" y en la siguiente línea en la pantalla imprimirá \"World!\"\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/next_row/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"not":{"name":"not","markdown":"# No\n\nReporta de lo contrario (inverso) del resultado booleano\n\n![not](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/not/not.png)\n\n## Cรณmo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **no** reporta **true** cuando el bloque booleano adentro reporta **false**.\n\nEl bloque **no** reporta **false** cuando el bloque booleano adentro reporta **true**.\n\nEl bloque booleano **no** reporta un valor **true** o **false** y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\nEste ejemplo reportara **true** cuando el botรณn de \"comprobar\" del VEX IQ Brain no se presiona. \n\n![not_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/not/not_sensors.png)\n\n![not_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/not/not_truth.png))  \n\n"},"object_in_front":{"name":"object_in_front","markdown":"# Objeto encontrado\n\nReporta si el sensor del Buscador de rango ultrasónico ve un objeto dentro de su campo de visión.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/object_in_front/object_in_front_block.png)\n\n## Como utilizar\n\n**Objeto encontrado** reporta **verdadero** cuando el sensor del Buscador de rango ultrasónico ve un objeto o superficie dentro de su campo de visión.\n\n**Objeto encontrado** reporta **falso** cuando el sensor del Buscador de rango ultrasónico no detecta un objeto o superficie.\n\nElija qué sensor del Buscador de rango ultrasónico va usar.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/object_in_front/object_in_front_distance.png)\n\nEl bloque Booleano **Objeto encontrado** reporta un valor **verdadero** o **falso**  y se usa en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis lados).\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/object_in_front/object_in_front_inserted.png)\n "},"optical_brightness":{"name":"optical_brightness","markdown":"# Brillo de color\n\nInforma del brillo de un objeto desde uno de los sensores ocular de color.\n\n![color_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_brightness/color_brightness.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Brillo de color** informa del brillo de un objeto. Los objetos blancos reportarán un brillo del 100%, los objetos negros notificarán un brillo del 0%. Todos los demás colores informarán de un brillo en algún lugar entre esos valores.\n\nElija qué Sensores Oculares de Color usar. El OjoDelantero está montado delante del robot y mira hacia adelante, mientras que el OjoAbajo está montado debajo del robot y mira hacia abajo.\n\n![color_brightness_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_brightness/color_brightness_eyes.png)\n\nEl bloque numerico **brillo de color** informa de un valor entre 0 y 100 valor y se utiliza en bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![color_brightness_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_brightness/color_brightness_insert.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo debe llevarse a cabo en el Playgorund de **Laberinto de Disco**. El robot avanzará hasta que el OjoAbajo reporte que el brillo de un objeto debajo de él es inferior al 100%. Luego girará a la izquierda y conducirá hacia adelante de nuevo.\n\n![color_brightness_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_brightness/color_brightness_example.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **brillo de color**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque reporter.\n\n![color_brightness_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_brightness/color_brightness_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![color_brightness_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_brightness/color_brightness_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_color":{"name":"optical_color","markdown":"# Detección de Color\n\nReporta si uno de los Color Sensor Eyes (Sensores Oculares de Color) detecta un color específico.\n\n![color_sensing_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_color/color_sensing_color.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Detección de Color** informa **true** cuando el Color Sensor Eye (Sensores Oculares de Color) detecta el color seleccionado.\n\n**Detección de Color** informa **false** cuando el Color Sensor Eye detecta un color diferente al seleccionado.\n\nElija qué Color Sensor Eye (Sensores Oculares de Color) usar. El OjoDelantero está montado delante del robot y mira hacia adelante, mientras que el OjoAbajo está montado debajo del robot y mira hacia abajo.\n\n![color_sensing_color_eye](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_color/color_sensing_color_eye.png)\n\nElija el color que desea detectar.\n\n![color_sensing_color_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_color/color_sensing_color_color.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano de **detección de color** informa de un valor **true** o **false** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis caras).\n\n![color_sensing_color_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_color/color_sensing_color_shape.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo debe llevarse a cabo en el área de juegos de **Laberinto de Discos**. Conducirá hacia adelante con el lápiz hacia abajo hasta que detecte un objeto con un color, y luego cambiará el color del lápiz y se dará la vuelta.\n\n![color_sensing_color_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_color/color_sensing_color_example.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque de **detección de color**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reportero.\n\n![color_sensing_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_color/color_sensing_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![color_sensing_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_color/color_sensing_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_near_object":{"name":"optical_near_object","markdown":"# Sensor Ocular cerca del objeto\n\nInforma si el Color Sensor Eye (Sensor Ocular de Color) está lo suficientemente cerca de un objeto para detectar un color.\n\n![color_near_object](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_near_object/color_near_object.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Color near object** reports **true** when the Color Sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.\n\n**Sensor Ocular cerca del objeto** reporta **falso** cuando el Color Sensor (Sensor de Color) no está lo suficientemente cerca de un objeto con colores detectables.\n\nElija qué Sensor Ocular de Color usar. El OjoDelantero está montado delante del robot y mira hacia adelante, mientras que el OjoAbajo está montado debajo del robot y mira hacia abajo.\n\n![color_near_object_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_near_object/color_near_object_eyes.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano **objeto cercano** informa de un valor **true** o false** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis lados).\n\n![color_near_object_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_near_object/color_near_object_shape.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo debe llevarse a cabo en el PLayground **Liberinto de Disco**. Se conduce hacia adelante hasta que el Color Sensor (Sensor de Color) OjoDelantero informa que está cerca de un objeto con color detectable, y luego se conduce hacia atrás.\n\n![color_near_object_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_near_object/color_near_object_example.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **color cerca del objeto**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reportero.\n\n![color_near_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_near_object/color_near_object_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![color_near_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/optical_near_object/color_near_object_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"or":{"name":"or","markdown":"# O\n\nReporta si una de las dos condiciones booleanas son verdaderas.\n\n![or](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/or/or.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **o** reporta **true** cuando una (o ambas) de las condiciones son **true**. \n\nEl bloque **o** reporta **false** cuando ambas condiciones son **false**.\n\nEl bloque booleano **o** reporta **true** o **false** valor y cabe en bloques con espacios hexagonales (de seis lados).\n\n![or_sensors2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/or/or_sensors2.png)\n\nEl bloque **o** reporta **true** cuando (o ambos) se presionan el VEX IQ Bumbper y el VEX IQ Touch LED y reporta **false** sólo cuando el parachoques y el LED táctil no se presionan.\n\n##Ejemplo\n\nEl bloque **o** reporta **true** cuando el **sensor de distancia** detecta un objete, y/o el **OjoFrontal** detecta un objeto. \n\nEl bloque **or** reporta **false** solo cuando el **sensor de distancia** no detecta un objete y el **OjoFrontal** no detecta un objeto.\n\n![or_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/or/or_truth.png)\n"},"position":{"name":"position","markdown":"# Posición del Robot\n\nReporta de la posición de las coordenadas X e Y del robot.\n\n![position_of_robot_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/position/position_of_robot_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Posición del Robot** informa de la posición de coordenadas X o Y del robot en números enteros como milímetros (mm) o pulgadas (pulgadas).\n\nElija si desea devolver la coordenada X o la coordenada Y.\n\n![position_of_robot_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/position/position_of_robot_coord.png)\n\nElija si el valor se notifica en mm o pulgadas.\n\n![position_of_robot_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/position/position_of_robot_unit.png)\n\nEl bloque de reportero **posición del robot** se utiliza en bloques con espacios circulares. \n\n![position_of_robot_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/position/position_of_robot_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **posición del robot**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reportero.\n\n![position_of_robot_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/position/position_of_robot_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![position_of_robot_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/position/position_of_robot_monitor.png)\n\n"},"position_angle":{"name":"position_angle","markdown":"# Ángulo de posición en grados\n\nInforma el ángulo del robot en grados.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_block.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\n**Ángulo de posición en grados** informa el ángulo actual del robot.\n\nEl bloque reportero **ángulo de posición en grados** se usa en bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_inserted.png)\n"},"pressing_bumper":{"name":"pressing_bumper","markdown":"# Presionando el Parachoques\n\nInforma si el Bumper Switch (Interruptor del Parachoques) está pulsado.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Presionando el Parachoques** informa **true** si se está presionando el interruptor de parachoques.\n\n**Presionando el Parachoques** Informa **falso** si no está presionando el interruptor del parachoques.\n\nElija el Interruptor del Parachoques que desea utilizar.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano **presionando el parachoques** informa de un valor **true** o **false** y se utiliza en bloques con espacios hexagonales (seis caras).\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitoreo de Valor\n\nPara supervisar el valor del bloque **presionando el parachoques**, seleccione la casilla de verificación situada junto al bloque de reportero.\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nA continuación, abra la Consola de Monitor para ver los bloques de reporteros seleccionados para la supervisión.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)\n\n"},"print":{"name":"print","markdown":"# Impresión\n\nImprime valores o texto en Print Console.\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/print/look_print.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nEl bloque **imprimir** imprimirá datos dentro de la pestaña Ventana del monitor\n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/print/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nTodos los bloques de reportero se imprimirán como números enteros (enteros) de forma predeterminada. Use el bloque **establecer precisión de impresión** para ajustar el número de decimales impresos.\n\nImprime palabras y números.\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/print/print_words.png)\n\nImprima el valor informado de una variable.\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/print/print_variable1.png)  \n\nImprimir el cálculo reportado de un operador.\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/print/print_addition.png)  \n\nImprima el valor informado desde un sensor o dispositivo.\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/print/print_sensor.png)\n\n\n\n\n"},"procedures_call":{"name":"procedures_call","markdown":"# Mis bloques\n\nMis bloques se utilizan para crear bloques personalizados.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_call/my_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Mis bloques** se utilizan para crear una secuencia de bloques que se pueden utilizar varias veces a lo largo de un proyecto. \n\n**Mis bloques** se hacen utilizando el botón **Crear un bloque**.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_call/make_a_block.png)\n\n**Mis bloques** puede tener múltiples parámetros - etiquetas de texto, variables numéricas y variables booleanas - para agregar más funcionalidad a su bloque personalizado.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_call/make_variable.png)  \n\nPara utilizar un **mi bloque**, adjuntar la llamada **mi bloque** a un bloque de tapa. Cuando un  **mi bloque** se alcanza en su proyecto, la pila dentro de **mi bloque** se ejecutará utilizando los valores de parámetro especificados.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_call/trigger_function.png)\n\nAdjunte una pila de bloques a la **Mis bloques** \"define\" un bloque tapa para establecer las acciones que se producen cuando un **mi bloque** se utiliza.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_call/attach_function.png)\n\n***Nota: La creación de varios Mis Bloques con las mismas etiquetas y variables puede provocar errores al ejecutar el proyecto en los Playgrounds.***\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este ejemplo, el robot utilizará **Mis bloques** para:\n\n* Conduzca hacia delante 300 mm\n* Dibuja un cuadrado de 300 mm\n* Gire a la derecha 15 grados\n* Establezca el color del lápiz en rojo\n* Dibuja un cuadrado de 400mm\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_call/square_example.png)\n"},"procedures_definition":{"name":"procedures_definition","markdown":"# Mis bloques\n\nMis bloques se utilizan para crear bloques personalizados.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_definition/my_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Mis bloques** se utilizan para crear una secuencia de bloques que se pueden utilizar varias veces a lo largo de un proyecto. \n\n**Mis bloques** se hacen utilizando el botón **Crear un bloque**.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_definition/make_a_block.png)\n\n**Mis bloques** puede tener múltiples parámetros - etiquetas de texto, variables numéricas y variables booleanas - para agregar más funcionalidad a su bloque personalizado.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_definition/make_variable.png)  \n\nPara utilizar un **mi bloque**, adjuntar la llamada **mi bloque** a un bloque de tapa. Cuando un  **mi bloque** se alcanza en su proyecto, la pila dentro de **mi bloque** se ejecutará utilizando los valores de parámetro especificados.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_definition/trigger_function.png)\n\nAdjunte una pila de bloques a la **Mis bloques** \"define\" un bloque tapa para establecer las acciones que se producen cuando un **mi bloque** se utiliza.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_definition/attach_function.png)\n\n***Nota: La creación de varios Mis Bloques con las mismas etiquetas y variables puede provocar errores al ejecutar el proyecto en los Playgrounds.***\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este ejemplo, el robot utilizará **Mis bloques** para:\n\n* Conduzca hacia delante 300 mm\n* Dibuja un cuadrado de 300 mm\n* Gire a la derecha 15 grados\n* Establezca el color del lápiz en rojo\n* Dibuja un cuadrado de 400mm\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/procedures_definition/square_example.png)\n"},"random":{"name":"random","markdown":"# Al azar\n![random](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/random/random.png)\n\nInforma de un valor aleatorio entre los valores más bajos y más altos del bloque.\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nLa longitud del bloque **al azar** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nEl bloque **al azar** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEste ejemplo utiliza un bloque **al azar** para aleatorizar la velocidad del chasis.\n\n![random_drivetrain](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/random/random_drivetrain.png)\n\nEste ejemplo utiliza un bloque **al azar** para aleatorizar la longitud de un bloque de espera.\n\n![random_control](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/random/random_control.png)\n\n"},"remainder":{"name":"remainder","markdown":"# El resto\n\nDivide el primer valor por el segundo valor y reporta el resto.\n\n![remainder](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/remainder/remainder.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **el resto** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLos números que se dividen uniformemente reportarán un resto de cero.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **el resto** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares.\n\nEste ejemplo dividirá 7 y 2 e informará el resto para imprimir \"1\" en la pantalla de VEX IQ Brain.\n\n![remainder_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/remainder/remainder_numbers.png)"},"repeat":{"name":"repeat","markdown":"# Repetir\n\nUn bloque 'C' que repite los bloques contenidos en el interior durante un número determinado de veces.\n\n![repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/repeat/repeat.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPrimero introduzca un valor para indicar cuántas veces se va a utilizar la pila **repetir** los bloques contenidos en el interior.\n \nEl bloque 'C' **repetir** puede aceptar enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLos bloques 'C' también se pueden poner dentro unos de otros. Este concepto es llamado anidando que puede ayudar a ahorrar el tiempo programando comportamientos diferentes.\n\n![repeat_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/repeat/repeat_select.png)\n\nEste ejemplo repetirá la reproducción de un efecto de sonido cada 3 segundos durante 5 veces.\n\n![repeat_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/repeat/repeat_example.png)"},"repeat_until":{"name":"repeat_until","markdown":"# Repita hasta que\n\nUn bloque 'C' que repite los bloques dentro hasta que se notifica que la condición booleana es **true**.  \n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/repeat_until/repeat_until.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **Repita hasta que** sólo comprobará la condición booleana al principio de cada bucle. \n\nSi la condición booleana se reporta como **false**, los bloques dentro de **repita hasta que** se ejecutará.\n\nSi la condición booleana se reporta como **true**, los bloques dentro de **repita hasta que** se omitirá.\n\nEl bloque 'C' **repita hasta que** puede aceptar bloques hexagonales (de seis lados) como su condición.\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/repeat_until/repeat_until_2.png)\n\n\n"},"reset_timer":{"name":"reset_timer","markdown":"# Restablecer temporizador\n\nRestablece el temporizador del VEX IQ Brain.\n\n![resetTimer](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/reset_timer/resetTimer.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl temporizador del VEX IQ Brain comienza al principio de cada proyecto. El bloque **restablecer temporizador** se utiliza para restablecer el temporizador a 0 segundos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este ejemplo, el temporizador se restablecerá antes de mover el chasis hacia delante durante 3 segundos. Después de 3 segundos, el chasis se detendrá.\n\n![resetTimer_exampleRevised](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/reset_timer/resetTimer_exampleRevised.png)"},"round":{"name":"round","markdown":"# Redondear\n\nRedondea el valor introducido al entero más cercano.\n\n![round](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/round/round.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nDecimales que son 0.5 o mayores **redondean** hacia arriba y decimales menores de 0.5 **redondean** hacia abajo.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **redondear** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nEl bloque **redondear** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![round_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/round/round_numbers.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo redondeará 2.7 e imprimirá \"3\".\n\n![round_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/round/round_variables.png)\n\n\n\n"},"set_2d_array_to":{"name":"set_2d_array_to","markdown":"# Establezca la lista 2D a\n\nEstablece cada elemento de la lista 2D en los valores introducidos.\n\n![set_2d_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nAl crear una lista 2D, puede establecer el número de las **columnas** de la lista entre 1 y 10 elementos y el número de las **filas** de la lista entre 1 y 10 elementos.\n\nElija la lista 2D que desea utilizar. La lista 2D también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **establezca la lista 2D a** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_inserted.png)\n\n\n"},"set_array_to":{"name":"set_array_to","markdown":"# Establecer lista a\n\nEstablece cada valor de una lista en los valores introducidos.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_array_to/set_list_to.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nLa longitud de la lista (1 a 10 elementos) se establece cuando se crea la lista.\n\nElija la lista que desea utilizar. La lista también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_array_to/set_list_to_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **establecer lista a** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_array_to/set_list_to_2.png)\n\nEn este ejemplo, se establecerá la lista en valores iniciales y, a continuación, se informará del elemento #3 (90) de la lista para establecer la posición del brazo. \n\n![set_list_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_array_to/set_list_to_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_boolean_variable":{"name":"set_boolean_variable","markdown":"# Establecer variable booleana\n\nEstablece una variable booleana en un valor dado.\n\n![set_boolean_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la variable booleana que desea utilizar. Las variables booleanas también se pueden cambiar de nombre o eliminar. \n\n![set_boolean_block_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_choice.png)\n\nElija un valor booleano.\n\n![set_boolean_block_value](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_value.png)\n\nEl bloque booleano **set boolean variable** puede aceptar bloques hexagonal (de seis lados).\n\n![set_boolean_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_inserted.png)"},"set_bumper":{"name":"set_bumper","markdown":"# Establecer el color del parachoques LED\n\nEstablece el color del VEX VR LED Bumper.\n\n![set_bumper_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_bumper/set_bumper_color.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija el color que se mostrará.  \n\n![set_bumper_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_bumper/set_bumper_color2.png)\n\nPara apagar el **parachoques LED**, ajustar el color a apagado.\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el **parachoques LED** a verde.\n\n![set_bumper_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_bumper/set_bumper_example.png)"},"set_drive_heading":{"name":"set_drive_heading","markdown":"# Establecer dirección del chasis\n\nEstablece el valor de rumbo del giroscopio de la transmisión.\n\n![setDriveHeading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_drive_heading/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **establecer dirección del chasis** se puede utilizar para ajustar la posición del chasis a cualquier rumbo en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj, como se muestra en la imagen de abajo.\n\n![TopDownBot01](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_drive_heading/TopDownBot01.jpg)\n\n**Establecer dirección del chasis** acepta de un rango de **0 a 360** grados.\n\nEl bloque **establecer dirección del chasis** puede aceptar enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n\n"},"set_drive_rotation":{"name":"set_drive_rotation","markdown":"# Establecer la rotación del chasis\n\nEstablece el ángulo de rotación del chasis.\n\n![drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_drive_rotation/drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **establecer la rotación del chasis** bloque se puede utilizar para establecer el ángulo de rotación del chasis a cualquier valor positivo o negativo dado.\n\nEl bloque **establecer la rotación del chasis** bloque puede aceptar enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará girar el robot un total de 210 grados:\n\n![turntorotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_drive_rotation/turntorotation_example.png)\n\n- Da veulta a la derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) para girar 120 grados.\n- Establezca la posición actual del robot como rotación de -45 grados.\n- Da veulta a la derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) 90 grados adicionales (-45 grados a 45 grados) en función del valor de rotación establecido del bloque anterior."},"set_drive_velocity":{"name":"set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Establecer velocidad de Conducción\n\nEstablece la velocidad de Conducción.\n\n![set_drive_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Establecer velocidad de Conducción** aceptando un rango de **-100% a 100%**. Puede utilizar este bloque para obtener un movimiento mรกs preciso del robot. La velocidad predeterminada es **50%**\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo establecerรก la conducciรณn para que gire a una velocidad del 10% antes de conducir.\n\n![set_drive_velocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity_example.png)\n"},"set_item_of_2d_array":{"name":"set_item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Reemplazar elemento de la lista 2D\n\nActualiza un elemento de la lista 2D a un nuevo valor.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nIntroduzca la fila del elemento (primer número) y la posición de la columna (segundo número) dentro de la lista que se va a reemplazar.\n\nElija la lista que desea utilizar. La lista también se puede cambiar de nombre o eliminar.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **reemplazar elemento** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block_2.png)\n"},"set_item_of_array":{"name":"set_item_of_array","markdown":"# Reemplazar elemento\n\nActualiza un elemento de la lista a un nuevo valor.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la lista que desea utilizar. La lista también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_item_of_array/replace_item_options.png)\n\nEl bloque **reemplazar elemento** bloque puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block_2.png)\n"},"set_magnet_state":{"name":"set_magnet_state","markdown":"# Energizar electroimán\n\nEstablece el VEX VR Robot Electromagnet en dos modos diferentes: **elevar** o **bajar**.\n\n![set_magnet_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nSeleccione esta opción para configurar ese electroimán para elevar o bajar.\n\n* **elevar** - atrae un objeto al electroimán\n* **bajar** - libera cualquier objeto que el electroimán está sosteniendo\n\n\n![set_magnet_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el robot para conducir 200 mm hacia adelante, **Electromagnet** eleva para recoger un objeto, conducir hacia adelante 200mm, a continuación, baja el objeto.\n\n![set_magnet_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_example.png)\n"},"set_pen_color":{"name":"set_pen_color","markdown":"# Establecer el color de la pluma\n\nEstablece el color del VEX VR Pen.\n\n![set_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_pen_color/set_pen_color.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija el color de la línea que se dibujará.  \n\n![set_pen_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_pen_color/set_pen_color2.png)\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el color de la pluma en azul.\n\n![set_pen_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_pen_color/set_pen_color_example.png)\n"},"set_print_color":{"name":"set_print_color","markdown":"# Establecer color de impresión\n\nEstablece el color del texto impreso en Print Console.\n\n![set_print_color](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_print_color/set_print_color.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nEl bloque **establecer color de impresión** se usa con el bloque **imprimir** para establecer el color del texto impreso en Print Console. El color se puede configurar en negro, rojo, verde o azul.\n\n![set_print_color_options](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_print_color/set_print_color_options.png)  \n\nLas letras, los números, los valores de los sensores y los valores variables se pueden imprimir en el conjunto de colores utilizando el bloque **establecer color de impresión**.\n\n![set_print_color_hello](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_print_color/set_print_color_hello.png)  \n\nCualquier bloque **print** utilizado después del **color de impresión establecido** se imprimirá en el color establecido más recientemente dentro del programa.\n\n![set_print_color_text](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_print_color/set_print_color_text.png) "},"set_print_precision":{"name":"set_print_precision","markdown":"# Establecer precisión de impresión\n\nEstablece el número de dígitos que aparecen después del punto decimal cuando se utilizan bloques de impresión para imprimir variables y valores en Print Console.\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_print_precision/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nElija el nivel de precisión que los bloques de reportero insertados se imprimirán en la pantalla.\n\n* Unos (1) (Defecto)\n* Décimas (0.1)\n* Centésimos (0.01)\n* Milésimas (0.001)\n* Todos los dígitos (0.000001)\n\nLos bloques informadores impresos en la pantalla se redondearán al nivel de precisión más cercano. Por ejemplo, 1.67 se imprimirá como 2 con precisión y 1.7 con precisión de décimas.\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_print_precision/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_turn_velocity":{"name":"set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Establecer la velocidad de dar vuelta\n\nEstablece la velocidad de las vueltas del chasis.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Establecer la velocidad de dar vuelta** sólo establecerá la velocidad del chasis, pero no hará que el chasis se mueva. Un bloque **vuelta** todavía se requiere.\n\n**Establecer la velocidad de dar vuelta** acepta de un rango de **-100% a 100%** o **-127rpm a 127rpm**.\n\nEstableciendo la velocidad de giro del chasis a un valor negativo hará que el chasis de vuelta en la dirección opuesta.\n\nEstableciendo la velocidad de giro de una transmisión en 0 hará que la transmisión se detenga.\n\nEl bloque **Establecer la velocidad de dar vuelta** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis gire a la izquierda lentamente a una velocidad del 25%.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_variable":{"name":"set_variable","markdown":"# Establecer variable\n\nEstablece una variable en un valor determinado.\n\n![set_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_variable/set_variable.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la variable que desea utilizar. Las variables también se pueden cambiar de nombre o eliminar. \n\n![set_variable_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_variable/set_variable_choose.png)\n\nEl bloque **establecer variable** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![set_variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/set_variable/set_variable_inserted.png)\n\n"},"size_of_array":{"name":"size_of_array","markdown":"# Longitud de la lista\n\nReporta el número de elementos de una lista.  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/size_of_array/size_of_array.png)\n\n# Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la lista que desea utilizar. La lista también puede ser renombrada o eliminada.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/size_of_array/size_of_array_select.png)\n\nEl bloque reportero **Longitud de la lista** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares.\n\n![size_of_array_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/size_of_array/size_of_array_insert.png)"},"stop_driving":{"name":"stop_driving","markdown":"# Dejar de conducir\n\nDetiene el chasis.\n\n![stopDriving_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/stop_driving/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **dejar de conducir** hará que el Drivetrain se detenga por completo.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo detendrá el chasis después de 3 segundos.\n\n![stopDriving_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/stop_driving/stopDriving_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_project":{"name":"stop_project","markdown":"# Detener proyecto\n\nDetiene un proyecto en ejecución.\n\n![stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/stop_project/stop_project.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **detener proyecto** hará que un proyecto en ejecución se detenga.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo conducirá el Robot VR en un cuadrado de 500 mm y luego detendrá el proyecto.\n\n![stop_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/stop_project/stop_project2.png)"},"subtract":{"name":"subtract","markdown":"# Restar\n\nResta un valor de otro y reporta la diferencia.\n\n![subtract](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/subtract/subtract.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Restar** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nLa longitud del bloque **restar** cabe en bloques que aceptan espacios circulares. \n\nRestar dos números.\n\n![subtract_example1](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/subtract/subtract_example1.png)\n\nRestar con valores de variables.\n\n![subtract_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/subtract/subtract_example2.png)  \n\nReste con los valores de los sensores o dispositivos\n\n![subtract_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/subtract/subtract_example3.png)"},"timer_value":{"name":"timer_value","markdown":"# Valor del temporizador\n\nReporta el valor del temporizador VEX IQ Brain en segundos.\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/timer_value/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl temporizador comienza a 0 segundos cuando el programa se inicia e informa de valores decimales.\n\nEl bloque de reportero **valor del temporizador** se utiliza dentro de bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/timer_value/timerValue_inserted.png)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo moverá el Drivetrain hacia adelante y reproducirá un efecto de sonido durante 4.5 segundos antes de realizar una vuelta de 90 grados.\n\n![timerValue_example_revised](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/timer_value/timerValue_example_revised.png)\n"},"turn":{"name":"turn","markdown":"# Dar vuelta\n\nGira el chasis por siempre.\n\n![turn_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn/turn_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta** girará el Drivetrain para siempre hasta que se utilice un nuevo bloque Drivetrain o se detenga el programa.\n\nElija la dirección en la que se moverá el chasis.\n\n![turn_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn/turn_direction.png)"},"turn_for":{"name":"turn_for","markdown":"# Dar vuelta por\n\nGira el chasis durante una distancia determinada.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_for/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija la dirección en la que girará el chasis.\n\n![turn_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_for/turn_for_direction.png)  \n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\nEstablezca la distancia que se moverá el tren de mando introduciendo un número de grados.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_for/turn_for_block_2.png)\n\nPor defecto, otros bloques van a **esperar** hasta que el chasis termine de girar.\n\nPuede seleccionar la flecha para expandir **y no espera** - esto hará que otros bloques continúen funcionando mientras el chasis gira.\n\n![turn_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_for/turn_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"turn_to_heading":{"name":"turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Dar vuelta a dirección de\n\nGira un Drivetrain a un dirección específico con el VR Gyro Sensor.\n\n![turn_to_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta a dirección de** se puede utilizar para girar el chasis a cualquier encabezado en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj como se muestra en la imagen de abajo.\n\n![TopDownBot01](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_to_heading/TopDownBot01.jpg)\n\nBasado en el rumbo actual del Gyro, **dar vuelta a dirección de** determinará la dirección a girar.\n\n**Dar vuelta a dirección de** acepta de un rango **0.00 a 359.99** grados.\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta a dirección de** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis haga cuatro vueltas: \n\n![turn_to_heading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\n- Izquierda a 45 grados\n- Izquierda a 90 grados\n- Izquierda a 270 grados\n- Derecha a 180 grados\n\nPor defecto, otros bloques van a **esperar** hasta que el chasis termine de girar.\n\nPuede seleccionar la flecha para expandir **y no espera** - esto hará que otros bloques continúen funcionando mientras el chasis gira.\n\n![turn_to_heading_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_anddontwait.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"turn_to_rotation":{"name":"turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Dar vuelta a rotación\n\nDa vuelta al chasis a un ángulo de rotación específico utilizando el sensor Gyro de entrada.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_iq.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta a rotación** se puede utilizar para girar el Chasis a un valor positivo (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) o negativo (en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj).\n\nSobre la base del valor **rotación** actual del chasis, **dar vuelta a rotación** determinará en qué dirección girar.\n\nEl bloque **dar vuelta a rotación** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis haga cuatro vueltas: \n\n![turn_to_rotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_to_rotation/turn_to_rotation_example.png)\n\n- Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) a 90 grados\n- Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) a 180 grados\n- Izquierda (en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj) a -45 grados\n- Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) a 0 grados\n\nPor defecto, otros bloques  **esperarán** hasta que el Chasis termine de girar.\n\nPuede seleccionar la flecha para expandir **y no espera** - esto hará que otros bloques continúen funcionando mientras el Chasis da vuelta.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq.png)"},"variable":{"name":"variable","markdown":"# Variable\n\nReporta del valor asignado a la variable.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/variable/variable.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nBloques **variable** se utilizan para informar de un valor almacenado dentro de la variable. Utilice el bloque **definir variable** para definir o actualizar el valor de una variable.\n\n![variable_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/variable/variable_cursor.png)\n\nEl bloque de reportero **variable** se utiliza en bloques con espacios circulares.\n\n![variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/variable/variable_inserted.png)\n\nLas variables se crean con el botón **hacer una variable**.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/variable/make_variable.png)  \n"},"wait":{"name":"wait","markdown":"# Espera\n\nEspera una cantidad específica de tiempo antes de pasar al siguiente bloque.\n\n![wait](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/wait/wait.png)\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nElija una cantidad de tiempo para **espera**.\n\nEl bloque **espera** puede aceptar decimales, enteros o bloques numéricos.\n\n![wait_select](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/wait/wait_select.png)\n\n\n"},"wait_until":{"name":"wait_until","markdown":"# Espera hasta que\n\nEspera a que un bloque booleano reporta **true** antes de pasar al siguiente bloque.\n\n![wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/wait_until/wait_until.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n**Espera hasta que** comprobará repetidamente un bloque de reportero booleano y no se moverá al siguiente bloque hasta que el bloque de informe booleano informe informe **true**.\n\nEl bloque booleano **espera hasta que** puede aceptar bloques con forma hexagonal (seis lados).\n\n![wait_until_2](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/wait_until/wait_until_2.png)\n\n"},"when_started":{"name":"when_started","markdown":"# Cuando empieza\n\nEjecuta la pila adjunta de bloques cuando se inicia el proyecto.\n\n![when_started](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/when_started/when_started.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl evento **cuando empieza** Se ejecutará cuando el programa se inicie desde el menú de VEX IQ Brain o desde el botón **correr** en bloques de VEXcode IQ.\n\nTodos los nuevos programas incluirán automáticamente un bloque **cuando empieza**. Los proyectos pueden soportar hasta 3 bloques **cuando empieza**.\n\n"},"while":{"name":"while","markdown":"# Mientras\n\nUn bloque 'C' que repite los bloques dentro mientras que la condición booleana se notifica que es **true**.\n\n![while](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/while/while.png)\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl bloque **mientras** sólo comprobará la condición booleana al principio de cada bucle. \n\nSi la condición booleana se notifica como **true**, los bloques dentro de **while** se ejecutará.\n\nSi la condición booleana se notifica como **false**, los bloques dentro de **while** se omitirá.\n\nEl bloque 'C' **mientras** puede aceptar bloques hexagonales (de seis lados) como su condición.\n\n![while_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/es-419/while/while_inserted.png)"}},"python":{"control_break":{"name":"control_break","markdown":"# Romper\n\nSale de un ciclo repetitivo inmediatamente.\n\n```don\nbreak\n```\n\n## Cómo utilizar\n\nCuando se agrega dentro de un bucle, el comando `break` saldrá del bucle en el que se está ejecutando actualmente.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo conducirá al robot y verificará si se ha presionado su `right_bumper`.\n\nSi el parachoques se **presiona**, el comando `break` saldrá del ciclo de mientras. El robot dejará de conducir hacia adelante.\n\n```don\nwhile(true):\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if(right_bumper.pressed()):\n        break\n\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"break"},"control_for":{"name":"control_for","markdown":"# Por\n\nUn tipo de bucle que repite el código contenido en su cuerpo para un número determinado de iteraciones.\n\n```\nfor value in range(10):\n    pass\n\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nPrimero, reemplace `value` con una variable que **iterará** a través de la función `range ()`.\n\nSegundo, reemplace `10` con un valor para especificar el final de la iteración.\n\nAgregue un espacio de cuatro espacios usando la **barra espaciadora** para incluir declaraciones como parte de la declaración de control `for`.\n\nUna declaración `for` no debe contener un bloque de código vacío. Si es necesario, agregue una declaración `pass` a una declaración` for` previamente vacía.\n\nTenga en cuenta que los bucles for comienzan en el índice **0** en lugar de **1**.\n\nLos bucles **Por** también se pueden anidar uno dentro del otro.\n\n## Ejemplo \n\nEl siguiente bucle imprimirá los números del 0 al 2:\n\n```\nfor i in range(3):\n   brain.print(i)\n   brain.new_line()\n```\nEsto imprimirá:\n```\n0\n1\n2\n```","signature":"for value in range (10"},"control_if":{"name":"control_if","markdown":"# Si\n\nSe utiliza una declaración de control para decidir si se ejecutará un bloque de código en función de lo proporcionado `condition`.\n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\n    \n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nReemplace la palabra `condition` con una expresión que puede evaluar como 'True' o 'False'. Una evaluación a `True` permitirá que se ejecuten las declaraciones con sangría debajo de la declaración `if`.\n\n\nAgregue una espacio de cuatro espacios usando **la barra espaciadora** para incluir declaraciones como parte de la declaración de control `if`.\n\nUna declaracion `if` no debe contener un bloque de código vacío. Si es necesario, agregue una declaración `pass` a una declaración` if` previamente vacía.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEsta instrucción `if` se ejecutará si `my_variable` es igual a 1, haciendo que el robot avance.\n\n```don\nmyVariable = 1\n\nif (my_variable == 1):\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"if condition:\\n\\tpass"},"control_if_else":{"name":"control_if_else","markdown":"# Si / si no\nUna declaración de control de flujo que ejecuta un bloque de comandos si una condición se evalúa como verdadera, u otro bloque de comandos si una condición se evalúa como falsa.\n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\nelse:\n    pass\n\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nLa instrucción `if` es condicional y verificará si su condición se evalúa como **Verdadero** o **Falso**. Es posible combinar muchas condiciones con el uso de operadores lógicos como `and`,`or`, y `not`. \n\nSi la totalidad de la condición es **Verdadero**, se ejecutará el código de bloqueo debajo de `if condition:`. Si la totalidad de la condición es **Falsa**, el código de bloqueo debajo de la instrucción `else:` se ejecutará en su lugar.\n\nLos bloques de código debajo de `if` y` else` deben tener margen de cuatro espacios.\n\nUna instrucción `if / else` no debe contener bloques de código vacíos. Si es necesario, agregue las declaraciones `pass` a una declaración `if / else` previamente vacía.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste código `if / else` establecerá el color del lápiz en NEGRO si la variable es igual a 1. Si no lo hace, establecerá el color del lápiz en VERDE.\n\n```don\nif (myVariable == 1):\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\nelse:\n    pen.set_pen_color(GREEN)\n```","signature":"if conditio"},"control_stop_project":{"name":"control_stop_project","markdown":"# Detener Proyecto\n\nDetiene un proyecto en ejecución.\n\n```don\nstop_project()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEste comando hará que se detenga un proyecto en ejecución.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste proyecto dibuja un cuadrado negro de 500 milímetros en el Playground.  El comando stop_project detiene el proyecto una vez completado el cuadrado.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 500,MM)\n\tdrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\nstop_project()\n```","signature":"stop_project()"},"control_wait":{"name":"control_wait","markdown":"# Esperar\n\nEspere una cantidad de tiempo específica antes de pasar al siguiente comando.\n\n```don\nwait(TIME, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEstablezca una cantidad de tiempo en `UNITS` para que su programa **espere** antes de ejecutar comandos adicionales. `UNITS` puede ser `SECONDS` o` MSEC` (milisegundos).\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo detendrá el tren motriz después de que un robot haya sido impulsado hacia adelante durante 3 segundos.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"wait(1, SECONDS)"},"control_wait_until":{"name":"control_wait_until","markdown":"# Mientras no\nUna declaración de control que continúa en bucle hasta que la expresión se vuelve **verdadera**.\n\n```don\nwhile not condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nLa instrucción `while not` verificará repetidamente la condición y no se moverá al siguiente comando hasta que la condición informe **verdadero**\n\nSi la condición se evalúa como **falso**, se ejecutará el código dentro de `while not`.\n\nSi la condición se evalúa como **verdadera**, se omitirá el código dentro de 'while not' y se ejecutará el siguiente comando después de la instrucción 'while not'.\n\nLa instrucción `while not` acepta cualquier expresión que se evalúe como `True` o `False` como su condición. Puede colocar booleanos y expresiones utilizando operadores lógicos y de comparación.\n\nTambién puede usar valores numéricos; **0** se evalúa como `False`, y cualquier **número distinto de cero** se evalúa como`True`.\n\nAgregue un margen de cuatro espacios usando la **barra espaciadora** para incluir declaraciones como parte de la instrucción de control `while not`.\n\nUna instrucción `while not` no debe contener un bloque de código vacío. Si es necesario, agregue una declaración `pass` a una declaración anteriormente vacía` while not`.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste bucle `while not` imprimirá \"Hello\" en líneas separadas hasta que el valor de my_variable sea igual a 5.\n\nmyVariable = 0\n\n```\nwhile not my_variable == 5:\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n    brain.new_line()\n    my_variable += 1\n```","signature":"while not expressio"},"control_while":{"name":"control_while","markdown":"# Mientras\nUn bucle que repite el código en siempre que la expresión de condición se evalúe como **verdadero**.\n\n```don\nwhile condition:\n   pass\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl ciclo `while` solo verificará la condición dentro del paréntesis al comienzo de cada ciclo.\n\nSi la condición se evalúa como **verdadera**, se ejecutará el código dentro de `while`.\n\nSi la condición se evalúa como **falsa**, se omitirá el código dentro de `while`.\n\nEl bucle `while` acepta cualquier cosa que se evalúe como`True` o `False` como condición. Puede colocar booleanos y expresiones utilizando operadores lógicos y de comparación.\n\nTambién puede usar valores numéricos; **0** se evalúa como `False`, y cualquier **número distinto de cero** se evalúa como`True`.\n\nAgregue un margen de cuatro espacios usando la **barra espaciadora** para incluir declaraciones como parte de la instrucción de control `while`.\n\nUna instrucción `while` no debe contener un bloque de código vacío. Si es necesario, agregue una declaración `pass` a una declaración `while` previamente vacía.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste bucle `while` continuará imprimiendo la palabra \"Hello\"en líneas separadas cuando el valor del temporizador sea inferior a 50 segundos.\n\n```\nwhile(brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 50.0):\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n    brain.new_line()\n```","signature":"while expressio"},"drivetrain_drive":{"name":"drivetrain_drive","markdown":"# Conducir\n\nMueve el tren motriz para siempre en la dirección especificada dentro de los paréntesis. Todos los motores del tren motriz conducen hacia adelante o hacia atrás a la velocidad establecida usando el comando `drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()`. La velocidad predeterminada es 50%. Los valores negativos harán que el tren motriz avance para enfrente con una entrada REVERSA y en reversa con una entrada ADELANTE.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.drive()` activara el tren motriz  para siempre, hasta que se use un nuevo comando el tren motriz o se detenga el programa.\n\nUse el argumento `FORWARD` en letras mayúsculas para mover el Drivetrain hacia adelante.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nUse el argumento `REVERSE` en letras mayúsculas para mover el Drivetrain en reversa.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(REVERSE)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.drive(FOWARD)"},"drivetrain_drive_for":{"name":"drivetrain_drive_for","markdown":"# Conducir por\n\nMueve el tren motriz por una distancia dada. Todos los motores del tren motriz conducen hacia adelante o hacia atrás a la velocidad establecida por el comando `drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()`. Después de que el tren motriz haya movido la distancia de entrada, los motores se detendrán.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(DIRECTION, distance, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEstablezca la distancia que recorrerá el tren motriz proporcionando tres argumentos.\n`drivetrain.drive_for(direction, distance, units)`\n\n- dirección - ya sea `FORWARD` o `REVERSE`\n- distancia - valor numerico\n- unidad - ya sea `MM` o `INCHES`\n\nEl `drivetrain.drive_for()` acepta valores numéricos para `DISTANCE`. Los valores negativos harán que el robot VR funcione en el lado opuesto de la entrada 'DIRECTION'.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 5.0, INCHES)\n```\n\nEl comando 'Drive for' es por defecto un comando de bloqueo; no se ejecutará ningún otro comando hasta que el tren de transmisión haya completado su movimiento.\n\n## Parámetro opcional\n\nSe puede proporcionar un cuarto parámetro opcional para hacer que el comando 'Drive For' sea un comando sin bloqueo:`wait=False`\n\n## Ejemplo\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 50, MM, wait=False)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)"},"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Establecer velocidad de conducción\n\nEstablece la velocidad del Drivetrain para los comandos de conducir y conducir por. La velocidad predeterminada es 50 por ciento.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\n`drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()` establecerá la velocidad del tren motriz cuando se use con un comando `drive()` o `drive_for()`. No hará que el tren motriz se mueva cuando se use solo.\n\n`drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()` acepta dos argumentos, un valor numérico de **- 100 a 100**, y el argumento de unidad 'PORCENTAJE' escrito en mayúsculas.\n\nEstablecer la velocidad de manejo de un tren motriz en un valor negativo hará que el tren motriz conduzca opuesto a la 'DIRECTION' colocada entre paréntesis del comando `drive()` o `drive_for()`.\n\nEstablecer la velocidad de conducción de un tren motriz a 0 hará que el tren motriz no se mueva, incluso cuando se usa un comando `drive()` o `drive_for()`","signature":"drivetrain.set_drive_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_set_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_set_heading","markdown":"# Establecer direccion\n\nEstablece la direccion exacta del giroscopio en tren motriz.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_heading(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.set_heading()` se puede usar para establecer la posición del tren motriz en cualquier encabezado dado. Este comando se puede usar para restablecer la orientación del giroscopio en el tren motriz cuando la direccion se establece en un valor de 0.\n\n`drivetrain.set_heading()` acepta un valor numérico entre  **0 y 360** como es el primer argumento, y `DEGREES` escrito en mayúsculas como su segundo argumento.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_heading(0, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.set_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_set_rotation","markdown":"# Establecer rotación\n\nEstablece el ángulo de rotación del giroscopio en el tren motriz.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Como utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.set_rotation()` se puede usar para establecer el ángulo de rotación del Drivetrain en cualquier valor positivo o negativo dado.\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.set_rotation()` puede aceptar valores enteros o decimales.\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.set_rotation()` **no** tiene un límite de 0-359 grados. El robot girará tantos grados como se establezca el primer argumento, los números positivos girarán el robot en sentido horario y los números negativos lo girarán en sentido antihorario. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que gire el robot en un total de 210 grados:\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(120, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(-45, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(45, DEGREES)\n```\n\n- Turn right (clockwise) to rotation 120 degrees.\n- Set the robot's current position as rotation of -45 degrees.\n- Turn right (clockwise) an additional 90 degrees (-45 degrees to +45 degrees) based on the set rotation value from the previous command.","signature":"drivetrain.set_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Establecer la Velocidad de Dar Vuelta\n\nEstablece la velocidad de veuletas del chasis.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\n`drivetrain.set_turn_velocity()` establecerá la velocidad del chasis al girar, pero no hará que el chasis se mueva cuando se usa sin un comando de 'turn' o 'turn_for'.\n\n`drivetrain.set_turn_velocity` acepta un rango de **-100 a 100** como primer argumento. Toma las unidades, `PERCENT`, escrito en letras mayúsculas como el segundo argumento.\n\nEstablecer la velocidad de vuelta del chasis en un valor negativo hará que el chasis gire en el opuesto de la dirección pasada en los siguientes comandos de giro.\n\nEstablecer la velocidad de giro del chasis en 0 evitará que el chasis gire, incluso cuando se utiliza un comando 'turn' o 'turn_for'.","signature":"drivetrain.set_turn_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_stop":{"name":"drivetrain_stop","markdown":"# Parar\n\nPara el chasis.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando 'drivetrain.stop()' detiene el chasis. No toma ningún argumento entre paréntesis. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo detendrá el chasis después de que el Robot haya conducido hacia adelante durante 3 segundos.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"drivetrain.stop()"},"drivetrain_turn":{"name":"drivetrain_turn","markdown":"# Dar Vuelta\n\nHara que el chasis de vuelta a la derecha o a la izquierda.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando \"drivetrain.turn\" girará el Drivetrain en la dirección dada por siempre hasta que se utilice un nuevo comando drivetrain, o hasta que se detenga el programa.\n\nUtilice el argumento 'LEFT' en letras mayúsculas para girar el chasis a la izquierda.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(LEFT)\n```\n\nUtilice el argumento 'RIGHT' en letras mayúsculas para girar el chasis a la derecha.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)"},"drivetrain_turn_for":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_for","markdown":"# Dar Vuelta Por\n\nGira el chasis durante un número especificado de grados.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(DIRECTION, ANGLE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEstablezca el número de grados que girará el chasis proporcionando tres argumentos.\n\n- direction (dirección) - RIGHT o LEFT (DERECHA O IZQUIERDA)\n- angle (ángulo) - un valor numérico\n- units (unidades) - DREGREES (grados)\n\nEl drivetrain.turn_for() acepta valores numéricos para el ángulo. Los valores negativos harán que el robot VR gire en lo contrario de la entrada por DIRECTION.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_for(LEFT, 90, DEGREES)`\n\nEl comando 'turn_for' es de forma predeterminada un comando de bloqueo. Impide que se ejecute cualquier código hasta que el chasis haya completado su turno.\n\n## Parámetro opcional\n\nSi el cuarto argumento se establece en 'wait=False', comandos de procedimiento se les permitirá ejecutar incluso antes de que el chasis ha completado su turno.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT, 90, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Dar Vuelta a la Dirección\n\nGira el chasis a una dirección específica cuando se utilizan los sensores Gyro o Inertial.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading(ANGLE, UNITS)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.turn_to_heading` se puede utilizar para girar el chasis a cualquier dirección dado en el sentido de las agujas del reloj.\n\nBasado en la dirección actual del Giroscopio, `drivetrain.turn_to_heading` determinará la dirección a girar.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` acepta valores numéricos en el rango de **0.00 a 359.99** cuando utilice la unidad `DEGREES` (grados).\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis haga cuatro vueltas: \n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(45.0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(90.0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(270.0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(180.0, DEGREES)\n```\n\n- Right to 45 degrees (Izquierda a 45 grados)\n- Right to 90 degrees (Izquierda a 90 grados)\n- Left to 270 degrees (Derecha a 270 grados)\n- Left to 180 degrees (Derecha a 180 grados)\n\nEl comando `turn_to_heading` por defecto bloqueará los comandos de procedimiento hasta que el giro del chasis se haya completado.\n\n## Parámetros Optionales\n\nPuede establecer `wait=False` como tercer parámetro para prevenir el comando `turn_to_heading` de bloquear los comandos de procedimiento de ejecución hasta que el giro del chasis se haya completado.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(180.0, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Dar vuelta a la Rotación\n\nGira el chasis a un ángulo de rotación específico.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(ANGLE, DEGREES)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation` se puede utilizar para girar el chasis a un valor positivo (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj) o negativo (en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj).\n\nBasado en la rotación actual del chasis, `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation` determinará la dirección a girar..\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_rotation()` puede aceptar valores numéricos.\n\nLos valores numéricos **no** son limitados al rango de **0 - 359.99** grados. Los giros serán absolutos y pueden hacer que el robot gire más de una vez si es necesario. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo hará que el chasis haga cuatro vueltas:\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90.0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(180, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(-45, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(0, DEGREES)\n```\n\n- Right (Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj)) a 90 grados\n- Right (Derecha (en el sentido de las agujas del reloj)) a 180 grados\n- Left (Izquierda (en sentido opuesto al de las agujas del reloj)) a -45 grados\n- Right (Derecha(en el sentido de las agujas del reloj)) a 0 grados\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation` por defecto bloqueará los comandos de procedimiento hasta que el giro del chasis se haya completado.\n\n## Parámetro opcional\n\nPuede establecer un tercer parámetro  `wait=False` para evitar el comando `turn_to_rotation' de bloquear los comandos de procedimiento hasta que se complete el giro del chasis.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(180.0, DEGREES, wait=False)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"function_name":{"name":"function_name","markdown":"# Función\n\nLas funciones son bloques de código que se utilizan para realizar una acción.\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara crear una función, cree un nombre de función significativo. Esto significa que el nombre de la función debe describir con precisión lo que hace.\n\nUtilice la **palabra clave** `def` antes del nombre de la función para definir la función.\n\nIncluya paréntesis `()` y encierra parámetros opcionales después del nombre de la función, seguido por un colon`:`. Múltiples parámetros deben estar separados por comas `(parameter1, parameter2)`.\n\nEl cuerpo de la función debe sangrar cuatro espacios. Si no hay ningún código para que la función se ejecute, un `pass` debe incluirse en la definición de la funciónn.\n\n```don\ndef my_function():\n    pass\n```\n\n## Ejemplo 1\n\nEsta función no tiene parámetros e imprimirá \"Hello\" en la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\ndef print_hello_world():\n    brain.print(\"Hello World\")\n```\n## Ejemplo 2\n\nEsta función toma un parámetro, `name` e imprimirá el valor de `name` en la pantalla de la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\ndef print_name(name):\n    brain.print(name)\n```","signature":"def function_name(parameters):\\n\\tpass"},"looks_brain_clear":{"name":"looks_brain_clear","markdown":"# Borrar\n\nBorra todas las filas de la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nbrain.clear()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nUtilícelo para eliminar todas las líneas de la Consola de impresión. Este comando no toma argumentos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Hello\" durante un segundo y luego borrará toda la pantalla antes de imprimir \"Goodbye\"\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nbrain.clear()\nbrain.print(\"Goodbye\")\n```","signature":"brain.clear()"},"looks_brain_new_line":{"name":"looks_brain_new_line","markdown":"# Nueva Fila\n\nEstablece el cursor en una nueva línea en la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nbrain.new_line()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nUtilice esta opción para ir a una nueva fila dentro de la Consola de impresión. Cualquier comando posterior de 'brain.print()' se imprimirá en una nueva línea dentro de la Consola de impresión. Este comando no toma ningún argumento.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá \"Hello\", luego en la línea siguiente de la pantalla, imprimirá \"World\".\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\nbrain.new_line()\nbrain.print(\"World\")\n```","signature":"brain.new_line()"},"looks_brain_print":{"name":"looks_brain_print","markdown":"# Imprimir\n\nImprime valores o texto en la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `brain.print()` imprimirá sus argumentos en la Consola de Impresión. \n\nEste comando puede tomar cualquier número de argumentos colocados entre sus paréntesis, incluyendo:\n\n- strings (cadenas) - cualquier combinación de letras, números o símbolos que se imprimirán exactamente como están escritos. Incluya la frase entre comillas y coloque esta frase directamente dentro de los paréntesis:  \n\t`brain.print(\"This is my string.\")`\n- numeric values (valores numéricos) - estos se imprimirán como números enteros por defecto*\n- variables - el valor almacenado de cualquier variable declarada previamente\n- valores reportados de otros comandos, como comandos de detección \n\n*La precisión de impresión se puede cambiar añadiendo una final argumento de `precision=` separados de otros argumentos por una coma. Establece `precision=1` imprimir con el decimal de décimas '0.1', establece `precision=2` para imprimir con el decimal centésimas, coloque '0.01'. El número asignado será igual al número de decimales impreso después del punto deicimal; hasta 'precisión=9' '0.000000001'.\n\nEl número impreso se redondea, hacia arriba o hacia abajo, al valor más cercano del lugar de precisión seleccionado.\n\n## Ejemplo 1\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"1 + 2 + Words\")\n```\nEsto imprimirá:\n \n`1 + 2 + Words` \n\na la Consola de Impresión.\n\n## Ejemplo 2\n\n```don\nbrain.print(1.67, precision=0)\nbrain.new_line()\nbrain.print(1.67, precision=1)\n```\nEsto imprimirá:\n\n```don\n2\n1.7\n```\na la Consola de Impresión.\n\n## Ejemplo 3\n\n```don\nmy_variable = 10\nbrain.print(my_variable + 5)\n```\n\nEsto imprimirá: \n\n`15` \n\na la Consola de Impresión.","signature":"brain.print(\"Hello\")"},"looks_brain_set_print_color":{"name":"looks_brain_set_print_color","markdown":"# Establecer Color de Impresión\n\nEstablece el color del texto impreso en la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nbrain.set_print_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija el color del texto impreso en la Consola de Impresión. El argumento debe estar escrito en mayúsculas. El color se puede establecer en `BLACK` (Negro), `RED` (Rojo), `GREEN` (Verde), or `BLUE` (Azul).\n\nLas letras, los números, los valores del sensor y los valores variables se pueden imprimir en el color seleccionado establecido por el comando 'set_print_color()'. \n\nCualquier comando 'brain.print()' usado después del 'set_print_color()' lo hará imprima en el color que haya establecido más recientemente en el programa.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo imprimirá 'GO!' en verde, y 'STOP!' en rojo a la Consola de Impresión.\n\n```don\nset_print_color(GREEN)\nbrain.print(\"GO!\")\nset_print_color(RED)\nbrain.print(\"STOP!\")\n```","signature":"brain.set_print_color(BLACK)"},"looks_monitor_sensor":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor","markdown":"# Sensor del Monitor\n\nLa función Sensor del Monitor se utiliza para añadir sensores a la consola del monitor.\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara supervisar los valores del sensor en la consola del monitor, utilice la función 'monitor_sensor()' añadiendo los identificadores de los sensores como parámetros de cadena. Esta función debe llamarse al inicio de la programa.\n\nA continuación se muestra la lista de identificadores de sensor compatibles:\n\n- `brain.timer_time`\n- `drivetrain.is_done`\n- `drivetrain.is_moving`\n- `drivetrain.heading`\n- `drivetrain.rotation`\n- `left_bumper.pressed`\n- `right_bumper.pressed`\n- `distance.found_object`\n- `distance.get_distance`\n- `front_eye.near_object`\n- `front_eye.detect`\n- `front_eye.brightness`\n- `down_eye.near_object`\n- `down_eye.detect`\n- `down_eye.brightness`\n- `location.position`\n- `location.position_angle`\n\nAñadir sensores a la consola del monitor:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"brain.timer_time\", \"drivetrain.is_done\")\n```\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn el siguiente ejemplo, `brain.timer_time` y `drivetrain.is_done` son identificadores de sensor que se añaden a la consola del monitor con la función 'monitor_sensor'.\n\nJusto al principio del programa (antes de que el robot se mueva), ambos sensores mostrarán el valor de '0' y 'True', respectivamente. \n\nMientras que se ejecuta el programa, el valor de 'brain.timer_time' aumenta con el tiempo, mientras que el 'drivetrain.is_done' se convierte en 'False'. Luego, después de que el robot haya completado sus movimientos, 'drivetrain.is_done' se vuelve 'True' de nuevo.\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"brain.timer_time\", \"drivetrain.is_done\")\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 600, MM, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM, wait=True)\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_variable":{"name":"looks_monitor_variable","markdown":"# Variable del Monitor\n\nLa función Variable del Monitor se utiliza para agregar variables a la consola del monitor.\n\n```don\nmonitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\", ...)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara supervisar los valores de las variables en la consola del monitor, utilice la función 'monitor_variable()' añadiendo los nombres de las variables como parámetros de cadena. Las variables que se deben supervisar deben estar en el ámbito mundial del programa.\n\n```don\nmy_variable = 0\nmy_boolean = False\n\ndef main():\n    global my_variable, my_boolean\n    brain.print(my_variable)\n    brain.new_line()\n    brain.print(my_boolean)\nvr_thread(main)\n```\n\nAdding variables to the monitor console:\n\n```don\nmonitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\")\n```\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn el ejemplo siguiente, 'my_variable' y 'my_boolean' son variables globales que se agregan a la consola del monitor con la función 'monitor_variable'.\n\nAl inicio del programa, ambas variables mostrarán el valor de '0' y 'False', respectivamente.A medida que se ejecuta el programa, el valor de 'mi_variable' se puede observar para aumentar de 0 a 4. \n\nAl final del programa, el valor de 'my_boolean' se convierte en 'True'.\n\n```don\nmy_variable = 0\nmy_boolean = False\n\ndef main():\n    global my_variable, my_boolean\n    monitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\")\n    for x in range(5):\n        my_variable = x\n    my_boolean = True\nvr_thread(main)\n```","signature":"monitor_variable()"},"looks_pen_move":{"name":"looks_pen_move","markdown":"# Establecer la posición de la pluma\n\nEstablece la posición de la pluma en VEX VR.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\nIntroduzca 'UP' (hacia arriba)  o 'DOWN' (hacia abajo) entre paréntesis.  \n**UP**: establecerá la pluma hacia arriba y no dibujará.\n**DOWN**: establecerá la pluma hacia abajo y se dibujará una línea en el Playground.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el lápiz hacia abajo y luego para arriba durante cuatro ciclos en incrementos de 50 mm, dibujando una línea discontinua en el Playground. \n\n```don\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 50, MM, wait=True)\n    pen.move(UP)\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 50, MM, wait=True)\n```","signature":"pen.move(DOWN)"},"looks_pen_set_pen_color":{"name":"looks_pen_set_pen_color","markdown":"# Establecer el color de la pluma\n\nEstablece el color de la pluma en VEX VR.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando 'pen.set pen color(BLACK)' acepta un argumento, una opción de color escrita en letras mayúsculas. Las posibles entradas son `BLACK` (negro), `RED` (rojo), `GREEN` (verde), or `BLUE` (azul).\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste ejemplo establecerá el color de la pluma en azul.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color(BLUE)\n```","signature":"pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)"},"magnet_energize":{"name":"magnet_energize","markdown":"# Energize Electromagnet\n\nSets the VR Robot Electromagnet to two different modes: **boost** or **drop**.\n\n```don\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\n```\n\n## How To Use\n\nEnter either `BOOST` or `DROP` within the parentheses.  \n**BOOST**: will attract a nearby magnetic object to the Electromagnet  \n**DROP**: will release any object the Electromagnet is holding \n\n## Example\n\nIn the Disk Mover playground, this example will have the robot drive forward 800mm, pick up an object, and then drop the object after driving 200mm in reverse.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 800, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 200, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(DROP)\n```","signature":"magnet_energize(BOOST)"},"sensing_brain_timer_reset":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_reset","markdown":"# Reiniciar Temporizador\n\nRestablece el temporizador del Brain.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl temporizador del Brain comienza al principio de cada proyecto.  función 'brain.timer_reset()' se utiliza para restablecer el temporizador a 0 segundos.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste proyecto hará conducir el tren motriz hacia adelante mientras que el temporizador en el Brain es menos de cinco segundos.  El comando 'brain.timer_reset()' se utiliza al principio del proyecto para restablecer el temporizador del cerebro.  \n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nUn comando wait necesita ser utilizado con bucles. \n\nObserve cómo la instrucción if contiene dos puntos al final - esto indica a Python que un bloque de instrucciones sigue","signature":"brain.timer_reset()"},"sensing_brain_timer_time":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_time","markdown":"# Valor del temporizador\n\nReporta del valor del temporizador del Brain en segundos.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_time(SECONDS)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl temporizador comienza en cero segundos cuando se inicia el proyecto y devuelve el valor del temporizador como un valor decimal.\n\n`brain.timer_time()` puede asignarse a variables, utilizarse en expresiones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores numéricos como parámetro.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste proyecto hara que conduzca el tren motriz hacia adelante mientras que el temporizador en el Brain es menos de cinco segundos. El comando brain.timer_reset() se utiliza en la condición en el bucle de While (mientras).\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nUn comando de espera necesita ser utilizado con loops. \n\nObserve cómo la instrucción while contiene dos puntos al final - esto indica a Python que un bloque de instrucciones sigue","signature":"brain.timer_time(SECONDS)"},"sensing_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Bumper Pressed\n\nReports if the Right Bumper or Left Bumper is pressed. \n\n`bumper.pressed()`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`bumper.pressed()` returns **true** if the Bumper is being pressed.\n\n`bumper.pressed()` returns **false** if the Bumper is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use. The VR Robot is equipped with a `right_bumper` and a `left_bumper`.\n\n```\nright_bumper.pressed()\nleft_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nThe `bumper.pressed()` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition for control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nleft_bumper_pressed = left_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif left_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Left bumper pressed\")\nif right_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Right bumper pressed\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(left_bumper.pressed())\n```","signature":"bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_distance_found_object":{"name":"sensing_distance_found_object","markdown":"# Objeto Encontrado a distancia\n\nReporta si el sensor de distancia incorporado ve un objeto o una superficie delante del robot. \n\n`distance.found_object()`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n`distance.found_object()` devuelve **true** cuando el Distance Sensor (Sensor de Distancia) incorporado ve un objeto o superficie dentro de su campo de visión, y dentro de 3000 mm del sensor.\n\n`distance.found_object()` devuelve **false** cuando el sensor de distancia incorporado no detecta un objeto o superficie dentro de 3000 mm.\n\nLa función `distance.found_object()` devuelve un valor **true** o **false**. Puede ser llamado y asignado a variables, ser usado como condición dentro de sentencias de control, o ser usado en funciones que toman valores booleanos como parámetro.\n\nEstablecer una variable:\n\n```\nfound_object = distance.found_object()\n```\n\nUsarlo en una instrucción de control:\n\n```\nif distance.found_object():\n\tbrain.print(\"Found object\")\n```\n\nPasándolo a otra función:\n\n```\nbrain.print(distance.found_object())\n```","signature":"distance.found_object()"},"sensing_distance_get_distance":{"name":"sensing_distance_get_distance","markdown":"# Distancia Desde\n\nInforma de la distancia del objeto más cercano desde el Distance Sensor (Sensor de Distancia).\n\n`distance.get_distance(UNITS)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nElija si `distance.get_distance(UNITS)` se notifica en milímetros o pulgadas reemplazando 'UNITS' (UNIDADES) por 'MM' o 'INCHES' (PULGADAS)como parámetros.\n\n```\ndistance.get_distance(MM)\ndistance.get_distance(INCHES)\n```\n\nLa función `distance.get_distance(UNITS)` se puede llamar y asignar a variables, utilizarse en instrucciones de comparación o utilizarse en funciones que toman valores numéricos como parámetro.\n\nEstablecer una variable:\n\n```\nmy_distance_mm = distance.get_distance(MM)\n```\nUsándolo en una declaración de comparación:\n\n```\nif distance.get_distance(INCHES) < 1:\n\tbrain.print(\"Close to object\")\n```\n\nPasándolo a otra función:\n\n```\nbrain.print(distance.get_distance(INCHES))\n```","signature":"distance.get_distance(MM)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Brillo del Down Eye sensor (sensor de ojo hacia abajo)\n\nDevuelve el brillo de un objeto.\n\n`down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` devuelve un valor numérico entre 0 y 100.  Devuelve un valor en porcentaje. Los objetos blancos reportarán un brillo del 100%, los objetos negros reportarán un brillo del 0%.  Todos los demás colores reportarán un brillo en algún lugar entre ese rango de valores.  \n\n`down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` se pueden asignar a variables, utilizarse en expresiones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores numéricos como parámetro.","signature":"down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Color del Down Eye Sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo)\n\nDevuelve el color específico detectado por el Down Eye sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo).\n\n`down_eye.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `down_eye.detect(RED)` devuelve un valor booleano. Devuelve **True** cuando el Down Eye Sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo) detecta la color seleccionado. Devuelve **False** cuando el Down Eye Sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo) detecta un color diferente del seleccionado.  \n\nElija el color del Down Eye Sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo) para detectar:\n\n* NONE (NINGUNO)\n* RED (ROJO)\n* GREEN (VERDE)\n* BLUE (AZUL)\n\n`down_eye.detect(RED)` se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en instrucciones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores booleanos como parámetro.\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este proyecto, el comando `down_eye.detect(GREEN)`  utiliza como una instrucción booleana. Si el comando `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` devuelve **True**, Green (Verde) se imprimirá en la Consola del Monitor.  Si el comando `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` devuelve  **False**, Not Green (No Verde) se mostrará en la Consola del Monitor.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if down_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            if down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n                brain.print(\"Green\")\n\n            else:\n                brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nEs necesario utilizar un comando de espera con bucles. \n\nObserve cómo la instrucción si/si-no contiene dos puntos al final de cada línea - esto indica a Python que un bloque de instrucciones sigue","signature":"down_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Down Eye Sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo) cerca del objeto\n\nDevuelve si el Down Eye Sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo) está lo suficientemente cerca de un objeto para detectar un color.\n\n`down_eye.near_object()`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `down_eye.near_object()` devuelve un valor booleano. Devuelve **True** cuando el Down Eye Sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo) está cerca de un objeto que tiene colores detectables, por ejemplo, un disco.  Devuelve  **False** cuando el Down Eye Sensor (Sensor de Ojo Hacia Abajo) no está lo suficientemente cerca de un objeto con colores detectables.  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` no detectará el suelo del Playground como un objeto, pero sí detectará colores (con la excepción de los dibujos de lápiz) en el suelo del Playground.  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en instrucciones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores booleanos como parámetro.  \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este proyecto, el comando `down_eye.near_object()` se utiliza como una instrucción booleana. Si el comando `down_eye.near_object()` devuelve **True**, la transmisión se detendrá, Near a Disk (Cerca de un Disco) se imprimirá en la consola del monitor y el proyecto se detendrá. Si el comando `down_eye.near_object()` devuelve  **False**, Not Near a Disk (No Cerca de un Disco) se imprimirá continuamente en nuevas líneas.\n\n```\n while True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if down_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            brain.print(\"Near a Disk\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near a Disk\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nEs necesario utilizar un comando de espera con bucles. \n\nObserve cómo la instrucción si/si-no contiene dos puntos al final de cada línea - esto indica a Python que un bloque de instrucciones sigue","signature":"down_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_drivetrain_heading":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_heading","markdown":"# Dirección del Tren Motriz\n\nDevuelve la dirección hacia la que se encuentra el tren motriz utilizando la posición angular actual del sensor Gyro.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.heading(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` devuelve un valor numérico.  El valor numérico en DEGREES (grados) aumentará al girar en el sentido de las agujas del reloj y una disminución al girar en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj.  \n\nEl comando `drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` devuelve un rango de 0.00 a 359.99 grados.  \n\n`drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` se pueden asignar a variables, utilizarse en expresiones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores numéricos como parámetro.","signature":"drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_done":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_done","markdown":"# Conducir ya Termino\n\nDevuelve si el Tren Motriz ha completado su movimiento.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.is_done()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` es un comando que devuelve un valor booleano. Devuelve **true** cuando los motores del tren motriz han completado su movimiento.\nDevuelve **false** cuando los motores del tren motriz siguen en movimiento.\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en instrucciones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores booleanos como parámetro.","signature":"drivetrain.is_done()"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving","markdown":"# Tren Motriz esta Moviendo\n\nReporta si el tren motriz se está moviendo actualmente.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` devuelve **true** cuando los motores del tren motriz se están moviendo.\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` devuelve **false** cuando los motores del tren motriz se detienen.\n\nLa función `drivetrain.is_moving()` devuelve un valor **true** o false**. Se puede llamar y asignar a variables, utilizarse como condición para instrucciones de control o utilizarse como parámetro de función.\n\nEstablecer una variable:\n\n```\nstill_driving = drivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\nUsarlo en una instrucción de control:\n\n```\nif drivetrain.is_moving():\n\tbrain.print(\"Still driving\")\n```\n\nPasándolo a otra función:\n\n```\nbrain.print(drivetrain.is_moving())\n```","signature":"drivetrain.is_moving()"},"sensing_drivetrain_rotation":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_rotation","markdown":"# Rotación del Tren Motriz\n\nDevuelve el ángulo de rotación del tren motriz.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\nEl comando `drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` reporta un valor numérico.  Devuelve un valor positivo cuando el tren motriz gira en el sentido de las agujas del reloj.  Devuelve un valor negativo cuando el tren motriz gira en sentido contrario a las agujas del reloj.\n\n`drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` se pueden asignar a variables, utilizarse en expresiones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores numéricos como parámetro.","signature":"drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Color Brightness\n\nReports the brightness of an object from one of the Color Sensor Eyes.\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` reports the brightness of an object. White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%. All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between that range of values.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```\nfront_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\ndown_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\nThe `eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` function returns a value between 0 and 100 value and can be assigned to variables, used in comparison statements, or in functions that take number values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\ncolor_brightness = front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\nUsing it in a comparison statement:\n\n```\nif down_eye.brightness(PERCENT) < 25:\n\tbrain.print(\"Dark color\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(front_eye.brightness(PERCENT))\n```","signature":"distance.distance(MM)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Detectando Color\n\nInformes si uno de los Color Sensor Eyes (sensor ocular de color) detecta un color específico.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` devuelve **true** cuando el Color Sensor Eye (sensor ocular de color) detecta el color seleccionado.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` devuelve **false** cuando el Color Sensor Eye (sensor ocular de color) detecta un color diferente al seleccionado.\n\nElija qué Color Sensor Eye (sensor ocular de color)usar. El 'front_eye' (ojo delantero) se monta delante del robot y mira hacia adelante, mientras que el 'down_eye' (ojo abajo) se monta debajo del robot y mira hacia abajo.\n\n```\nfront_eye.detect(RED)\ndown_eye.detect(RED)\n```\n\nElija el color que desea detectar. Reemplace el parámetro 'COLOR' por el color elegido para detectar.\n\n```\nNONE (NINGUNO)\nBLACK (NEGRO)\nRED (RED)\nGREEN (VERDE)\nBLUE (AZUL)\n```\n\n\nLa función `eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` devuelve un valor **true** o **false**. Se puede llamar y asignar a variables, usarse como condición dentro de instrucciones de control o usarse en funciones que toman valores booleanos como parámetro.\n\nEstablecer una variable:\n\n```\nfound_red = front_eye.detect(RED)\n```\n\nUtilizando en una instrucción de control:\n\n```\nif down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n\tbrain.print(\"On top of green\")\n```\n\nPasándolo a otra función:\n\n```\nbrain.print(down_eye.detect(BLUE))\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.detect(RED)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Color Near Object\n\nReports if the Color Sensor Eye is close enough to an object to detect a color.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` returns **true** when the Color Sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` returns **false** when the Color Sensor isn't close enough to an object with detectable colors.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```\nfront_eye.near_object()\ndown_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nThe `eye_sensor.near_object()` function returns a **true** or **false** value and can be assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\ncolor_obj_front = front_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif front_eye.near_object():\n\tbrain.print(\"Object in front\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(down_eye.near_object())\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.near_object()"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Brillo del Front Eye Sensor (sensor ocular delantero)\n\nDevuelve el brillo de un objeto.\n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` devuelve un valor numérico entre 0 y 100. Devuelve un valor en porcentaje. Los objetos blancos reportarán un brillo del 100%, los objetos negros reportarán un brillo del 0%. Todos los demás colores reportarán un brillo en algún lugar entre ese rango de valores.\n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` se pueden asignar a variables, utilizarse en expresiones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores numéricos como parámetro.","signature":"front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Color del Front Eye Sensor (Sensor del Ojo Delantero)\n\nDevuelve el color específico detectado por el Front Eye Sensor (Sensor del Ojo Delantero) .\n\n`front_eye.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `front_eye.detect(RED)` devuelve un valor Booleano.  Devuelve **True** cuando el Front Eye Sensor (Sensor del Ojo Delantero) detecta el color seleccionado.  Devuelve **False** cuando el sensor Front Eye Sensor (Sensor del Ojo Delantero) detecta un color diferente al seleccionado.  \n\nElija el color Front Eye Sensor (Sensor del Ojo Delantero) para detectar:\n\n* NONE (NINGUNO)\n* RED (ROJO)\n* GREEN (VERDE)\n* BLUE (AZUL)\n\n`front_eye.detect(RED)` se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en instrucciones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores booleanos como parámetro. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este proyecto, el comando `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` se utiliza como una instrucción booleana. Si el comando `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` devuelve **True**, Green se imprimirá en la Consola del Monitor. Si el comando `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` comando devuelve **False**, \"Not Green\" (\"No Verde\") se mostrará en la Consola del Monitor.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if front_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            if front_eye.detect(GREEN):\n                brain.print(\"Green\")\n\n            else:\n                brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nUn comando wait necesita ser utilizado con loops. \n\nObserve cómo la instrucción si/si-no contiene dos puntos al final de cada línea - esto indica a Python que un bloque de instrucciones sigue.","signature":"front_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Front Eye Sensor (Sensor de ojo delantero) cerca al objeto\n\nInforma si el Front Eye Sensor (Sensor de ojo delantero) está lo suficientemente cerca (dentro de 100 mm) de un objeto para detectar un color.\n\n`front_eye.near_object()`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `front_eye.near_object()` devuelve un valor booleano.  Devuelve  **True** cuando el Front Eye Sensor (Sensor de ojo delantero) está cerca a un objeto que tiene colores detectables.  Devuelve **False** cuando el Front Eye Sensor (Sensor de ojo delantero) no está lo suficientemente cerca de un objeto con colores detectables.  \n\n`front_eye.near_object()` se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en instrucciones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores booleanos como parámetro. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este proyecto, el comando `front_eye.near_object()` se utiliza como una instrucción booleana. Si el comando `front_eye.near_object()` devuelve **True**, el tren motriz se detendrá, Cerca de un objeto se imprimirá en la Consola del Monitor y el proyecto se detendrá. Si el comando `front_eye.near_object()` devuelve **False**, No Cerca de un Objeto se imprimirá continuamente en nuevas líneas.\n\n```\n while True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if front_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            brain.print(\"Near an Object\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n\n```\n \nUn comando de wait necesita ser utilizado con bucles. \n\nObserve cómo la instrucción si/si-no contiene dos puntos al final de cada línea - esto indica a Python que un bloque de instrucciones sigue","signature":"front_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_left_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_left_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Parachoques Izquierdo Presionado\n\nReturns if the Left Bumper is pressed.\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `left_bumper.pressed()` devuelve un valor booleano.  Devuelve **True** cuando se presiona el parachoques y **False** cuando el parachoques no está presionado.\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()` se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en instrucciones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores booleanos como parámetro.  \n\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este proyecto, el comando `left_bumper.pressed()` se utiliza como una instrucción booleana.  Si el sensor del parachoques izquierdo no está presionado, el tren motriz se moverá hacia adelante.  Si se pulsa el sensor del parachoques izquierdo, el tren motriz se detendrá.  \n\n```\nwhile True:\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n        if left_bumper.pressed():\n            drivetrain.stop()\n        else:\n            drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nEs necesario utilizar un comando de espera con bucles. \n\nObserve cómo la instrucción if/else (si-si no) contiene dos puntos al final de cada línea - esto indica a Python que un bloque de instrucciones sigue","signature":"left_bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_location_position":{"name":"sensing_location_position","markdown":"# Posición \n\nInforma de la posición de las coordenadas X e Y del robot.\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)` devuelve la posición de las coordenadas X o Y del Robot VR como valor numérico.\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)`  comando se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en expresiones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores numéricos como parámetro. \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste proyecto utiliza el comando location.position(AXIS, UNITS) ,(EJE, UNIDADES), para codificar el Robot VR para pasar al número 28 en Numbered Grid World Playground. El comando location.position(AXIS, UNITS), (EJE, UNIDADES), se utiliza como expresión booleana en este proyecto. \n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(X, MM) < 500:\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n\twait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT,90,DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(Y, MM) < -500:\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n\twait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"location.position(X, MM)"},"sensing_location_position_angle":{"name":"sensing_location_position_angle","markdown":"# Ángulo de Posición en Grados\n\nReporta ángulo del Robot en grados.\n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `location.position_angle(DEGREES)` devuelve el ángulo actual del Robot VR como valor numérico.  \n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)` comando se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en expresiones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores numéricos como parámetro.","signature":"location.position_angle(DEGREES)"},"sensing_right_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_right_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Parachoques derecho presionado\n\nDevuelve si está presionado el parachoques derecho.\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()`\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl comando `right_bumper.pressed()` devuelve un valor booleano.  Devuelve **True** cuando se presiona el parachoques y **False** cuando el parachoques no está presionado.\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()` se puede asignar a variables, utilizarse en instrucciones booleanas o utilizarse en otros comandos que toman valores booleanos como parámetro.  \n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEn este proyecto, el comando `right_bumper.pressed()` se utiliza como una instrucción booleana.  Si no se pulsa el sensor de parachoques derecho, el tren motriz avanzará.  Si se pulsa el sensor del parachoques derecho, el tren motriz se detendrá.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n        if right_bumper.pressed():\n            drivetrain.stop()\n        else:\n            drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nEs necesario utilizar un comando de espera con bucles. \n\nObserve cómo la instrucción if/else (si/si no) contiene dos puntos al final de cada línea - esto indica a Python que un bloque de instrucciones sigue.","signature":"right_bumper.pressed()"},"variables_my_2d_list":{"name":"variables_my_2d_list","markdown":"# Variable de lista 2D\n\nDe forma similar a una variable de List (lista), las variables de 2D List (Lista 2D) se utilizan para almacenar varios valores en un único nombre de variable. Sin embargo, las variables de lista 2D almacenan más de una lista.  \n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl nombre predeterminado de una variable 2D List (Lista 2D) es: \n\n```don\nmy_2d_list = [\n    \t[0,0,0],\n    \t[0,0,0],\n    \t[0,0,0]\n    ]\n```\n\nAl cambiar el nombre, las variables de lista 2D tienen las mismas reglas de nomenclatura que todas las demás variables:\n\n- El nombre no puede utilizar caracteres especiales (por ejemplo, un signo de exclamación)\n- El nombre no puede comenzar con un número\n- El nombre no puede utilizar espacios\n- El nombre no puede ser una palabra reservada en VEXcode VR (por ejemplo, Drivetrain o Tren Motriz)\n\nSe puede acceder a los elementos de datos de las matrices bidimensionales mediante dos índices. Un índice es la ubicación de un valor en Variable de Lista. Para acceder a un valor, utilice primero el nombre de la lista 2D y, a continuación, los dos índices entre corchetes.","signature":"my_2d_list = [\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0]\\n]"},"variables_my_boolean":{"name":"variables_my_boolean","markdown":"# Variable Booleana\n\nLas variables booleanas pueden contener valores **True** o **False**.\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara asignar un valor a una variable booleana, utilice el operador =.   \n\nEl nombre predeterminado de una variable numérica es my_boolean (mi_booleano). Aplique las siguientes reglas al cambiar el nombre de una variable numérica:\n\n- El nombre no puede utilizar caracteres especiales (por ejemplo, un signo de exclamación)\n- El nombre no puede comenzar con un número\n- El nombre no puede utilizar espacios\n- El nombre no puede ser una palabra reservada en VEXcode VR (por ejemplo, Drivetrain o Tren Motriz)","signature":"my_boolean = True"},"variables_my_list":{"name":"variables_my_list","markdown":"# Variables de Listas\n\nLas Variables de Listas se utilizan para almacenar varios valores en un único nombre de variable.\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nEl nombre predeterminado de una Variable de Lista es: `my_list = [0,0,0]`\n\nAl cambiar el nombre, las Variables de Listas tienen las mismas reglas de nomenclatura que todas las demás variables:\n\n- El nombre no puede utilizar caracteres especiales (por ejemplo, un signo de exclamación)\n- El nombre no puede comenzar con un número\n- El nombre no puede utilizar espacios\n- El nombre no puede ser una palabra reservada en VEXcode VR (por ejemplo, Drivetrain o Tren Motriz)\n\nra asignar una lista de valores a una Variable de Lista, utilice el operador = seguido de valores separados por comas, denominados elementos, entre corchetes.  \n\nSi los valores son números, las comas deben venir justo después de cada número. \n\n`my_list = [0,1,2]`\n\nSi los valores son cadenas, las comas deben salir de las comillas.\n\n`my_list = [“Drive”,”Forward”]`\n\nSe puede acceder a los elementos de datos de una Variable de Lista mediante un índice. Un índice es la ubicación de un valor en la Variable de Lista. A cada elemento dentro de una matriz se le asigna un entero, comenzando por cero. \n\n## Ejemplos\n\nEl proyecto utiliza la lista `my_list` (mi lista) en un for loop (bucle de por). Los valores de `my_list` son strings (cadenas).  La variable y es el iterador en el for loop (bucle de por).  Por lo tanto, el for loop (bucle de por) itera y, a continuación, imprime, cada elemento dentro de la Variable de Lista 'my list'.\n\n```don\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", ‘100%', \"fun!!\"]\nfor y in my_list:\n\tbrain.print(y)\n\tbrain.new_line()\n\twait(5, MSEC)\n```\n\nEste proyecto imprimirá:\n\n```don\nVEXcode VR\nis\n100%\nfun!!\n```\n\nEste proyecto no necesita un for loop (bucle de por) porque solo está imprimiendo un elemento de la Variable de Lista `my_list`. Para tener acceso a un elemento, coloque el índice del elemento entre corchetes después del nombre de la Variable de Lista.\n\n\n```don\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", '100%', \"fun!!\"]\nbrain.print(my_list[3])\nwait(5, MSEC)\n```  \n\nEste proyecto imprimirá:\n\n```\nfun!!\n```","signature":"my_list = [0,0,0]"},"variables_my_number":{"name":"variables_my_number","markdown":"# Variable Numérica\n\nLas variables numéricas se utilizan para almacenar valores numéricos.\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara asignar un valor a una variable numérica, utilice el operador =.   \n\nEl nombre predeterminado de una variable numérica es my_number.  Aplique las siguientes reglas al cambiar el nombre de una variable numérica:\n\n- El nombre no puede utilizar caracteres especiales (por ejemplo, un signo de exclamación)\n- El nombre no puede comenzar con un número\n- El nombre no puede utilizar espacios\n- El nombre no puede ser una palabra reservada en VEXcode VR (por ejemplo, Drivetrain o Tren Motriz)\n\n## Ejemplo\n\nEste proyecto tendrá el tren motriz hacia adelante para 150 mm y luego gire a la derecha 90 grados. \n\n```don\ndistance_to_travel = 150\nangle_to_turn = 90\n\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, distance_to_travel, MM)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, angle_to_turn, DEGREES)\n```\nEste proyecto creó dos variables y las nombró `distance_to_travel` (distancia para viajar) y  `angle_to_turn` (ángulo a girar).  Luego se utiliza un = para asignar un valor a cada variable. A continuación, las variables se utilizan en los comandos `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD)` y `drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT)`.","signature":"my_number = 1"},"variables_my_string":{"name":"variables_my_string","markdown":"# Variable de Cadena\n\nLas variables de cadena se utilizan para almacenar cadenas, que es una secuencia de caracteres.  Los caracteres pueden incluir números, letras y símbolos. \n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPara asignar un valor a una variable de cadena, utilice el operador =.   \n\nEl nombre predeterminado de una variable numérica es mi cadena.  Aplique las siguientes reglas al cambiar el nombre de una variable numérica:\n\n- El nombre no puede utilizar caracteres especiales (por ejemplo, un signo de exclamación)\n- El nombre no puede comenzar con un número\n- El nombre no puede utilizar espacios\n- El nombre no puede ser una palabra reservada en VEXcode VR (por ejemplo, Drivetrain o Tren Motriz)","signature":"my_string = \\\"Hello World\\\""},"vr_thread":{"name":"vr_thread","markdown":"# Subproceso VR\n\nLa función Subproceso VR debe utilizarse para llamar a funciones al nivel global del script.\n\n## Cómo Utilizar\n\nPuede tomar cualquier función que haya hecho, anteriormente y pasarla a  `vr_thread()` al nivel global. Se requiere llamar al menos a una función dentro de esto para ejecutar un programa. No es necesario hacer todas las llamadas de función con `vr_thread()`, solo aquellas que están a nivel global.\n\nSi se realiza más de una llamada `vr_thread()`, las funciones se ejecutarán en paralelo.\n\nLas funciones, en general, se ejecutarán en el orden en que se llamen. Sin embargo, si una función espera, pasará la ejecución a la siguiente función y continuará cuando finalice la otra función, o cuando la otra función también espere.\n\n```don\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nLas funciones que hacen que los subprocesos cambien:\n\n```\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.set_heading(0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(0, DEGREES)\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nany user created function\n\n```\nLlamar a un método con `vr_thread()` dentro de otro método provocará un error de tiempo de ejecución. Si ejecuta este programa:\n\n```\ndef main():\n    vr_thread(print_hello())\n\ndef print_hello():\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nTe dará un error que se ve así:\n\n```\nError: Traceback (most recent call last):\n  line 9, in vr_thread\n  line 5, in main\nNameError: name 'print_hello' is not defined\n```\n\n## Ejemplo 1\n\nHacemos una sola función que se ejecuta en el espacio global con `vr_thread()`. La función `move_drivetrain()` hará que el robot se mueva 200 mm y luego imprima cuando termine.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\n```\n## Ejemplo 2\n\nAhora añadimos la función `move_pen_down()`. Esto moverá el lápiz hacia abajo e imprimirá un mensaje. Hacemos la llamada `vr_thread(move_pen_down())` **después de** la llamada `vr_thread(move_drivetrain())`. Sin embargo, debido a que `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)` hace que la función espere, cambiará a la función `move_pen_down()` que moverá la pluma hacia abajo antes de que el robot termine de moverse.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\nvr_thread(move_pen_down())\n```\n\n## Ejemplo 3\n\nMover la `move_pen_down()` a la `move_drivetrain()` ahora la llamará de forma lentejuelas, por lo que no habrá dibujo de pluma ya que no cae hasta después de que el movimiento haya terminado. Esto también muestra que las funciones de llamada dentro de otras funciones se pueden hacer como la llamada de función normal.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n    move_pen_down()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\n```","signature":"vr_thread(function_name())"}}},"fi":{"blocks":{"add":{"name":"add","markdown":"# Lisää\n\nBlokki laskee kaksi lukua yhteen ts on yhteenlasku toiminto.\n\n![add](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/add/add.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**lisää** blokki hyväksyy desimaali- ja kokonaisluvut sekä numeeriset blokit.\n\n**lisää** blokkiin saa laittaa pyöreitä numero blokkeja ja se hin pyöreisiin tiloihin. \n\n\nKahden luvun yhteenlasku\n\n![add_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/add/add_numbers.png)\n\nKahden muuttujan yhteenlasku.\n\n![add_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/add/add_variables.png)  \n\nKahden anturiarvon yhteeenlasku\n\n![add_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/add/add_sensors.png)\n\n"},"and":{"name":"and","markdown":"# ja\n\nAntaa vastauksen , onko kaksi Boolean ehtoa molemmat voimassa yhtäaikaa. \n\n![and](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/and/and.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**ja** blokki antaa boolean arvon **tosi** kun molemmat ehdot ovat **totta**. \n\n**ja** blokki antaa boolean arvon **epätosi** kun molemmat ehdot eivät ole **totta**. \n\n**ja** Boolean blokki antaa vastauksen joko **tosi** tai **epätosi** ja sen voi sijoittaa muiden blokkien kuusikulmaisiin (salmiakkikuvio) kohtiin.\n\n![and_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/and/and_shape.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTämä esimerkki tarkistaa jos **FrontEye** on lähellä esinettä ja onko esine punainen.\n\n![and_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/and/and_sensors.png)\n\n**ja** antaa arvon **tosi** jos  **FrontEye** on lähellä esinettä ja esine on punainen.\n\n\n**ja** antaa arvon **epätosi** jos  **FrontEye** lähellä ei ole esinettä ja esine ei ole punainen tai toinen ehdoista on **epätosi**.\n\n\n\n![and_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/and/and_truth.png)"},"boolean_variable":{"name":"boolean_variable","markdown":"# Boolean muuuttuja\n\nAntaa Boolean muuttujan arvon.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/boolean_variable/boolean_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Boolean muuttuja** antaa muuttujan **tosi** tai **epätosi** arvon. Sen voi päivittää/asettaa **aseta boolean muuttuja** blokilla.\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/boolean_variable/boolean_set_value.png)\n\n**boolean muuttuja** saa arvon **tosi** or **epätosi** ja sen voi sijoitta kuusikulmaiseen (salmiakkikuvio) paikkaan muissa blokeissa.\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/boolean_variable/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nBoolean muuttuja luodaan  **Tee Boolean** painikkeella.\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/boolean_variable/boolean_button.png)  \n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nJos haluat nähsä muutttujan arvon, täppää checkbox kohtaa muuttuja rivillä.\n\n![boolean_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/boolean_variable/boolean_checkbox.png)\n\nSen jälkeen avaa  Monitori ikkuna/konnsoli, jossa **boolean** muuttujan arvo näkyy.\n\n![boolean_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/boolean_variable/boolean_monitor.png)\n\n"},"break":{"name":"break","markdown":"# Keskeytä\n\nVoit hypätä toistorakenteesta (silmukasta) pois haluamasi kohdalla\n\n![break](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/break/break.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nSilmukkarakenteen sisällä **keskeytä** blokki poistuu ko kohdasta silmukan jälkeiseen toimintoblokkiin. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nAJopeli ajaa eteenpäin. Jos V5 Aivojen näyttöä painaa niin ajo loppuu. \n\nJos näyttöä **painettu**, **keskeytä** blokki tekee suorituksen keskeytyksen ts ajopeli pysähtyy.\n\n![break_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/break/break_example.png)"},"broadcast":{"name":"broadcast","markdown":"# Lähetä\n\nLähetä välittää viesti blokille **when I receive** suoritettavaksi.\n\n![broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/broadcast/broadcast.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nLähettävä **lähetä** blokki jatkaa samall kuin viestin saanut blokki tekee oma tehtävää.\n\nValitse **Lähetä** blokkin kohde jota käytät.\n\n[broadcast_select](broadcast_select.png)"},"broadcast_and_wait":{"name":"broadcast_and_wait","markdown":"# Lähetä ja odota\n\nLähetä viestillä voi aktivoida **Kun saan viestin** blokin toiminnan ja jää odottaa ett bloki suoritetaan loppuun.\n\n![broadcast_and_wait_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/broadcast_and_wait/broadcast_and_wait_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Lähetä ja odota** odottaa kunnes kutsuttu blokki on suoritettu loppuun.\n\nValise **lähetä** blokki, jota haluat käyttää. Voit myös luoda uuden viestin.\n\n![broadcast_and_wait_event_option](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/broadcast_and_wait/broadcast_and_wait_event_option.png)"},"bumper":{"name":"bumper","markdown":"# Törmäys\n\nKertoo milloin törmäysanturiia on painettu.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n\n**Törmäys** antaa Boolean arvon **tosi** jos törmäysanturia on painettu.\n\n**Törmäys** antaa Boolean arvon **epätosi** jos törmäysanturia ei ole painettu.\n\nValise käyttämäsi törmäusanturi.\n\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\n**Törmäys** on Boolean blokki, jonka arvot ovat **totta ** tai **epätosi** ja sen voi sijoittaa blokkien kuusikulmaisiin (salmiakkikuvion) kohtiin.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nValise **törmäys** blokki täppäämällä checkbox -kohtaa anturin rivillä.\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nNäet arvon näytöllä, kun avaat Monitori-ikkunan.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)\n\n"},"change_variable":{"name":"change_variable","markdown":"# Vaihda muuttujan arvo\n\nMuuttaa muuuttujan arvon halutuksi arvoksi.\n\n![change_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/change_variable/change_variable.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValitse muuttuja, jota haluat käytttää **Vaihda muuttujan arvo** blokin avulla voit muuttaa nykyistä arvoa tai nimetä uudeleen tai poistaa muuttuja käytöstä.\n\n![change_variable_options](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/change_variable/change_variable_options.png)\n\n **Vaihda muuttujan arvo** blokki voi olla desimaali- ja kokonaisluku tai voi käsitellä numero blokkeja.\n\n![change_variable_value](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/change_variable/change_variable_value.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä asetetaan muuttujalle alkuarvo 25 ja muutetaan silmukassa **toista** joka kierroksella muuttujan arvoa +10.\n\n![change_variable_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/change_variable/change_variable_example2.png)"},"clear_all_rows":{"name":"clear_all_rows","markdown":"# Tyhjää kaikki rivit\n\nTyhjää kaikki rivit tulostus näytöllä/konsolilla.\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nKäytä **Tyhjää kaikki rivit** blokkia postaman kaikki näytön/konsolin rivit.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTulosta \"Hello\" yhden sekunnin ajan ja tyhjää sitter näyttö ja tulosta \"Goodbye\".\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"clear_row":{"name":"clear_row","markdown":"# Tyhjää rivi\n\nTyhjää valitut (1-5) rivit VEX IQ aivojen näytöllä.\n\n![clear_row](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/clear_row/clear_row.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Tyhjää rivi** blokissa voi käyttä kokonaislukuja tai muita numeerisia blokkeja välillä  **1 - 5** jotka voi tyhjätä.\n\n![row_column_index](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/clear_row/row_column_index.png)  \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkissä tulostetaan näytölle \"Hello\" kohtaan rivi 2 sarake 1 kolmen sekunnin ajan ennen rivin 2 tyhjäämistä.\n\n![clear_row_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/clear_row/clear_row_example.png)"},"comment":{"name":"comment","markdown":"# Kommentti\n\nOhjelmoija voi kirjotttaa projektiin kommenteja selventämään ohjelman toimintaa. \n\n![comment_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/comment/comment_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nAmmattilaiset ohjelmoijat kommentoivat aina ohjelmansa toimintaa niin että muutkin pystyvät mahdollisimman helposti päästä käsitykseen, miten ohjelma toimii ja mitä sen on tarkoitus tehdä. Selviää myös testaajille toimiiko ohjelma oikein. \n\nLisää **Kommentti** haluttuun kohtaan ohjelmaa. SItä ei suoriteta vaan jatka suoritusblokkien kirjottamista sen jälkeen.\n\n![comment_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/comment/comment_example3.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nKommentit eivät vaikuta varsinaiseen ohjelman toimintaan ja suoritukseen.\n\n![comment_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/comment/comment_example2.png)\n\n"},"distance_distance":{"name":"distance_distance","markdown":"# Etäisyys esineestä\n\nAntaa eteäisyyden lähimmästä esineestä.\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_distance/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValitse yksikkö jota **etäisyys esineestä** käyttää, joko mm tai tuumaa. \n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_distance/distance_from_unit.png)\n\n**etäisyys esineestä** voi käyttää blokkien pyöreissä kentissä.\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_distance/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nValise  **Etäisyys esineestä** blokki ja täppää checkbox valintaa ko rivin vasemmalla puolella.\n\n![distance_from_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_distance/distance_from_checkbox.png)\n\nAvaa Monitori -ikkuna / konsoli niin näet blokin arvon.\n\n![distance_from_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_distance/distance_from_monitor.png)\n\n"},"distance_found":{"name":"distance_found","markdown":"# Etäisyys esineeseen löytyy\n\nKeroo onko sisäinen Etäisyys anturi löytää esineen robotin edessä. \n\n![distance_found_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_found/distance_found_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Etäisyys esineeseen löytyy** antaa Boolean arvon **tosi** wkun sisäänrakennettu Etäisyys -anturi näkee esineen edessään alle 3000 mm matkalla (yläraja sensorin näkökyvylle).\n\n**Etäisyys esineeseen löytyy** antaa Boolean arvon **epätosi** wkun sisäänrakennettu Etäisyys -anturi ei näe esinettä edessään alle 3000 mm matkalla (yläraja sensorin näkökyvylle).\n\n**Etäisyys esineeseen löytyyt** reports **false** when the built in Distance sensor does not detect an object or surface within 3000 mm.\n\n![distance_found_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_found/distance_found_insert.png)\n\n## Esimerkki \n\nRobotti kääntyy oikealle kunnes näkee sineen . kUn esine löytyy niin robotti pysähtyy ja ajaa kohti esinettä.\n\n![distance_found_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_found/distance_found_example.png)\n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nValise  **Etäisyys esineeseen löytyy** blokki ja täppää checkbox kohtaa blokin vasemmalla puolella.\n\n![distance_found_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_found/distance_found_checkbox.png)\n\nAvaa Monitori -ikkuna/konsoli, niin näet muuttujan arvon näytöllä.\n\n![distance_found_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/distance_found/distance_found_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"divide":{"name":"divide","markdown":"# Jako\n\nSUorittaa ensimmäisen luvun jakamisen toisella luvulla ja antaa sen tuloksen.\n\n![divide](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/divide/divide.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Jako** hyväksyy desimaali - ja kokonaislukuja ja siinä voi käyttää numeerisia blokkeja.\n\n**jako** blokin voi sijoittaa muihin blokkeihin pyöreän tilan kohtiin. \n\nAnna luvut joille jako tehtään\n\n![divide_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/divide/divide_numbers.png)\n\nJakolasku numeroilla.\n\n![divide_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/divide/divide_variables.png)\n\nJakolaskui antureiden arvoilla.\n\n![divide_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/divide/divide_sensors.png)\n\n"},"drive":{"name":"drive","markdown":"# Aja\n\nAjopeli liikkuu ikuisesti (ellei joku ehto sitä pysäytä).\n\n![drive_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive/drive_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**aja** blokin avulla ajoneuvo liikkuu ikuisesti kunnes joku muu blokki sen keskeyttää tai ohjelma on loppunut.\n\nValise ajon suunta.\n\n![drive_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive/drive_direction.png)  \n"},"drive_for":{"name":"drive_for","markdown":"# Aja matka\n\nAjopeli kulkee valitun matkan.\n\n![drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_for/drive_for.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValise suunta, johon ajopeli kulkee.\n\n![drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_for/drive_for_direction.png)\n\n\nAseta arvo, jonka matkan ajopeli liikkuu ja yksikkö (mm tai tuumaa).\n\n**Aja matka** blokki voi sisältää desimaali- tai kokonaislukuja ja siihen voi asettaa numeerisen blokin.\n\n![drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_for/drive_for_units.png)\n\nOletuksena seuraavat blokit ohjelmassa odottaa kunnes ajo on päättynyt.\n\nVoit myös valita option **ja älä odota** , jolloin seuraava ohjelman blokki suoritetaan välittömästi vaikka ajo olisi kesken.\n\n![drive_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_for/drive_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"drive_heading":{"name":"drive_heading","markdown":"# Aja suuntaan\n\nAsettaa ajosuunnan ajopelin nykyisestä sijainnista(asennosta) käsin.\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_heading/drive_heading.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Aja suuntaan** arvo kasvaa myötäpäivään siirtyessä.\n\n**Aja suuntaan** on aina välillä **0.00 arvoon 359.99 astetta**.\n\n**Aja suuntaan** blokkia voi käyttää pyöreissä blokin kentissä.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_heading/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nValitse **Aja suuntaan** blokki ja täppää sen checkbox kenttää blokin vasemmalla puolella.\n\n![drive_heading_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_heading/drive_heading_checkbox.png)\n\nVoit seura arvoja avaamalla Monitori - ikkuna/konsoli.\n\n![drive_heading_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_heading/drive_heading_monitor.png)\n\n"},"drive_is_done":{"name":"drive_is_done","markdown":"# Ajo on suoritettu loppuun\n\nKertoo onko ajopelin ajo päättynyt.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## Kuinka voi käyttää\n\n**Ajo on suoritettu loppuun** antaa Boolean arvon **totta** kun ajopelin ajomoottorit ovat liikkumisen lopettaneet.\n\n**Ajo on suoritettu loppuun** antaa Boolean arvon **epätotta** kun ajopelin ajomoottorit pyörivät vielä.\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nJotta näet **Ajo on suoritettu loppuun** blokin arvon , täppää checkbox ruutua muuttujan vierellä.\n\n![drive_is_done_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_checkbox.png)\n\nSItten ajaa Monitori ikkuna/konsoli jossa näet sen arvon.\n\n![drive_is_done_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_monitor.png)"},"drive_is_moving":{"name":"drive_is_moving","markdown":"# Ajopeli liikkuu\n\nRaportoi jos ajopeli on tällä hetkellä liikkeessä.\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Ajopeli liikkuu** saa Boolean arvon **tosi** jos ajopeli ajomoottorit pyörivät.\n\n**Ajopeli liikkuu** saa Boolean arvon **epäi** jos ajopeli ajomoottoritovat pysähtyneet.\n\n**Ajopeli liikkuu** saa Boolean arvon **tosi** tai **epätosi** ja sen voi sijoittaa kuusikulmaisen (salmiakkikuvion) muodon kohtiin muissaa blokeissa.\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n\n## arvon seuranta\n\nvalise **Ajopeli liikkuu** blokki ja täppää kohtaa checkbox blokin edessä.\n\n![drive_is_moving_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_checkbox.png)\n\nAvaa Monitor -ikkuna / konsoli, jotta näet sen arvon näytöllä.\n\n![drive_is_moving_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_monitor.png)\n\n\n"},"drive_rotation":{"name":"drive_rotation","markdown":"# Aja kiertokulma\n\nNäyttää ajopelin kiertokulman Gyro anturilla.\n\n![drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_rotation/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Aja kiertokulma** antaa **postiivisen** arvon, kun ajopeli kääntyy **myötäpäivään**.\n\n**Aja kiertokulma** antaa **negatiiven** arvon kun ajopeli kääntyy **vastapäivään**.\n\n**Aja kiertokulma** blokkia voi käyttää toisten blokkien muuttujien pyöreissä kohdissa.\n\n![drive_rotation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_inserted.png)\n\n## Arvojen seuranta\n\nValitse **Aja kiertokulma** blokki ja täppää sen checkbox valintaa sen vasemmalla puolella.\n\n![drive_rotation_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_checkbox.png)\n\nAvaa Monitori ikkuna/konsoli niin näet arvot näytöllä.\n\n![drive_rotation_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_monitor.png)"},"equal_to":{"name":"equal_to","markdown":"# Yhtäsuuri\n\nKertoo on luku saman suuruinen kuin toinen lukue. \n\n![equal_to](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/equal_to/equal_to.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Yhtäsuuri** saa Boolean arvon **tosi** jos kaksi arvoa on yhtäsuurta.\n\n**Yhtäsuuri** saa Boolean arvon **epätosi** jos kaksi arvoa eivät ole yhtäsuurta.\n\n**Yhtäsuuri** blokkiin voi sijoottaaa desimaali- ja kokonaislukuja sekä numeerisen blokin.\n\n**Yhtäsuuri** Boolean blokin arvo voi olla **tosi** or **epätosi** ja sen voi sijoittaa kuusikulmaiseen (salmiakkikuvion) kentän paikalle.\n\n![equal_to_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/equal_to/equal_to_inserted.png)\n\nTässä esimerkissä ajopeli pysähtyy kun etäisyys anturi saa arvon 500 mm.\n\n![equal_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/equal_to/equal_to_example.png)"},"forever":{"name":"forever","markdown":"# Ikuisesti\n\nToistorakenne pyörii ikuisesti .\n\n![forever](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/forever/forever.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nvoit käyttää **Ikuisesti**  kun haluat blokin pyörivät ikuisesti.\n\n**Ikuisesti** loopista voi hypätä pois **Katkaise** blokilla toiston sisällä.\n\n![forever_example_2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/forever/forever_example_2.png)\n\nBlokkeja voi laittaa sisäkkäin. Sitä sanotaan nesting -käsitteellä."},"function":{"name":"function","markdown":"# Funktio\n\nsuorittaa halutun matematikkan funtion (laskutoimituksen).\n\n![function](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/function/function.png)\n\n* **abs** - itseisarvo\n* **floor** - pyöristää lähimpään kokonaislukuun\n* **ceiling** - pyöristää ylöspäin lähimpään kokonaislukuun\n* **sqrt** - neliöjuuri\n* **sin** - sin trigonometriassa\n* **cos** - cosini\n* **tan** - tangentti\n* **asin** - arcsine eli käänteis sin\n* **acos** - arccosine eli  käänteis cosf cosine\n* **atan** - arctan eli käänteis tangentti\n* **ln** - luonnollinen logaritmi (kantaluku e)\n* **log** - 10 logaritmi (kantaluku 10)\n* **e ^** - potenssi (e kantaluku)\n* **10 ^** - potenssi (10 kantaluku)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Funktio** blokki hyväksyy desimaali- ja kokonaislukuja ja numeerisen blokin arvoja sijoitettuna siihen.\n\nValitse **Funktio** optiolistalta.\n\n![function_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/function/function_select.png)\n\n**Funktio** blokin voi sijoitta toisiin blokkeihin niiden pyöresiin kohtiin. \n\n![function_fit](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/function/function_fit.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"greater_than":{"name":"greater_than","markdown":"# Suurempi kuin\n\nVertailee onko ensimmäinen luku isompi kuin toinen luku.  \n\n![greater_than](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/greater_than/greater_than.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Suurempi kuin** on Boolean arvo **tosi** jos ensonmäinen luku on isompi kuin jälkimmäinen luku. \n\n\n**Suurempi kuin** on Boolean arvo **epätosi** jos ensonmäinen luku on ei ole isompi kuin jälkimmäinen luku. \n\n**Suurempi kuin** bloki voi sisältää desimaali- ja kokonaislukuja ja numeerisia blokin arvoja.\n\n**Suurempi kuin** antaa Boolean arvot **tosi** or **epästosi** ja sen voi sijoittaa kuusikulmaiseen (salmiakkimuoto) tilaan toisessa blokissa.\n\n![greater_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/greater_than/greater_than_inserted.png)\n\nEsimerkissä LED n väri laitetaan vihreäksi kun kello on käynyt yli 50 sekuntia.\n\n![greater_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/greater_than/greater_than_example.png)\n"},"heading_of_gyro":{"name":"heading_of_gyro","markdown":"# Kulkusuunnan kulma\n\nAntaa 3-johto Gyro sensorin tai V5 Inertial sensorin nykyisen kulkusuunnan asteina.\n\n![heading_of_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Kulkusuunnan kulma** kasvaa kun ajopeli kääntyy myötäpäivään.\n\n**Kulkusuunnan kulma** arvo on aina välillä **0.00 ja 359.99**.\n\nValitse Gyro Sensori tai Inertial Sensori, jonka kulmaa tarkastelet.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro_gyro.png)\n\n**Kulkusuunnan kulma** voidaan sijoittaa blokkeihin, joissa on pyöreä kenttä käytössä.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro_inserted.png)\n"},"if_then":{"name":"if_then","markdown":"# Jos niin\n\nJos ehto on voimassa eli **tosi** niin toteutetaan niin haaran blokit.  \n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/if_then/if_then.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Jos niin** ehto tarkisaa Boolean ehdon vain yhden kerran. \n\nJos Boolean ehto on **tosi**, nin **Jos niin** haaran blokit suoritetaan.\n\nJos Boolean ehto on **epätosi**, nin **Jos niin** haaran blokit skipataan yli.\n\n**Jos niin** blokki hyväksyy kuusikulmaisen (salmiakkikuvion) kentän ehtoja.\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/if_then/if_then_2.png)\n\n"},"if_then_else":{"name":"if_then_else","markdown":"# Jos niin tai\n\nSuorittaa blokit jommasta kummasta **niin** -haarasta  tai  **tai** haarasta ehdon boolean arvosta.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/if_then_else/if_then_else.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Jos niin tai** 'blokki tehdään vain kerran. \n\nJos Boolean ehto on **tosi**, vain  **niin** -haaran blokit suoritetaan.\n\nJos Boolean ehto on **epätosi**, vain **tai** -haaran blokit suoritetaan.\n\n **Jos niin tai** blokin voi sijoittaa kuusikulmaisen (salmiakkikuvion) kenttiin.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/if_then_else/if_then_else_2.png)"},"item_of_2d_array":{"name":"item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# 2d taulukon arvo\n\nANtaa 2d taulukon alkion arvon. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValise 2d taulukko, jota haluat käyttää. Voit myös uudelleen nimetä tai poistaa koko taulukon.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nAnna 2d taulukon alkion rivinumero (ensinmäinen numero)ja sarakenumero (toinen numero). Tässä on valittu 2d taulukon 2 rivi ja 3 sarake.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\n2D taulukon alkio voi desimaa- tai koknaisluku tai se voi siältää numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)\n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nTAvaa Monitori -ikkuna/ konsole  ja paina 'Lisää taulukko painiketta oikealla alhaalla nurkassa.\n\n![array_monitor_add](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_2d_array/array_monitor_add.png)\n\nTäpp checkbox kenttää lisätyn taulokon rivillä. Näitä voi olla monta täpättynä , koska taulukom maksimikoko on 10 * 10 alkiota. Tallenna jotta alkio näkyy Montori ikkunassa.\n\n![array_monitor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_2d_array/array_monitor_select.png)\n\n Palaa Monitori-ikkunaan jotta voit nähdä taulukon arvot jotka valitsit.\n\n![array_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_2d_array/array_monitor.png)\n\n"},"item_of_array":{"name":"item_of_array","markdown":"# Alkio \n\nAntaa luettelon alkion. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_array/item_of_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValise luettelo, jota käsittelet, voitmyös uudellen nimetä tai poisaa luettelon.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_array/item_of_options.png)\n\nAnna luettelon alkion järjestysnumero. Esimerkissä 2 alkio on valittu. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_array/item_of_block_2.png)\n\n**Alkio** voi sisältää desimaali - jakkoknaisluvun tao numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nAJopelin nopeudeksi on valittu taulukon 3 alkio, jolloin ajopelin nopeus on 90 %.\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_array/item_of_example2.png)\n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nAvaa Monitori -ikkuna ja valise 'Lisää luettelo' nappi oikelta alakulmasta.\n\n![array_monitor_add](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_array/array_monitor_add.png)\n\nSitten täppääThen checkbox kohtaa taulukon vasemmalla puolella. Myös useamman aalkion voi täpätä näkyviin. Paina 'talleta' painiketta jotta saat alkion näkyviin Monitori-ikkunassa.\n\n![array_monitor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_array/array_monitor_select.png)\n\nPalaa Monitori - ikkunaan niin näet valitus luettelon arvot.\n\n![array_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/item_of_array/array_monitor.png)\n\n"},"length_of_2d_array":{"name":"length_of_2d_array","markdown":"# 2d taulukon pituus\n\nAntaa rivien ja sarakkeiden lukumäärän.  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValise 2d taulukko, jota haluat tarkastella. voit myös uudeleen nimetä tai poistaa taulukko käytöstä.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_select.png)\n\nValise mitä haluat raportoida, joko **rivit** or **sarakkeet**.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_option.png)\n\n**2d taulukon pituus** käy blokkeihin, jossa on pyöreä muuttuja tila.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_insert.png)"},"less_than":{"name":"less_than","markdown":"# Vähemmän kuin\n\nVertailuoperaattori jossa jos ensimmäinen arvo on vähemmän kuin toinen antaa totuusarvon. \n\n![less_than](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/less_than/less_than.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Vähemmän kuin** antaa Boolean arvon **tosi** jos ensimmäinen arvo on vähemmän kuin toisen arvo.\n\n**Vähemmän kuin** antaa Boolean arvon **epätosi** jos ensimmäinen arvo on ei ole vähemmän kuin toisen arvo.\n\n**Vähemmän kuin** Boolean blokki antaa Boolean arvo **tosi** or **epätosi** . Se voi laittaa kuusikulmaisiin (salmiakin muotoisiin) kohtiin blokeissa.\n\n**Vähemmän kuin** voi sisältää desimali- ja kokonaislukuja ja numeerisia blokkeja.\n\n![less_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/less_than/less_than_inserted.png)\n\nTämä esimerkki lataa **Magneetti** jos ajopelin ajosuunta on vähemmän kuin 50 astetta.\n"},"motor_is_done":{"name":"motor_is_done","markdown":"# Liike on suoritettu loppuun?\n\nKertoo onko V5 Älymoottorin liike suoritettu loppuun?.\n\n![motor_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**liike on suoritettu loppuun?** antaa Boolean arvon **tosi** kun valitun moottorin liike on suoritettu loppuun.\n\n**liike on suoritettu loppuun?** antaa Boolean arvon **epätosi** kun valitun moottori pyörii vielä.\n\nValise moottori mitä haluat käyttää.\n\n![motor_is_done_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_motor.png)\n\nT**liike on suoritettu loppuun?** Boolean blokki antaa arvot **tosi** tai **epätosi** ja sen voi sijoittaa kuusikulmaisen (salmiakkikuvion) blokin paikkióihin.\n\n![motor_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n\n"},"motor_is_spinning":{"name":"motor_is_spinning","markdown":"# Moottori pyörii\n\nKertoo pyöriikö V5 älymoottori tällä hetkellä.\n\n![motor_is_spinning](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Moottori pyörii** antaa arvon **tosi** kun moottori pyörii.\n\n**Moottori pyörii** antaa arvon **epäi** kun moottori ei pyöri.\n\nValise moottori.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning_motor.png)\n\n**Moottori pyörii** antaa Boolean arvon joko **tosi** or **apätosi**, jonka arvoa voidaan käyttää blokeissa, joissa on kuusikulmio (salmiakkikuvio) tila.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning_inserted.png)\n"},"move_pen":{"name":"move_pen","markdown":"# Aseta kynän paikka\n\nSets the position of the VEX VR Pen.\n\n![move_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/move_pen/move_pen.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValitse VR kynän paikka.\n\n![move_pen2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/move_pen/move_pen2.png)\n\n* ylös - kynä ei piirrä viivaa.\n* alas - kyvä piirtää viivaa.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä esimerkissä kynä piirtää punaisen viivan.\n\n![move_pen3](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/move_pen/move_pen3.png)\n\n"},"multiply":{"name":"multiply","markdown":"# Tulo\n\nTulo on kertolasku kahden muuttujan välillä ja antaa sen arvon.\n\n![multiply](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/multiply/multiply.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Tulo** hyväksyy deimaali- ja kokonaislukuja ja numeerisia blokkeja.\n\n**tulo** blokkin voi sijottaa blokkeissa pyöreisiin -muuttaja tilaan. \n\nTulo on kahden luvun kerolasku\n\n![multiply_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/multiply/multiply_numbers.png)\n\nKahden luvun tulo.\n\n![multiply_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/multiply/multiply_variables.png)\n\nKahden anturin antamien arvojen tulo.\n\n![multiply_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/multiply/multiply_sensors.png)\n"},"next_row":{"name":"next_row","markdown":"# Siirrä kursori seuraavalle riville\n\nSiirtää kursorin (osoittimen) Tulostus konsolin (näytön) seuraavalle riville ,kun käytät **tulosta** blokkia. \n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/next_row/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Siirrä kursori seuraavalle riville** blokki siirtää osoitimen seuraavalle riville näytöllä.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTämä esimekki tulostaa \"Hello\" ja sitten siirtää kursorin (osoittimen) seurrvalla riville ja tulostaa \"World!\"\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/next_row/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"not":{"name":"not","markdown":"# Ei\n\nAntaa vastakkaisen Boolean arvon muille  Boolean muuttujalle.\n\n![not](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/not/not.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**ei** antaa Boolean arvon **tosi** kun alkuperäisen Boolean lokin arvo on **epätosi**.\n\n\n**ei** antaa Boolean arvon **epätosi** kun alkuperäisen Boolean lokin arvo on **tosi**.\n\n**not** Boolean blokin voi sijoittaa kuusikulmaisen (salmiakin muotoisen) blokin kohtaan.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTämä esimerkki antaa totuusarvon **tosi** kun **Etäisyys anturi** ei havaitse esinettä.\n\n![not_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/not/not_sensors.png)\n\n![not_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/not/not_truth.png)  \n\n"},"object_in_front":{"name":"object_in_front","markdown":"# Esine löytyy?\n\nRaportoi kun Ultrasonic Range Finder -anturi näkee edessä esineen. \n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/object_in_front/object_in_front_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Esine löytyy** antaa Boolean arvon **tosi** jos Ultrasonic Range Finder -anturi löytää esineen kentän pinnalla näkökentässään.\n\n**Esine löytyy** antaa Boolean arvon **epätosi** jos Ultrasonic Range Finder -anturi ei löydä esinettä kentän pinnalla näkökentässään.\n\nValise haluamasi Ultrasonic Range Finder -anturi, jota käytät.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/object_in_front/object_in_front_distance.png)\n\n**Esine löytyy** Boolean antaa arvon **tosi** or **epätosi ** ´ja sitä voi käyttää kuusikulmaisien (salniakkikuvioisten) blokkien kentissä.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/object_in_front/object_in_front_inserted.png)\n "},"optical_brightness":{"name":"optical_brightness","markdown":"# Värin valoherkkyys\n\nAntaa esineen Eyes -valosensorien valoherkkyyden (kirkkausasteen) .\n\n![color_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_brightness/color_brightness.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Värin valovoimaisuus** aantaa esineen valoherkkyyden  (kirkkauden) Valkoiset esineet antaa arvon 100% ja musta antaa 0 %. Muut esineet ovat näiden arvojen välillä.\n\nValistse Eye sensori, jota haluat käyttää. FrontEye is robotin edessä ja DownEye on robotin alla ja suunta kohti lattiaa.\n\n![color_brightness_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_brightness/color_brightness_eyes.png)\n\n**Värin valoherkkyys** numeroblokki antaa arvon välillä (0,100) ja sen voi sijoittaa lausekkeena blokkeihin, joissa on pyöreä paikka blokille.\n\n![color_brightness_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_brightness/color_brightness_insert.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTämä esimerkki ajetaan **Disk Maze** pelikentällä. Robotti ajaa eteenpäin kunnes DownEye antaa alle 100% valo. Sen jälkeen see kääntyy vasemmalle ja ajaa eteenpäin.\n\n![color_brightness_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_brightness/color_brightness_example.png)\n\n## Arvojen seuranta\n\nJos haluat seurata **Värin valovherkkyys** arovja , täppää checkboxissa blokki käyttöön ko rivillä vasemmalta.\n\n![color_brightness_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_brightness/color_brightness_checkbox.png)\n\nSitten avaa Monitor ikkuna/konsoli nähdäksesi blokin arvon.\n\n![color_brightness_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_brightness/color_brightness_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_color":{"name":"optical_color","markdown":"# Värin tunnistus\n\nKertoo löytääkö Eye - anturi värin, jonka se halutaan tunnistaaaa.\n\n![color_sensing_color](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_color/color_sensing_color.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Värin tunnistus** antaa Boolean arvon **tosi** kun Eye -anturi havaitsee halutun värin.\n\n**Värin tunnistus** antaa Boolean arvon **epätosi** kun Eye -anturi ei havaitse halututtua väriä.\n\nValise Color Sensor Eye - anturi, jota haluat käyttää. FrontEye on ajopelin edessä ja näkee eteenpäin ja DownEye robotin alla ja näkee kohti pelikentän pintaa.\n\n![color_sensing_color_eye](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_color/color_sensing_color_eye.png)\n\nValise senssori,jota haluat käyttää..\n\n![color_sensing_color_color](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_color/color_sensing_color_color.png)\n\n**Värin tunnistus** Boolean blokki antaa arvon joko **tosi** tai **epätosi** ja sen voi sijoittaa toisten blokkien kuusikulmaisiin (salmiakkikuvio) paikkoihin.\n\n![color_sensing_color_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_color/color_sensing_color_shape.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkissä käytetään **Disk Maze** kenttää. Ajoneuvo menee eteenpäin kyvän kanssa kunnes se löytää halutun värin esineen. SItten se vaihtaa kynävärin ja kääntyy.\n\n![color_sensing_color_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_color/color_sensing_color_example.png)\n\n## Arvon seuranta\n\nJos haluaa seurata **color sensing** blokin arvoa, täppää checkboxia ko bloki rivillä vasemmalla.\n\n![color_sensing_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_color/color_sensing_checkbox.png)\n\nVoit avata Monitor -konsolin/ikkunan  jotta näet arvon sen näytöllä.\n\n![color_sensing_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_color/color_sensing_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_near_object":{"name":"optical_near_object","markdown":"# Lähellä esineen väri\n\nBlokki raportoi jos Color Sensor Eye on tarpeeksi lähellä esinettä tunnistamalla sen värin.\n\n![color_near_object](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_near_object/color_near_object.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Lähellä esineen väri** antaa arvon **tosi** kun värisensori tarpeeksi lähellä esinettä, jolla on havaitsemansa väriä.\n\n**Lähellä esineen väri** antaa arvon **epätosi** kun värisensori ei ole tarpeeksi lähellä esinettä, jolla on havaitsemansa väriä.\n\nValitse oikea värisenssori käyttöön. FrontEye on robotin edessä ja näkee suoraan eteen, kun taas DownEye on alhaalla ja näkee robotin alle esineet.\n\n![color_near_object_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_near_object/color_near_object_eyes.png)\n\n**lähellä esine** Boolean blokki raportoi **tosi** or **epätosi** arvoja ja sitä voidaan käyttää kuusikulmio (salmiakkikuvio) blokkien arvoina.\n\n![color_near_object_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_near_object/color_near_object_shape.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nAja tämä esimerkki **Disk Maze** pelikentällä. Ajopeli ajaa eteenpäin kunnes se havaitsee FrontEye värisensorilla esineen , jossa on kysyttyä väriä, sitten se ajaa taaksepäin.\n\n![color_near_object_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_near_object/color_near_object_example.png)\n\n## Arvojen monitorointi\n\nSaadaksesi arvot näkyviin **Lähellä esineen väri** blokista, täppää checkboxin valinta päälle.\n\n![color_near_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_near_object/color_near_object_checkbox.png)\n\nSitten avaa Minitori ikkuna nähdäksesi arvot konsolilla.\n\n![color_near_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/optical_near_object/color_near_object_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"or":{"name":"or","markdown":"# Tai\n\nLooginen vertailu operaattori \n\n![or](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/or/or.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**tai** on  **tosi** jos joku sen sitomista ehdoista on **tosi**. \n\n**tai** on **epätosi** jos molemmat sen sitomat ehdot ovat **epätosi**.\n\n**tai** Boolean blokki antaa **tosi** or **epätosi** arvon ja sopii asetettaviksa blokkeihin joissa on kuusikulmainen (salmiakki) tila.\n\n![or_sensors2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/or/or_sensors2.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\n**tai** blokki saa arvon **tosi** kun joko **Etäisyys anturi** havaitsee esineen ja/tai **FrontEye** havaitsee esineen. \n\n**tai** block saa arvon **epätosi** vain jos molemmat **Etäisyys anturi** ei havaitse esineen ja/tai **FrontEye** ei havaitse esineen.\n\n![or_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/or/or_truth.png)"},"position":{"name":"position","markdown":"# Robotin paikka\n\nRaportoi X ja Y koordinaatit robotin nykyisestä paikasta.\n\n![position_of_robot_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position/position_of_robot_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Robotin paikka** raportoi X ja Y koordinaatit joko mm tai tuumina.\n\nVoit valita kumman koordinaatin haluta ts joko X koordinaatti tai Y koordinaatti.\n\n![position_of_robot_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position/position_of_robot_coord.png)\n\nValise yksijkkö, joko mm tai tuumat.\n\n![position_of_robot_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position/position_of_robot_unit.png)\n\n**Robotin paikka** koordinaattia voi käyttää ympyrämuotoisissa blokeissa. \n\n![position_of_robot_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position/position_of_robot_inserted.png)\n\n## Arvojen monitorointi\n\nVoit ruksata **Robotin paikka**  ominaisuuden nähtäväksi täppäämällä rivi checkboxissa .\n\n![position_of_robot_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position/position_of_robot_checkbox.png)\n\nAvaa Monitori konsole ikkuna, jotta näet arvot monitorissa.\n\n![position_of_robot_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position/position_of_robot_monitor.png)\n\n"},"position_angle":{"name":"position_angle","markdown":"# Paikan kulma asteina\n\nRaportoin robotin paikan kulman asteina.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Paikan kulma asteina** robotin tänhetkisen asennon kulman.\n\n **Paikan kulma asteina** arvoa voi käyttää pyöreissä blokeissa arvona. \n\n![position_of_robot_angle_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_inserted.png)\n\n## Kulman monitorointi\n\nJos haluat monitoroida konsolilla  **Paikan kulma asteina** blokin arvoa, valitse ja täppää checkboxissa ko rivi.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_checkbox.png)\n\nAvaa Monitori -ikkuna, jossa arvot näkyvät.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_monitor.png)\n\n"},"pressing_bumper":{"name":"pressing_bumper","markdown":"# Törmäysanturi\n\nRaportoi millon törmäysanturia on painettu.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Törmäysanturi** antaa arvon **tosi** jos anturia on painettu.\n\n**Törmäysanturi** antaa arvon **epätosi** jos anturia ei ole painettu..\n\nValise törmäysanturi, jota haluat käyttää.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\n**Törmäysanturi** on Boolean muuttuja arvoina joko **tosi** tai **epätosi**. Sitä voi käyttää kuusikulmaisissa (samiakin muotoisissa) blokeissa.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Arvon monitorointi\n\nJos haluat monitoroida **Törmäysanturi** arvoa, täppää checkboxissa ko rivin käyttöön.\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nAvaaMonitori -ikkuna nähdäksesi anturin Boolean arvon.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)\n\n"},"pressing_limit":{"name":"pressing_limit","markdown":"# Raja ylitetty\n\nRaportoi onko Raja -anturia on painettu.\n\n![pressing_limit](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/pressing_limit/pressing_limit.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Raja ylitetty** raportoi **totta** jos Raja -anturia nn painettu.\n\n**Raja ylitetty** reports **false** jos Raja -anturia ei ole painettu.\n\nValise käyttämäs Eaha anturi.\n\n![pressing_limit_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/pressing_limit/pressing_limit_bumper.png)\n\n**Raja ylitetty** on Boolean blokki, joka antaa arvon **tosi** tai **epätosi** ja se voidaan sijoittaa blokkeihin , joissa on kuusikulma (salmiakki muoto) arvokenttä.\n\n![pressing_limit_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/pressing_limit/pressing_limit_inserted.png)\n "},"print":{"name":"print","markdown":"# Tulosta\n\nTulostaa numeron tai tekstin Tulostus konsolille.\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/print/look_print.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Tulosta** blokki tulostaa Monitor -ikkunaan \n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/print/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nKaikki luvut tulostetaan kokonaislukuina, elle ole aikaisemmin käytetty **Aseta tulostusmuoto** blokissa eri desimaalimäärät tulostukseen.\n\nTulostaa sanoja ja numeroita.\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/print/print_words.png)\n\nTulostaa myös blokkien arvoja.\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/print/print_variable1.png)  \n\nTulostaa laskutoimituksen tuloksen..\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/print/print_addition.png)  \n\nTulostaa myös sensorin arvoja.\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/print/print_sensor.png)\n\n\n"},"procedures_call":{"name":"procedures_call","markdown":"# Oma Blokki\n\nOma blokki toiminnolla voi luoda omia blokkeja (aliohjelmia). Eityisesti jos samaa blokkinippua kutsutaan monta kertaa , kannattaa se niputtaa Oma Blokiksi.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_call/my_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi toimia\n\n**Oma Blokki** toiminnoilla luodaan nippu blokkeja, jota voi kutsua useamman kerran ohjelmassa. \n\n**Oma Blokki** tehdään **Tee Oma blokki** painikkeella.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_call/make_a_block.png)\n\n**Oma Blokki** voi sisältää useamman välitystiedon (parametrin). Parametrien muoto voi olla tekstilabel (on vain selittävää tekstiä) , numero muuttuja tai boolean muuttuja ja näin saadaan joustavaa toiminnallisuutta omaan blokkiin.\n\nKun halua kutsua **Oma Blokki** blokkia , käytä kutsuvaa **Oma Blokki** hattublokkia (syntyi samalla kun loit Oma Blokkisi). \n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_call/make_variable.png)  \n\n\n**Oma Blokki** toiminnon sisällä voit käyttää kutsujan välittämiä parametriarvoja vetämällä parametri toimintoblokkien punaisiin pyöreisiin arvokohtiin .\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_call/trigger_function.png)\n\nKun halua kutsua **Oma Blokki** blokkia , käytä kutsuvaa **Oma Blokki** hattublokkia (syntyi samalla kun loit Oma Blokkisi). \n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_call/attach_function.png)\n\n***Huom!Luomalla Oma Blokki käyttäen samannimisiä tekstilabelejä ja muuttujia voi aiheuttaa virheen ohjelman suorituksessa pelikentällä.***\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä esimerkissä robotti tekee **Oma blokki** ohjelmassa seuraavat komennot. Kutsuva blokki antaa parametriarvon 300 mm kutsuparametrissaan.\n\n* Aja eteenpäin 300mm\n* Piirrä 300 mm neliö \n* Käänny oikealle 15 astetta\n* Vaihda kynäväri punaiseksi\n* Piirrä 400mm neliö\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_call/square_example.png)\n"},"procedures_definition":{"name":"procedures_definition","markdown":"# Oma Blokki\n\nOma blokki toiminnolla voi luoda omia blokkeja (aliohjelmia). Eityisesti jos samaa blokkinippua kutsutaan monta kertaa , kannattaa se niputtaa Oma Blokiksi.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_definition/my_block.png)\n\n## Miten toimia\n\n**Oma Blokki** toiminnoilla luodaan nippu blokkeja, jota voi kutsua useamman kerran ohjelmassa. \n\n**Oma Blokki** tehdään **Tee Oma blokki** painikkeella.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_definition/make_a_block.png)\n\n**Oma Blokki** voi sisältää useamman välitystiedon (parametrin). Parametrien muoto voi olla tekstilabel (on vain selittävää tekstiä) , numero muuttuja tai boolean muuttuja ja näin saadaan joustavaa toiminnallisuutta omaan blokkiin.\n\nKun halua kutsua **Oma Blokki** blokkia , käytä kutsuvaa **Oma Blokki** hattublokkia (syntyi samalla kun loit Oma Blokkisi). \n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_definition/make_variable.png)  \n\n\n**Oma Blokki** toiminnon sisällä voit käyttää kutsujan välittämiä parametriarvoja vetämällä parametri toimintoblokkien punaisiin pyöreisiin arvokohtiin .\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_definition/trigger_function.png)\n\nKun halua kutsua **Oma Blokki** blokkia , käytä kutsuvaa **Oma Blokki** hattublokkia (syntyi samalla kun loit Oma Blokkisi). \n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_definition/attach_function.png)\n\n***Huom!Luomalla Oma Blokki käyttäen samannimisiä tekstilabelejä ja muuttujia voi aiheuttaa virheen ohjelman suorituksessa pelikentällä.***\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä esimerkissä robotti tekee **Oma blokki** ohjelmassa seuraavat komennot. Kutsuva blokki antaa parametriarvon 300 mm kutsuparametrissaan.\n\n* Aja eteenpäin 300mm\n* Piirrä 300 mm neliö \n* Käänny oikealle 15 astetta\n* Vaihda kynäväri punaiseksi\n* Piirrä 400mm neliö\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/procedures_definition/square_example.png)\n"},"random":{"name":"random","markdown":"# Satunnaisluku\n\nAntaa satunnaisarvon alimman ja ylimmän lukuvälin alueella.\n\n![random](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/random/random.png)\n\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Satunnaisluku** blokin voi asettaa blokkeihin, joissa on paikka ympyrätilalle \n\n**Satunnaisluku** voi olla desimaaliluku, kokonaisluku tain numeerinen blokki.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä esimerkissä **Satunnaisluku** bloki antaa satunnaisluvun ajopelin nopeudeksi.\n\n![random_drivetrain](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/random/random_drivetrain.png)\n\nTässä esimerkissä **Satunnaisluku** blokki antaa satunnaisluvun Odota -bokin kestoon.\n\n![random_control](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/random/random_control.png)\n\n"},"remainder":{"name":"remainder","markdown":"# Jakojäännös\n\nJakaa ensimmäisen luvun toisella ja aantaa vastauksena jakojäännöksen laskusta.\n\n![remainder](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/remainder/remainder.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Jakojäännös** hyväksyy desimaali- ja kokonaislukuja ja numeerisia blokkeja.\n\nJos jakaa samalla luvulla itsellään jakojäännös on 0.\n\n**Jakojäännös** blokin arvon voi sijoittaa pyöreän blokin arvoksi.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nJaetaan luku 7 luvulla 2 ja jakojäännösaarvolla Vex VR robottia käännetään oikealle.\n\n![remainder_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/remainder/remainder_numbers.png)"},"repeat":{"name":"repeat","markdown":"# Toista\n\nSilmukan sisällä olet blokit toistetaan valitun kierrosmäärin.\n\n![repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/repeat/repeat.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nAnnettu arvo määrää kuinka monta **toista** kierrosta jatketaan.\n\n**Toista** muuttuja voi sisältää kokonaislukuja tai numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\nToistojen sisään voi tehdä muita toistoja. Käsitettä nimitetään termillä Nesting.\n\n![repeat_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/repeat/repeat_select.png)"},"repeat_until":{"name":"repeat_until","markdown":"# Toista kunnes\n\nToista kunnes blokki toistaa silmukkaa niin kauan että sen Boolean ehto toteutuu ts on **tosi**.  \n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/repeat_until/repeat_until.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Toista kunnes** tarkistaa vain sen Boolean ehdon toteutumista aina silmukan alussa. \n\nJos Boolean ehto on **epätosi**,niin **Toista kunnes** suorittaa uuden silmukan.\n\nJos Boolean ehto on **tosi**, niin **Toista kunnes** silmukka päättyy.\n\n**Toista kunnes** hyväksuyy kuusikulmaisia (salmiakkimuotoisia) blokkeja ehdoksi.\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/repeat_until/repeat_until_2.png)\n\n\n"},"reset_timer":{"name":"reset_timer","markdown":"# Nollaa kello\n\nNollaa Aivojen kellon.\n\n![resetTimer](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/reset_timer/resetTimer.png)\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nvojen kello käynnistyy ohjelman alusta. **Nollaa kello** blokkia käytettän kun halutaan nollata kello jossain kohtaa myöhemmin.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä kello nollataan ennnekuin ajetaan ajopelillä eteenpäin 3 sekuntia. sen jölkeen robotti pysäytetään.\n\n![resetTimer_exampleRevised](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/reset_timer/resetTimer_exampleRevised.png)"},"round":{"name":"round","markdown":"# Pyöristä\n\nPyöristää luvun haluttuun desimaaliesitykseen.\n\n![round](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/round/round.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nDesimaalit jotka ovat 0.5 tai suurempia **pyöristä** bloki pyöristää ylöspäin ja desimaalit alle 0.5 **round** alaspäin.\n\n**Pyöristä** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces, and can accept circular blocks in them as well. \n\n**Pyöristä** hyväksuu desimaali- ja kokonaislukuja sekä numeerisia blokeja.\n\n![round_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/round/round_numbers.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä luku 2.7 pyöristetään arvoon 3 ja Vex VR ajopeli ajaa eteenpäin 3 tuumaa.\n\n![round_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/round/round_variables.png)\n\n\n\n"},"set_2d_array_to":{"name":"set_2d_array_to","markdown":"# Aseta 2d taulukko\n\nAsettaa 2d taulukon alkioden arvot.\n\n![set_2d_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nKun luot 2d taulukon, voit asettaa sille 1 - 10 riviä ja saraketta.\n\nValitse taulukko, jota haluat käsitellä. Voit myös uudeleen nimetä tai poistaa taulukon.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_options.png)\n\n**aseta 2d taulukko** hyväksyy desimaali- ja kokonaislukuja sekä numeerisen blokin arkioita.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_inserted.png)\n\n"},"set_array_to":{"name":"set_array_to","markdown":"# Aseta luettelon arvot\n\nAsettaa jokaiselle luettelon alkiolle arvon.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_array_to/set_list_to.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nKun luo luettelon, pitää määritellä sen pituus välillä 1 -10.\n\nValitse luettelo, jota haluat käyttää, tai uuudelleen nimetä tai poistaa käytöstä.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_array_to/set_list_to_options.png)\n\n**Aseta luettelon arvot** sallii desimaali- ja kokonaislukuja sekä numeeristen blokien arvoja.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_array_to/set_list_to_2.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nLuettelon alkion #3 arvo asetetaan ajopelin kääntymiskulma. \n\n![set_list_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_array_to/set_list_to_example.png)\n\n"},"set_boolean_variable":{"name":"set_boolean_variable","markdown":"# Aseta boolean muuttuja\n\nAseta boolean muuttujalle arvo.\n\n![set_boolean_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValise boolean muuttuja, jota haluat käyttää. Boolean muuttujan voi myös nimetä uudelleen tai poistaa kytöstä.\n\n![set_boolean_block_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_choice.png)\n\nValise muuttuja.\n\n![set_boolean_block_value](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_value.png)\n\n**Aseta boolean muuttuja** blokiin voi asettaa kuusikulmaisen (salmiakin mutoisen) blokin.\n\n![set_boolean_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_inserted.png)"},"set_bumper":{"name":"set_bumper","markdown":"# Aseta LED törmäysanturin väri\n\nAsettaa Vex VR LED törmäysanturin värin.\n\n\n![set_bumper_color](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_bumper/set_bumper_color.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValise väri, jonka haluat näyttää.  \n\n![set_bumper_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_bumper/set_bumper_color2.png)\n\nKäännä **Aseta LED törmäysanturi** pois , niin väri sammuu.\n\nTässä esimerkissä väriksi valitaan **Aseta LED törmäysanturi** vihreäksi.\n\n![set_bumper_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_bumper/set_bumper_example.png)"},"set_drive_heading":{"name":"set_drive_heading","markdown":"# Aseta ajosuunta\n\nAsettaa nykyisen ajosuunnan kulmaarvon.\n\n![setDriveHeading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_drive_heading/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Aseta ajosuunta** voi asettaa kesken ajon Ajopelin suunnan uudelleen esimerkiski nollata ajosuunta arvolla 0.\n\n**Aseta ajosuunta** hyväksyy kaikkia arvot välillä **0 - 360** asetta.\n\n**Aseta ajosuunta** voi sisältää kokonaislukuja tai numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\n\n"},"set_drive_rotation":{"name":"set_drive_rotation","markdown":"# Aseta kiertokulma\n\nAsettaa ajopelin lähtö kiertokulmaksi halutun astemäärän.\n\n![drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_drive_rotation/drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nT**Aseta kiertokulma** blokin arvoksi voi asettaa mikä tahansa postitiivin tai negatiivinen arvo.\n\n**Aseta kiertokulma** blokki hyväksyy konaislukuja ja numeerisia blokkien arvoja.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkissä robotti kääntyy 210 astetta myötäpäivään:\n\n![turntorotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_drive_rotation/turntorotation_example.png)\n\n- käänny oikealle (myötäpäivään) 120 astetta\n- aseta kiertokulman uudeksi lähtöarvoksi -45 astetta\n- käänny oikealle (myötäpäivään) 90 degrees (-45 astetta asentoon +45 asetta) ."},"set_drive_velocity":{"name":"set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Aseta ajonopeus\n\nAsetetaan ajopelin nopeus.\n\n![set_drive_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Aseta ajonopeus** arvoksi käy prosenttivälistä **0% arvoon 100%**. Oletusnopeus on aina **50%**\n\n## EEsimerkki\n\nTEsimerkissä ajopeli ajaa nopeutta 10% .\n\n![set_drive_velocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity_example.png)"},"set_item_of_2d_array":{"name":"set_item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Korvaa 2d taulukon alkio\n\nKorvaa 2d taulukon tietty alkio .\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nAnna taulukon rivi (eka numero) ja sarake (toinen numero) jonka arvon muutatetaan.\n\nValitse taulukko, jonka arvoja halutaan muuttaa.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_options.png)\n\nThe **Korvaa 2d taulukon alkio** voi olla desimaali- tai kokonaisluku tai numeerinen blokki.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block_2.png)\n"},"set_item_of_array":{"name":"set_item_of_array","markdown":"# Korvaa luettelon alkio\n\nKorvaa luettelon valitun alkion arvon\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValitse luettelo, jota käytätää. Luettelon voi myös uudelleeen nimetä tai tuhota.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_item_of_array/replace_item_options.png)\n\n**replace item** Luetteelon alkio voi olla desimaali- tai kokonaisluku tai numeerisen blokin arvo.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block_2.png)\n"},"set_led":{"name":"set_led","markdown":"# Aseta LED\n\nLaittaa LED päälle tai pois.\n\n![set_led_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_led/set_led_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValitse LED jota käytät.\n\n![set_led_block_device](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_led/set_led_block_device.png)\n\nValitse onko LED päällä tai pois päältä.\n\n![set_led_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_led/set_led_block_setting.png)\n\n"},"set_magnet_state":{"name":"set_magnet_state","markdown":"# Lataa sähkömagneetti\n\nLataa sähkömagneetin valmiuden joko: **pidä** or **pudota**.\n\n![set_magnet_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nSelect to set that electromagnet to boost or drop\n\n* **pidä** - vetää metalliesinettä puoleensa\n* **drop** - pudottaa magneetista metalliesineen\n\n\n![set_magnet_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\nTTässä esimekrissä robotti ajaa 200 mm ja **sähkömagneetti** ottaa ja pitää esinettä ajoajan  200mm kunnes pudottaa esineen.\n\n![set_magnet_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_example.png)\n"},"set_motor_position":{"name":"set_motor_position","markdown":"# Aseta moottori asento\n\nAsettaa V5 älymoottorin encoder laskurin haluttuun arvoon.\n\n![setMotorPosition_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_motor_position/setMotorPosition_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**aseta moottori asento**blokin avulla voit muuttaa moottorin encoder arvoa halutuksi arvoksi.\n\nYleensä **Aseta moottori asento** blokissa arvo nollataan ts resetoidaan alkuasentoon.\n\nValitse V5 älymoottiri, jota haluat käyttää.\n\n![setMotorPosition_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_motor_position/setMotorPosition_motor.png)\n\nYksikkönö voi käyttää asteita tai kierroksia.\n\n![setMotorPosition_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_motor_position/setMotorPosition_unit.png)"},"set_motor_timeout":{"name":"set_motor_timeout","markdown":"# Aseta moottorin kulkuaika\n\nAsettaa kellosta V5 älymoottorin moottorin kulkuajan.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_motor_timeout/setMotorTimeout_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nMoottorin pyörimisajan ylärajan tarkoitus on estää tilanteita ettei moottori saavuta tavoiteasentoa esim mekaanisen kouran tai nostokäden esteen vuoksi. Silloin ohjelma pystyy myös jatkamaan seuraavien blokkien suoritusta.\n\n**Aseta moottorin kulkuaika** blokkiin voi asettaa desimaali- ja kokonaisluvun sekä numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä esimerkissä kulkuaika rajoitetaan 2 sekuntiin ts  **Aseta moottorin kulkuaika** arvoksi 2 sekuntti, vaikka Kouramootori ei olisi saavuttanut 270 astetta. Kun raja tai maali saavutettu Kouramoottorin asento muuttu arvoon 0.\n\n![setMotorTimeout_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_motor_timeout/setMotorTimeout_example.png)"},"set_pen_color":{"name":"set_pen_color","markdown":"# Aseta kynän väri\n\nAsettaa Vex VR kynän värin.\n\n![set_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_pen_color/set_pen_color.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää.\n\nValise kynänvärin, jota haluat käyttää.\n\n![set_pen_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_pen_color/set_pen_color2.png)\n\nEsimerkissä kyvän väriksi on valittu siniseksi\n\n![set_pen_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_pen_color/set_pen_color_example.png)\n"},"set_print_color":{"name":"set_print_color","markdown":"# Aseta tulostus väri\n\nAsettaa tulostusvärin näytölle.\n\n![set_print_color](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_print_color/set_print_color.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**aseta tulostus väri** käytetään **Tulosta** blokissa tulostusvärin asetukseen. Käytetyt värit ovat musta,punainen,sininen ja virhreä. \n\n![set_print_color_options](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_print_color/set_print_color_options.png)  \n\nKirjaimet, numerot, sensoriarvot ja muuttujien arvot voidaan tulostaa **aseta tulostus väri** blokin värillä. \n\n![set_print_color_hello](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_print_color/set_print_color_hello.png)  \n\n**tulosta** blokkia käytetään sen jälkeen kun **aseta tulostus väri** yleensä ohjelman sisällä.\n\n![set_print_color_text](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_print_color/set_print_color_text.png) "},"set_print_precision":{"name":"set_print_precision","markdown":"# Set print precision\n\nAsettaa desimaalitarkkuuden aivojen näytölle tulostettaessa.\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_print_precision/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## Miten voit käyttää\n\nValitse tarkkuus, jolla haluat tulostaa desimaaliluvun näytölle.\n\n* Kokonaislukuna (1) (Default)\n* Yhdellä desimaalilla (0.1)\n* Sadasosina (0.01)\n* Tuhannesosina (0.001)\n*  Tarkin vaihtoehto (0.000001)\n\nTulostaessa käytetään turmaalia pyöristystä. Esimerkiksi  1.67 tulostuu 2 kun kokonaislukuna ja 1.7 yhdellä desimaalilla.\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_print_precision/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_turn_velocity":{"name":"set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Aseta pyörimisnopeus\n\nAsettaa aajopelin kääntymisnopeuden.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**aseta pyörimisnopeus** asettaa vain kääntymisnopeuden. Jotta ajopeli liikkuisi., vaaditaa myös **käänny** blokki.\n\n**Aseta pyörimisnopeus** hyväksyy prosentit välilla **0% arvoon 100%**.\n\nJos nopeus asetetaan arvoon 0, ajoneuvo pysähtyy.\n\nThe **Aseta pyörimisnopeus** sallii desimaali - ja kokonaisluvut sekä numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\nTössö ajoneuvo kääntyy hitaalla 25 % nopeudella vasemmalle.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_variable":{"name":"set_variable","markdown":"# Aseta muuttuja\n\nAseta muuttua antaa muuttujalle arvon\n\n![set_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_variable/set_variable.png)\n\n## Miten voit käyttää\n\nValise muuttuja, jota haluat muuttaa. Voit myös uudelleen nimetä tai poistaa muuttujan. \n\n![set_variable_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_variable/set_variable_choose.png)\n\n**aseta muuttuja** blokkiin voi asettaa desimaali - ja kokonaisluvun ja numeerisen blokina arvon.\n\n![set_variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/set_variable/set_variable_inserted.png)\n\n"},"size_of_array":{"name":"size_of_array","markdown":"# Luettelon pituus\n\nAntaa vastauksena luettelon pituuden  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/size_of_array/size_of_array.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValise luettel, jota käytät. Luettelon voi myös uudeleen nimetä tai poistaa.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/size_of_array/size_of_array_select.png)\n\n**Luettelon pituus** voidaan sijoittaa blokkeihin, joissa pyöreämuotoinen muuttaja on sallittu.\n\n![size_of_array_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/size_of_array/size_of_array_insert.png)"},"spin":{"name":"spin","markdown":"# Pyöri\n\nV5 Äly moottori pyörii kunnes se pysäytetään.\n\n![spin_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin/spin_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**pyöri** blokki pyörittää moottoria ikuisesti kunnes seuraava moottori blokki käynnistyy tai ohjelma loppuu.\n\nValise moottori jota haluat käyttää.\n\n![spin_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin/spin_motor.png)\n\nValise suunta pyörimiselle. Oletussuuntia voi muutta Laitteet Ikkunassa, jos oletus tuntuu epäloogiselta. \n\n![spin_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin/spin_direction.png)"},"spin_for":{"name":"spin_for","markdown":"# Pyöritä matka\n\nV5 äly moottoripyörii tietyn matkan.\n\n![spin_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_for/spin_for_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Pyöritä matka** kertoo moottorille minkä matkan ja mihin suuntaan lähtöpaikasta moottori kulkee.\n\nValise moottori, jota haluat käyttää.\n\n![Pyöritä matka](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_for/spin_for_motor.png)\n\nValitse pyörimissuunta. Voit päivittää moottorin asetussuunta myös Laite ikkunasta , jos suunta ei tunnu loogiselta. \n\n![spin_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_for/spin_for_direction.png)  \n\n**Pyöritä matka** hyväksyy desimaaliluvu, kokonaisluvun ja numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\nValise käytetty yksikkö joko asteita tai kierroksia.\n\n![spin_for_value](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_for/spin_for_value.png)\n\nOletuksen ohjelma odottaa ehdolla **odota** kunnes moottori on liikkunut loppuun matkaan.\n\nVoit valita myös option  **ja älä odota** . SIlloine jatketaan ohjelman suoritusta seuraavasta blokista pyörimisen aikana.\n\n![spin_for_dont_wait_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_for/spin_for_dont_wait_block.png)"},"spin_to_position":{"name":"spin_to_position","markdown":"# Pyöri asentoon\n\nV5 älymoottori pyörii halutuun asentoon.\n\n![spin_to_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_to_position/spin_to_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Pyöri asentoon** pyörittää moottoria tiettyyn asentoon alkuperäisestä lähtöasennosta.**Pyöri asentoon** huomioi suunnan nykyisestä asennosta suhteessa alkuperäiseen.\n\nValitse moottori jota käytät.\n\n![spin_to_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_to_position/spin_to_motor.png)\n\n**Pyöri asentoon** blokki hyväksyy desimaali- ja kokonaisluvut sekä numeeristen blokkien arvot.\n\nTksikkönö käy asteet ja kierrokset.\n\n![spin_to_value](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_to_position/spin_to_value.png)\n\nOletuksena odotetaan koko pyörimisen **odota** loppuun ennen seuraavan blokin suoritusta.\n\nVoit optiona valita **älä odota** - jolloin suoritetaan seuraava blokki samaan ainaakn kun moottori pyörii.\n\n![spin_to_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/spin_to_position/spin_to_anddontwait.png)"},"stop_driving":{"name":"stop_driving","markdown":"# Pysähdy\n\nPysäyttää ajopelin.\n\n![stopDriving_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/stop_driving/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**pysähdy** blokki pysäyttää ajopelin liikkumisen.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nAjopeli pysäytetään 3 sekunnin kuluttua.\n\n![stopDriving_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/stop_driving/stopDriving_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_motor":{"name":"stop_motor","markdown":"# Pysäytä moottori\n\nPysäyttää moottorin pyörimisen.\n\n![stopMotor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/stop_motor/stopMotor_block.png)\n\n## Miten voit käyttää\n\nValitse moottori, jonka haluat pysäyttää.\n\n![stop_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/stop_motor/stop_motor.png)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkissä moottori pyörii 3 sekuntia ennen pysähtymistä\n\n![stopMotor_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/stop_motor/stopMotor_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_project":{"name":"stop_project","markdown":"# Stop project\n\nStops a running project.\n\n![stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/stop_project/stop_project.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **stop project** block will cause a running project to stop. \n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the VR Robot in a 500mm square and then stop the project.\n\n![stop_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/stop_project/stop_project2.png)"},"subtract":{"name":"subtract","markdown":"# Vähännä\n\nTekee kahden luvun vähennyslaskun.\n\n![subtract](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/subtract/subtract.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**vähännä** hyväksyy numero desimaali ja kokonaisluku muodot ja myös numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\n**vähännä** blokki hyväksyy numeroiden lisäksi pyöreät bloki muodo blokit. \n\nVähennyslasku käyttää numeroita.\n\n![subtract_example1](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/subtract/subtract_example1.png)\n\nVähennyslasku käyttää muuttujan arvoa.\n\n![subtract_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/subtract/subtract_example2.png)  \n\nVähennyslasku käyttää sensorin arvoa.\n\n![subtract_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/subtract/subtract_example3.png)"},"timer_value":{"name":"timer_value","markdown":"# Kello\n\nAntaa ajan sekunteina ohjelman alusta.\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/timer_value/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nKello lähtee aika käyntiin arvosta 0 kun ohjelma käynnistyy ja antaa aina kuluneen ajan suorituksen alusta desimaalilukuna.\n\nArvon saa  **kello sekunteina** is used inside of blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/timer_value/timerValue_inserted.png)\n\n## Kellon seuranta\n\nVoit käyttää ehtolauseissa kelloa antamaa arvoa \n\n![timerValue_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/timer_value/timerValue_checkbox.png)\n\nKello kulunut aika näkyy samalla myös VR monitorissa\n\n![timerValue_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/timer_value/timerValue_monitor.png)\n\n"},"turn":{"name":"turn","markdown":"# Käänny\n\nAjopeli kääntyy ikuisesti\n\n![turn_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn/turn_block.png)\n\n## Kuinka voi käyttää\n\n **käänny** blokki kääntää ajopeliä ikuisesti, voidaan käyttää silmukassa niin että joku ehto pysäyttää kääntymisen.\n\nBlokissa valitaan suunta, mihin ajopeli kääntyy.\n\n![turn_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn/turn_direction.png)"},"turn_for":{"name":"turn_for","markdown":"# Käänny astetta\n\nAjopeli kääntyy määrätyn määrän asteita haluttuun suuntaan.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_for/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValiset suunta johon käännytään.\n\n![turn_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_for/turn_for_direction.png)  \n\n\nAseta astemäärä kuinka paljon ajopeli kääntyy.\n\n**käänny astetta** blokki voi sisältää desimaaleja, kokonaisluvun tai numeerisen arvon blokkeja.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_for/turn_for_block_2.png)\n\nOletuksena  **odota kunnes** odottaa käännöksen loppuun.\n\nVoit valita nuolesta myös valinta **ja älä odota** , jolloin suoritetaan seuraava blokki projektissa.\n\n![turn_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_for/turn_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"turn_to_heading":{"name":"turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Käänny kulkusuunnasta\n\nKääntää ajopelin kulkusuunnasta annettuun astesuuntakohtaan ympyrän kehällä 0-360 astetta myötäpäivään kiertäen. \n\n![turn_to_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Käänny kulkusuunnasta** blokilla ajopeli kääntyy annettuun astesuuntaan ympyrän kehän pisteeseen 0-360 astetta nykyisestä kulkusuunnasta.\n\n**Käänny kulkusuunnasta** anneetu astemäärä määrää suunnan gyron avulla itse mihin kääntyy.\n\n**Käänny kulkusuunnasta** hyväksyy vain asteluvut välillä **0.00 arvoon 359.99** astetta.\n\n**Käänny kulkusuunnasta** blokki hyväksyy desimaaliluvun, kokonaisluvun tai numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkissä ajopeli tekee 4 käännöstä: \n\n![turn_to_heading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\n- Oikealle kohtaan 45 astetta\n- Oikealle kohtaan 90 astetta\n- Oikealla kohtaan 270 astetta\n- Vasemmalle kohtaan 180 astetta\n\nOletuksena seuraavat blokit  **odota** odottaa kunnes ajopeli on kääntynyt.\n\nVoit valita myös  **ja älä odota** - jolloin ajopeli suorittaa blokin jälkeiset toiminnot käännöksen aikana.\n\n![turn_to_heading_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_anddontwait.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"turn_to_rotation":{"name":"turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Käänny asentoon \n\nAjopeli kääntyy halututun astemäärän verran Gyro -anturin mukaan alkuperäisestä kulkusuunnasta ajon alussa. \n\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_iq.png)\n\n## Miten voidaan käyttää\n\n**Käänny asentoon** ajopeli kääntyy vastapäivään, jos  annettu - asteita ja myötäpäivään, jos annettu + asteita.\n\nKääntyminen perustuu ajopelin lähtöasentoon ja **kiertokulma** muuttujaan asetettuun arvoon ja  **käänny astentoon** etumerkin mukaiseen kääntymissuuntaan\nSyöttöarvoon **käänny asentoon ** voi syöttää numeron, kokonaisluvun tai muun numeroblokin arvon\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTässä esimerkissä ajopeli tekee 4 käännöstä \n\n![turn_to_rotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_to_rotation/turn_to_rotation_example.png)\n\n- Oikealle (myötäpäivään)  90 astetta\n- Oikealle (myötäpäivään)  180 astetta\n- Vasemmalle (vastapäivään)  -45 asetta\n- Oikeall (myötäpäivään)  0 astetta\nOletuksena  **odota** kunnes ajopeli on kääntynyt loppuu.\n\nJa jos valitset  **ja älä odota** - aiheuttaa seuraavien blokkien suorituksen käännöksen aikana.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq.png)"},"variable":{"name":"variable","markdown":"# Oma Muuttuja\n\nMuuttuja on numeerinen nimetty ohjelman muistipaikka. \n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/variable/variable.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Oma Muuttuja** blokkia käytetään määrittämään nimetty muistipaikka, joka on aina numeerinen arvo. Sitä voi käyttää ohjelman ajan. Sen nykyinen arvo näkyy **Oma muuttuja** blokilla tai asettamalla siihen jonkun anturin antama arvo.\n\n![variable_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/variable/variable_cursor.png)\n\n**Oma Muuttuja** blokin arvoa voidaan sijoittaa \n\n![variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/variable/variable_inserted.png)\n\nMuuttuja luodaan **Tee muuttuja** painikkeella.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/variable/make_variable.png)  \n\n## Muuttujarvon käyttö \n\nValise muuttaja täppäämällä sen nimen vasempaa valintakohtaa \n\n![variable_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/variable/variable_checkbox.png)\n\nKun avaat Monitori -ikkunan, voit seurata **Oma Muuttuja** arvoa.\n\n![variable_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/variable/variable_monitor.png)\n\n"},"wait":{"name":"wait","markdown":"# Odota\n\nOdota blokilla saat ohjelman odottamaan halutun ajan \n\n![wait](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/wait/wait.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValitse haluamasi odotusaika **odota**.\n\n**odota** blokkiiin voit lattaa desimaali, kokonaislukuvun tai numeerisen blokin arvon.\n\n![wait_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/wait/wait_select.png)\n\n\n"},"wait_until":{"name":"wait_until","markdown":"# Odota kunnes\n\nAsettaa Boolean ehdon suorituksen jatkumiseen \n\n![wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/wait_until/wait_until.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Odota kunnes** tarkistaa jatkuvasti Boolean ehtonsa toteutumista. Projekti jatkaa seuraavan blokin suoritukseen vasta kun se toteutuu ts arvo on  **tosi**.\n\n**Odota kunnes** Boolean blokin ehdoksi voi sijoittaa kuusikulma (salmiakkimuodon) blokin arvon.\n\n![wait_until_2](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/wait_until/wait_until_2.png)\n\n"},"when_brain_screen":{"name":"when_brain_screen","markdown":"# Kun näyttöä \n\nSuoritetaan valitut blokit ehdon sisällä.\n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_brain_screen/when_brain_pressed.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Kun näyttöä** V5 koskettaa voi laukaista halututtujen blokkien toiminnan.  \n\nYhdessä näytön ominaisuuden **näytön asema** kanssa käytettynä voi tarkentaa mitä missäkin kohtaan näyttöä koskettaessa eri blokeilla tapahtuu.\n\n Toiminnon käynnistämiseen valitaan kumpi tapahtumista painonappia **painettu** or **vapautettu** valitaan\n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_brain_screen/when_brain_pressed_condition.png)"},"when_broadcasted":{"name":"when_broadcasted","markdown":"# Kun saan viestin\n\nKäynnistää toiminnon blokit kun määritelty sanoma on **lähetä viesti**\n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_broadcasted/when_broadcast.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nMäärittele sanoma jota kuuunnellaan . Myös uusi sanoma voidaan luoda sen jälkeen.\n\n![when_broadcast_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_broadcasted/when_broadcast_select.png)\n\n **Kun saan viestin** blokki käynnistyy vain kun se on viritetty **lähetä viesti** or **lähetä viesti ja odota** eri kutsublokeista."},"when_bumper":{"name":"when_bumper","markdown":"# Kun törmää\n\nSuorittaaa valitut blokit kun törmäysanturia on painettu tai se vapautuu\n\n![when_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_bumper/when_bumper.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nValise haluamasi Törmäysanturi\n\n![when_bumper_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_bumper/when_bumper_select.png)\nValitse toiminnot mitkä haluat suorítettavan kun anturia on  **painettu** or **vapautettu** \n\n![when_bumper_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_bumper/when_bumper_condition.png)\n"},"when_limit":{"name":"when_limit","markdown":"# Kun rajakytkin\n\nSuorittaa toiminnon alta blokit kun kytkintä on painettu  \n//Runs the attached stack of blocks when the selected Limit Switch sensor is pressed or released.\n\n![when_limit](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_limit/when_limit.png)\n\n## Miten voi käytettää\n\nValitse ensin mitä rajakytkintä käytät\n\n![when_limit_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_limit/when_limit_select.png)\nValise liipaisu joka käynnistää tapahtuman - **painettu** or **vapautettu** -\n\n![when_limit_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_limit/when_limit_condition.png)\n"},"when_started":{"name":"when_started","markdown":"# Kun ohjelma käynnistyy\n\n\nKun projektin suoritus käynnistyy niin tämän käynnistysblokin kaikki alla olevat komentoblokit suoritetaan.\n//Runs the attached stack of blocks when the project is started.\n\n![when_started](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_started/when_started.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\n**Kun ohjelma käynnistyy** tapahtuma suoritetaan **käynnistä** nappia painamalla Vexcode VR Blocks -ohjelmassa.\n\nKaikki uudet projektit sisältävät automaattisesti **Kun ohjelma käynnistyy** -blokin.\n"},"when_timer":{"name":"when_timer","markdown":"# Kun kello\n\nSuorittaa silmukan blokkeja niin kauan kun V5 Aivojen kello on enemmän kuin annettu arvo \n\n![when_timer](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_timer/when_timer.png)\n\n## Miten voi käyttää\n\nAivojen kello käynnistyy aina kun projektin alusta.\n\nValitse aikamäärä, jonka jälkeen **when timer** tapahtuma suoritetaan kerran kun aivojen kello on enemmän kuin **greater than** .\n\n![when_timer_select](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/when_timer/when_timer_select.png)\n\n**Kun kello** aikaan käy sekä desimaali että kokonaisluvut.\n\n**Kun kello** blokki ei voi **ei voi** hyväksyä muita raportoituja blokkeja."},"while":{"name":"while","markdown":"# Kun voimassa\n\nToistorakenne, joka toistuu niin kauan kuin Boolean ehto on voimassa **true**.\n  \n\n![while](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/while/while.png)\n\n## Miten voi käytettää\n\n**Kun voimassa** blokki tarkistaa vain Boolean ehdon toiston alussa \n\nJos Boolean ehto on voimassa **totta**, rakenteen **epätosi** sisällä olevat blokit suoritetaan\n\nJos Boolean ehto ei ole voimassa **epätosi**, hypätään suorittaan rakenteen **Kun voimassa** jälkeisiä blokkeja. \n\n\n **Kun voimassa** toistorakenne voi sisältää vain kuusikulmaisia (salmiakkimallisia) ehtoja\n\n![while_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/fi/while/while_inserted.png)"}},"python":{"control_break":{"name":"control_break","markdown":"# Keskeytä\n\nAiheuttaa luupista poistumisen heti.\n\n```don\nbreak\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nKun `break` (keskeytä) -komento on jossain kohtaan luuppia, se aiheuttaa luupista postumisen seuraavaan komentoonn luupin jälkeen. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa ajopeli ajaa eteenpäin kunnes Silmä havaitsee esineen lähellä.  Jos Boolean ehto on tosi, break -komento toteutuu ja siirrytään luupista seuraavaan komentoon, joka keskeyttää ajopelin liikkumisenJo.\n\n```don\nwhile True:\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if front_eye.near_object():\n            break\n    drivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"break"},"control_for":{"name":"control_for","markdown":"# Toista kertaa\n\nToistetaan komennot halutun kertamäärän.\n\n```don\nfor value in range(10):\n    pass\n\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nToistorakenteita käytetään toistamaan koodiryhmä halutun määrän. Toistomäärä kerrotaan range() funktiossa tai listalla/luettelolla\n\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa piirretään 500 millimetrin musta neliö pelikentälle toistorakenteella for repeat_count ja range() -funktiossa on annettu toistokerta 4.  \n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n\tdrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 500,MM)\n\tdrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nThe variable repeat_count was used to replace value.\n\nOhjelma käyttää muuttujaa y iteraattorina ja luetteloa (myList) kertoo montako kertaa iteraatio toistetaan. \n\n```don\nmyList = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", â€˜100%', \"fun!!\"]\nfor y in myList:\n\tbrain.print(y)\n\tbrain.new_line()\n\twait(5, MSEC)\n```\n\nTämä ohjelma tulostaa:\n\n```don\nVEXcode VR\nis\n100%\nfun!!\n```\n\nHuomaa että jos toistorakenne sisältää kaksoispisteen : lopussa, niin se on merkki Pythonille suorittaa sen jälkeiset komennot luupissa","signature":"for value in range (10"},"control_if":{"name":"control_if","markdown":"# Jos\n\nOhjauskomennossa suoritetaan ryhmäkomentoja jos tarkasteluehto on **True** (tosi)\n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\n    \n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nJos -komento siältää Boolean ehdon, jota tarkastelaan.\n\nJos Boolean ehto on tosi, Jos -komennon sisällä olevat komennot suoritetaan.\n\nJos Boolean ehto on epätosi, Jos -komennon yli hypätään seuraavaan komentoon.\n\n## Esimerkit\n\nOhjelmassa käytetään Boolean ehtoa Kun  ensiksi ajamaan ajopeliä 5 sekuntia. Ajon aikana kynä piirtää viivaa. Kun haarassa oleva Jos-haaran Boolean ehto muutttaa kynävärin puneiseksi kun 2.5 sekonyia on kulunut. \n\n```don\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.timer_reset()\n    while brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if brain.timer_time(SECONDS) > 2.5:\n            pen.set_pen_color(RED)\n       wait(5, MSEC)\n    drivetrain.stop()\n```\nOdota -komento on tarpeellinen toistorakenteissa.  VEXcode VR's selainarkkitehtuurissa pieni odotus -komento (5 millisekuntia) tarvitaan, että ohjelma kerkiää kommunikoida Pelikentällä piirtämisen/liikkumisen kanssa. \n\nHuomaa että Jos-komento sisältää lopuussa kaksoispisteen (:). Se kertoo Python-k'ääntäjälle että sitä seuraa useampi komento alla.\n\nOhjelmassa Jos -komennon Boolean ehdot määräävät, mitä robotti tekee eri ehdoilla Etusilmän värihavaintojen perusteella.  \n\n```don\n while True:\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        if front_eye.detect(NONE):\n            wait(5, MSEC)\n            drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if front_eye.detect(GREEN):\n            wait(5, MSEC)\n            drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\n        if front_eye.detect(BLUE):\n            wait(5, MSEC)\n            drivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nOdota-komento pitää aina olle toistorakenteissa. \n\nJos Jos-ehdon lopussa on kaksoispiste (.), sen jälkeen tulee useampi komento, jotka pitää Jos- haarassa suorittaa","signature":"if condition:\\n\\tpass"},"control_if_else":{"name":"control_if_else","markdown":"# Jos niin muuten\nOhjausrakenne suorittaa komennot Jos -haarasta, jos Boolean -ehto on **True** (tosi) tai Muuten-haarasta jos Boolean -ehto on **False** (epätosi)\n\n```don\n\nif condition:\n    pass\nelse:\n    pass\n\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nJos niin Muuten -kometo käyttää Boolean muuttujaan ehdossa.  \n\nJos Boolean -ehto on **True** (tosi), Jos- haaran komennot suoritetaan.\n\nJos Boolean -ehto on **False** (epätosi), Muuten- haaran komennot suoritetaan.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa käytetään Jos niin Muuten komentoa, ja ajopeli liikkuu Silmä-sensorin tietojen perusteella.  Jos Silmä -sensori havaitsee esineen, niin robotti ajaa eteenpäin, muuten robotti kääntyy.  Ohjelma käyttää Linnan rauniot -pelikenttää.\n\n```don\nwhile True:\n        if distance.found_object(): \n            drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        else:\n            drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nJos niin muuten -komennossa Boolean ehtoa tutkitaan vain kerran. Käytetään toistorakennetta Kun, niin Jos niin muuten -tarkastelu voidaan tehdä jatkuvasti.  \n\nMuista käyttää Odota-komentoa toistorakenteissa (20 ms), jotta pelikentän näyttö pysyy mukana.\n\nJos Jos niin muuten -komennoissa on kaksoispiste lopussa, haaaran alla olevat komennot suoritetaan kaikki.","signature":"if conditio"},"control_stop_project":{"name":"control_stop_project","markdown":"# Pysäytä ohjelma\n\nLopettaa ohjelman suorituksen.\n\n```don\nstop_project()\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nKomento lopettaa ohjelman suorituksen.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelma piirtää 500 mm neliön pelikentälle mustalla värillä.  Pysäytä-komento päättää ohjelman suorituksen kun neliö on valmis.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 500,MM)\n\tdrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\nstop_project()\n```","signature":"stop_project()"},"control_wait":{"name":"control_wait","markdown":"# Odota\n\nOhjelma odottaa annetun sekuntim,määrän ennen kuin siirrytään seuraavaan komentoon.\n\n```don\nwait(TIME, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nAseta sekuntimäärä komentoon. Määrä voi olla millisekunteina tai sekunteina.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOjhelmassa ajopeli pysähtyy , kunnes se on ajanut 3 sekuntia eteenpäin.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\nParametrit aina isoilla kirjaimilla.","signature":"wait(1, SECONDS)"},"control_wait_until":{"name":"control_wait_until","markdown":"# Kun ei\n\nKun ei -komento suorittaa toistuvasti blokin komentoja niin kauan kuin ehto on **True** (tosi).  Kun ei -luuppi vastaa Odota kunnes-komentoa Blocks o-hjelmoinnissa. \n\nThis allows a project to continually check the condition of the sensor and block the flow of a project until that sensor condition is met.  \n\n\n```don\nwhile not condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nKoska Python ei sisällä Odota kunnes-komentoa (joka löytyy VEXcode blocks ohjelmasta), Kun ei komento korvaa sen .  Kun ei A while not loop in Python can be used similarly to a wait until block in Python.  \n\n\n## Esimerkki:\n\nKummassakin esimerkissä ajopeli ajaa eteenpäin kunnes Painokytkintä on painettu.\n\n\n\n```don\nwhile left_bumper.pressed() == False:\n    drive.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"while not expressio"},"control_while":{"name":"control_while","markdown":"# Kun\nLuuppi toistetaan niin kauan kun Booolean -ehto on **true** (tosi).   \n\n```don\nwhile condition:\n   pass\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nKun -luuppi käyttää Boolean ehtoa.  \n\nKun -luuppi tarkistaa Booolean -ehdon aina luupin alussa.\n\nJos Boolean -ehto on tosi TRUE, luupin sisällä olevat komennot suoritetaan ennen seuraavaa tarkistusta.  \n\nJos Boolean -ehto on epätosi FALSE, ohitetaan luupin sisällä olevat komennot ja siirrytään ohjelman seuraavaan komentoon.  \n\n\n## Esimerkit\n\nOhjelma käyttää Boolean -ehtoa , kun ajopeli ajaa eteenpäin kun Etäisyysanturin antama arvo on isompi kuin 50 millimetriä.  \n\n```don\nwhile distance.get_distance(MM)>50:\n    drive.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nOhjelmaassa on Kun -luupissa Boolean -ehto ja ajopeli ajaa eteenpäin kunnes Painokytkintä on painettu.\n\n```don\nwhile right_bumper.pressed() == False:\n       drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n       wait(5,MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nOdota -kometo pitää aina olla luupin sisällä (5 millisekuntia) Pelikentän- päivityksen vuoksi.\n\nJos Kun -haaran rivin lopussa on kaksoispiste (:), kaikki komennot sen jälkeen suoritetaan.","signature":"while expressio"},"drivetrain_drive":{"name":"drivetrain_drive","markdown":"# Aja\n\nLiikuttaa ajopeliä ikuisesti sulkujen sisällä parametrin osoittamaan suuntaan.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.drive()` komento ajaa ajopeliä ikuisesti, kunnes uusi ajo -komento suoritetan tai ohjelma päättyy.\n\nOletuksen ajopelin nopeus komennossa `drivetrain.drive()` on 50%.\n\n## Esimerkit\n\nTämä esimerkki liikuttaa ajopeliä eteenpäin ikuisesti.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nTäss ajopeli liikkuu takaisinpäin ikuisesti.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(REVERSE)\n```\n\nHuomaa että parametrit kirjoitetaan isoilla kirjaimilla","signature":"drivetrain.drive(FOWARD)"},"drivetrain_drive_for":{"name":"drivetrain_drive_for","markdown":"# Aja matka\n\nAjaa ajopeliä tietyn matkan haluttuun suuntaan.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(DIRECTION, distance, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nAseta matka ja suunta ajopeli liikkuu:\n\n- Direction - suunta joko `FORWARD` (eteenpäin) tai `REVERSE` (taaksepäin)\n- Distance - matka numeroarvona\n- Units - yksikkö joko `MM`(millimetri) or `INCHES` (tuumaa)\n\n## Esimerkki\nEsimerkissä ajopeli liikkuu 200 millimetriä eteenpäin.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n```\n\nKomento aiheuttaa ajopelin liikkuvan 150 tuumaa taaksenpäin.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 150, INCHES)\n```\n\nHuomaa että parametri on kirjoitettava isoilla kirjaimilla.","signature":"drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)"},"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Aseta ajopelin nopeus\n\nAsettaa ajopelin nopeuden, jota käytetään `drivetrain.drive` ja `drivetrain.drive_for` komennoissa. \n\nJos komentoa käytetään ajopelin ajon aikana, niin nopeus päivittyy uudeksi nopeudeksi kesken ajon.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()` hyväksyy arvot välillä 0% - 100%. Oletuksena nopeus on 50%.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkisä ajopelia jaa eteenpäin 200 millimetriä nopeudella 25%\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(25, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n```\nParametrit pitää kirjoittaa aina isoilla kirjaimilla","signature":"drivetrain.set_drive_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_set_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_set_heading","markdown":"# Aseta ajosuunta\n\nAsettaa ajopelin Gyrolle ajosuunnan arvon.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_heading(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.set_heading()` komennolla voidaan muuttaa ajopelin nykyinen ajosuunnan arvo. Voit resetoida arvon asettamalla arvoksi 0.\n\n`drivetrain.set_heading()` kometo hyväksyy arvot välillä **0 - 360** astetta.","signature":"drivetrain.set_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_set_rotation","markdown":"# Aseta ajopelin kääntymiskulma\n\nAsettaa ajopelin kääntymiskulmalle uuden arvon VEX IQ Gyro Sensorissa.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.set_rotation()` komennolla voi ajopelin kääntymiskulmaa asettaa mihin postiiviseen tai negatiiviseen kääntymiskulmaan haluaa.","signature":"drivetrain.set_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Aseta kääntymisnopeus\n\nAsettaa ajopelin kääntymisnopeuden. \n\nJos asetus tehdään kesken kääntymisen nopeus päivitetään ajon aikana.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.set_turn_velocity()` hyväksyy arvot välillä 0% - 100%. Oletus on 50%.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkissä ajopeli kääntyy oikealla 90 astetta nopeudella 25%\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(25, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```\n\nParametrit kirjoitetaan aina isoilla kirjaimilla","signature":"drivetrain.set_turn_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_stop":{"name":"drivetrain_stop","markdown":"# Pysähdy\n\nPysäyttää ajopelin.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nThe `drivetrain.stop()` komento pysäyttää ajopelin. `drivetrain.stop()` komento ei vaadi parametria sulkujen sisään.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkissä VEX IQ robotti liikkuu eteenpäin 3 sekuntia ja sen jälkeen pysähtyy.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nParametrit kirjoitetaan aina isoilla kirjaimilla.","signature":"drivetrain.stop()"},"drivetrain_turn":{"name":"drivetrain_turn","markdown":"# Käänny\n\nKääntää ajopeliä ikuisesti vasemmalle tai oikealle.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`Drivetrain.turn` komento kääntää ajopeliä ikuisuuden kunnes uusi ajopelikomento suoritetaan tai ohjelma loppuu.\n\nKäytä **left** (vasemmalle) argumenttia, kun ajopeli kääntyy vasemmalle.\n\n##Esimerkit\n\nTämä komento kääntää ajopeliä oikealle ikuisesti.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n```\nTämä komento kääntää ajopeliä vasemmalle ikuisesti.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(LEFT)\n```\n\nHuomaa että rparametri kirjoitetaan isoilla kirjaimiolla.","signature":"drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)"},"drivetrain_turn_for":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_for","markdown":"# Käänny määrän\n\nKääntää ajopelin tietyn kulman haluttuun suuntaan.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(DIRECTION, ANGLE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nAseta `direction` (suunta) ja määrä, jonka ajopeli kääntyy. Suuntavalinnat ovat **left** (vasempaan) tai **right** (oikeaan) ja määrä annetaan asteina.\n\n\n- Direction - (suunta) joko RIGHT tai LEFT\n- Angle - kulma numeerinen arvo\n- Units - DEGREES (astetta)\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nKomento kääntää ajopeliä oikealle 90 astetta.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```\nKomento kääntää ajopeliä vasemmalle 90 astetta.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Käänny ajosuuntaan\n\nKääntää ajopeliä tiettyyn ajosuuntaan VEX IQ Gyron avulla.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading(ANGLE, UNITS)`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`Drivetrain.turnToHeading` komentoa voi käyttää, kun kääntää ajopeliä tiettyyn vastapäiväiseen ajosuuntaan kuten kuvassa.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` hyväksyy arvot väliltä **0.00 - 359.99** ajosuunta parametriksi.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` hyväksyy desimaali- ja kokonaislukuja. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nAjopeli kääntyy oikealla 45 astetta\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(45, DEGREES)\n```\nAjopeli kääntyy vasemmalle 45 astetta\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(315, DEGREES)\n```\nParametrit kirjoitrtaan aina isoilla kirjaimilla.","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Käännä kiertokulmaa\n\nKääntää ajopelin kiertokulmaa VEX IQ Gyro Sensorin avulla.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(ANGLE, DEGREES)`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_rotation` komentoa voi käyttää kääntämään ajopeliä tiettyyn asentoon , positiiviseen (vastapäivään) tai negatiiviseen (myötäpäivään) arvoon.\n\nPerustuu ajopelin nykyiselle kääntymiskulmalle, `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation` määrittää mihin suuntaan käännytään.\n\n`Kääntymiskulmaa` parametria **ei** ole rajattu arvoihin välillä **0 - 359.99 astetta**. Käännös on absoluuttinen ja kääntää robottia useammin kuin kerran jos tarvis.\n\n## \n\nTämä komento kääntää ajopeliä 90 astetta.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES)\n```\n\nTämä komento kääntää ajopelin asentoa -45 asetta\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(-45, DEGREES)\n```\n\nParametrit pitää kirjoittaa isoilla kirjaimilla.","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"function_name":{"name":"function_name","markdown":"# Funktiot\n\nFunktiot ovat kokoelma koodia, jota voi kutsua useammin.\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nAnna funktiollesi kuvaava nimi, joka kertoo sen toiminnan tarkoituksen.\n\nKäytä **keyword** avaintekstiä  `def` ennen nimeä, jolla saat määriteltyä funktion olemassaolon.\n\nLisää `()` parametreille, nimeä jokainen. Eroita useampi parametri pilkulla `(parameter1, parameter2)`. Kirjoita funktion komennot merkin `:` jälkeen riveille.\n\nKomentojen alkupaikka riveillä pitää olla aina 4 tyhjälyönnin jälkeen. Jos funktiolle ei ole komentoka, kirjoita sana 'pass` funktion määrityksessä.\n\n```don\ndef my_function():\n    pass\n```\n\n## Esimerkki 1\n\nTällä funktiolla ei ole parametreja ja se kirjoittaa sanan \"Hello\" Tulostus konsolille.\n\n```don\ndef print_hello_world():\n    brain.print(\"Hello World\")\n```\n## Esimerkki 2\n\nTässä funktiossa on yksi parametetri `name` ja se kirjoittaa sille välitetyn sisällön `name` Tulostus konsolille.\n\n```don\ndef print_name(name):\n    brain.print(name)\n```","signature":"def function_name(parameters):\\n\\tpass"},"looks_brain_clear":{"name":"looks_brain_clear","markdown":"# Tyhjää kaikki rivit\n\nTyhjää Tulostus-konsolin kaikki rivit.\n\n```don\nbrain.clear()\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`brain.clear` komento tyhjää Tulostus-konsolin kaikki rivit. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTOhjelmassa tulostetaan sana Hello yhden sekunnin ajan ja tyhjätään Tulostus-konsoli ja tulostetaan sana Goodbye. \n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nbrain.clear()\nbrain.print(\"Goodbye\")\n```","signature":"brain.clear()"},"looks_brain_new_line":{"name":"looks_brain_new_line","markdown":"# Siirrä kursori uudelle riville\n\nAsettaa kursorin seuraavalle riville Tulostus-konsolilla.\n\n```don\nbrain.new_line()\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nbrain.new_line() komento asettaa kursorin Tulostus-konsolissa seuraavalle riville. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTulostetaan sana Drive, ja siirretään kursori seuraavalle riville ja tulostetaan sana Forward.\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Drive\")\nbrain.new_line()\nbrain.print(\"Forward\")\n```","signature":"brain.new_line()"},"looks_brain_print":{"name":"looks_brain_print","markdown":"# Tulosta\n\nTulostaa lukuja tai tekstiä Tulostus-konsolille.\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`brain.print(“HELLO”)` komento tulostaa dataa (sanoja ja nmeroita) Tulostus -konsolille. Oletuksena on Tulostus -konsolin ensimmäinen rivi.\n\n## Esimerkkejä\n\nTulostetaan teksti VEX.  Kun tulostetaan numeroita tai tekstiä, tulostettava teksti pitää olla heittomerkeissä (joko yksinkertaiset (‘) tai tupla (“)).\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"VEX\")\n```\n\nTTulostetaan teksti VEX.  Jos tulostetaan muuttuja-arvoja, niitä ei tarvitse eikä pidä laittaa heittomerkkeihin, ainoastaan sulkujen sisään.  \n\n```don\nmy_variable = \"VEX\"\nbrain.print(my_variable)\n```","signature":"brain.print(\"Hello\")"},"looks_brain_set_print_color":{"name":"looks_brain_set_print_color","markdown":"# Aseta tulostus väri\n\nAsetttaa tulostksen tekstin värin Tulostus -konsolille.\n\n```don\nbrain.set_print_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nValitse tulsotusväri:\n\n- BLACK (musta)\n- RED (punainen)\n- GREEN (vihreä)\n- BLUE 8sininen)\n\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nTulostetaan sana `GO!` vihreällä ja sana `STOP!` punaisella Tulostus-konsolille.\n\n```don\nset_print_color(GREEN)\nbrain.print(\"GO!\")\nset_print_color(RED)\nbrain.print(\"STOP!\")\n```","signature":"brain.set_print_color(BLACK)"},"looks_monitor_sensor":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor","markdown":"# Monitoroi Sensorin arvo\n\nMonitor sensorin arvo -kometoa käytetään , että Monitori-ikkunassa nähdään halutun sensorin antama arvo. \n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nJos haluat nähdä jonkun sensorin antama arvo Monitori-ikkunassa, käytä `monitor_sensor()` funktiota ja lisää heittomerkeissä parametrina. Funktiota pitää kutsua heti ohjelman alussa.\n\nAlla lista tuetuista sensoriarvoista:\n\n- `brain.timer_time` (kello)\n- `drivetrain.is_done` (ajo päättynyt)\n- `drivetrain.is_moving` (ajo kesken)\n- `drivetrain.heading` (ajosuunta)\n- `drivetrain.rotation`(kääntymiskulma)\n- `left_bumper.pressed` (Vasenta painokytkintä painettu)\n- `right_bumper.pressed` (Oikeaa painokytkintä painettu)\n- `distance.found_object` (Etäisyysanturi havaitsee esineen)\n- `distance.get_distance` (Etäisyysanturi antaa esineen etäisyyden)\n- `front_eye.near_object` (Etusilmä-sensori havaitsee lähellä olevan esineen)\n- `front_eye.detect` (Etusilmä-sensori havaitsee)\n- `front_eye.brightness` (Etusilmä-sensori esineen valovoima)\n- `down_eye.near_object` (Alassilmä-sensori havaitsee lähellä olevan esineen)\n- `down_eye.detect` (Alassilmä-sensori havaitsee)\n- `down_eye.brightness` (Alassilmä-sensori esineen valovoima)\n- `location.position` (Paikka kentällä)\n- `location.position_angle` (Paikan kulma kentällä)\n\nLisää sensoriarvot monitor_sensor() komentoon heittomerkeissä:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"left_bumper.pressed\")\n```\nJos haluat monotoroida usemapia sensoriarvoja, erotteele ne pilkulla monitor_sensor() komennossa:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(“distance.get_distance”, “drivetrain.rotation”, “brain.timer_time”)\n```\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa käytetään location.position sensorin arvoa antamaan VR Robotin paikka sekä X että Y koordinaattina kentällä (sekä mm että tuumaa), kun se ajaa neliöliikeen.  \nRaportoidaan myös `location.position_angle` toisena sensoriarvona monitor_sensor() komennossa.  \n\n```don\ndef main():\n    monitor_sensor(\"location.position\",\"location.position_angle\")\n    for i in range(4):\n        wait(1, SECONDS)\n        drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_variable":{"name":"looks_monitor_variable","markdown":"# Näytä muuttujan arvo\n\nNäytä muuttujan arvo komentoa käytetään kun halutaan nähdä muuttujan arvo Monitori -konsolilla.\n\n```don\nmonitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\", ...)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nJos haluat lisä muuttujan Monitori -konsolille, valitse `monitor_variable()` komento. Lisää muuttuja heittomerkeissä komennossa. Kun muuttujia käytetään monitor_variable() komennossa, niiden pitää olla globaaleja muuttujia.\n\n## Esimerkit\n\nOhjelmassa VR Robotti kerää levyn Levyn kuljetus -pelikentällä Disk Mover Playground, muuttujan arvo muuttuu aina yhdellä.\n\n```don\ndef main():\n    global diskCount\n    monitor_variable(\"diskCount\")\n    while diskCount<3:\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        while not down_eye.near_object():\n            wait(5, MSEC)\n        \n        magnet.energize(BOOST)\n        diskCount += 1\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT,180,DEGREES)\n        \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        while distance.get_distance(MM)>150:\n            wait(5,MSEC)\n        magnet.energize(DROP)\n        \n        drivetrain.turn_for(LEFT,180,DEGREES)\n```","signature":"monitor_variable()"},"looks_pen_move":{"name":"looks_pen_move","markdown":"# Liikuta kynä\n\nLiikuttaa VR kynää.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nValitse VR kynän asento - joko **UP** (ylös) tai **DOWN** (alas)\n  \n**UP** - kynä ylhäällä ja ei piirrä viivaa pelikentälle\n\n**DOWN** - kynä alhaalla ja piirtää viivaa pelikentälle\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa VR kynä piirtää mustaa viivaa kun ajopeli liikkuu eteenpäin.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"pen.move(DOWN)"},"looks_pen_set_pen_color":{"name":"looks_pen_set_pen_color","markdown":"# Aseta kynän väri\n\nAsettaa VR kynän värin.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nValitse VR kynän väri:\n\n - BLACK (musta)\n - RED (punainen)\n - GREEN (vihreä)\n - BLUE (sininen)\n\n \n## Esimerkki\n\nEsimerkissä VR kyvä piirtää sinistä viivaa kun ajopeli ajaa eteenpäin.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLUE)\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)"},"magnet_energize":{"name":"magnet_energize","markdown":"# Vahvista tarttuminen sähkömagneetilla\n\nSähkömagneettin voi asettaa kahdelle eri moodille: **boost** (tehostaa) tai **drop** (pudottaa).\n\n```don\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nAnna joko `BOOST` tai `DROP` suluissa.\n\n**BOOST**: magneetti tarttuu metalliesineeseen \n**DROP**: magneetti pudottaa metalliesineen \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nLrvyhen siirto kentällä robotti ajaa eteenpäin 800mm, tarttuu esineeseen ja pudottaa sen kun ajopeli on peruuttanut 200mm.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 800, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 200, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(DROP)\n```","signature":"magnet_energize(BOOST)"},"sensing_brain_timer_reset":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_reset","markdown":"# Nollaa kello\n\nNollaa aivojen kellon.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nTAivojen kello käynnistyy ohjelman alussa. `brain.timer_reset()` funktioita käytetään nollaamaan kello taas asroon 0 kesken ohjelman. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa ajopeli liikkuu eteenpäin ensimmäisen 5 sekunnin ajan.  Brain.timer_reset() komentoa käytetään ohjelman alussa resetoimaan kello arvoon 0 ennen ajoa.  \n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nOdota -komento tarvitaan aina toistorakenteissa. \n\nKun Jos-komennon rivillä on lopussa kaksoipiste (:), niin Python suorittaa kaikki sen jälkeiset komennot.","signature":"brain.timer_reset()"},"sensing_brain_timer_time":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_time","markdown":"# Kellon aika\n\nRaportoi Aivojen kelloaikaa sekunteina.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_time(SECONDS)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nKello käunnistyy ajasta 0 kun ohjelma käynnistyy ja kellon aika -funktio palauttaa kuluneen ajan desimaalilukuna yksikkönä sekuntia.\n\n`brain.timer_time()` arvon voi asetaa muuttujiin, sitä voi käyttää Boolean -lausekkeissa ja muissa komennoissa numeerisena parametriarvona.\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nAjopeli ajaa eteenpäin ensimmäiset 5 sekuntia.  Brain.timer_time() -komentoa käytetään Kun -luupin ehdossa.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n\twait(5,MSEC)\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nOdota -kommento tarvitaan aina toistorakenteissa. \n\nKoska Kun-komennossa on lopussa kaksoispiset (:), kaikki sen alla olevay komennot kuuluvat toistorakenteeseen.","signature":"brain.timer_time(SECONDS)"},"sensing_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Bumper Pressed\n\nReports if the Right Bumper or Left Bumper is pressed. \n\n`bumper.pressed()`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`bumper.pressed()` returns **true** if the Bumper is being pressed.\n\n`bumper.pressed()` returns **false** if the Bumper is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use. The VR Robot is equipped with a `right_bumper` and a `left_bumper`.\n\n```\nright_bumper.pressed()\nleft_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nThe `bumper.pressed()` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition for control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nleft_bumper_pressed = left_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif left_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Left bumper pressed\")\nif right_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Right bumper pressed\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(left_bumper.pressed())\n```","signature":"bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_distance_found_object":{"name":"sensing_distance_found_object","markdown":"# Etäisyysanturi havaitsee esineen\n\nRaportoi jos etäisyysanturi havaitsee esineen tai esinepinnan robotin edessä. \n\n`distance.found_object()`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`distance.found_object()` palauttaa arvon **true** kun etäisyysanturi havaitsee esineen tai pinnan enintään 3000 mm sensorin edessä.\n\n`distance.found_object()` palauttaa arvon **false** kun etäisyysanturi ei havaitse esinettä tai pintaa enintään 3000 mm sensorin edessä.\n\n`distance.found_object()` funktio palauttaa arvon **true** tai **false** value. Sen voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, ehtoihin toistorakenteissa tai funktioissa parametreina.\n\nMuuttujassa:\n\n```\nfound_object = distance.found_object()\n```\n\nToistorakenteessa:\n\n```\nif distance.found_object():\n\tbrain.print(\"Found object\")\n```\n\nToisen funktion parametrina:\n\n```\nbrain.print(distance.found_object())\n```","signature":"distance.found_object()"},"sensing_distance_get_distance":{"name":"sensing_distance_get_distance","markdown":"# Etäisyys\n\nraportoi Etäisyysanturin antaman arvoin lähimmästä esineestä.\n\n`distance.get_distance(UNITS)`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nValitse yksijkkö, jota `distance.get_distance(UNITS)` raportoi joko millimetrejä tai tuumia korvaamalla 'UNITS` yksikköparametrina joko `MM` tai `INCHES`.\n\n```\ndistance.get_distance(MM)\ndistance.get_distance(INCHES)\n```\n\n`distance.get_distance(UNITS)` funktion arvon voi sijoittaa muuttujaan, vertailulausekkeeseen tai käyttää toisen funktion parametrina.\n\nSijoitus muuttujaan:\n\n```\nmy_distance_mm = distance.get_distance(MM)\n```\nSijoitus vertailulausekkeessa:\n\n```\nif distance.get_distance(INCHES) < 1:\n\tbrain.print(\"Close to object\")\n```\n\nKäyttö toisen funktion parametrina:\n\n```\nbrain.print(distance.get_distance(INCHES))\n```","signature":"distance.get_distance(MM)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Alas katsova Silmä Esineen valovoima/kirkkaus\n\nPalauttaa esineen valovoimakkuuden/kirkkauden.\n\n`down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nThe `down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` palauttaa numeerisen arvon välillä 0 - 100.  Yksikö on prosenttia.  Valkoiset esineet saavat arvon 100%, mustat taas arvon 0%.  Kaikki muut värit raportoituu näiden välille..  \n\n`down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` arvon voi sijoittaa muuttujiin,Boolean ehtolausekkeisiin ja toisiin numero komentoihin parametrina.","signature":"down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Alas osoittava Silmä havaitsee tietyn värin\n\nPalauttaa totuusarvon jos AlasSilmä havaitsee tietyn värin .\n\n`down_eye.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`down_eye.detect(RED)` komento palauttaa Boolean arvon.  Palauttaa **True** kun AlasSilmä havaitsee halutun värin ja palauttaa arvon **False** kun se ei havaitse haluttua väriä.  \n\nValitse väri, jota etsitään:\n\n* NONE (ei mitään)\n* RED (punainen)\n* GREEN (vihreä)\n* BLUE (sininen)\n\n`down_eye.detect(RED)` funktion voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, Boolean ehtooon ja toisiin funktioihin, joilla on Boolean parametri. \n\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` komentoa käytetään ja jos `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` komento palauttaa arvon **True**, Green -sana kirjoitetaan Monitori -konsolille, muuten  Not Green -sana tulostetaan konsolille.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if down_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            if down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n                brain.print(\"Green\")\n\n            else:\n                brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nOdota -komento on pakollinen toistorakenteissa. \n\nKun if/else komennon rivillä on kaksoipiste, niin kaikki rivin alla olevat komennot Python suorittaa ts kuuluvat ehtoon.","signature":"down_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Alas katsova silmä havaitsee läheisen esineen värin\n\nRaportoi, jos AlasSilmä on tarpeeksi lähellä (alle 100 mm) esinettä ja havaitsee värin.\n\n`down_eye.near_object()`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n\n`down_eye.near_object()` komento palauttaa Boolean arvon **True** kun Alassilmä on tarpeeksi lähellä väriesinettä, komento taas palauttaa Boolean arvon **False** kun Alassilmä ei ole tarpeeksi lähellä väriesinettä..  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` ei havaitse pelikentän lattiaa, muuta se haavaitsee värit lattialla poislukien kyvän piirtämiä viivoja.  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, Boolean lausekkeeseen ja käyttää muissa komennoissa Boolean parametrina. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa `down_eye.near_object()` komentoa käytetään Boolean lausekkeessa. Jos`down_eye.near_object()` palauttaa Boolean arvon **True** , ajopeli pysähtyy ja tulostaa Monitor konsolille \"Near a Disk\"ja ohjelman suoritus loppuu. Jos `down_eye.near_object()` komento palauttaa arvon **False**, Not Near a Disk -teksti kirjoitetaan jatkuvasti uudelle riville konsolia. \n\n```\n while True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if down_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            brain.print(\"Near a Disk\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near a Disk\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\n \nOdota -komento tarvitaan aina toistorakenteisiin. \n\nif/else komennossa on lopussa kaksoispiste, jolloin Python suorittaa kaikki komennot alta","signature":"down_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_drivetrain_heading":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_heading","markdown":"# Ajosuunta\n\nPaluttaa ajopelin ajosuuntana Gyro sensorin nykyisen ajosuunnan.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.heading(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` komento palauttaa numeroarvon.  Numeerinen arvo asteena (DEGREES) wkasvaa, kun käännytään vastapäivään ja vähenee kun käännytään myötäpäivään.  \n\n`drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` komento saa arvot välillä 0.00 - 359.99 astetta.  \n\n`drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` arvon voi sjijoittaa muuttujiin, Boolean lausekkeisdiin ja käyttää parametreina numeerisissa arvoissa.","signature":"drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_done":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_done","markdown":"# Ajo on päättynyt\n\nPalauttaa tiedon ajo on päättynyt.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.is_done()\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` palauttaa Boolean arvon. Jos arvo on **true** (tosi), ajoneuvo on päättänyt ajon.\nJos arvo on **false** (epätosi), ajoneuvo on edelleen liikeellä.\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` funtion voi sijoittaa Boolean lausekkeisiin ja sitä voi käyttää muissa komennoissa, joissa on Boolean parametriarvo.","signature":"drivetrain.is_done()"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving","markdown":"# Ajopeli liikkuu\n\nRaportoi, liikkuuko ajopeli.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` palauttaa arvon **true** (tosi) kun ajopelin moottorit liikkuvat.\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` palauttaa arvon **false** (epätosi) kun ajopelin moottorit ovat pysähtyneet.\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` funktio palauttaa arvon  joko **true** tai **false** . Sen voi sijoittaa muuttujiin , sitä voi käyttää ohjauslausekkieden ehdoissa  tai funktioparametrina.\n\nSijoitus muuttujaan:\n\n```\nstill_driving = drivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\nOhjauslausekeessa käyttö:\n\n```\nif drivetrain.is_moving():\n\tbrain.print(\"Still driving\")\n```\n\nSijoittaa toiseen funktioon:\n\n```\nbrain.print(drivetrain.is_moving())\n```","signature":"drivetrain.is_moving()"},"sensing_drivetrain_rotation":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_rotation","markdown":"# Ajon kääntymiskulma\n\nPalauttaa ajopelin kääntymiskulman.\n\n```\ndrivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## Mitn käytetään\n\n`drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` komentoi raportoi numeerisen arvon.  Se palauttaa postiivisen arvon jos ajopeli on kääntynyt vastapäivään ja negatiivisen arvoin, jos käännös on myötäpäivään.\n\n`drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, olla Boolean ehtolausekkeissa ja sijoittaa parametrina numeerisiin kenttiin.","signature":"drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Color Brightness\n\nReports the brightness of an object from one of the Color Sensor Eyes.\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` reports the brightness of an object. White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%. All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between that range of values.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```\nfront_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\ndown_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\nThe `eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` function returns a value between 0 and 100 value and can be assigned to variables, used in comparison statements, or in functions that take number values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\ncolor_brightness = front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\nUsing it in a comparison statement:\n\n```\nif down_eye.brightness(PERCENT) < 25:\n\tbrain.print(\"Dark color\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(front_eye.brightness(PERCENT))\n```","signature":"distance.distance(MM)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Color Sensing\n\nReports if one of the Color Sensor Eyes detects a specific color.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` returns **true** when the Color Sensor Eye detects the selected color.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` returns **false** when the Color Sensor Eye detects a different color than the one selected.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```\nfront_eye.detect(RED)\ndown_eye.detect(RED)\n```\n\nChoose which color to detect. Replace the `COLOR` parameter with the chosen color to detect.\n\n```\nNONE\nBLACK\nRED\nGREEN\nBLUE\n```\n\n\nThe `eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` function returns a **true** or **false** value. It can be called and assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\nfound_red = front_eye.detect(RED)\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n\tbrain.print(\"On top of green\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(down_eye.detect(BLUE))\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.detect(RED)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Color Near Object\n\nReports if the Color Sensor Eye is close enough to an object to detect a color.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()`\n\n## How To Use\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` returns **true** when the Color Sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` returns **false** when the Color Sensor isn't close enough to an object with detectable colors.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The `front_eye` is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the `down_eye` is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n```\nfront_eye.near_object()\ndown_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nThe `eye_sensor.near_object()` function returns a **true** or **false** value and can be assigned to variables, be used as a condition within control statements, or be used in functions that take boolean values as a parameter.\n\nSetting a variable:\n\n```\ncolor_obj_front = front_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nUsing it in a control statement:\n\n```\nif front_eye.near_object():\n\tbrain.print(\"Object in front\")\n```\n\nPassing it to another function:\n\n```\nbrain.print(down_eye.near_object())\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.near_object()"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# EtuSilmä Esineen valovoima/kirkkaus\n\nPalauttaa esineen valovoimakkuuden/kirkkauden.\n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` palauttaa numeerisen arvon välillä 0 - 100.  Yksikö on prosenttia.  Valkoiset esineet saavat arvon 100%, mustat taas arvon 0%.  Kaikki muut värit raportoituu näiden välille.  \n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` arvon voi sijoittaa muuttujiin,Boolen ehtolausekkeisiin ja toisiin numero komentoihin parametrina.","signature":"front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# EtuSilmä havaitsee tietyn värin\n\nPalauttaa totuusarvon jos EtuSilmä havaitsee tietyn värin .\n\n`front_eye.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`front_eye.detect(RED)` komento palauttaa Boolean arvon.  Palauttaa **True** kun EtuSilmä havaitsee halutun värin ja palauttaa arvon **False** kun se ei havaitse haluttua väriä.  \n\nValitse väri, jota etsitään:\n\n* NONE (ei mitään)\n* RED (punainen)\n* GREEN (vihreä)\n* BLUE (sininen)\n\n`front_eye.detect(RED)` funktion voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, Boolean ehtooon ja toisiin funktioihin, joilla on Boolean parametri. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` komentoa käytetään ja jos `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` komento palauttaa arvon **True**, Green -sana kirjoitetaan Monitori -konsolille, muuten  Not Green -sana tulostetaan konsolille.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if front_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            if front_eye.detect(GREEN):\n                brain.print(\"Green\")\n\n            else:\n                brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nOdota -komento on pakollinen toistorakenteissa. \n\nKun if/else komennon rivillä on kaksoipiste, niin kaikki rivin alla olevat komennot Python suorittaa ts kuuluvat ehtoon.","signature":"front_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Etusilmä havaitsee läheisen esineen värin\n\nRaportoi, jos EtuSilmä on tarpeeksi lähellä (alle 100 mm) esinettä ja havaitsee värin.\n\n`front_eye.near_object()`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`front_eye.near_object()` komento palauttaa Boolean arvon **True** kun Etusilmä on tarpeeksi lähellä väriesinettä, komento taas palauttaa Boolean arvon **False** kun Etusilmä ei ole tarpeeksi lähellä väriesinettä.  \n\n`front_eye.near_object()` voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, Boolean lausekkeeseen ja käyttää muissa komennoissa Boolean parametrina. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa `front_eye.near_object()` komentoa käytetään Boolean lausekkeessa. Jos`front_eye.near_object()` palauttaa Boolean arvon **True** , ajopeli pysähtyy ja tulostaa Monitor konsolille \"Near an Object\"ja ohjelman suoritus loppuu. Jos `front_eye.near_object()` kometo palauttaa arvon **False**, Not Near an Object -teksti kirjoitetaan jatkuvasti uudelle riville konsolia. \n\n```\n while True:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        if front_eye.near_object(): \n            drivetrain.stop()\n            brain.print(\"Near an Object\")\n            stop_project()\n\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n            brain.new_line()\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n\n```\n \nOdota -komento tarvitaan aina toistorakenteisiin. \n\nif/else komennossa on lopussa kaksoispiste, jolloin Python suorittaa kaikki komennot alta","signature":"front_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_left_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_left_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Vasenta painokytkintä painettu\n\nPalauttaa tiedon, onko vasenta painonappia painettu.\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()` komento palauttaa Boolean arvon  **True** (tosi) kun painokytkintä on painettu ja **False** (Epätosi) kun painokytkintä ei ole painettu.\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()` arvon voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, sitä voi käyttää Boolean lausekkeissa tai parametrina Boolean arvoisissa kentissä.  \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa `left_bumper.pressed()` komentoa käytetään Boolean lausekeessa.  Jos vasenta painokytkintä ei ole painettu, ajopeli liikkuu eteenpäin.  Jos vasenta painokytkintä on painettu, ajopeli pysähtyy.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n        if left_bumper.pressed():\n            drivetrain.stop()\n        else:\n            drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nOdota -komento tarvitaan aina toistorakenteisiin. \n\nJos niin muuten -komennon loussa on kaksoispiste, jolloin Python suorittaa kaikki ko blokin komennot alla.","signature":"left_bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_location_position":{"name":"sensing_location_position","markdown":"# Paikka kentällä \n\nRaportoi robotin X- tai Y- koordinaatin kentällä.\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)`\n\n## Miten kytetään\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)` palauttaa X- tai Y -koordinaatin , jossa VR robotti on kentällä numeerisen arvona.\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)`  arvon voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, Boolean lausekkeisiin sekä muitten komentojen numeerisiin parametreihin. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa location.position(AXIS, UNITS) komentoa käytetään siirtämään robotti numeron 28 kohdalle kentällä Numeroidulla Portti maailma -pelikentällä. Ohjelmassa location.position(AXIS, UNITS) -komentoa käytetään Boolean lausekkeessa. \n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(X, MM) < 500:\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n\twait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT,90,DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(Y, MM) < -500:\n\tdrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n\twait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"location.position(X, MM)"},"sensing_location_position_angle":{"name":"sensing_location_position_angle","markdown":"# Asentokulma asteina\n\nRaportoi robotin asentokulman asteina.\n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)` komento palöauttaa nykyisen VR robotin asentokulman asteina.  \n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)` arvon voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, Boolean lausekkeisiin ja toisten komentojen numeerisiin parametreihin.","signature":"location.position_angle(DEGREES)"},"sensing_right_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_right_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Oikeaa painokytkintä painettu\n\nPalauttaa tiedon, onko oikeaa painonappia painettu.\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()`\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()` komento palauttaa Boolean arvon  **True** (tosi) kun painokytkintä on painettu ja **False** (Epätosi) kun painokytkintä ei ole painettu.\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()` arvon voi sijoittaa muuttujiin, sitä voi käyttää Boolean lausekkeissa tai parametrina Boolean arvoisissa kentissä.  \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa `right_bumper.pressed()` komentoa käytetään Boolean lausekeessa.  Jos oikeaa painokytkintä ei ole painettu, ajopeli liikkuu eteenpäin.  Jos oikeaa painokytkintä on painettu, ajopeli pysähtyy.\n\n```\nwhile True:\n        wait(5,MSEC)\n        if right_bumper.pressed():\n            drivetrain.stop()\n        else:\n            drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nOdota -komento tarvitaan aina toistorakenteisiin. \n\nJos niin muuten -komennon loussa on kaksoispiste, jolloin Python suorittaa kaikki ko blokin komennot alla.","signature":"right_bumper.pressed()"},"variables_my_2d_list":{"name":"variables_my_2d_list","markdown":"# 2D taulukko\n\n\n2D taulukkomuuttujaan saa annettua useita arvoja. 2D taulukkoon saa tuplasti arvoja verrattuna luetteloon.  \n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nOletus 2D taulukko on: \n\n```don\nmy_2d_list = [\n    \t[0,0,0],\n    \t[0,0,0],\n    \t[0,0,0]\n    ]\n```\n\n2D taulukon nimeämisessä on samat säännöt kuin muilla muuttujilla:\n\n- Ei erikoismerkkejä (esim !,?,/)\n- Nimen ensimmäinen kirjain ei saa olla numero\n- Nimessä ei saa olla tyhjiä merkkejä\n- VEXcode VR varatut sanat on kiellettyjä  (esim Drivetrain)\n\nAlkion arvon 2d taulukossa saa antamalla alkion 2 indeksiä. Saat alkion nimetystä 2D taulukosta antamalla indeksit hakasuluissa.","signature":"my_2d_list = [\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0]\\n]"},"variables_my_boolean":{"name":"variables_my_boolean","markdown":"# Boolean muuttuja\n\nBoolean muuttujat saavat arvot **True** tai **False**.\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nMuuttujann voi asetaa arvon ooperaattorilla = .   \n\nOletusnimi on my_boolean.  Seuraavat säännöt on kielletty nimeämisessä:\n\n- Ei erikoismerkkejä (esim !,?,/,()\n- Ensimmäinen merkki ei voi olla numero\n- Nimessä ei saa olla tyhjiä merkkejä\n- VEXcode VR varatut sanat on kielletty (esim. Drivetrain)","signature":"my_boolean = True"},"variables_my_list":{"name":"variables_my_list","markdown":"# Luettelo \n\nLuetteloon voi sijoittaa useamman muuttujaarvon.\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nOletusnimi on `my_list = [0,0,0]`\n\nNimeämisessä on samat ehdot kuin muissa muuttujissa:\n\n- Ei erikoismerkkejä (esim !,?,/)\n- Ensimmäinen kirjain ei voi olla numero\n- Nimesä ei saa olla tyhjiä merkkejä\n- VEXcode VR varatus sanat on kielletty (esim Drivetrain)\n\nTVoist asettaa arvoja luettelloon operaattorilla = ,  alkiot pilkulla eroteltuna yhdessä hakasuluissa.  \n\nJos luettelossa on numeroita, kirjoita pilkku heti numeron jälkeen. \n\n`my_list = [0,1,2]`\n\nJos alkiot on merkkijonoja, laita ne lainausmerkkien sisään.\n\n`my_list = [“Drive”,”Forward”]`\n\nLuettelon arvoja saa käyttämällä luettelon järjestyslukuindeksiä. Ensimmäisen alkion indeksi on 0. \n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa käytetään luetteloa `my_list` toistolausekkeessa.  Alkiot ovat tekstiä.  Muuttuja y is the iteraatori toistolausekkeessa.  for -looppi iteroi ja tulostaa alkioindeksit 0,1,2,3 ja tulostaa `my_list` -taulukon alkiot.\n\n```don\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", ‘100%', \"fun!!\"]\nfor y in my_list:\n\tbrain.print(y)\n\tbrain.new_line()\n\twait(5, MSEC)\n```\n\nOhjelma tulostaa luettelon alkioiden arvot:\n\n```don\nVEXcode VR\nis\n100%\nfun!!\n```\n\nTässä taas ei tarvita toistoa, kun vain `my_list` -luttelon viimeinen alkio indeksillä 3 tulostetaan.\n\n\n```don\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", '100%', \"fun!!\"]\nbrain.print(my_list[3])\nwait(5, MSEC)\n```  \n\nTässä ohjelman tulostus konsolille:\n\n```\nfun!!\n```","signature":"my_list = [0,0,0]"},"variables_my_number":{"name":"variables_my_number","markdown":"# Numeerinen muuttuja\n\nNumeerinen muuttuja sisältää numeroarvoja\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nKun sijoitar arvon muuttujaan, käytä operaatiota = .   \n\nNumeerisen muuttujan oletusnimi on my_number.  Noudata seuraavia nimeämissääntöjä muuttujalle:\n\n- Ei erikoismerkkejä (esim ?,#,/,())\n- Ensimmäinen merkki ei voi olla numero\n- Nimen merkkien välissä ei voi olla tyhjää\n- Varattuja VEXcode VR (esim Drivetrain) sanoja ei saa käyttää\n\n## Esimerkki\n\nOhjelmassa ajopeli ajaa eteenpäin 150 mm ja kääntyy oikealle 90 astetta. \n\n```don\ndistance_to_travel = 150\nangle_to_turn = 90\n\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, distance_to_travel, MM)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, angle_to_turn, DEGREES)\n```\nOhjelmassa on määritelty kaksi muuttujaa `distance_to_travel` ja `angle_to_turn`.  A = avulla asetetaan lähtöarvot muuttujille. Muuttujaia käytetään `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD)` ja `drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT)` komennoissa.","signature":"my_number = 1"},"variables_my_string":{"name":"variables_my_string","markdown":"# Merkkimuuttuja\n\nMerkkimuuttujaa voi käyttää, kun haluaa käyttää tekstijonoja muuttujan arvona.  Tekstiä voi olla : numerot, kirjaimey ja symbolit. \n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nVoit asettaa tekstiä muuttujaan operaatiolla = .   \n\nOletusnimi on my_string.  Noudat seuraavia ehtoja nimeämisessä:\n\n- Ei erokoismerkkejä (esim !,?,/,())\n- Nimi ei voi alkaa numerolla\n- Nimessä ei voi olla tyhjiä välejä merkkien välillä\n- VEXcode VR varatut sanat on kielletty (esim. Drivetrain)","signature":"my_string = \\\"Hello World\\\""},"vr_thread":{"name":"vr_thread","markdown":"# VR Säie\n\nVR Säie -funktiota käytetään, kun sitä kutsutaan call funktioita toimimaan yleiskäyttöisesti/globaalisti.\n\n## Miten käytetään\n\nVoit minkä tahansa tekemäsi funktion muuttaa yleiskäyttöiseksi `vr_thread()` funktioksi. Jotta se toimii, sitä pitää kutsua ainakin kerran. Sinun ei tarvitse muuttaa kaikkia funktioita globaaleiksi `vr_thread()` funktioiksi, vain ne joita käytetään globaalisti.\n\nJos globaalia funktiota `vr_thread()` kutsutaan, niin se suoritetaan rinnakkaisesti samaan aikaan kutsujan kanssa.\n\nFunktiot suoritetaan kutsujärjestyksessä. Kuitenkin jos funktio jää odottamaan , se siirtää suorituksen seuraavalle funktiolle ja jatkaa vasta kun toinen on päättynyt tai toinenkin jää odottamaan seuraavan suoritusta.\n\n```don\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nFunktiot, jotka jäävät odottaan:\n\n```\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.set_heading(0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(0, DEGREES)\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nany user created function\n\n```\nMetodin kutsu `vr_thread()` toisen metodin sisällä aiheuttaa ajonaikaisen virheen. Jos suoritat tämän ohjelman:\n\n```\ndef main():\n    vr_thread(print_hello())\n\ndef print_hello():\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nAnnetaan virheilmoitus:\n\n```\nError: Traceback (most recent call last):\n  line 9, in vr_thread\n  line 5, in main\nNameError: name 'print_hello' is not defined\n```\n\n## Esimerkki 1\n\nTee funktio, joka suoritetaan `vr_thread()` funktiona . Funktio `move_drivetrain()` ajaa robottia 200 mm ja tulostaa näytölle kun ajo on päättynyt.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\n```\n## Esimerkki 2\n\nLisätään `move_pen_down()` funktio. Siinä kynä laitetaan alas ja tulostetaan viesti **Done moving** .Teemme `vr_thread(move_pen_down())` säikeen **after** säikeen `vr_thread(move_drivetrain())` kutsun jälkeen. Kuitenkin koska `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)` pakottaa seuraavan funktion odottavan, niin `move_pen_down()` funktio suoritetaan vasta kun robotti on pysähtynyt.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\nvr_thread(move_pen_down())\n```\n\n## Esimerkki 3\n\nSiirtämällä `move_pen_down()` funktioon `move_drivetrain()` aiheuttaa sen kutsussa jonossa ja mitää piirtämistä ei tapahdu ennenkuin liie on loppunut. Kutsufunktioita voi kutsua toisten funktioiden sisältä.\n\n```don\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n    move_pen_down()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\n```","signature":"vr_thread(function_name())"}}},"lv":{"blocks":{"add":{"name":"add","markdown":"# Saskaitīt\n\nSaskaita kopā jebkuras divas vērtības un uzrāda summu.\n\n![add](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/add/add.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Saskaitīt”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nBloku **“Saskaitīt”** var ievietot noapaļotos laukumos, kā arī paša bloka noapaļotajos laukumos var ievietot citus noapaļotos blokus.\n\nSaskaitiet divus ciparus.\n\n![add\\_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/add/add_numbers.png)\n\nSaskaitiet vērtības un mainīgos.\n\n![add\\_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/add/add_variables.png)\n\nSaskaitiet dažādas vērtības no sensoriem vai ierīcēm.\n\n![add\\_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/add/add_sensors.png)"},"and":{"name":"and","markdown":"# Un\n\nNorāda, ja divas Būla vērtības ir patiesas.\n\n![and](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/and/and.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Un”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), kad abas vērtības ir **“true”** (Patiess).\n\nBloks **“Un”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), kad abas vērtības ir **“false”** (Nepatiess).\n\nBūla bloks **“Un”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess). Tajā var ievietot blokus ar sešstūru malām.\n\n![and\\_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/and/and_shape.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā tiks pārbaudīts, vai **“FrontEye”** (Priekšējais sensors) atrodas tuvu objektam un vai objekts ir sarkanā krāsā.\n\n![and\\_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/and/and_sensors.png)\n\nBloks **“Un”** norādīs **“true”** (Patiess), ja abi apstākļi būs patiesi — **“FrontEye”** (Priekšējais sensors) atrodas tuvu objektam un **“FrontEye”** (Priekšējais sensors) uztver sarkanu krāsu.\n\nBloks **“Un”** norādīs **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja priekšējā sensora tuvumā neuztver objektu vai sarkano krāsu, vai abus.\n\n![and\\_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/and/and_truth.png)"},"boolean_variable":{"name":"boolean_variable","markdown":"# Būla mainīgais\n\nNorāda Būla mainīgā vērtību.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/boolean_variable/boolean_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Būla mainīgais”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), kas ievietota mainīgā blokā. Izmantojiet bloku  **“iestatīt Būla mainīgo”**, lai iestatītu vai atjaunotu Būla mainīgā vērtību.\n\n![boolean\\_set\\_value](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/boolean_variable/boolean_set_value.png)\n\nBūla bloks **“Būla mainīgais”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar sešstūru malu laukumiem.\n\n![boolean\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/boolean_variable/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nBūla mainīgos izveido, izmantojot pogu **“Izveidot Būla mainīgo”**.\n\n![make\\_boolean\\_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/boolean_variable/boolean_button.png)"},"break":{"name":"break","markdown":"# Pārtraukt\n\nNekavējoties pārtrauc cikla darbību.\n\n![break](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/break/break.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nKad tiek pievienots pie “C” bloka, kas atkārtojas, bloks **“Pārtraukt”** noslēdz ciklu, kurā ir ievietots šis bloks.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nPiemērā piedziņas sistēma tiek pārvietota uz priekšu, pēc tam tiek pārbaudīts vai V5 robota smadzenes skārienekrāns ir nospiests.\n\nJa ekrāns ir **nospiests**, bloks **“Pārtraukt”** noslēdz ciklu ar neierobežotu atkārtošanu un aptur piedziņas sistēmu.\n\n![break\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/break/break_example.png)"},"bumper":{"name":"bumper","markdown":"# Saskares slēdzis nospiests\n\nNorāda, ja saskares slēdzis ir nospiests.\n\n![pressing\\_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\n**“Saskares slēdzis”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja saskares slēdzis ir nospiests.\n\n**“Saskares slēdzis”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja saskares slēdzis ir nospiests.\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru saskares slēdzi izmantosiet.\n\n![pressing\\_bumper\\_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nBūla bloks **“Saskares slēdzis”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar sešstūru malu laukumiem.\n\n![pressing\\_bumper\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Vērtību uzraudzīšana\n\nLai uzraudzītu bloka **“Saskares slēdzis”** vērtību, atzīmējiet norādīšanas bloka izvēles rūtiņu.\n\n![pressing\\_bumper\\_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nPēc tam atveriet uzraudzīšanas konsoli, lai skatītu norādīšanas blokus, kas tika atlasīti uzraudzīšanai.\n\n![pressing\\_bumper\\_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)"},"change_variable":{"name":"change_variable","markdown":"# Mainīt mainīgo\n\nNomaina mainīgo pret doto vērtību.\n\n![change\\_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/change_variable/change_variable.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru mainīgo lietot. Atlasīto mainīgo var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![change\\_variable\\_options](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/change_variable/change_variable_options.png)\n\nBloks **“Mainīt mainīgo”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![change\\_variable\\_value](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/change_variable/change_variable_value.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā tiek palielināts mainīgais, kas iestata piedziņas ātrumu par 10 vienībām katru reizi, kad cikla funkcija **“Atkārtot ciklu”** ir iespējota.\n\n![change\\_variable\\_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/change_variable/change_variable_example2.png)"},"clear_all_rows":{"name":"clear_all_rows","markdown":"# Notīrīt visas rindas\n\nNotīra visas rindas drukas konsolē.\n\n![clear\\_all\\_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzmantojiet bloku **“Notīrīt visas rindas”**, lai notīrītu visas rindas drukas konsolē.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā ekrānā tiks drukāts “Hello”, un pēc vienas sekundes viss ekrāns tiek notīrīts un ekrānā tiek drukāts “Goodbye”.\n\n![clear\\_all\\_rows\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows_example.png)"},"comment":{"name":"comment","markdown":"# Komentārs\n\nĻauj programmētājiem pierakstīt informāciju, kas palīdz aprakstīt viņu programmu.\n\n![comment\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/comment/comment_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nProfesionāli programmētāji savās programmās izmanto komentārus, lai palīdzētu citiem izprast savu kodu. Komentāri var palīdzēt arī problēmu risināšanā un sadarbībā.\n\nPievienojiet tekstu blokā **“Komentārs”**, vispirms atlasot caurspīdīgo taisnstūri un nodzēšot tekstu. Pēc tam ievadiet jauno tekstu.\n\n![comment\\_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/comment/comment_example3.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nProgrammas vai bloki, kuri ir ietverti komentārus, netiek ietekmēti.\n\n![comment\\_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/comment/comment_example2.png)"},"distance_distance":{"name":"distance_distance","markdown":"# Distance no\n\nNorāda distanci līdz tuvākajam objektam.\n\n![distance\\_from\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_distance/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties vai **“Distance no”** tiek norādīta milimetros vai collās.\n\n![distance\\_from\\_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_distance/distance_from_unit.png)\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Distance no”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![distance\\_from\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_distance/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n## Vērtību uzraudzīšana\n\nLai uzraudzītu bloka **“Distance no”** vērtību, atzīmējiet norādīšanas bloka izvēles rūtiņu.\n\n![distance\\_from\\_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_distance/distance_from_checkbox.png)\n\nPēc tam atveriet uzraudzīšanas konsoli, lai skatītu norādīšanas blokus, kas tika atlasīti uzraudzīšanai.\n\n![distance\\_from\\_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_distance/distance_from_monitor.png)"},"distance_found":{"name":"distance_found","markdown":"# Distancē atrasts objekts\n\nNorāda, vai iebūvētais distances sensors robota priekšpusē ir uztvēris objektu.\n\n![distance\\_found\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_found/distance_found_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Distancē atrasts objekts”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja iebūvētais distances sensors uztver objektu vai virsmu savā uztveršanas lokā — 3000 mm sensora diapazonā.\n\nBloks **“Distancē atrasts objekts”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja iebūvētais distances sensors neuztver objektu vai virsmu 3000 mm sensora diapazonā.\n\nBūla bloks **“Distancē atrasts objekts”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar sešstūru malu laukumiem.\n\n![distance\\_found\\_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_found/distance_found_insert.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nProgrammā “Castle Crashers Playground” gadījumā šis piemērs virzīsies uz priekšu, ja distances sensors uztvers objektu, pretējā gadījumā tas veiks 45° pagriezienu pa labi.\n\n![distance\\_found\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_found/distance_found_example.png)\n\n## Vērtību uzraudzīšana\n\nLai uzraudzītu bloka **“Distancē atrasts objekts”** vērtību, atzīmējiet norādīšanas bloka izvēles rūtiņu.\n\n![distance\\_found\\_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_found/distance_found_checkbox.png)\n\nPēc tam atveriet uzraudzīšanas konsoli, lai skatītu norādīšanas blokus, kas tika atlasīti uzraudzīšanai.\n\n![distance\\_found\\_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/distance_found/distance_found_monitor.png)"},"divide":{"name":"divide","markdown":"# Dalīt\n\nDala pirmo vērtību ar otru vērtību un norāda iegūto rezultātu.\n\n![divide](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/divide/divide.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Dalīt”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nBloku **“Dalīt”** var ievietot noapaļotos laukumos, kā arī paša bloka noapaļotajos laukumos var ievietot citus noapaļotos blokus.\n\nIevadiet skaitļus, ko vēlaties dalīt.\n\n![divide\\_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/divide/divide_numbers.png)\n\nDalīt ar mainīgo vērtībām.\n\n![divide\\_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/divide/divide_variables.png)\n\nDalīt ar dažādām vērtībām, kas iegūtas no sensoriem vai ierīcēm.\n\n![divide\\_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/divide/divide_sensors.png)"},"drive":{"name":"drive","markdown":"# Kustība\n\nNepārtraukti pārvieto piedziņas sistēmu.\n\n![drive\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive/drive_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Kustība”** nepārtraukti darbina piedziņas sistēmu, kamēr netiek ievietots jauns piedziņas sistēmas bloks vai programma tiek apturēta.\n\nIzvēlieties virzienu, kādā tiek pārvietota piedziņas sistēma.\n\n![drive\\_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive/drive_direction.png)"},"drive_for":{"name":"drive_for","markdown":"# Pārvietot par\n\nPārvieto piedziņas sistēmu norādītajā distancē.\n\n![drive\\_for](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_for/drive_for.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties virzienu, kādā tiks pārvietota piedziņas sistēma.\n\n![drive\\_for\\_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_for/drive_for_direction.png)\n\nIestatiet piedziņas sistēmas distanci, ievadot vērtību un izvēloties mērvienību (collas vai milimetri).\n\nBloks **“Pārvietot par”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![drive\\_for\\_units](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_for/drive_for_units.png)\n\nPēc noklusējuma, citi bloki darbosies ar funkciju **“gaidīt”**, kamēr piedziņas sistēma būs pabeigusi kustību.\n\nSpiežot uz bultiņas, jūs variet atlasīt funkciju **“un negaidīt”**, kas atļauj citiem blokiem turpināt darboties, kamēr piedziņas sistēma ir kustībā.\n\n![drive\\_for\\_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_for/drive_for_anddontwait.png)"},"drive_heading":{"name":"drive_heading","markdown":"# Kustības virzība\n\nIzmantojot pastāvošo žiroskopa sensora leņķisko pozīciju, norāda piedziņas sistēmas kustības virzienu.\n\n![drive\\_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_heading/drive_heading.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nNorādīšanas bloks **“Kustības virzība”** norāda izmaiņas virzībā, kad tiek veikta rotācija pulksteņrādītāju virzienā.\n\nBloks **“Kustības virzība”** norāda diapazonu no **0,00 līdz 359,99 grādiem**.\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Kustības virzība”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![drive\\_heading\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_heading/drive_heading_inserted.png)"},"drive_is_done":{"name":"drive_is_done","markdown":"# Kustība ir pabeigta\n\nNorāda, ka piedziņas sistēma ir pabeigusi kustību.\n\n![drive\\_is\\_done\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Kustība ir pabeigta”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja piedziņas sistēmas dzinējs ir pabeidzis kustību.\n\nBloks **“Kustība ir pabeigta”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja piedziņas sistēmas dzinējs nav pārtraucis kustību.\n\nBūla bloks **“Kustība ir pabeigta”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar sešstūru malu laukumiem.\n\n![drive\\_is\\_done\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_inserted.png)"},"drive_is_moving":{"name":"drive_is_moving","markdown":"# Piedziņa ir kustībā\n\nNorāda, vai piedziņas sistēma atrodas kustībā.\n\n![drive\\_is\\_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Piedziņa ir kustībā”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja piedziņas sistēmas dzinējs nav pārtraucis kustību.\n\nBloks **“Piedziņa ir kustībā”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja piedziņas sistēmas dzinējs ir apstājies.\n\nBūla bloks **“Piedziņa ir kustībā”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar sešstūru malu laukumiem.\n\n![drive\\_is\\_moving\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)"},"drive_rotation":{"name":"drive_rotation","markdown":"# Piedziņas rotācija\n\nNorāda piedziņas sistēmas rotācijas leņķi, ja konfigurācijā ir iekļauts žiroskopa sensors.\n\n![drive\\_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_rotation/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Piedziņas rotācija”** norāda **pozitīvu** vērtību, ja piedziņas sistēma griežas **pulksteņrādītāju** virzienā.\n\nBloks **“Piedziņas rotācija”** norāda **negatīvu** vērtību, ja piedziņas sistēma griežas **pretēji pulksteņrādītāju** virzienam.\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Piedziņas rotācija”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![drive\\_rotation\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_inserted.png)"},"equal_to":{"name":"equal_to","markdown":"# Vienāds ar\n\nNorāda vai pirmā vērtība ir tādi pati vai vienāda ar otro vērtību.\n\n![equal\\_to](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/equal_to/equal_to.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Vienāds ar”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja abas vērtības ir vienādas.\n\nBloks **“Vienāds ar”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja abas vērtības ir atšķirīgas.\n\nBloks **“Vienāds ar”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nBūla bloks **“Vienāds ar”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un tajā var ievietot blokus ar sešstūru malām.\n\n![equal\\_to\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/equal_to/equal_to_inserted.png)\n\nŠajā piemērā kustība tiek apturēta, ja distance ir vienāda ar 500 mm.\n\n![equal\\_to\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/equal_to/equal_to_example.png)"},"forever":{"name":"forever","markdown":"# Bezgalīgi\n\nCikla bloks, kas bezgalīgi atkārto jebkuru bloku, kas tajā ir ievietots.\n\n![bezgalīgi](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/forever/forever.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIevietojiet bloku cikla blokā **“Bezgalīgi”**, lai ievietotā bloka darbības atkārtotu **bezgalīgi**.\n\nBloka **“Bezgalīgi”** ciklu var noslēgt, ja tajā tiek ievietots bloks **“Pārtraukt”**.\n\n![forever\\_example\\_2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/forever/forever_example_2.png)\n\nCikla blokus var arī ievietot vienu otrā. Šādu metodi sauc par ligzdošanu, kuru izmantojot var ietaupīt laiku, veidojot dažādas programmas darbības."},"function":{"name":"function","markdown":"# Funkcija\n\nVeic atlasīto matemātisko funkciju.\n\n![function](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/function/function.png)\n\n* **abs** — absolūtā vērtība\n* **floor** — noapaļo uz zemāko veselo skaitli\n* **ceiling** — noapaļo uz lielāko veselo skaitli\n* **sqrt** — kvadrātsakne\n* **sin** — sinuss\n* **cos** — kosinuss\n* **tan** — tangenss\n* **asin** — arksinuss vai inversais sinuss\n* **acos** — arkkosinuss vai inversais kosinuss\n* **atan** — arktangens vai inversais tangenss\n* **ln** — dabīgā logaritma konstante e\n* **log** — logaritma bāze 10\n* **e \\^** — konstantes e pakāpes\n* **10 \\^** — 10 bāzes pakāpes\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Funkcija”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nIzvēlieties bloka **“Funkcija”** matemātisko darbību opciju sarakstā.\n\n![function\\_select](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/function/function_select.png)\n\nBloku **“Funkcija”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![function\\_fit](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/function/function_fit.png)"},"greater_than":{"name":"greater_than","markdown":"# Lielāks par\n\nNorāda vai pirmā vērtība ir lielāka par otro vērtību.\n\n![greater\\_than](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/greater_than/greater_than.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Lielāks par”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja pirmā vērtība ir lielāka par otro vērtību.\n\nBloks **“Lielāks par”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja pirmā vērtība ir mazāka vai vienāda ar otro vērtību.\n\nBloks **“Vienāds ar”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nBūla bloks **“Vienāds ar”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar tukšu sešstūru laukumu.\n\n![greater\\_than\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/greater_than/greater_than_inserted.png)\n\nŠajā piemērā LED saskares slēdzis tiks iekrāsots zaļš, ja taimeris pārsniegs 50 sekundes.\n\n![greater\\_than\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/greater_than/greater_than_example.png)"},"if_then":{"name":"if_then","markdown":"# “If then” (Ja, tad)\n\nCikla bloks, kas darbina ievietotos blokus, ja Būla norādītais apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“true”** (Patiess).\n\n![if\\_then](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/if_then/if_then.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nCikla bloks **“if then”** (Ja, tad) pārbauda tikai vienreiz norādīto Būla apstākli.\n\nJa Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“true”** (Patiess), tad bloki, kas ir ievietoti blokā **“if then”** (Ja, tad), tiks darbināti.\n\nJa Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“false”** (Nepatiess), tad bloki, kas ir ievietoti blokā **“if then”** (Ja, tad), tiks izlaisti.\n\nCikla bloks **“if then”** (Ja, tad) var apstiprināt sešstūru malu blokus, kā norādītu apstākli.\n\n![if\\_then](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/if_then/if_then_2.png)"},"if_then_else":{"name":"if_then_else","markdown":"# “If then else” (Ja, tad, citādāk)\n\nCikla bloks, kas darbina blokus, kas atrodas bloka **“if then else”** (Ja, tad, citādāk) pirmajā vai otrajā daļā, atbilstoši norādītajai Būla vērtībai.\n\n![if\\_then\\_else](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/if_then_else/if_then_else.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nCikla bloks **“if then else”** (Ja, tad, citādāk) pārbauda tikai vienreiz norādīto Būla apstākli.\n\nJa Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“true”** (Patiess), tad bloki, kas ir ievietoti daļā **“if”** (Ja), tiks darbināti.\n\nJa Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“false”** (Nepatiess), tad bloki, kas ir ievietoti daļā **“else”** (citādāk), tiks darbināti.\n\nCikla bloks **“if then else”** (Ja, tad, citādāk) var apstiprināt sešstūru malu blokus, kā norādītu apstākli.\n\n![if\\_then\\_else](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/if_then_else/if_then_else_2.png)"},"item_of_2d_array":{"name":"item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# 2D saraksta vienums\n\nNorāda 2D saraksta vienuma vērtību.\n\n![item\\_of\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru 2D sarakstu lietot. Atlasīto 2D sarakstu var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![item\\_of\\_options](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nIevadiet vienuma rindas (pirmais numurs) un kolonnas (otrais numurs) pozīciju uzrādāmajā sarakstā. Šeit tiek norādīta vienuma pozīcija 2. rindas 3. kolona 2D sarakstā.\n\n![item\\_of\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\nBloks **“2D saraksta vienums”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![item\\_of\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)"},"item_of_array":{"name":"item_of_array","markdown":"# Vienums\n\nNorāda saraksta vienuma vērtību.\n\n![item\\_of\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/item_of_array/item_of_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru sarakstu lietot. Atlasīto sarakstu var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![item\\_of\\_options](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/item_of_array/item_of_options.png)\n\nIevadiet vienuma pozīcijas numuru, kas norādīts sarakstā. Šajā gadījumā saraksta otrais vienums (2. Vienums) ir norādīts.\n\n![item\\_of\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/item_of_array/item_of_block_2.png)\n\nBloks **“Vienums”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā piedziņas sistēmas ātrumu iestata ar to vērtību, kas norādīta jūsu saraksta 3. vienumam. Piedziņas sistēma tagad ar 90% no pilna ātruma  pārvietosies uz priekšu.\n\n![item\\_of\\_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/item_of_array/item_of_example2.png)"},"length_of_2d_array":{"name":"length_of_2d_array","markdown":"# 2D saraksta garums\n\nNorāda 2D saraksta rindu vai kolonu skaitu.\n\n![size\\_of\\_array](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru 2D sarakstu lietot. Atlasīto 2D sarakstu var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![size\\_of\\_array\\_select](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_select.png)\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru vērtību norādīt. 2D saraksts var norādīt **rindas** vai **kolonas**.\n\n![size\\_of\\_array\\_select](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_option.png)\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“2D saraksta garums”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![size\\_of\\_array\\_select](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_insert.png)"},"less_than":{"name":"less_than","markdown":"# Mazāks par\n\nNorāda vai pirmā vērtība ir mazāka par otro vērtību.\n\n![less\\_than](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/less_than/less_than.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Mazāks par”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja pirmā vērtība ir mazāka par otro vērtību.\n\nBloks **“Mazāks par”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja pirmā vērtība ir lielāka vai vienāda ar otro vērtību.\n\nBūla bloks **“Mazāks par”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar tukšu sešstūru laukumu.\n\nBloks **“Mazāks par”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![less\\_than\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/less_than/less_than_inserted.png)\n\nŠajā piemērā tiks darbināts **magnēts**, ja piedziņas virzības norādītie grādi ir mazāki par 50."},"move_pen":{"name":"move_pen","markdown":"# Iestatīt pildspalvas pozīciju\n\nIestata “VEX VR” pildspalvas pozīciju\n\n![move\\_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/move_pen/move_pen.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties VR pildspalvas pozīciju\n\n![move\\_pen2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/move_pen/move_pen2.png)\n\n* Augšup — pildspalva nezīmēs līniju uz laukuma.\n* Lejup — pildspalva zīmēs krāsainu līniju uz laukuma.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā pildspalva zīmēs sarkanu līniju uz laukuma.\n\n![move\\_pen3](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/move_pen/move_pen3.png)"},"multiply":{"name":"multiply","markdown":"# Reizināt\n\nReizina jebkuras divas vērtības un uzrāda iznākumu.\n\n![multiply](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/multiply/multiply.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Reizināt”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nBloku **“Reizināt”** var ievietot noapaļotos laukumos, kā arī paša bloka noapaļotajos laukumos var ievietot citus noapaļotos blokus.\n\nReizina divus skaitļus.\n\n![multiply\\_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/multiply/multiply_numbers.png)\n\nReizina, izmantojot mainīgo vērtības.\n\n![multiply\\_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/multiply/multiply_variables.png)\n\nReizina, izmantojot dažādas vērtības, kas iegūtas no sensoriem vai ierīcēm.\n\n![multiply\\_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/multiply/multiply_sensors.png)"},"next_row":{"name":"next_row","markdown":"# Iestatīt kursoru nākamajā rindā\n\nDrukas konsolē iestata kursoru nākamajā rindā, izmantojot blokus **“Drukāt”**.\n\n![set\\_cursor\\_next\\_row](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/next_row/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzmantojiet bloku **“iestatīt kursoru nākamajā rindā”**, lai pārvietotu kursoru ekrāna nākamajā rindā.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā tiks drukāts “Hello”, un ekrāna nākamajā rindā tiks drukāts “World!”.\n\n![set\\_cursor\\_next\\_row\\_code\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/next_row/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"not":{"name":"not","markdown":"# Nav\n\nNorāda pretējo (apgriezto) iegūto Būla vērtību.\n\n![not](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/not/not.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Nav”** norāda **“true“** (Patiess), ja Būla blokā tiek norādīta vērtība **“false”** (Nepatiess).\n\nBloks **“Nav”** norāda **“false“** (Nepatiess), ja Būla blokā tiek norādīta vērtība **“true”** (Patiess).\n\nBūla bloks **“Nav”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess). Tajā var ievietot blokus ar sešstūru malām.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā tiks norādīta vērtība **“true”** (Patiess), ja **distances sensors** neuztver objektu.\n\n![not\\_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/not/not_sensors.png)\n\n![not\\_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/not/not_truth.png)"},"optical_brightness":{"name":"optical_brightness","markdown":"# Krāsu spilgtums\n\nNorāda objekta spilgtumu, izmantojot vienu no krāsu uztverošajiem acu sensoriem.\n\n![color\\_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_brightness/color_brightness.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\n**“Krāsu spilgtums”** norāda objekta spilgtumu. Baltie objekti ziņos par spilgtumu 100%, melnie objekti ziņos par spilgtumu 0%. Visas pārējās krāsas ziņos par spilgtumu kaut kur starp šīm vērtībām.\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru krāsu sensora aci izmantot. Priekšējais acu sensors ir uzstādīts robota priekšpusē un ir pavērsts uz priekšu, bet apakšējais acu sensors ir uzstādīts robota apakšā un ir pavērsts uz leju.\n\n![color\\_brightness\\_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_brightness/color_brightness_eyes.png)\n\nSkaitļu bloks **“Krāsu spilgtums”** norāda vērtības no 0 līdz 100, un to var ievietot blokos ar noapaļotu tukšu laukumu.\n\n![color\\_brightness\\_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_brightness/color_brightness_insert.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠāds piemērs būtu jāveic **“Disk Maze Playground”**. Robots virzīsies uz priekšu, līdz apakšējā acs uztvers, ka zem tā esošā objekta spilgtums ir mazāks par 100%. Pēc tam tas pagriežas pa kreisi un atkal tiek virzīts uz priekšu.\n\n![color\\_brightness\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_brightness/color_brightness_example.png)\n\n## Vērtību uzraudzīšana\n\nLai uzraudzītu bloka **“Krāsu spilgtums”** vērtību, atzīmējiet norādīšanas bloka izvēles rūtiņu.\n\n![color\\_brightness\\_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_brightness/color_brightness_checkbox.png)\n\nPēc tam atveriet uzraudzīšanas konsoli, lai skatītu norādīšanas blokus, kas tika atlasīti uzraudzīšanai.\n\n![color\\_brightness\\_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_brightness/color_brightness_monitor.png)"},"optical_color":{"name":"optical_color","markdown":"# Krāsu uztveršana\n\nNorāda, vai krāsu uztverošais acu sensors uztver noteiktu krāsu.\n\n![color\\_sensing\\_color](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_color/color_sensing_color.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Krāsu uztveršana”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), kad krāsu uztverošais acu sensors uztver atlasīto krāsu.\n\nBloks **“Krāsu uztveršana”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), kad krāsu uztverošais acu sensors uztver citu krāsu nevis atlasīto krāsu.\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru krāsu sensora aci izmantot. Priekšējais acu sensors ir uzstādīts robota priekšpusē un ir pavērsts uz priekšu, bet apakšējais acu sensors ir uzstādīts robota apakšā un ir pavērsts uz leju.\n\n![color\\_sensing\\_color\\_eye](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_color/color_sensing_color_eye.png)\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru krāsu nepieciešams uztvert.\n\n![color\\_sensing\\_color\\_color](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_color/color_sensing_color_color.png)\n\nBūla bloks **“Krāsu uztveršana”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar tukšu sešstūru laukumu.\n\n![color\\_sensing\\_color\\_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_color/color_sensing_color_shape.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠāds piemērs būtu jāveic **“Disk Maze Playground”**. Robots pārvietosies uz priekšu ar nolaistu pildspalvu, tiklīdz tiks uztverts objekts ar krāsu, pēc tam tiks nomainīta pildspalvas krāsa un robots apgriezīsies otrādi.\n\n![color\\_sensing\\_color\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_color/color_sensing_color_example.png)\n\n## Vērtību uzraudzīšana\n\nLai uzraudzītu bloka **“Krāsu uztveršana”** vērtību, atzīmējiet norādīšanas bloka izvēles rūtiņu.\n\n![color\\_sensing\\_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_color/color_sensing_checkbox.png)\n\nPēc tam atveriet uzraudzīšanas konsoli, lai skatītu norādīšanas blokus, kas tika atlasīti uzraudzīšanai.\n\n![color\\_sensing\\_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_color/color_sensing_monitor.png)"},"optical_near_object":{"name":"optical_near_object","markdown":"# Acs sensors tuviem objektiem\n\nNorāda, vai krāsu uztverošais acu sensors ir pietiekami tuvu objektam, lai uztvertu krāsu.\n\n![color\\_near\\_object](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_near_object/color_near_object.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Krāsa pie objekta”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja krāsu sensors ir pietiekami tuvu objektam, kuram ir uztverama krāsa.\n\nBloks **“Krāsa pie objekta”** norāda **“nepatiess”** (Patiess), ja krāsu sensors nav pietiekami tuvu objektam, kuram ir uztverama krāsa.\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru krāsu sensora aci izmantot. Priekšējais acu sensors ir uzstādīts robota priekšpusē un ir pavērsts uz priekšu, bet apakšējais acu sensors ir uzstādīts robota apakšā un ir pavērsts uz leju.\n\n![color\\_near\\_object\\_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_near_object/color_near_object_eyes.png)\n\nBūla bloks **“Krāsa pie objekta”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess), un to var ievietot blokos ar tukšu sešstūru laukumu.\n\n![color\\_near\\_object\\_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_near_object/color_near_object_shape.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠāds piemērs būtu jāveic **“Disk Maze Playground”**. Robots pārvietosies uz priekšu, tiklīdz apakšējais acu sensors norāda, ka ir tuvu objektam ar uztveramu krāsu, pēc tam robots pārvietosies atpakaļgaitā.\n\n![color\\_near\\_object\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_near_object/color_near_object_example.png)\n\n## Vērtību uzraudzīšana\n\nLai uzraudzītu bloka **“Krāsa pie objekta”** vērtību, atzīmējiet norādīšanas bloka izvēles rūtiņu.\n\n![color\\_near\\_object\\_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_near_object/color_near_object_checkbox.png)\n\nPēc tam atveriet uzraudzīšanas konsoli, lai skatītu norādīšanas blokus, kas tika atlasīti uzraudzīšanai.\n\n![color\\_near\\_object\\_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/optical_near_object/color_near_object_monitor.png)"},"or":{"name":"or","markdown":"# Vai\n\nNorāda, ja vieno no divām Būla vērtībām ir patiesa.\n\n![or](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/or/or.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Vai”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), kad viena (vai abas) vērtība ir **“true”** (Patiess).\n\nBloks **“Vai”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), kad abas vērtības ir **“false”** (Nepatiess).\n\nBūla bloks **“Vai”** norāda vērtību **“true”** (Patiess) vai **“false”** (Nepatiess). Bloku var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir tukšs sešstūru laukums.\n\n![or\\_sensors2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/or/or_sensors2.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nBloks **“Vai”** norāda **“true”** (Patiess), ja **distances sensors** uztver objektu un/vai **priekšējais acu sensors** uztver objektu.\n\nBloks **“Vai”** norāda **“false”** (Nepatiess), ja **distances sensors** neuztver objektu un/vai arī **priekšējais acu sensors** neuztver objektu.\n\n![or\\_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/or/or_truth.png)"},"position":{"name":"position","markdown":"# Robota pozīcija\n\nNorāda robota koordinātu X vai Y pozīciju.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position/position_of_robot_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Robota pozīcija”** norāda robota koordinātu X vai Y pozīciju veselos skaitļos, kas apzīmē milimetrus (mm) vai collas.\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru koordinātu norādīt — koordinātu X vai koordinātu Y.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position/position_of_robot_coord.png)\n\nIzvēlieties, vai vērtība tiks norādīta milimetros vai collās.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position/position_of_robot_unit.png)\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Robota pozīcija”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position/position_of_robot_inserted.png)\n\n## Vērtību uzraudzīšana\n\nLai uzraudzītu bloka **“Robota pozīcija”** vērtību, atzīmējiet norādīšanas bloka izvēles rūtiņu.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position/position_of_robot_checkbox.png)\n\nPēc tam atveriet uzraudzīšanas konsoli, lai skatītu norādīšanas blokus, kas tika atlasīti uzraudzīšanai.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position/position_of_robot_monitor.png)"},"position_angle":{"name":"position_angle","markdown":"# Pozīcijas leņķis grādos\n\nNorāda robota leņķi grādos.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_angle\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Pozīcijas leņķis grādos”** norāda pašreizējo robota leņķi.\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Pozīcijas leņķis grādos”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_angle\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_inserted.png)\n\n## Vērtību uzraudzīšana\n\nLai uzraudzītu bloka **“Pozīcijas leņķis grādos”** vērtību, atzīmējiet norādīšanas bloka izvēles rūtiņu.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_angle\\_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_checkbox.png)\n\nPēc tam atveriet uzraudzīšanas konsoli, lai skatītu norādīšanas blokus, kas tika atlasīti uzraudzīšanai.\n\n![position\\_of\\_robot\\_angle\\_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_monitor.png)"},"print":{"name":"print","markdown":"# Drukāt\n\nDrukā vērtības vai tekstu drukas konsolē.\n\n![look\\_print](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/print/look_print.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Drukāt”** drukā datus uzraudzīšanas loga cilnē.\n\n![monitor\\_tab\\_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/print/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nVisi norādīšanas bloki pēc noklusējuma tiks drukāti kā veseli skaitļi. Izmantojiet bloku **“Iestatīt drukāšanas precizitāti”**, lai pielāgotu drukātā skaitļa norādīto decimāldaļu.\n\nDrukā vārdus un skaitļus.\n\n![print\\_words](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/print/print_words.png)\n\nDrukā norādītā mainīgā vērtību.\n\n![print\\_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/print/print_variable1.png)\n\nDrukā norādītā operatora aprēķinu.\n\n![print\\_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/print/print_addition.png)\n\nDrukā norādītā sensora vai ierīces vērtību.\n\n![print\\_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/print/print_sensor.png)"},"procedures_call":{"name":"procedures_call","markdown":"# Mani bloki\n\nSadaļā “Mani bloki” ir iespējams izveidot pielāgotus blokus.\n\n![my\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_call/my_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nSadaļu **“Mani bloki”** izmanto, lai izveidotu bloku virkni, kuru iespējams atkārtoti ievietot projektā.\n\nPielāgotos blokus sadaļā **“Mani bloki”** izveido, izmantojot pogu **“Izveidot bloku”**.\n\n![make\\_a\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_call/make_a_block.png)\n\nSadaļas **“Mani bloki”** pielāgotajiem blokiem var būt dažādi parametri — teksta apzīmējumi, skaitliskie mainīgie un Būla mainīgie, lai pielāgotajiem blokiem dotu papildu funkcionalitāti.\n\n![make\\_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_call/make_variable.png)\n\nLai izmantotu pielāgoto bloku no sadaļas **“Mani bloki”**, pievienojiet **“Mani bloki”** definēto bloku pie pamata bloka. Kad programma ir nonākusi pie pielāgotā bloka no sadaļas **“Mani bloki”**, izveidotais steks pielāgotajā blokā no sadaļas **“Mani bloki”** tiks palaists, izmantojot noteiktās parametru vērtības.\n\n![trigger\\_function](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_call/trigger_function.png)\n\nPievienojiet bloku steku “definētajā” pamata blokā no sadaļas **“Mani bloki”**, lai iestatītu darbības, kuras tiks veiktas, kad tiek izmantots sadaļas **“Mani bloki”** pielāgotais bloks.\n\n![attach\\_function](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_call/attach_function.png)\n\n***Piezīme! Izveidojot vairākus sadaļas “Mani bloki” blokus ar vienādiem nosaukumiem un mainīgajiem var izraisīt kļūdas, kad palaidīsiet savu projektu Rotaļlaukumā.***\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā robots izmantos sadaļas **“Mani bloki”** pielāgoto bloku, lai:\n\n* Pārvietoties uz priekšu 300 mm\n* Uzzīmēt 300 mm kvadrātu\n* Pagriezt pa labi par 15 grādiem\n* Iestatīt pildspalvas krāsu sarkanu\n* Uzzīmēt 400mm kvadrātu\n\n![square\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_call/square_example.png)"},"procedures_definition":{"name":"procedures_definition","markdown":"# Mani bloki\n\nSadaļā “Mani bloki” ir iespējams izveidot pielāgotus blokus.\n\n![my\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_definition/my_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nSadaļu **“Mani bloki”** izmanto, lai izveidotu bloku virkni, kuru iespējams atkārtoti ievietot projektā.\n\nPielāgotos blokus sadaļā **“Mani bloki”** izveido, izmantojot pogu **“Izveidot bloku”**.\n\n![make\\_a\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_definition/make_a_block.png)\n\nSadaļas **“Mani bloki”** pielāgotajiem blokiem var būt dažādi parametri — teksta apzīmējumi, skaitliskie mainīgie un Būla mainīgie, lai pielāgotajiem blokiem dotu papildu funkcionalitāti.\n\n![make\\_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_definition/make_variable.png)\n\nLai izmantotu pielāgoto bloku no sadaļas **“Mani bloki”**, pievienojiet **“Mani bloki”** definēto bloku pie pamata bloka. Kad programma ir nonākusi pie pielāgotā bloka no sadaļas **“Mani bloki”**, izveidotais steks pielāgotajā blokā no sadaļas **“Mani bloki”** tiks palaists, izmantojot noteiktās parametru vērtības.\n\n![trigger\\_function](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_definition/trigger_function.png)\n\nPievienojiet bloku steku “definētajā” pamata blokā no sadaļas **“Mani bloki”**, lai iestatītu darbības, kuras tiks veiktas, kad tiek izmantots sadaļas **“Mani bloki”** pielāgotais bloks.\n\n![attach\\_function](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_definition/attach_function.png)\n\n***Piezīme! Izveidojot vairākus sadaļas “Mani bloki” blokus ar vienādiem nosaukumiem un mainīgajiem var izraisīt kļūdas, kad palaidīsiet savu projektu Rotaļlaukumā.***\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā robots izmantos sadaļas **“Mani bloki”** pielāgoto bloku, lai:\n\n* Pārvietoties uz priekšu 300 mm\n* Uzzīmēt 300 mm kvadrātu\n* Pagriezt pa labi par 15 grādiem\n* Iestatīt pildspalvas krāsu sarkanu\n* Uzzīmēt 400mm kvadrātu\n\n![square\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/procedures_definition/square_example.png)"},"random":{"name":"random","markdown":"# Nejaušs\n\nNorāda nejaušu vērtību, kas atbilst diapazonam starp zemāko un lielāko bloka vērtību.\n\n![random](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/random/random.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloku **“Nejaušs”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\nBloks **“Nejaušs”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n## Piemēri\n\nŠajā piemērā tiek izmantots bloks **“Nejaušs”**, lai iegūtu nejaušu piedziņas sistēmas ātrumu.\n\n![random\\_drivetrain](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/random/random_drivetrain.png)\n\nŠajā piemērā tiek izmantots bloks **“Nejaušs”**, lai iegūtu nejaušu uzgaidīšanas bloka garumu.\n\n![random\\_control](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/random/random_control.png)"},"remainder":{"name":"remainder","markdown":"# Atlikums\n\nDala pirmo vērtību ar otru vērtību un norāda iegūto atlikumu.\n\n![remainder](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/remainder/remainder.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Atlikums”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nSkaitļi, kas dalās vienādi, uzrādīs nulles atlikumu.\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Atlikums”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā tiks dalīts 7 un 2, pēc tam ar iegūto atlikumu tiks pagriezts pa labi “VEX VR” robots par 1 grādu.\n\n![remainder\\_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/remainder/remainder_numbers.png)"},"repeat":{"name":"repeat","markdown":"# Atkārtot\n\nCikla bloks, kas atbilstoši iestatītajam skaitam, atkārto tajā ievietoto bloku darbības.\n\n![repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/repeat/repeat.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nVispirms ievadiet vērtību, kas norādīs skaitu, cik reizes **jāatkārto** stekā ievietoto bloku darbības.\n\nBloks **“Atkārtot”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nCikla blokus var arī ievietot vienu otrā. Šādu metodi sauc par ligzdošanu, kuru izmantojot var ietaupīt laiku, veidojot dažādas programmas darbības.\n\n![repeat\\_select](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/repeat/repeat_select.png)"},"repeat_until":{"name":"repeat_until","markdown":"# Atkārtot, tiklīdz\n\nCikla bloks, kas atkārto ievietoto bloku darbības, tiklīdz Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“true”** (Patiess).\n\n![repeat\\_until](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/repeat_until/repeat_until.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Atkārtot, tiklīdz”** pārbauda Būla apstākli tikai katra cikla sākumā.\n\nJa Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“false”** (Nepatiess), tad tiks veiktas darbības blokiem, kuri ir ievietoti blokā **“Atkārtot, tiklīdz”**.\n\nJa Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“true”** (Patiess), tad bloki, kas ir ievietoti blokā **“Atkārtot, tiklīdz”**, tiks izlaisti.\n\nCikla blokā **“Atkārtot, tiklīdz”** var ievietot sešstūru malu blokus, kā apstākļa vērtību.\n\n![repeat\\_until](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/repeat_until/repeat_until_2.png)"},"reset_timer":{"name":"reset_timer","markdown":"# Atiestatīt taimeri\n\nAtiestata robota smadzenes taimeri.\n\n![resetTimer](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/reset_timer/resetTimer.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nRobota smadzenes taimeris tiek palaists katra projekta sākumā. Bloks **“Atiestatīt taimeri”** tiek izmantots, lai atiestatītu taimeri atpakaļ uz 0 sekundēm.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā taimeris tiks atiestatīts pirms piedziņas sistēma tik pārvietota uz priekšu 3 sekundes. 3 sekundēm izrietot, piedziņas sistēma tiks apturēta.\n\n![resetTimer\\_exampleRevised](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/reset_timer/resetTimer_exampleRevised.png)"},"round":{"name":"round","markdown":"# Noapaļot\n\nNoapaļo vērtību uz tuvāko veselo skaitli.\n\n![round](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/round/round.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nDecimāldaļskaitļi, kas ir 0,5 un lielāki, tiek **noapaļoti** uz augšu, bet decimāldaļskaitļi, kas ir mazāki par 0,5, tiek **noapaļoti** uz leju.\n\nBloku **“Noapaļot”** var ievietot blokos ar tukšiem apaļu malu laukumiem, kā arī paša bloka apaļu malu laukumos var ievietot citus apaļu malu blokus.\n\nBloks **“Noapaļot”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![round\\_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/round/round_numbers.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā tiek noapaļots 2,7, pēc tam “VEX VR” robots pārvietojas uz priekšu par 3 collām.\n\n![round\\_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/round/round_variables.png)"},"set_2d_array_to":{"name":"set_2d_array_to","markdown":"# Iestatīt 2D sarakstu\n\nIestata visiem 2D saraksta vienumiem ievadīto vērtību.\n\n![set\\_2d\\_list\\_to](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzveidojot 2D sarakstu, ir iespējams ievadīt saraksta **kolonu** skaitu no 1 līdz 10 un saraksta **rindu** skaitu no 1 līdz 10.\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru 2D sarakstu lietot. Atlasīto 2D sarakstu var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![set\\_list\\_to\\_options](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_options.png)\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt 2D sarakstu”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![set\\_list\\_to](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_inserted.png)"},"set_array_to":{"name":"set_array_to","markdown":"# Iestatīt sarakstu\n\nIestata visiem saraksta vienumiem ievadīto vērtību.\n\n![set\\_list\\_to](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_array_to/set_list_to.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzveidojot sarakstu, ir iespējams iestatīt saraksta garumu no 1 līdz 10.\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru sarakstu lietot. Atlasīto sarakstu var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![set\\_list\\_to\\_options](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_array_to/set_list_to_options.png)\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt sarakstu”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![set\\_list\\_to](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_array_to/set_list_to_2.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā sarakstam tiks iestatītas sākotnējās vērtības, pēc tam tiks norādīts saraksta 3. vienums (90), lai iestatītu piedziņas rotāciju.\n\n![set\\_list\\_to\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_array_to/set_list_to_example.png)"},"set_boolean_variable":{"name":"set_boolean_variable","markdown":"# Iestatīt Būla mainīgo\n\nIestata Būla mainīgajam doto vērtību.\n\n![set\\_boolean\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru Būla mainīgo izmantosiet. Būla mainīgo var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![set\\_boolean\\_block\\_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_choice.png)\n\nIzvēlieties Būla mainīgo.\n\n![set\\_boolean\\_block\\_value](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_value.png)\n\nBlokā **“Iestatīt Būla mainīgo”** var ievietot sešstūru malu blokus.\n\n![set\\_boolean\\_block\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_inserted.png)"},"set_bumper":{"name":"set_bumper","markdown":"# Iestatīt LED saskares slēdža krāsu\n\nIestata “VEX VR” saskares slēdža krāsu.\n\n![set\\_bumper\\_color](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_bumper/set_bumper_color.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kāda krāsa tiks uzrādīta.\n\n![set\\_bumper\\_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_bumper/set_bumper_color2.png)\n\nLai izslēgtu **LED saskares slēdzi**, iestatiet krāsu ar vērtību “off” (izslēgta).\n\nŠajā piemērā tiks iestatīta zaļa **LED saskares slēdža** krāsa.\n\n![set\\_bumper\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_bumper/set_bumper_example.png)"},"set_drive_heading":{"name":"set_drive_heading","markdown":"# Iestatīt kustības virzību\n\nIestata piedziņas sistēmas žiroskopa virzības vērtību.\n\n![setDriveHeading\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_drive_heading/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloku **“Iestatīt kustības virzību”** var izmantot, lai iestatītu piedziņas sistēmas pozīciju ar jebkādu virzību. Šo bloku var izmantot, lai atiestatītu piedziņas sistēmas žiroskopa orientāciju, ja virzībai ir iestatīta vērtība 0.\n\nBlokam **“Iestatīt kustības virzību”** var iestatīt vērtību ar **0 līdz 360** grādiem.\n\nBloks **“iestatīt kustības virzību”** atpazīst veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus."},"set_drive_rotation":{"name":"set_drive_rotation","markdown":"# Iestatīt piedziņas rotāciju\n\nIestata piedziņas sistēmas rotācijas leņķi.\n\n![drivetrain\\_setdriverotation\\_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_drive_rotation/drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloku **“Iestatīt piedziņas rotāciju”** var izmantot, lai iestatītu piedziņas sistēmas rotācijas leņķi, izmantojot jebkādu pozitīvo vai negatīvo vērtību.\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt piedziņas rotāciju”** atpazīst veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā robots pagriezīsies pavisam kopā par 210 grādiem.\n\n![turntorotation\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_drive_rotation/turntorotation_example.png)\n\n- Pagriezieties pa labi (pulksteņrādītāju virzienā), veicot 120 grādu rotāciju.\n- Iestatiet robota pašreizējās pozīcijas rotāciju uz -45 grādiem.\n- Pagriezieties pa labi (pulksteņrādītāju virzienā), veicot papildu 90 grādus (no -45 līdz +45 grādiem), kas atbilst iepriekšējā bloka iestatītajai rotācijas vērtībai."},"set_drive_velocity":{"name":"set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Iestatīt kustības ātrumu\n\nIestata piedziņas sistēmas ātrumu.\n\n![set\\_drive\\_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt kustības ātrumu”** atpazīst vērtības diapazonā no **0% līdz 100%**. Šo bloku var izmantot, lai iegūtu precīzāku kustību no robota. Noklusējuma ātrums ir **50%**\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā piedziņas sistēmai tiks iestatīts 10% ātrums pirms kustības uzsākšanas.\n\n![set\\_drive\\_velocity\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity_example.png)"},"set_item_of_2d_array":{"name":"set_item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Aizvietot 2D saraksta vienumu\n\nIestata 2D saraksta vienumam jaunu ievadītu vērtību.\n\n![replace\\_item\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIevadiet saraksta aizvietojamā vienuma pozīcijas rindu (pirmais skaitlis) un kolonu (otrais skaitlis).\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru sarakstu lietot. Atlasīto sarakstu var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![replace\\_item\\_options](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_options.png)\n\nBloks **“Aizvietot vienumu”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![replace\\_item\\_block\\_2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block_2.png)"},"set_item_of_array":{"name":"set_item_of_array","markdown":"# Aizvietot vienumu\n\nIestata saraksta vienumam jaunu ievadītu vērtību.\n\n![replace\\_item\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru sarakstu lietot. Atlasīto sarakstu var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![replace\\_item\\_options](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_item_of_array/replace_item_options.png)\n\nBloks **“Aizvietot vienumu”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![replace\\_item\\_block\\_2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block_2.png)"},"set_magnet_state":{"name":"set_magnet_state","markdown":"# Pieslēgt strāvu elektromagnētam\n\nIestata “VEX VR” robota elektromagnētu divos dažādos režīmos: **“pievilkt”** vai **“atlaist”**.\n\n![set\\_magnet\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nAtlasiet un iestatiet elektromagnētu, lai pievilktu vai atlaistu objektu\n\n* **pievilkt** — pievelk objektu pie elektromagnēta\n* **atlaist** — atlaiž visus objektus, ko pievilcis elektromagnēts\n\n![set\\_magnet\\_block\\_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\nŠajā piemērā tiks iestatīta robota kustība uz priekšu par 200 mm. **Elektromagnēts** tiks ieslēgts režīmā “pievilkt”, lai paceltu objektu, pēc tam robots pārvietosies par 200 mm uz priekšu un atbrīvos objektu no elektromagnēta.\n\n![set\\_magnet\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_example.png)"},"set_pen_color":{"name":"set_pen_color","markdown":"# Iestatīt pildspalvas krāsu\n\nIestata “VEX VR” pildspalvas krāsu.\n\n![set\\_pen\\_color](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_pen_color/set_pen_color.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kādā krāsā tiks zīmēta līnija.\n\n![set\\_pen\\_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_pen_color/set_pen_color2.png)\n\nŠajā piemērā tiks iestatīta zila pildspalvas krāsa.\n\n![set\\_pen\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_pen_color/set_pen_color_example.png)"},"set_print_color":{"name":"set_print_color","markdown":"# Iestatīt drukas krāsu\n\nIestatīt drukas konsoles drukātā teksta krāsu.\n\n![set\\_print\\_color](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_print_color/set_print_color.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt drukas krāsu”** tiek izmantots kopā ar bloku **“Drukāt”**, lai iestatītu drukas konsoles drukātā teksta krāsu. Var iestatīt melnu, sarkanu, zaļu un zilu krāsu.\n\n![set\\_print\\_color\\_options](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_print_color/set_print_color_options.png)\n\nBurtus, skaitļus, sensora vērtības un mainīgo vērtības var drukāt krāsā, kāda iestatīta, izmantojot bloku **“Iestatīt drukas krāsu”**.\n\n![set\\_print\\_color\\_hello](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_print_color/set_print_color_hello.png)\n\nJebkurš bloks **“drukāt”**, kas tiek izmantots pēc bloka **“Iestatīt drukas krāsu”**, drukās krāsā, kura tika pēdējo reizi iestatīta programmā.\n\n![set\\_print\\_color\\_text](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_print_color/set_print_color_text.png)"},"set_print_precision":{"name":"set_print_precision","markdown":"# Iestatīt drukāšanas precizitāti\n\nIestata ciparu skaitu, kas parādās aiz decimālzīmes, izmantojot drukāšanas blokus, lai drukātu mainīgos un vērtības drukas konsolē.\n\n![print\\_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_print_precision/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties precizitātes līmeni, kādā uz ekrāna tiks drukāti ievietotie norādīšanas bloki.\n\n* Veseli skaitļi (1) (Noklusējuma iestatījums)\n* Desmitdaļa (0,1)\n* Simtdaļa (0,01)\n* Tūkstošdaļa (0,001)\n* Visi cipari (0,000001)\n\nNorādīšanas bloki, kas tiek drukāti uz ekrānu, tiks noapaļoti uz tuvāko, precīzo skaitli. Piemēram, 1,67 tiks drukāts kā 2, ja ir izvēlēti veseli skaitļi, un 1,7, ja ir atlasīts norādīt desmitdaļas.\n\n![print\\_precision\\_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_print_precision/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_turn_velocity":{"name":"set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Iestatīt pagrieziena ātrumu\n\nIestata piedziņas sistēmas pagrieziena ātrumu.\n\n![setTurnVelocity\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt pagrieziena ātrumu”** iestatīs piedziņas sistēmas ātrumu, bet neveiks piedziņas sistēmas kustību. Kustībai būs nepieciešams pievienot bloku **“Pagriezt”**.\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt pagrieziena ātrumu”** atpazīst vērtības diapazonā no **0% līdz 100%**.\n\nIestatot piedziņas sistēmas pagrieziena ātrumu uz 0, tiks apturēta piedziņas sistēma.\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt pagrieziena ātrumu”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![setTurnVelocity\\_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā piedziņas sistēma lēnām veiks pagriezienu pa kreisi ar ātrumu iestatītu uz 25%.\n\n![setTurnVelocity\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_example.png)"},"set_variable":{"name":"set_variable","markdown":"# Iestatīt mainīgo\n\nIestata mainīgajam doto vērtību.\n\n![set\\_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_variable/set_variable.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru mainīgo izmantosiet. Mainīgo var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![set\\_variable\\_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_variable/set_variable_choose.png)\n\nBloks **“Iestatīt mainīgo”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![set\\_variable\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/set_variable/set_variable_inserted.png)"},"size_of_array":{"name":"size_of_array","markdown":"# Saraksta garums\n\nNorāda saraksta vienumu skaitu.\n\n![size\\_of\\_array](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/size_of_array/size_of_array.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties, kuru sarakstu lietot. Atlasīto sarakstu var arī pārdēvēt vai izdzēst.\n\n![size\\_of\\_array\\_select](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/size_of_array/size_of_array_select.png)\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Saraksta garums”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![size\\_of\\_array\\_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/size_of_array/size_of_array_insert.png)"},"stop_driving":{"name":"stop_driving","markdown":"# Apturēt kustību\n\nApturēt piedziņas sistēmu.\n\n![stopDriving\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/stop_driving/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Apturēt kustību”** pilnībā apturēs piedziņas sistēmas kustību.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā piedziņas sistēma tiks apturēta pēc 3 sekundēm.\n\n![stopDriving\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/stop_driving/stopDriving_example.png)"},"stop_project":{"name":"stop_project","markdown":"# Apturēt projektu\n\nAptur projekta izpildi.\n\n![stop\\_project](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/stop_project/stop_project.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Apturēt projektu”** izraisīs projekta pārtraukšanu.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠis piemērs vadīs VR Robot 500 mm kvadrāta laukumā un pēc tam apturēs projektu.\n\n![stop\\_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/stop_project/stop_project2.png)"},"subtract":{"name":"subtract","markdown":"# Atņemt\n\nAtņem vienu vērtību no otras vērtības un norāda atšķirību.\n\n![subtract](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/subtract/subtract.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Atņemt”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\nBloku **“Atņemt”** var ievietot noapaļotos laukumos, kā arī paša bloka noapaļotajos laukumos var ievietot citus noapaļotos blokus.\n\nVeic divu skaitļu atņemšanu.\n\n![subtract\\_example1](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/subtract/subtract_example1.png)\n\nAtņemšana, izmantojot vērtības un mainīgos.\n\n![subtract\\_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/subtract/subtract_example2.png)\n\nAtņemšana, izmantojot dažādas vērtības, kas iegūtas no sensoriem vai ierīcēm.\n\n![subtract\\_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/subtract/subtract_example3.png)"},"timer_value":{"name":"timer_value","markdown":"# Taimera vērtība\n\nNorāda robota smadzeņu vērtību sekundēs\n\n![timerValue\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/timer_value/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nTaimeris sāk skaitīt no 0 sekundēm, kad tiek uzsākta programma, un norāda decimāldaļskaitļa vērtības.\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Taimera vērtība”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![timerValue\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/timer_value/timerValue_inserted.png)"},"turn":{"name":"turn","markdown":"# Pagriezt\n\nNepārtraukti veic piedziņas sistēmas pagriešanu.\n\n![turn\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn/turn_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Pagriezt”** nepārtraukti darbina piedziņas sistēmu, kamēr netiek ievietots jauns piedziņas sistēmas bloks vai programma tiek apturēta.\n\nIzvēlieties virzienu, kādā tiks pagriezta piedziņas sistēma.\n\n![turn\\_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn/turn_direction.png)"},"turn_for":{"name":"turn_for","markdown":"# Pagriezt par\n\nVeic piedziņas sistēmu rotāciju atbilstoši pēc dotā grādu skaitļa.\n\n![turn\\_for\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_for/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nIzvēlieties virzienu, kādā tiks pagriezta piedziņas sistēma.\n\n![turn\\_for\\_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_for/turn_for_direction.png)\n\nIestatiet, cik tālu piedziņas sistēma pārvietosies, ievadot grādu skaitli.\n\nBloks **“Pagriezt par”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![turn\\_for\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_for/turn_for_block_2.png)\n\nPēc noklusējuma, citi bloki darbosies ar funkciju **“gaidīt”**, kamēr piedziņas sistēma būs pabeigusi pagriezienu.\n\nSpiežot uz bultiņas, jūs variet atlasīt funkciju **“un negaidīt”**, kas atļauj citiem blokiem turpināt darboties, kamēr piedziņas sistēma veic pagriezienus.\n\n![turn\\_for\\_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_for/turn_for_anddontwait.png)"},"turn_to_heading":{"name":"turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Pagrieziena virzība\n\nPagriež piedziņas sistēmu noteiktā virzībā, izmantojot iebūvēto žiroskopa sensoru.\n\n![turn\\_to\\_heading\\_block](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloku **“Pagrieziena virzība”** var izmantot, lai pagrieztu virzību piedziņas sistēmai pulksteņrādītāja virzienā.\n\nVadoties pēc pašreizējās žiroskopa virzības, bloks **“Pagrieziena virzība”** nosaka, kādā virzienā jāveic pagrieziens.\n\nBlokam **“Pagrieziena virzība”** var iestatīt vērtību ar **0,00 līdz 359,99** grādiem.\n\nBloks **“Pagrieziena virzība”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā piedziņas sistēma veiks četrus pagriezienus:\n\n![turn\\_to\\_heading\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\n- 45 grādu pagrieziens pa labi\n- 90 grādu pagrieziens pa labi\n- 270 grādu pagrieziens pa labi\n- 180 grādu pagrieziens pa kreisi\n\nPēc noklusējuma, citi bloki darbosies ar funkciju **“gaidīt”**, kamēr piedziņas sistēma būs pabeigusi pagriezienu.\n\nSpiežot uz bultiņas, jūs variet atlasīt funkciju **“un negaidīt”**, kas atļauj citiem blokiem turpināt darboties, kamēr piedziņas sistēma veic pagriezienus.\n\n![turn\\_to\\_heading\\_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_anddontwait.png)"},"turn_to_rotation":{"name":"turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Pagrieziena rotācija\n\nPagriež piedziņas sistēmu noteiktā rotācijas leņķī, izmantojot iebūvēto žiroskopa sensoru.\n\n![drivetrain\\_turntorotation\\_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_iq.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloku **“Pagrieziena rotācija”** var izmantot, lai pagrieztu piedziņas sistēmu, ievadot pozitīvu (pulksteņrādītāja virzienā) vai negatīvu (pretēji pulksteņrādītāja virzienam) vērtību.\n\nVadoties pēc pašreizējās piedziņas sistēmas **rotācijas** vērtības, bloks **“Pagrieziena rotācija”** nosaka, kādā virzienā jāveic pagrieziens.\n\nBloks **“Pagrieziena rotācija”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n## Piemērs\n\nŠajā piemērā piedziņas sistēma veiks četrus pagriezienus:\n\n![turn\\_to\\_rotation\\_example](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_to_rotation/turn_to_rotation_example.png)\n\n- Pagrieziens pa labi (pulksteņrādītāja virzienā) uz 90 grādiem\n- Pagrieziens pa labi (pulksteņrādītāja virzienā) uz 180 grādiem\n- Pagrieziens pa kreisi (pretēji pulksteņrādītāja virzienam) uz -45 grādu\n- Pagrieziens pa labi (pulksteņrādītāja virzienā) uz 0 grādiem\n\nPēc noklusējuma, citi bloki darbosies ar funkciju **“gaidīt”**, kamēr piedziņas sistēma būs pabeigusi pagriezienu.\n\nSpiežot uz bultiņas, jūs variet atlasīt funkciju **“un negaidīt”**, kas atļauj citiem blokiem turpināt darboties, kamēr piedziņas sistēma veic pagriezienus.\n\n![drivetrain\\_turntorotation\\_dontwait\\_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq.png)"},"variable":{"name":"variable","markdown":"# Mainīgais\n\nNorāda skaitlisko vērtību, kas piešķirta mainīgajam.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/variable/variable.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Mainīgais”** tiek izmantots, lai norādītu skaitlisko vērtību, kas ieadīta mainīgajam. Izmantojiet bloku **“Iestatīt mainīgo”**, lai iestatītu vai atjauninātu mainīgā skaitlisko vērtību.\n\n![variable\\_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/variable/variable_cursor.png)\n\nNorādīšanas bloku **“Mainīgais”** var ievietot blokos, kuriem ir noapaļots tukšs laukums.\n\n![variable\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/variable/variable_inserted.png)\n\nMainīgos izveido, izmantojot pogu **“Izveidot mainīgo”**.\n\n![make\\_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/variable/make_variable.png)"},"wait":{"name":"wait","markdown":"# Gaidīt\n\nUzgaida noteiktu laika periodu pirms sāk nākamā bloka darbību.\n\n![wait](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/wait/wait.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nNorādiet laiku, cik ilgi **jāgaida**.\n\nBloks **“Gaidīt”** atpazīst decimāldaļskaitļus, veselus skaitļus vai skaitliskus blokus.\n\n![wait\\_select](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/wait/wait_select.png)"},"wait_until":{"name":"wait_until","markdown":"# Gaidīt, tiklīdz\n\nUzgaida, tiklīdz Būla bloks norāda **“true”** (Patiess), lai sāktu nākamā bloka darbību.\n\n![wait\\_until](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/wait_until/wait_until.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Gaidīt, tiklīdz”** atkārtoti pārbaudīs Būla norādīšanas bloku, un nepāries uz nākamo bloka darbību, kamēr Būla norādīšanas blokā netiks norādīts **“true”** (Patiess).\n\nBūla blokā **“Gaidīt, tiklīdz”** var ievietot sešstūru malu blokus.\n\n![wait\\_until\\_2](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/wait_until/wait_until_2.png)"},"when_started":{"name":"when_started","markdown":"# Kad startēts\n\nTiek palaists pievienoto bloku steks, kad tiek palaists projekts.\n\n![when\\_started](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/when_started/when_started.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nFunkcija **“Kad startēts”** tiks palaista, kad projekts tiek startēts, izmantojot “VEXCODE VR” bloku pogu **“Sākt”**.\n\nVisiem jaunajiem projektiem automātiski tiks pievienots bloks **“Kad startēts”**."},"while":{"name":"while","markdown":"# Kamēr\n\nCikla bloks, kas atkārto ievietoto bloku darbības, tiklīdz Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“true”** (Patiess).\n\n![kamēr](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/while/while.png)\n\n## Kā lietot\n\nBloks **“Kamēr”** pārbauda Būla apstākli tikai katra cikla sākumā.\n\nJa Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“true”** (Patiess), tad bloki, kas ir ievietoti blokā **“Kamēr”**, tiks darbināti.\n\nJa Būla apstāklis tiek norādīts kā **“false”** (Nepatiess), tad bloki, kas ir ievietoti blokā **“Kamēr”**, tiks izlaisti.\n\nCikla blokā **“Kamēr”** var ievietot sešstūru malu blokus, kā apstākļa vērtību.\n\n![while\\_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/lv/while/while_inserted.png)"}}},"pl":{"blocks":{"add":{"name":"add","markdown":"# Dodaj\n\nDodaje do siebie dwie dowolne wartości i podaje sumę.\n\n![add](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/add/add.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Dodaj** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nBlok **dodaj** pasuje do bloków, które posiadają okrągłe wolne miejsca i mogą zawierać inne okrągłe bloki. \n\n\nDodawanie dwóch liczb.\n\n![add_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/add/add_numbers.png)\n\nDodaj wartości ze zmiennych.\n\n![add_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/add/add_variables.png)  \n\nDodaj wartości z czujników lub urządzeń.\n\n![add_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/add/add_sensors.png)\n\n"},"and":{"name":"and","markdown":"# I\n\nPotwierdza czy oba warunki logiczne są prawdziwe. \n\n![and](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/and/and.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **i** raportuje **prawda** kiedy oba warunki są **prawda**. \n\nBlok **i** raportuje **fałsz** kiedy jeden lub oba warunki są **fałsz**.\n\nBlok **i** raportuje **prawda** lub **fałsz** i pasuje do bloków z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![and_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/and/and_shape.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie sprawdzamy czy **OkoPrzednie** znajduje się bloko obiektu i ten obiekt jest czerwony.\n\n![and_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/and/and_sensors.png)\n\n\nBlok **i** zwróci **prawda** kiedy zarówno **OkoPrzednie** jest blisko obiektu i **OkoPrzednie** wykrywa go jako czerwony.\n\nBlok **i** zwróci **fałsz** kiedy **OkoPrzednie** nie jest blisko obiektu, kiedy **OkoPrzednie** nie rozpoznaje go jako czerwonego lub oba.\n\n![and_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/and/and_truth.png)"},"boolean_variable":{"name":"boolean_variable","markdown":"# Zmienna logiczna\n\nPodaje wartość zmiennej logicznej.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/boolean_variable/boolean_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **zmienna logiczna** zwraca wartość **prawda** lub **fałsz** w zależności od przechowywanej wewnątrz zmiennej. Użyj bloku **set zmienna logiczna**, aby ustawić lub zaktualizować wartość zmiennej logicznej.\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/boolean_variable/boolean_set_value.png)\n\nBlok **zmienna logiczna** zmiennej logicznej zwraca **prawda** lub **fałsz** value i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/boolean_variable/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nWartość logiczna jest tworzona po naciśnięciu przycisku **stwórz zmienną logiczną**.\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/boolean_variable/boolean_button.png)  \n\n## Monitorowanie Wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartość bloku **zmienna logiczna**, zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![boolean_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/boolean_variable/boolean_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić **zmienna logiczna** bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![boolean_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/boolean_variable/boolean_monitor.png)\n\n"},"break":{"name":"break","markdown":"# Przerwa\n\nPrzerywa istniejącą pętlę.\n\n![break](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/break/break.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nPo dodaniu do powtarzającego się bloku „C”, blok ** przerwa ** zakończy pętlę, w której się aktualnie znajduje.\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie układ napędowy porusza się do przodu, a czujnik oka sprawdza czy jest blisko obiektu. Jeśli obiekt zostanie wykryty, blok **przerwa* spowoduje wyjście programy z pętli na zawsze. Następnie wykonywany jest blok **zatrzymaj jazdę** więc układ napędowy zatrzyma się.\n\n![break_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/break/break_example.png)"},"broadcast":{"name":"broadcast","markdown":"# Nadaj\n\nBlok nadaj nadaje komunikat aby odtworzyć wszystkie stosy zaczynające sie od **kiedy otrzymam**, bloki te oczekuja na nadawaną wiadomość.\n\n![broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/broadcast/broadcast.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nStos, który wysyła ** nadaj **, będzie nadal uruchamiał bloki w tym samym czasie, co inne stosy, które odbierają wiadomość.\n\nWybierz, która wwiadomość ma być nadana **nadaj**. Można również stworzyć nową wiadomość.\n\n![broadcast_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/broadcast/broadcast_select.png)"},"broadcast_and_wait":{"name":"broadcast_and_wait","markdown":"# Nadaj i czekaj\n\nNadaje wiadomośc, aby aktywować wszystkie stosy, które zaczynaja się od  **when I receive** blok ten czeka na otzrymanie wiadomości jednocześnie blokując resztę stosu.\n\n![broadcast_and_wait_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/broadcast_and_wait/broadcast_and_wait_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nStos wysyłający zgłoszenie **broadcast and wait** zatrzyma się, aż inne stosy odbierające zgłoszoną wiadomość zostaną zakończone.\n\nWybierz wiadomość do **broadcast**. Można również utworzyć nową wiadomość.\n\n![broadcast_and_wait_event_option](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/broadcast_and_wait/broadcast_and_wait_event_option.png)"},"bumper":{"name":"bumper","markdown":"# Naciśnięto zderzak?\n\nInformuje o naciśnięciu włącznika zderzaka.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Naciśnięto zderzak** komunikuje **prawda** jeśli włącznik zderzaka został naciśnięty.\n\n**Naciśnięto zderzak** komunikuje **fałsz** jeśli włącznik zderzaka nie został naciśnięty.\n\nWybierz, którego przełącznika zderzaka użyć.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nBlok **Naciśnięto zderzak** zwraca wartość logiczną **prawda** lub **fałsz** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie Wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartośćbloku  **Naciśnięto zderzak** zaznacz kwadrat wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę monitorowania, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)\n\n"},"change_variable":{"name":"change_variable","markdown":"# Zmień zmienną\n\nZmienia zmienną o podaną wartość.\n\n![change_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/change_variable/change_variable.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz zmienną, której chcesz użyć - wybranej zmiennej można również zmienić nazwę lub ją usunąć.\n\n![change_variable_options](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/change_variable/change_variable_options.png)\n\nBlok **zmień zmienną** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![change_variable_value](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/change_variable/change_variable_value.png)\n\n## Przykład\nW tym przykładzie zmieniamy zmienną, która ustawia prędkość napędu, o 10 za każdym razem gdy zotanie wykonana pętla **powtórz pętlę**.\n\n![change_variable_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/change_variable/change_variable_example2.png)"},"clear_all_rows":{"name":"clear_all_rows","markdown":"# Wyczyść wszystkie wiersze\n\nCzyści wszystkie wiersze w Konsoli.\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nUżyj bloku **Wyczyść wszystkie wiersze** aby usunąć wszystkie linie w Konsoli\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie wyświetlasz \"Hello\" przez jedną sekundę a następnie czyścisz cały ekran przed wyświetlenniem \"Goodbye\".\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"clear_row":{"name":"clear_row","markdown":"# Wyczyść wiersz\n\nCzyści pojedynczy wiersz na ekranie VEX IQ.\n\n![clear_row](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/clear_row/clear_row.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **Clear row** akceptuje liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne z przedziału od ** 1 do 5 **, aby ustawić wiersz do wyczyszczenia.\n\n![row_column_index](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/clear_row/row_column_index.png)  \n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie wyświetlimy \"Hello\" w wierszu 2 w kolumnie 1 przez 2 sekundy przed wyczyszczeniem wiersza.\n\n![clear_row_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/clear_row/clear_row_example.png)"},"comment":{"name":"comment","markdown":"# Komentarz\n\nUmożliwia programistom pisanie informacji pomagających opisać program.\n\n![comment_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/comment/comment_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nProfesjonalni programiści używają komentarzy w swoich programach, aby pomóc innym zrozumieć ich kod. Komentarze mogą również pomóc w rozwiązywaniu problemów i we współpracy.\n\nDodaj tekst do bloku **Comment**, najpierw wybierając przezroczysty prostokąt i usuwając istniejący tekst. Następnie wpisz nowy tekst.\n\n![comment_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/comment/comment_example3.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nKomentarze nie zmieniają programu ani otaczających go bloków.\n\n![comment_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/comment/comment_example2.png)\n\n"},"distance_distance":{"name":"distance_distance","markdown":"# Odległość od\n\nPodaje odległość do najbliższego obiektu.\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_distance/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, czy **distance from** ma być podawana w milimetrach czy calach.\n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_distance/distance_from_unit.png)\n\nBlok **distance from** jest używany wewnątrz bloków z okrągłymi pustymi miejscami.\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_distance/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartość odległości bloku **distance from**, zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![distance_from_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_distance/distance_from_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę , aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![distance_from_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_distance/distance_from_monitor.png)\n\n"},"distance_found":{"name":"distance_found","markdown":"# Czujnik odległości wykrył obiekt\n\nInformuje, czy wbudowany czujnik odległości znalazł obiekt przed robotem.\n\n![distance_found_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_found/distance_found_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Distance found object** podaje **true** gdy wbudowany czujnik odległości widzi obiekt lub powierzchnię w swoim polu widzenia oraz w odległości do 3000 mm od czujnika.\n\n**Distance found object** podaje **false** gdy wbudowany czujnik odległości nie widzi obiektu lub powierzchni w swoim polu widzenia oraz w odległości do 3000 mm od czujnika.\n\nBlok **distance found object** podaje wartośc logiczną **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![distance_found_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_found/distance_found_insert.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nNa boisku Castle Crashers robot jedzie do przodu, jeśli czujnik odległości wykryje obiekt, w przeciwnym razie wykona obrót w prawo o 45 °.\n\n![distance_found_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_found/distance_found_example.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie Wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartość bloku **distance found object**, zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![distance_found_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_found/distance_found_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![distance_found_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/distance_found/distance_found_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"divide":{"name":"divide","markdown":"# Podziel\n\nDzieli pierwszą wartość przez drugą wartość i podaje resztę.\n\n![divide](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/divide/divide.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Divide** akceptuje liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nBlok **divide** pasuje do bloków, które akceptują okrągłe wypełnienia, i mogą również zawierać okrągłe bloki.\n\nWprowadź liczby do podzielenia.\n\n![divide_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/divide/divide_numbers.png)\n\nPodziel przez wartości zmiennych.\n\n![divide_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/divide/divide_variables.png)\n\nPodzielić przez wartość z czujników lub urządzeń.\n\n![divide_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/divide/divide_sensors.png)\n\n"},"drive":{"name":"drive","markdown":"# Jedź\n\nZaczyna poruszać napędem na zawsze.\n\n![drive_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive/drive_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **drive** uruchomi napęd, do momentu użycia nowego bloku na zawsze lub zatrzymania programu.\n\nWybierz kierunek, w którym będzie się poruszał układ napędowy.\n\n![drive_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive/drive_direction.png)  \n"},"drive_for":{"name":"drive_for","markdown":"# Jedź o\n\nPrzesuwa układ napędowy na zadaną odległość.\n\n![drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_for/drive_for.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, w którym kierunku będzie się poruszał układ napędowy.\n\n![drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_for/drive_for_direction.png)\n\n\nUstaw, jak daleko przesunie się układ napędowy, wprowadzając wartość i wybierając jednostkę miary (cale lub milimetry).\n\nBlok **Drive for** akceptuje liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_for/drive_for_units.png)\n\nDomyślnie, inne bloki **wait** aż układ napędowy zakonczy ruch.\n\nMożna strzałka wybrać opcję **and don't wait** z listy rozwijanej - spowoduje to, że inne bloki będą nadal działać, podczas gdy układ napędowy będzie się poruszał.\n\n![drive_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_for/drive_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"drive_heading":{"name":"drive_heading","markdown":"# Pozycja jazdy\n\nPodaje kierunek, w którym zwrócony jest układ napędowy, na podstawie aktualnego położenia kątowego czujnika żyroskopowego (żyroskopowego).\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_heading/drive_heading.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **drive heading** zgłasza wzrost kursu podczas obracania zgodnie z wskazówkami zegara (w prawo).\n\n**Drive heading** podaje zakres od **0.00 do 359.99 degrees**.\n\nBlok **drive heading** jest używany wewnątrz bloków z okrągłymi pustymi miejscami.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_heading/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitoować wartośc bloku **drive heading** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![drive_heading_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_heading/drive_heading_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![drive_heading_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_heading/drive_heading_monitor.png)\n\n"},"drive_is_done":{"name":"drive_is_done","markdown":"# Zakończona jazda\n\nZgłasza, czy układ napędowy zakończył ruch.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Drive is done** zwraca **true** kiedy silniki układu napędowego zakończą swój ruch.\n\n**Drive is done** zwraca **false** kiedy silniki układu napędowego dalej sie poruszają.\n\nBlok **drive is done** zwraca wartośc logiczną **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartości **drive is done** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![drive_is_done_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![drive_is_done_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_monitor.png)"},"drive_is_moving":{"name":"drive_is_moving","markdown":"# Napęd się porusza\n\nInformuje czy układ napędowy jest w ruchu.\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Drive is moving** zwraca **true** kiedy silniki  są w ruchu.\n\n**Drive is moving** zwraca **false** kiedy silniki nie ruszają się.\n\nBlok **drive is moving** zwraca wartośc logiczną **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie Wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartość bloku **drive is moving** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![drive_is_moving_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![drive_is_moving_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_monitor.png)\n\n\n"},"drive_rotation":{"name":"drive_rotation","markdown":"# Obrót napędu w stopniach\n\nPodaje kąt obrotu układu napędowego w przypadku konfiguracji z czujnikiem żyroskopowym.\n\n![drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_rotation/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Drive rotation** zwraca **positive** kiedy napęd obraca się w **clockwise** .\n\n**Drive rotation** zwraca **negative** kiedy napęd obraca się w **counter-clockwise**.\n\nBlok **drive rotation** jest używany wewnątrz bloków z okrągłymi pustymi miejscami.\n\n![drive_rotation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAy monitorować wartość **drive rotation** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![drive_rotation_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsol, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![drive_rotation_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_monitor.png)"},"equal_to":{"name":"equal_to","markdown":"# Równe z\n\nPodaje, czy pierwsza wartość jest taka sama lub równa drugiej wartości. \n\n![equal_to](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/equal_to/equal_to.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Equal to** zwraca **true** gdy te dwie wartości są dokładnie takie same\n\n**Equal to** zwraca **false** gdy te dwie wartości nie są takie same\n\nBlok **equal to** akceptuje liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nBlok **equal to** zwraca zmienna logiczną **true** lub **false** i pasuje do bloków z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscam.\n\n![equal_to_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/equal_to/equal_to_inserted.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykładzie jazda zakończy się, jeśli odległość w mm wyniesie 500.\n\n![equal_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/equal_to/equal_to_example.png)"},"forever":{"name":"forever","markdown":"# Bez końca\n\nBlok typu „C”, który bez końca wszystkie bloki zawarte w środku.\n\n![forever](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/forever/forever.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nUmieśc bloki w bloku **forever** aby te czynności powtarzały się **forever**.\n\nMożesz wyjść z pętli **forever**umiesszczając blok **break** wewnątrz.\n\nBloki typu'C' można również umieścić jeden w drugim. Pojęcie to nazywa się zagnieżdżaniem, które może pomóc zaoszczędzić czas podczas programowania różnych zachowań.\n\n## Przykład 1\n\nRobot będzie kontynuował jazdę do przodu od jednej do trzech sekund, a następnie skręci w prawo od jednej do trzech sekund. Działanie to powtarza się, dopóki program nie zostanie zatrzymany.\n\n![forever_example_2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/forever/forever_example_2.png)\n"},"function":{"name":"function","markdown":"# Funkcje\n\nWykonuje wybraną funkcję matematyczną.\n\n![function](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/function/function.png)\n\n* **abs** - wartośc absolutna\n* **floor** - zaokrągla w dół do najbliższej liczby całkowitej\n* **ceiling** - zaokrągla w górę do najbliższej liczby całkowitej\n* **sqrt** - pierwiastek kwadratowy\n* **sin** - sinus\n* **cos** - cosinus\n* **tan** - tanges\n* **asin** - arcsine lub odwrotność sinusa\n* **acos** - arccosine lub odwrotność cosinusa\n* **atan** - arctan lub odwrotność tangensa\n* **ln** - podstawa logarytmu naturalnego e\n* **log** - logarytm o podstawie 10\n* **e ^** - potęga e\n* **10 ^** - potęga 10\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **function** akceptuje liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nWyberz mateematyczną **function** z listy opcji.\n\n![function_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/function/function_select.png)\n\nBlok **function** pasuje do bloków, które akceptują okrągłe miejsca.\n\n![function_fit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/function/function_fit.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"greater_than":{"name":"greater_than","markdown":"# Wiekse niż\n\nPodaje, czy pierwsza wartość jest większa niż druga wartość.  \n\n![greater_than](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/greater_than/greater_than.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Greater than** zwraca **true** gdy pierwsza wartość jest większa niż druga wartość.\n\n**Greater than** zwraca **false** gdy pierwsza wartość jest mniejsza lub równa drugiej wartości.\n\nBlok **greater than** akceptuje liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nBlok **greater than** zwraca wartość logiczną **true** lub **false** i pasuje do bloków z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![greater_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/greater_than/greater_than_inserted.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie układ napędowy zatrzyma się, jeśli kąt położenia w stopniach jest większy niż 90.\n\n![greater_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/greater_than/greater_than_example.png)\n"},"heading_of_gyro":{"name":"heading_of_gyro","markdown":"# Kąt kursu\n\nPodaje aktualny kurs 3-przewodowego czujnika żyroskopowego lub czujnika inercyjnego V5 w stopniach.\n\n![heading_of_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro.png)\n\n## Jak uzywać\n\nBlok **heading of** podaje zmiane kursu zgodnie ze wskazwkami zegara\n\n**Heading of** podaje zakres **0.00 to 359.99**.\n\nWybierz, który czujnik żyroskopowy / czujnik bezwładności chcesz sprawdzić.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_gyro](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro_gyro.png)\n\nBlok **heading of** jest używany w blokach z okrągłymi pustymi miejscami.\n\n![heading_of_gyro_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/heading_of_gyro/heading_of_gyro_inserted.png)\n"},"if_then":{"name":"if_then","markdown":"# Jeśli to \n\nBlok typu 'C' uruchamia bloki wewnątrz, jeśli warunek logiczny ma wartość **true**.  \n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/if_then/if_then.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **if then** 'C' sprawdzi warunek logiczny tylko raz.\n\nJeśli wartość logiczna jest **true**, blok wewnątrz **if then** zostanie wykonany.\n\nJeśli wartość logiczna jest **false**, blok wewnątrz **if then** zostanie pominięty.\n\nBlok **if then** może przyjąć sześciokątne (sześcioboczne) bloki jako warunek.\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/if_then/if_then_2.png)\n\n"},"if_then_else":{"name":"if_then_else","markdown":"# Jeśli to w przeciwnym razie\n\nBlok typu 'C' Block that uruchamia bloki wewnątrz pierwszej lub drugiej części **if then else**, na podstawie zgłoszonej wartości logicznej.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/if_then_else/if_then_else.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **if then else** sprawdzi warunek logiczny tylko raz.\n\nJeśli wartość logiczna jest **true**, blok wewnątrz **if** zostanie wykonany.\n\nJeśli wartość logiczna jest **false**, blok wewnątrz **else** zostanie wykonany.\n\nBlok **if then else** akceptuje jako warunek sześciokątne (sześcioboczne) bloki.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/if_then_else/if_then_else_2.png)"},"item_of_2d_array":{"name":"item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Element z listy 2D\n\nPodaje wartość elementu na liście 2D.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, której listy 2D chcesz użyć. Liście 2D można również zmienić nazwę lub usunąć.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nWprowadź lokalizację pozycji na liście: wiersz (pierwszy numer) i kolumna (druga liczba), która ma być raportowana. Tutaj zgłaszana jest pozycja o lokalizacji wiersz 2, kolumna 3 na liście 2D.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\nBlok **item of 2D list** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartość listy 2D, otwórz konsolę i kliknij przycisk „Dodaj listy” w prawym dolnym rogu.\n\n![array_monitor_add](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_2d_array/array_monitor_add.png)\n\nNastępnie zaznacz pole wyboru obok listy 2D, która ma zostać dodana. Jeśli zdefiniowano więcej niż jedną listę 2D, można zaznaczyć wiele pól wyboru. Kliknij „Zapisz”, aby dodać listę (listy) do konsoli monitora.\n\n![array_monitor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_2d_array/array_monitor_select.png)\n\nWróć do konsoli, aby wyświetlić listy 2D wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![array_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_2d_array/array_monitor.png)\n\n"},"item_of_array":{"name":"item_of_array","markdown":"# Element z\n\nPodaje wartość danej pozycji na liście.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_array/item_of_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz listę, której chcesz użyć. Liście można również zmienić nazwę lub ją usunąć.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_array/item_of_options.png)\n\nWprowadź numer pozycji elementu na liście. Tutaj raportowana jest druga pozycja na liście (pozycja 2).\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_array/item_of_block_2.png)\n\nBlok **item of** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie ustawimy prędkość Napędu na wartość zapisaną w pozycji 3 mojej listy. Układ napędowy będzie teraz poruszał się do przodu z prędkością 90%.\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_array/item_of_example2.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartość listy, otwórz konsolę i kliknij przycisk „Dodaj listy” w prawym dolnym rogu.\n\n![array_monitor_add](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_array/array_monitor_add.png)\n\nNastępnie zaznacz pole wyboru obok listy, którą chcesz dodać. Jeśli zdefiniowano więcej niż jedną listę, można zaznaczyć wiele pól wyboru. Kliknij „Zapisz”, aby dodać listę (listy) do konsoli.\n\n![array_monitor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_array/array_monitor_select.png)\n\nWróć do konsoli, aby wyświetlić listę(y) wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![array_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/item_of_array/array_monitor.png)\n\n"},"length_of_2d_array":{"name":"length_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Długośc listy 2D\n\nPodaje liczbę wierszy lub kolumn na liście 2D.\n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, której listy 2D chcesz użyć. Liście 2D można również zmienić nazwę lub ją usunąć.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_select.png)\n\nWybierz wartość do sprawdzenia. Lista 2D może raportować **rows** lub **columns**.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_option.png)\n\nBlok **length of 2D list** pasuje do bloków, które akceptują okrągłe bloki.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_insert.png)"},"less_than":{"name":"less_than","markdown":"# Mniej niż\n\nPodaje, czy pierwsza wartość jest mniejsza niż druga wartość.\n\n![less_than](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/less_than/less_than.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Less than** zwraca **true** gdy pierwsza wartość jest mniejsza niż druga wartość.\n\n**Less than** zwraca **false** gdy pierwsza wartość jest większa lub równa drugiej wartości.\n\nBlok **less than** zwraca wartośc logiczną **true** lub **false** i pasuje do bloków z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\nBlok **less than** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![less_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/less_than/less_than_inserted.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład pobudzi **Magnet** jeśli kierunek jazdy w stopniach jest mniejszy niż 50.\n\n![less_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/less_than/less_than_example.png)\n"},"motor_is_done":{"name":"motor_is_done","markdown":"# Ruch zakończony\n\nInformuje o zakończeniu ruchu wybranego silnika VR.\n\n![motor_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_block.png)\n\n## Jak uzywać\n\n**Motor is done** zwraca **true** kiedy wybrany silnik zakończy swój ruch.\n\n**Motor is done** zwraca **true** kiedy wybrany silnik nadal się porusza.\n\nWybierz silnik, którego chcesz użyć.\n\n![motor_is_done_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_motor.png)\n\nBlok **motor is done** zwraca wartość logiczną **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![motor_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/motor_is_done/motor_is_done_inserted.png)\n\n\n"},"motor_is_spinning":{"name":"motor_is_spinning","markdown":"# Silnik sie obraca\n\nInformuje, czy wybrany silnik VR aktualnie się obraca.\n\n![motor_is_spinning](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Motor is spinning** zwraca **true** kiedy obraca się wybrany silnik.\n\n**Motor is spinning** zwraca **false** gdy wybrany silnik jest zatrzymany.\n\nWybierz silnik, którego chcesz użyć.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning_motor.png)\n\nBlok **motor is spinning** zwraca wartośc logiczną **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![motor_is_spinning_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/motor_is_spinning/motor_is_spinning_inserted.png)\n"},"move_pen":{"name":"move_pen","markdown":"# Porusz pisakiem robota\n\nUstawia pozycję pióra VEX VR.\n\n![move_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/move_pen/move_pen.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz pozycję pióra VR\n\n![move_pen2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/move_pen/move_pen2.png)\n\n* góra - długopis nie będzie rysował linii na tablicy.\n* dół - długopis narysuje na tablicy kolorową linię.\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie narysujemy czerwoną linię na planszy.\n\n![move_pen3](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/move_pen/move_pen3.png)\n\n"},"multiply":{"name":"multiply","markdown":"# Mnożenie\n\nMnoży dowolne dwie wartości i podaje iloczyn.\n\n![multiply](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/multiply/multiply.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Multiply** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nBlok  **multiply** pasuje do bloków, które akceptują okrągłe bloki, i mogą również zawierać okrągłe bloki. \n\nPomnóż dwie liczby.\n\n![multiply_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/multiply/multiply_numbers.png)\n\nPomnóż przez wartości zmiennych.\n\n![multiply_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/multiply/multiply_variables.png)\n\nPomnóż przez wartości z czujników lub urządzeń.\n\n![multiply_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/multiply/multiply_sensors.png)\n"},"next_row":{"name":"next_row","markdown":"# Ustaw kursor w następnym wierszu\n\nUstawia kursor w nowym wierszu w programie Print Console podczas używania bloku **print**. \n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/next_row/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **set cursor to next row** ustawia kursor w nowej linii na wyświetlaczu.\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie wypiszemy „Hello”, a w następnej linii wyświetlacza - „World!”\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/next_row/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"not":{"name":"not","markdown":"# Nie\n\nPodaje przeciwieństwo (odwrotność) podanej wartości logicznej.\n\n![not](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/not/not.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **not** zwraca **true** kiedy wartośc logiczna wewnątrz to **false**.\n\nBlok **not** zwraca **false** kiedy wartośc logiczna wewnątrz to **true**.\n\nBlok **not** zwraca wartośc logiczną **true** lub **false** i pasuje do bloków z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykad zwróci **true** kiedy **Distance sensor** nie wykrywa obiektu.\n\n![not_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/not/not_sensors.png)\n\n![not_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/not/not_truth.png)  \n\n"},"object_in_front":{"name":"object_in_front","markdown":"# Wykryto obiekt\n\nInformuje, czy czujnik odległości widzi obiekt w swoim polu widzenia. \n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/object_in_front/object_in_front_block.png)\n\n## Jak używac\n\n**Object found** zwraca **true** gdy czujnik odległości wykryje obiekt lub powierzchnię w swoim polu widzenia.\n\n**Object found** zwraca **false** gdy czujnik odległości nie wykryje przedmiotu lub powierzchni.\n\nWybierz, który czujnik odległości ma być używany.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/object_in_front/object_in_front_distance.png)\n\nBlok **object found** zwraca wartość logioczną **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![object_in_front](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/object_in_front/object_in_front_inserted.png)\n "},"optical_brightness":{"name":"optical_brightness","markdown":"# Jasność koloru\n\nPodaje jasność obiektu z jednego z czujników koloru.\n\n![color_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_brightness/color_brightness.png)\n\n## Jak uzywać\n\n**Color brightness** informuje o jasności obiektu. Białe obiekty będą zgłaszać jasność 100%, czarne obiekty będą zgłaszać jasność 0%. Wszystkie inne kolory będą zgłaszać jasność pomiędzy tymi wartościami.\n\nWybierz, który czujnik koloru ma być używany. FrontEye - PrzednieOko jest zamontowane z przodu robota i skierowany do przodu, podczas gdy DownEye - DolneOko jest zamontowane poniżej robota i skierowane w dół.\n\n![color_brightness_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_brightness/color_brightness_eyes.png)\n\nBlok numeryczny **color brightness** zgłasza wartość z przedziału od 0 do 100 i jest używany w blokach z pustymi okrągłumi przestrzeniami.\n\n![color_brightness_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_brightness/color_brightness_insert.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład należy przeprowadzić na boisku **Disk Maze**. Robot będzie jechał do przodu, aż DownEye zgłosi, że jasność obiektu pod nim jest mniejsza niż 100%. Następnie skręci w lewo i znowu pojedzie do przodu.\n\n![color_brightness_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_brightness/color_brightness_example.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorowac wartość bloku **color brightness** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![color_brightness_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_brightness/color_brightness_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![color_brightness_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_brightness/color_brightness_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_color":{"name":"optical_color","markdown":"# Czujnik koloru\n\nInformuje, czy jeden z czujników koloru wykryje określony kolor.\n\n![color_sensing_color](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_color/color_sensing_color.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Color sensing** zwraca **true** gdy czujnik wykryje wybrany kolor.\n\n**Color sensing** zwraca **false** gdy czujnik wykryje inny kolor niż wybrany kolor.\n\nWybierz, który czujnik koloru ma być używany. FrontEye - okoPrzednie jest zamontowane z przodu robota i skierowane do przodu, podczas gdy DownEye - OkoDolne jest zamontowane poniżej robota i skierowane w dół.\n\n![color_sensing_color_eye](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_color/color_sensing_color_eye.png)\n\nWybierz kolor do wykrycia.\n\n![color_sensing_color_color](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_color/color_sensing_color_color.png)\n\nBlok **color sensing** zwraca wartość logiczną **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![color_sensing_color_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_color/color_sensing_color_shape.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład należy wykonać na boisku **Disk Maze**. Robot będzie jechał do przodu z opuszczonym piórem, aż wykryje obiekt z kolorem, a następnie zmieni kolor pióra i obróci się.\n\n![color_sensing_color_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_color/color_sensing_color_example.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorowac wartość **color sensing** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![color_sensing_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_color/color_sensing_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę , aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![color_sensing_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_color/color_sensing_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_near_object":{"name":"optical_near_object","markdown":"# Czujnik oka blisko obiektu\n\nInformuje, czy oko czujnika koloru znajduje się wystarczająco blisko obiektu, aby wykryć kolor.\n\n![color_near_object](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_near_object/color_near_object.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Color near object** zwraca **true** kiedy czujnik koloru znajduje się blisko obiektu i może wykryć kolor.\n\n**Color near object** zwraca **false** kiedy czujnik koloru nie jest dostatecznie blisko obiektu o wykrywalnych kolorach.\n\nWybierz, które oko czujnika koloru ma być używane. FrontEye - czujnik OkaPrzedniego jest zamontowany z przodu robota i skierowany do przodu, podczas gdy DownEye - czujnik OkaDolnego jest zamontowany poniżej robota i jest skierowany w dół.\n\n![color_near_object_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_near_object/color_near_object_eyes.png)\n\nBlok **near object** zwraca wartośc logiczna **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![color_near_object_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_near_object/color_near_object_shape.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład powinien zostać wykonany na boisku **Disk Maze**. Roobot będzie jechał do przodu, aż czujnik koloru FrontEye zgłosi, że znajduje się w pobliżu obiektu o wykrywalnym kolorze, a następnie zacznie jechać do tyłu.\n\n![color_near_object_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_near_object/color_near_object_example.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorowć wartości bloku **color near object** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![color_near_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_near_object/color_near_object_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![color_near_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/optical_near_object/color_near_object_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"or":{"name":"or","markdown":"# Lub\n\nZwraca, jeśli spełniony jest jeden z dwóch warunków logicznych.\n\n![or](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/or/or.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **or** zwraca **true** gdy jeden (lub oba) z warunków są spełnione **true**. \n\nBlok **or** zwraca **false** gdy oba warunki są spełnione**false**.\n\nBlok **or** zwraca wartośc logiczną **true** or **false**  i pasuje do bloków z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![or_sensors2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/or/or_sensors2.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nBlok **or** zwraca **true** kiedy, albo **Distance sensor** wykrywa i obiekt i / lub **FrontEye** wykrywa obiekt. \n\nBlok **or** zwraca **false** tylko wtedy, gdy oba **Distance sensor** nie wykrywa obiektu oraz **FrontEye** nie wykrywa obiektu.\n\n![or_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/or/or_truth.png)"},"position":{"name":"position","markdown":"# Pozycja robota\n\nPodaje położenie współrzędnych X lub Y robota.\n\n![position_of_robot_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position/position_of_robot_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Position of robot** podaje położenie współrzędnych X lub Y robota w liczbach całkowitych w milimetrach (mm) lub calach (cale).\n\nWybierz, czy zwrócić współrzędną X, czy współrzędną Y.\n\n![position_of_robot_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position/position_of_robot_coord.png)\n\nWybierz, czy wartość ma być podawana w mm czy w calach.\n\n![position_of_robot_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position/position_of_robot_unit.png)\n\nBlok **position of robot** jest używany w blokach z okrągłymi pustymi miejscami. \n\n![position_of_robot_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position/position_of_robot_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby onitrować wartośc bloku **position of robot** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![position_of_robot_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position/position_of_robot_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![position_of_robot_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position/position_of_robot_monitor.png)\n\n"},"position_angle":{"name":"position_angle","markdown":"# Kąt położenia w stopniach\n\nPodaje kąt położenia robota w stopniach.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Position angle in degrees** zgłasza aktualny kąt położenia robota.\n\nBlok **position angle in degrees** jest używany w blokach z okrągłymi pustymi miejscami.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartości bloku **position angle in degrees** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_monitor.png)\n\n"},"position_of_motor":{"name":"position_of_motor","markdown":"# Pozycja silnika\n\nPodaje ilość obrotów silnika VR.\n\n![position_of_motor_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position_of_motor/position_of_motor_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Position of motor** podaje położenie silnika VR w liczbach całkowitych dla stopni i liczbach dziesiętnych dla obrotów.\n\nWybierz silnik VR, którego chcesz użyć.\n\n![position_of_motor_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position_of_motor/position_of_motor_motor.png)\n\nWybierz, czy wartość jest podawana w stopniach czy ilościach obrotów.\n\n![position_of_motor_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/position_of_motor/position_of_motor_unit.png)\n"},"pressing_bumper":{"name":"pressing_bumper","markdown":"# Naciśnięcie zderzaka\n\nInformuje o naciśnięciu włącznika zderzaka.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Pressing bumper** zwraca **true** jeśli włącznik zderzaka jest wciśnięty.\n\n**Pressing bumper** zwraca **false** jeśli włącznik zderzaka nie jest wciśnięty.\n\nWybierz, którego przełącznika zderzaka użyć.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nBlok **pressing bumper** zwraca wartość logiczną **true** lub **false**  i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartości bloku **pressing bumper** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/pressing_bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)\n\n"},"pressing_limit":{"name":"pressing_limit","markdown":"# Pressing limit\n\nInformuje o naciśnięciu wyłącznika krańcowego.\n\n![pressing_limit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/pressing_limit/pressing_limit.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Pressing limit** zwraca **true** jeśli wyłącznik krańcowy jest wciśnięty.\n\n**Pressing limit** zwraca **false** jeśli wyłącznik krańcowy nie jest wciśnięty.\n\nWybierz, którego wyłącznik krańcowy chcesz użyć.\n\n![pressing_limit_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/pressing_limit/pressing_limit_bumper.png)\n\nBlok **pressing limit** zwraca wartośc logiczną **true** lub **false** i jest używany w blokach z sześciokątnymi (sześciobocznymi) pustymi miejscami.\n\n![pressing_limit_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/pressing_limit/pressing_limit_inserted.png)\n "},"print":{"name":"print","markdown":"# Wyświetl\n\nWyświetla wartości lub tekst na ekranie.\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/print/look_print.png)\n\n## Jak używać\nBlok **print** wyświetli dane w zakładce Okno monitora\n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/print/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nWszystkie bloki będą domyślnie wyświetlane jako liczby całkowite. Użyj bloku **set print precision** aby dostosować liczbę wyświetlanych miejsc dziesiętnych.\n\nWyświetl słowa i liczby\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/print/print_words.png)\n\nWyświetl zgłoszoną wartość ze zmiennej.\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/print/print_variable1.png)  \n\nWyświetl wartość obliczeń.\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/print/print_addition.png)  \n\nWyświetl wartość z czujnika lub urządzenia.\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/print/print_sensor.png)\n\n\n"},"procedures_call":{"name":"procedures_call","markdown":"# Moje bloki\n\nMoje bloki służą do tworzenia niestandardowych bloków.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_call/my_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**My blocks** służą do tworzenia sekwencji bloków, których można używać wielokrotnie w całym projekcie. \n\n**My blocks** tworzy się poprzez naciśnięcie przycisku **Make a Block**.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_call/make_a_block.png)\n\n**My blocks** mogą mieć wiele parametrów - etykiety tekstowe, zmienne numeryczne i zmienne logiczne - aby dodać więcej funkcji do niestandardowego bloku.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_call/make_variable.png)  \n\nAby użyć **my block**, dołącz **my block** do bloku \"kapeluszowego\". Kiedy blok **my block** zostaje zaczytany przez program, stok wewnątrz **my block** zostanie uruchomiony z określonymi wartościami parametrów.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_call/trigger_function.png)\n\nDołącz stos bloków do **my blocks** \"zdefiniuj\" blok \"kapeluszowy\", aby ustawić kacje, które maja być wykonane, gdy używany jest **my block**.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_call/attach_function.png)\n\n***Uwaga: Tworzenie wielu Moich bloków z tymi samymi etykietami i zmiennymi może powodować błędy podczas uruchamiania projektu na boisku.***\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przyładzie robot użyje **my blocks** do wykonania akcji:\n\n* Jedź do przodu 300 mm\n* Narysuj kwadrat 300 mm\n* Skręć w prawo o 15 stopni\n* Ustaw kolor pióra na czerwony\n* Narysuj kwadrat 400 mm\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_call/square_example.png)\n"},"procedures_definition":{"name":"procedures_definition","markdown":"# Moje bloki\n\nMoje bloki służą do tworzenia niestandardowych bloków.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_definition/my_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**My blocks** służą do tworzenia sekwencji bloków, których można używać wielokrotnie w całym projekcie. \n\n**My blocks** tworzy się poprzez naciśnięcie przycisku **Make a Block**.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_definition/make_a_block.png)\n\n**My blocks** mogą mieć wiele parametrów - etykiety tekstowe, zmienne numeryczne i zmienne logiczne - aby dodać więcej funkcji do niestandardowego bloku.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_definition/make_variable.png)  \n\nAby użyć **my block**, dołącz **my block** do bloku \"kapeluszowego\". Kiedy blok **my block** zostaje zaczytany przez program, stok wewnątrz **my block** zostanie uruchomiony z określonymi wartościami parametrów.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_definition/trigger_function.png)\n\nDołącz stos bloków do **my blocks** \"zdefiniuj\" blok \"kapeluszowy\", aby ustawić kacje, które maja być wykonane, gdy używany jest **my block**.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_definition/attach_function.png)\n\n***Uwaga: Tworzenie wielu Moich bloków z tymi samymi etykietami i zmiennymi może powodować błędy podczas uruchamiania projektu na boisku.***\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przyładzie robot użyje **my blocks** do wykonania akcji:\n\n* Jedź do przodu 300 mm\n* Narysuj kwadrat 300 mm\n* Skręć w prawo o 15 stopni\n* Ustaw kolor pióra na czerwony\n* Narysuj kwadrat 400 mm\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/procedures_definition/square_example.png)\n"},"random":{"name":"random","markdown":"# Losowo\nPoodaje losową wartość między najwyższą a najwyższą warością w bloku.\n\n![random](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/random/random.png)\n\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok  **losowo** pasuje do bloków, które akceptują okrągłe bloki.\n\nBlok **losowo** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n## Przykłady\n\nW tym przykładzie użyjemy bloku **losowo** aby losowo ustawić prędkość układu napędowego.\n\n![random_drivetrain](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/random/random_drivetrain.png)\n\nW tym przykładzie użyjemy bloku **losowo** aby losowo określić długość bloku oczekiwania.\n\n![random_control](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/random/random_control.png)\n\n"},"remainder":{"name":"remainder","markdown":"# Remainder\n\nDivides the first value by the second value and reports the remainder.\n\n![remainder](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/remainder/remainder.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **remainder** block can accept decimals, integers, or numeric blocks.\n\nNumbers that divide evenly will report a remainder of zero.\n\nThe length of the **remainder** block fits in blocks that accept circular spaces.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will divide 7 and 2 and return the remainder to turn the VEX VR Robot 1 degree to the right.\n\n![remainder_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/remainder/remainder_numbers.png)"},"repeat":{"name":"repeat","markdown":"# Powtórz\n\nBlok typu 'C'powtarza bloki zawarte w środku przez określoną liczbę razy.\n\n![repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/repeat/repeat.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nNajpierw wprowadź wartość, aby określić, ile razy będzie się  stos wewnątrz **repeat**.\n \nBlok typu 'C' **repeat** akceptuje liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nBloki typu 'C' można również umieścić jeden w drugim. Pojęcie to nazywa się zagnieżdżaniem, które może pomóc zaoszczędzić czas podczas programowania różnych zachowań.\n\n![repeat_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/repeat/repeat_select.png)"},"repeat_until":{"name":"repeat_until","markdown":"# Powtarzaj do\n\nBlok typu 'C, który powtarza bloki wewnątrz, aż zostanie wykonany warunek logiczny: **prawda**.  \n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/repeat_until/repeat_until.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **repeat until** sprawdzi warunek logiczny tylko na początku każdej pętli.\n\nKiedy warunek logiczny jest zwrócony jako **fałsz**, bloki wewnątrz **repeat until** zostaną wykonane.\n\nKiedy warunek logiczny jest zwrócony jako **prawda**, bloki wewnątrz **repeat until** zostaną pominięte.\n\nBlok typu 'C' **repeat until** może przyjąć sześciokątne (sześcioboczne) bloki jako warunek.\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/repeat_until/repeat_until_2.png)\n\n\n"},"reset_timer":{"name":"reset_timer","markdown":"# Resetuj licznik\n\nResetuje licznik Mózgu robota.\n\n![resetTimer](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/reset_timer/resetTimer.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nLicznik rozpoczyna pracę po urochumieniu projektu. Blok **reset timer** jest używany by zresetować licznik do 0s.\n\n## Przykład\n\nW tym przykładzie licznik czasu zostanie zresetowany przed przesunięciem układu napędowego do przodu przez 3 sekundy. Po upływie 3 sekund układ napędowy zatrzyma się.\n\n![resetTimer_exampleRevised](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/reset_timer/resetTimer_exampleRevised.png)"},"round":{"name":"round","markdown":"# Zaokrąglij\n\nZaokrągla wprowadzoną wartość do najbliższej liczby całkowitej.\n\n![round](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/round/round.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nLiczby dziesiętne o wartości 0,5 lub większej **round** są w górę, a mniejsze niż 0.5 **round** w dół.\n\nBlok **round** block pasuje do bloków, które akceptują okrągłe bloki, i może również akceptować okrągłe bloki.\n\nBlok **round** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![round_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/round/round_numbers.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład zaokrągli 2,7 i przesunie robota VEX VR do przodu o „3” cale.\n\n![round_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/round/round_variables.png)\n\n\n\n"},"set_2d_array_to":{"name":"set_2d_array_to","markdown":"# Ustaw listę 2D na\n\nUstawia wszystkie pozycje na liście 2D na wprowadzone wartości.\n\n![set_2d_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nTworząc listę 2D, możesz ustawić liczbę **columns** między 1 a 10 a liczbę **rows** listy między 1 a 10.\n\nWybierz, której listy 2D chcesz użyć. Liście 2D można również zmienić nazwę lub ją usunąć.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_options.png)\n\nBlok **set 2D list to** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_inserted.png)\n\n"},"set_array_to":{"name":"set_array_to","markdown":"# Ustaw listę na\n\nUstawia wszystkie pozycje na liście na wprowadzone wartości.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_array_to/set_list_to.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nPodczas tworzenia listy można ustawić długość listy od 1 do 10 elementów.\n\nWybierz listę, której chcesz użyć. Liście można również zmienić jej nazwę lub usunąć.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_array_to/set_list_to_options.png)\n\nBlok **set list to** akceptuje liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_array_to/set_list_to_2.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład ustawi listę na wartości początkowe, a następnie zgłosi element nr #3 (90) z listy, aby ustawić obroty napędu. \n\n![set_list_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_array_to/set_list_to_example.png)\n\n"},"set_boolean_variable":{"name":"set_boolean_variable","markdown":"# Ustaw zmienną logiczną\n\nUstawia zmienną logiczną na podaną wartość.\n\n![set_boolean_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz zmienną logiczną, której chcesz użyć. Można również zmieniać nazwy lub usuwać zmienne logiczne.\n\n![set_boolean_block_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_choice.png)\n\nWybierz wartość logiczną.\n\n![set_boolean_block_value](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_value.png)\n\nBlok **set boolean variable** akceptuje bloki o kształcie sześciokątnym (sześciobocznym).\n\n![set_boolean_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_inserted.png)"},"set_bumper":{"name":"set_bumper","markdown":"# Ustaw kolor diody LED zderzaka\n\nUstawia kolor diody LED robota VEX VR.\n\n![set_bumper_color](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_bumper/set_bumper_color.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz kolor jaki ma być wyświetlany.  \n\n![set_bumper_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_bumper/set_bumper_color2.png)\n\nAby wyłączyć **LED Bumper**, wyłącz kolor.\n\nW tym przykładzie kolor **LED Bumper** zostanie ustawiony na zielony.\n\n![set_bumper_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_bumper/set_bumper_example.png)"},"set_drive_heading":{"name":"set_drive_heading","markdown":"# Ustaw kierunek napędu\n\nUstawia wartość kursu żyroskopu.\n\n![setDriveHeading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_drive_heading/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **set drive heading** block może być użyty do ustawienia pozycji napędu na dowolny kurs. Ten blok może być użyty do zresetowania orientacji żyroskopu układu napędowego, gdy kurs jest ustawiony na wartość 0.\n\n**Set drive heading** akceptuje zakres od **0 do 360** stopni.\n\nBlok **set drive heading** akceptuje liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n\n"},"set_drive_rotation":{"name":"set_drive_rotation","markdown":"# Ustaw rotację\n\nUstawia kąt obrotu układu napędowego.\n\n![drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_drive_rotation/drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **set drive rotation** można użyć do ustawienia kąta obrotu napędu na dowolną dodatnią lub ujemną wartość.\n\nBlok **set drive rotation** akceptuje liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład obróci robota łącznie o 210 stopni:\n\n![turntorotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_drive_rotation/turntorotation_example.png)\n\n- Obróć w prawo (zgodnie ze wskazówkami zegara) o rotację 120 stopni.\n- Ustaw aktualną pozycję robota jako obrót -45 stopni.\n- Obróć w prawo (zgodnie ze wskazówkami zegara) na dodatkowe 90 stopni (z -45 stopni do +45 stopni) na podstawie ustawionej wartości obrotu z poprzedniego bloku."},"set_drive_velocity":{"name":"set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Ustaw prędkość napędu\n\nUstawia prędkość układu napędowego.\n\n![set_drive_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Set drive velocity** akceptuje zakres **0% do 100%**. Możesz użyć tego bloku, aby uzyskać dokładniejszy ruch robota. Domyślna prędkość to **50%**\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład ustawi napęd tak, aby obracał się z prędkością 10%.\n\n![set_drive_velocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity_example.png)"},"set_item_of_2d_array":{"name":"set_item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Zastąp pozycję listy 2D\n\nAktualizuje element na liście 2D do nowej wartości.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWprowadź pozycję wiersza (pierwszy numer) i kolumny (drugi numer) pozycji na liście, która ma zostać zastąpiona.\n\nWybierz listę, której chcesz użyć. Liście można również zmienić nazwę lub ją usunąć.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_options.png)\n\nBlok **replace item** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block_2.png)\n"},"set_item_of_array":{"name":"set_item_of_array","markdown":"# Zamień wartość\n\nAktualizuje pozycję na liście do nowej wartości.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nWybierz listę, której chcesz użyć. Liście można również zmienić nazwę lub ją usunąć.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_item_of_array/replace_item_options.png)\n\nBlok **replace item** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block_2.png)\n"},"set_led":{"name":"set_led","markdown":"# Ustaw LED\n\nWłącza i wyłącza LED.\n\n![set_led_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_led/set_led_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, którego LED użyć.\n\n![set_led_block_device](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_led/set_led_block_device.png)\n\nWybierz czy LED jest włączony czy wyłączony.\n\n![set_led_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_led/set_led_block_setting.png)\n\n"},"set_magnet_state":{"name":"set_magnet_state","markdown":"# Pobudź elektromagnes\n\nUstawia elektromagnes robota VEX VR na dwie możliwe opcje: **boost** or **drop**.\n\n![set_magnet_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, aby ustawić przyciąganie lub opadanie z elektromagnesu.\n\n* **boost** - przyciąga obiekt do elektromagnesu\n* **drop** - zwalnia każdy przedmiot trzymany przez elektromagnes\n\n\n![set_magnet_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\nW tym przykładzie robot będzie jechał do przodu o 200 mm, **Electromagnet** będzie przyciągał obiekt, przejedzie 200 mm do przodu, a następnie upuści przedmiot.\n\n![set_magnet_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_example.png)\n"},"set_motor_position":{"name":"set_motor_position","markdown":"# Ustaw pozycję silnika\n\nUstawia silnik robota VEX VR na wprowadzoną wartość.\n\n![set_motor_position](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_position/set_motor_position.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **motor position** można użyć do ustawienia dowolnej pozycji silnika.\n\nZazwyczaj, blok **motor position** służy do ustawienia pozycji na 0, aby zresetować pozycję enkodera silnika.\n\nWybierz silnik, którego chcesz użyć.\n\n![set_motor_position_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_position/set_motor_position_select.png)\n\nBlok **motor position** akceptuje wartości liczbowe jako dane wejściowe.\n\nWybierz jednostkę miary w stopniach lub obrotach.\n\n![set_motor_position_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_position/set_motor_position_unit.png)\n"},"set_motor_timeout":{"name":"set_motor_timeout","markdown":"# Ustaw limit czasu silnika\n\nUstawia limit czasu dla poleceń ruchu silnika robota VEX VR.\n\n![set_motor_timeout](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_timeout/set_motor_timeout.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nLimit czasu silnika służy do zapobiegania obrotom silnika, które nie osiągają swojej pozycji po określonym czasie, od zatrzymania kolejnych bloków w programie. Przykładem silnika, który nie osiąga swojej pozycji, jest ramię lub kleszcze, które osiągają swoje mechaniczne ograniczenie (maksimum zakresu ruchów) i nie mogą zakończyć ruchu.\n\nBlok **set motor timeout** może przyjmować wartości liczbowe jako dane wejściowe.\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład zakończy polecenie ustalone dla silnika po 2 sekundach, jeśli silnik podnośnika nie osiągnie pozycji 270 stopni. Po osiągnięciu limitu czasu lub celu silnik podnośnika przesunie się do pozycji 0 stopni.\n\n![set_motor_timeout_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_timeout/set_motor_timeout_example.png)\n"},"set_motor_velocity":{"name":"set_motor_velocity","markdown":"# Ustaw prędkość silnika\n\nUstawia prędkość obrotów silnika.\n\n![set_motor_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_velocity/set_motor_velocity.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok akceptuje zakres od -100% do 100%.\n\nUstawienie prędkości silnika na wartość ujemną spowoduje wirowanie silnika do tyłu.\n\nUstawienie prędkości silnika na 0 spowoduje, że silnik w ogóle się nie obraca.\n\nWybierz silnik, którego chcesz użyć.\n\n![set_motor_velocity_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_velocity/set_motor_velocity_select.png)\n\nWybierz, czy prędkość jest ustawiona w %, czy w obrotach na minutę (obr / min).\n\n![set_motor_velocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_velocity/set_motor_velocity_unit.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład ustawi silnik podnośnika tak, aby powoli obracał się z prędkością 25% do pozycji 90 stopni.\n\n![set_motor_velocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_motor_velocity/set_motor_velocity_example.png)\n"},"set_pen_color":{"name":"set_pen_color","markdown":"# Ustaw kolor pisaka\n\nUstawia kolor pisaka robota VEX VR.\n\n![set_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_pen_color/set_pen_color.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz kolor linii, która zostanie narysowana.\n\n![set_pen_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_pen_color/set_pen_color2.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład ustawi kolor pióra na niebieski.\n\n![set_pen_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_pen_color/set_pen_color_example.png)\n"},"set_print_color":{"name":"set_print_color","markdown":"# Ustaw kolor druku\n\nUstawia kolor napisów wyświetlanych na konsoli.\n\n![set_print_color](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_print_color/set_print_color.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **set print color** jest używany z blokiem **print** aby ustawić kolor tekstu wyświetlanego w konsoli. Kolor można ustawić na czarny, czerwony, zielony lub niebieski.\n\n![set_print_color_options](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_print_color/set_print_color_options.png)  \n\nLitery, cyfry, wartości czujników i wartości zmiennych mogą być wyświetlane w kolorze ustawionym przez blok **set print color**. \n\n![set_print_color_hello](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_print_color/set_print_color_hello.png)  \n\nKażdy blok**print** użyty po bloku **set print color** będzie wyświetlać w kolorze ostatnio ustawionym w programie.\n\n![set_print_color_text](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_print_color/set_print_color_text.png) "},"set_print_precision":{"name":"set_print_precision","markdown":"# Ustaw precyzję wydruku\n\nUstawia liczbę cyfr, które pojawiają się po przecinku podczas używania bloków drukowania do wyświetlania zmiennych i wartości w konsoli.\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_print_precision/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz poziom precyzji, z jakim wstawione bloki będą drukowane na ekranie.\n\n* Całości (1) (domyślnie)\n* Dziesiętne (0,1)\n* Setne (0,01)\n* Tysięczne (0,001)\n* Wszystkie cyfry (0,000001)\n\nWartości wydrukowane na wyświetlaczu zostaną zaokrąglone do najbliższego poziomu dokładności. Na przykład 1,67 zostanie wydrukowane jako 2 z dokładnością do jedności i 1,7 z dokładnością do dziesiątych.\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_print_precision/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_turn_velocity":{"name":"set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Ustaw prędkość skrętu\n\nUstawia prędkość skrętów układu napędowego.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Set turn velocity** ustawi prędkość układu napędowego, ale nie spowoduje ruchu układu napędowego. Blok **turn** jest nadal wymagany.\n\n**Set turn velocity** akceptuje zakres od **0% do 100%**.\n\nUstawienie prędkości skrętu w napędzie na 0 spowoduje zatrzymanie napędu.\n\nBlok **set turn velocity** akceptuje liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\n## Przykład\nTen przykład spowoduje, że układ napędowy skręci powoli, z prędkością 25%, w lewo.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_variable":{"name":"set_variable","markdown":"# Ustaw zmienną\n\nUstawia zmienną na daną wartość.\n\n![set_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_variable/set_variable.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz zmienną, której chcesz użyć. Zmiennym można również zmieniać nazwy lub je usuwać.\n\n![set_variable_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_variable/set_variable_choose.png)\n\nBlok **set variable** akceptuje liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![set_variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/set_variable/set_variable_inserted.png)\n\n"},"size_of_array":{"name":"size_of_array","markdown":"# Długość listy\n\nPodaje liczbę elementów na liście.\n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/size_of_array/size_of_array.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz listę, której chcesz użyć. Liście można zmienić nazwę lub ją usunąć.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/size_of_array/size_of_array_select.png)\n\nBlok **length of list** pasuje do bloków, które akceptują okrągłe bloki.\n\n![size_of_array_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/size_of_array/size_of_array_insert.png)"},"spin":{"name":"spin","markdown":"# Obracaj\n\nObraca silnik robota VEX VR Motor do zatrzymania.  \n![motor_spin](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin/motor_spin.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok obracaj będzie uruchamiał silnik bez końca, dopóki nie zostanie użyty nowy blok silnika lub program nie zostanie zatrzymany.\n\nWybierz silnik, którego chcesz użyć.\n\n![motor_spin_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin/motor_spin_select.png)\n\nWybierz kierunek obrotów silnika.\n\n![motor_spin_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin/motor_spin_direction.png)\n\n"},"spin_for":{"name":"spin_for","markdown":"# Obracaj o\n\nObraca silnik robota VEX VR Motor o zadaną wartość.\n![spin_for](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_for/spin_for.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **Spin For** będzie obracać silnik o określoną liczbę stopni.\n\nWybierz silnik, którego chcesz użyć.\n\n![spin_for_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_for/spin_for_select.png)\n\nWybierz, w którym kierunku silnik będzie się obracał.\n\n![spin_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_for/spin_for_direction.png)\n\n\nBlok **Spin For** akceptuje wartości liczbowe jako dane wejściowe.\n\nWybierz jednostkę miary w stopniach lub obrotach.\n\n![spin_for_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_for/spin_for_unit.png)\n\n\nDomyślnie inne bloki będą **wait** do zakonczenia pracy silnika.\n\nMożesz wybrać strzałkę, aby rozwinąć i uwzględnić **and don't wait** - spowoduje to, że inne bloki będą nadal działać, podczas gdy silnik się obraca.\n\n![spin_for_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_for/spin_for_wait.png)\n"},"spin_to_position":{"name":"spin_to_position","markdown":"# Obróć do pozycji\n\nObraca silnik robota VEX VR do ustalonej pozycji.\n\n![spin_to_position](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_to_position/spin_to_position.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **Spin to Position** obróci silnik do pewnego stopnia.\n\nWybierz silnik, którego chcesz użyć.\n\n![spin_to_position_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_to_position/spin_to_position_select.png)\n\nBlok **Spin to Position** może przyjmować wartości liczbowe jako dane wejściowe.\n\nWybierz jednostkę miary w stopniach lub obrotach.\n\n![spin_to_position_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_to_position/spin_to_position_unit.png)\n\nDomyślnie inne bloki będą **wait** do zakończenia akcji silnika.\n\nMożesz wybrać strzałkę, aby rozwinąć i uwzględnić **and don't wait** - spowoduje to, że inne bloki będą nadal działać, podczas gdy silnik się obraca.\n\n![spin_to_position_wait](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/spin_to_position/spin_to_position_wait.png)\n"},"stop_driving":{"name":"stop_driving","markdown":"# Zatrzymaj jazdę\n\nZatrzymuje napęd.\n\n![stopDriving_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/stop_driving/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **stop driving** spowoduje calkowite unieruchomienie napędu.\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład zatrzyma układ napędowy po 3 sekundach.\n\n![stopDriving_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/stop_driving/stopDriving_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_motor":{"name":"stop_motor","markdown":"# Zatrzymaj silnik\n\nZatrzymuje silnik.  \n![stop_motor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/stop_motor/stop_motor.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz silnik do zatrzymania.\n\n![stop_motor_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/stop_motor/stop_motor_select.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład spowoduje obrócenie silnika podnośnika do przodu przez 3 sekundy przed zatrzymaniem.\n\n![stop_motor_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/stop_motor/stop_motor_example.png)"},"stop_project":{"name":"stop_project","markdown":"# Zatrzymaj projekt\n\nZatrzymuje uruchomiony projekt.\n\n![stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/stop_project/stop_project.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **stop project** spowoduje zatrzymanie uruchomionego projektu.\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład przesuwa robota VR po kwadracie o boku 500 mm, a następnie zatrzymuje projekt.\n\n![stop_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/stop_project/stop_project2.png)"},"subtract":{"name":"subtract","markdown":"# Odejmowanie\n\nOdejmuje jedna wartość od drugiej i wyświetla różnicę.\n\n![subtract](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/subtract/subtract.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Subtract** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nBlok **subtract** pasuje do bloków, które akceptują okrągłe bloki, i akceptuje również okrągłe bloki.\n\nOdejmij dwie liczby.\n\n![subtract_example1](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/subtract/subtract_example1.png)\n\nOdejmij wartości od zmiennych.\n\n![subtract_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/subtract/subtract_example2.png)  \n\nOdejmij wartości z czujników lub urządzeń.\n\n![subtract_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/subtract/subtract_example3.png)"},"timer_value":{"name":"timer_value","markdown":"# Wartość licznika\n\nPodaje wartość licznika czasu w sekundach.\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/timer_value/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nLicznik czasu startuje od 0 sekund po uruchomieniu programu i podaje wartość licznika jako wartość dziesiętną.\n\nBlok **timer value** jest używany wewnątrz bloków z okrągłymi pustymi miejscami.\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/timer_value/timerValue_inserted.png)\n\n## Monitorowanie Wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartość licznika czasu, zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![timerValue_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/timer_value/timerValue_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę monitora, aby wyświetlić bloki wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![timerValue_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/timer_value/timerValue_monitor.png)\n\n"},"turn":{"name":"turn","markdown":"# Skręć\n\nCały czas obraca układ napędowy.\n\n![turn_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn/turn_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **turn** będzie cały czas obracał napęd, do momentu użycia nowego bloku napędu lub zatrzymania programu.\n\nWybierz kierunek skrętu układu napędowego.\n\n![turn_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn/turn_direction.png)"},"turn_for":{"name":"turn_for","markdown":"# Obróć o\n\nObraca układ napędowy o określoną liczbę stopni.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_for/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, w którym kierunku skręci się układ napędowy.\n\n![turn_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_for/turn_for_direction.png)  \n\n\nUstaw, jak daleko przesunie się układ napędowy, wprowadzając liczbę stopni.\n\nBlok **turn for** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_for/turn_for_block_2.png)\n\nDomyślnie inne bloki będą **wait** dopóki układ napędowy nie skręci.\n\nMożesz wybrać strzałkę, aby rozwinąć opcje i wybrać **and don't wait** - spowoduje to, że inne bloki będą nadal działać, podczas gdy układ napędowy będzie się obracał.\n\n![turn_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_for/turn_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"turn_to_heading":{"name":"turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Zmień pozycję do\n\nUstawia układ napędowy na określony kurs za pomocą wbudowanego czujnika żyroskopowego.\n\n![turn_to_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBloku **turn to heading** można użyć do obrócenia układu napędowego na dowolny podany kierunek w prawo.\n\nNa podstawie aktualnego kursu żyroskopu, **turn to heading** określi kierunek skrętu.\n\n**Turn to heading** akceptuje zakres **0.00 do 359.99** stopni.\n\nBlok **turn to heading** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nDomyślnie inne bloki będą **wait** dopóki układ napędowy nie skręci.\n\nMożesz wybrać strzałkę, aby rozwinąć opcje i wybrać **and don't wait** - spowoduje to, że inne bloki będą nadal działać, podczas gdy układ napędowy będzie się obracał.\n\n![turn_to_heading_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_anddontwait.png)\n\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład spowoduje, że układ napędowy wykona cztery obroty:\n\n![turn_to_heading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\n- Prawo do 45 stopni\n- Prawo do 90 stopni\n- Prawo do 270 stopni\n- W lewo do 180 stopni\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"turn_to_rotation":{"name":"turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Skręć do obrotu\n\nUstawia układ napędowy na określony kąt obrotu za pomocą wbudowanego czujnika żyroskopowego.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_iq.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **turn to rotation** można użyć, aby ustawić układ napędowy na zadaną wartość dodatnią (zgodnie z ruchem wskazówek zegara) lub ujemną (przeciwnie do ruchu wskazówek zegara).\n\nNa podstawie obecnej wartości **rotation** napędu, **turn to rotation** określi kierunek skrętu.\n\nBlok **turn to rotation** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\nDomyślnie inne bloki będą **wait** dopóki układ napędowy nie skręci.\n\nMożesz wybrać strzałkę, aby rozwinąć opcjie i wybrać **and don't wait** - to spowoduje, że inne bloki będą nadal działać, podczas gdy układ napędowy będzie się obracał.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq.png)\n\n## Przykład\n\nTen przykład spowoduje, że układ napędowy wykona cztery obroty:\n\n![turn_to_rotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/turn_to_rotation/turn_to_rotation_example.png)\n\n- W prawo (zgodnie z ruchem wskazówek zegara) do 90 stopni\n- W prawo (zgodnie z ruchem wskazówek zegara) do 180 stopni\n- W lewo (przeciwnie do ruchu wskazówek zegara) do -45 stopni\n- W prawo (zgodnie z ruchem wskazówek zegara) do 0 stopni\n"},"variable":{"name":"variable","markdown":"# Zmienna\n\nPodaje wartość liczbową przypisaną do zmiennej.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/variable/variable.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBloki **Variable** służą do zgłaszania wartości liczbowej przechowywanej wewnątrz zmiennej. Użyj bloku **set variable** aby ustawić lub zaktualizować wartość liczbową zmiennej.\n\n![variable_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/variable/variable_cursor.png)\n\nBlok **variable** jest używany w blokach z okrągłymi pustymi miejscami.\n\n![variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/variable/variable_inserted.png)\n\nZmienne są tworzone przy użyciu przycisku **Make a Variable**.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/variable/make_variable.png)  \n\n## Monitorowanie wartości\n\nAby monitorować wartość bloku **variable** zaznacz pole wyboru obok bloku.\n\n![variable_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/variable/variable_checkbox.png)\n\nNastępnie otwórz konsolę, aby wyświetlić bloki **variable** wybrane do monitorowania.\n\n![variable_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/variable/variable_monitor.png)\n\n"},"wait":{"name":"wait","markdown":"# Czekaj\n\nCzeka przez określony czas, zanim przejdzie do następnego bloku.\n\n![wait](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/wait/wait.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz czas **wait**.\n\nBlok **wait** może akceptować liczby dziesiętne, liczby całkowite lub bloki numeryczne.\n\n![wait_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/wait/wait_select.png)\n\n\n"},"wait_until":{"name":"wait_until","markdown":"# Czekaj do\n\nCzeka na zwrócenie od bloku logicznego **prawda** przed przejściem do następnego bloku.\n\n![wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/wait_until/wait_until.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\n**Wait until** będzie wielokrotnie sprawdzać blok wartości logicznej i nie przejdzie do następnego bloku, dopóki wartośc logiczna nie zwróci **prawda**.\n\nBlok logiczny **wait until** akceptuje bloki o kształcie sześciokątnym (sześciobocznym).\n\n![wait_until_2](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/wait_until/wait_until_2.png)\n\n"},"when_brain_screen":{"name":"when_brain_screen","markdown":"# Kiedy ekran Mózgu\n\nUruchamia dołączony stos bloków po naciśnięciu lub zwolnieniu ekranu dotykowego Mózgu V5.\n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_brain_screen/when_brain_pressed.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nZdarzenie **when brain screen** może być używane do tworzenia działań lub zachowań opartych na interakcji dotykowej z ekranem dotykowym Mózgu V5. \n\nUżywająć razem z blokiem **screen position**, użytkownicy mogą tworzyć niestandardowe interfejsy do swojego V5, wykrywając, kiedy i gdzie pojawiają się dotknięcia na ekranie.\n\nWybierz, które działanie wywoła zdarzenie - **pressed** or **released** \n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_brain_screen/when_brain_pressed_condition.png)"},"when_broadcasted":{"name":"when_broadcasted","markdown":"# Kiedy otrzymam\n\nUruchamia dołączony stos bloków, gdy wybrana wiadomość zostanie odebrana z bloku **broadcasted**.\n\n![when_broadcast](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_broadcasted/when_broadcast.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz wiadomość, której chcesz słuchać. Można również utworzyć nową wiadomość.\n\n![when_broadcast_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_broadcasted/when_broadcast_select.png)\n\nBlok **when I receive** zostanie uruchomiony tylko z bloku **broadcast** lub **broadcast and wait** w innym stosie."},"when_bumper":{"name":"when_bumper","markdown":"# Kiedy zderzak\n\nUruchamia dołączony stos bloków po naciśnięciu lub zwolnieniu wybranego czujnika zderzaka.\n\n![when_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_bumper/when_bumper.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, którego przełącznika zderzaka użyć.\n\n![when_bumper_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_bumper/when_bumper_select.png)\n\nWybierz, które działanie wywoła zdarzenie - **pressed** lub **released** \n\n![when_bumper_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_bumper/when_bumper_condition.png)\n"},"when_limit":{"name":"when_limit","markdown":"# Kiedy limit\n\nUruchamia dołączony stos bloków po naciśnięciu lub zwolnieniu wybranego czujnika wyłącznika krańcowego.\n\n![when_limit](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_limit/when_limit.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nWybierz, którego wyłącznika krańcowego chcesz użyć.\n\n![when_limit_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_limit/when_limit_select.png)\n\nWybierz, które działanie wywoła zdarzenie - **pressed** or **released** \n\n![when_limit_condition](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_limit/when_limit_condition.png)\n"},"when_started":{"name":"when_started","markdown":"# Po uruchomieniu\n\nUruchamia dołączony stos bloków podczas uruchamiania projektu.\n\n![when_started](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_started/when_started.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok zdarzenia **when started** zostanie uruchomiony, gdy projekt zostanie uruchomiony z przycisku **start** w blokach VEXcode VR.\n\nWszystkie nowe projekty będą automatycznie zawierać blok **when started**.\n\n"},"when_timer":{"name":"when_timer","markdown":"# Kiedy zegar\n\nUruchamia dołączony stos bloków, gdy licznik czasu mózgu V5 jest większy niż podana wartość.\n\n![when_timer](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_timer/when_timer.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nLicznik czasu zaczyna się na początku każdego projektu.\n\nWybierz czas. Blok wydarzenia **when timer** uruchomi się, gdy zegar Mózgu jest ustawiony **greater than** na daną wartość.\n\n![when_timer_select](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/when_timer/when_timer_select.png)\n\nBlok **when timer** może akceptować wartości dziesiętne lub całkowite.\n\nBlok **when timer**, **cannot** nie akceptuj innych bloków."},"while":{"name":"while","markdown":"# Kiedy\n\nBlok typu 'C' powtarza bloki wewnątrz, podczas gdy zwrócony jest warunek logiczny **prawda**.  \n\n![while](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/while/while.png)\n\n## Jak używać\n\nBlok **while** sprawdzi warunek logiczny tylko na początku każdej pętli.\n\nKiedy wartość logiczna jest zwrócona jako **prawda**, bloki wewnątrz **while** zostaną uruchomione.\n\nKiedy wartość logiczna jest zwrócona jako **fałsz**, bloki wewnątrz **while** zostaną pominięte.\n\nBlok typu 'C' **while** może przyjąć sześciokątne (sześcioboczne) bloki jako warunek.\n\n![while_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/pl/while/while_inserted.png)"}}},"ru":{"blocks":{"add":{"name":"add","markdown":"# Сумма\n\nСумма двух объектов.\n\n![add](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/add/add.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\n**Сумма** производится между десятичными, целыми и числовыми блоками.\n\nБлок **суммы** может быть включён в блоки с овальными рамками для аргументов и может принимать в качестве значений такие же овальные блоки. \n\n\nСумма двух чисел.\n\n![add_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/add/add_numbers.png)\n\nСумма числа и переменной.\n\n![add_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/add/add_variables.png)  \n\nСумма числа и значений, получаемых с датчиков.\n\n![add_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/add/add_sensors.png)\n\n"},"and":{"name":"and","markdown":"# Логическое И\n\nВыражение возвращает правду, если оба аргумента оператора И - правда.\n\n![and](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/and/and.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **и** возвращает **правду** в случае, если оба значения **правда**. \n\nБлок **и** возвращает **ложь** в случае, если хотя бы один из блоков **ложь**.\n\nЛогический блок **и** возвращает **правду** или **ложь** и может быть помещён в блоки, содержащие шестиугольные рамки.\n\n![and_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/and/and_shape.png)\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере **передний датчик цвета** близко к объекту и объект красный.\n\n![and_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/and/and_sensors.png)\n\nБлок **и** вернёт **правду**, если объект рядом с **передним датчиком цвета** и **передний датчик цвета** видит красный.\n\nБлок **и** вернёт **ложь** в случае, если перед **передним датчиком цвета** нет объекта, **передний датчик цвета** не видит красный или оба условия не выполнены.\n\n![and_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/and/and_truth.png)"},"boolean_variable":{"name":"boolean_variable","markdown":"# Логическая переменная\n\nСоздаёт переменную логического типа.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/boolean_variable/boolean_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **логической переменной** возвращает значение **правды** или **лжи**, хранящееся внутри переменной. Используйте блок **задания логической переменной** для обновления или изменения значения переменной.\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/boolean_variable/boolean_set_value.png)\n\nБлок **логической переменной**  - логический и возвращает **правду** или **ложь**, и может использоваться в блоках, содержащих шестиугольные вставки.\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/boolean_variable/boolean_inserted.png)\n\nЛогическая переменная создаётся с помощью кнопки **Создать логическую переменную**.\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/boolean_variable/boolean_button.png)  \n"},"break":{"name":"break","markdown":"# Прервать\n\nНезамедлительно заканчивает текущий цикл.\n\n![break](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/break/break.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nПри добавлении в блок цикла (блок 'C') блок **прервать** незамедлительно заканчивает выполнение цикла. \n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере Трансмиссия движется вперёд, проверяя нажатие экрана контроллера V5.\n\nЕсли экран **нажат**, блок **прервать** останавливает цикл и трансмиссию.\n\n![break_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/break/break_example.png)"},"bumper":{"name":"bumper","markdown":"# Pressing bumper\n\nReports if the Bumper Switch is pressed.\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **true** if the Bumper Switch is being pressed.\n\n**Pressing bumper** reports **false** if the Bumper Switch is not pressed.\n\nChoose which Bumper Switch to use.\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\nThe **Pressing bumper** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **pressing bumper** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![pressing_bumper_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/bumper/pressing_bumper_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![pressing_bumper_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/bumper/pressing_bumper_monitor.png)\n\n"},"change_variable":{"name":"change_variable","markdown":"# Изменить переменную\n\nИзменяет значение переменной на предложенное значение.\n\n![change_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/change_variable/change_variable.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите переменную, которую необходимо изменить. Выбранная переменная может быть переименована или удалена.\n\n![change_variable_options](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/change_variable/change_variable_options.png)\n\nБлок **изменения переменной** может содержать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![change_variable_value](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/change_variable/change_variable_value.png)\n\n## Пример\nВ данном примере значение переменной, соответствующее скорости, изменяется на 10 каждый раз при прохождении **повторяющегося цикла**.\n\n![change_variable_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/change_variable/change_variable_example2.png)"},"clear_all_rows":{"name":"clear_all_rows","markdown":"# Clear all rows\n\nClears all the rows in the Print Console.\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nUse the **clear all rows** block to remove all of the lines in the Print Console.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" for one second and then clear the entire display before printing \"Goodbye\".\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"comment":{"name":"comment","markdown":"# Комментарий\n\nПозволяет разработчикам делать пометки во время написания кода. \n\n![comment_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/comment/comment_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nПрофессиональные программисты используют комментарии для понимания кода другими разработчиками. Комментарии также помогают в устранению ошибок и командной разработке. \n\nДобавьте текст в блок **комментария**, в первую очередь удалив существующий текст из прямоугольной области блока, и добавьте новый текст.\n\n![comment_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/comment/comment_example3.png)\n\n## Пример\n\nКомментарии не изменяют программу и блоки вокруг себя.\n\n![comment_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/comment/comment_example2.png)\n\n"},"distance_distance":{"name":"distance_distance","markdown":"# Distance from\n\nReports the distance of the nearest object.\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_distance/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose whether **distance from** is reported in millimeters or inches. \n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_distance/distance_from_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **distance from** is used inside blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_distance/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **distance from** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![distance_from_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_distance/distance_from_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![distance_from_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_distance/distance_from_monitor.png)\n\n"},"distance_found":{"name":"distance_found","markdown":"# Distance found object\n\nReports whether the built in Distance sensor has found an object in front of the robot. \n\n![distance_found_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_found/distance_found_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Distance found object** reports **true** when the built in Distance sensor sees an object or surface within its field of view, and within 3000 mm of the sensor.\n\n**Distance found object** reports **false** when the built in Distance sensor does not detect an object or surface within 3000 mm.\n\nThe **distance found object** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![distance_found_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_found/distance_found_insert.png)\n\n## Example\n\nIn the Castle Crashers Playground, this example will drive forward if the Distance sensor detects an object, otherwise it will make a 45° right turn.\n\n![distance_found_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_found/distance_found_example.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **distance found object** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![distance_found_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_found/distance_found_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![distance_found_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/distance_found/distance_found_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"divide":{"name":"divide","markdown":"# Деление\n\nОперация делит первое значение на второе для получения частного.\n\n![divide](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/divide/divide.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\n**Деление** работает с десятичными, целыми или числовыми блоками.\n\nБлок **деления** может быть включён в блоки с овальными рамками для аргументов и может принимать в качестве значений такие же овальные блоки. \n\n\nВведите числа, с которыми необходимо произвести операцию деления.\n\n![divide_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/divide/divide_numbers.png)\n\nДеление между переменными.\n\n![divide_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/divide/divide_variables.png)\n\nДеление значений, получаемых с различных устройств.\n\n![divide_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/divide/divide_sensors.png)\n\n"},"drive":{"name":"drive","markdown":"# Движение\n\nЗапускает непрерывное движение трансмиссии.\n\n![drive_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive/drive_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **движения** запускает непрерывное движение трансмиссии до тех пор, пока не будет использован другой блок для трансмиссии или программа не будет остановлена.\n\nВыберите направление движения для запуска трансмиссии.\n\n![drive_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive/drive_direction.png)  \n"},"drive_for":{"name":"drive_for","markdown":"# Движение на расстояние\n\nРеализует проезд трансмиссии на определённое расстояние.\n\n![drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_for/drive_for.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите направление движения трансмиссии.\n\n![drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_for/drive_for_direction.png)\n\nУстановите числовое значение расстояния и выберите единицу измерения (миллиметры или дюймы).\n\nБлок **движения на расстояние** принимает десятичные, целые и числовые значения..\n\n![drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_for/drive_for_units.png)\n\nПо умолчанию остальные блоки **ждут** остановки трансмисии.\n\nВы можете выбрать из раскрывающегося списка **и не ждать** для того, чтобы параллельно с движением выполнялись другие блоки.\n\n![drive_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_for/drive_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"drive_heading":{"name":"drive_heading","markdown":"# Курс движения\n\nСообщает о направлении движения трансмиссии, используя текущее угловое положение гироскопа.\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_heading/drive_heading.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **курс движения** сообщает об увеличении угла при вращении по часовой стрелке.\n\n**Курс движения** возвращает значения **от 0.00 до 359.99 градусов**.\n\nБлок **курс движения** спользуется внутри блоков, имеющих овальные вставки.\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_heading/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_is_done":{"name":"drive_is_done","markdown":"# Движение закончено\n\nСообщает об окончании движения трансмиссии.\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **движение закончено** возвращает **правду**, когда приводы трансмиссии закончили свою работу.\n\nБлок **движение закончено** возвращает **ложь**, когда приводы трансмиссии всё ещё работают.\n\nБлок **движение закончено** - логический и возвращает **правду** или **ложь**, и может использоваться в блоках, содержащих шестиугольные вставки.\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_is_moving":{"name":"drive_is_moving","markdown":"# Движение продолжается\n\nСообщает о движении трансмиссии.\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **движение продолжается** возвращает **правду**, когда приводы трансмиссии работают.\n\nБлок **движение продолжается** возвращает **ложь**, когда приводы остановились.\n\nБлок **движение продолжается** - логический и возвращает **правду** или **ложь**, и может использоваться в блоках, содержащих шестиугольные вставки.\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_rotation":{"name":"drive_rotation","markdown":"# Вращение движения\n\nСообщает об угле вращения трансмисии в соответствии с настройками гироскопа.\n\n![drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_rotation/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **вращение движения** возвращает **положительное** значение в случае поворота трансмиссии **по часовой стрелке**.\n\nБлок **вращение движения** возвращает **отрицательное** значение в случае поворота трансмиссии **против часовой стрелке**.\n\nБлок **вращение движения** может использовать в своей структуре овальные блоки.\n\n![drive_rotation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_inserted.png)\n"},"equal_to":{"name":"equal_to","markdown":"# Сравнение\n\nСообщает равно ли первое значение второму. \n\n![equal_to](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/equal_to/equal_to.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **сравнения** возвращает **правду**, когда значения равны.\n\nБлок **сравнения** возвращает **ложь**, когда значения не равны.\n\nБлок **сравнения** десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\nБлок **сравнения** - логический, возвращает **правду** или **ложь** и используется в блоках, содержащих шестигольные рамки.\n\n![equal_to_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/equal_to/equal_to_inserted.png)\n\nВ данном примере робот остановится перед препятствием на расстоянии 500 мм.\n\n![equal_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/equal_to/equal_to_example.png)"},"forever":{"name":"forever","markdown":"# Бесконечный цикл\n\n'C' подобный блок цикла бесконечно запускает блоки внутри себя.\n\n![forever](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/forever/forever.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nПоместите блоки в **бесконечный цикл** для повторения этих блоков **бесконечно**.\n\nДля выхода из **бесконечного цикла** вы можете использовать блок **прервать**.\n\n![forever_example_2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/forever/forever_example_2.png)\n\n'C' подобные блоки могут быть помещены друг в друга. Данная концепция называется \"вложенностью\", которая обеспечивает более разнообразное поведение роботов."},"function":{"name":"function","markdown":"# Математические операции\n\nРаботает в соответствие с выбранной функцией.\n\n![function](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/function/function.png)\n\n* **abs** - модуль числа\n* **floor** - округляет в меньшую сторону до ближайшего целого числа\n* **ceiling** - округляет в большую сторону до ближайшего целого числа\n* **sqrt** - корень из числа\n* **sin** - синус\n* **cos** - косинус\n* **tan** - тангенс\n* **asin** - арксинус\n* **acos** - арккосинус\n* **atan** - арктангенс\n* **ln** - натуральный логарифм по основанию e (экспонента)\n* **log** - десятичный логарифм по основанию 10\n* **e ^** - возведение e (экспоненты) в степень\n* **10 ^** - возведение десяти (10) в степень\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **математических операций** принимает десятичные, целый или числовые блоки.\n\nВыберите нужную **математическую операцию** из списка.\n\n![function_select](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/function/function_select.png)\n\nБлок **математических операций** может быть использован в блоках с овальной вставкой. \n\n![function_fit](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/function/function_fit.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"greater_than":{"name":"greater_than","markdown":"# Больше\n\nСообщает больше ли значение слева по сравнению со значением справа.  \n\n![greater_than](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/greater_than/greater_than.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **больше** возвращает **правду**, когда первое значение больше второго. \n\nБлок **больше** возвращает **ложь**, когда первое значение меньше или равно второму. \n\nБлок **больше** принимает десятичные, целый или числовые блоки.\n\nБлок **больше** - логический и возвращает **правду** или **ложь**, и может использоваться в блоках, содержащих шестиугольные вставки.\n\n![greater_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/greater_than/greater_than_inserted.png)\n\nВ данном примере LED датчик загорится зелёным цветом как только значение таймера превысит пятьдесят (50) секунд\n\n![greater_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/greater_than/greater_than_example.png)\n"},"if_then":{"name":"if_then","markdown":"# Если тогда\n\n'C' подобный блок запускает блоки внутри себя, если условие после \"если\" правдиво.\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/if_then/if_then.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **если тогда** в первую очередь проверяет условие. \n\nЕсли условие **правда**, то выполняются блоки внутри **если тогда**.\n\nЕсли условие **ложь**, то блоки внутри **если тогда** пропускаются и управление передаётся дальше.\n\nБлок **если тогда** может принимать шестиугольные блоки в качестве условия.\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/if_then/if_then_2.png)\n\n"},"if_then_else":{"name":"if_then_else","markdown":"# Если тогда иначе\n\n'C' подобный блок **если тогда иначе** запускает блоки в первой или второй части в зависимости от условия.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/if_then_else/if_then_else.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **если тогда иначе** в первую очередь проверяет условие. \n\nЕсли условие **правда**, то выполняются блоки внутри **если тогда**.\n\nЕсли условие **ложь**, то выполняются блоки внутри **иначе**.\n\nБлок **если тогда иначе** может принимать шестиугольные блоки в качестве условия.\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/if_then_else/if_then_else_2.png)"},"item_of_2d_array":{"name":"item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Элемент двумерного массив\n\nВозвращает значение указанного элемента массива.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите двумерный массив, который вы бы хотели использовать. Кроме того, двумерный массив может быть удалён или переименован.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\nВведите номер строки (первое число) и номер столбца (второе число) элемента, значение которого вы бы хотели получить. В этом примере выбран элемент из третьей строки и третьего столбца.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\nБлок **элемента двумерного массива** принимает значения десятичных, целых или числовых блоков.\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)"},"item_of_array":{"name":"item_of_array","markdown":"# Элемент списка\n\nВозвращает значение элемента выбранного списка. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/item_of_array/item_of_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите список. Список может быть переименован или удалён.\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/item_of_array/item_of_options.png)\n\nВведите номер элемента в списке. В данном примере выбран второй элемент списка. \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/item_of_array/item_of_block_2.png)\n\nБлок **элементов списка** принимает десятичные, целый или числовые блоки.\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере скорость трансмиссии будет равна значению третьего элемента списка. Теперь трансмиссия движется вперёд со скоростью 90%.\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/item_of_array/item_of_example2.png)\n\n"},"length_of_2d_array":{"name":"length_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Длина 2D списка\n\nВозвращает число строк или столбцов двумерного списка.\n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите 2D список. Двумерный список может быть переименован или удалён.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_select.png)\n\nВыберите значение, которое необходимо узнать. Двумерный список может сообщить число **строк** или **столбцов**.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_option.png)\n\nБлок **длины 2D списка** может быть использован в блоках с овальными вставками.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_insert.png)"},"less_than":{"name":"less_than","markdown":"# Меньше\n\nСообщает меньше ли значение слева по сравнению со значением справа.  \n\n![less_than](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/less_than/less_than.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **меньше** возвращает **правду**, когда первое значение меньше второго. \n\nБлок **меньше** возвращает **ложь**, когда первое значение больше или равно второму. \n\nБлок **меньше** принимает десятичные, целый или числовые блоки.\n\nБлок **меньше** - логический и возвращает **правду** или **ложь**, и может использоваться в блоках, содержащих шестиугольные вставки.\n\n\n![less_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/less_than/less_than_inserted.png)\n\nВ данном примере происходит активация **Магнита**, если курс угла поворота меньше 50.\n"},"move_pen":{"name":"move_pen","markdown":"# Переместить перо робота\n\nУстанавливает положение пера VEX VR.\n\n![move_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/move_pen/move_pen.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите положение пера VR.\n\n![move_pen2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/move_pen/move_pen2.png)\n\n* вверх - перо не будет рисовать на поверхности поля.\n* вниз - перо будет рисовать на поверхности поля.\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере перо нарисует красную линию при движении робота.\n\n![move_pen3](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/move_pen/move_pen3.png)\n\n"},"multiply":{"name":"multiply","markdown":"# Умножение\n\nПроизводит умножение двух указанных значений.\n\n![multiply](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/multiply/multiply.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\n**Умножение** принимает десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\nБлок **умножения** может быть включён в блоки с овальными рамками для аргументов и может принимать в качестве значений такие же овальные блоки. \n\nПеремножение двух чисел.\n\n![multiply_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/multiply/multiply_numbers.png)\n\nПеремножение переменных.\n\n![multiply_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/multiply/multiply_variables.png)\n\nПеремножение показаний устройств.\n\n![multiply_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/multiply/multiply_sensors.png)\n"},"next_row":{"name":"next_row","markdown":"# Set cursor to next row\n\nSets the cursor to a new line in the Print Console when using the **print** blocks. \n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/next_row/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## How To Use\n\nUse the **set cursor to next row** block to set the cursor to a new line in the display.\n\n## Example\n\nThis example will print \"Hello\" and on the next line in the display it will print \"World!\"\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/next_row/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"not":{"name":"not","markdown":"# Отрицание\n\nВозвращает противоположное значение для указанного логического блока.\n\n![not](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/not/not.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **отрицания** возвращает **правду**, когда блок внутри содержит **ложь**.\n\nБлок **отрицания** возвращает **ложь**, когда блок внутри содержит **правду**.\n\nЛогический блок **отрицания** возвращает **правду** или **ложь** и может быть помещён в блоки, содержащие шестиугольные рамки.\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере блок возвращает **правду**, когда **Датчик расстояния** не видит объект.\n\n![not_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/not/not_sensors.png)\n\n![not_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/not/not_truth.png)  \n\n"},"optical_brightness":{"name":"optical_brightness","markdown":"# Color brightness\n\nReports the brightness of an object from one of the Color Sensor Eyes.\n\n![color_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_brightness/color_brightness.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Color brightness** reports the brightness of an object. White objects will report a brightness of 100%, black objects will report a brightness of 0%. All other colors will report a brightness somewhere between those values.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The FrontEye is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the DownEye is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n![color_brightness_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_brightness/color_brightness_eyes.png)\n\nThe **color brightness** Number block reports a value between 0 and 100 value and is used in blocks with circular spaces.\n\n![color_brightness_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_brightness/color_brightness_insert.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example should be carried out in the **Disk Maze** Playground. The robot will drive forward until the DownEye reports the brightness of an object beneath it to be less than 100%. Then it will turn left and drive forward again.\n\n![color_brightness_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_brightness/color_brightness_example.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **color brightness** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![color_brightness_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_brightness/color_brightness_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![color_brightness_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_brightness/color_brightness_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_color":{"name":"optical_color","markdown":"# Color sensing\n\nReports if one of the Color Sensor Eyes detects a specific color.\n\n![color_sensing_color](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_color/color_sensing_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Color sensing** reports **true** when the Color Sensor Eye detects the selected color.\n\n**Color sensing** reports **false** when the Color Sensor Eye detects a different color than the one selected.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The FrontEye is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the DownEye is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n![color_sensing_color_eye](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_color/color_sensing_color_eye.png)\n\nChoose which color to detect.\n\n![color_sensing_color_color](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_color/color_sensing_color_color.png)\n\nThe **color sensing** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![color_sensing_color_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_color/color_sensing_color_shape.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example should be carried out in the **Disk Maze** Playground. It will drive forward with the pen down until it detects an object with a color, and then it will change the pen color and turn around.\n\n![color_sensing_color_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_color/color_sensing_color_example.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **color sensing** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![color_sensing_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_color/color_sensing_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![color_sensing_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_color/color_sensing_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_near_object":{"name":"optical_near_object","markdown":"# Eye Sensor near object\n\nReports if the Color Sensor Eye is close enough to an object to detect a color.\n\n![color_near_object](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_near_object/color_near_object.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Color near object** reports **true** when the Color Sensor is close to an object that has detectable colors.\n\n**Color near object** reports **false** when the Color Sensor isn't close enough to an object with detectable colors.\n\nChoose which Color Sensor Eye to use. The FrontEye is mounted in front of the robot and faces forward, while the DownEye is mounted below the robot and faces downward.\n\n![color_near_object_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_near_object/color_near_object_eyes.png)\n\nThe **near object** Boolean block reports a **true** or **false** value and is used in blocks with hexagonal (six-sided) spaces.\n\n![color_near_object_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_near_object/color_near_object_shape.png)\n\n## Example\n\nThis example should be carried out in the **Disk Maze** Playground. It will drive forward until the FrontEye Color Sensor reports it is near an object with detectable color, then drive in reverse.\n\n![color_near_object_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_near_object/color_near_object_example.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **color near object** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![color_near_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_near_object/color_near_object_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![color_near_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/optical_near_object/color_near_object_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"or":{"name":"or","markdown":"# Или\n\nВыражение возвращает правду, если хотя бы один из аргументов - правда.\n\n\n![or](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/or/or.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **или** возвращает **правду** в случае, если хотя бы одно значение **правда**. \n\nБлок **или** возвращает **ложь** в случае, если оба значения **ложь**.\n\nЛогический блок **или** возвращает **правду** или **ложь** и может быть помещён в блоки, содержащие шестиугольные рамки.\n\n![or_sensors2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/or/or_sensors2.png)\n\n## Пример\n\nБлок **или** возвращает **правду**, когда **датчик расстояния** обнаружил объект и/или **FrontEye** обнаружил объект. \n\nБлок **или** возвращает **ложь**, когда **датчик расстояния** не обнаружил объект и **FrontEye** не обнаружил объект. \n\n![or_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/or/or_truth.png)"},"position":{"name":"position","markdown":"# Position of Robot\n\nReports the X or Y coordinate position of the Robot.\n\n![position_of_robot_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position/position_of_robot_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position of robot** reports the X or Y coordinate position of the Robot in integer numbers as either millimeters (mm) or inches (inches).\n\nChoose whether to return the X coordinate, or Y coordinate.\n\n![position_of_robot_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position/position_of_robot_coord.png)\n\nChoose whether the value is reported in mm or inches.\n\n![position_of_robot_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position/position_of_robot_unit.png)\n\nThe reporter block **position of robot** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_robot_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position/position_of_robot_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **position of robot** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![position_of_robot_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position/position_of_robot_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![position_of_robot_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position/position_of_robot_monitor.png)\n\n"},"position_angle":{"name":"position_angle","markdown":"# Position angle in degrees\n\nReports angle of the Robot in degrees.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_block.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\n**Position angle in degrees** reports the current angle of the robot.\n\nThe reporter block **position angle in degrees** is used in blocks with circular spaces. \n\n![position_of_robot_angle_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_inserted.png)\n\n## Value Monitoring\n\nTo monitor the value of the **position angle in degrees** block, select the checkbox next to the reporter block.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_checkbox.png)\n\nThen, open the Monitor Console to view the reporter blocks selected for monitoring.\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_monitor.png)\n\n"},"print":{"name":"print","markdown":"# Print\n\nPrints values or text in the Print Console.\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/print/look_print.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **print** block will print data within the Monitor Window tab\n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/print/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\nAll reporter blocks will be printed as integer (whole) numbers by default. Use the **set print precision** block to adjust the number of decimal places printed.\n\nPrint words and numbers.\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/print/print_words.png)\n\nPrint the reported value from a variable.\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/print/print_variable1.png)  \n\nPrint the reported calculation of an operator.\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/print/print_addition.png)  \n\nPrint the reported value from a sensor or device.\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/print/print_sensor.png)\n\n\n"},"procedures_call":{"name":"procedures_call","markdown":"# Мои блоки\n\nСоздаёт блок с функционалом, описываемым пользователем.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_call/my_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\n**Мой блок** используется для создания нового блока и позволяет использовать его неограниченное число раз в данной программе. \n\n**Мой блок** создаётся нажатием кнопки **Создать блок**.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_call/make_a_block.png)\n\nБлок **Мои блоки** может принимать в себя разные параметры на вход: текст, числа или логические переменные. Это позволяет расширить функциональность блока.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_call/make_variable.png)  \n\nДля использования **вашего блока** поместите **ваш блок** в основное тело программы. Когда программа достигнет **вашего блока**, то запустятся блоки внутри **вашего блока**.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_call/trigger_function.png)\n\nПрикрепите необходимые блоки к **вашим блокам**, чтобы определить действия, которые будут воспроизводиться при вызове **вашего блока**.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_call/attach_function.png)\n\n***Примечание: Создание блоков с одинаковыми именами может привести к ошибке в работе программы на Игровых полях.***\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном пример **мой блок** запускает следующие действия:\n\n* Движение вперёд 300 мм\n* Изображение квадрата со строной 300 мм\n* Поворот направо на 15 градусов\n* Установка красного цвета пера\n* Изображение квадрата со строной 400 мм \n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_call/square_example.png)\n"},"procedures_definition":{"name":"procedures_definition","markdown":"# Мои блоки\n\nСоздаёт блок с функционалом, описываемым пользователем.\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_definition/my_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\n**Мой блок** используется для создания нового блока и позволяет использовать его неограниченное число раз в данной программе. \n\n**Мой блок** создаётся нажатием кнопки **Создать блок**.\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_definition/make_a_block.png)\n\nБлок **Мои блоки** может принимать в себя разные параметры на вход: текст, числа или логические переменные. Это позволяет расширить функциональность блока.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_definition/make_variable.png)  \n\nДля использования **вашего блока** поместите **ваш блок** в основное тело программы. Когда программа достигнет **вашего блока**, то запустятся блоки внутри **вашего блока**.\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_definition/trigger_function.png)\n\nПрикрепите необходимые блоки к **вашим блокам**, чтобы определить действия, которые будут воспроизводиться при вызове **вашего блока**.\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_definition/attach_function.png)\n\n***Примечание: Создание блоков с одинаковыми именами может привести к ошибке в работе программы на Игровых полях.***\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном пример **мой блок** запускает следующие действия:\n\n* Движение вперёд 300 мм\n* Изображение квадрата со строной 300 мм\n* Поворот направо на 15 градусов\n* Установка красного цвета пера\n* Изображение квадрата со строной 400 мм \n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/procedures_definition/square_example.png)\n"},"random":{"name":"random","markdown":"# Случайное значение\n\nВозвращает случайное значение между наименьшим и наибольшим значениями блока.\n\n![random](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/random/random.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **случайного значения** может использоваться в блоках с овальными вставками. \n\nБлок **случайного значения** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере блок **случайного значения** случайно устанавливает скорость привода в выбранном диапазоне.\n\n![random_drivetrain](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/random/random_drivetrain.png)\n\nВ данном примере блок **случайного значения** задаёт случайную величину для блока ожидания.\n\n![random_control](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/random/random_control.png)\n\n"},"remainder":{"name":"remainder","markdown":"# Остаток от деления\n\nДелит первое значение на второе и возвращает остаток от деления.\n\n![remainder](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/remainder/remainder.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **остатка от деления** может принимать десятичные, целые и числовые блоки.\n\nБлок возвращает ноль в случае, если одно число кратно другому.\n\nБлок **остатка от деления** может быть использован в блоках с круглыми вставками.\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере остаток от деления семи на один равен одному, поэтому робот VEX VR повернётся на один градус направо.\n\n![remainder_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/remainder/remainder_numbers.png)"},"repeat":{"name":"repeat","markdown":"# Цикл повторения\n\n'C' подобный блок повторяет действия внутри себя заданное число раз.\n\n![repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/repeat/repeat.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nЗначение перед действиями блока **повторения** определяет число повторений.\n \n 'C' подобный блок **повторения** принимает целые или числовые блоки.\n\n'C' подобные блоки могут быть использованы внутри похожих блоков. Данная концепция называется \"вложенностью\" и помогает программировать более сложные алгоритмы.\n\n![repeat_select](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/repeat/repeat_select.png)"},"repeat_until":{"name":"repeat_until","markdown":"# Цикл повторять пока\n\n'C' подобный блок цикла работает до тех пор, пока условие **ложь**.\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/repeat_until/repeat_until.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nЦикл **повторять пока** проверяет значение условия на каждой итерации цикла.\n\nЕсли условие **ложь**, то действия внутри цикла **повторять пока** будут запущены.\n\nЕсли условие **правда**, то действия внутри цикла **повторять пока** будут пропущены.\n\n'C' подобный блок цикла **повторять пока** может принимать в качестве условия шестиугольные блоки.\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/repeat_until/repeat_until_2.png)\n\n\n"},"reset_timer":{"name":"reset_timer","markdown":"# Сбросить таймер\n\nОбнуляет таймер контроллера.\n\n![resetTimer](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/reset_timer/resetTimer.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nТаймер начинает работу в начале программы. Используйте блок **сброса таймера** для обнуления текущего значения таймера.\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере таймер будет обнулён перед движением трансмиссии вперёд три секунды. После трёх секунд трансмиссия закончит работу.\n\n![resetTimer_exampleRevised](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/reset_timer/resetTimer_exampleRevised.png)"},"round":{"name":"round","markdown":"# Окргулить\n\nОкругляет заданное число до ближайшего целого значения.\n\n![round](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/round/round.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nДесятичные числа будут **округляться** меньшую сторону, если десятичная часть меньше 0.5, и в большую сторону, если десятичная часть больше или равна 0.5.\n\nБлок **округления** может быть использован в блоках с овальными вставками и сам может принимать блоки овальной формы.\n\nБлок **округления** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки. \n\n![round_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/round/round_numbers.png)\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере робот проедет три дюйма вперёд после округления 2,7.\n\n![round_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/round/round_variables.png)\n\n\n\n"},"set_2d_array_to":{"name":"set_2d_array_to","markdown":"# Задать 2D массив\n\nУстанавливает значения двумерного массива.\n\n![set_2d_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nПри создании двумерного массива вы можете указать число **строк** и **столбцов** варьирующиеся в пределах от 1 до 10.\n\nВыберите двумерный массив. Массив может быть переименован или удалён.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_options.png)\n\nБлок **задания 2D массива** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_inserted.png)\n\n"},"set_array_to":{"name":"set_array_to","markdown":"# Установить элементы списка\n\nЗадаёт значения списка.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_array_to/set_list_to.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nПри создании списка вы можете задать длину списка в диапазоне от 1 до 10 элементов.\n\nВыберите список. Список может быть переименован или удалён.\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_array_to/set_list_to_options.png)\n\nБлок **установки элементов списка** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_array_to/set_list_to_2.png)\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере устанавливаются значения списка, а затем элемент под номером три используется для установки значения вращения трансмиссии.\n\n![set_list_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_array_to/set_list_to_example.png)\n\n"},"set_boolean_variable":{"name":"set_boolean_variable","markdown":"# Установить логическую переменную\n\nУстанавливает значение логической переменной.\n\n![set_boolean_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите логическую переменную. Логические переменные могут быть переименованы или удалены.\n\n![set_boolean_block_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_choice.png)\n\nВыберите логическую переменную.\n\n![set_boolean_block_value](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_value.png)\n\nБлок **установки логической переменной** может быть использован в блоках с шестиугольными вставками.\n\n![set_boolean_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_inserted.png)"},"set_bumper":{"name":"set_bumper","markdown":"# Установить цвет переднего LED\n\nУстанавливает цвет переднего LED.\n\n![set_bumper_color](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_bumper/set_bumper_color.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите цвет для отображения.  \n\n![set_bumper_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_bumper/set_bumper_color2.png)\n\nДля выключения **LED датчика** установите цвет в выключенное состояние.\n\nВ данном примере цвет **LED датчика** установлен в зелёный.\n\n![set_bumper_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_bumper/set_bumper_example.png)"},"set_drive_heading":{"name":"set_drive_heading","markdown":"# Установить курс движения\n\nУстанавливает значение курса трансмиссии.\n\n![setDriveHeading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_drive_heading/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **установки курса** используется для установки значения курса. Блок может быть использован для установки курса в ноль.\n\nБлок **установки курса** принимает значения в диапазоне **от 0 до 360** градусов.\n\nБлок **установки курса** может принимать целые или числовые блоки.\n\n\n"},"set_drive_rotation":{"name":"set_drive_rotation","markdown":"# Установка вращения\n\nУстановка угла вращения трансмиссии.\n\n![drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_drive_rotation/drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **установки вращения** может быть использован для установки положительного или отрицательного значения угла вращения.\n\nБлок **установки вращения** может принимать целые или числовые блоки.\n\n## Примеры\n\nВ этом примере робот повернётся в общей сложности на 210 градусов:\n\n![turntorotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_drive_rotation/turntorotation_example.png)\n\n- Повернуть направо (по часовой стрелке) до 120 градусов.\n- Установите текущую позицию вращения в -45 градусов.\n- Поверните направо (по часовой стрелке) на 90 градусов (от -45 до 45 градусов), полагаясь на установленное значение вращения."},"set_item_of_2d_array":{"name":"set_item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# Замена элементов 2D списка\n\nУстанавливает новое значение для выбранного элемента.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВведите положение элемента (сначала номер строки, затем номер столбца) элемента, который вы бы хотели заменить.\n\nВыберите список. Список может быть удалён или переименован.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_options.png)\n\nБлок **замены элементов 2D списка** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block_2.png)\n"},"set_item_of_array":{"name":"set_item_of_array","markdown":"# Замена элемента списка\n\nУстанавливает новое значение для выбранного элемента списка.\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите список. Список может быть переименован или удалён.\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_item_of_array/replace_item_options.png)\n\nБлок **замены элементов списка** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block_2.png)\n"},"set_magnet_state":{"name":"set_magnet_state","markdown":"# Активация магнита\n\nУстанавливает магнит в один из выбранных режимов: **активации** и **деактивации**.\n\n![set_magnet_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите действие для магнита:\n\n* **активация** - притягивает элементы к магниту\n* **деактивация** - отпускает любые элементы, притянутые магнитом ранее\n\n\n![set_magnet_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\nВ данном примере первые 200 мм **электромагнит** будет захватывать объекты, а следующие 200 мм - отпустит все объекты.\n\n![set_magnet_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_example.png)\n"},"set_pen_color":{"name":"set_pen_color","markdown":"# Установить цвет пера\n\nУстанавливает цвет пера VEX VR.\n\n![set_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_pen_color/set_pen_color.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыбирает цвет пера.  \n\n![set_pen_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_pen_color/set_pen_color2.png)\n\nВ данном примере блок устанавливает цвет пера синим.\n\n![set_pen_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_pen_color/set_pen_color_example.png)\n"},"set_print_color":{"name":"set_print_color","markdown":"# Set print color\n\nSets the color of text printed to the Print Console.\n\n![set_print_color](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_print_color/set_print_color.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **set print color** block is used with the **print** block to set the color of text printed in the Print Console. The color can be set to black, red, green or blue. \n\n![set_print_color_options](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_print_color/set_print_color_options.png)  \n\nLetters, numbers, sensor values and variable values can all be printed in the color set by the **set print color** block. \n\n![set_print_color_hello](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_print_color/set_print_color_hello.png)  \n\nAny **print** block used after the **set print color** will print in the color most recently set within the program.\n\n![set_print_color_text](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_print_color/set_print_color_text.png) "},"set_print_precision":{"name":"set_print_precision","markdown":"# Set print precision\n\nSets the number of digits that appear after the decimal point when using print blocks to print variables and values to the Print Console.\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_print_precision/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nChoose the level of precision that inserted reporter blocks will be printed to the screen.\n\n* Ones (1) (Default)\n* Tenths (0.1)\n* Hundredths (0.01)\n* Thousandths (0.001)\n* All Digits (0.000001)\n\nReporter blocks printed to the display will round to the nearest level of precision. For example, 1.67 will be printed as 2 at ones precision and 1.7 at tenths precision.\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_print_precision/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_turn_velocity":{"name":"set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Установить скорость поворота\n\nУстанавливает скорость поворота трансмиссии.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **установки скорости поворота** задаёт скорость поворота трансмиссии, но не запускает движение трансмиссии. Для запуска движения требуется блок **поворота**.\n\n**Скорость поворота** может быть задана в диапазоне **от 0% до 100%**.\n\nУстановка скорости поворота в ноль останавливает движение трансмиссии.\n\nБлок **установки скорости поворота** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере трансмиссия осуществляет медленный поворот налево на скорости 25%.\n\n![setTurnVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_variable":{"name":"set_variable","markdown":"# Установка значения переменной\n\nЗадаёт указанное значение переменной\n\n![set_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_variable/set_variable.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите переменную. Переменные могут быть переименованы или удалены.\n\n![set_variable_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_variable/set_variable_choose.png)\n\nБлок **установки переменных** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![set_variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/set_variable/set_variable_inserted.png)\n\n"},"size_of_array":{"name":"size_of_array","markdown":"# Длина массива\n\nВозвращает число элементов списка. \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/size_of_array/size_of_array.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите список. Список может быть переименован или удалён.\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/size_of_array/size_of_array_select.png)\n\nБлок **длины массива** может быть использован в блоках с круглыми вставками.\n\n![size_of_array_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/size_of_array/size_of_array_insert.png)"},"stop_driving":{"name":"stop_driving","markdown":"# Остановка движения\n\nОстанавливает трансмиссию.\n\n![stopDriving_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/stop_driving/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **остановки движения** останавливает движение трансмиссии при использовании блока.\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере остановка трансмисии произойдёт через три секунды.\n\n![stopDriving_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/stop_driving/stopDriving_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_project":{"name":"stop_project","markdown":"# Stop project\n\nStops a running project.\n\n![stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/stop_project/stop_project.png)\n\n## How To Use\n\nThe **stop project** block will cause a running project to stop. \n\n## Example\n\nThis example will drive the VR Robot in a 500mm square and then stop the project.\n\n![stop_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/stop_project/stop_project2.png)"},"subtract":{"name":"subtract","markdown":"# Вычитание\n\nПолучает разность между первым и вторым значениями.\n\n![subtract](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/subtract/subtract.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **вычитания** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\nБлок **вычитания** может быть использован в блоках с овальными вставками, и сам может использовать овальные блоки.\n\nВычитание двух чисел.\n\n![subtract_example1](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/subtract/subtract_example1.png)\n\nВычитание из переменной числа.\n\n![subtract_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/subtract/subtract_example2.png)  \n\nВычитание значений датчиков и устройств.\n\n![subtract_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/subtract/subtract_example3.png)"},"timer_value":{"name":"timer_value","markdown":"# Значение таймера\n\nВозвращает текущее значение таймера в секундах.\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/timer_value/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nТаймер запускается в начале программы, и его значение является десятичным числом.\n\nБлок **значение таймера** используется в блоках с круглыми вставками.\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/timer_value/timerValue_inserted.png)\n"},"turn":{"name":"turn","markdown":"# Поворот\n\nБесконечно поворачивает трансмиссию.\n\n![turn_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn/turn_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **поворота** будет поворачивать трансмиссию бесконечно, пока не будет использован другой блок для остановки.\n\nВыберите направление поворота трансмиссии.\n\n![turn_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn/turn_direction.png)"},"turn_for":{"name":"turn_for","markdown":"# Поворот на\n\nПоворачивает трансмиссию на определённую градусную меру.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_for/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите направление поворота трансмиссии.\n\n![turn_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_for/turn_for_direction.png)  \n\nУстановите угол поворота трансмиссии.\n\nБлок **поворот на** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_for/turn_for_block_2.png)\n\nПо умолчанию, другие блоки будут **ждать** окончания поворота трансмисии.\n\nВы можете установить значение **и не ждать** для выполнения других действий параллельно с поворотом.\n\n![turn_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_for/turn_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"turn_to_heading":{"name":"turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Повернуть на курс\n\nПоворот трансмиссии на определённый курс, установленный с помощью гироскопа.\n\n![turn_to_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **поворота на курс** может быть использован для поворота трансмиссии по часовой стрелке к любому углу.\n\nНаправление поворота с помощью блока **поворота на курс** определяется текущим положением гироскопа.\n\nБлок **поворота на курс** принимает значения  в диапазоне **от 0.00 до 359.00** градусов.\n\nБлок **поворота на курс** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n## Пример\n\nВ данном примере трансмиссия сделает четыре поворота: \n\n![turn_to_heading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\n- Поворот направо до 45 градусов\n- Поворот направо до 90 градусов\n- Поворот направо до 270 градусов\n- Поворот налева до 180 градусов\n\n\nПо умолчанию, другие блоки будут **ждать** окончания поворота трансмисии.\n\nВы можете установить значение **и не ждать** для выполнения других действий параллельно с поворотом.\n\n![turn_to_heading_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_anddontwait.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"turn_to_rotation":{"name":"turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Повернуть к вращению\n\nПоворот трансмиссии на определённый угол, установленный с помощью гироскопа.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_iq.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **поворота к вращению** может быть использован для поворота по часовой стрелке (положительного поворота) и против часовой стрелки (отрицательного поворота).\n\nНаправление поворота трансмиссии определяется текущим значением **вращения**.\n\nБлок **поворота к вращению** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n## Example\n\nВ данном примере трансмиссия сделает четыре поворота: \n\n![turn_to_rotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_to_rotation/turn_to_rotation_example.png)\n\n- Направо (по часовой стрелке) до 90 градусов\n- Направо (по часовой стрелке) до 180 градусов\n- Налево (против часовой стрелке) до -45 градусов\n- Направо (по часовой стрелке) до 0 градусов\n\nПо умолчанию, другие блоки будут **ждать** окончания поворота трансмисии.\n\nВы можете установить значение **и не ждать** для выполнения других действий параллельно с поворотом.\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq.png)"},"variable":{"name":"variable","markdown":"# Переменная\n\nЗадаёт числовое значение переменной.\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/variable/variable.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлоки **переменных** используются для присваивания числового значения переменной. Используйте блок **задания переменной** для обновления значения переменной.\n\n![variable_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/variable/variable_cursor.png)\n\nБлок **переменной** может быть использован в блоках с овальными вставками.\n\n![variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/variable/variable_inserted.png)\n\nПеременные создаются нажатием кнопки **создания переменных**.\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/variable/make_variable.png)  \n"},"wait":{"name":"wait","markdown":"# Ждать\n\nЖдёт указанное время перед переходом к следующим блокам.\n\n![wait](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/wait/wait.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nВыберите требуемое значение времени в блоке **ждать**.\n\nБлок **ждать** может принимать десятичные, целые или числовые блоки.\n\n![wait_select](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/wait/wait_select.png)\n\n\n"},"wait_until":{"name":"wait_until","markdown":"# Ждать пока\n\nЖдёт изменения значения логического блока внутри на **правду**.\n\n![wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/wait_until/wait_until.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **ждать пока** непрерывно проверяет условие внутри, пока значение внутри блока не станет **правдой**.\n\nБлок **ждать пока** может принимать шестиугольные блоки.\n\n![wait_until_2](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/wait_until/wait_until_2.png)\n\n"},"when_started":{"name":"when_started","markdown":"# Начало программы\n\nЗапускает блоки при запуске программы.\n\n![when_started](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/when_started/when_started.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nБлок **начала программы** начинает работу при нажатии кнопки **запуска** программы.\n\nВсе проекты автоматически содержат блок **начала программы**.\n\n"},"while":{"name":"while","markdown":"# Пока\n\n'C' подобный блок цикла работает до тех пор, пока условие **правда**.\n\n![while](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/while/while.png)\n\n## Как использовать?\n\nЦикл **пока** проверяет значение условия на каждой итерации цикла.\n\nЕсли условие **правда**, то действия внутри цикла **пока** будут запущены.\n\nЕсли условие **ложь**, то действия внутри цикла **пока** будут пропущены.\n\n'C' подобный блок цикла **пока** может принимать в качестве условия шестиугольные блоки.\n\n![while_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/ru/while/while_inserted.png)"}}},"zh-CN":{"blocks":{"add":{"name":"add","markdown":"# 加法\n\n把任意两个值加起来，并报告总和。\n\n![add](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/add/add.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**加法**可接受小数、整数或数字指令块。\n\n**加法**指令块可被用在接受圆形空白的指令块中，也可以接受其他圆形指令块。\n\n\n两个数字相加。\n\n![add_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/add/add_numbers.png)\n\n加法中的加数可以为变量。\n\n![add_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/add/add_variables.png)  \n\n加法中的加数可以为传感器或设备的采集值。\n\n![add_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/add/add_sensors.png)\n\n"},"and":{"name":"and","markdown":"# 与\n\n报告是否两个布尔条件均为真值。\n\n![and](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/and/and.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**与**指令块在两个条件均为**真**时，报告**真**值。\n\n**与**指令块在一个（或两个）条件为**假**时，报告**假**值。\n\n**与**指令块报告**真**或**假**值，且可被用在六角形空白的指令块中。\n\n![and_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/and/and_shape.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将检验**FrontEye**是否有物体靠近和检测的物体颜色是否为红色。\n\n![and_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/and/and_sensors.png)\n\n当**FrontEye**有物体靠近和**FrontEye**识别的颜色为红色，**与**指令块将返回**真**值。\n\n当**FrontEye**检测没有物体靠近或者**FrontEye**识别的颜色不为红色，**与**指令块将返回**假**值。\n\n![and_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/and/and_truth.png)"},"boolean_variable":{"name":"boolean_variable","markdown":"# 布尔变量\n\n报告一个布尔变量的值。\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/boolean_variable/boolean_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**布尔变量**指令块用于报告存储在该变量内的一个**真**或**假**值。使用**设定布尔变量**指令块来设定或更新一个布尔变量的值。\n\n![boolean_set_value](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/boolean_variable/boolean_set_value.png)\n\n**布尔变量**指令块报告一个**真**或**假**值，且可被用在六角形空白的指令块中。\n\n![boolean_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/boolean_variable/boolean_inserted.png)\n\n布尔变量通过**定义一个布尔变量**按钮生成。\n\n![make_boolean_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/boolean_variable/boolean_button.png)  \n"},"break":{"name":"break","markdown":"# 退出循环\n\n直接退出一个正在重复的循环。\n\n![break](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/break/break.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当添加到C型循环指令块时，**退出循环**指令块将退出它当前所在的循环。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示底盘正向驱动并检验FrontEye是否靠近一个物体。 \n\n如果检测到一个物体，**退出循环**将导致程序退出永久循环，然后执行**停止驱动**指令块，并且底盘停止运动。\n\n![break_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/break/break_example.png)"},"bumper":{"name":"bumper","markdown":"# 碰撞开关按下\n\n报告是否按下了碰撞开关。\n\n![pressing_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/bumper/pressing_bumper.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n如果按下了碰撞开关，则**碰撞开关按下**报告**真**值。\n\n如果未按下碰撞开关，则**碰撞开关按下**报告**假**值。\n\n选择要使用的碰撞开关。\n\n![pressing_bumper_bumper](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/bumper/pressing_bumper_bumper.png)\n\n**按碰撞开关** 布尔指令块报告 **真** 或 **假** 值，并适用于六边形（六边）空白指令块中。\n\n![pressing_bumper_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/bumper/pressing_bumper_inserted.png)\n\n"},"change_variable":{"name":"change_variable","markdown":"# 修改变量\n\n根据指定的值修改变量。\n\n![change_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/change_variable/change_variable.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择使用哪一个变量 - 被选择的变量也可以重命名或者删除。\n\n![change_variable_options](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/change_variable/change_variable_options.png)\n\n**修改变量**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![change_variable_value](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/change_variable/change_variable_value.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示每**重复循环**循环一次，设定底盘速度的变量值将增加10.\n\n![change_variable_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/change_variable/change_variable_example2.png)"},"clear_all_rows":{"name":"clear_all_rows","markdown":"# 清除所有行\n\n清除打印控制台中的所有行。\n\n![clear_all_rows](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n使用**清除所有行**指令块删除打印控制台中的所有行。\n\n## 示例\n\n本示例将演示打印“Hello”一秒钟，然后清除所有行再打印“Goodbye”。\n\n![clear_all_rows_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/clear_all_rows/clear_all_rows_example.png)"},"comment":{"name":"comment","markdown":"# 备注\n\n允许程序员编写信息来帮助描述他们的程序。\n\n![comment_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/comment/comment_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n专业的程序员全程使用备注来帮助其他人理解他们的代码。备注同样有助于故障定位和合作。 \n\n首先，通过选择透明方块删除现有文本，然后，输入新文本，从而实现添加文本到**备注**指令块。\n\n![comment_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/comment/comment_example3.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n备注不改变程序或它周围的指令块。\n\n![comment_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/comment/comment_example2.png)\n\n"},"distance_distance":{"name":"distance_distance","markdown":"# 距离\n\n报告最近物体的距离。\n\n![distance_from_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/distance_distance/distance_from_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择是以毫米还是英寸报告**距离**。\n\n![distance_from_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/distance_distance/distance_from_unit.png)\n\n**距离**指令块适用于圆形空白指令块中。\n\n![distance_from_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/distance_distance/distance_from_inserted.png)\n\n## 参数值监控\n\n要监控**距离**指令块的值，勾选指令块旁边的方框。\n\n![distance_from_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/distance_distance/distance_from_checkbox.png)\n\n\n然后，打开监控控制台来查看选定监视的指令块。\n\n![distance_from_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/distance_distance/distance_from_monitor.png)\n\n"},"distance_found":{"name":"distance_found","markdown":"# 距离找到对象\n\n报告内置距离传感器是否在机器人前面找到了物体。\n\n![distance_found_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/distance_found/distance_found_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当内置距离传感器在其视野范围内且距离传感器3000毫米以内时，**发现距离的物体**报告**真实**。\n\n当内置距离传感器未检测到3000毫米范围内的物体或表面时，“发现距离的物体”报告“错误”。\n\n\n“发现距离的对象”布尔块报告“ 真正”或“ 假\n”值，并用于具有六边形（六边）空格的块中。\n![distance_found_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/distance_found/distance_found_insert.png)\n\n## 例\n\n在Castle Crashers Playground中，如果距离传感器检测到物体，则此示例将向前行驶，否则将向右转45°。\n![distance_found_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/distance_found/distance_found_example.png)\n\n\n\n"},"divide":{"name":"divide","markdown":"# 除法\n\n第一个值除以第二个值并报告除法运算值。\n\n![divide](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/divide/divide.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**除法**可以接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n**除法**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中，也可以接受圆形指令块。  \n\n输入数字来做除法。\n\n![divide_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/divide/divide_numbers.png)\n\n除法中可以用变量值。\n\n![divide_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/divide/divide_variables.png)\n\n除法中可以用来自传感器或者设备的采集值。\n\n![divide_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/divide/divide_sensors.png)\n\n"},"drive":{"name":"drive","markdown":"# 驱动\n\n永远驱动底盘。\n\n![drive_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive/drive_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**驱动**指令块将永远运行底盘直到使用一个新的底盘指令块或程序停止。\n\n选择底盘将要驱动的方向。\n\n![drive_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive/drive_direction.png)  \n"},"drive_for":{"name":"drive_for","markdown":"# 驱动至\n\n底盘驱动至一个指定的距离。\n\n![drive_for](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_for/drive_for.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择底盘将要驱动的方向。\n\n![drive_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_for/drive_for_direction.png)\n\n\n通过填入一个数值，选择测量的单位（毫米或者英寸）来设定底盘将驱动多远。\n\n**驱动至**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块\n\n![drive_for_units](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_for/drive_for_units.png)\n\n默认情况下，其他指令块将**等待**直到底盘驱动结束。\n\n你可以选择箭头展开**并且不等待** - 这一指令将导致其他指令块在底盘驱动的同时继续运行。\n\n![drive_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_for/drive_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"drive_heading":{"name":"drive_heading","markdown":"# 驱动归位\n\n通过使用陀螺仪传感器的当前角度位置报告底盘的方向。\n\n![drive_heading](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_heading/drive_heading.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**驱动归位**报告顺时针旋转时数值增加。\n\n**驱动归位**报告范围为**0.00 到 359.99度**。\n\n**驱动归位**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中。\n\n![drive_heading_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_heading/drive_heading_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_is_done":{"name":"drive_is_done","markdown":"# 驱动已结束\n\n报告底盘驱动是否完成。\n\n![drive_is_done_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当底盘电机完成驱动时，**驱动已结束**报告**真**值。\n\n当底盘电机仍在驱动，**驱动已结束**报告**假**值。\n\n**驱动已结束**指令块报告一个**真**或**假**值，且可被用在六角形空白指令块中。\n\n![drive_is_done_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_is_done/drive_is_done_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_is_moving":{"name":"drive_is_moving","markdown":"# 驱动在继续\n\n报告底盘是否正在驱动。\n\n![drive_is_moving](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当底盘电机正在驱动，**驱动在继续**报告**真**值。\n\n当底盘电机已停止，**驱动在继续**报告**假**值。\n\n**驱动在继续**指令块报告一个**真**或**假**值，且可被用在六角形空白指令块中。\n\n![drive_is_moving_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_is_moving/drive_is_moving_inserted.png)\n"},"drive_rotation":{"name":"drive_rotation","markdown":"# 驱动转向\n\n当底盘配置一个陀螺仪时，报告底盘转向角度值。\n\n![drive_rotation](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_rotation/drive_rotation.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当底盘**顺时针**方向转动时，**驱动转向**报告一个**正**值。\n\n当底盘**逆时针**方向转动时，**驱动转向**报告一个**负**值。\n\n**驱动转向**指令块被用在圆形空白指令块中。\n\n![drive_rotation_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/drive_rotation/drive_rotation_inserted.png)\n"},"equal_to":{"name":"equal_to","markdown":"# 等于\n\n 报告第一个值和第二个值是否相同（相等）。\n\n![equal_to](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/equal_to/equal_to.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当两个值完全相同，**等于**报告**真**值。\n\n当两个值不相同，**等于**报告**假**值。\n\n**等于**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n**等于**布尔指令块报告一个 **真** 或 **假**值，且可被用在六角形空白指令块中。\n\n![equal_to_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/equal_to/equal_to_inserted.png)\n\n本例将演示如果距离等于500mm,将停止驱动。\n\n![equal_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/equal_to/equal_to_example.png)"},"forever":{"name":"forever","markdown":"# 永久循环\n\n永久循环指令块是一个C型指令块，它会永久重复执行所包含的任何指令块。\n\n![forever](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/forever/forever.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n将指令块放入**永久循环**C型指令块从而永远重复这些动作。\n\n你可以通过放入一个**退出循环**指令块退出一个**永久循环**。\n\n![forever_example_2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/forever/forever_example_2.png)\n\nC型指令块也可以互相被放入，这个概念叫做嵌套，在编程不同行为时可以帮助节省时间。"},"function":{"name":"function","markdown":"# 函数\n\n执行一个选定的函数。\n\n![function](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/function/function.png)\n\n* **绝对值** - 绝对值\n* **下取整** - 向下取最近的整数值\n* **上取整** - 向上取最近的整数值\n* **平方根** - 平方根\n* **sin** - 正弦函数\n* **cos** - 余弦函数\n* **tan** - 正切函数\n* **asin** - 反正弦函数\n* **acos** - 反余弦函数\n* **atan** - 反正切函数\n* **ln** - 以自然数e为底的对数\n* **log** - 以10为底的对数\n* **e ^** - 自然数e的幂次方\n* **10 ^** - 10的幂次方\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**函数**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n从下拉列表中选择数学**函数**。\n\n![function_select](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/function/function_select.png)\n\n**函数**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中。 \n\n![function_fit](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/function/function_fit.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"greater_than":{"name":"greater_than","markdown":"# 大于\n\n报告第一个值是否大于第二个值。\n\n![greater_than](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/greater_than/greater_than.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当第一个值比第二个值大，**大于**报告**真**值。\n\n当第一个值小于或等于第二个值，**大于**报告**假**值。\n\n**大于**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n**大于**指令块报告一个**真** 或 **假**值，且可被用在六角形空白指令块中。\n\n![greater_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/greater_than/greater_than_inserted.png)\n\n本例将演示如果位标角度值大于90度时，底盘停止驱动。\n\n![greater_than_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/greater_than/greater_than_example.png)\n"},"if_then":{"name":"if_then","markdown":"# 如果 那么\n \n**如果 那么**是一个C型指令块，如果布尔条件报告为**真**值，则执行所包含的任何指令块。\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/if_then/if_then.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**如果 那么**C型指令块只会检测一次布尔条件值。\n\n如果布尔条件报告为**真**值，**如果 那么**中的指令块将运行。\n\n如果布尔条件报告为**假**值，**如果 那么**中的指令块将被跳过。\n\n**如果 那么**C型指令块可接受六角形指令块作为它的条件。\n\n![if_then](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/if_then/if_then_2.png)\n\n"},"if_then_else":{"name":"if_then_else","markdown":"# 如果 那么 否则\n\n**如果 那么 否则**是一个C型指令块，它根据报告的布尔值来运行**如果 那么 否则**中的第一部分或第二部分。\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/if_then_else/if_then_else.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n **如果 那么 否则**C型指令块只会检验一次布尔条件。\n\n如果布尔条件报告为**真**值，则**如果**部分的指令块将被运行。\n\n如果布尔条件报告为**假**值，则**否则**部分的指令块将被运行。\n\n**如果 那么 否则**C型指令块可接受六角形指令块作为它的条件。\n\n![if_then_else](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/if_then_else/if_then_else_2.png)"},"item_of_2d_array":{"name":"item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# 二维数组元素\n\n报告一个二维数组中某个元素的值。\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择使用哪一个二维数组。该二维数组也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_options.png)\n\n输入数组内被报告的元素的行（第一个数字）和列（第二个数字）位置。此处，被报告的元素位置为二维数组中第2行，第3列。\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_2.png)\n\n**二维数组元素**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/item_of_2d_array/item_of_2d_list_inserted.png)"},"item_of_array":{"name":"item_of_array","markdown":"# 元素\n\n报告一个数组中某个元素的值。 \n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/item_of_array/item_of_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择使用哪一个数组。该数组也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![item_of_options](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/item_of_array/item_of_options.png)\n\n输入数组内元素的位置序号。此处，报告数组中第2个元素。\n\n![item_of_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/item_of_array/item_of_block_2.png)\n\n**元素**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示底盘速度设定为数组中第3个元素存储的值，底盘将以90%的速度正向驱动。\n\n![item_of_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/item_of_array/item_of_example2.png)\n\n"},"length_of_2d_array":{"name":"length_of_2d_array","markdown":"# 二维数组长度\n\n报告一个二维数组的行数和列数。  \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择使用哪一个二维数组。该二维数组也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_select.png)\n\n选择报告哪个值，二维数组可以报告**行数** 或 **列数**。\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_option.png)\n\n**二维数组长度**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中。\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/length_of_2d_array/size_of_2d_list_insert.png)"},"less_than":{"name":"less_than","markdown":"# 小于\n\n报告第一个值是否比第二个值小。 \n\n![less_than](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/less_than/less_than.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当第一个值比第二个值小，**小于**报告**真**值。\n\n当第一个值大于或等于第二个值，**小于**报告**假**值。\n\n**小于**指令块报告一个**真** 或 **假**值，且可被用在六角形空白指令块中。\n\n**小于**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![less_than_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/less_than/less_than_inserted.png)\n\n本例将演示如果驱动归位角度值小于50，将使磁铁通电。\n"},"move_pen":{"name":"move_pen","markdown":"# 设定画笔位置\n\n设定VEX VR画笔的位置。\n\n![move_pen](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/move_pen/move_pen.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择VEX VR画笔的位置。\n\n![move_pen2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/move_pen/move_pen2.png)\n\n* 向上 - 这支画笔将在面板上不会画一条线。\n* 向下 - 这支画笔将在面板上画一条有颜色的线。\n\n## 示例\n\n本示例将演示在面板上画一条红色的线。\n\n![move_pen3](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/move_pen/move_pen3.png)\n\n"},"multiply":{"name":"multiply","markdown":"# 乘法\n\n乘以两个值并报告乘积。\n\n![multiply](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/multiply/multiply.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**乘法**可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n**乘法**可被用在圆形空白指令块中，也可以接受圆形指令块。\n\n乘以两个数字。\n\n![multiply_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/multiply/multiply_numbers.png)\n\n乘以变量值。\n\n![multiply_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/multiply/multiply_variables.png)\n\n乘以来自传感器或者设备的采集值。\n\n![multiply_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/multiply/multiply_sensors.png)\n"},"next_row":{"name":"next_row","markdown":"# 设定光标至下一行\n\n\n使用**打印**指令块时，在打印控制台中将光标设置到下一行。\n\n![set_cursor_next_row](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/next_row/set_cursor_next_row.png)\n\n\n## 如何应用\n\n使用“设定光标至下一行”指令块将移动光标至下一行。\n\n## 示例\n\n本示例将演示打印“Hello”，并在下一行显示“World!”。\n\n![set_cursor_next_row_code_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/next_row/set_cursor_next_row_code_example.png)"},"not":{"name":"not","markdown":"# 非\n\n报告布尔结果的相反值。\n\n![not](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/not/not.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**false**.当布尔指令块中报告**假**值，**非**指令块报告**真**值。\n\n**true**.当布尔指令块中报告**真**值，**非**指令块报告**假**值。\n\n**非**指令块报告一个**真** 或 **假**值，且可被用在六角形空白指令块中。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示当**测距仪**没有检测到一个物体时，报告**真**值。 \n\n\n![not_sensors](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/not/not_sensors.png)\n\n![not_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/not/not_truth.png)  \n\n"},"optical_brightness":{"name":"optical_brightness","markdown":"# 颜色亮度\n\n报告其中一个辨色传感器检测对象的亮度。\n\n![color_brightness](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_brightness/color_brightness.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**颜色亮度**报告一个对象的亮度。白色物体的亮度为100％，黑色物体的亮度为0％。所有其他颜色将报告这之间的某个值。\n\n选择使用哪一个辨色传感器。 前辨色传感器安装在机器人的前方并面向前方，下辨色传感器安装在机器人的下方并面向下方。\n\n![color_brightness_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_brightness/color_brightness_eyes.png)\n\n**颜色亮度**指令块报告0到100之间的值，且适用于圆形空白指令块中。\n\n![color_brightness_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_brightness/color_brightness_insert.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n此示例应在“磁碟迷宫”场地中进行。机器人将向前行驶，直到下辨色传感器报告其下方的物体的亮度小于100％。然后它将左转并再次向前行驶。\n\n![color_brightness_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_brightness/color_brightness_example.png)\n\n## 参数值监控\n\n要监控**颜色亮度**指令块的值，勾选指令块旁边的方框。\n\n![color_brightness_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_brightness/color_brightness_checkbox.png)\n\n然后，打开监控控制台来查看选定监控的指令块。\n\n![color_brightness_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_brightness/color_brightness_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_color":{"name":"optical_color","markdown":"# 颜色感应\n\n报告其中一个辨色传感器是否检测到特定颜色。\n\n![color_sensing_color](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_color/color_sensing_color.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当辨色传感器检测到所选颜色时，**颜色感应**报告**真**值。\n\n当辨色传感器检测到与所选颜色不同的颜色时，**颜色感应**报告**假**值。\n\n选择使用哪一个辨色传感器。前辨色传感器安装在机器人的前方并面向前方，下辨色传感器安装在机器人的下方并面向下方。\n\n![color_sensing_color_eye](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_color/color_sensing_color_eye.png)\n\n选择要检测的颜色。\n\n![color_sensing_color_color](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_color/color_sensing_color_color.png)\n\n**颜色感应**布尔指令块报告**真**或**假**值，并适用于六角形空白指令块中。\n\n![color_sensing_color_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_color/color_sensing_color_shape.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n此示例应在“磁碟迷宫”场地中进行。它会在笔向下的情况下向前驱动，直到检测到带有颜色的物体，然后它将改变笔的颜色并转过来。\n\n![color_sensing_color_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_color/color_sensing_color_example.png)\n\n## 参数值监控\n\n要监控**颜色感应**指令块的值，勾选指令块旁边的方框。\n\n![color_sensing_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_color/color_sensing_checkbox.png)\n\n然后，打开监控控制台以查看选定监控的指令块。\n\n![color_sensing_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_color/color_sensing_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"optical_near_object":{"name":"optical_near_object","markdown":"# 辨色传感器靠近物体\n\n报告辨色传感器是否足够靠近物体以检测颜色。\n\n![color_near_object](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_near_object/color_near_object.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当辨色传感器靠近具有可检测颜色的对象时，“靠近对象的颜色”报告为“真”。\n\n当辨色传感器与具有可检测颜色的对象的距离不够近时，“靠近对象的颜色”报告为“ 假”。\n\n选择要使用的辨色传感器。 前辨色传感器安装在机器人的前方并面向前方，而下辨色传感器安装在机器人的下方并面向下方。\n\n![color_near_object_eyes](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_near_object/color_near_object_eyes.png)\n\n**靠近物体** 布尔指令块报告为 **真** 或**假**值，并适用于六角形空白指令块中。\n\n![color_near_object_shape](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_near_object/color_near_object_shape.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n此示例应在**磁碟迷宫**场地中进行。它将向前行驶，直到前辨色传感器报告靠近且可检测到颜色物体为止，然后反向行驶。\n\n![color_near_object_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_near_object/color_near_object_example.png)\n\n## 参数值监控\n\n要监控**靠近对象颜色**指令块的值，勾选指令块旁边的方框。\n\n![color_near_object_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_near_object/color_near_object_checkbox.png)\n\n然后，打开监控控制台来查看选定监控的指令块。\n\n![color_near_object_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/optical_near_object/color_near_object_monitor.png)\n\n\n\n"},"or":{"name":"or","markdown":"# 或\n\n报告两个布尔条件中的一个是否为真。\n\n![or](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/or/or.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当一个（或两个）条件为**真**值，**或**指令块报告**真**值。 \n\n当两个条件均为**假**值，**或**指令块报告**假**值。\n\n**或**布尔指令块报告一个**真** 或 **假**值，且可被用在六角形空白指令块中。\n\n![or_sensors2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/or/or_sensors2.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n本示例将演示当测距仪检测到物体，并且（或者）**FrontEye**检测到物体， **或**指令块报告**真**值。\n\n本示例将演示只有当测距仪没有检测到物体，并且**FrontEye**没有检测到物体，**或**指令块报告**假**值。\n\n![or_truth](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/or/or_truth.png)"},"position":{"name":"position","markdown":"# 机器人位置\n\n报告机器人的X或Y坐标位置。\n\n![position_of_robot_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position/position_of_robot_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**机器人位置**以毫米（mm）或英寸（inchs）的整数形式报告机器人的X或Y坐标位置。\n\n选择是返回X坐标还是返回Y坐标。\n\n![position_of_robot_coord](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position/position_of_robot_coord.png)\n\n选择是以毫米还是英寸为单位报告的值。\n\n![position_of_robot_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position/position_of_robot_unit.png)\n\n报告程序块**机器人位置**用于圆形空间的块中。\n\n![position_of_robot_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position/position_of_robot_inserted.png)\n\n## 参数值监控\n\n要监控**机器人位置**指令块的值，勾选指令块旁边的方框。\n\n![position_of_robot_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position/position_of_robot_checkbox.png)\n\n然后，打开监控控制台来查看选定监控的指令块。\n\n![position_of_robot_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position/position_of_robot_monitor.png)\n\n"},"position_angle":{"name":"position_angle","markdown":"# 位标角度值\n\n报告机器人的位标角度值（以度为单位）。\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**位标角度值**报告机器人的当前角度。\n\n**位标角度值**指令块适用于圆形空白指令块。\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_inserted.png)\n\n## 参数值监控\n\n要监控**位标角度值**指令块的值，请勾选指令块旁边的方框。\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_checkbox](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_checkbox.png)\n\n然后，打开监控控制台来查看选定监控的指令块。\n\n![position_of_robot_angle_monitor](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/position_angle/position_of_robot_angle_monitor.png)\n\n"},"print":{"name":"print","markdown":"# 打印\n\n在打印控制台中打印值或文本。\n\n![look_print](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/print/look_print.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n该块将在“监控窗口”选项卡中打印数据。\n\n![monitor_tab_icon](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/print/monitor_tab_icon.png)\n\n默认情况下，所有指令块都将数字打印为整数，使**设定打印精度**指令块来调整打印的小数位数。\n\n打印字母和数字。\n\n![print_words](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/print/print_words.png)\n\n打印变量的值。\n\n![print_variable1](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/print/print_variable1.png)  \n\n打印运算符计算结果。\n\n![print_addition](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/print/print_addition.png)  \n\n打印传感器或设备的感应参数值。\n\n![print_sensor](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/print/print_sensor.png)\n\n\n"},"procedures_call":{"name":"procedures_call","markdown":"# 我的指令块\n\n我的指令块用于生成自定义指令块。\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_call/my_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**我的指令块**用于生成一系列可以在一个程序中被多次使用的指令块。\n\n**我的指令块**使用**创建一个指令块**按钮来创建。\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_call/make_a_block.png)\n\n**我的指令块**可以包含多个参数 - 文本标签，数字变量和布尔变量 - 来添加更多的功能到你的自定义指令块中。\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_call/make_variable.png)  \n\n要使用一个**我的指令块**，附着调用**我的指令块**到一个帽状指令块。当**我的指令块**出现在你的程序中，包含在**我的指令块**中的程序段将使用指定的参数值运行。\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_call/trigger_function.png)\n\n附着一个程序段到**我的指令块**“定义”帽状指令块，来设定当一个**我的指令块**使用时发生的动作。\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_call/attach_function.png)\n\n***注意：生成多个带有相同标签和变量的 我的指令块 可能导致下载你的程序时报错。***\n\n## 示例\n\n该示例中，机器人将使用**我的指令块**来：\n\n* 正向驱动300mm\n* 画一个边长为300mm的正方形\n* 向右转15度\n* 设置画笔颜色为红色\n* 画一个边长为400mm的正方形\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_call/square_example.png)\n"},"procedures_definition":{"name":"procedures_definition","markdown":"# 我的指令块\n\n我的指令块用于生成自定义指令块。\n\n![my_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_definition/my_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**我的指令块**用于生成一系列可以在一个程序中被多次使用的指令块。\n\n**我的指令块**使用**创建一个指令块**按钮来创建。\n\n![make_a_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_definition/make_a_block.png)\n\n**我的指令块**可以包含多个参数 - 文本标签，数字变量和布尔变量 - 来添加更多的功能到你的自定义指令块中。\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_definition/make_variable.png)  \n\n要使用一个**我的指令块**，附着调用**我的指令块**到一个帽状指令块。当**我的指令块**出现在你的程序中，包含在**我的指令块**中的程序段将使用指定的参数值运行。\n\n![trigger_function](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_definition/trigger_function.png)\n\n附着一个程序段到**我的指令块**“定义”帽状指令块，来设定当一个**我的指令块**使用时发生的动作。\n\n![attach_function](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_definition/attach_function.png)\n\n***注意：生成多个带有相同标签和变量的 我的指令块 可能导致下载你的程序时报错。***\n\n## 示例\n\n该示例中，机器人将使用**我的指令块**来：\n\n* 正向驱动300mm\n* 画一个边长为300mm的正方形\n* 向右转15度\n* 设置画笔颜色为红色\n* 画一个边长为400mm的正方形\n\n![square_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/procedures_definition/square_example.png)\n"},"random":{"name":"random","markdown":"# 随机数\n\n报告指令块中从最小到最大的一个随机值。\n\n![random](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/random/random.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**随机数**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中。\n\n**随机数**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例使用一个**随机数**指令块来随机化底盘速度。\n\n![random_drivetrain](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/random/random_drivetrain.png)\n\n本例使用一个**随机数**指令块来随机化一个等待指令块的时间长度。\n \n![random_control](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/random/random_control.png)\n\n"},"remainder":{"name":"remainder","markdown":"# 取余\n\n用第二个值除第一个值并报告余数。\n\n![remainder](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/remainder/remainder.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**取余**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n可整除的数字将报告取余为零。\n\n**取余**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示7除以2并将余数值返回从而使VEX VR机器人向右转动1度。\n\n![remainder_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/remainder/remainder_numbers.png)"},"repeat":{"name":"repeat","markdown":"# 重复\n\n重复指令块是一个C型指令块，它会以设定的次数重复执行所包含的任何指令块。\n\n![repeat](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/repeat/repeat.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n首先，输入一个值来指示指令块所含指令段将要**重复**多少次。\n \n**重复**C型指令块可接受整数或数字指令块。\n\nC型指令块也可以互相被放入，这个概念叫做嵌套，在编程不同行为时可以帮助节省时间。\n\n![repeat_select](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/repeat/repeat_select.png)"},"repeat_until":{"name":"repeat_until","markdown":"# 重复直到\n\n重复直到指令块是一个C型指令块，它会重复执行所包含的任何指令块直到布尔条件报告为**真**值。\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/repeat_until/repeat_until.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**重复直到**指令块只在每次循环开始时检验布尔条件。 \n\n如果布尔条件报告为**假**值，在**重复直到**指令块中的指令块将运行。\n\n如果布尔条件报告为**真**值，在**重复直到**指令块中的指令块将被跳过。\n\n**重复直到**C型指令块可接受六角形指令块作为它的条件。\n\n![repeat_until](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/repeat_until/repeat_until_2.png)\n\n\n"},"reset_timer":{"name":"reset_timer","markdown":"# 重置计时器\n\n重置主控器的计时器。\n\n![resetTimer](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/reset_timer/resetTimer.png)\n\n## 如何应用 \n\n主控器计时器在每个程序开始时计时。**重置计时器**指令块用于重置计时器返回0秒。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例中，计时器被重置，底盘正向驱动3秒，3秒钟过后，底盘将停止。\n\n![resetTimer_exampleRevised](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/reset_timer/resetTimer_exampleRevised.png)"},"round":{"name":"round","markdown":"# 取整\n\n将输入的值取整至最近的整数。\n\n![round](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/round/round.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n小数位等于或大于0.5向上**取整**，小数位小于0.5向下**取整**.\n\n**取整**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中，也可接受圆形指令块放入其中。\n\n**取整**指令块接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![round_numbers](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/round/round_numbers.png)\n\n## 示例\n \n本例将取整2.7并驱动VEX VR机器人向前“3”英寸。\n\n![round_variables](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/round/round_variables.png)\n\n\n\n"},"set_2d_array_to":{"name":"set_2d_array_to","markdown":"# 设定二维数组\n\n通过输入数值来设定二维数组每个元素值。\n\n![set_2d_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to.png)\n\n## 如何应用 \n\n当生成一个二维数组时，你可以设定数组的**列数**数值在1到10之间，设定数组**行数**数值在1到10之间。\n\n选择使用哪一个二维数组，该二维数组也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_options.png)\n\n**设定二维数组**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_2d_array_to/set_2d_list_to_inserted.png)\n\n"},"set_array_to":{"name":"set_array_to","markdown":"# 设定数组\n\n通过输入数值来设定数组每个元素值。\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_array_to/set_list_to.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n数组的长度（1到10）在数组生成时已设定。\n\n选择使用哪一个数组，该数组也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![set_list_to_options](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_array_to/set_list_to_options.png)\n\n**设定数组**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![set_list_to](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_array_to/set_list_to_2.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将设定数组初始值，然后报告数组第3个元素（90）从而设定底盘转向。\n\n![set_list_to_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_array_to/set_list_to_example.png)\n\n"},"set_boolean_variable":{"name":"set_boolean_variable","markdown":"# 设定布尔变量\n\n设定一个布尔变量为指定值。\n\n![set_boolean_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择使用哪一个布尔变量。布尔变量也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![set_boolean_block_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_choice.png)\n\n选择一个布尔值。\n\n![set_boolean_block_value](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_value.png)\n\n**设定布尔变量**指令块可接受六角形指令块。\n\n![set_boolean_block_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_boolean_variable/set_boolean_block_inserted.png)"},"set_bumper":{"name":"set_bumper","markdown":"# 设定LED碰撞开关颜色\n\n设定VEX VR LED碰撞开关的颜色。\n\n![set_bumper_color](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_bumper/set_bumper_color.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择将要显示的颜色。\n\n![set_bumper_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_bumper/set_bumper_color2.png)\n\n要关闭**LED碰撞开关**，设定颜色为关闭。\n\n本例将设定**LED碰撞开关**为绿色。\n\n![set_bumper_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_bumper/set_bumper_example.png)"},"set_drive_heading":{"name":"set_drive_heading","markdown":"# 设定底盘归位\n\n设定底盘陀螺仪归位值。\n\n![setDriveHeading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_drive_heading/setDriveHeading_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**设定底盘归位**指令块可用于设定底盘为任意指定的归位。当归位值设定为0时该指令块可用于重置底盘陀螺仪的朝向。\n\n**设定底盘归位**接受范围为**0到360**度。\n\n**设定底盘归位**指令块可接受整数或数字指令块。\n\n\n"},"set_drive_rotation":{"name":"set_drive_rotation","markdown":"# 设定底盘转向\n\n设定底盘转向的角度值。\n\n![drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_drive_rotation/drivetrain_setdriverotation_iq.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**设定底盘转向**指令块可用于设定底盘转向角度至任意指定的正值或负值。\n\n**设定底盘转向**指令块可接受整数或数字指令块。\n\n## 示例\n\n该示例将使机器人一共转动210度:\n\n![turntorotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_drive_rotation/turntorotation_example.png)\n\n- 右转（顺时针）至转向120度。\n- 设定机器人当前位置为转向-45度。\n- 根据上一条指令块设定的转向角度值再次右转（顺时针）90度（-45度到+45度）。"},"set_drive_velocity":{"name":"set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# 设定驱动速度\n\n设定底盘的驱动速度。\n\n![set_drive_velocity](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**设定驱动速度**接受的范围是**0％到100％**。您可以使用此指令块从机器人获得更准确的运动。默认速度是**50％**。\n\n## 示例\n\n本示例将演示在驱动之前设置底盘的驱动速度为10％。\n\n![set_drive_velocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_drive_velocity/set_drive_velocity_example.png)"},"set_item_of_2d_array":{"name":"set_item_of_2d_array","markdown":"# 置换二维数组元素\n\n更新二维数组中某个元素为一个新的值。\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n输入数组内将要被置换元素的行（第一个数）和列（第二个数）位置。\n\n选择使用哪一个数组，该数组也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_options.png)\n\n**置换元素**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_item_of_2d_array/replace_item_2d_block_2.png)\n"},"set_item_of_array":{"name":"set_item_of_array","markdown":"# 置换元素\n\n更新数组中某个元素的值。\n\n![replace_item_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择使用哪一个数组，该数组也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![replace_item_options](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_item_of_array/replace_item_options.png)\n\n**置换元素**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![replace_item_block_2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_item_of_array/replace_item_block_2.png)\n"},"set_magnet_state":{"name":"set_magnet_state","markdown":"# 使电磁铁\n\n设定VEX VR机器人电磁铁的两种模式：**加磁**或**消磁**\n\n![set_magnet_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择设定电磁铁加磁或消磁\n\n* **加磁** - 吸附一个物体到电磁铁上\n* **消磁** - 释放电磁铁吸附的物体\n\n![set_magnet_block_setting](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_block_setting.png)\n\n本示例将设定机器人向前运动200mm，**电磁铁**将加磁来捡起一个物体，继续向前运动200mm，然后释放这个物体。\n\n![set_magnet_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_magnet_state/set_magnet_example.png)\n"},"set_pen_color":{"name":"set_pen_color","markdown":"# 设定画笔颜色\n\n设定VEX VR画笔的颜色。\n\n![set_pen_color](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_pen_color/set_pen_color.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择将要画的线条颜色。\n\n![set_pen_color2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_pen_color/set_pen_color2.png)\n\n本例将设定画笔颜色为蓝色。\n\n![set_pen_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_pen_color/set_pen_color_example.png)\n"},"set_print_color":{"name":"set_print_color","markdown":"# 设定打印颜色\n\n设定打印到打印控制台的文本颜色。\n\n![set_print_color](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_print_color/set_print_color.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**设定打印颜色**指令块配合**打印**指令块使用，来设定打印到打印控制台的文本颜色。颜色可被设定为黑色，红色，绿色或蓝色。\n\n![set_print_color_options](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_print_color/set_print_color_options.png)  \n\n字母，数字，传感器参数值和变量值都可以以**设定打印颜色**指令块设定的颜色来打印。\n\n![set_print_color_hello](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_print_color/set_print_color_hello.png)  \n\n任何用在**设定打印颜色**指令块之后的**打印**指令块将打印程序中最近设定的颜色。\n\n![set_print_color_text](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_print_color/set_print_color_text.png) "},"set_print_precision":{"name":"set_print_precision","markdown":"# 设定打印精度\n\n设置使用打印指令块将变量和值打印到打印控制台时小数点后出现的位数。\n\n![print_precision](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_print_precision/set_print_precision.png)\n\n## 如何使用\n\n选择将要打印到屏幕的指令块的精度级别。\n\n个位（1）（默认）\n十分位（0.1）\n百分位（0.01）\n千分位（0.001）\n全数字（0.000001）\n打印指令块将会取整到最近的精度级别打印到屏幕。如下示例，1.67将会以个位精度打印为2且十分位精度为1.7。\n\n![print_precision_choice](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_print_precision/print_precision_choice.png)"},"set_turn_velocity":{"name":"set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# 设定转向速度\n\n设定底盘转向速度。\n\n![setTurnVelocity_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**设定转向速度**只设定底盘速度但不会使得底盘移动。仍然须要一个**转**指令块。\n\n**设定转向速度**接受范围为 **-100% 到 100%**。\n\n设定一个底盘转向速度为0将导致底盘停止。\n\n**设定转向速度**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![setTurnVelocity_unit](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_unit.png)\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示底盘以25%速度缓慢向左转。\n\n![setTurnVelocity_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_turn_velocity/setTurnVelocity_example.png)\n\n\n"},"set_variable":{"name":"set_variable","markdown":"# 设定变量\n\n设定变量为一个指定值。\n\n![set_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_variable/set_variable.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择使用哪一个变量，这些变量也可以被重命名或删除。 \n\n![set_variable_choose](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_variable/set_variable_choose.png)\n\n**设定变量**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![set_variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/set_variable/set_variable_inserted.png)\n\n"},"size_of_array":{"name":"size_of_array","markdown":"# 数组长度\n\n报告一个数组中元素的数量。 \n\n![size_of_array](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/size_of_array/size_of_array.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择使用哪一个数组。该数组也可以被重命名或删除。\n\n![size_of_array_select](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/size_of_array/size_of_array_select.png)\n\n**数组长度**报告指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中。\n\n![size_of_array_insert](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/size_of_array/size_of_array_insert.png)"},"stop_driving":{"name":"stop_driving","markdown":"# 停止驱动\n\n停止底盘。\n\n![stopDriving_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/stop_driving/stopDriving_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**停止驱动**指令块将导致底盘完全停止。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示底盘正向驱动3秒钟后停止。\n\n![stopDriving_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/stop_driving/stopDriving_example.png)\n\n"},"stop_project":{"name":"stop_project","markdown":"# 停止程序\n\n停止一个正在运行的程序。\n\n![stop_project](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/stop_project/stop_project.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**停止程序**指令块将导致一个正在运行的程序停止。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示驱动VR机器人画出一个边长为500mm的正方形，然后停止程序。\n\n![stop_project2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/stop_project/stop_project2.png)"},"subtract":{"name":"subtract","markdown":"# 减法\n\n从一个值减去另一个值并报告差值。\n\n![subtract](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/subtract/subtract.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**减法**可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n**减法**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中，也可接受圆形指令块放入其中。\n\n两个数字相减。\n\n![subtract_example1](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/subtract/subtract_example1.png)\n\n减法中可以用变量值。\n\n![subtract_example2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/subtract/subtract_example2.png)  \n\n减法中可以用传感器或设备采集值。\n\n![subtract_example3](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/subtract/subtract_example3.png)"},"timer_value":{"name":"timer_value","markdown":"# 计时器值\n\n报告主控器计时器秒数值。\n\n![timerValue_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/timer_value/timerValue_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n在程序开始时，计时器从0秒开始计时并报告小数值。\n\n**计时器值**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中。\n\n![timerValue_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/timer_value/timerValue_inserted.png)\n"},"turn":{"name":"turn","markdown":"# 转\n\n永远转动底盘。\n\n![turn_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn/turn_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**转**指令块将永远转动底盘直到使用一个新的底盘指令块或程序停止。\n\n选择底盘将要转动的方向。\n\n![turn_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn/turn_direction.png)"},"turn_for":{"name":"turn_for","markdown":"# 转动\n\n将底盘转动一定的度数。\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_for/turn_for_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择底盘将要转动的方向。\n\n![turn_for_direction](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_for/turn_for_direction.png)  \n\n\n通过输入一个数字度数来设定底盘将要转动多少。\n\n**转动**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![turn_for_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_for/turn_for_block_2.png)\n\n默认情况下，其它指令块将**等待**直到底盘完成转动。\n\n你可以选择箭头来展开**并且不等待** - 这将导致其它指令块在底盘转动时同时继续运行。\n\n![turn_for_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_for/turn_for_anddontwait.png)\n"},"turn_to_heading":{"name":"turn_to_heading","markdown":"# 归位至\n\n使用内置陀螺仪转动底盘至一个特定的归位位置。\n\n![turn_to_heading_block](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_block.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**归位至**指令块可用于转向底盘至任意顺时针归位位置。\n\n根据陀螺仪当前归位，**归位至**将决定转向的方向。\n\n**归位至**接受范围为**0.00 到 359.99**度。\n\n**归位至**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n## 示例\n\n本例将演示底盘做四次转向：\n\n![turn_to_heading_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_example.png)\n\n- 右转至45度\n- 右转至90度\n- 右转至270度\n- 左转至180度\n\n默认情况下，其他指令块将**等待**直到底盘完成转向。\n\n你可以选择箭头来展开**并且不等待** - 这将导致底盘转向的同时其他指令块继续运行。\n\n![turn_to_heading_anddontwait](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_to_heading/turn_to_heading_anddontwait.png)\n\n\n\n\n\n"},"turn_to_rotation":{"name":"turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# 转向至\n\n使用内置陀螺仪转动底盘至一个特定的转向角度值。\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_iq.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**转向至**指令块可用于转动底盘至一个指定的正值（顺时针）或负值（逆时针）。\n\n根据底盘当前**转向**值，**转向至**将决定转动的方向。\n\n**转向至**指令块可接受小数，整数或数字指令块。\n\n## 示例\n\n该示例将导致底盘做四次转动：\n\n![turn_to_rotation_example](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_to_rotation/turn_to_rotation_example.png)\n\n- 右转（顺时针）至90度\n- 右转（顺时针）至180度\n- 左转（逆时针）至-45度\n- 右转（顺时针）至0度\n\n默认情况下，其他指令块将**等待**直到底盘转动完成。\n\n你可以选择箭头来展开 **并且不等待** - 这将导致底盘转动的同时其他指令块继续运行。\n\n![drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/turn_to_rotation/drivetrain_turntorotation_dontwait_iq.png)"},"variable":{"name":"variable","markdown":"# 变量\n\n报告赋值给变量的值。\n\n![variable](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/variable/variable.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**变量**指令块用于报告存储在变量中的值。使用**设定变量**指令块来设定或更新一个变量的值。\n\n![variable_cursor](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/variable/variable_cursor.png)\n\n**变量**指令块可被用在圆形空白指令块中。\n\n![variable_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/variable/variable_inserted.png)\n\n变量都由**定义一个变量**按钮生成。\n\n![make_variable](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/variable/make_variable.png)  \n"},"wait":{"name":"wait","markdown":"# 等待\n\n在移动到下一条指令块之前等待特定数量的时间。\n\n![wait](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/wait/wait.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n选择**等待**的时间数量。 \n\n**等待**指令块可接受小数，整数，或数字指令块。\n\n![wait_select](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/wait/wait_select.png)\n\n\n"},"wait_until":{"name":"wait_until","markdown":"# 等到\n\n在移动到下一条指令块前，等待布尔指令块报告**真**值。\n\n![wait_until](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/wait_until/wait_until.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**等到**将重复检验布尔指令块，且不会移动到下一条指令块，直到这个布尔指令块报告**真**值。\n\n**等到**指令块可接受六角形空白指令块。\n\n![wait_until_2](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/wait_until/wait_until_2.png)\n\n"},"when_started":{"name":"when_started","markdown":"# 当开始\n\n当程序开始时，运行随后的指令段。\n\n![when_started](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/when_started/when_started.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n当程序从VEXcode VR Blocks**开始**按钮开始时，**当开始**指令块将会运行。\n\n所有新建程序将会自动包含一个**当开始**指令块。\n\n"},"while":{"name":"while","markdown":"# 当\n\n当布尔条件报告为**真**时，重复执行内部指令段的C型指令块。\n\n![while](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/while/while.png)\n\n## 如何应用\n\n**当**指令块只会在每次循环开始时检查布尔条件。\n\n如果布尔条件报告为**真**，包含在**当**指令块中的指令块将运行。\n\n如果布尔条件报告为**假**，包含在**当**指令块中的指令块将被跳过。\n\n**当**C型指令块可接受六角形指令块作为它的条件。\n\n![while_inserted](static/help/vr/blocks/zh-CN/while/while_inserted.png)"}}},"hu-HU":{"python":{"control_break":{"name":"control_break","markdown":"# Break\n\nAzonnal kilép egy ismétlődő ciklusból\n\n```don\nbreak\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nHa egy cilus belsejébe adjuk ki, a `break` parancs kilép a ciklusból, amelyben éppen van.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program addig hajtja a robotot előre, amíg a szemérzékelő észleli, hogy egy tárgy közelében van. Ha ez a logikai feltétel igaz, akkor a break parancs a while ciklusban életbe lép és leállítja a robotot.\n\n```don\nwhile True:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if front_eye.near_object():\n        break\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if utasítás tartalmaz kettőspontot a végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy az utasítások blokkja következik","signature":"break"},"control_for":{"name":"control_for","markdown":"# For\n\nEgy ciklus ami ismételten végrehajtódik a parancsban megadott tartomány eléréséig.\n\n```don\nfor value in range(10):\n    pass\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA ciklusokhoz egy kódblokk megismétlését használják számos iterációhoz. Mindegyik for ciklus tartalmaz egy iterátort/ciklusváltozót és vagy a range () tartomány függvényt, vagy egy listát\n\n\n## Példa \n\nEz a program egy for ciklus segítségével rajzol egy 500 milliméteres fekete négyzetet a játszótérre. Az ismétlés_szám/repeat_count változó az iterátor/ciklusváltozó. \nA range () függvény megmondja a Pythonnak, hogy négyszer ismételje meg a For cikluson belüli kódblokkot. A range előállítja az értékek egy sorozatát a for ciklus ciklusváltozója számára. Ezek 0-val kezdődnek és a lenti esetben nem tartalmazza a 4-et. Tehát az utasítás: Hajtsd végre a blokkot az repeat_count = 0, majd 1, aztán 2, végül 3 értékkel!\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 500,MM)\n    drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nAz ismétlés_szám/repeat_count változót használtuk az érték helyettesítésére.\n\nA program az y változót használja iterátorként/ciklusváltozó, és a lista (myList) megmondja a Pythonnak, hogy hányszor kell végrehajtania a ciklust. \nTehát az utasítás: Hajtsd végre a blokkot az y = \"VEXcode VR\", majd \"is\", aztán \"100%\", végül \"fun!!\" értékkel!\n\n```don\nmyList = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", \"100%\", \"fun!!\"]\nfor y in myList:\n    brain.print(y)\n    brain.new_line()\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n```\n\nEz a program ezt írja ki:\n\n```don\nVEXcode VR\nis\n100%\nfun!!\n```\n\nMinden programnál vegye figyelembe, hogy a for ciklus kettőspontot tartalmaz a végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"for value in range (10"},"control_if":{"name":"control_if","markdown":"# If\n\nParancsblokk végrehajtására használt vezérlő utasítás, If ha a feltétel **Igaz** értékű\n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\n    \n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nAz if utasítás feltételként logikai értéket és logikai kifejezéseket használ.\n\nHa a logikai feltétel igaznak van jelentve, az if utasítás belsejében lévő blokkok végrehajtódnak.\n\nHa a logikai feltétel hamisnak minősül, az if utasítás belsejében lévő blokkok átugrásra kerülnek.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program egy logikai kifejezést használ, hogy először öt másodpercig előre mozogjon a robot. Amíg a robot előre halad, a toll vonalt fog húzni. Ezután egy másik logikai kifejezést használ egy if utasítással a vonal színének pirosra változtatására 2,5 másodperc után. \n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if brain.timer_time(SECONDS) > 2.5:\n        pen.set_pen_color(RED)\n    wait(5, MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\nA várakozási parancsot cilussal kell használni. A VEXcode VR böngészőalapú architektúrája miatt egy rövid (5 milliszekundum) várakozási parancsot kell elhelyezni minden ciklusban annak érdekében, hogy a Python projekt folytassa a kommunikációt a Játszótérrel.\n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if utasítás tartalmaz kettőspontot a végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy az utasítások blokkja következik\n\nEz a projekt logikai utasításokat használ if-utasításokkal. A robot előrehalad, ha a szemérzékelő nem észlel színt, jobbra fordul, ha a szemérzékelő zöldet észlel, és leáll, ha a szemérzékelő kéket.  \n\n```don\nwhile True:\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n    if front_eye.detect(NONE):\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if front_eye.detect(GREEN):\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\n    if front_eye.detect(BLUE):\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        drivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA várakozási parancsot cilussal kell használni.\n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if utasítás tartalmaz kettőspontot a végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy az utasítások blokkja következik","signature":"if condition:\\n\\tpass"},"control_if_else":{"name":"control_if_else","markdown":"# If/else\nFeltételes utasítás, amely végrehajtja a parancsok egyik blokkját, ha a feltételt **True** -nak értékeli, vagy egy másik parancs-blokkot, ha a feltételt **False** -nak értékeli.\n\n```don\nif condition:\n    pass\nelse:\n    pass\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nAz if / else utasítás logikai értékeket és logikai kifejezéseket használ feltételként.\n\nHa a logikai feltétel igaz, akkor az if ág belsejében lévő program sorok fognak végrehajtódni.\n\nHa a logikai feltétel hamisnak minősül, akkor az if ág belsejében lévő program sorok átugrásra kerülnek, és az else ág belsejében lévő program sorok fognak végrehajtódni.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program egy Logikai/Boolean utasítást használ az if / else utasítással a robot mozgatásához a szemérzékelő által jelentett értéknek megfelelően. Ha a szemérzékelő egy tárgyat észlel, a VR robot halad előre. Ha a szemérzékelő nem észlel semmilyen tárgyat, a robot megfordul. Ez a program a Castle Crasher játszótérrel használható.\n\n```don\nwhile True:\n    if distance.found_object(): \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    else:\n        drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nAz if / else utasítás csak egyszer ellenőrzi a Logikai/Boolean utasítást. Ezért az if / else utasítás egy while True ciklusba kerül, lehetővé válik a Logikai/Boolean utasítás ismételt ellenőrzése.\n\nA várakozási parancsot ciklussal kell használni. A VEXcode VR böngészőalapú architektúrája miatt egy rövid (5 milliszekundum) várakozási parancsot kell elhelyezni minden ciklusban annak érdekében, hogy a Python program folytassa a kommunikációt a Játszótérrel.\n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if utasítás tartalmaz kettőspontot a végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy az utasítások blokkja következik.","signature":"if conditio"},"control_stop_project":{"name":"control_stop_project","markdown":"# Stop Project\n\nLeállít egy futó programot.\n\n```don\nstop_project()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nEz a parancs leállítja a futó programokat.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program egy 500 milliméteres fekete négyzetet rajzol a padlóra. A stop_project parancs a négyzet befejezése után leállítja a programot.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\nfor repeat_count in range(4):\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 500,MM)\n    drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90,DEGREES)\nstop_project()\n```","signature":"stop_project()"},"control_wait":{"name":"control_wait","markdown":"# Wait\n\nVárakozás egy bizonyos ideig, mielőtt a program a következő parancsra lépne.\n\n```don\nwait(TIME, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nÁllítsa be, mennyi ideig várjon a program, mielőtt a következő parancsra lépne. Az értéke lehet másodpercben vagy ezredmásodpercben.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a példa leállítja a robotot, miután három másodpercig előre ment.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paramétereit nagybetűkkel írják.","signature":"wait(1, SECONDS)"},"control_wait_until":{"name":"control_wait_until","markdown":"# While Not\n\nA while not loop ismételten végrehajtja a parancsokat, mindaddig, amíg a feltétel **True** értéket ad meg. Egy ideig a ciklus blokkolja a projekt folyamatát - hasonlóan a várakozás parancshoz.\n\nEz lehetővé teszi, hogy a program folyamatosan ellenőrizze az érzékelő állapotát és blokkolja a program folytatását, amíg az adott érzékelő feltétele nem teljesül.\n\n\n```don\nwhile not condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA Python nem tartalmaz várakozás parancsot (mint például a VEXcode blokk alapú módjában), ezért a while not ciklusra itt hivatkozunk. A Python while not ciklusát hasonlóan lehet használni, mint a Python wait until block parancsát. Például:\n\nMindkét példában a robot előre halad, amíg az ütköző érzékelőjét megnyomják.\n\nUgyanez a viselkedés megtehető egy darab ciklussal is. Például:\n\n```don\nwhile left_bumper.pressed() == False:\n    drive.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nEz azt is eredményezi, hogy a robot előre halad, amíg az ütköző érzékelőjét megnyomják.","signature":"while not expressio"},"control_while":{"name":"control_while","markdown":"# While\n\nEgy ciklus, amely ismételten végrehajtja a kódot a ciklus belselyében, mindaddig, amíg a feltétel kifejezés **true** értéket ad.   \n\n```don\nwhile condition:\n    pass\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA while ciklus feltételként logikai értéket vagy logikai kifejezéseket használ.\n\nA while ciklus csak egyszer ellenőrzi a logikai állapotot a ciklus elején.\n\nHa a logikai feltétel értéke IGAZ, akkor a while ciklus belsejében lévő blokkok egyszer futnak, mielőtt újra ellenőrzi a feltételt.\n\nHa a logikai feltétel HAMIS értékű, akkor a while ciklus belsejében lévő blokkok átugrásra kerülnek.\n\nFigyeljen, hogy olyan feltételt adjon meg, ami nem okoz végtelen ciklust. A ciklus feltételeként megadott kifejezés a ciklus belsejében tudjon hamisra változni.\n\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program logikai kifejezéssel mozgatja a robotot előre, mindaddig amíg a távolságérzékelő értéke meghaladja az 50 millimétert.  \n\n```don\nwhile distance.get_distance(MM)>50:\n    drive.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nEz a program Boolean logikai utasítással mozgatja előre a robotot, amíg az ütköző érzékelőjét meg nem nyomja valami.\n\n```don\nwhile right_bumper.pressed() == False:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5,MSEC)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA várakozási parancsot ciklussal kell használni. A VEXcode VR böngészőalapú architektúrája miatt egy rövid (5 milliszekundum) várakozási parancsot kell elhelyezni minden ciklusban annak érdekében, hogy a Python projekt folytassa a kommunikációt a Játszótérrel.\n\nFigyelje meg, hogy a while ciklus tartalmaz kettőspontot a végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"while expressio"},"drivetrain_drive":{"name":"drivetrain_drive","markdown":"# Drive\n\nFolyamatosan mozgatja a robotot a zárójelben megadott irányba.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.drive()` parancs folyamatosan mozgatja a robotot, amíg új parancsot nem kap a robot vagy a program le nem áll.\n\nAz alapértelmezett sebesség a \"drivetrain.drive ()\" számára 50%.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a parancs folyamatosan előre mozgatja a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nEz a parancs folyamatosan visszafelé mozgatja a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(REVERSE)\n```\n\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paramétereit nagybetűkkel írják","signature":"drivetrain.drive(FOWARD)"},"drivetrain_drive_for":{"name":"drivetrain_drive_for","markdown":"# Drive For\n\nEgy adott távolságra mozgatja a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(DIRECTION, distance, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nÁllítsa be a távolságot és az irányt, amelyen a robot mozgatni fog, az alábbiak mindegyikének megadásával:\n\n- Direction - either `FORWARD` or `REVERSE` - Irány - vagy \"ELŐRE\" vagy \"HÁTRA\"\n- Distance - a numeric value - Távolság - egy numerikus érték\n- Units - either `MM`(millimeters) or `INCHES` - Mértékegységek: vagy „MM” (milliméter), vagy „INCHES”\n\n## Példa\nEz a parancs 200 milliméterrel előre viszi a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n```\n\nEz a parancs 150 hüvelyknyire vissza viszi a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 150, INCHES)\n```\n\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paraméterei nagybetűkkel vannak megírva.","signature":"drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)"},"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_drive_velocity","markdown":"# Set Drive Velocity\n\nBeállítja a robot sebességét\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.set_drive_velocity()` parancs elfogadja a 0% és 100% közötti tartományformát. A robot alapértelmezett sebessége 50%.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a példa program a robotot 200 mm-rel előrelendíti 25% -os sebességgel\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_drive_velocity(25, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n```\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paramétereit nagybetűkkel írják","signature":"drivetrain.set_drive_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_set_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_set_heading","markdown":"# Set Heading\n\nBeállítja a robot gyro irányértékét.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_heading(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.set_heading()` paranccsal beállíthatjuk a robot pozícióját bármely adott fejlécre. Ezzel a paranccsal vissza lehet állítani a robot giroszkópjának tájolását, \nha a fejléc értéke 0.\n\nA `drivetrain.set_heading()` parancs **0 és 360** fok közötti tartományt fogad el.","signature":"drivetrain.set_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_set_rotation","markdown":"# Set Rotation\n\nBeállítja a robot forgási szögét.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(VALUE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.set_rotation()` paranccsal beállítható a robot forgási szöge bármely adott pozitív vagy negatív értékre.","signature":"drivetrain.set_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity":{"name":"drivetrain_set_turn_velocity","markdown":"# Set Turn Velocity\n\nBeállítja a robot motor fordulatszámát.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(VELOCITY, PERCENT)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.set_turn_velocity()` parancs 0% és 100% közötti tartományt fogad el. Az alapértelmezett sebesség 50%.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a példa program a robotot 90 fokkal jobbra fordítja 25% -os sebességgel\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.set_turn_velocity(25, PERCENT)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```\n\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paramétereit nagybetűkkel írják","signature":"drivetrain.set_turn_velocity(50, PRECENT)"},"drivetrain_stop":{"name":"drivetrain_stop","markdown":"# Stop\n\nLeállítja a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.stop()` parancs hatására a robot teljesen leáll. A `drivetrain.stop()` parancs nem igényel paramétereket a zárójelében.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a példa program leállítja a robotot, miután 3 másodpercig ment előre.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\nwait(3, SECONDS)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paraméterei nagybetűkkel vannak megírva.","signature":"drivetrain.stop()"},"drivetrain_turn":{"name":"drivetrain_turn","markdown":"# Turn\n\nFolyamatosan jobbra vagy balra fordítja a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(DIRECTION)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.turn()` parancs folyamatosan elfordítja a robotot a DIRECTION iránynak megfelelően, amíg egy új parancsot nem kap a robot vagy a program le nem áll.\nA DIRECTION - irány, két értéket vehet fel LEFT - bal és RIGHT - jobb.\n\n## Példa \n\nEz a parancs folyamatosan jobbra fordítja a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(RIGHT)\n```\n\nEz a parancs folyamatosan balra fordítja a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn(LEFT)\n```\n\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paramétereit nagybetűkkel írják.","signature":"drivetrain.turn(RIGHT)"},"drivetrain_turn_for":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_for","markdown":"# Turn For\n\nMegfordítja a robotot egy adott szögben.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(DIRECTION, ANGLE, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nÁllítsa be a szöget és az irányt, amelyet a robotot el fogja fordítani, az alábbiak mindegyikének megadásával:\n\n- Direction - either RIGHT or LEFT - Irány - jobbra vagy balra\n- Angle - a numeric value - Szög - numerikus érték\n- Units - DEGREES - Egységek - FOKOK\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a parancs 90 fokkal jobbra fordítja a robotot.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```\n\nEz a példa a robotot 90 fokkal balra fordítja.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_heading":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_heading","markdown":"# Turn To Heading\n\nA robotot egy meghatározott irányba (szögbe) fordítja a beépített giroszkóp érzékelő segítségével.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_heading(ANGLE, UNITS)`\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` paranccsal a robot bármelyik óramutató járásával megegyező irányba fordulhat.\n\nA gyro szenzor aktuális iránya alapján a `drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` parancs határozza meg, melyik irányba forduljon.\n\nA `drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` **0,00 és 359,99** fok közötti tartományt fogad el.\n\nA `drivetrain.turn_to_heading()` képes tizedesjegyeket, egész számokat vagy numerikus blokkokat elfogadni. \n\n## Példa\n\nEz a parancs 45 fokkal jobbra fordítja a robotot\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(45, DEGREES)\n```\n\nEz a parancs 45 fokosra balra fordítja a robotot\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(315, DEGREES)\n```\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paramétereit nagybetűkkel írják.","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES)"},"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation":{"name":"drivetrain_turn_to_rotation","markdown":"# Turn To Rotation\n\nA robotot egy meghatározott forgási szögbe fordítja a beépített giroszkóp érzékelő segítségével.\n\n`drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(ANGLE, DEGREES)`\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation()` paranccsal a robot adott pozitív (óramutató járásával megegyező) vagy negatív (óramutató járásával ellentétes) értékre állítható.\n\nA robot aktuális **forgási** értéke alapján a `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation()` határozza meg, melyik irányba forduljon.\n\nA `drivetrain.turn_to_rotation()` képes tizedes vagy egész számok elfogadására.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a parancs 90 fokkal elfordítja a robotot\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES)\n```\n\nEz a parancs -45 fokkal visszafordítja a robotot\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(-45, DEGREES)\n```\n\nVegye figyelembe, hogy az egyes parancsok paramétereit nagybetűkkel írják.","signature":"drivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES)"},"function_name":{"name":"function_name","markdown":"# Functions\n\nA függvények egy művelet végrehajtására használt kódblokkok.\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nFunkció létrehozásához hozzon létre egy értelmes függvénynevet. Ez azt jelenti, hogy a függvény nevének pontosan le kell írnia, hogy mit csinál.\n\nHasználja a ** keyword ** `def` a függvény neve előtt a függvény meghatározásához.\n\nTartalmazzon zárójeleket \"()\", amelyek opcionális paramétereket tartalmaznak a függvény neve után, majd kettőspont következik \":\". Több paramétert vesszővel kell elválasztani a ((paraméter1, paraméter2).\n\nA függvény törzsét négy szóközzel kell beljebb vinni. Ha a végrehajtandó függvényhez nincs kód, akkor a függvénydefinícióba bele kell foglalni egy \"pass\" szót.\n\n```don\ndef my_function():\n    pass\n```\n\n## Példa 1\n\nEnnek a függvénynek nincsenek paraméterei, és a \"Hello\" szót írja ki a Print Console-ra.\n\n```don\ndef print_hello_world():\n    brain.print(\"Hello World\")\n```\n## Példa 2\n\nEnnek a függvénynek egy paramétere van, a \"name\" és a \"name\"-ben átadott értéket kiírja a Print Console-ra.\n\n```don\ndef print_name(name):\n    brain.print(name)\n```","signature":"def function_name(parameters):\\n\\tpass"},"looks_brain_clear":{"name":"looks_brain_clear","markdown":"# Clear\n\nTörli a Print Console összes sorát.\n\n```don\nbrain.clear()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nAz `brain.clear` parancs törli a Print Console összes sorát. \n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program egy másodpercig kiírja a Hellót, majd a Goodbye szó kiírása előtt törli a sorokat a Print Console-ban. \n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\nwait(1, SECONDS)\nbrain.clear()\nbrain.print(\"Goodbye\")\n```","signature":"brain.clear()"},"looks_brain_new_line":{"name":"looks_brain_new_line","markdown":"# New Line\n\nA kurzort egy új sorra állítja a Print Console-ban.\n\n```don\nbrain.new_line()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nAz `brain.new_line()` parancs a kurzort a Print Console következő sorára állítja.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program kiiratja a Drive szót, majd a kijelző következő sorában az Forward szót.\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Drive\")\nbrain.new_line()\nbrain.print(\"Forward\")\n```","signature":"brain.new_line()"},"looks_brain_print":{"name":"looks_brain_print","markdown":"# Print\n\nÉrtékeket vagy szöveget ír a Print Console-ra.\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"Hello\")\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `brain.print(“HELLO”)` parancs adatokat (szavakat és számokat) ír ki a Print Console-ra. Minden új program elkezdi kiírni a Print Console első sorát.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a példa kiírja a VEX- szöveget. Számok vagy szöveg kiiratásakor idézőjelekkel kell körülvenni őket (akár egy (’), akár a dupla („) idézőjelek működni fognak).\n\n```don\nbrain.print(\"VEX\")\n```\n\nEz a példa is kiírja a VEX szót. Figyelje meg, hogy mivel egy változó van a `brain.print()` parancson belül, a változót nem kell zárójelbe zárni.  \n\n```don\nmy_variable = \"VEX\"\nbrain.print(my_variable)\n```","signature":"brain.print(\"Hello\")"},"looks_brain_set_print_color":{"name":"looks_brain_set_print_color","markdown":"# Set Print Color\n\nBeállítja a Print Console-ra kiíratott szöveg színét.\n\n```don\nbrain.set_print_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nVálassza ki a kiiratni kívánt szöveg színét:\n\n- BLACK -fekete\n- RED - piros\n- GREEN - zöld\n- BLUE - kék\n\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a példa zöld színnel nyomtatja a \"GO!\", Piros színnel a \"STOP!\" Szót a Print Console-ra.\n\n```don\nset_print_color(GREEN)\nbrain.print(\"GO!\")\nset_print_color(RED)\nbrain.print(\"STOP!\")\n```","signature":"brain.set_print_color(BLACK)"},"looks_monitor_sensor":{"name":"looks_monitor_sensor","markdown":"# Monitor Sensor\n\nA Monitor érzékelő paranccsal érzékelőket adhat a monitor konzolhoz, így az érzékelő értékei láthatók és megfigyelhetők.\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"sensor_name\", \"another_sensor_name\", ...)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nAz érzékelő értékeinek figyeléséhez a monitor konzolon használja a `monitor_sensor()` funkciót úgy, hogy karakterlánc-paraméterként hozzáadja az érzékelők azonosítóit. Ezt a funkciót a program indításakor kell meghívni.\n\nAz alábbiakban a támogatott érzékelő-azonosítók listája található:\n\n- `brain.timer_time`\n- `drivetrain.is_done`\n- `drivetrain.is_moving`\n- `drivetrain.heading`\n- `drivetrain.rotation`\n- `left_bumper.pressed`\n- `right_bumper.pressed`\n- `distance.found_object`\n- `distance.get_distance`\n- `front_eye.near_object`\n- `front_eye.detect`\n- `front_eye.brightness`\n- `down_eye.near_object`\n- `down_eye.detect`\n- `down_eye.brightness`\n- `location.position`\n- `location.position_angle`\n\nHa érzékelőazonosítókat ad hozzá a `monitor_sensor()` parancshoz, azokat karakterláncként kell hozzáadni - ezért idézőjelekkel kell ellátni:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(\"left_bumper.pressed\")\n```\nA `monitor_sensor()` parancsba több vesszővel elválasztott érzékelő-azonosító adható:\n\n```don\nmonitor_sensor(“distance.get_distance”, “drivetrain.rotation”, “brain.timer_time”)\n```\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program a location.position érzékelő azonosítóját használja arra, hogy a VR robot X és Y pozícióját (mm-ben és hüvelykben) mutassa, ahogy egy négyzetben mozog. Ezenkívül a location.position_angle érzékelő azonosítóját is használja a VR robot szögének fokokban való megjelenítéséhez. Mindkét érzékelőazonosító a `motor_sensor()` parancsban található.  \n\n```don\ndef main():\n    monitor_sensor(\"location.position\",\"location.position_angle\")\n    for i in range(4):\n        wait(1, SECONDS)\n        drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\n```","signature":"monitor_sensor()"},"looks_monitor_variable":{"name":"looks_monitor_variable","markdown":"# Monitor Variable\n\nA Monitor Variable paranccsal változókat adhat hozzá a monitor konzolhoz, hogy a változók értéke látható és figyelhető legyen.\n\n```don\nmonitor_variable(\"my_variable\", \"my_boolean\", ...)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nVáltozók hozzáadásához a monitor konzolhoz használja a `monitor_variable()` parancsot. Adja hozzá a változó (k) nevét karaktersorozatként. Változók használatakor a `monitor_variable()` parancsban globális változóként kell beállítani őket.\n\n## Példa\n\nEbben a programban, valahányszor a VR Robot felvesz egy lemezt a Disk Mover Playgroundon, a diskCount változó értéke 1-gyel nő.\n\n```don\ndef main():\n    global diskCount\n    monitor_variable(\"diskCount\")\n    while diskCount<3:\n        wait(5, MSEC)\n        \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        while not down_eye.near_object():\n            wait(5, MSEC)\n        \n        magnet.energize(BOOST)\n        diskCount += 1\n        drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT,180,DEGREES)\n        \n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n        while distance.get_distance(MM)>150:\n            wait(5,MSEC)\n        magnet.energize(DROP)\n        \n        drivetrain.turn_for(LEFT,180,DEGREES)\n```","signature":"monitor_variable()"},"looks_pen_move":{"name":"looks_pen_move","markdown":"# Set Pen Position\n\nBeállítja a VR toll helyzetét.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nVálassza ki a VR toll helyzetét - **UP** vagy **DOWN**\n  \n**UP** - a toll nem fog vonalat húzni a palóra\n\n**DOWN** - a toll színes vonalat rajzol a padlóra\n\n## Példa\n\nEzzel a programmal a VR Pen fekete vonalat rajzol, miközben a robot halad előre.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"pen.move(DOWN)"},"looks_pen_set_pen_color":{"name":"looks_pen_set_pen_color","markdown":"# Set Pen Color\n\nBeállítja a VR toll színét.\n\n```don\npen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nVálassza ki a VR toll színét:\n\n - BLACK - fekete\n - RED - piros\n - GREEN - zöld\n - BLUE - kék\n\n \n## Példa\n\nEbben a példában a VR Pen kék vonalat rajzol, miközben a robot halad előre.\n\n```don\npen.move(DOWN)\npen.set_pen_color(BLUE)\ndrivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```","signature":"pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)"},"magnet_energize":{"name":"magnet_energize","markdown":"# Az elektromágnes bekapcsolása\n\nKétféle üzemmódba állítja a VR robot elektromágneset: **boost** vonzz vagy **drop** leejt.\n\n```don\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nÍrja be a zárójelbe a „BOOST” vagy a „DROP” szót.\n\n**BOOST**: vonzza a közeli mágneses tárgyakat az elektromágneshez\n**DROP**: elenged minden olyan tárgyat, amelyet az elektromágnes tart \n\n## Példa:\n\nA Disk Mover játszótéren, ebben a példában a robot 800 mm-t halad előre, felvesz egy tárgyat, majd 200 mm-t hátramegy és ledobja a tárgyat.\n\n```don\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 800, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(BOOST)\ndrivetrain.drive_for(REVERSE, 200, MM, wait=True)\nmagnet.energize(DROP)\n```","signature":"magnet_energize(BOOST)"},"sensing_brain_timer_reset":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_reset","markdown":"# Reset Timer\n\nAlaphelyzetbe állítja a robot Agy időzítőjét.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nAz agy időzítője minden program elején kezdődik. A `brain.timer_reset()` funkcióval visszaállíthatja az időzítőt 0 másodpercre.\n\n## Példa:\n\nEz a program a robotot addig hajtja előre, míg az agy időzítője kevesebb, mint öt másodperc. Az `brain.timer_reset()` paranccsal a program elején a robot Agy időzítőjét lehet alaphelyzetbe állítani.  \n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA várakozási wait parancsot ciklusban kell használni. \n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az utasítás kettőspontot tartalmaz a sor végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"brain.timer_reset()"},"sensing_brain_timer_time":{"name":"sensing_brain_timer_time","markdown":"# Timer Value\n\nJelzi másodpercekben a robot Agy időzítőjének értékét.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_time(SECONDS)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nAz időzítő a program indulásakor nulla másodperccel indul, és az időzítő értékét tizedesértékként adja vissza.\n\nA `brain.timer_time()` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, használható logikai kifejezésekben, vagy más parancsokban, amelyek numerikus értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.\n\n## Példa\n\nEz a program a robotot hajtja előre, míg a robot agy időzítőjének értéke kevesebb, mint öt másodperc. Az `brain.timer_reset()` parancsot a While ciklus feltétel előtt használjuk.\n\n```don\nbrain.timer_reset()\nwhile brain.timer_time(SECONDS) < 5:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```\n\nA várakozási parancsot ciklussal kell használni.\n\nFigyelje meg, hogy a while utasítás tartalmaz kettőspontot a végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"brain.timer_time(SECONDS)"},"sensing_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# Bumper Pressed\n\nJelzi, ha a jobb vagy a bal ütközőt benyomják. \n\n`bumper.pressed()`\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\n`bumper.pressed()` **true** ha az ütköző be van nyomva.\n\n`bumper.pressed()`  **false** ha az ütköző nincs benyomva.\n\nVálassza ki a használni kívánt ütköző érzékelőt. The VR Robot `right_bumper` és `left_bumper` van ellátva.\n\n```\nright_bumper.pressed()\nleft_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nA `bumper.pressed()` függvény ** true ** vagy ** false ** értéket ad vissza. Meghívható és hozzárendelhető változókhoz, feltételként használható a vezérlő utasításokhoz, vagy használható olyan funkciókban, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.\n\nVáltozó beállítása:\n\n```\nleft_bumper_pressed = left_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\nHasználata vezérlő utasításban:\n\n```\nif left_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Left bumper pressed\")\nif right_bumper.pressed():\n\tbrain.print(\"Right bumper pressed\")\n```\n\nÁtadás egy másik függvénynek:\n\n```\nbrain.print(left_bumper.pressed())\n```","signature":"bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_distance_found_object":{"name":"sensing_distance_found_object","markdown":"# Distance Found Object\n\nJelzi, hogy a beépített távolságérzékelő \"lát-e\" tárgyat vagy felületet a robot előtt. \n\n```python\ndistance.found_object()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\n`distance.found_object()` **True** értéket ad vissza, ha a beépített távolságérzékelő egy tárgyat vagy felületet lát látómezőjében az érzékelőtől 3000 mm-en belül.\n\n`distance.found_object()` **False** értéket ad vissza, ha a beépített távolságérzékelő nem észlel 3000 mm-en belül tárgyat vagy felületet.\n\nA `distance.found_object()` függvény **igaz** vagy **hamis** értéket ad vissza. Meghívható és hozzárendelhető változókhoz, feltételként használható a vezérlő utasításokban, vagy olyan funkciókban használható, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.\n\nVáltozó beállítása:\n\n```python\nfound_object = distance.found_object()\n```\n\nHasználata vezérlő utasításban:\n\n```python\nif distance.found_object():\n    brain.print(\"Found object\")\n```\n\nÁtadása egy másik függvénynek:\n\n```python\nbrain.print(distance.found_object())\n```","signature":"distance.found_object()"},"sensing_distance_get_distance":{"name":"sensing_distance_get_distance","markdown":"# Distance From\n\nJelzi a legközelebbi tárgy távolságát a távolságérzékelőtől.\n\n```python\ndistance.get_distance(UNITS)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nVálassza ki, hogy a `distance.get_distance (UNITS)` milliméterben vagy hüvelykben kerüljön-e megadásra, az„ UNITS ”paraméter helyett„ MM ”vagy„ INCHES ”paraméterrel.\n\n```python\ndistance.get_distance(MM)\ndistance.get_distance(INCHES)\n```\n\nA `distance.get_distance (UNITS)` függvény meghívható és hozzárendelhető változókhoz, használható összehasonlító utasításokban, vagy olyan funkciókban használható, amelyek a számértékeket veszik fel paraméterként.\n\nA változó beállítása:\n\n```python\nmy_distance_mm = distance.get_distance(MM)\n```\nÖsszehasonlító utasításban történő használata:\n\n```python\nif distance.get_distance(INCHES) < 1:\n    brain.print(\"Close to object\")\n```\n\nÁtadása egy másik függvénynek:\n\n```python\nbrain.print(distance.get_distance(INCHES))\n```","signature":"distance.get_distance(MM)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Down Eye fényérzékelő\n\nVisszaadja az tárgy fényerejét.\n\n```python\ndown_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` parancs 0 és 100 közötti numerikus értéket ad vissza. A fehér tárgyak 100% -os, a fekete tárgyak 0% -os fényerőt jelentenek. Az összes többi szín egy fényerőt jelent az értéktartomány között.  \n\n`down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, használható logikai kifejezésekben, vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek numerikus értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.","signature":"down_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Down Eye színérzékelő\n\nVisszaadja a Down Eye érzékelő által észlelt színt.\n\n```python\ndown_eye.detect(COLOR)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `down_eye.detect(RED)` parancs logikai értéket ad vissza. **True** értéket ad vissza, amikor a Down Eye érzékelő észleli a kiválasztott színt. **Hamis** értéket ad vissza, ha a Down Eye érzékelő a kiválasztott színtől eltérő színt érzékel. \n\nVálassza ki a Down Eye érzékelő színét az észleléshez:\n\n* NONE - nincs szín\n* RED - piros\n* GREEN - zöld\n* BLUE - kék\n\n`down_eye.detect(RED)` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, felhasználható logikai utasításokban vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.\n\n## Példa\n\nEgy példa a `down_eye.detect(GREEN)` parancsot logikai utasításként használják. Ha a `down_eye.detect (GREEN)` parancs **True** értékkel tér vissza, akkor a GREEN/Zöld felirat jelenik meg a Monitor Console-on. Ha a `down_eye.detect (GREEN)` parancs **False** értékkel tér vissza, akkor a Not Green/Nincs zöld jelenik meg a Monitor Console-on.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if down_eye.near_object(): \n        drivetrain.stop()\n        if down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n            brain.print(\"Green\")\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n            stop_project()\n    else:\n        brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n        brain.new_line()\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n```\n   \nA várakozási wait parancsot ciklusban kell használni. \n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if / else utasítás kettőspontot tartalmaz minden sor végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"down_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_down_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Down Eye közeli tárgy érzékelő\n\nVisszatér, ha a Down Eye sensor lefelé néző érzékelő elég közel van egy objektumhoz a szín észleléséhez.\n\n```python\ndown_eye.near_object()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `down_eye.near_object()` parancs logikai értéket ad vissza. **True** értéket ad vissza, ha a Down Eye érzékelő közel van egy olyan tárgyhoz, amelynek érzékelhető színei vannak, például egy Disk, lemez. **Hamis** értéket ad vissza, ha a lefelé néző érzékelő nincs elég közel egy érzékelhető színű tárgyhoz.  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` nem fogja tárgyként érzékelni a padlót (Playground floor), de érzékelni fogja a színeket (pl.: a tollrajzokat) a padlón (Playground floor).  \n\n`down_eye.near_object()` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, felhasználható logikai utasításokban vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként. \n\n## Példa\n\nEgy példa a `down_eye.near_object()` parancsot logikai utasításként használják. Ha a `down_eye.near_object()` parancs **True** értéket ad vissza, az előre menés leáll, a Monitor Console-on megjelenik a Near a Disk/Lemez közelében felirat és a program leáll. Ha a `down_eye.near_object´()` parancs a **False** értéket adja vissza, akkor a Not Near a Disk/Nincs lemez a közelben felirat jeleneik meg folyamatosan.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if down_eye.near_object(): \n        drivetrain.stop()\n        brain.print(\"Near a Disk\")\n        stop_project()\n    else:\n        brain.print(\"Not Near a Disk\")\n        brain.new_line()\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n\nA várakozási wait parancsot ciklusban kell használni. \n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if / else utasítás kettőspontot tartalmaz minden sor végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"down_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_drivetrain_heading":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_heading","markdown":"# Drive Heading\n\nVisszaadja a robot irányát a giroszkóp érzékelő jelenlegi szögpozíciójának felhasználásával.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.heading(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` a parancs számértéket ad vissza. A számérték (FOKOKBAN) nőni fog, ha az óramutató járásával megegyező irányban forog, és csökken, ha az óramutató járásával ellentétes irányban forog.\n\nA `drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` parancs **0.00 és 359.99** fok közötti értéket ad vissza.  \n\n`drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, használható logikai kifejezésekben, vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek numerikus értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.","signature":"drivetrain.heading(DEGREES)"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_done":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_done","markdown":"# Drive Is Done\n\nVisszatér, ha a robot befejezte mozgását.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.is_done()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` egy olyan parancs, amely logikai értéket ad vissza. **True** értékre tér vissza, amikor a robot motorjai befejezték mozgásukat.\n**False** értéket ad vissza, amikor a robot motorjai még mozognak/forognak.\n\n`drivetrain.is_done()` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, felhasználható logikai utasításokban vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.","signature":"drivetrain.is_done()"},"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_is_moving","markdown":"# Drive Is Moving\n\nJelzi, ha a motorok forognak\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` **True** igaz értékkel tér vissza, ha a Drivetrain's motorok forognak.\n\n`drivetrain.is_moving()` **False** hamis értékkel tér vissza, ha a Drivetrain's motorok nem forognak, állnak.\n\nA `drivetrain.is_moving()` függvény **True** vagy **False** értéket ad vissza. Meghívható és hozzárendelhető változókhoz, feltételként használható a vezérlő utasításokhoz, vagy használható függvényparaméterként.\n\nA változó beállítása:\n\n```python\nstill_driving = drivetrain.is_moving()\n```\n\nHasználata vezérlő utasításban:\n\n```python\nif drivetrain.is_moving():\n    brain.print(\"Still driving\")\n```\n\nÁtadás egy másik függvénynek:\n\n```python\nbrain.print(drivetrain.is_moving())\n```","signature":"drivetrain.is_moving()"},"sensing_drivetrain_rotation":{"name":"sensing_drivetrain_rotation","markdown":"# Drive Rotation\n\nVisszaadja a robot forgási szögét.\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` parancs számszerű értéket jelent. Pozitív értéket ad vissza, amikor a robot az óramutató járásával megegyező irányba fordul. Negatív értéket ad vissza, amikor a robot az óramutató járásával ellentétes irányba fordul.\n\n`drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, használható logikai kifejezésekben, vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek numerikus értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.","signature":"drivetrain.rotation(DEGREES)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Color Brightness\n\nJelzi a tárgy fényerejét az egyik színérzékelő szemről.\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)`\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\n`eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` egy tárgy fényerejét jelzi. A fehér tárgy 100% -os, a fekete tárgy 0% -os fényerőt jelentenek. Az összes többi szín egy fényerőt jelent az értéktartomány között.\n\nVálassza ki a színérzékelő szemet. Az „front_eye” a robot elé van szerelve és előre néz, míg az „down_eye” a robot alá és lefelé néz.\n\n```\nfront_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\ndown_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\nA `eye_sensor.brightness(PERCENT)` function egy 0 és 100 közötti értéket ad vissza, és hozzárendelhető változókhoz, használhatók összehasonlító utasításokban, vagy olyan függvényekben, amelyek paraméterként a számértékeket veszik fel.\n\nVáltozó beállítása:\n\n```\ncolor_brightness = front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\nÖsszehasonlító utasításban történő használata:\n\n```\nif down_eye.brightness(PERCENT) < 25:\n\tbrain.print(\"Dark color\")\n```\n\nÁtadása egy másik függvénynek:\n\n```\nbrain.print(front_eye.brightness(PERCENT))\n```","signature":"distance.distance(MM)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Color Sensing\n\nJelzi, ha az egyik színérzékelő szem egy adott színt észlel.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)`\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` ** true ** értéket ad vissza, ha a szenzor érzékeli a kiválasztott színt.\n\n`eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` ** false ** értéket ad vissza, ha az érzékelő (Color Sensor Eye) más színt észlel, mint a kiválasztott.\n\nVálassza ki a színérzékelőt. Az front_eye a robot elé van szerelve és előre néz, míg az down_eye a robot alá és lefelé néz.\n\n```\nfront_eye.detect(RED)\ndown_eye.detect(RED)\n```\n\nVálassza ki az észlelni kívánt színt. Cserélje ki a `COLOR` paramétert az érzékelni kívánt színre.\n\n```\nNONE - nincs szín\nBLACK - fekete\nRED - piros\nGREEN - Zöld\nBLUE - Kék\n```\n\n\nA `eye_sensor.detect(COLOR)` függvény ** true ** vagy ** false ** értéket ad vissza. Meghívható és hozzárendelhető változókhoz, feltételként használható a vezérlő utasításokban, vagy olyan funkciókban használható, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.\n\nVáltozó beállítása:\n\n```\nfound_red = front_eye.detect(RED)\n```\n\nHasználata vezérlő utasításban:\n\n```\nif down_eye.detect(GREEN):\n\tbrain.print(\"On top of green\")\n```\n\nÁtadása egy másik függvénynek:\n\n```\nbrain.print(down_eye.detect(BLUE))\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.detect(RED)"},"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Color Near Object\n\nJelzi, ha a Color Sensor Eye elég közel van egy tárgy színének észleléséhez.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()`\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` ** true ** értéket ad vissza, ha a színérzékelő közel van egy olyan tárgyhoz, amelynek érzékelhető színei vannak.\n\n`eye_sensor.near_object()` ** hamis ** értéket ad vissza, ha a színérzékelő még nincs elég közel egy érzékelhető színű tárgyhoz.\n\nVálassza ki a színérzékelő szemet. Az front_eye a robot elé van szerelve és előre néz, míg az down_eye a robot alá és lefelé néz.\n\n```\nfront_eye.near_object()\ndown_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nA `eye_sensor.near_object()` a függvény egy ** true ** vagy ** false ** értéket ad vissza, és változókhoz rendelhető, feltételként használható a vezérlő utasításokban, vagy olyan funkciókban használható, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.\n\nA változó beállítása:\n\n```\ncolor_obj_front = front_eye.near_object()\n```\n\nHasználata vezérlő utasításban:\n\n```\nif front_eye.near_object():\n\tbrain.print(\"Object in front\")\n```\n\nÁtadás egy másik függvénynek:\n\n```\nbrain.print(down_eye.near_object())\n```","signature":"eye_sensor.near_object()"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_brightness","markdown":"# Front Eye fényérzékelő\n\nVisszaadja a tárgy fényerejét.\n\n```python\nfront_eye.brightness(PERCENT)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` parancs 0 és 100 közötti numerikus értéket ad vissza. A fehér tárgyak 100% -os, a fekete tárgyak 0% -os fényerőt jelentenek. Az összes többi szín egy fényerőt jelent az értéktartomány között.\n\n`front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, használható logikai kifejezésekben, vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek numerikus értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.","signature":"front_eye.brightness(PERCENT)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_detect","markdown":"# Front Eye színérzékelő\n\nVisszaadja az első szem érzékelő, Front Eye sensor által észlelt színt.\n\n```python\nfront_eye.detect(COLOR)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `front_eye.detect(RED)` parancs logikai értéket ad vissza. **True** értéket ad vissza, amikor az elülső szem érzékelő észleli a kiválasztott színt. **False** értéket ad vissza, ha az Első szem érzékelő a kiválasztott színtől eltérő színt észlel.  \n\nVálassza ki az első szem érzékelő színét az érzékeléshez:\n\n* NONE - nincs szín\n* RED - piros\n* GREEN - zöld\n* BLUE - kék\n\n`front_eye.detect(RED)` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, felhasználható logikai utasításokban vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.\n\n## Példa\n\nEgy példa a `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` parancs logikai utasításként való használatára. Ha a `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` parancs **True** értékkel tér vissza, akkor a GREEN zöld kiírás kerül a Monitor Console-ra. Ha a `front_eye.detect(GREEN)` parancs visszatérési értéke **False** hamis, a Not Near an Object, nincs tárgy a közelben jelenik meg a Monitor Console-ban.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if front_eye.near_object(): \n        drivetrain.stop()\n        if front_eye.detect(GREEN):\n            brain.print(\"Green\")\n        else:\n            brain.print(\"Not Green\")\n            stop_project()\n    else:\n        brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n        brain.new_line()\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n   \nA várakozási wait parancsot ciklusban kell használni. \n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if / else utasítás kettőspontot tartalmaz minden sor végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"front_eye.detect(RED)"},"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object":{"name":"sensing_front_eye_sensor_near_object","markdown":"# Front Eye közeli tárgy érzékelő\n\nJelzi, ha az elülső szem érzékelő elég közel van (100 mm-en belül) egy tárgyhoz a szín észleléséhez.\n\n```python\nfront_eye.near_object()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `front_eye.near_object()` parancs logikai értéket ad vissza. **True** (igaz) értéket ad vissza, ha az elülső szem érzékelő közel van egy olyan tárgyhoz, amelynek érzékelhető színei vannak. **False** (hamis) értéket ad vissza, ha az elülső szem érzékelő nincs elég közel egy érzékelhető színű tárgyhoz.  \n\n`front_eye.near_object()` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, felhasználható logikai utasításokban vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként. \n\n## Példa\n\nEgy példa a `front_eye.near_object()` parancs logikai utasításként való használatára. Ha a `front_eye.near_object()` parancs **True** értékkel tér vissza, az előrehaladás leáll és a Near an Object azaz tárgy a közelben írodik ki a Monitor Console-ra és a program leáll. Amíg a `front_eye.near_object()` parancs **False** értékkel tér vissza, addig Not Near Object azaz nincs tárgy a közelben írodik ki a Monitor Console-ra folyamatosan.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    if front_eye.near_object(): \n        drivetrain.stop()\n        brain.print(\"Near an Object\")\n        stop_project()\n    else:\n        brain.print(\"Not Near an Object\")\n        brain.new_line()\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n```\n \nA várakozási wait parancsot ciklusban kell használni. \n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if / else utasítás kettőspontot tartalmaz minden sor végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"front_eye.near_object()"},"sensing_left_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_left_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# A baloldali ütköző érzékelő\n\nVissza jelez, ha megnyomják a baloldali ütközőt\n\n```python\nleft_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `left_bumper.pressed()` parancs visszatérési értéke Boolean (logikai) érték. Az érték igaz, **True** amikor az ütköző benyomódik és hamis, **False** amikor az ütköző nincs benyomva.\n\n`left_bumper.pressed()` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, felhasználható logikai utasításokban vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.  \n\n\n## Példa\n\nEgy példa a `left_bumper.pressed()` parancs logikai utasításként való használatára.  Ha a baloldali ütköző érzékelő (Left Bumper sensor) nincs benyomva, a robot (Drivetrain) megy előre. Ha az érzékelő (Left Bumper sensor) be van nyomva, a robot (Drivetrain) megáll.  \n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    if left_bumper.pressed():\n        drivetrain.stop()\n    else:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nA várakozási (wait) parancsot egy ciklusban kell használni. \n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if / else utasítás kettőspontot tartalmaz minden sor végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következiks","signature":"left_bumper.pressed()"},"sensing_location_position":{"name":"sensing_location_position","markdown":"# Pozíció \n\nJelzi a robot X vagy Y koordinátában lévő helyzetét.\n\n```python\nlocation.position(AXIS, UNITS)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)` számértékként adja vissza a VR robot X vagy Y koordinátában lévő helyzetét.\n\n`location.position(AXIS, UNITS)` a parancs változókhoz rendelhető, használható logikai kifejezésekben, vagy más parancsokban, amelyek numerikus értékeket vesznek paraméterként. \n\n## Példa\n\nPélda a location.position(AXIS, UNITS) parancs a VR robot kódolásához, hogy a 28-as számra lépjen a Számozott Rács Világjátéktéren (Numbered Grid World Playground). A location.position(AXIS, UNITS) parancsot logikai kifejezésként használják ebben a projektben. \n\n```python\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES)\nwhile location.position(X, MM) < 500:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n\ndrivetrain.stop()\ndrivetrain.turn_for(LEFT,90,DEGREES)\n\nwhile location.position(Y, MM) < -500:\n    drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n\ndrivetrain.stop()\n```","signature":"location.position(X, MM)"},"sensing_location_position_angle":{"name":"sensing_location_position_angle","markdown":"# A pozíció szöge fokban\n\nJelzi a robot szögét fokban.\n\n```python\nlocation.position_angle(DEGREES)\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `location.position_angle(DEGREES)` a parancs a VR robot aktuális szögét adja vissza szám (numerikus) értékként.  \n\n`location.position_angle(DEGREES)` parancs  változókhoz rendelhető, használható logikai kifejezésekben, vagy más parancsokban, amelyek numerikus értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.","signature":"location.position_angle(DEGREES)"},"sensing_right_bumper_pressed":{"name":"sensing_right_bumper_pressed","markdown":"# A jobb oldali ütköző érzékelő\n\nVissza jelez, ha megnyomják a jobb oldali ütközőt\n\n```python\nright_bumper.pressed()\n```\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA `right_bumper.pressed()` parancs visszatérési értéke Boolean (logikai) érték.  Az érték igaz, **True** amikor az ütköző benyomódik és hamis, **False** amikor az ütköző nincs benyomva.\n\n`right_bumper.pressed()` hozzárendelhető változókhoz, felhasználható logikai utasításokban vagy más olyan parancsokban, amelyek logikai értékeket vesznek fel paraméterként.  \n\n## Példa\n\nEgy példa a `right_bumper.pressed()` parancs logikai utasításként való használatára. Ha a jobb oldali ütköző érzékelő (Right Bumper sensor) nincs benyomva, a robot (Drivetrain) megy előre. Ha az érzékelő (Right Bumper sensor) be van nyomva, a robot (Drivetrain) megáll.\n\n```python\nwhile True:\n    wait(5,MSEC)\n    if right_bumper.pressed():\n        drivetrain.stop()\n    else:\n        drivetrain.drive(FORWARD)\n```\n\nA várakozási (wait) parancsot egy ciklusban kell használni. \n\nFigyelje meg, hogy az if / else utasítás kettőspontot tartalmaz minden sor végén - ez azt jelzi a Python számára, hogy egy utasításblokk következik","signature":"right_bumper.pressed()"},"variables_my_2d_list":{"name":"variables_my_2d_list","markdown":"# 2D lista változó\n\nA Lista változóhoz hasonlóan a 2D Lista változókat is több érték egyetlen változó nevében történő tárolására használják. A 2D lista változók azonban egynél több listát tárolnak. Beágyazott lista.Pl.: beagyazott = [\"hello\", 2.0, 5, [10, 20]]\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\nA 2D Lista változó alapértelmezett neve: \n\n```python\nmy_2d_list = [\n    [0,0,0],\n    [0,0,0],\n    [0,0,0]\n]\n```\n\nÁtnevezéskor a 2D List változók ugyanazokkal az átnevezési szabályokkal rendelkeznek, mint az összes többi változó:\n\n- A név nem tartalmazhat speciális karaktereket (pl. Felkiáltójel)\n- A név nem kezdődhet számmal\n- A név nem tartalmazhat szóközt\n- A név nem lehet fenntartott szó a VEXcode VR-ben (pl. Hajtás, Drivetrain)\n\nA kétdimenziós tömbök adatelemei két index segítségével érhetők el. Az index egy érték helye a Lista változóban. Az érték eléréséhez először használja a 2D lista nevét, majd a szögletes zárójelbe zárt két indexet. beagyazott = [\"hello\", 2.0, 5, [10, 20]], tehát brain.print(beagyazott[3][1]) , az eredmény:20","signature":"my_2d_list = [\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0],\\n\\t[0,0,0]\\n]"},"variables_my_boolean":{"name":"variables_my_boolean","markdown":"# Boolean (azaz logikai) változó\n\nÉrtéke vagy igaz, vagy hamis. **True** vagy **False** . A nevét a brit matematikusról, George Boole-ról kapta.\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA logikai változó értékének hozzárendeléséhez használja az = operátort.   \n\nA logikai változó alapértelmezett neve a my_boolean. A logikai változó átnevezésekor alkalmazza a következő szabályokat:\n\n- A név nem tartalmazhat speciális karaktereket (pl. Felkiáltójel)\n- A név nem kezdődhet számmal\n- A név nem tartalmazhat szóközt\n- A név nem lehet fenntartott szó a VEXcode VR-ben (pl. Hajtás, Drivetrain)","signature":"my_boolean = True"},"variables_my_list":{"name":"variables_my_list","markdown":"# Lista változó\n\nA listaváltozókat több érték egyetlen változóban történő tárolására használjuk.\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA Lista változó alapértelmezett neve: `my_list = [0,0,0]`\n\nÁtnevezéskor a listaváltozók ugyanazokkal az átnevezési szabályokkal rendelkeznek, mint az összes többi változó:\n\n- A név nem tartalmazhat speciális karaktereket (pl. Felkiáltójel)\n- A név nem kezdődhet számmal\n- A név nem tartalmazhat szóközt\n- A név nem lehet fenntartott szó a VEXcode VR-ben (pl. Hajtás, Drivetrain)\n\nHa egy Lista változóhoz rendel egy értéklistát, használja az = operátort, majd az értékeket vesszővel válassza el és tegye szögletes zárójelek közé. Ezek lesznek a lista elemei.  \n\nHa az értékek számok, akkor a vesszőknek közvetlenül a számok után kell lenni. \n\n`my_list = [0,1,2]`\n\nHa az értékek karakterláncok, akkor a vesszőknek az idézőjeleken kívül kell lenniük.\n\n`my_list = [“Drive”,”Forward”]`\n\nA List változó adatelemeihez index segítségével férhet hozzá. Az index egy érték helye a List változóban. A tömb belsejében lévő minden elemhez egy egész szám tartozik, kezdve a nullával. Az értékeket, amelyek a listát alkotják elemeknek nevezzük.\n\n## Példa\n\nA példa egy `my_list` nevű lista változót használ egy for ciklusban. A `my_list` értékei karakterláncok. Az y ciklusváltozó (iterátor) a for ciklusban. Ezért a for ciklus megismétli, majd kiírja a \"my_list\" listaváltozó minden elemét.\n\n```python\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", ‘100%', \"fun!!\"]\nfor y in my_list:\n    brain.print(y)\n    brain.new_line()\n    wait(5, MSEC)\n```\n\nAmikor a példát futtatjuk, a kimenet így néz ki:\n\n```python\nVEXcode VR\nis\n100%\nfun!!\n```\n\nEbben a példában nincs szükség for ciklusra, mert csak a `my_list` listaváltozó egyik elemét iratjuk ki. A lista egy elemének eléréséhez írjuk az elem indexét a szögletes zárójelbe a lista változó neve után.\n\n\n```python\nmy_list = [\"VEXcode VR\", \"is\", '100%', \"fun!!\"]\nbrain.print(my_list[3])\nwait(5, MSEC)\n```  \n\nAmikor a példát futtatjuk, a kimenet így néz ki:\n\n```python\nfun!!\n```","signature":"my_list = [0,0,0]"},"variables_my_number":{"name":"variables_my_number","markdown":"# Numerikus változó\n\nA numerikus változókat a numerikus értékek tárolására használják.\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nEgy numerikus változó értékének hozzárendeléséhez használja az = operátort.   \n\nA numerikus változó alapértelmezett neve a my_number. A numerikus változó átnevezésekor alkalmazza a következő szabályokat:\n\n- A név nem tartalmazhat speciális karaktereket (pl. Felkiáltójel)\n- A név nem kezdődhet számmal\n- A név nem tartalmazhat szóközt\n- A név nem lehet fenntartott szó a VEXcode VR-ben (pl. Hajtás, Drivetrain)\n\n## Példa\n\nEbben a példában a robot 150 mm-t halad előre, majd 90 fokkal jobbra fordul. \n\n```python\ndistance_to_travel = 150\nangle_to_turn = 90\n\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, distance_to_travel, MM)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, angle_to_turn, DEGREES)\n```\nEbben a példában két változót hoztunk létre, a nevük `distance_to_travel` és `angle_to_turn`.  Az = után értéket rendeltünk minden változóhoz. A változókat ezután felhasználjuk a `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD)` és `drivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT)` parancsokban.","signature":"my_number = 1"},"variables_my_string":{"name":"variables_my_string","markdown":"# Karaterlánc változó\n\nA karakterlánc-változókat a karakterláncok tárolására használják. A karakterek lehetnek számok, betűk és szimbólumok.\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nA karakterlánc-változó értékének hozzárendeléséhez használja az = operátort.   \n\nA karakterlánc változó alapértelmezett neve a my_string. A karakterlánc változó átnevezésekor alkalmazza a következő szabályokat:\n\n- A név nem tartalmazhat speciális karaktereket (pl. Felkiáltójel)\n- A név nem kezdődhet számmal\n- A név nem tartalmazhat szóközt\n- A név nem lehet fenntartott szó a VEXcode VR-ben (pl. Hajtás, Drivetrain)","signature":"my_string = \\\"Hello World\\\""},"vr_thread":{"name":"vr_thread","markdown":"# VR Thread\n\nPárhuzamos programok készítése VR Thread vagy szál függvény segítségével. A VR Thread függvényt a függvények globális szintű meghívására kell használni.\n\n## Hogyan kell használni\n\nBármelyik korábban elkészített függvényt átadhatja a `vr_thread()` függvénynek és globális szinten használhatja. De legalább egy függvény meghívása szükséges a program futtatásához. Nem kell minden függvényhívást végrehajtani a `vr_thread()` paranccsal, csak azokat, amelyeket globális szinten akar használni. Miért van erre szükség? Mert a robotunk egyszerre több dologra is képes. Pl.: tud egyszerre távolságot érzékelni, villogni, figyelni az ütközökre, tollat le/fel emelni és előre haladni. \n\nHa egynél több `vr_thread()` hívást hoz létre, akkor a függvények párhuzamosan fognak végrehajtódni. Így tudunk többszálú programot készíteni. Azaz párhuzamosan futó programokat.\n\nA függvények általában az általunk meghívott sorrendben fognak végrehajtódni. Ha azonban egy függvény várakozik, akkor a végrehajtást átadja a következő függvénynek, és folytatja, amikor a másik függvény befejeződik, vagy amikor a másik függvény is vár.\n\n```python\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nA Thread vagy szálak váltását okozó funkciók:\n\n```python\ndrivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_for(RIGHT, 90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_heading(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.turn_to_rotation(90, DEGREES, wait=True)\ndrivetrain.set_heading(0, DEGREES)\ndrivetrain.set_rotation(0, DEGREES)\nwait(1, SECONDS)\n```\nEgy másik metódus belsejében lévő `vr_thread()` metódus meghívása futásidejű hibát okoz. Pl.: Ha futtatja ezt a programot:\n\n```python\ndef main():\n    vr_thread(print_hello())\n\ndef print_hello():\n    brain.print(\"Hello\")\n\nvr_thread(main)\n```\nIlyen hibát fog kapni:\n\n```python\nError: Traceback (most recent call last):\n  line 9, in vr_thread\n  line 5, in main\nNameError: name 'print_hello' is not defined\n```\n\n## Példa 1\n\nEgyetlen függvényt készítünk, amelyet a globális térben hajtunk végre a `vr_thread()` segítségével. A `move_drivetrain()` függvénnyel a robot 200 mm-rel elmozdul, majd kiírja, hogy elkészült. (\"Done moving\")\n\n```python\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\n```\n## Példa 2\n\nMost hozzáadtuk a `move_pen_down()` függvényt. Ezzel a tollat lefelé mozgatja és üzenetet írki. A `vr_thread (move_pen_down())` **után** hajtjuk végre a `vr_thread (move_drivetrain())` hívást. Mivel azonban a `drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)` várakoztató függvény, ezért átvált a `move_pen_down()` funkcióra, amely lefelé mozgatja a tollat, mielőtt a robot elmozdulna.\n\n```python\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\nvr_thread(move_pen_down())\n```\n\n## Példa 3\n\nHa a `move_pen_down()` elemet áthelyezi a `move_drivetrain()` definiált függvénybe, akkor ezt sorozatosan hívjuk, így rajz nem lesz, mivel csak a mozgás befejezése után megy le a toll. Ez azt is megmutatja, hogy a többi függvényen belüli funkciók meghívása ugyanúgy elvégezhető, mint a normál funkcióhívás.\n\n```python\ndef move_drivetrain():\n    drivetrain.drive_for(FORWARD, 200, MM)\n    brain.print(\"Done moving\")\n    brain.new_line()\n    move_pen_down()\n\ndef move_pen_down():\n    pen.set_pen_color(BLACK)\n    pen.move(DOWN)\n    brain.print(\"Pen down\")\n    brain.new_line()\n\nvr_thread(move_drivetrain())\n```","signature":"vr_thread(function_name())"}}}};/* harmony default export */ __webpack_exports__["default"] = (data);

